/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/data/ReviewCritique.jsonl:48:{"decision": "accepted", "title": "Leveraging Automated Unit Tests for Unsupervised Code Translation ", "body_text": "\n\nL EVERAGING A UTOMATED U NIT T ESTS FOR U NSUPERVISED C ODE T RANSLATION\n\n Anonymous authors Paper under double-blind review\n\nA BSTRACT\n\n With little to no parallel data available for programming languages, unsupervised methods are well-suited to source code translation. However, the majority of unsu- pervised machine translation approaches rely on back-translation, a method devel- oped in the context of natural language translation and one that inherently involves training on noisy inputs. Unfortunately, source code is highly sensitive to small changes; a single token can result in compilation failures or erroneous programs, unlike natural languages where small inaccuracies may not change the meaning of a sentence. To address this issue, we propose to leverage an automated unit-testing system to \ufb01lter out invalid translations, thereby creating a fully tested parallel cor- pus. We found that \ufb01ne-tuning an unsupervised model with this \ufb01ltered data set signi\ufb01cantly reduces the noise in the translations so-generated, comfortably out- performing the state-of-the-art for all language pairs studied. In particular, for Java \u2192 Python and Python \u2192 C++ we outperform the best previous methods by more than 16% and 24% respectively, reducing the error rate by more than 35%.\n\n1 I NTRODUCTION\n\n Ancient languages such as COBOL still underpin much of the \ufb01nancial industry and government ser- vices. Their outdated structures and thinning developer bases induce costs and severely slow down development, prompting businesses to modernize their codebases. For instance, the Commonwealth Bank of Australia spent around $750 million over 5 years to migrate its COBOL codebase to a more recent language. More generally, most large companies own code written in several programming languages, which can hinder interoperability and make programmers less ef\ufb01cient. Automatic trans- lation systems could make codebase migrations faster and cheaper, and help programmers learn new languages or understand existing code. However, translation systems are not as effective for source code as for natural languages. Rule- based systems are still commonly used, but they are never exhaustive due to the considerable number of translation rules that should be written to translate every function and object from every standard library. Unlike in natural languages, there is little to no parallel data available for source code, making it impossible to train standard machine translation models. Recently, TransCoder (Roziere et al., 2020) showed that unsupervised methods can be used to translate source code. However, it is trained without any supervised signal and only learns the semantics of tokens from their contexts. As shown in Figure 1, it can confuse tokens that have different semantics in different languages, for instance the \ufb02oat division in Python and integer division in C++ and Java which use the token / or more subtle operator priority differences (e.g. Java prioritizes == over & unlike Python and C++). While small inaccuracies often merely hinder comprehension in natural languages, they often make the entire translation erroneous in the context of programming languages. TransCoder leverages back-translation (Sennrich et al., 2015), an effective data-augmentation scheme where the model translates source sequences to generate training data for the target-to- source direction, and vice versa. Although being highly effective in low-resource translation, back- translation also has issues, as the model is trained on potentially invalid input-output pairs. Neural machine translation models being highly sensitive to input noise (Belinkov & Bisk, 2017; Khayral- lah & Koehn, 2018), this can severely deteriorate the performance. Fortunately, many programming languages come with relatively mature tools and technologies for automated test data generation. In this paper, we propose to leverage these tools to guide the trans- 1 though it corresponds to the integer division in Java and to the \ufb02oat division in Python. In the third example, a function reversing a char array, TransCoder does not manage to translate the Java Stack object into the right Python object and uses the unsafe str parameter name. In all three cases, TransCoder-ST manages to leverage the semantic information contained in unit tests to translate the function correctly. lation process, weeding out unsuccessful translations, thereby increasing the overall con\ufb01dence in the machine translation process. The topic of automated test data generation has been active for over three decades in the software engineering research community (Myers, 1979; Miller & Spooner, 1976). There are now many existing mature tools for test data generation, both open source research tools (Fraser & Arcuri, 2011; Lakhotia et al., 2013; Cadar et al., 2008), and production testing systems (Alshahwan et al., 2018; Tillmann et al., 2014). Because of its pivotal impact on practical software engineering, auto- mated testing remains a highly active research area (Anand et al., 2013), with the result that future automated testing advances will lead to ongoing improvement in automated translation. We use one such open source automated test generation tool, EvoSuite (Fraser & Arcuri, 2011), in this paper. EvoSuite is a well-established test generation tool for Java which uses coverage metrics (Chekam et al., 2017) and mutation scores (Jia & Harman, 2011) to generate high-quality tests. It has been widely used in the Software Testing research literature for test data generation although it has not, hitherto, been used as part of an automated translation approach guided by mutation, the topic of the present paper. More generally, software testing tools have been largely ignored by the machine learning commu- nity. In this paper, we propose to use automatically created unit tests to guide unsupervised trans- lation models for programming languages. More precisely, we create unit tests automatically for a large number of functions from the source dataset. Since the unit tests are composed of simple inputs and asserts, they can easily be translated to semantically equivalent tests in the target languages us- ing simple scripts. Using our unit-tests and a pre-trained unsupervised translation model, we create parallel datasets by translating functions and selecting the translations that have the same semantics as the original function for the tested inputs. Overall, we make the following contributions: \u2022 We introduce a novel approach, TransCoder-ST (for Self-Trained), that leverages an au- tomated unit test generation pipeline to \ufb01lter out invalid translations and reduce the noise coming from the back-translation process in unsupervised machine translation. \u2022 We present two implementations of this approach (online and of\ufb02ine), and show that it sig- ni\ufb01cantly outperforms the previous state of the art in code translation on all the language pairs we considered. In particular, we improve the state of the art for translating between Java, Python and C++ by an average of 12.6% Computational Accuracy (CA@1), corre- sponding to an average relative improvement of 25.5%. For Python \u2192 C++, we improve the CA@1 by 24%, reducing the error rate by 35.7% compared to previous models. 2 \u2022 We generate multilingual unit tests for hundreds of thousands of Java functions and create a large parallel dataset of 135,000 parallel functions between Java, Python, and C++. \u2022 Our method is completely unsupervised and could easily be generalized to other program- ming languages and unit test creation tools.\n\n2 R ELATED WORK\n\n Unit Test Generation. Software testing is challenging due to the large number of possibilities to be tested, and the inherent cost of covering reasonable representative sample (Myers, 1979). When test design is performed by humans, the cost can be prohibitive so much research over the last three decades has focused on automating the process of test generation (Anand et al., 2013). Although automated test generation has been studied since the mid-1970s (Miller & Spooner, 1976), it was only in the last decade that industrial-strength tools have become widely available. There are now several test data generation tools for languages, including C (Cadar et al., 2008; Lakhotia et al., 2013) and Java (Fraser & Arcuri, 2011). Popular test data generation techniques include symbolic execution of the code (Cadar & Sen, 2013), dynamic execution guided by a \ufb01tness function (Harman et al., 2015), and hybrids of these two techniques (Baars et al., 2011). Recently, neural networks have also been used successfully to generate unit tests (Tufano et al., 2020). One of the most well-established and widely-used open source tools for test data generation is the EvoSuite system (Fraser & Arcuri, 2011). EvoSuite uses search based software engineering (SBSE) (Harman et al., 2012) to generate test cases. Like all SBSE techniques, EvoSuite is guided by \ufb01tness functions, in this case aimed at capturing the test suite\u2019s coverage of the code being tested. We use EvoSuite in our work for three reasons: it is publicly available in open source (thereby facilitating replication), it is under current active development (thereby supporting future work), and it is widely used by other researchers (thereby enabling interoperability). In order to assess the effectiveness of the test suites generated, we use mutation testing, a topic also widely-studied since the 1970s (DeMillo et al., 1978). A mutant is a version of the program into which a fault is deliberately inserted, thereby assessing the test suite\u2019s ability to detect this fault (Jia & Harman, 2011). For a given set of mutants and a test suite, the mutation score is de\ufb01ned to be the proportion of mutants for which the test suite distinguishes the behavior of the mutant from that of the original program. The mutant score is thus a proxy for the fault-revealing power of the test suite on a set of simulated faults (the mutants). Mutation scores have been empirically demonstrated to be correlated to real fault revelation (Chekam et al., 2017), motivating our adoption of this approach. Machine Learning for Programming Languages. In recent years, deep learning methods have been used to tackle various tasks in software engineering, with a particular interest in bug detection and repair (Wang et al., 2017; Chen et al., 2019; Allamanis et al., 2018; Tarlow et al., 2019; Murali et al., 2020; Dinella et al., 2020; Yasunaga & Liang, 2020; Tufano et al., 2019) and code comple- tion (Li et al., 2018; Liu et al., 2020; Kim et al., 2020; Svyatkovskoy et al., 2020). Unsupervised pre-training methods for code based on BERT (Kanade et al., 2020; Feng et al., 2020), BART (Ah- mad et al., 2021) or other objectives tailored to source code (Guo et al., 2020; Roziere et al., 2021) have shown strong results on benchmarks such as CodeXGLUE (Lu et al., 2021). Recently, Hendrycks et al. (2021) evaluated the competence of several language models for solving coding challenges. Chen et al. (2021) trained a large trained a large model to generate programs from docstrings and are able to solve 28.8% of the problems in their HumanEval dataset. They generate code solving problem statements written in natural language. The goal of code translation is also to generate code solving a speci\ufb01c problem, but the input (code written in a different programming language) is more precise and often more concise. Translation of Programming Languages Several studies used statistical methods to translate between programming languages. Early methods extracted parallel datasets and trained phrase- based models to translate between C# and Java (Nguyen et al., 2013; Karaivanov et al., 2014) or from Python 2 to Python 3 (Aggarwal et al., 2015). Later, Chen et al. (2018) proposed a tree-to-tree neural network to translate between CoffeeScript and JavaScript and between C# and Java using the dataset created by Nguyen et al. (2013). However, these approaches are limited to a few language 3 pairs for which small parallel datasets were created manually (e.g. C#-Java) or can be created with rule-based (e g. Python 2-Python 3 and CoffeeScript-JavaScript). Instead, Roziere et al. (2020) proposed TransCoder, an unsupervised model that leverages the prin- ciples of unsupervised machine translation (Lample et al., 2018), to translate between Python, Java and C++. They showed that their method outperforms well-established rule-based baselines, does not require any parallel data or expert knowledge, and can easily be generalized to other languages. They pre-trained their model with the Masked Language Modeling (MLM) objective of Devlin et al. (2018), and trained it with the denoising auto-encoding (DAE) (Vincent et al., 2008) and the back-translation (BT) (Sennrich et al., 2015) objectives. Later, Roziere et al. (2021) showed that augmenting MLM with a deobfuscation objective (dubbed DOBF) can substantially improve the performance of TransCoder. In the rest of the paper, we will refer to their model as DOBF. Even though unsupervised methods can be trained on large amounts of data, they sometimes lack the signal needed to differentiate between semantically different tokens that often occur in similar contexts (see Figure 1). There is a need for a method providing supervised signal directly related to the semantics of the code without manually crafted parallel datasets.\n\n3 M ETHOD 3.1 P ARALLEL DATA CREATION\n\n Parallel unit test generation: We use EvoSuite to automatically generate unit tests for Java func- tions. EvoSuite is a well-established open source tool for automated test generation in Java, which is still under active development and frequently used. It is designed for Java programs but its search- based technique is general and could be used for any programming language. Unit tests can be thought of as lists of inputs and asserts testing the semantics of a program (e.g., the output of the function, the side effects on its arguments such as sorting the input list). EvoSuite uses evolutionary methods to derive tests that maximize a criteria such as code coverage or mutation score. During its search, each candidate solution in EvoSuite is a test input. The candidate inputs are evolved using crossover and mutation, and \ufb01ltered by a \ufb01tness function (e.g., mutation score). With each genera- tion the \ufb01tness improves until it reaches a plateau or the budget is exhausted. The \ufb01nal test inputs are wrapped up as test cases. Each program is associated to a test suite containing a series of test cases. Figure 3 shows an example of a test case generated by EvoSuite. 4 . Parallel test suites selection: Some test suites created by EvoSuite only cover a few parts of the semantics of functions. We only trust the translations veri\ufb01ed by test suites which examine the function semantics thoroughly. We use the mutation score, which is the most effective test assessment metric in the literature (Jia & Harman, 2011), to pick out these test suites. The mutation score is computed through mutation testing, in which mutants (i.e., program variants with syntactic changes) are generated from the original program based on a set of transformation rules (more details in Appendix A.2). A mutant is said to be killed if at least one test from the test suite has different results on the mutant and the original program. Otherwise, the mutant is said to survive. The mutation score is the ratio of killed mutants. A test suite with a higher mutation score checks the code semantics more thoroughly. We adopt a strict strategy in test suite selection: we keep only the Unit test suites with a mutation score larger than 90% for building the parallel dataset. Parallel dataset building: The generated test suites can be used to test the semantics of programs written in any programming language as long as there is a clear mapping between the types of the output and parameters in the original language and the language of the translated unit tests. We transform the generated Java tests into C++ and Python tests with identical inputs and expected outputs and side effects (i.e., assertions). In practice, we selected the Java functions which can be compiled and run in isolation and with simple output and parameter types. These types are the Java primitive types (e.g. int , long , bool , float ...), standard data types (e.g. Integer , Double , String ...), array and List or ArrayList types of elements of supported types (e.g. double[] , List<Integer> ...). We use the best unsupervised translation models available for Java to Python and Java to C++ trans- lation, namely TransCoder (Roziere et al., 2020) for Java to C++ and DOBF (Roziere et al., 2021) for Java to Python. For each Java function, we generate 20 Python and C++ translations with beam search and select the \ufb01rst element in the beam that passes the unit tests. The created tests are exe- cuted against the translated functions. If all the tests pass, the Python and C++ functions have the same semantics assessed by the generated tests. Our method is illustrated in Figure 2.\n\n3.2 T RAINING METHOD\n\n Our parallel data generation method relies on a pre-existing model to translate from Java to Python and C++. There is little parallel data for these tasks and the best performing published models are unsupervised. TransCoder (Roziere et al., 2020) is trained using the MLM, denoising and back- translation objectives and is able to translate between Java, C++ and Python. DOBF (Roziere et al., 2021) provides clear improvements over TransCoder for translating between Java and Python but was not trained on C++. Therefore, we use DOBF to translate from Java to Python and TransCoder to translate from Java to C++. When \ufb01ne-tuning, we also reload these models. For DOBF, we initialize the C++ language embeddings with those of Java. The parallel examples we generate can be used to improve the performance of pre-existing trans- lation models. Since the number of examples we generate also depends on the performance of the translation model, it creates a positive feedback loop where improving the model allows to improve the parallel dataset which in turn can be used to improve the model again. We propose of\ufb02ine and online approaches to use our method to maximize the unsupervised translation performance. 5 Of\ufb02ine training. With the of\ufb02ine training method, we use the method described in Section 3.1 to create parallel Java \u2194 Python, Java \u2194 C++ and Python \u2194 C++ datasets using every input Java function we selected. For the \ufb01rst iteration, we \ufb01ne-tune the model on these parallel examples until convergence. We can iterate this process by selecting the best checkpoints for Java \u2192 Python and Java \u2192 C++ using the validation dataset and using them to generate new parallel datasets, which can in turn be used to train a better model. We iterate this process until convergence, i.e. when we see no signi\ufb01cant improvements on the validation set. Online training. With the online method, we create parallel examples on the \ufb02y while training the model. Compared to the of\ufb02ine method, it allows to always use the last model to generate new examples and it is much more convenient to automate. However, this process can be unstable if done naively. For instance, the model can start over-\ufb01tting only a few examples and stop generating anything that passes the unit tests for any other example. In order to stabilize the training, we follow Likhomanenko et al. (2020) and implement a cache mechanism storing the previous examples that passed the unit tests. At each step, the model can either train on parallel functions sampled from the cache or create new parallel functions to add to the cache. When an example is sampled, we remove it from the cache with a given probability. The online training allows the model to always bene\ufb01t from the performance of the latest model and the cache mechanism ensures that the model does not forget the correct examples that it was able to generate at previous time steps.\n\n3.3 E VALUATION\n\n In the context of natural languages, machine translation models are generally benchmarked against a reference solution using the BLEU score (Koehn, 2009; Bahdanau et al., 2014; Vaswani et al., 2017). Early studies on source code translation used the same metric to evaluate the quality of the generated functions (Nguyen et al., 2013; Karaivanov et al., 2014; Aggarwal et al., 2015; Barone & Sennrich, 2017), or the exact match score which requires the translation to be exactly equal to the ground truth (Chen et al., 2018). However, these metrics fail to capture the semantics of the code and typically correlate poorly with the correctness of the generated function, prompting the use of new metrics checking if the generated solution passes series of test cases (Kulal et al., 2019; Roziere et al., 2020; Hendrycks et al., 2021; Chen et al., 2021). We evaluate our models on the validation and test sets of TransCoder. It contains a few hundreds of parallel functions extracted from GeeksforGeeks along with associated unit tests. As our TransCoder and DOBF baselines, we evaluate our models with the CA@N metric, which checks if any of the top-N solutions proposed by the model passes all the corresponding unit tests. This metric can be computed independently of the beam size (as long as the beam size is greater or equal to N).\n\n4 E XPERIMENTS 4.1 T RAINING DETAILS\n\n Model architecture. We use a sequence-to-sequence model with attention composed of an en- coder and a decoder model with a transformer architecture (Vaswani et al., 2017). In order to pro- vide fair comparisons, we use the exact same architecture as TransCoder: an encoder and a decoder of 6 layers each, a hidden dimension of 1024 and 8 attention heads. We limit the size of the input to 512 tokens. Roziere et al. (2021) train models with two different architectures. For Java \u2194 Python, we compare ourselves to the version of DOBF using the same architecture as TransCoder. We ini- 6 tialize our models with either the best TransCoder checkpoint for Java \u2192 C++ or the best DOBF checkpoint for Java \u2192 Python with C++ language embeddings initialized with those of Java. Datasets. As TransCoder and DOBF, we use the GitHub public dataset available on Google Big- Query \ufb01ltered to keep only projects with permissive licences. As our unit test creation tool can only be used on Java code, we only use the Java \ufb01les and we select only the functions that can be com- piled in isolation. We obtain a dataset containing 333,542 Java functions. We run EvoSuite with a budget of 20 seconds and a criterion including the line, branch, cbranch and output coverages, as well as the weak and strong mutation scores. We set the maximum absolute value of integers that can be generated as an input to \u221a 2 31 \u2212 1 to limit the number of over\ufb02ows. We manage to obtain high-quality (mutation score > 0.9 and at least two asserts) test cases for 103,488 functions. See Figures 3 and 5, 6 in the appendix for examples of selected and \ufb01ltered out test suites. Training details. During the training, we alternate between batches for every source and target language so that language pairs for which we managed to create more parallel examples are not overrepresented in our training batches. For the online version, we set a cache warm-up parameter to ensure that we always generate new parallel examples if there are less than 500 examples in the cache for any language pair. Otherwise, we sample from the cache with probability 0 . 5 , or generate new examples, train on them once and put them in the cache also with probability 0 . 5 . The sampled elements are removed from the cache with probability 0 . 3 , so that each element we create is trained on about 4 times in average before being removed from the cache. We initialize the cache with parallel examples created of\ufb02ine. During beam decoding, we compute the score of generated sequences by dividing the sum of token log-probabilities by l \u03b1 where l is the sequence length. We found that taking \u03b1 = 0 . 5 (and penalizing long generations) leads to the best performance on the validation set.\n\n4.2 R ESULTS AND DISCUSSION\n\n Results. In Tables 2 and 3, we compare the results of our of\ufb02ine and online training methods with those of TransCoder and DOBF. DOBF outperforms TransCoder for the Java \u2194 Python pair. We compare our models against the best baseline for each language pair and direction. Training on the generated parallel examples brings substantial improvements for every language pair, direction, and metric. Of\ufb02ine training already provides clear improvements over the baseline after one iteration. The computational accuracy (CA@1) computed with beam size 10 is higher for every direction and it is substantially higher for the language pairs involving Python. It allows to reduce the error rate of the best baseline by 22.4% for Java \u2192 Python. In average, it increases the CA@1 by 7.9% over the best previous models, and reduces the error rate by 17.0%. In the second iteration, the model is trained on more than 25% more examples for Java \u2194 Python and C++ \u2194 Python and a few more examples for Java \u2194 Python (see Table 1). It results in improvements in every direction except for C++ \u2192 Java where there is no signi\ufb01cant improvement and C++ \u2192 Python where we observe a small regression. The average improvement over the \ufb01rst iteration is 1.5% point. 7 Although the model for the third iteration is trained on more parallel samples its performance on the test set of TransCoder is actually worse than after the second iteration. After two iterations, the model learned to generate more samples that pass the unit tests but some of them are actually incompatible with the types of translations expected by TransCoder (e g. example with over\ufb02ows in Figure 4) causing the computational accuracy score to go down The online self-training method provides further improvements over training on the pseudo-labeled examples of\ufb02ine. It outperforms every other method in every case except the second iteration of of\ufb02ine training for Java \u2192 C++. In average, this model outperforms the baseline by 12.6% points, corresponding to an error rate reduction of 25.5%. For Python \u2192 C++, it improves previous perfor- mance by more than 24% points, which corresponds to reducing the error rate by 35.7%. Examples of avoided errors can be found in Figure 1 and Appendix B. Overall, all our models signi\ufb01cantly improve previous results. As shown in Table 3, these improvements are stable across several beam sizes and CA@n metrics. The CA@20 metric shows that the number of examples for which none of the 20 elements in the beam are correct is reduced by more than 22% with online self-training. It indicates that, even though we train only on the output of the model, our method does much more than reordering the elements in the beam and allows the model to \ufb01nd correct solutions that were not assigned a high probability by the baseline model. See Table 6 in the appendix for more results. Ablation study. The results of our ablation study are shown in Table 4. Training online with no cache makes the training much less stable. The model improves at the beginning of training and we can select a few checkpoints where it performs well, but it ends up over-\ufb01tting a few examples it generated and the performance drops after a few epochs. Starting with an empty cache slows down the training and hinders generalization, leading to a clear drop in performance. We also try removing the minimum mutation score requirement for the model with no initial cache, which leads to even lower scores as the model is trained partly on lower-quality parallel data. 8 All these models were trained using a self-training beam size of 20 when generating new examples. Training with greedy decoding is much faster since computing the results for all the 20 elements of the beam is costly. However, generating new examples with greedy decoding leads to a loss of about two percentage points in average compared to our full model using beams of size 20. It shows that initializing the cache of the model with beam size 20 is not suf\ufb01cient and creating new examples with beam search is necessary to reach our best performance. Our full model provides some improvements over the ablated versions for every language pair and direction, except over the model trained with greedy decoding for Python \u2192 C++ translation. Evaluating with beam size 10 (still returning only the \ufb01rst element) leads to some improvements for every language pair. Limitations. We found that the unit tests we create with this method are sometimes incompatible with those of the test set of TransCoder, and that the capacity of a model to generate functions that pass these unit tests is not perfectly correlated to its score on the test set. It raises the deeper issue of de\ufb01ning what constitutes a correct translation. For instance, most programmers would translate a factorial function implemented with long integers into a factorial function implemented with Python\u2019s integer type. However, these functions are not semantically equivalent since the Java implementation would return a negative number for the input 21 due to integer over\ufb02ow while the Python implementation would return 21! correctly. The human developers who wrote the parallel functions in the test set of TransCoder often assumed that these functions would only be used on a limited domain where no over\ufb02ow occurs (see Figure 4). However, the test cases of EvoSuite and TransCoder are not limited to this domain and they sometimes assert different semantics. By using the test suites from EvoSuite as source of truth, we sometimes train the model to generate translations that are more rigorous but also less natural. static int factorial( int n){ if (n < 2) return 1; return n * factorial(n - 1); } def factorial(n): if n < 2: return 1 return n * factorial(n-1)\n\n5 C ONCLUSION\n\n In this paper, we introduced a novel method to grow a parallel corpus for automated code translation, from completely monolingual data. We leverage multilingual unit tests to \ufb01lter good pseudo-labels, improving the model, and in turn the candidate translations. We show that both of\ufb02ine and online methods substantially improve the state of the art in unsupervised code translation, with an average improvement of 12.6% points in computational accuracy, and up to 24% points for Python \u2192 C++, corresponding to translation error rate reductions of 25.5% and 35.7% respectively. Our method would automatically gain from improvements of automatic unit test generation tools. We could also increase the size of the dataset we generate by using test creation tools written for other languages than Java, or by generating tests with EvoSuite on translated examples. Similarly, we could also extract the semantics of human-written unit tests found in open-source projects to ob- tain larger, and possibly higher-quality datasets. In this paper, we focused on translation correctness and our parallel example validation criterion was only based on semantics. It could be supplemented with other requirements, such as a speci\ufb01c code formatting or the output of linters to generate code verifying arbitrary criteria. Finally, the approach presented in this paper could easily be transferred to natural languages. Although there is no concept of unit tests in natural language, traditional grammar and syntax checkers could be used to \ufb01lter out some incorrect generations, and reduce the noise coming from the back-translation process. Neural machine translation systems being highly sensitive to noise coming from parallel data (Belinkov & Bisk, 2017; Khayrallah & Koehn, 2018), this may improve the performance in low-resource machine translation signi\ufb01cantly. 9\n\nR EPRODUCIBILITY\n\n We made sure to use the same architecture and framework as previous works in source code transla- tion so that our results are comparable (see Section 4.1). We submit our code with this submission, along with a ReadMe \ufb01le detailing clear steps to reproduce our results, including a script to set-up a suitable environment. We will open-source our code and release our trained models. Our models were trained using standard hardware (Tesla V100 GPUs) and libraries (e.g. Pytorch, Cuda) for machine-learning research.\n\nE THICAL CONSIDERATIONS\n\n In this paper, we improve source code translation methods. Our methods could facilitate codebase migrations and interoperability, encouraging companies to move away from ancient programming languages and making software developers more ef\ufb01cient. Although increased ef\ufb01ciency could re- duce the number of developers needed to perform a task, its impact on the labor market is unclear as lower costs would also lower the bar for starting new projects and increase the demand for software engineers. Today, the demand for software engineering skills is high despite (or thanks to) the devel- opment of software (e.g. git, IDEs), programming languages (e.g. python), libraries (e.g. pytorch) and methodologies (e.g. continuous deployment) improving the ef\ufb01ciency of software developers, and we believe that the development of automatic translation tools would not drastically affect the prospects of software developers either. In the long term, the migration of codebases written in anti- quated programming languages (e.g. COBOL) could negatively impact experts in those languages, who are in particularly high demand at the moment. However, it would also bene\ufb01t society by facil- itating debugging and updating software still used in most of our \ufb01nancial transactions and for many government services, and by increasing the demand for other software engineering skills\n\n. R EFERENCES\n\n , 10 11 12 13\n\nA M ULTILINGUAL U NIT T ESTS C REATION A.1 O UR UNIT TESTS\n\n ( b e { int r = 1 while (e > 0) { if ((e & 1) == 1) r = r * b; b = b * b; e = e >> 1; } return r; } . .\n\nA.\n\n In mutation testing, mutants are programs transformed from the original programs based on a series of syntactic transformation rules called mutation operators. Mutation testing consists in introducing minor syntactic faults on the code and running the tests against the mutated code. A strong test suite is expected to detect the code changes by having at least one test failing. Table 5 shows the examples of mutation operators adopted in EvoSuite when generating mutants (Fraser & Arcuri, 2015). A mutant is said to be killed by a test case if the output of this test case on the mutant is different from its output on the original program (i.e., the test fails the mutant). Otherwise, the mutant is said to be survived. Figure 7 shows an example of a mutant generated by changing the < in the return statement into > . The test with input (-800, -800, -1), as shown by Figure 3, does not kill this generated mutant, because its outputs on the original program and the mutant are the same. 14 Mutation score is considered as the most effective criteria in accessing the fault-revealing ability of test suites. Other criteria, such as code coverage, are weak: they check only whether the test executes the code, but do not check whether the execution result is correct. A test suite without any assertions can achieve 100% code coverage, but could not detect any faults.\n\nB T RANSLATION EXAMPLES\n\n n } in similar contexts. Self-training helps the model to avoid such mistakes. 15 .min(minXor, arr[i]^arr[j]); return minXor; } int findLongestConseqSubseq( int arr[], int n ){ unordered_set< int > S; for ( int i = 0; i < n; i++) S.insert(arr[i]); int ans = 0; for ( int i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (S.find(arr[i]-1)==S.end()){ int j = arr[i]; while (S.find(j) != S.end()) j++; ans = max(ans, j - arr[i]); } } return ans; } public static int findLongestConseqSubseq( int arr[], int n ){HashSet<Integer> S = new HashSet<Integer>(); for ( int i = 0; i < n; i++) { S.add(arr[i]); } int ans = 0; for ( int i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (S.contains(arr[i] - 1)) { int j = arr[i]; while (S.contains(j)) { j++; }ans = Math.max(ans, j - arr[i]); } } return ans; } public static int findLongestConseqSubseq( int arr[], int n ){HashSet<Integer> S = new HashSet<>(); for ( int i = 0; i < n; i++) { S.add(arr[i]); } int ans = 0; for ( int i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (S.contains(arr[i] - 1)== false ){ int j = arr[i]; while (S.contains(j)) { j++; }ans = Math.max(ans, j - arr[i]) In the \ufb01rst example computed the mini- mum XOR between two elements of an array, TransCoder erroneously translates INT_MAX into Integer.MIN_VALUE . This value is used in similar contexts (i.e. to compute a maximum in- stead of a minimum) but is inappropriate here. TransCoder-ST manages to correct this and outputs a function with the right semantics. In the second example, where the function computes the size of the largest subset of elements of the list that could form a sequence of consecutive integers, TransCoder- ST manages to translate the semantics of S.find(arr[i]-1)==S.end() appropriately while TransCoder translates it into its negation. 16 (n < cl str was not trained on any semantic signal, manages to instantiate a priority queue object but instantiates a max queue instead of a min queue. TransCoder-ST, which was trained with some supervised signal directly linked to the semantics of the code, manages to instantiate the right type of priority queue. Input Java function TransCoder Python Translation TransCoder-ST translation static int divisorSum( int n) { int sum = 0; for ( int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) sum +=(n / i) * i; return sum; } def divisor_sum(n): sum = 0 for i in range(1 , n + 1): sum +=(n / i) ** i return sum def divisor_sum(n): sum = 0 for i in range(1 , n + 1): sum +=(n // i) * i return sum staticboolean check( int degree[], int n){ int deg_sum = 0; for ( int i = 0; i < n; i ++) { deg_sum += degree[i]; } return (2*(n-1)==deg_sum); } def check(degree, n): deg_sum = 0 for i in range(n): deg_sum += degree[i] return (2**(n-1)==deg_sum) def check(degree, n): deg_sum = 0 for i in range(n): deg_sum += degree[i] return (2*(n-1)==deg_sum) static int decimalToBinary( int N){ int B_Number = 0; int cnt = 0; while (N != 0) { int rem = N % 2; double c = Math.pow(10, cnt); B_Number += rem * c; N /= 2; cnt ++; } return B_Number; } def decimal_to_binary(N): B_Number = 0 cnt = 0 while N != 0: rem = N % 2 c 17 It leads to our model generating more templates (three times more often for our online model trained the longest). 18\n\nC E XTRA R ESULTS C.1 B EAM REORDERING\n\n We also evaluate a simpler method where we create unit tests for the Java functions in the test dataset and use them to reorder the elements of the beam at test time. We compute the results of the tests for every proposed C++ or Python translation and prioritize the elements that pass the unit tests. As shown on Table 6 reordering the elements of the beam at test time when translating from Java leads only to small improvements compared to the best baseline (up to 1.7% CA@1 for Java \u2192 Python) and the scores of this method are far from those obtained when requiring any of the 10 element of the beam to be correct (i e. CA@10). It can be explained by the fact that the tests generated by EvoSuite on these functions can have low mutation scores and be insuf\ufb01cient to thor- oughly test the semantics of the functions. Moreover, the tests we create are sometimes incompatible with those of our test set (see Figure 4 for an example) 19", "review#1": {"review": [{"segment_text": "Summary Of The Paper:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This paper is aimed at using the automatic generation of unit tests as a means to perform transpilation (i.e., the translation of code from one language to another).", "topic_class_1": "summary", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The authors demonstrate their technique from Java to Python and from Python to C++.", "topic_class_1": "summary", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "They claim that their approach outperforms prior methods by +16% and +24% over prior state-of-the-art.", "topic_class_1": "summary", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Result", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Main Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Overall, I like this paper.", "topic_class_1": "attitude & sentiment", "topic_class_2": "Reviewer Opinion", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The idea of using unit tests for transpilation is, although not formally guaranteed, a reasonable approach for transpilation in my opinion.", "topic_class_1": "positive comments to the ideas", "topic_class_2": "Novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "topic_class_1": "weakness on clarity", "topic_class_2": "Paper Writing", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It's my hope that if this paper is accepted, that the authors will polish the writing slightly.", "topic_class_1": "suggestion on writting", "topic_class_2": "Paper Writing", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "For example: \"translation systems are not as effective for source code as for natural languages.\"", "topic_class_1": "detail/example on misclaim", "topic_class_2": "Misleading Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This is a weird claim.", "topic_class_1": "detail/example on misclaim", "topic_class_2": "Misleading Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The work that Alvin Cheung (Berkeley) and JRK (MIT) for Maaz Ahmad (UW Washington / Adobe Research) is wildly effective and has automatically transpiled ~300 functions with a geometric mean performance improvement of 3.36x.", "topic_class_1": "detail/example on misclaim", "topic_class_2": "Misleading Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Moreover, this approach is formally verified unlike the authors approach.", "topic_class_1": "detail/example on misclaim", "topic_class_2": "Misleading Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "In addition, it's already being used in production quality code (Adobe Photoshop v.21).", "topic_class_1": "detail/example on misclaim", "topic_class_2": "Misleading Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "topic_class_1": "weakness: lack of literature review", "topic_class_2": "Literature Review", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "topic_class_1": "weakness: lack of literature review", "topic_class_2": "Literature Review", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "topic_class_1": "weakness: lack of literature review", "topic_class_2": "Literature Review", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "topic_class_1": "weakness: lack of literature review, reference", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "topic_class_1": "weakness summary, minor issuse", "topic_class_2": "Reviewer Opinion", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Summary Of The Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "TL;LD: it's an important emerging area.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the research area", "topic_class_2": "Significance of the Topic", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It's a reasonable (although not formal) way to perform transpilation.", "topic_class_1": "significance of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Using self-supervision is likely one of the most important aspects of the system because 99% of the code that we have today is currently unlabeled.", "topic_class_1": "significance of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "To my knowledge, only a handful of labeled datasets exist (e.g., POJ-104, Google Code Jam, IBM/MIT's Project CodeNet, Microsoft's CodeXGLUE, etc.)", "topic_class_1": "significance of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Weak accept.", "topic_class_1": "decision", "topic_class_2": "Overal Opinion", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}], "score": {"Correctness": " 1", "Technical Novelty And Significance": " 2", "Empirical Novelty And Significance": " 2", "Flag For Ethics Review": " NO.", "Recommendation": " 6", "Confidence": " 3"}}, "review#2": {"review": [{"segment_text": "Summary Of The Paper:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This work presents a way to fine-tune code translation models by providing weak indirect supervision from automated test case generation tools", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The proposed approach has 3 main components", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Components", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Pre-trained models for code translation: the authors use Transcoder and DOBF for this.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Components", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Automated test case generation tool: the authors use EvoSuite, an evolutionary computing based automated test case generation tool for Java, and", "topic_class_1": "summary of the tool", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Components", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Dataset: the authors collect approximately 330k Java code samples from Github with permissive licenses", "topic_class_1": "summary of the dataset", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Components", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The proposed approach then proceeds as follows:", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Pipeline", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It starts by creating unit tests for Java code samples using EvoSuite", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Pipeline", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It uses the generated expected input / output pairs to create unit tests for other languages -- Python and C++", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Pipeline", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The method then translates code samples from one language to another and then validates the generated translation using the unit tests created in the step above", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Pipeline", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "In this manner, the approach collects parallel translation data samples that are later used for fine-tuning the model", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method Pipeline", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The authors propose 2 variants of the method: offline and online", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "In the offline variant, the approach utilizes all the parallel code samples collected to fine-tune the model, while", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "In the online variant, the approach maintains a cache which is updated with new examples generated from the model", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Models fine-tuned on parallel data created in this manner show substantial increase in performance over TransCoder and DOBF models.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the model", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Main Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Strengths:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "A simple method is proposed to fine-tune existing code translation models without the need to manually curate parallel data", "topic_class_1": "summary of the strength", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The method does not require any changes to the training procedure of the model, and can be independently applied in the fine-tuning stage", "topic_class_1": "summary of the strength", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The method can potentially be applied to fine-tune a model for characteristics other than correctness.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the strength", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "for ex, runtime efficiency, code style, etc.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the strength", "topic_class_2": "Method Potential", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Results show significant improvement over the baseline TransCoder and DOBF models", "topic_class_1": "summary of the strength", "topic_class_2": "Result", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Weaknesses:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "topic_class_1": "question on alterantive method", "topic_class_2": "Concern about the Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "topic_class_1": "question on alterantive method", "topic_class_2": "Concern about the Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "topic_class_1": "question on assumption", "topic_class_2": "Concern about the Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Concern on the Quaity of Generated Unit Test", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Concern on the Quaity of Generated Unit Test", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Concern on the Quaity of Generated Unit Test", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Concern on the Quaity of Generated Unit Test", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Concern on the Quaity of Generated Unit Test", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Concern on the Quaity of Generated Unit Test", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the assupmtion", "topic_class_2": "Method Generalizability", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "topic_class_1": "weakness on evaluation", "topic_class_2": "Concern on Evaluation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on evaluation", "topic_class_2": "Concern on Evaluation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on evaluation", "topic_class_2": "Concern on Evaluation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on evaluation", "topic_class_2": "Concern on Evaluation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on evaluation", "topic_class_2": "Concern on Evaluation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Other concerns/Suggestions:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The authors mention that they collected data from GitHub with permissive licenses.", "topic_class_1": "data license issue", "topic_class_2": "Ask for Dataset Details", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Can the authors please share the list of licenses used for this data collection step?", "topic_class_1": "data license issue", "topic_class_2": "Ask for Dataset Details", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Having this information in the appendix will provide a reference point for future work in this space on which licenses might be acceptable to use when working with GitHub data.", "topic_class_1": "data license issue", "topic_class_2": "Ask for Dataset Details", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "[1] Tufano, Michele, et al. \"Unit Test Case Generation with Transformers.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2009.05617 (2020).", "topic_class_1": "reference", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "[2] Lukasczyk, Stephan, Florian Kroi\u00df, and Gordon Fraser.\"Automated Unit Test Generation for Python.\"International Symposium on Search Based Software Engineering.Springer, Cham, 2020.", "topic_class_1": "reference", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "[3] Vogl, Sebastian, et al.\"EVOSUITE at the SBST 2021 Tool Competition.\"2021 IEEE/ACM 14th International Workshop on Search-Based Software Testing (SBST).IEEE, 2021.", "topic_class_1": "reference", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "[4] Hendrycks, Dan, et al.\"Measuring Coding Challenge Competence With APPS.\"arXiv preprint arXiv:2105.09938 (2021).", "topic_class_1": "reference", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "[5] Chen, Mark, et al.\"Evaluating large language models trained on code.\"arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.03374 (2021).", "topic_class_1": "reference", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Summary Of The Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The proposed method achieve significant improvements over the baseline TransCoder and the DOBF models.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Result", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Weakness: Generalizability ", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Weakness: Generalizability ", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}], "score": {"Correctness": " 4", "Technical Novelty And Significance": " 2", "Empirical Novelty And Significance": " 2", "Flag For Ethics Review": " NO.", "Recommendation": " 5", "Confidence": " 4"}}, "review#3": {"review": [{"segment_text": "Summary Of The Paper:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The paper proposes a technique to generate parallel training data for machine translation between programming languages.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This data is generated based on specifics of the programming languages and the availability of programming tools in these languages.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Using this additional data, the paper shows that machine translation using state-of-the-art transformers between programming languages significantly improves over prior data collection methods with back-translation.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary of Result", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Main Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The paper is well written, easy to follow and presents an interesting and novel idea.", "topic_class_1": "writting", "topic_class_2": "Paper Writing, Novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Technically, the work touches many parts - \u201cstatic\u201d text-to-text transformer, executing code, checking tests passing, observing and optimising test coverage.", "topic_class_1": "research area", "topic_class_2": "Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The paper has quite some effort into it in getting precise results using different decoding strategies and has ablation studies on these factors.", "topic_class_1": "strength on method", "topic_class_2": "Experiment, Ablation Study", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "What would have been more interesting though is how well would it work with differences in the test generation/acceptance strategies.", "topic_class_1": "suggestion", "topic_class_2": "Result", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The experiment about dropping mutation scores is the most interesting one from this perspective.", "topic_class_1": "strength on ablation study", "topic_class_2": "Doubt on Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "If I understand correctly, it seems to have no huge effect on the results (other things like the cache are also ablated?).", "topic_class_1": "strength on ablation study", "topic_class_2": "Doubt on Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Probably a related question is if computing mutation scores is needed or one can replace it with much cheaper code coverage metrics.", "topic_class_1": "strength on ablation study", "topic_class_2": "Doubt on Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the lack of exploration of the external tools", "topic_class_2": "Question on Details of a Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the lack of exploration of the external tools", "topic_class_2": "Question on Details of a Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Question on Details of a Method Component", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Suggestion", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Question on Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on evaluation soundness", "topic_class_2": "Data Filtering", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "I would be happy to get a confirmation of this from the authors.", "topic_class_1": "ask for clarification", "topic_class_2": "Data Filtering", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Summary Of The Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The idea of the paper is specific to translation between programming languages and may be of interest to a subset of the ICLR community.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Review Summary\u2019s ", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "There are interesting ideas and overall good evaluation of the paper.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Overal Opinion", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}], "score": {"Correctness": " 3", "Technical Novelty And Significance": " 4", "Empirical Novelty And Significance": " 3", "Flag For Ethics Review": " NO.", "Recommendation": " 8", "Confidence": " 4"}}, "review#4": {"review": [{"segment_text": "Summary Of The Paper:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The paper introduces a pipeline to generate parallel data corpus for code-to-code translation instrumented with unit tests, starting with monolingual data in Java programming language.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "This allows to improve data quality over noisy backtranslation approach and subsequently improve the model accuracy finetuned on these data.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The approach is evaluated on the code-to-code translation against TransCoder baseline.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the paper", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The authors also introduce two methods (online and offline) to train on these data, substantially improving upon the state of the art in unsupervised code translation, and reducing the error rate.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the contribution", "topic_class_2": "Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Main Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The paper is well written and easy to understand.", "topic_class_1": "strength of writting", "topic_class_2": "Paper Writing", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It provides a very nice overview of related work and the history of software testing.", "topic_class_1": "strength of literature review", "topic_class_2": "Related Work", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The core methods is very practical and easy to reproduce.", "topic_class_1": "strength of reproducibility", "topic_class_2": "Method", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on novelty", "topic_class_2": "Literature Review", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on novelty", "topic_class_2": "Literature Review", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on novelty", "topic_class_2": "Literature Review", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Nevertheless, a technique that would work in a multilingual code-to-code translation setting is non-trivial and novel.", "topic_class_1": "strength on novelty (multilingual)", "topic_class_2": "Method Novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Generating a high-quality parallel corpus starting with monolingual data is non-trivial, it relies on a pre-trained translation model for the target task, and translating unit tests generated by EvoSuite from Java to target languages using heuristics and mapping of types.", "topic_class_1": "strength on novelty", "topic_class_2": "Method Novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Using mutation scores to select test suites is also novel.", "topic_class_1": "strength on novelty", "topic_class_2": "Method Novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Question on Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Question on Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Question on Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "topic_class_1": "weakness on the generalizability of the underlying assumption", "topic_class_2": "Question on Claim", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on error analysis", "topic_class_2": "Question on Error Analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "topic_class_1": "weakness on error analysis", "topic_class_2": "Question on Error Analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "topic_class_1": "weakness on error analysis", "topic_class_2": "Question on Error Analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "topic_class_1": "weakness on error analysis", "topic_class_2": "Question on Error Analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "topic_class_1": "weakness on error analysis", "topic_class_2": "Question on Error Analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "topic_class_1": "weakness on error analysis", "topic_class_2": "Question on Error Analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Maybe I am missing something, but why would generated test set mismatch to TransCoder tests set be an issue?", "topic_class_1": "question on clarity", "topic_class_2": "Question on Baseline, Comparison", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Are you generating tests sets for evaluation as well?", "topic_class_1": "question on clarity", "topic_class_2": "Question on Baseline, Comparison", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Does this change the baseline (published) TransCoder computational accuracy numbers?", "topic_class_1": "question on clarity", "topic_class_2": "Question on Baseline, Comparison", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Minor:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "For multiple published papers the authors cite corresponding arXiv pre-prints.", "topic_class_1": "citation suggestion", "topic_class_2": "Suggestion on Citation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "For instance:", "topic_class_1": "examples", "topic_class_2": "Suggestion on Citation", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Miltiadis Allamanis, Marc Brockschmidt, and M. Khademi. Learning to represent programs with graphs.ArXiv, abs/1711.00740, 2018 -> Published at ICLR 2018", "topic_class_1": "examples", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Dzmitry Bahdanau, Kyunghyun Cho, and Yoshua Bengio.Neural machine translation by jointly learning to align and translate.-> published at ICLR 2015 \u2026 etc", "topic_class_1": "examples", "topic_class_2": "Reference", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "maximize a criteria such as code coverage or mutation score -> \"maximize criteria such as code coverage or mutation score\"", "topic_class_1": "typo", "topic_class_2": "Typo, Grammer Issue", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "\u201cOtherwise, the mutant is said to be survived\u201d -> \u201cOtherwise, the mutant is said to have survived\u201d", "topic_class_1": "typo", "topic_class_2": "Typo, Grammer Issue", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "A1 caption (right), should perhaps be changed to \u201cA generated unit test suite\u201d", "topic_class_1": "writting suggestion", "topic_class_2": "Suggestion on Figure Caption", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "Summary Of The Review:", "topic_class_1": "section title", "topic_class_2": "Section Title", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "A well written paper that introduces a practical method to generate a supervised parallel corpus for code-to-code translation.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Review Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}, {"segment_text": "The findings are generally well supported by evaluation, but some additional tests could strengthen the paper.", "topic_class_1": "summary of the review", "topic_class_2": "Review Summary", "reliability": "Yes", "explanation": NaN, "error_type": null}], "score": {"Correctness": " 4", "Technical Novelty And Significance": " 3", "Empirical Novelty And Significance": " 2", "Flag For Ethics Review": " NO.", "Recommendation": " 8", "Confidence": " 4"}}}
/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/ai_assignments.json:1:[{"pair_id": 0, "paper_idx": 0, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For the AMA prompts on Appendix H, it seems that some tasks (DROP, SST, BoolQ, COPA) only do either prompt() or answer() stage, not both. How can we decide \"automatically\" when to do only prompt() or answer() or both depending on the target task?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 0}, {"pair_id": 1, "paper_idx": 0, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, this paper contains some unclearness, especially on weak supervision and how the predictions are mapped to the output space. See Weakness part for details.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 1}, {"pair_id": 2, "paper_idx": 0, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I think still I am a little not sure, also about whether re-formatting is necessary for aggregation to work well.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 2}, {"pair_id": 3, "paper_idx": 0, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, this paper contains some unclearness, especially on weak supervision and how the predictions are mapped to the output space. See Weakness part for details.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 1}, {"pair_id": 4, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 3}, {"pair_id": 5, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Novelty: The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 4}, {"pair_id": 6, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the lack of technical novelty & contribution makes this work slightly below the acceptance bar of the conference.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 5}, {"pair_id": 7, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 6}, {"pair_id": 8, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 7}, {"pair_id": 9, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One key question not answered in this context is how much of HTML structure analysis and understanding is truly required for these questions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 8}, {"pair_id": 10, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One key question not answered in this context is how much of HTML structure analysis and understanding is truly required for these questions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 8}, {"pair_id": 11, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I examined the supplementary material provided by the authors and only found the data files.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 9}, {"pair_id": 12, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Either code or more detailed experimental setup should be provided in the supplementary material", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 10}, {"pair_id": 13, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Either code or more detailed experimental setup should be provided in the supplementary material", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 10}, {"pair_id": 14, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is better to make the codes available for reproducing these results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 11}, {"pair_id": 15, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Models that are trained on task specific data to compare against is questionable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 12}, {"pair_id": 16, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 7}, {"pair_id": 17, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I expect to see more task-specific models such as StructuralLM [1], MarkupLM [2] that respect the tree structure of HTML (or some other methods mentioned in the paper) to be evaluated against.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 13}, {"pair_id": 18, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I expect to see more task-specific models such as StructuralLM [1], MarkupLM [2] that respect the tree structure of HTML (or some other methods mentioned in the paper) to be evaluated against.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 13}, {"pair_id": 19, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 6}, {"pair_id": 20, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 7}, {"pair_id": 21, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Basically, I need to see the following question answered: \"For specific web tasks, do I want to use a HTML specific model trained on the small dataset or just to fine-tune LLMs\"?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 14}, {"pair_id": 22, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 7}, {"pair_id": 23, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "On MiniWoB, it's not very surprising that using a larger model with pre-training, you can beat smaller models from scratch.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 15}, {"pair_id": 24, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 6}, {"pair_id": 25, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Novelty: The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 4}, {"pair_id": 26, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 6}, {"pair_id": 27, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 7}, {"pair_id": 28, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 7}, {"pair_id": 29, "paper_idx": 1, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the lack of technical novelty & contribution makes this work slightly below the acceptance bar of the conference.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 5}, {"pair_id": 30, "paper_idx": 2, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors should have tried to an ablation type of approach in equation 1, to check if concatenation alone, element wise dot product alone or absolute difference alone or a combination of any two would work better with the scoring function.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 16}, {"pair_id": 31, "paper_idx": 2, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors should have tried to an ablation type of approach in equation 1, to check if concatenation alone, element wise dot product alone or absolute difference alone or a combination of any two would work better with the scoring function.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 16}, {"pair_id": 32, "paper_idx": 2, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As mentioned in the paper, NLI models do not transfer well to out-of-domain NLI samples, not to mention non-NLI tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 17}, {"pair_id": 33, "paper_idx": 2, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, I do have some concerns about their claims that this helps in transfer learning to other NLI tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 18}, {"pair_id": 34, "paper_idx": 2, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As mentioned in the paper, NLI models do not transfer well to out-of-domain NLI samples, not to mention non-NLI tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 17}, {"pair_id": 35, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The ThinkSum framework, however, is too general such that almost all existing methods fall into this framework, if you think scoring is the Think step, and argmax is the sum step.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 19}, {"pair_id": 36, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Figure 1 is confusing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 20}, {"pair_id": 37, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Many new terminologies (in different colors) are introduced: some are completely unnecessary (e.g. product aggregation) and some do not have any clear explanations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 21}, {"pair_id": 38, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There is a lack of technical details. In particular, it is not exactly clear how the sum step is performed. If this follows a probabilistic framework, please write down the steps formally.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 22}, {"pair_id": 39, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Without clear writing, it is hard to evaluate novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 23}, {"pair_id": 40, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The writing is not clear, making it hard to evaluate the contribution of this paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 24}, {"pair_id": 41, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Without clear writing, it is hard to evaluate novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 23}, {"pair_id": 42, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The writing is not clear, making it hard to evaluate the contribution of this paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 24}, {"pair_id": 43, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In general, probabilistic inference may tend to become less reliable in this case so does that have a major influence on the SUM process.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 25}, {"pair_id": 44, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The ThinkSum framework, however, is too general such that almost all existing methods fall into this framework, if you think scoring is the Think step, and argmax is the sum step.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 19}, {"pair_id": 45, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The ThinkSum framework, however, is too general such that almost all existing methods fall into this framework, if you think scoring is the Think step, and argmax is the sum step.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 19}, {"pair_id": 46, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Despite the extensive demonstrations performed in this study, the authors need to present the ThinkSum paradigm in a more organized and clear way: even the core idea of ThinkSum is not clearly explained the examples throughout the paper do not help much explaining ThinkSum paradigm.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 26}, {"pair_id": 47, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Weaknesses One general issue may be how does the model scale up as the sets produced by the prompts to LLMs become large.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 27}, {"pair_id": 48, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This is doable for 5 choices, but not so much for 100 choices or more (in SQuAD-type QA, the output could be any combinations of tokens) which is extremely expensive.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 28}, {"pair_id": 49, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In general, probabilistic inference may tend to become less reliable in this case so does that have a major influence on the SUM process.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 25}, {"pair_id": 50, "paper_idx": 3, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The novelty may not be as high since it mainly is an integration of methods but the impact of the approach seems significant.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 29}, {"pair_id": 51, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The proposed method -- using contextual embeddings instead of static token embeddings in decoder for autoregressive retrieval -- is novel, intuitive, and inspiring.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 30}, {"pair_id": 52, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The proposed model follows the framework of generative retrieval models but applies contextualized token embeddings.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 31}, {"pair_id": 53, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main weakness comes from the experiment setup.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 32}, {"pair_id": 54, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The setup is questionable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 33}, {"pair_id": 55, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "GENRE uses BART-large whereas this paper uses T5, so the focus on comparing directly with GENRE seems a bit misguided.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 34}, {"pair_id": 56, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main KILT results only have the original GENRE (using a different pre-trained model, BART) and CGR (even though the authors listed DPR and SEAL, the numbers are not in the same comparable settings).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 35}, {"pair_id": 57, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "GENRE uses BART-large whereas this paper uses T5, so the focus on comparing directly with GENRE seems a bit misguided.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 34}, {"pair_id": 58, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "GENRE uses BART-large whereas this paper uses T5, so the focus on comparing directly with GENRE seems a bit misguided.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 34}, {"pair_id": 59, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Figure 1 is a bit confusing to me (especially Cape(1), Cape(2)).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 36}, {"pair_id": 60, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I can guess what it does, but it's never fully clear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 37}, {"pair_id": 61, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The proposed model follows the framework of generative retrieval models but applies contextualized token embeddings.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 31}, {"pair_id": 62, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The novelty contribution is somehow limited.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 38}, {"pair_id": 63, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The novelty contribution is somehow limited.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 38}, {"pair_id": 64, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The novelty contribution is somehow limited.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 38}, {"pair_id": 65, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The paper proposes a reasonable way to use external knowledge in generative retrievers, but the quality of innovation, experiments, and writing seems to fall below the standard of ICLR.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 39}, {"pair_id": 66, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Based on my inference, the approach is reasonable but not terribly surprising.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 40}, {"pair_id": 67, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Limited technological contribution.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 41}, {"pair_id": 68, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The proposed method makes sense but the contribution of adding a contextualized embedding space might be considered as light.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 42}, {"pair_id": 69, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Limited technological contribution.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 41}, {"pair_id": 70, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The proposed method makes sense but the contribution of adding a contextualized embedding space might be considered as light.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 42}, {"pair_id": 71, "paper_idx": 6, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear how the model guarantees a correct generation of key descriptors of a document, if the generative retrieval model and the contextual language model were not trained with it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 43}, {"pair_id": 72, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The tasks may be too simple / short / toy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 44}, {"pair_id": 73, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is quite hard to draw many conclusions about the best performing systems because their training strategies are not public, and it is not clear what types of task ambiguity have been seen during training.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 45}, {"pair_id": 74, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the paper relies heavily on opaque models, and it is a little hard to draw meaningful conclusions about the best performing systems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 46}, {"pair_id": 75, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The tasks may be too simple / short / toy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 44}, {"pair_id": 76, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The tasks may be too simple / short / toy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 44}, {"pair_id": 77, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Besides, the empirical observation and proposed fine-tuning method are pretty straightforward and not surprising.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 47}, {"pair_id": 78, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The observation that the best language model achieve similar performance to human on such ambiguous task is not surprising, even though the setting is synthetic and simple.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 48}, {"pair_id": 79, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As the authors mention, AmbiBench is a minimal complexity testbed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 49}, {"pair_id": 80, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, for the same reason, I am less convinced about the generality of findings reported here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 50}, {"pair_id": 81, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This paper only studies the ambiguity of a few toy tasks, which only require simple lexical understanding and the texts are synthetic and very short.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 51}, {"pair_id": 82, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear how these findings would extend to more complex task types.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 52}, {"pair_id": 83, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It would be more interesting to see what the ambiguity is in real-world NLP tasks, and how they can be handled.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 53}, {"pair_id": 84, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is quite hard to draw many conclusions about the best performing systems because their training strategies are not public, and it is not clear what types of task ambiguity have been seen during training.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 45}, {"pair_id": 85, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The reproducibility is unclear, as the authors do not mention whether the benchmark will be released.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 54}, {"pair_id": 86, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As the authors mention, AmbiBench is a minimal complexity testbed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 49}, {"pair_id": 87, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The six synthetic tasks are quite simplistic and limited in scope.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 55}, {"pair_id": 88, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear how these findings would extend to more complex task types.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 52}, {"pair_id": 89, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear how these findings would extend to more complex task types.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 52}, {"pair_id": 90, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "What are some other types of task ambiguities in NLP tasks, maybe more subtle than simply occluding a category name?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 56}, {"pair_id": 91, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the current study focuses on a very narrow type of ambiguity, and it's not clear how much these insights will extend to other types of task ambiguity.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 57}, {"pair_id": 92, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the current study focuses on a very narrow type of ambiguity, and it's not clear how much these insights will extend to other types of task ambiguity.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 57}, {"pair_id": 93, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As the authors mention, AmbiBench is a minimal complexity testbed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 49}, {"pair_id": 94, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This paper only studies the ambiguity of a few toy tasks, which only require simple lexical understanding and the texts are synthetic and very short.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 51}, {"pair_id": 95, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "What are some other types of task ambiguities in NLP tasks, maybe more subtle than simply occluding a category name?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 56}, {"pair_id": 96, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "What about in tasks other than sentence classification?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 58}, {"pair_id": 97, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It would be more interesting to see what the ambiguity is in real-world NLP tasks, and how they can be handled.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 53}, {"pair_id": 98, "paper_idx": 7, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Although it is an important challenge in fine-tuning language models, this paper only studies a few toy tasks and the proposed method is not novel.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 59}, {"pair_id": 99, "paper_idx": 8, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This part can be more convincing if the authors can provide more experiments and analyses.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 60}, {"pair_id": 100, "paper_idx": 8, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This part can be more convincing if the authors can provide more experiments and analyses.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 60}, {"pair_id": 101, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 61}, {"pair_id": 102, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 61}, {"pair_id": 103, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 61}, {"pair_id": 104, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 61}, {"pair_id": 105, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 62}, {"pair_id": 106, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 62}, {"pair_id": 107, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Although they do make sense and are reasonable choices for a multimodal framework, I see little novelty here.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 63}, {"pair_id": 108, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 62}, {"pair_id": 109, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Originality: Apart from some novelties of the pretraining objectives, the method reuses some of the existing architectures in a hierarchical way.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 64}, {"pair_id": 110, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 62}, {"pair_id": 111, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "UniDoc does not improve the performance of document classification tasks over previous methods.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 65}, {"pair_id": 112, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Although the authors argue that it attributes to the difference of OCR engines, they should explain the magnitude of this difference.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 66}, {"pair_id": 113, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "They say the difference between EasyOCR and Tesseract, but should report performance metric and qualitative analysis on the RVL-CDIP dataset to compare real Microsoft OCR and EasyOCR.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 67}, {"pair_id": 114, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I feel that the methods/model architectures are largely putting together existing pieces of work from vision and language fields.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 68}, {"pair_id": 115, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In Sec 3.1, the proposed feature extraction, quantization, and gated cross-attention modules are all rather straightforward variations of existing work (i.e., the essential mechanisms are not new).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 69}, {"pair_id": 116, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Originality: Apart from some novelties of the pretraining objectives, the method reuses some of the existing architectures in a hierarchical way.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 64}, {"pair_id": 117, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The methods are not novel/insightful as they are largely borrowing from existing work.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 70}, {"pair_id": 118, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One thing that appears weird to me is the frozen sentence encoders initialized with the weights trained on NLI and STS datasets--if they have been fine-tuned on those tasks, they may capture too corpus-specific features to generalize to other domains (like the new pre-training documents).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 71}, {"pair_id": 119, "paper_idx": 9, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There are concerns about the generalization ability of the model since it uses dataset-specific text encoders.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 72}, {"pair_id": 120, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Another relevant approach to model selection is to use some additional, held-out, tasks to find model hyper-parameters and reuse them on all other tasks without any additional tuning.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 73}, {"pair_id": 121, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors include comparisons with tuning on a held-out validation set for most, but not all experiments. I think it\u2019d be helpful to include those comparisons for all experiments and lean more on these comparisons over comparisons with random baselines. In particular, could you provide the results for Figure 3?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 74}, {"pair_id": 122, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A related question is are the few-shot datasets balanced? This might not affect LAMA (also another reason why LAMA is so different than other few-shot tasks) but it will other classification tasks like SuperGLUE experiments. In meta-learning literature shots usually mean the number of examples per class. So leave-one-out will leave out one example of one class and make the few-shot support data imbalanced, which can, in turn, prefer hyper-parameters that favor the minority class. The more appropriate thing to do would be leave-m-out where m is the number of classes. It will actually be useful to have an ablation over k for leave-k-out and in particular, I feel this might have more of a bearing on the experiments in section 3.5. In case the few-shot data is not balanced, which is not standard, then that introduces a lot of additional variability from unbalanced datasets (or choice of the few-shot examples/classes) that also confound the results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 75}, {"pair_id": 123, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Another relevant approach to model selection is to use some additional, held-out, tasks to find model hyper-parameters and reuse them on all other tasks without any additional tuning.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 73}, {"pair_id": 124, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I think this setting is termed as multi-distribution few-shot learning in section 2 and is also a reasonable strategy to select hyper-parameters.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 76}, {"pair_id": 125, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While this may not always be possible for prompt-based methods, it is suitable for other methods like those in section 4.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 77}, {"pair_id": 126, "paper_idx": 10, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the contribution of this work is limited by its scope. The results for true few-shot learning are interesting but are not widely applicable. Because most previous work, including those being criticized, focus on the multi-distribution or tuned few-shot learning settings and make assumptions like a validation set is available or optimal hyperparameters for other tasks could be re-used for the new few-shot tasks. Indeed, future works should be explicit about their model selection method to make fair comparison. But this has been a well-known issue for few-shot learning under additional assumptions. For example, researchers in computer vision have been working on datasets with standard splits for fair comparison [1]. The true few-shot learning setup might not be entirely impactful.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 78}, {"pair_id": 127, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "C. The novelty of the proposed method: It is true that using USE or BERT-Score in evaluating the quality of the generated adversarial samples is costly and cannot obtain promising results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 79}, {"pair_id": 128, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "C. The novelty of the proposed method: It is true that using USE or BERT-Score in evaluating the quality of the generated adversarial samples is costly and cannot obtain promising results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 79}, {"pair_id": 129, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I am worried about the novelty.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 80}, {"pair_id": 130, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The novelty may not be enough.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 81}, {"pair_id": 131, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Yet the proposed method is somewhat trivial since it only uses an ensemble of classifiers and uses the cosine similarity to calculate the final similarity score calculation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 82}, {"pair_id": 132, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The motivation is not clear. The paper found that previous encoders fail to deal well with antonyms, but it seems that the proposed method did nothing special about antonyms either.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 83}, {"pair_id": 133, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The motivation is not clear. The paper found that previous encoders fail to deal well with antonyms, but it seems that the proposed method did nothing special about antonyms either.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 83}, {"pair_id": 134, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Several technical decisions are not justified.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 84}, {"pair_id": 135, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Some designs need more justifications. E.g., what is the criterion when choosing the 7 datasets and why the threshold is just set as 2.5.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 85}, {"pair_id": 136, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "SPE can be considered as a sentence similarity metric.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 86}, {"pair_id": 137, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, this paper neither verifies its performance on standard sentence similarity benchmarks nor justifies that this metric is only suitable for adversarial attack situations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 87}, {"pair_id": 138, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There are several issues with the experiments", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 88}, {"pair_id": 139, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Quality: the paper has a good motivation that catches a small yet vital problem in adversarial attacks in texts and proposes a very simple method as a solution, yet the evaluation is somewhat unfair which may hurt the quality evolution of the method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 89}, {"pair_id": 140, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Yet the proposed method is somewhat trivial since it only uses an ensemble of classifiers and uses the cosine similarity to calculate the final similarity score calculation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 82}, {"pair_id": 141, "paper_idx": 11, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Yet the proposed method is somewhat trivial since it only uses an ensemble of classifiers and uses the cosine similarity to calculate the final similarity score calculation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 82}, {"pair_id": 142, "paper_idx": 12, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the presentation/clarity of the paper can be significantly improved, as suggested in the \u201cweakness\u201d section.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 90}, {"pair_id": 143, "paper_idx": 12, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the presentation/clarity of the paper can be significantly improved, as suggested in the \u201cweakness\u201d section.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 90}, {"pair_id": 144, "paper_idx": 12, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Besides, figure 5 would benefit from square-axis.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 91}, {"pair_id": 145, "paper_idx": 12, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main text should be self-contained and the reader should not have to rely on the appendix to understand the details.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 92}, {"pair_id": 146, "paper_idx": 12, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "\u2018We select 5 tasks where teacher can most significantly improve\u2026\u2019 - I don\u2019t understand the motivation behind these choices.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 93}, {"pair_id": 147, "paper_idx": 12, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the paper chooses to do so instead of reporting all datasets, especially because the paper did not report these two sets of 5 datasets separately and discuss how their results differ.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 94}, {"pair_id": 148, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The use of BERT-base scale models naturally leaves open the question of whether anything changes at larger scale.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 95}, {"pair_id": 149, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the fine-tuning paradigm becomes effective at these scales, recent research suggests that zero-shot capabilities tend to emerge at larger model sizes.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 96}, {"pair_id": 150, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Regardless of the RoBERTa-base experiments, it remains unclear how the results transfer to auto-regressive models, and how relevant they are to the web-scale pre-training of very large language models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 97}, {"pair_id": 151, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While experiments would be unrealistic here, I would appreciate a fairer and more in-depth discussion in the paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 98}, {"pair_id": 152, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I think the analysis of model size is quite helpful, since a natural question is whether or not these results are due to the ELECTRA small model being too small to benefit from pre-training on massive upstream datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 99}, {"pair_id": 153, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I think the analysis of model size is quite helpful, since a natural question is whether or not these results are due to the ELECTRA small model being too small to benefit from pre-training on massive upstream datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 99}, {"pair_id": 154, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I think the analysis of model size is quite helpful, since a natural question is whether or not these results are due to the ELECTRA small model being too small to benefit from pre-training on massive upstream datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 99}, {"pair_id": 155, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Second, there is no evidence presented in this paper that using the model confidence to select the correct model should lead to good ensemble performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 100}, {"pair_id": 156, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Is there any way to apply majority-vote style ensembling instead, to get around the issues with uncalibrated model confidence levels?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 101}, {"pair_id": 157, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Is there any way to apply majority-vote style ensembling instead, to get around the issues with uncalibrated model confidence levels?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 101}, {"pair_id": 158, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This is a general desired property of models, but not one that is guaranteed and in particular not one that is explored in this paper (at least not reported), which makes it it not very surprising that these experiments failed.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 102}, {"pair_id": 159, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The discussion on how to construct artificial long-term dependencies is very interesting, but it also is a little bit orthogonal to the remainder of the paper; at least with the current framing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 103}, {"pair_id": 160, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 104}, {"pair_id": 161, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In the introduction the authors make the strong claim that \u201c[...] the benefits from pretraining in this case cannot be attributed to knowledge transfer\u201d, while in Section 2 the authors are more nuanced and allow for the possibility that \u201csufficient knowledge is [...] present in the downstream dataset\u201d.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 105}, {"pair_id": 162, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I am aware that size doesn't explain it all, but I am also far from convinced that the corpora being downstream corpora makes an important difference here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 106}, {"pair_id": 163, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In the introduction the authors make the strong claim that \u201c[...] the benefits from pretraining in this case cannot be attributed to knowledge transfer\u201d, while in Section 2 the authors are more nuanced and allow for the possibility that \u201csufficient knowledge is [...] present in the downstream dataset\u201d.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 105}, {"pair_id": 164, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It seems possible that the models can acquire linguistic and factual knowledge from these datasets during pre-training, which cannot be learnt efficiently from only predicting the downstream task labels.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 107}, {"pair_id": 165, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I am aware that size doesn't explain it all, but I am also far from convinced that the corpora being downstream corpora makes an important difference here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 106}, {"pair_id": 166, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 104}, {"pair_id": 167, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 104}, {"pair_id": 168, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "That is, a baseline of pretraining on a general pretrain corpus of similar size to the downstream corpora is missing and is very important to shed light on the results here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 108}, {"pair_id": 169, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(2) Related to (1), these datasets are all text classification datasets that are pretty similar, so it's somewhat unsurprising to me that pre-training on downstream corpora works well when fine-tuning on other datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 109}, {"pair_id": 170, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 104}, {"pair_id": 171, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I am aware that size doesn't explain it all, but I am also far from convinced that the corpora being downstream corpora makes an important difference here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 106}, {"pair_id": 172, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 104}, {"pair_id": 173, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I do think the results are somewhat surprising and novel, although there's a bit of overclaiming (since I suspect that these results are largely due to the similarity of the downstream datasets).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 110}, {"pair_id": 174, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, I do think that this conclusion could be better-qualified (e.g., this is not a general conclusion, but one about text classification datasets).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 111}, {"pair_id": 175, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(1) It's not clear that the results answer the question of \"Our results suggest that in many scenarios, performance gains attributable to pretraining are driven primarily by the pretraining objective itself and are not always attributable to the incorporation of massive datasets\"", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 112}, {"pair_id": 176, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Is the conclusion from these results basically that you need far less pre-training in order to get good finetuning performance?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 113}, {"pair_id": 177, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Furthermore, it's not clear to me that this paper directly answers the question of whether pre-training data scale or pre-training objective is the key to better transfer performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 114}, {"pair_id": 178, "paper_idx": 13, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Furthermore, it's not clear to me that this paper directly answers the question of whether pre-training data scale or pre-training objective is the key to better transfer performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 114}, {"pair_id": 179, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Although the usage of documentation for code generation is novel, it is somewhat simple from a technical point of view.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 115}, {"pair_id": 180, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Beyond this, the technique itself appears to be essentially the same as prior work: they use standard retrieval techniques based on existing algorithms and pre-trained models, and their generation models are also models that have been used for code or text generation before.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 116}, {"pair_id": 181, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, in terms of technical novelty, already existing models and techniques are used for retrieval and generation.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 117}, {"pair_id": 182, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Quality: Well-designed and experiments and well-written work.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 118}, {"pair_id": 183, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The idea of retrieving documentation for prompting seems interesting and novel to me.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 119}, {"pair_id": 184, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, in terms of technical novelty, already existing models and techniques are used for retrieval and generation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 117}, {"pair_id": 185, "paper_idx": 14, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, in terms of technical novelty, already existing models and techniques are used for retrieval and generation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 117}, {"pair_id": 186, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The study is mainly empirical.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 120}, {"pair_id": 187, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors should provide more details about the three-layer neural network as the projection P (\u00b7).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 121}, {"pair_id": 188, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Second, the baselines used for comparison are not complete, which makes me concern about the effectiveness of the model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 122}, {"pair_id": 189, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "-- the motivation and contribution for XEVAL are great-- the explanation of the dataset is lacking.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 123}, {"pair_id": 190, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Presentation can be improved, especially the organization of the paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 124}, {"pair_id": 191, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "\u2013 fragmented", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 125}, {"pair_id": 192, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Model is largely based on prior work.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 126}, {"pair_id": 193, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Baselines: It is unfair to compare the proposed method against baselines like BERT which are not designed to produce fixed length encodings.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 127}, {"pair_id": 194, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Another point I just noticed, although the authors claimed that their model is better than SBERT, and did a comparison with SBERT-large, they did not compare with SBERT-base, which makes the conclusion unreliable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 128}, {"pair_id": 195, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Second, the baselines used for comparison are not complete, which makes me concern about the effectiveness of the model.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 122}, {"pair_id": 196, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There are many settings considered in the paper and it is unclear if the proposed contributions are truly significant due to weak baselines are differences in data used for training different methods.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 129}, {"pair_id": 197, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Ideally, the model should be compared against a skip-thought baseline or an unsupervised sentence representation learning method that uses the same resources for training.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 130}, {"pair_id": 198, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Data used for pre-training: It is difficult to gauge how good the method is in comparison to other models due to differences in the data used for pre-training.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 131}, {"pair_id": 199, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Ideally, the model should be compared against a skip-thought baseline or an unsupervised sentence representation learning method that uses the same resources for training.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 130}, {"pair_id": 200, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I would appreciate a better and fair comparison of the Skip-Thought model by using same transformer encoder and same training corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 132}, {"pair_id": 201, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Ideally, the model should be compared against a skip-thought baseline or an unsupervised sentence representation learning method that uses the same resources for training.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 130}, {"pair_id": 202, "paper_idx": 15, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There are still some concerns about the novelty of this paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 133}, {"pair_id": 203, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The hypothesis of the form \"This text talks about a female occupation\" refers to the text of the premise, rather than the events or entities in it.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 134}, {"pair_id": 204, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, it talks about the form of the premise, rather than its meaning.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 135}, {"pair_id": 205, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Of course, there's nothing wrong with this, but it breaks a crucial assumptions about how the NLI data (in particular the SNLI data) was sourced: the events and entities in the hypothesis refer to the events and entities in the premise as much as possible.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 136}, {"pair_id": 206, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 137}, {"pair_id": 207, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 137}, {"pair_id": 208, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The ground truth for the proposed examples seems to be neutral (occupations are neither male nor female), so I would like to know how often evaluated systems actually predict this.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 138}, {"pair_id": 209, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 139}, {"pair_id": 210, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 139}, {"pair_id": 211, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 139}, {"pair_id": 212, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 139}, {"pair_id": 213, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 140}, {"pair_id": 214, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 140}, {"pair_id": 215, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 140}, {"pair_id": 216, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 140}, {"pair_id": 217, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "2.The bias measurement forces the models to predict either entailment or contradiction, where in fact the ground truth answer, in my opinion, for the proposed NLI examples, is neutral.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 141}, {"pair_id": 218, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For all we know, the models are correctly predicting that with high probability, but the measurement is forcing a renormalization between entailment and contradiction examples (Section 3.2).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 142}, {"pair_id": 219, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The hypothesis of the form \"This text talks about a female occupation\" refers to the text of the premise, rather than the events or entities in it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 134}, {"pair_id": 220, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The hypothesis of the form \"This text talks about a female occupation\" refers to the text of the premise, rather than the events or entities in it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 134}, {"pair_id": 221, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, \"the doctor is operating\" becomes \"the teacher is operating\", which may not fit the realistic situation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 143}, {"pair_id": 222, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In the constructed dataset, the hypothesis is generated by templates and looks like the context is not very related to the premise.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 144}, {"pair_id": 223, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, it talks about the form of the premise, rather than its meaning.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 135}, {"pair_id": 224, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Of course, there's nothing wrong with this, but it breaks a crucial assumptions about how the NLI data (in particular the SNLI data) was sourced: the events and entities in the hypothesis refer to the events and entities in the premise as much as possible.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 136}, {"pair_id": 225, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, in most of NLI datasets, the premise and hypothesis are usually related.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 145}, {"pair_id": 226, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "So the domain of training set and their constructed evaluation set are different.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 146}, {"pair_id": 227, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In contrast, the word \"text\" in the hypotheses constructed in this work refers to the entire text of the premise, and not its entities and events.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 147}, {"pair_id": 228, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It can be reasonable for models to perform not well and have the bias on the evaluation set, since the domain changes a lot.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 148}, {"pair_id": 229, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One way to fix the issue is to change the hypothesis templates to use the same (or similar) words as the premises, and replace the occupation word with a gendered word.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 149}, {"pair_id": 230, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For instance, replace \"he\" or \"his\" in the existing hypothesis with \"she\" or \"her\" so you can have female hypothesis and male hypothesis.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 150}, {"pair_id": 231, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 139}, {"pair_id": 232, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 139}, {"pair_id": 233, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "When analyzing the results, the authors disregard the neutral case.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 151}, {"pair_id": 234, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Why do we have to force a choice between entail and contradict, when a stereotype-free model would actually predict neutral?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 152}, {"pair_id": 235, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 137}, {"pair_id": 236, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 137}, {"pair_id": 237, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The ground truth for the proposed examples seems to be neutral (occupations are neither male nor female), so I would like to know how often evaluated systems actually predict this.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 138}, {"pair_id": 238, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "When analyzing the results, the authors disregard the neutral case.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 151}, {"pair_id": 239, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The constructed dataset contains only entailment pairs. How about analyzing the contradiction cases as well?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 153}, {"pair_id": 240, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "When analyzing the results, the authors disregard the neutral case.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 151}, {"pair_id": 241, "paper_idx": 16, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This seems like it would be a problem for the \"delta P\" and \"B\" measurement.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 154}, {"pair_id": 242, "paper_idx": 18, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Calling this entire setup \u201czero-shot\u201d is a bit of a stretch.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 155}, {"pair_id": 243, "paper_idx": 18, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The generator can be seen as zero-shot for generating task-specific examples, but the fine-tuned classifier not so much.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 156}, {"pair_id": 244, "paper_idx": 18, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For this reason, the truly zero-shot baseline seems weak and it is unsurprising that the classifier fine-tuned on synthetic data outperforms it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 157}, {"pair_id": 245, "paper_idx": 18, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For this reason, the truly zero-shot baseline seems weak and it is unsurprising that the classifier fine-tuned on synthetic data outperforms it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 157}, {"pair_id": 246, "paper_idx": 18, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Not to mention the impact brought by prompts, decoding strategies, etc.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 158}, {"pair_id": 247, "paper_idx": 18, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For this reason, the truly zero-shot baseline seems weak and it is unsurprising that the classifier fine-tuned on synthetic data outperforms it.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 157}, {"pair_id": 248, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "(i) BERT is old now. We have already seen a lot of studies of BERT (in the so-called \u2018Bertology\u2019-literature), but isn\u2019t it time to move on?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 159}, {"pair_id": 249, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There\u2019s the popular alternatives (RoBERTa, GPT-2, t5, etc.), but also a lot of faster, fairer, more interpretable alternatives.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 160}, {"pair_id": 250, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Authors use just BERT as pretrained model and a LSTM with GloVe embeddings as part of the experimentation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 161}, {"pair_id": 251, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I think authors fail to properly state all the possible explanations to support the existence of transfer learning with scrambled or random input, If transfer learning were the explanation of why scrambled input keeps showing transfer learning capabilities an experiment training with the original data while tested on scrambled or vice versa is needed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 162}, {"pair_id": 252, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The analysis section can be largely improved. Three out of four experiments show inconclusive results.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 163}, {"pair_id": 253, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Also, the observations made are marginally novel or significant, there are not many new results compared to prior observations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 164}, {"pair_id": 254, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The title is about pre-trained models, but only BERT is discussed throughout the paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 165}, {"pair_id": 255, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "And the reason why LSTM is more robust is that the model size is very small such that it can relearn all weights.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 166}, {"pair_id": 256, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My concern is that these scrambling techniques might push your dataset outside of real word adversarial attacks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 167}, {"pair_id": 257, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(b) The shift does not seem to add value over other synthetic shifts that have already been studied, e.g., word-level shuffling, character-level shuffling, etc.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 168}, {"pair_id": 258, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The title is about pre-trained models, but only BERT is discussed throughout the paper.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 165}, {"pair_id": 259, "paper_idx": 19, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I would also have liked to see a stronger motivation for focusing on frequency.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 169}, {"pair_id": 260, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "S4.3 For contrastive baseline, I'm not sure how to interpret \"pairwise distant but internally compact\" in the context here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 170}, {"pair_id": 261, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, I have some concerns regarding to the presentation and the empirical results: (1) CCL+few shot data augmentation: the description is \"prompt with only an instruction and zero or more ID training examples\", then how many ID training examples are involved in CCL+few shot?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 171}, {"pair_id": 262, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "S5.1 it was a bit difficult to follow the various comparisons made in ref to Table 1.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 172}, {"pair_id": 263, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "E.g., I had a hard time figuring out what is being compared at \"This accuracy decrease is smaller\"", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 173}, {"pair_id": 264, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "If it is sensitive to noise, then why it renders descent results on the other three datasets?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 174}, {"pair_id": 265, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "If it is sensitive to noise, then why it renders descent results on the other three datasets?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 174}, {"pair_id": 266, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Improvements over the baselines are not fully convincing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 175}, {"pair_id": 267, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example, when discussing vanilla outperforming OE+NP (\"We attribute this behavior to the noisiness of generation: some novelty prompted examples are similar to ID examples\"), although I intuitively agree with this sentiment, it would be great to have some empirical support by quantifying example similarity.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 176}, {"pair_id": 268, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The new data augmentation, while resulting in performance improvements over kFolden and Contrastive, also has more dependencies, i.e., access to GPT3.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 177}, {"pair_id": 269, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "S5.3 Generator size is concerned only with the example generator, while the novel-label generator is always GPT3?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 178}, {"pair_id": 270, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "replace GPT-3 with GPT-2) impact the performance much.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 179}, {"pair_id": 271, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "What happens if one generates novel labels with smaller models?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 180}, {"pair_id": 272, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "What happens if one generates novel labels with smaller models?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 180}, {"pair_id": 273, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The new loss function outperforms OE mostly because OE seems to fail on TREC-10 (else, it's competitive).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 181}, {"pair_id": 274, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(3) Looking at Table 1, I am curious why OE+novelty prompting performs such badly on TREC-10.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 182}, {"pair_id": 275, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If it is sensitive to noise, then why it renders descent results on the other three datasets?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 174}, {"pair_id": 276, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The new loss function outperforms OE mostly because OE seems to fail on TREC-10 (else, it's competitive).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 181}, {"pair_id": 277, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "OE seems to do badly on TREC-10 for some reason, but it generally seems to match (or sometimes outperform) the new proposed loss on the other corpora.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 183}, {"pair_id": 278, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The biggest negative of the paper, in my view, is that the performance gains (particularly of the new loss function) are marginal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 184}, {"pair_id": 279, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The biggest negative of the paper, in my view, is that the performance gains (particularly of the new loss function) are marginal.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 184}, {"pair_id": 280, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "If the proposed method only works well on very large pretrained generative models, the computational cost could be extremely high, since the authors generate 100k OOD samples.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 185}, {"pair_id": 281, "paper_idx": 20, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors method can only work with text classification tasks, which is great, but --- some of the competing methods are more general, and the Wikitext + CCL method (which simulates the more general setting of not having GPT-3 but only an OOD set) only marginally outperforms prior solutions (kFolden, for example, doesn't even depend on the external set).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 186}, {"pair_id": 282, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Another concern I have is that the proposed model can only work in the scenarios where sentence-level representation is required.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 187}, {"pair_id": 283, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, as shown in Table 2, the proposed method is not better than SpanBERT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 188}, {"pair_id": 284, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "So, what are the advantages of the proposed method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 189}, {"pair_id": 285, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, as shown in Table 2, the proposed method is not better than SpanBERT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 188}, {"pair_id": 286, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "So, what are the advantages of the proposed method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 189}, {"pair_id": 287, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear how this model helps in practice since it is achieving similar performance as other models like SpanBERT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 190}, {"pair_id": 288, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My concern is the significancy of the results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 191}, {"pair_id": 289, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear how this model helps in practice since it is achieving similar performance as other models like SpanBERT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 190}, {"pair_id": 290, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since the improvement of the proposed method is marginal (~ 1% avg) and fine-tuning BERT on GLUE tasks might have high variance, authors should report the mean and std over multiple runs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 192}, {"pair_id": 291, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Employing span-level information in PrLMs was proposed in previous works such as SpanBERT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 193}, {"pair_id": 292, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Although the tasks between the two papers are different, the novelty of the paper is limited.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 194}, {"pair_id": 293, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "So, what are the advantages of the proposed method.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 189}, {"pair_id": 294, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My concern is the significancy of the results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 191}, {"pair_id": 295, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, as shown in Table 2, the proposed method is not better than SpanBERT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 188}, {"pair_id": 296, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As shown in Table 1, compared with the baselines, the proposed method does not bring remarkable promotion.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 195}, {"pair_id": 297, "paper_idx": 22, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since the improvement of the proposed method is marginal (~ 1% avg) and fine-tuning BERT on GLUE tasks might have high variance, authors should report the mean and std over multiple runs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 192}, {"pair_id": 298, "paper_idx": 24, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper is hard to follow and would benefit from a clearer focus rather than the broad range of topics covered here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 196}, {"pair_id": 299, "paper_idx": 24, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper is hard to follow and would benefit from a clearer focus rather than the broad range of topics covered here.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 196}, {"pair_id": 300, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Lack of statistical tests to evaluate significance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 197}, {"pair_id": 301, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In some cases improvements are quite small.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 198}, {"pair_id": 302, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In some cases improvements are quite small.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 198}, {"pair_id": 303, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I have some difficulty to assess the improvements that this approach brings.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 199}, {"pair_id": 304, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A detailed analysis of precision/recall would be useful, and whether the approach improves multi-class decision boundaries, or only the boundary with the negative class.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 200}, {"pair_id": 305, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Figure 1 illustrates that the decision boundaries learned by conventional classification models are \"open\", which makes it hard to distribute the universum class instances accurately.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 201}, {"pair_id": 306, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, I doubt if this is really the case -- in the context of using deep neural nets (DNNs) as the feature encoder, the data representations  (i.e., $\\boldsymbol{h}$) and decision boundaries ($f(\\boldsymbol{h})$) are jointly learned, so even if the universum class instances have quite different semantics, it should not be hard for DNNs to map them into one compact cluster in the last layer's representation space.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 202}, {"pair_id": 307, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There is little analysis to show how the learned boundaries can better model class boundaries.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 203}, {"pair_id": 308, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A detailed analysis of precision/recall would be useful, and whether the approach improves multi-class decision boundaries, or only the boundary with the negative class.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 200}, {"pair_id": 309, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A detailed analysis of precision/recall would be useful, and whether the approach improves multi-class decision boundaries, or only the boundary with the negative class.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 200}, {"pair_id": 310, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A detailed analysis of precision/recall would be useful, and whether the approach improves multi-class decision boundaries, or only the boundary with the negative class.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 200}, {"pair_id": 311, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper is unclear in many technical details.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 204}, {"pair_id": 312, "paper_idx": 25, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "the arguments are hard to follow, and there is little analysis on the results to support the arguments.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 205}, {"pair_id": 313, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The hierarchical relationship labels are not convincing enough. It is even difficult for humans to predict whether two sentences are from the same document. Enforcing the model to learn the \"same document\" label might cause the overfitting of the sentence representations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 206}, {"pair_id": 314, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the motivation for this idea is very vague -- how can one accurately/unambiguously define the real difference between a sentence and a paragraph, and the difference between a paragraph and a document?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 207}, {"pair_id": 315, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The hierarchical relationship labels are not convincing enough. It is even difficult for humans to predict whether two sentences are from the same document. Enforcing the model to learn the \"same document\" label might cause the overfitting of the sentence representations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 206}, {"pair_id": 316, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "If the distinction between sentences, paragraphs and documents is even hard to tell for humans, then I don't see how the so-called \"paragraph-level relationships\" newly introduced in TSP can actually make a big difference compared to a simple combination of previous methods (NSP+SOP).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 208}, {"pair_id": 317, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The ablation study also shows that order information is more important than hierarchical structure information.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 209}, {"pair_id": 318, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "If the distinction between sentences, paragraphs and documents is even hard to tell for humans, then I don't see how the so-called \"paragraph-level relationships\" newly introduced in TSP can actually make a big difference compared to a simple combination of previous methods (NSP+SOP).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 208}, {"pair_id": 319, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 210}, {"pair_id": 320, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The empirical gain of TSP does not seem convincing to me: Although TSP provides significant gains on GLUE/SuperGLUE average score, the gains are mainly from small/unstable tasks, like CoLA, MRPC, RTE and STS-B.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 211}, {"pair_id": 321, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 210}, {"pair_id": 322, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 210}, {"pair_id": 323, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 210}, {"pair_id": 324, "paper_idx": 26, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Quality: The contribution of the paper is not convincing due to the problematic empirical evaluation (see cons above).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 212}, {"pair_id": 325, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 213}, {"pair_id": 326, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 214}, {"pair_id": 327, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Many terms were introduced without definitions, the sentence frequently omitted important contexts, and syntax was often broken.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 215}, {"pair_id": 328, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The paper is somewhat hard to read due to syntactic/semantic issues, and I guess I possibly drops some context to assess the paper correctly.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 216}, {"pair_id": 329, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The biggest flaw is that it is unclear whether anything presented in the work is truly novel or unknown to the machine translation community.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 217}, {"pair_id": 330, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 214}, {"pair_id": 331, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 213}, {"pair_id": 332, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 213}, {"pair_id": 333, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 218}, {"pair_id": 334, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 218}, {"pair_id": 335, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 218}, {"pair_id": 336, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The work can be jointly summarized as \u201clanguage modeling can yield an initial translation model whose performance can boosted tremendously by back-translation\u201d, but no single aspect of this is new, nor is the success of the combination of the various pieces at all surprising to anyone who has been following the unsupervised translation literature over the past two years.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 219}, {"pair_id": 337, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Only one non-English language is considered.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 220}, {"pair_id": 338, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 221}, {"pair_id": 339, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 222}, {"pair_id": 340, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 223}, {"pair_id": 341, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 221}, {"pair_id": 342, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 223}, {"pair_id": 343, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Only one non-English language is considered.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 220}, {"pair_id": 344, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 224}, {"pair_id": 345, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Only one non-English language is considered.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 220}, {"pair_id": 346, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 224}, {"pair_id": 347, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The performance on these languages is crucial because the true value of unsupervised translation lies in low-resource language pairs, and their omission puts into question the validity of this method in realistic settings.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 225}, {"pair_id": 348, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 226}, {"pair_id": 349, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 227}, {"pair_id": 350, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 227}, {"pair_id": 351, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It would be more exciting, if we also observe the significant improvement of the proposed method on some distant language pairs, such as English-Chinese and English-Japanese.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 228}, {"pair_id": 352, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 226}, {"pair_id": 353, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, do we expect GPT-3 to also excel at Hindi < - - > English translation?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 229}, {"pair_id": 354, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 230}, {"pair_id": 355, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As the proposed method replaces the bilingual dictionary with few-shot amplification, it seems to me that the proposed method may achieve better performance on distant language pairs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 231}, {"pair_id": 356, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 232}, {"pair_id": 357, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 230}, {"pair_id": 358, "paper_idx": 27, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 232}, {"pair_id": 359, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The novelty of this paper is limited.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 233}, {"pair_id": 360, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Multiple choices prompting (MCP) has been used in other QA tasks, such as TruthfulQA and RACE.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 234}, {"pair_id": 361, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The novelty of this paper is limited.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 233}, {"pair_id": 362, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 235}, {"pair_id": 363, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 235}, {"pair_id": 364, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "No novel/brand new ideas.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 236}, {"pair_id": 365, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The submission is an analysis paper rather than finding something new.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 237}, {"pair_id": 366, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The novelty of this paper is limited.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 233}, {"pair_id": 367, "paper_idx": 28, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Multiple choices prompting (MCP) has been used in other QA tasks, such as TruthfulQA and RACE.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 234}, {"pair_id": 368, "paper_idx": 30, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Limitations of the work has not been discussed by the authors.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 238}, {"pair_id": 369, "paper_idx": 30, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The most important part, I think, is the data expansion part described in Section 2.2.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 239}, {"pair_id": 370, "paper_idx": 30, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 240}, {"pair_id": 371, "paper_idx": 30, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 241}, {"pair_id": 372, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the author didn't compare with tensor train decomposition due to time constraints.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 242}, {"pair_id": 373, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 243}, {"pair_id": 374, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 244}, {"pair_id": 375, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 245}, {"pair_id": 376, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 246}, {"pair_id": 377, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 246}, {"pair_id": 378, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Some other method(s) are missing in the related works.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 247}, {"pair_id": 379, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example: [1]", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 248}, {"pair_id": 380, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 243}, {"pair_id": 381, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I also recommend exploring the systems impact of running training and inference using tucker decomposed layers and why say 50x reduction in parameter count, does not lead to a 50x improvement in RPS (Table 4).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 249}, {"pair_id": 382, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Also, the presentation and the rationale behind the idea are not clear to me.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 250}, {"pair_id": 383, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 251}, {"pair_id": 384, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 251}, {"pair_id": 385, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 251}, {"pair_id": 386, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since the matrices across layers often have different scaling, and since tensor decomposition is approximating some reconstruction error (i.e. L2 in the case of SVD), it's not clear that performing decomposition with all the matrices makes sense.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 252}, {"pair_id": 387, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example, why do you jointly compress all the layers, including all the FFN weights and attention weights?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 253}, {"pair_id": 388, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 254}, {"pair_id": 389, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In addition, the reason for splitting each FFN weight matrix into 4 seems to make it possible to combine it with attention weights.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 255}, {"pair_id": 390, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 245}, {"pair_id": 391, "paper_idx": 31, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I realize that some of variants have already been studied in the literature (e.g. Mao et al., Noach and Goldberg), but since the setup is not identical, it is crucial that the proposed approach is compared against both II and III.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 256}, {"pair_id": 392, "paper_idx": 32, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This paper needs analyses in terms of how the proposed method addresses the catastrophic forgetting issue compared to baseline models, since the main claim of the RIFT is to tackle this issue.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 257}, {"pair_id": 393, "paper_idx": 33, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 258}, {"pair_id": 394, "paper_idx": 33, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Is the added robust prefix the same for the entire test set, or the same within a batch but different across batches, or unique for every test example?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 259}, {"pair_id": 395, "paper_idx": 33, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 260}, {"pair_id": 396, "paper_idx": 33, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 261}, {"pair_id": 397, "paper_idx": 33, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 260}, {"pair_id": 398, "paper_idx": 34, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 262}, {"pair_id": 399, "paper_idx": 34, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 263}, {"pair_id": 400, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 264}, {"pair_id": 401, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 265}, {"pair_id": 402, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 266}, {"pair_id": 403, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 267}, {"pair_id": 404, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 268}, {"pair_id": 405, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 269}, {"pair_id": 406, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 269}, {"pair_id": 407, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 268}, {"pair_id": 408, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 267}, {"pair_id": 409, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 270}, {"pair_id": 410, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 271}, {"pair_id": 411, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 272}, {"pair_id": 412, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 270}, {"pair_id": 413, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 272}, {"pair_id": 414, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 269}, {"pair_id": 415, "paper_idx": 35, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 271}, {"pair_id": 416, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 273}, {"pair_id": 417, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 274}, {"pair_id": 418, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 275}, {"pair_id": 419, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 276}, {"pair_id": 420, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 276}, {"pair_id": 421, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 277}, {"pair_id": 422, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 278}, {"pair_id": 423, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 279}, {"pair_id": 424, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 280}, {"pair_id": 425, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 277}, {"pair_id": 426, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 277}, {"pair_id": 427, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 277}, {"pair_id": 428, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 281}, {"pair_id": 429, "paper_idx": 37, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 282}, {"pair_id": 430, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 283}, {"pair_id": 431, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 284}, {"pair_id": 432, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 285}, {"pair_id": 433, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 286}, {"pair_id": 434, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 287}, {"pair_id": 435, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 284}, {"pair_id": 436, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 288}, {"pair_id": 437, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 289}, {"pair_id": 438, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 290}, {"pair_id": 439, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Lack of some human validation", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 291}, {"pair_id": 440, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 284}, {"pair_id": 441, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 292}, {"pair_id": 442, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 293}, {"pair_id": 443, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 283}, {"pair_id": 444, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 294}, {"pair_id": 445, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 295}, {"pair_id": 446, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 296}, {"pair_id": 447, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 297}, {"pair_id": 448, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 297}, {"pair_id": 449, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 294}, {"pair_id": 450, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Lack of some human validation", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 291}, {"pair_id": 451, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Lack of some human validation", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 291}, {"pair_id": 452, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 295}, {"pair_id": 453, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 298}, {"pair_id": 454, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 298}, {"pair_id": 455, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Lack of some human validation", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 291}, {"pair_id": 456, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 299}, {"pair_id": 457, "paper_idx": 38, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 300}, {"pair_id": 458, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 301}, {"pair_id": 459, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\".", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 302}, {"pair_id": 460, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 303}, {"pair_id": 461, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 304}, {"pair_id": 462, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 305}, {"pair_id": 463, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 306}, {"pair_id": 464, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 307}, {"pair_id": 465, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 308}, {"pair_id": 466, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 309}, {"pair_id": 467, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 310}, {"pair_id": 468, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 310}, {"pair_id": 469, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 311}, {"pair_id": 470, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 309}, {"pair_id": 471, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 312}, {"pair_id": 472, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 313}, {"pair_id": 473, "paper_idx": 39, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 309}, {"pair_id": 474, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 314}, {"pair_id": 475, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 315}, {"pair_id": 476, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 315}, {"pair_id": 477, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 316}, {"pair_id": 478, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 317}, {"pair_id": 479, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 316}, {"pair_id": 480, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 316}, {"pair_id": 481, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 318}, {"pair_id": 482, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 319}, {"pair_id": 483, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 320}, {"pair_id": 484, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 314}, {"pair_id": 485, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 321}, {"pair_id": 486, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 322}, {"pair_id": 487, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 323}, {"pair_id": 488, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 324}, {"pair_id": 489, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 325}, {"pair_id": 490, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 326}, {"pair_id": 491, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 314}, {"pair_id": 492, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 327}, {"pair_id": 493, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 328}, {"pair_id": 494, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 329}, {"pair_id": 495, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 330}, {"pair_id": 496, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 331}, {"pair_id": 497, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 332}, {"pair_id": 498, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 333}, {"pair_id": 499, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 333}, {"pair_id": 500, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 334}, {"pair_id": 501, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 328}, {"pair_id": 502, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 335}, {"pair_id": 503, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 336}, {"pair_id": 504, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 337}, {"pair_id": 505, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 337}, {"pair_id": 506, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 338}, {"pair_id": 507, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 339}, {"pair_id": 508, "paper_idx": 41, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 340}, {"pair_id": 509, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 341}, {"pair_id": 510, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 342}, {"pair_id": 511, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 343}, {"pair_id": 512, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 344}, {"pair_id": 513, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 345}, {"pair_id": 514, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 346}, {"pair_id": 515, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 347}, {"pair_id": 516, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 347}, {"pair_id": 517, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 347}, {"pair_id": 518, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 348}, {"pair_id": 519, "paper_idx": 42, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 348}, {"pair_id": 520, "paper_idx": 43, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 349}, {"pair_id": 521, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 350}, {"pair_id": 522, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 350}, {"pair_id": 523, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 350}, {"pair_id": 524, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 351}, {"pair_id": 525, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 350}, {"pair_id": 526, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 352}, {"pair_id": 527, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 353}, {"pair_id": 528, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 353}, {"pair_id": 529, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 354}, {"pair_id": 530, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 355}, {"pair_id": 531, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 354}, {"pair_id": 532, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 356}, {"pair_id": 533, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 356}, {"pair_id": 534, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 357}, {"pair_id": 535, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 351}, {"pair_id": 536, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 358}, {"pair_id": 537, "paper_idx": 44, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 359}, {"pair_id": 538, "paper_idx": 45, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 360}, {"pair_id": 539, "paper_idx": 45, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 360}, {"pair_id": 540, "paper_idx": 45, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 361}, {"pair_id": 541, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 362}, {"pair_id": 542, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 363}, {"pair_id": 543, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 363}, {"pair_id": 544, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 364}, {"pair_id": 545, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 365}, {"pair_id": 546, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Limited novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 366}, {"pair_id": 547, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 363}, {"pair_id": 548, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Limited novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 366}, {"pair_id": 549, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "(1) limited novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 367}, {"pair_id": 550, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 368}, {"pair_id": 551, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 369}, {"pair_id": 552, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 369}, {"pair_id": 553, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Limited novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 366}, {"pair_id": 554, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "(1) limited novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 367}, {"pair_id": 555, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 370}, {"pair_id": 556, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 371}, {"pair_id": 557, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 372}, {"pair_id": 558, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 370}, {"pair_id": 559, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 368}, {"pair_id": 560, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 371}, {"pair_id": 561, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 372}, {"pair_id": 562, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 373}, {"pair_id": 563, "paper_idx": 46, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 374}, {"pair_id": 564, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 375}, {"pair_id": 565, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 375}, {"pair_id": 566, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 376}, {"pair_id": 567, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 377}, {"pair_id": 568, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 378}, {"pair_id": 569, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 379}, {"pair_id": 570, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 380}, {"pair_id": 571, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 381}, {"pair_id": 572, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 382}, {"pair_id": 573, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 379}, {"pair_id": 574, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 378}, {"pair_id": 575, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 383}, {"pair_id": 576, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 384}, {"pair_id": 577, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 385}, {"pair_id": 578, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 377}, {"pair_id": 579, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 384}, {"pair_id": 580, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 386}, {"pair_id": 581, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 387}, {"pair_id": 582, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 386}, {"pair_id": 583, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 388}, {"pair_id": 584, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 389}, {"pair_id": 585, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 388}, {"pair_id": 586, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 390}, {"pair_id": 587, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 388}, {"pair_id": 588, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 391}, {"pair_id": 589, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 392}, {"pair_id": 590, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 384}, {"pair_id": 591, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 382}, {"pair_id": 592, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 379}, {"pair_id": 593, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 393}, {"pair_id": 594, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 379}, {"pair_id": 595, "paper_idx": 47, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 388}, {"pair_id": 596, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 394}, {"pair_id": 597, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 395}, {"pair_id": 598, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 396}, {"pair_id": 599, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 397}, {"pair_id": 600, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Three MLPs?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 398}, {"pair_id": 601, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 399}, {"pair_id": 602, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 400}, {"pair_id": 603, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Three MLPs?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 398}, {"pair_id": 604, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 401}, {"pair_id": 605, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 401}, {"pair_id": 606, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In Introduction you talk about the frozen weights of LLM but do not motivate why you decide to keep them frozen until the Discussion section.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 402}, {"pair_id": 607, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 403}, {"pair_id": 608, "paper_idx": 48, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 403}, {"pair_id": 609, "paper_idx": 49, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 404}, {"pair_id": 610, "paper_idx": 49, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 404}, {"pair_id": 611, "paper_idx": 49, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 404}, {"pair_id": 612, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 405}, {"pair_id": 613, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 406}, {"pair_id": 614, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As previously mentioned, the authors do not properly tackle the open question --- flat minima and generalization which might still seem to be controversial topic.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 407}, {"pair_id": 615, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 408}, {"pair_id": 616, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 408}, {"pair_id": 617, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One possible explanation is that the flatness could be a consequence of scaling up the model size, and the good performance is brought by the large model size, too.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 409}, {"pair_id": 618, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors need to have more evidence to prove that flatness is the main reason that leads to good performance, otherwise, they may be both the consequence of another factor (like model size).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 410}, {"pair_id": 619, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Second, while this paper points that pretraining loss is not a reliable indicator of downstream performance, a simple remedy is to evaluate on downstream tasks during pretraining and compare models accordingly, which is likely already done in practice.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 411}, {"pair_id": 620, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is understandable that large-scale experiments are expensive and are not expected, but given the rather empirical motivation of the paper, some validation on real data seems desirable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 412}, {"pair_id": 621, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Moreover, they need to tone down their claim since their theoretical analysis is limited to very specific controlled experiment which is far from realistic scenario.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 413}, {"pair_id": 622, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Although this paper tackles interesting research question, it is unclear that flatter minima actually leads to better generalizations since it does not compare relevant baselines and lack of justification.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 414}, {"pair_id": 623, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Although this paper tackles interesting research question, it is unclear that flatter minima actually leads to better generalizations since it does not compare relevant baselines and lack of justification.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 414}, {"pair_id": 624, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "maybe I am missing the point and I am happy to be corrected, but the arguments made in this submission or the observations obtained are already well-known empirically, especially in the case of zero-shot and few-shot learning with very large pre-trained transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 415}, {"pair_id": 625, "paper_idx": 50, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In addition, the theorems mentioned in the submission don't seem necessary, or, in any case, support the arguments or the observations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 416}, {"pair_id": 626, "paper_idx": 51, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 417}, {"pair_id": 627, "paper_idx": 51, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 417}, {"pair_id": 628, "paper_idx": 51, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Considering a GPT-2 is incorporated additional for data generation, it is not surprising to observe improvements.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 418}, {"pair_id": 629, "paper_idx": 51, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This can only show that using additional LM for data augmentation is useful and such a claim is provided in many existing papers.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 419}, {"pair_id": 630, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The motivation is clear but corresponding empirical analysis are missing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 420}, {"pair_id": 631, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 421}, {"pair_id": 632, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 421}, {"pair_id": 633, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 421}, {"pair_id": 634, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "From the Table 2 results, which use the WRENCH benchmark implementations (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.11377.pdf) the reported performance numbers don't seem to match what was reported in the original WRENCH paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 422}, {"pair_id": 635, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I have some concerns on the reported numbers (replicating WRENCH and reported above)", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 423}, {"pair_id": 636, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There are virtues to a simple method for improving performance, say an deep empirical dive into k-fold estimation when designing labeling functions, but this manuscript doesn't provide enough details to be very illuminating.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 424}, {"pair_id": 637, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As estimating T* and Y* is the core of ULF, the paper should have some analyses on whether T* and Y* are estimated more correctly after each iteration.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 425}, {"pair_id": 638, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "more analyses are needed to understand the quality of T* and Y*.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 426}, {"pair_id": 639, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Clarity: the paper is quite difficult to read as the cross-validation-based confidence estimation description is quite dense.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 427}, {"pair_id": 640, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper suffers from clarity issues.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 428}, {"pair_id": 641, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example, the feature-based and deep ULF methods need more methods elaboration and details", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 429}, {"pair_id": 642, "paper_idx": 52, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It's hard to draw any strong insights from the experiments as presented.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 430}, {"pair_id": 643, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Within the paper, this is left very vague.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 431}, {"pair_id": 644, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 432}, {"pair_id": 645, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 433}, {"pair_id": 646, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Moreover, it is impossible to learn C4 from the single WS example.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 434}, {"pair_id": 647, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Because there was a lack of analysis of the rule type, focusing only on an abstract idea of simplicity based on a particular method of compression, I found this unconvincing as a study of a general simplicity prior.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 435}, {"pair_id": 648, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This makes me unsure of the interpretation of the results in this paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 436}, {"pair_id": 649, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 437}, {"pair_id": 650, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 438}, {"pair_id": 651, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 437}, {"pair_id": 652, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 439}, {"pair_id": 653, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 440}, {"pair_id": 654, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 441}, {"pair_id": 655, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 441}, {"pair_id": 656, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 442}, {"pair_id": 657, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Most of the paper is pretty clear, although I had trouble understanding section 3.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 443}, {"pair_id": 658, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 437}, {"pair_id": 659, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 438}, {"pair_id": 660, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 439}, {"pair_id": 661, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 444}, {"pair_id": 662, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 445}, {"pair_id": 663, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 446}, {"pair_id": 664, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 446}, {"pair_id": 665, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 447}, {"pair_id": 666, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 432}, {"pair_id": 667, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 433}, {"pair_id": 668, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 442}, {"pair_id": 669, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 442}, {"pair_id": 670, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 448}, {"pair_id": 671, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "From the definition of \"capturing a concept\" in #154 we have \"iff for every S we have that p \udbff\udc11 S iff c \udbff\udc11 S.\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 449}, {"pair_id": 672, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 450}, {"pair_id": 673, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In what sense does accuracy improve once you've captured a concept?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 451}, {"pair_id": 674, "paper_idx": 54, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 440}, {"pair_id": 675, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In particular, the paper uses \"fairness\" without clearly explaining how fairness is measured.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 452}, {"pair_id": 676, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The fairness metric used seemed a bit ad hoc.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 453}, {"pair_id": 677, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, it is hard to understand the experiment sections without referring to the appendix in detail.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 454}, {"pair_id": 678, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 455}, {"pair_id": 679, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I had a hard time processing the multitude of experimental results. In particular, it's not clear what the numbers represent in each table.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 456}, {"pair_id": 680, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I would clearly describe in the caption of each figure what the rows/columns represent and what metric is used.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 457}, {"pair_id": 681, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Similar problems happen in Table 3.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 458}, {"pair_id": 682, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Not clear what WR50 means.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 459}, {"pair_id": 683, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Last but not least, I think \"fairness specifications\" is misleading and quite an overblown term for what the paper is about;", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 460}, {"pair_id": 684, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 461}, {"pair_id": 685, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The word choice of \"diversity\" sounds strange, given the evaluation criterion.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 462}, {"pair_id": 686, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 461}, {"pair_id": 687, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 455}, {"pair_id": 688, "paper_idx": 55, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For weaknesses, the authors could be clearer on what fairness metric is being used for tables such as Table D.3.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 463}, {"pair_id": 689, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 464}, {"pair_id": 690, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "CodeGen model (single-turn) is only evaluated on a single dataset with only 164 problems. Why is the model not evaluated on other publicly available coding datasets such as MBPP and APPS?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 465}, {"pair_id": 691, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Novelty is limited since there are already several pre-training/dataset collection papers in this area.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 466}, {"pair_id": 692, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is unclear why Table 2 only has information about CodeGen-Mono and not the other models. More data here would be more convincing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 467}, {"pair_id": 693, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The result will stand out to compare against Codex, the state-of-the-art program synthesis model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 468}, {"pair_id": 694, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 464}, {"pair_id": 695, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The MTPB dataset contribution needs more explanation regarding its practical use cases and its limited capability to handle more complex programs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 469}, {"pair_id": 696, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 464}, {"pair_id": 697, "paper_idx": 56, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(-) MTPB (from a cursory qualitative evaluation of examples) seems to rely on very fine-grained prompts. In many examples in App F, the prompts are substantially longer than the required code.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 470}, {"pair_id": 698, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My main concern with this approach is that the paper does not discuss how to apply this approach to more general problems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 471}, {"pair_id": 699, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 472}, {"pair_id": 700, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As a result, outside of tasks such as arithmetic operations where the dataset can be created automatically (and one could argue that you wouldn't use a task specific LM as the paper doesn't deal with multiple tasks or out-of-distribution problems), the applicability of this approach is minimal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 473}, {"pair_id": 701, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 472}, {"pair_id": 702, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "On the negative side, the solution proposed is formulated as a fully supervised problem, meaning for each task the exact steps needed to solve the problem needs to be annotated.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 474}, {"pair_id": 703, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This is not a scalable approach as for realistic real-world applications obtaining detailed annotations for reasoning problems is an expert task which is expensive to scale to large datasets.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 475}, {"pair_id": 704, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Unfortunately, I don't see much impact from this work in practical terms.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 476}, {"pair_id": 705, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "So, in its current form this does not enable any new abilities as we could use the base algorithm instead of using expensive LMs.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 477}, {"pair_id": 706, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In terms of the insight from a learning point-of-view, I also do not see anything surprising in this work.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 478}, {"pair_id": 707, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The novelty is minimal as the solution proposed is rather trivial as it doesn't try to solve the hard problem of reasoning without direct supervision, or other settings such as few-shot/zero-shot/multi-task and formulates the problem as a fully supervised task.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 479}, {"pair_id": 708, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "So the high accuracy of the resulting method is not that surprising.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 480}, {"pair_id": 709, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My main concern with this approach is that the paper does not discuss how to apply this approach to more general problems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 471}, {"pair_id": 710, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Therefore, it would be great if the authors could explain the limitation of the problems that RoT could solve and how to design proper subproblems in order to use RoT to train the models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 481}, {"pair_id": 711, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the technique only seems to work for problems for which we already have algorithms, and it is unclear to me if this could be extended to more general problems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 482}, {"pair_id": 712, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the technique only seems to work for problems for which we already have algorithms, and it is unclear to me if this could be extended to more general problems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 482}, {"pair_id": 713, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The problems considered here are very simple, and an algorithm to solve them needs to be known in order to generate the training data.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 483}, {"pair_id": 714, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The problems considered here are very simple, and an algorithm to solve them needs to be known in order to generate the training data.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 483}, {"pair_id": 715, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 484}, {"pair_id": 716, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 485}, {"pair_id": 717, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 485}, {"pair_id": 718, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 485}, {"pair_id": 719, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 486}, {"pair_id": 720, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "On the negative side, the solution proposed is formulated as a fully supervised problem, meaning for each task the exact steps needed to solve the problem needs to be annotated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 474}, {"pair_id": 721, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 484}, {"pair_id": 722, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 486}, {"pair_id": 723, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as it requires people to feed it with hand-designed subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 487}, {"pair_id": 724, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 484}, {"pair_id": 725, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As a result, outside of tasks such as arithmetic operations where the dataset can be created automatically (and one could argue that you wouldn't use a task specific LM as the paper doesn't deal with multiple tasks or out-of-distribution problems), the applicability of this approach is minimal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 473}, {"pair_id": 726, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Therefore, it would be great if the authors could explain the limitation of the problems that RoT could solve and how to design proper subproblems in order to use RoT to train the models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 481}, {"pair_id": 727, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 486}, {"pair_id": 728, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as it requires people to feed it with hand-designed subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 487}, {"pair_id": 729, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 484}, {"pair_id": 730, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One important point missing from this work is how to systematically construct subproblems given any problem in order to teach the model learn the recursion of thoughts.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 488}, {"pair_id": 731, "paper_idx": 57, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 472}, {"pair_id": 732, "paper_idx": 58, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The baseline WebChild in Table 2 seems to extract a much higher number of tuples at 4.6M, while the proposed model ROBERTANET only extract 6741 tuples.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 489}, {"pair_id": 733, "paper_idx": 58, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It can be WebChild having much higher coverage, but this difference was not discussed in the paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 490}, {"pair_id": 734, "paper_idx": 58, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This, in my opinion, is not reasonable because it would result in a sparse KG.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 491}, {"pair_id": 735, "paper_idx": 58, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The effectiveness is not significant compared with SOTA baselines.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 492}, {"pair_id": 736, "paper_idx": 58, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The effectiveness is not significant compared with SOTA baselines.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 492}, {"pair_id": 737, "paper_idx": 58, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The experiment is not comprehensive.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 493}, {"pair_id": 738, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The methodological novelty of the paper is fairly low.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 494}, {"pair_id": 739, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper completely reuses the T5 strategy for multi-task learning and just scales it up with more tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 495}, {"pair_id": 740, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The scientific value of this work predominantly comes from the scale of their multi-task setting, and their analyses.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 496}, {"pair_id": 741, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While this scaling could have been an interesting study, the ablations and empirical investigation don\u2019t ask very interesting questions for this to be useful. I didn't find any significant recommendations for scaling tasks that can be reusable in future work or even applicable to models other than T5. Moreover, prior work (Aghajanyan et al., 2021) also studied most of these questions already, in the context of two pre-trained models, though in a slightly different setting. Over this and much other related work on multi-task and multi-lingual learning, the current paper only seems to contribute new results using the T5 model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 497}, {"pair_id": 742, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Many of the results presented in motivating the work are over random subsets of pre-training tasks and random seeds for training. However, none of the results seem to give an idea of the variance of performance, nor any indication of how many different random subsets of tasks were used for the results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 498}, {"pair_id": 743, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The task scaling experiment (2.5) seems to be core to the contribution of the paper. Similar scaling experiments are also present in Aghajanyan et al., (2021). However, apart from the lack of an exposition of the variance in the quantities presented, I found this analysis to be very underwhelming.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 499}, {"pair_id": 744, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, the models use the same R parameter across numbers of tasks, which seems unfair to low-task settings given that it was tuned on 107 tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 500}, {"pair_id": 745, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The task scaling experiment (2.5) seems to be core to the contribution of the paper. Similar scaling experiments are also present in Aghajanyan et al., (2021). However, apart from the lack of an exposition of the variance in the quantities presented, I found this analysis to be very underwhelming.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 499}, {"pair_id": 746, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There are some details missing regarding Section 2.4, especially with respect to random seeds.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 501}, {"pair_id": 747, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, I am concerned that this will be used as a point of reference to say that randomly adding more tasks always helps, for which the paper doesn't provide sufficient evidence.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 502}, {"pair_id": 748, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Additionally, the models use the same learning rate regardless of batch-size, ostensibly a learning rate that was tuned for a batch-size of 512.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 503}, {"pair_id": 749, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Section 2.5 uses the same learning rate for different batch sizes, which might result in unfair comparison given there are previous works suggesting how batch size, learning rate could have compound effects on the downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 504}, {"pair_id": 750, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Section 2.5 compares different numbers of tasks trained on different batch-sizes.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 505}, {"pair_id": 751, "paper_idx": 59, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In section 2.4, will R beyond 10 still lead to consistent improvement of the SuperGLUE performance is unclear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 506}, {"pair_id": 752, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Overall the idea of this work is novel, but the description is very hard to follow.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 507}, {"pair_id": 753, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Clarity: some important terms are not defined at all or until later in the paper, including the key term of external knowledge (which commonly means something else actually, as in knowledge graphs).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 508}, {"pair_id": 754, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The methods (and experiments) are described informally, or obscurely, where the specific choices made are not motivated or compared to alternatives.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 509}, {"pair_id": 755, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There are some typos and style issues.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 510}, {"pair_id": 756, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Clarity: some important terms are not defined at all or until later in the paper, including the key term of external knowledge (which commonly means something else actually, as in knowledge graphs).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 508}, {"pair_id": 757, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The methods (and experiments) are described informally, or obscurely, where the specific choices made are not motivated or compared to alternatives.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 509}, {"pair_id": 758, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, one might wonder why only the result of UnifiedQA is shown?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 511}, {"pair_id": 759, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "why not also compare to the case using knowledge 100% of the time?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 512}, {"pair_id": 760, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The experiment result also need more explanations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 513}, {"pair_id": 761, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Comparing the results in Table 2 and 3, on many of the tasks, using Thrust to partially utilize the external knowledge drops the performance compared to when all queries are answered with external knowledge (e.g., 70.2 \u2192 56.8 (75% of queries) on e-SNLI UnifiedQA-base, 80 \u2192 73.4% (75% of queries) for TriviaQA UnifiedQA-3b).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 514}, {"pair_id": 762, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "why not also compare to the case using knowledge 100% of the time?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 512}, {"pair_id": 763, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This paper is very unclear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 515}, {"pair_id": 764, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiments miss some important baselines.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 516}, {"pair_id": 765, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Table 2, if my understanding is correct, with knowledge case is presented after the vertical bar (|).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 517}, {"pair_id": 766, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If that's true, please fix the description of the model which states the opposite.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 518}, {"pair_id": 767, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Such an important typo makes the paper difficult to understand.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 519}, {"pair_id": 768, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 520}, {"pair_id": 769, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 520}, {"pair_id": 770, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 520}, {"pair_id": 771, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, the authors did not indicate the number of clusters they used in the experiments, and how many knowledge instances they used to augment a query (which seems to be one).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 521}, {"pair_id": 772, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, the authors did not indicate the number of clusters they used in the experiments, and how many knowledge instances they used to augment a query (which seems to be one).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 521}, {"pair_id": 773, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors only compare Thrust with random baseline, but in order to show the effectiveness of Thrust as a scoring metric, they should compare the algorithm with baselines simpler than Thrust but better than random, e.g., using BM25 scores.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 522}, {"pair_id": 774, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 523}, {"pair_id": 775, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 523}, {"pair_id": 776, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors claim that they try to simulate the real-world case where they have limited bandwidth to retrieve external knowledge from the perspective of cost-efficiency, but the claim is not persuasive because the way they choose the portion of the queries to use external knowledge is through relative comparison between queries after scoring all other queries, and not through absolute scoring on each independent query, e.g., by applying a score threshold.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 524}, {"pair_id": 777, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There are some typos and style issues.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 510}, {"pair_id": 778, "paper_idx": 60, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There are some typos and style issues.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 510}, {"pair_id": 779, "paper_idx": 61, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Few tasks here require complex reasoning, or deeper language understanding.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 525}, {"pair_id": 780, "paper_idx": 61, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Until then. it's unclear to me how this approach generalizes beyond simple tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 526}, {"pair_id": 781, "paper_idx": 61, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, I'm not quite convinced if this approach can scale to more challenging NLP tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 527}, {"pair_id": 782, "paper_idx": 61, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Instead, most of the tasks can be solved with human-level accuracy using just the enumeration of a few input/output pairs as prompts (Fig. 4). These pairs would be available in any case since they are needed by the proposed method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 528}, {"pair_id": 783, "paper_idx": 61, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Translation task is only done for single words. Semantic tasks from GLUE are at 90+% accuracy with simple transformer models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 529}, {"pair_id": 784, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The idea of converting all the tasks to an unified format is not new, and has been explored in earlier works like DecaNLP, UFO-Entail, T5 and more recently in the CLUES few-shot benchmark.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 530}, {"pair_id": 785, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For instance, the authors evaluate their models on held-out NLI tasks, but train their models on paraphrase identification tasks that bear some similarity with the entailment tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 531}, {"pair_id": 786, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Can the authors report the multi-task model performance on NLI tasks without using the paraphrase identification tasks?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 532}, {"pair_id": 787, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Repeated experiments with different subsets of training/eval tasks can help shed some light as well.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 533}, {"pair_id": 788, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, we still rely on a vast amount of supervised data. In a way, the comparison with GPT-3 is not too fair.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 534}, {"pair_id": 789, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 535}, {"pair_id": 790, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 535}, {"pair_id": 791, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 536}, {"pair_id": 792, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 536}, {"pair_id": 793, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, there are no comparison with auto-regressive model with the same pretraining and zero-shot task evaluation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 537}, {"pair_id": 794, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "What would also be interesting is to see/what's missing in the paper, is to carefully study the difference in domains of the training tasks, versus zero-shot evaluation tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 538}, {"pair_id": 795, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 539}, {"pair_id": 796, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This is important since it is often difficult to explicitly partition tasks into disjoint sets.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 540}, {"pair_id": 797, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 539}, {"pair_id": 798, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 539}, {"pair_id": 799, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The claim of \"true\" zero-shot generalization needs to be further supported by two more experiments as highlighted in the main review, namely, (i) analyzing the overlap in text from the MTL setup with that of the unseen tasks, and (ii) ablating paraphrase identification tasks to find the impact on NLI tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 541}, {"pair_id": 800, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The only evaluation on the size of multi-task training dataset is presented on BigBench dataset in Figure 5, which is different than FLAN zero-shot evaluation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 542}, {"pair_id": 801, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The only evaluation on the size of multi-task training dataset is presented on BigBench dataset in Figure 5, which is different than FLAN zero-shot evaluation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 542}, {"pair_id": 802, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Therefore I think it would make the paper much stronger if you were to show how that baseline performs on the tasks in Figure 5.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 543}, {"pair_id": 803, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the evaluated model (T0) is using pretrained weights of T5 for training, which implicitly learned multi-tasking during unsupervised pretraining of T5 model. This makes the evaluation biased and an unfair comparison to GPT3 model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 544}, {"pair_id": 804, "paper_idx": 62, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, there are no comparison with auto-regressive model with the same pretraining and zero-shot task evaluation.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 537}, {"pair_id": 805, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 545}, {"pair_id": 806, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 546}, {"pair_id": 807, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 545}, {"pair_id": 808, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 546}, {"pair_id": 809, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "But I would argue this is mostly an artifact of the experimental setup, as prefixing generation with D-string (of length L) only eliminates exposure bias up to position L, and then both evaluated systems (according to the generation process defined Section 4.1) use a standard auto-regressive LM generation process that makes them both subject to the exposure bias problem.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 547}, {"pair_id": 810, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A more appropriate reframing would be to talk about the kinds of effects exposure bias couldn\u2019t possibly be having, e.g. that exposure bias may only result in strange word choice but not total degeneration.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 548}, {"pair_id": 811, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "So, the small performance difference between a preference M-prefixed and D-prefixed generation may very well be due to both setups being almost identical (same model, same auto-regressive inference algorithm, and the only difference is the prefix \u2013 which human raters are not even asked to judge directly).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 549}, {"pair_id": 812, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main issue I have is that the formalization of exposure bias is not well-founded and I do not believe a convincing argument is made the claims of relatively little (or even no) exposure bias are actually supported by the experiments.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 550}, {"pair_id": 813, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, using the corpus-BLEU of individual samples in a fraction creates the potential for completely inaccurate estimates.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 551}, {"pair_id": 814, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Even assuming corpus-BLEU works, there is no guarantee that it acts as an estimate of individual samples, in the exact same way that BLEU is known not to be accurate for individual sentences (Burch, 2006).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 552}, {"pair_id": 815, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Corpus-BLEU has been used in many papers, though I don\u2019t know of any showing actual correlation with a human metric, but we can at least say that it has precedent being used cumulatively.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 553}, {"pair_id": 816, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Even assuming corpus-BLEU works, there is no guarantee that it acts as an estimate of individual samples, in the exact same way that BLEU is known not to be accurate for individual sentences (Burch, 2006).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 552}, {"pair_id": 817, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors claim that auto-regressive models appear to have \u201cself-recovery ability\u201d that mitigate any exposure bias, but I would say that it is hard to claim anything about self-recovery (at least in terms of automatic evaluation) when the evaluation metric operates over such a small window (<=3 words).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 554}, {"pair_id": 818, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 555}, {"pair_id": 819, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 555}, {"pair_id": 820, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 546}, {"pair_id": 821, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 546}, {"pair_id": 822, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "A more appropriate reframing would be to talk about the kinds of effects exposure bias couldn\u2019t possibly be having, e.g. that exposure bias may only result in strange word choice but not total degeneration.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 548}, {"pair_id": 823, "paper_idx": 63, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 545}, {"pair_id": 824, "paper_idx": 64, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I am bit unconvinced about claiming that this work requires no external architecture in Table 1.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 556}, {"pair_id": 825, "paper_idx": 64, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "But this claim could be controversial since adding new tokens (for prompts and labels) on the pre-trained language models' token embedding seems more efficient than pre-training language models that already have unused or special tokens in their token embedding. I know many PLMs already have unused tokens in practice, but I would like to treat this claim theoretically.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 557}, {"pair_id": 826, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Similar studies were conducted on a single node with 8 GPUs as noted by the authors. Though that setup had considerably more computational resources the total volume of computation was still a fraction of the amount used by many large research institutions. In light of that work, the scenario presented in this paper may seem somewhat derivative and only marginally interesting.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 558}, {"pair_id": 827, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The investigation of modifications lack convincing experiments. For example, only one task performance (MNLI) is reported for when studying the impact of training hyper-parameters. Other tasks can have a different trend.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 559}, {"pair_id": 828, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The biggest missing piece is an ablation study on the improvement from various architecture changes.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 560}, {"pair_id": 829, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "But it's unknown which change(s) brought the bigger improvement.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 561}, {"pair_id": 830, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 562}, {"pair_id": 831, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 563}, {"pair_id": 832, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 562}, {"pair_id": 833, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This paper (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=F5uYcwABMu), for example, clearly demonstrates that models with near-identical pretraining loss can perform very differently on downstream tasks due to implicit biases.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 564}, {"pair_id": 834, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 562}, {"pair_id": 835, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 565}, {"pair_id": 836, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 563}, {"pair_id": 837, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 563}, {"pair_id": 838, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 563}, {"pair_id": 839, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 565}, {"pair_id": 840, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, I am not sure if ICLR is the best venue for this work. There are no new theoretical or algorithmic contributions nor any new insight into representation learning. This does not change my belief that this is an informative paper to many in the community, but it might find a more suitable audience if submitted to venues such as EMNLP. (Disclaimer: I do not regularly publish in this area. I am happy to defer to more experienced reviewers and the AC on this point.)", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 566}, {"pair_id": 841, "paper_idx": 66, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Clarity is clear, however, novelty is limit.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 567}, {"pair_id": 842, "paper_idx": 67, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While it's good to quantify the performance of models on time-stratified datasets, many conclusions are obvious, such as the synthetic experiments on question answering by shifting the end year of training data, as well as the experiments on self-containing reading comprehension.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 568}, {"pair_id": 843, "paper_idx": 67, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While the conclusions in this paper might appear obvious, the analyses of this paper are thorough and insightful and the released datasets appear to be useful.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 569}, {"pair_id": 844, "paper_idx": 67, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Though the results might not be surprising (discussed well in L439) this paper defines the task in a good way for other work to (possibly?)", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 570}, {"pair_id": 845, "paper_idx": 68, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My biggest concern is on the underlying assumption of this paper---the model should not be impacted by the incorrect labels.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 571}, {"pair_id": 846, "paper_idx": 68, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It's controversial here because when we say in-context \"learning\", we actually want the model to be truthful to the provided demonstration examples.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 572}, {"pair_id": 847, "paper_idx": 69, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Notation are sometimes confusing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 573}, {"pair_id": 848, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 574}, {"pair_id": 849, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 574}, {"pair_id": 850, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As the paper acknowledge, SAP requires running the whole encoder-decoder architecture in an autoregressive loop. This can be slow.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 575}, {"pair_id": 851, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Particularly critical is that caching isn't anymore possible (wherein caching is generally usable to speed up autoregressive decoding in causal decoders), because the encoder is bidirectional and in the autoregressive loop.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 576}, {"pair_id": 852, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed SAP method on the mT5 models, however, claims that the performance improvements come from the Bidirectional LMs, which is not reliable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 577}, {"pair_id": 853, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "My main concern is, however, that if XGLM is not using bootstrapping+self-amplification etc.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 578}, {"pair_id": 854, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "in Table 2 I am not sure how fair of a comparison it is. Perhaps with those techniques XGLM would exceed mT5 by a high margin.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 579}, {"pair_id": 855, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "in Table 2 I am not sure how fair of a comparison it is. Perhaps with those techniques XGLM would exceed mT5 by a high margin.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 579}, {"pair_id": 856, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As the paper acknowledge, SAP requires running the whole encoder-decoder architecture in an autoregressive loop. This can be slow.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 575}, {"pair_id": 857, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Particularly critical is that caching isn't anymore possible (wherein caching is generally usable to speed up autoregressive decoding in causal decoders), because the encoder is bidirectional and in the autoregressive loop.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 576}, {"pair_id": 858, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The method is applicable to mT5 style models, but the long-term solution is probably to adapt mT5 models with causal LM or prefix LM objectives.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 580}, {"pair_id": 859, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 574}, {"pair_id": 860, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed SAP method on the mT5 models, however, claims that the performance improvements come from the Bidirectional LMs, which is not reliable.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 577}, {"pair_id": 861, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This part of performance improvement may only come from SAP's effect on mT5, or SAP's effect on the encoder-decoder architecture model.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 581}, {"pair_id": 862, "paper_idx": 70, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Not only that, the underperforming attempt of running SAP on the T5 1.1 proves the unreliability of this conclusion.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 582}, {"pair_id": 863, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 583}, {"pair_id": 864, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The training efficiency remains unclear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 584}, {"pair_id": 865, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The memory/parameter efficiency of LoRA is exaggerated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 585}, {"pair_id": 866, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "the authors mention a 2/3 reduction in VRAM by not storing the gradients of the previous steps in Adam, which is true but it is important to mention that the gradient of the original weights is still needed (although not stored) at the current optimization step to compute the gradient for the low-ranked weights (if I understood correctly your method).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 586}, {"pair_id": 867, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If it is GPU memory, then the 2/3 saving of training memory is quite surprising to me\u2014LoRA still needs to build the forward and backward computation graphs for the whole model.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 587}, {"pair_id": 868, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Could the authors clarify?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 588}, {"pair_id": 869, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 589}, {"pair_id": 870, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 589}, {"pair_id": 871, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The training efficiency remains unclear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 584}, {"pair_id": 872, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 583}, {"pair_id": 873, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Since LoRA is able to meet this requirement by not merging the pretrained weights (W_0) and low-rank weights (BA), it will be interesting if the author can report the comparison results of inference latency among (no-merging) LoRA and other methods.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 590}, {"pair_id": 874, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 591}, {"pair_id": 875, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 591}, {"pair_id": 876, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The training efficiency of LoRA is a bit unclear.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 592}, {"pair_id": 877, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 589}, {"pair_id": 878, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 589}, {"pair_id": 879, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Missing comparison to the SOTA models.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 593}, {"pair_id": 880, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This is important for the reader to understand how far an efficient model is compared to the current SOTA.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 594}, {"pair_id": 881, "paper_idx": 71, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Missing comparison to the SOTA models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 593}, {"pair_id": 882, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "And why were 67 other colors inserted in the color prompts?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 595}, {"pair_id": 883, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Also for the color experiments, it is not clear whether 60 or 6+57 or 70 (as mentioned in B.1.1 in the appendix) prompts were used.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 596}, {"pair_id": 884, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One of the few questions about the color experiment results was to what extent the model was backing off to predict less specific color names, since it is something that is not well-captured in top-1 accuracy (though maybe some combination of this and distance evaluation starts to get at it).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 597}, {"pair_id": 885, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In short, by relying on the few-shot learning paradigm to extract the conceptual structure of concern, we're limited to extracting conceptual structure that can surface through few-shot learning (just like, for example, linear probes can only extract linearly separable features).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 598}, {"pair_id": 886, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This ends up being more a measure of the few-shot learning ability of the model than the conceptual grounding capabilities of the model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 599}, {"pair_id": 887, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example, upon seeing the test representation (e.g., [0,1,0,0] in the spatial domain, or RGB (140, 0, 255) in the color domain), the model can use a simple strategy: copying the concept of a bunch of most similar representations in the in-line prompt examples (e.g., [0, 1, 0, 0, 0], or RGB (145, 0, 255)).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 600}, {"pair_id": 888, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This strategy would not involve the concept of \u201cleft\u201d or \u201cpink\u201d, and is robust to the rotation transformation (while not robust to the random transformation, if each point in the world was transformed independently).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 601}, {"pair_id": 889, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example, in Section 2.1, the authors say that all models are pretrained on the 40GB OPENAI-WT dataset, but this is not true for GPT-3?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 602}, {"pair_id": 890, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There seems to be some confusion about the training data.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 603}, {"pair_id": 891, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(3) Though the paper investigates an interesting problem, the overall takeaway of this work is not very clear to me.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 604}, {"pair_id": 892, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It's not clear to me if the large models are exhibiting conceptual generalization/grounding abilities much larger than those afforded by such baselines (once we move out of the few-shot learning format).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 605}, {"pair_id": 893, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It's not clear to me if the large models are exhibiting conceptual generalization/grounding abilities much larger than those afforded by such baselines (once we move out of the few-shot learning format).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 605}, {"pair_id": 894, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One of the few questions about the color experiment results was to what extent the model was backing off to predict less specific color names, since it is something that is not well-captured in top-1 accuracy (though maybe some combination of this and distance evaluation starts to get at it).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 597}, {"pair_id": 895, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Including all n-1 concepts besides the target one, or many such concepts (eg, 60 colors) provides a very strong constraint in the input (in my view contradicting the \"only a small number of examples\" claim in the paper's abstract).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 606}, {"pair_id": 896, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The inclusion of a bunch of distinct concepts in the input can potentially also provide the model with a pragmatic cue that it will be presented with a new/unseen concept label if it receives a new/unseen kind of input, which adds another confound.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 607}, {"pair_id": 897, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It seemed arbitrary.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 608}, {"pair_id": 898, "paper_idx": 73, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The inclusion of a bunch of distinct concepts in the input can potentially also provide the model with a pragmatic cue that it will be presented with a new/unseen concept label if it receives a new/unseen kind of input, which adds another confound.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 607}, {"pair_id": 899, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Their approach is an extension of Electra rather than something fundamentally new.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 609}, {"pair_id": 900, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The sections do appear in my pdf\u2019s reader table of content, but not the text.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 610}, {"pair_id": 901, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The exposition could be improved.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 611}, {"pair_id": 902, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The exposition could be improved.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 611}, {"pair_id": 903, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "1. While the paper claim the importance of language modeling capability of pre-trained models, the authors did not conduct experments on generation tasks that are more likely to require a well-performing language model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 612}, {"pair_id": 904, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "What I find missing is that the paper hasn\u2019t addressed the claim about missing language modelling benefit (line 107-113).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 613}, {"pair_id": 905, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Experiments on word similarity and SquAD in section 5.3 cannot really reflect the capability of language modeling.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 614}, {"pair_id": 906, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "As the prompt-based approach [1,2,3] is gaining popularity recently, I wonder if COCO-LM can be used in this setup where the model is required to generate (i.e., assessing language modeling capability).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 615}, {"pair_id": 907, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This is something MLM such as RoBERTA, BERT are doing quite well.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 616}, {"pair_id": 908, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 617}, {"pair_id": 909, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 617}, {"pair_id": 910, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The performance of Megatron and COCO-LM is close to other approaches, for examples, RoBERTa, ELECTRA, and DeBERTa, which are with similar sizes as COCO-LM.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 618}, {"pair_id": 911, "paper_idx": 74, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If the author claim that COCO-LM is parameter-efficient, the conclusion is also applicable to the above related works.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 619}, {"pair_id": 912, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While there are clear strengths, one weakness is that one may need to define structural properties in different types of datasets that one might use.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 620}, {"pair_id": 913, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "1.Structural locality inherent in datasets need to be captured by a set of custom locality feature functions which requires prior knowledge of the domain of the datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 621}, {"pair_id": 914, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "1.Structural locality inherent in datasets need to be captured by a set of custom locality feature functions which requires prior knowledge of the domain of the datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 621}, {"pair_id": 915, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 622}, {"pair_id": 916, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 622}, {"pair_id": 917, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "2. Marginal improvements in results", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 623}, {"pair_id": 918, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the improvements due to the proposed method are marginal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 624}, {"pair_id": 919, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 625}, {"pair_id": 920, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 625}, {"pair_id": 921, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It is unclear how does the method generalise across different tasks and datasets, i.e., beyond two datasets.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 626}, {"pair_id": 922, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 622}, {"pair_id": 923, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Using locality info in authors' experiments leads to minor improvement.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 627}, {"pair_id": 924, "paper_idx": 75, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 622}, {"pair_id": 925, "paper_idx": 76, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One major question I have is novelty.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 628}, {"pair_id": 926, "paper_idx": 76, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The idea to fine-tune large LMs on private datasets is not new [1]. So is the idea to hold most of the pretrained weights constant and only update a small subset of the parameters (e.g., [3]). Thus, this paper is a combination of existing state-of-the-art techniques on several fronts including LoRA [3] for dimension reduction and FFT based method (4) for privacy accounting. While this is solid engineering work and demonstrates the feasibility of integrating DP into NLP, I am not sure if it has enough novelty to be published as a separate research paper. References: [1] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training [2] Parameter-efficient transfer learning for nlp. [3] LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models [4] Numerical composition of differential privacy", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 629}, {"pair_id": 927, "paper_idx": 76, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One major question I have is novelty.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 628}, {"pair_id": 928, "paper_idx": 76, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the contribution in terms of the technical novelty is not much. [1] The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, 2014. [2] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training.2020.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 630}, {"pair_id": 929, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There is no discussion or analysis on the performance of this new model on \u201ctail entities\u201d (entities that have few examples in the training set).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 631}, {"pair_id": 930, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "(1) if one wishes to use this type of model for much larger entity vocabularies, it is likely that a larger fraction of entities will have low number of examples, and", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 632}, {"pair_id": 931, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 633}, {"pair_id": 932, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "How can you improve the model to link rare entities not in the Wikipedia pages?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 634}, {"pair_id": 933, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There is no discussion or analysis on the performance of this new model on \u201ctail entities\u201d (entities that have few examples in the training set).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 631}, {"pair_id": 934, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "would it do better on popular entities or more long tail ones?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 635}, {"pair_id": 935, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiments on cold start, as well as table 5 which shows performances on entities divided by name match is pretty cool!", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 636}, {"pair_id": 936, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 633}, {"pair_id": 937, "paper_idx": 77, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 633}, {"pair_id": 938, "paper_idx": 78, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 637}, {"pair_id": 939, "paper_idx": 78, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 637}, {"pair_id": 940, "paper_idx": 78, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The compared baseline is weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 638}, {"pair_id": 941, "paper_idx": 78, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiment results are relatively weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 639}, {"pair_id": 942, "paper_idx": 78, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the experiment part is relatively weak and cannot prove the empirical contribution of this paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 640}, {"pair_id": 943, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 641}, {"pair_id": 944, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 642}, {"pair_id": 945, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 641}, {"pair_id": 946, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 642}, {"pair_id": 947, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 641}, {"pair_id": 948, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 642}, {"pair_id": 949, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, the first section repeatedly referred to the COBOL applications, but we maybe not be able to reflect the experiments into this application due to large discrepancy between languages.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 643}, {"pair_id": 950, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main problem with this approach is that the performance suffers significantly with CodeNet dataset. The reason behind similarity-based data augmentation will work is because for implementing similar functionalities often similar variable names are used. However, such an assumption may not be true for CodNet where developers did independent implementations. Thus, the dropping of performance for CodeNet is not surprising. However, this raises questions about the usability of this technique.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 644}, {"pair_id": 951, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 645}, {"pair_id": 952, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main problem with this approach is that the performance suffers significantly with CodeNet dataset. The reason behind similarity-based data augmentation will work is because for implementing similar functionalities often similar variable names are used. However, such an assumption may not be true for CodNet where developers did independent implementations. Thus, the dropping of performance for CodeNet is not surprising. However, this raises questions about the usability of this technique.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 644}, {"pair_id": 953, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, the results show the approach suffered for independently developed code (CodeNet), which is the most realistic case.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 646}, {"pair_id": 954, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 647}, {"pair_id": 955, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 647}, {"pair_id": 956, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 647}, {"pair_id": 957, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Although I recognized that the main contribution of this paper is not proposing a sophisticated method to align samples, I thought the paper need more attention to design efficient strategy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 648}, {"pair_id": 958, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Although I recognized that the main contribution of this paper is not proposing a sophisticated method to align samples, I thought the paper need more attention to design efficient strategy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 648}, {"pair_id": 959, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 649}, {"pair_id": 960, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Also, the algorithm seems a simple greedy method and may be heavily affected by the sampling order.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 650}, {"pair_id": 961, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In its current form, it seems like this is preliminary work and with some more sophistication (in method + data sets) it is going to be a good future task.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 651}, {"pair_id": 962, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 649}, {"pair_id": 963, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The alignment algorithm need to be improved.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 652}, {"pair_id": 964, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In its current form, it seems like this is preliminary work and with some more sophistication (in method + data sets) it is going to be a good future task.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 651}, {"pair_id": 965, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The alignment algorithm need to be improved.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 652}, {"pair_id": 966, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In particular, good directions can include in-the-wild performance when tested on something outside of coding-problem data sets (especially since those are so well-organized by language and problem).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 653}, {"pair_id": 967, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The motivation of the paper includes \"modernizing legacy applications,\" but the experiments does not reflect this perspective.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 654}, {"pair_id": 968, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In particular, good directions can include in-the-wild performance when tested on something outside of coding-problem data sets (especially since those are so well-organized by language and problem).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 653}, {"pair_id": 969, "paper_idx": 79, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 645}, {"pair_id": 970, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The technical contribution is incremental, without significant novelty.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 655}, {"pair_id": 971, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 656}, {"pair_id": 972, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The 3 proposed methods can all be summarized as hyperparameter/model tuning on the retrieval-based LMs, i.e., tuning the search engine, input prompting, and ensemble strategy.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 657}, {"pair_id": 973, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 658}, {"pair_id": 974, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 659}, {"pair_id": 975, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "What is the advantage comparing to other few-shot learning of retrieval-augmented models, such as: Gautier Izacard, Patrick Lewis, Maria Lomeli, Lucas Hosseini, Fabio Petroni, Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Armand Joulin, Sebastian Riedel, Edouard Grave, Few-shot Learning with Retrieval Augmented Language Modelsm 2022:", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 660}, {"pair_id": 976, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 659}, {"pair_id": 977, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 656}, {"pair_id": 978, "paper_idx": 80, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is not clear whether the improvement come from the high quality of google\u2019s retrieved contents or from the retrieval-augmented method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 661}, {"pair_id": 979, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The formulation of the CKL problem is overly simplified. It basically only considers a single corpus (D_1) for updating the knowledge of previously learned LMs. A general setup should consider a streaming version of D_1 and make it a sequence of sub-corpus (D_1, D_2, ..., D_T) that arrive at different time steps. Also, the associated tasks in UpdatedLAMA and NewLAMA should reflect such a time series --- that is, the streaming version of the current probing tasks. The current formulation described in Section 3.1 only has a single time step. It's more like an offline learning problem with the forgetting constraints but not an (online) continual learning problem. The proposed setup is thus a bit far from the motivation of studying CKL --- maintaining an ever-changing LM.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 662}, {"pair_id": 980, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, only a two-phase setting is considered.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 663}, {"pair_id": 981, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "More experiments can be explored, such as five-phase or controlling the differences in the distribution of data at different phases.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 664}, {"pair_id": 982, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The analysis of the CKL methods is not deep enough.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 665}, {"pair_id": 983, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experimental findings in this paper are somewhat trivial.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 666}, {"pair_id": 984, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This work is quite insightful for us to understand more about how LM continuous learning works, although I think more experiments could be beneficial as I mentioned in the weakness section.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 667}, {"pair_id": 985, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Although the authors mentioned that \u201cWe leave more exploration of applying CKL methods on decoder-only models such as GPT-2 architecture as future work\u201d, they should still mention this critical discrepancy in the main body of the paper so that the readers are aware of the context of the findings.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 668}, {"pair_id": 986, "paper_idx": 81, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper only performs experiments with an encoder-decoder model (T5). The experimental results will be more convincing if more pre-trained language models (such as GPT) are included. For example, we can explore the ability of different PLMs to avoid catastrophic forgetting and to acquire new knowledge while preserving invariant knowledge.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 669}, {"pair_id": 987, "paper_idx": 82, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "This is supported by the fact that the paper's analysis of the proposed importance score mechanism doesn't show comprehensible patterns (or the paper doesn't talk about it).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 670}, {"pair_id": 988, "paper_idx": 82, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiments are not well-designed, and the results only show marginal improvements over RoBERTa.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 671}, {"pair_id": 989, "paper_idx": 82, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main weakness is that the proposed extension to the baseline is relatively complex and rather heavy-weight in terms of new parameters (introducing ~25% more parameters compared to the baseline according to Table 2), yet only achieves a very marginal relative improvement of 0.2 percent over the baseline.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 672}, {"pair_id": 990, "paper_idx": 82, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The idea of HIRE model is very similar to utilizing ELMo in down-stream tasks, where the hidden representations of each layer are weighted.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 673}, {"pair_id": 991, "paper_idx": 82, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, unfortunately, both components are not novel to me.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 674}, {"pair_id": 992, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Reproducibility is limited.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 675}, {"pair_id": 993, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I am not so positive about its reproducibility, since there are no statements about (a plan for open sourcing) code for reproducing the experiments.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 676}, {"pair_id": 994, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "One point I did not like very much is the fact the authors mention two external baselines at the end of their related work section (the ensemble methods proposed for e.g. machine translation), but do not compare their methods against those baselines.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 677}, {"pair_id": 995, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "You also did not include any such ensembles as your baselines. Why?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 678}, {"pair_id": 996, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Novelty is a bit limited, lack of comparison with other common OOD approaches, and the generalization of the proposed approach is questionable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 679}, {"pair_id": 997, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Novelty is a bit limited, lack of comparison with other common OOD approaches, and the generalization of the proposed approach is questionable.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 679}, {"pair_id": 998, "paper_idx": 83, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Page 9, Section 5, last paragraph: \"In this work, we focus on developing scores that can be readily derived from the generative model itself, without much increase in computation.\" -> You did not compute how much time it takes to run your proposed models compared to the baselines.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 680}, {"pair_id": 999, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Qualitative analysis - Given that it is a generative paper, I'd like to see examples of how the generated texts look like for different labels. How different are those from the ones present in the training sample?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 681}, {"pair_id": 1000, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Some automatic metrics/human evaluation should be introduced to measure the quality of the generated data.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 682}, {"pair_id": 1001, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "On the same line, I'd like to see how well the meta-weight generation can learn the importance of various tokens. For example, authors provide an example that the token \"movie\" might not be relevant to generate texts for positive/negative movie reviews. Does such properties automatically emerge from the meta-learning procedure?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 683}, {"pair_id": 1002, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Susceptibility to hyper-parameter tuning - The optimization procedure is performing three interleaved gradient updates at every iteration.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 684}, {"pair_id": 1003, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Besides, it is well known that meta-learning methods are notoriously hard to tune and can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices. It is hard to tune the framework on a large PLM with a small batch size.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 685}, {"pair_id": 1004, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I'd like authors to comment on that along with the overall computational cost for this procedure.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 686}, {"pair_id": 1005, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "On the same line, I'd like to see how well the meta-weight generation can learn the importance of various tokens. For example, authors provide an example that the token \"movie\" might not be relevant to generate texts for positive/negative movie reviews. Does such properties automatically emerge from the meta-learning procedure?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 683}, {"pair_id": 1006, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Generalizability of the ensembled classifier - Can the ensembled classifier generalize to other methods which also produce synthetic samples to augment training set? If so, how much gain can we tentatively expect to obtain with those methods?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 687}, {"pair_id": 1007, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "-The paper is lacking more qualitative analysis and discussion.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 688}, {"pair_id": 1008, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It would be nice to provide more generated samples for given k-shots and for different tasks, and to discuss how they contribute to a better performance.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 689}, {"pair_id": 1009, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "-The paper uses a learnable \u201cw\u201d as a feedforward net with 100-dimension hidden layer, but it is not explained why this is the only considered option.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 690}, {"pair_id": 1010, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For instance, other options such as self-attention layers, do not seem to be considered.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 691}, {"pair_id": 1011, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since this part is important for learning the token weights in the meta-weighted objective function, the structure of this part can be further explored, for instance by providing more ablation analysis.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 692}, {"pair_id": 1012, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "- The compared baselines are not comprehensive.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 693}, {"pair_id": 1013, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "-The paper states that \u201cgeneral idea of meta-learning is to formulate a meta objective to enable automatic learning of hyperparameters\u201d, which is not entirely true, since that can be considered only as a subset of the meta-learning goals.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 694}, {"pair_id": 1014, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In general, meta-learning is aiming to learn good representations and accrue meta-knowledge by observing batches of few-shot tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 695}, {"pair_id": 1015, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a meta-learning framework is not clear and a bit sudden.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 696}, {"pair_id": 1016, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a bi-level problem is not clear. What is the interpretation behind it? It seems that the bi-level structure is not necessary in this case, since there is no strict hierarchy of the inner and outer objectives ($L_\\text{gen}$ and $L_{disc}$): the order of inner and outer objectives can be simply swapped to achieve the same goal(i.e. using unweighted generation loss in the outer loop and weighted discriminative loss in the inner loop). The bi-level form here is more like to introduce \u201ctechnical contribution\u201d.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 697}, {"pair_id": 1017, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper does not conduct quantitative and qualitative analysis to measure the quality of the generated text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 698}, {"pair_id": 1018, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper does not conduct quantitative and qualitative analysis to measure the quality of the generated text.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 698}, {"pair_id": 1019, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "-The paper is lacking more qualitative analysis and discussion.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 688}, {"pair_id": 1020, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Some automatic metrics/human evaluation should be introduced to measure the quality of the generated data.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 682}, {"pair_id": 1021, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It would be nice to provide more generated samples for given k-shots and for different tasks, and to discuss how they contribute to a better performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 689}, {"pair_id": 1022, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "- Could the authors add the ablation where D_train and D_gen is together used for fine-tuning?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 699}, {"pair_id": 1023, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "-There is a lack of experiments without the data augmentation done by the autoregressive language model. To have a better understanding of how the data generation part helps, the authors should provide a baseline where the model is fine-tuned without augmentation. This will offer a fair comparison to the models trained w/o augmentation in Table 1, and also can highlight the contribution of the classification-only part of the method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 700}, {"pair_id": 1024, "paper_idx": 84, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "-There is a lack of experiments without the data augmentation done by the autoregressive language model. To have a better understanding of how the data generation part helps, the authors should provide a baseline where the model is fine-tuned without augmentation. This will offer a fair comparison to the models trained w/o augmentation in Table 1, and also can highlight the contribution of the classification-only part of the method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 700}, {"pair_id": 1025, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 701}, {"pair_id": 1026, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 701}, {"pair_id": 1027, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 702}, {"pair_id": 1028, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 702}, {"pair_id": 1029, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Ideally the author should look deeper into how are the retrieval results helping the model to make predictions that are otherwise difficult.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 703}, {"pair_id": 1030, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "3, The paper claims to have experimental evidence that instance-adaptive selection is superior to task-adaptive selection of the context type.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 704}, {"pair_id": 1031, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "But this claim did not seem to be backed up by numbers.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 705}, {"pair_id": 1032, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper lacks ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component of the proposed method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 706}, {"pair_id": 1033, "paper_idx": 85, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Suggested Ablation Studies & Analyses", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 707}, {"pair_id": 1034, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Tab 1b is confusing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 708}, {"pair_id": 1035, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I don't understand what each number means.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 709}, {"pair_id": 1036, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I don't see a solid explanation evaluation in this paper.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 710}, {"pair_id": 1037, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 711}, {"pair_id": 1038, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Prototype quality: prototypes do not look very convincing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 712}, {"pair_id": 1039, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I checked examples of prototypes in the appendix but the 1:1 label distribution (positive vs. negative) seems to me they are human-picked.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 713}, {"pair_id": 1040, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 711}, {"pair_id": 1041, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 711}, {"pair_id": 1042, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 711}, {"pair_id": 1043, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 711}, {"pair_id": 1044, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 714}, {"pair_id": 1045, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Touching different approaches is important here since the way ProtoTrex handle explanation might not easily extend to all other cases.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 715}, {"pair_id": 1046, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 716}, {"pair_id": 1047, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 717}, {"pair_id": 1048, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "How to determine these hyperparameters?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 718}, {"pair_id": 1049, "paper_idx": 86, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 719}, {"pair_id": 1050, "paper_idx": 88, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 720}, {"pair_id": 1051, "paper_idx": 88, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 720}, {"pair_id": 1052, "paper_idx": 88, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "In addition, this paper proposes to have a fixed number of global MoE layers, which is probably not suitable when a pre-training dataset has very diverse tokens.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 721}, {"pair_id": 1053, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "In other words, I'd argue that with or without recitation, the objective remains the same (i.e., masked or next-word prediction), so this motivation to me feels a bit mis-directed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 722}, {"pair_id": 1054, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 723}, {"pair_id": 1055, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 724}, {"pair_id": 1056, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "(2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 724}, {"pair_id": 1057, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 725}, {"pair_id": 1058, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper would be much stronger with experiments on GPT3, Instruct-GPT3 (davinci-002), and larger language models (larger in-house LMs?).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 726}, {"pair_id": 1059, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "It's not really clear from the paper whether recitation helps with larger scale, which I think is important for the generalizability of the method [1].", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 727}, {"pair_id": 1060, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 728}, {"pair_id": 1061, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 728}, {"pair_id": 1062, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I think experiments on InstructGPT [4], T0 [3] or FLAN [2, 7] will be especially interesting, since it's been fine-tuned on instructions / examples / human preferences.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 729}, {"pair_id": 1063, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "A major advantage of retrieval augmented systems is their applicability on (1) tail distribution information; (2) generalization to information which was not present in the model's training set (like COVID for BERT).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 730}, {"pair_id": 1064, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I think these are important limitations of the proposed method, and (1) is not really discussed (2 is just mentioned in the conclusion).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 731}, {"pair_id": 1065, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 725}, {"pair_id": 1066, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For instance, ATLAS [5] gets 42% on NQ with 64 examples and a smaller model, while the best number in this paper is 32% (5-10x larger model).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 732}, {"pair_id": 1067, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 725}, {"pair_id": 1068, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Note that I do think it's incredible that closed book LMs are doing so well, but practically (from a performance stand-point) it may be better to just retrieve some text from the web rather than ask an LM to generate it with few-shot examples.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 733}, {"pair_id": 1069, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "What is the size of the index + docs (compressed) of BM25?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 734}, {"pair_id": 1070, "paper_idx": 89, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 723}, {"pair_id": 1071, "paper_idx": 90, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 735}, {"pair_id": 1072, "paper_idx": 90, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The number of examples is not significant, with only 250 examples, compared to GMS8K with 8.5K examples, so the results may not be generalized if more examples are translated and added to the benchmark.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 736}, {"pair_id": 1073, "paper_idx": 90, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The number of examples is not significant, with only 250 examples, compared to GMS8K with 8.5K examples, so the results may not be generalized if more examples are translated and added to the benchmark.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 736}, {"pair_id": 1074, "paper_idx": 90, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 735}, {"pair_id": 1075, "paper_idx": 90, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "And the empirical verification of CoT on more datasets are also not sufficient for another new paper.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 737}, {"pair_id": 1076, "paper_idx": 90, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 735}, {"pair_id": 1077, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 738}, {"pair_id": 1078, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 738}, {"pair_id": 1079, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Hard to replicate: several proposed datasets are downloaded from the web, it will be hard to download the same files.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 739}, {"pair_id": 1080, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Also, there will be some challenges to reproduce this work with respect to the datasets that should be addressed as well.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 740}, {"pair_id": 1081, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Hard to replicate: several proposed datasets are downloaded from the web, it will be hard to download the same files.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 739}, {"pair_id": 1082, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Also, there will be some challenges to reproduce this work with respect to the datasets that should be addressed as well.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 740}, {"pair_id": 1083, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "No details on the specific hardware and software used.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 741}, {"pair_id": 1084, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper writing ignores many details that are helpful for reproducing the method.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 742}, {"pair_id": 1085, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 738}, {"pair_id": 1086, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The model is only evaluated on the language modeling task.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 743}, {"pair_id": 1087, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 744}, {"pair_id": 1088, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "More tasks would be helpful to indicate whether the proposed method is general enough in terms of data distribution.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 745}, {"pair_id": 1089, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 744}, {"pair_id": 1090, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 746}, {"pair_id": 1091, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Throughout the paper the authors only compare their model to baselines that they designed and trained.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 747}, {"pair_id": 1092, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 748}, {"pair_id": 1093, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 749}, {"pair_id": 1094, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The numbers in the compressive transformer are very different from the numbers your models obtain, so I\u2019m assuming that you preprocessed that dataset differently and so have a different vocabulary, making those results incomparable?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 750}, {"pair_id": 1095, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 746}, {"pair_id": 1096, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors don\u2019t provide any speed or memory usage statistics.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 751}, {"pair_id": 1097, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Of course, it\u2019s totally fine if this model runs slower or uses more memory than the baseline- I expect such preliminary investigations to potentially be nonoptimal, with later work making it an actually viable model.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 752}, {"pair_id": 1098, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I didn't find clear comparisons of training speed and GPU memory usage between different models. It is valuable for the readers to have a sense of what kind of resource is needed to train or host such models.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 753}, {"pair_id": 1099, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "But I think it's important to discuss these things.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 754}, {"pair_id": 1100, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The authors do not compare their model to any previously published model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 755}, {"pair_id": 1101, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 738}, {"pair_id": 1102, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 748}, {"pair_id": 1103, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 749}, {"pair_id": 1104, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 756}, {"pair_id": 1105, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 756}, {"pair_id": 1106, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Results are very close for each memory size and there are no error measurements (e.g., std dev).", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 757}, {"pair_id": 1107, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This makes understanding the results on each dataset, for the different models, harder.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 758}, {"pair_id": 1108, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "For example, on the arxiv dataset the authors talk about a drop from 2.927 to 2.877 perplexity between two different models, but is a 0.05 perplexity difference really statistically significant here?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 759}, {"pair_id": 1109, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "No details on the specific hardware and software used.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 741}, {"pair_id": 1110, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "there are some benchmarks that require models to be able to handle long inputs, such as document-level QA datasets and document-level retrieval.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 760}, {"pair_id": 1111, "paper_idx": 91, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 744}, {"pair_id": 1112, "paper_idx": 93, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Two out of the three tasks are generation tasks in which BLEU or ROUGE-2 evaluation scores are difficult to interpret, i.e. it is very difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 761}, {"pair_id": 1113, "paper_idx": 93, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "This statement is only true \"on average\" and overlooks the huge differences between different tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 762}, {"pair_id": 1114, "paper_idx": 93, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Furthermore, it is not clear where the results are significant different among systems.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 763}, {"pair_id": 1115, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While the work has its merit in decreasing the biases in some metrics, It is bad using those dataset as leaderboard without looking into them.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 764}, {"pair_id": 1116, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper mentioned that bias measurements like WEAT have been questioned.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 765}, {"pair_id": 1117, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "While the work has its merit in decreasing the biases in some metrics, It is bad using those dataset as leaderboard without looking into them.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 764}, {"pair_id": 1118, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "[1] elaborates on the few problems with CrowS-Pairs and StereoSet.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 766}, {"pair_id": 1119, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The experimental results, especially in Table 4, are not very convincing.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 767}, {"pair_id": 1120, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I am concerned about the evaluation of bias.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 768}, {"pair_id": 1121, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I am concerned about the evaluation of bias.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 768}, {"pair_id": 1122, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "However, Stereo-set and Crows-Pairs have also been criticized [1].", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 769}, {"pair_id": 1123, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the work has its merit in decreasing the biases in some metrics, It is bad using those dataset as leaderboard without looking into them.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 764}, {"pair_id": 1124, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "As recent studies showed, intrinsic measures of bias do not necessarily correlate with bias measures on a concrete, down-stream task that can be used in an application.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 770}, {"pair_id": 1125, "paper_idx": 94, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Without such an evaluation for an application oriented task, it is hard to understand whether the few points gained by the new model will lead to significant bias improvement in a downstream task.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 771}, {"pair_id": 1126, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 772}, {"pair_id": 1127, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 772}, {"pair_id": 1128, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Related to the point above, I would have liked to see more ablations on the vote-k selection process. How important is it to use the model confidence \u201cstratification\u201d strategy in the algorithm? This is particularly useful to know since, given a very large set of unlabeled examples |U|, it can be costly to run all u in |U| model predictions to collect confidence scores.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 773}, {"pair_id": 1129, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "There is also no discussion on label distribution in the vote-k process.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 774}, {"pair_id": 1130, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Since the selective annotation is based entirely on similarities derived from sentence embeddings, there is nothing explicit ensuring that the label distribution over the selected subset is not skewed.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 775}, {"pair_id": 1131, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 772}, {"pair_id": 1132, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "One particularly confusing part of vote-k: it was not immediately clear to me how this differs or is better than simple k-means clustering to discover centroids?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 776}, {"pair_id": 1133, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I do not think the proposed method is novel, though it is effective.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 777}, {"pair_id": 1134, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The novelty of this work is mixed: the idea of using retrieval for in-context learning prompts is not new (this work cites Liu et al., 2022 and Rubin et al., 2022)..", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 778}, {"pair_id": 1135, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I do not think the proposed method is novel, though it is effective.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 777}, {"pair_id": 1136, "paper_idx": 95, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "But novelty here is (1) in combining these ideas to make a practical system, and (2) the experimental work demonstrating some insights comparing this with fine-tuning and other selective annotation schemes.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 779}, {"pair_id": 1137, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 780}, {"pair_id": 1138, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 780}, {"pair_id": 1139, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The gains are not super high.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 781}, {"pair_id": 1140, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Is there an easier task where this method leads to strong performance but ordinary pretraining does not?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 782}, {"pair_id": 1141, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I felt this was quite separate from the theoretical analysis. It does not follow from the theoretical results that adding similar sentences will be a good thing. As a result, having these in the same paper looked incoherent to me.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 783}, {"pair_id": 1142, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The only two tasks examined are sentence similarity tasks (which seem a bit more like a sanity check), and NaturalQuestions.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 784}, {"pair_id": 1143, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It wasn't intuitive for me that it'd be useful for NQ.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 785}, {"pair_id": 1144, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "It's particularly surprising to me that this works so well on NQ, and I wish the authors had dug a bit deeper into this, but I also recognize that page limits exist.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 786}, {"pair_id": 1145, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "3. The experimental results are weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 787}, {"pair_id": 1146, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 788}, {"pair_id": 1147, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 789}, {"pair_id": 1148, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 790}, {"pair_id": 1149, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 791}, {"pair_id": 1150, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "3.The experiments are limited.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 792}, {"pair_id": 1151, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 788}, {"pair_id": 1152, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 793}, {"pair_id": 1153, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "3. The experimental results are weak.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 787}, {"pair_id": 1154, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The gains are not super high.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 781}, {"pair_id": 1155, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 793}, {"pair_id": 1156, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 789}, {"pair_id": 1157, "paper_idx": 96, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 793}, {"pair_id": 1158, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "How could the findings benefit the community? The design principle of EncDec-based NMT is the sub-optimal performance/efficiency of LM-based NMT, and this paper just says: \"yes, the intuition is true\", which is not exciting at all.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 794}, {"pair_id": 1159, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Given that the encoder-decoder paradigm dominantly governs machine translation (also from the experiment part of this paper that LMs underperform EncDec most of the time. ), for what reason should we need to consider a shift to a unified language model for such a seq2seq task? If the zero-shot transfer is the case, why not directly fix the off-target issue for EncDec and preserve the good of translation performance, instead of changing the paradigm?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 795}, {"pair_id": 1160, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "How could the findings benefit the community? The design principle of EncDec-based NMT is the sub-optimal performance/efficiency of LM-based NMT, and this paper just says: \"yes, the intuition is true\", which is not exciting at all.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 794}, {"pair_id": 1161, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The paper conducted extensive experiments to show how scaling affects LMs for MT, however, few suggestions based on the findings are given for future development of MT.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 796}, {"pair_id": 1162, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors should answer the question: how could the paper findings benefit the community?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 797}, {"pair_id": 1163, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The authors should answer the question: how could the paper findings benefit the community?", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 797}, {"pair_id": 1164, "paper_idx": 97, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The paper conducted extensive experiments to show how scaling affects LMs for MT, however, few suggestions based on the findings are given for future development of MT.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 796}, {"pair_id": 1165, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The idea of using entity abstraction is straightforward and not very novel as doing POS tagging for input is widely used in many NLP models, especially in traditional NLP pipelines.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 798}, {"pair_id": 1166, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "While the performance on synthetic datasets is promising, on realistic datasets the proposed method fails to improve the performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 799}, {"pair_id": 1167, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Therefore the effectiveness of the method is not fully supported by the experiments.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 800}, {"pair_id": 1168, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 801}, {"pair_id": 1169, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "There is very minor boost in performance on QA datasets.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 802}, {"pair_id": 1170, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The goal of the proposed model should be improving real-world data, instead of synthetic data (CLUTTR in this paper).", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 803}, {"pair_id": 1171, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 804}, {"pair_id": 1172, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, in that case you can not prove that the proposed methods are still beneficial on a model that achieves way better baseline results.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 805}, {"pair_id": 1173, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiment part lacks analysis on the model which could provide more intuitions on how the model works.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 806}, {"pair_id": 1174, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 807}, {"pair_id": 1175, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 807}, {"pair_id": 1176, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The experiment part lacks analysis on the model which could provide more intuitions on how the model works.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 806}, {"pair_id": 1177, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 801}, {"pair_id": 1178, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 801}, {"pair_id": 1179, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 808}, {"pair_id": 1180, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 808}, {"pair_id": 1181, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 804}, {"pair_id": 1182, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 809}, {"pair_id": 1183, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 809}, {"pair_id": 1184, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The performance of baselines are far below the current top performers.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 810}, {"pair_id": 1185, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Also, the empirical findings lack deeper analysis and were merely conducted on a single and toy-sized pretrained language model.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 811}, {"pair_id": 1186, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "However, in that case you can not prove that the proposed methods are still beneficial on a model that achieves way better baseline results.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "No", "seg_id": 805}, {"pair_id": 1187, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 812}, {"pair_id": 1188, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 807}, {"pair_id": 1189, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 807}, {"pair_id": 1190, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 812}, {"pair_id": 1191, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 801}, {"pair_id": 1192, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal.", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 812}, {"pair_id": 1193, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 1, "judged_text": "For instance, we could dive deep into why the model variants perform differently between CLUTRR and HotpotQA & CoQA.", "other_human_label": "No", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 813}, {"pair_id": 1194, "paper_idx": 98, "judged_side": 2, "judged_text": "If there is a new reasoning dataset, which architecture in Table 1 should people use?", "other_human_label": "Yes", "same_human_label": "Yes", "seg_id": 814}]
/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/ai_judge_shard_0.js:30:const TASKS = [{"seg_id": 0, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "For the AMA prompts on Appendix H, it seems that some tasks (DROP, SST, BoolQ, COPA) only do either prompt() or answer() stage, not both. How can we decide \"automatically\" when to do only prompt() or answer() or both depending on the target task?"}, {"seg_id": 1, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "However, this paper contains some unclearness, especially on weak supervision and how the predictions are mapped to the output space. See Weakness part for details."}, {"seg_id": 2, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "I think still I am a little not sure, also about whether re-formatting is necessary for aggregation to work well."}, {"seg_id": 3, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs)."}, {"seg_id": 4, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Novelty: The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs)."}, {"seg_id": 5, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "However, the lack of technical novelty & contribution makes this work slightly below the acceptance bar of the conference."}, {"seg_id": 6, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?"}, {"seg_id": 7, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs."}, {"seg_id": 8, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "One key question not answered in this context is how much of HTML structure analysis and understanding is truly required for these questions."}, {"seg_id": 9, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "I examined the supplementary material provided by the authors and only found the data files."}, {"seg_id": 10, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Either code or more detailed experimental setup should be provided in the supplementary material"}, {"seg_id": 11, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "It is better to make the codes available for reproducing these results."}, {"seg_id": 12, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Models that are trained on task specific data to compare against is questionable."}, {"seg_id": 13, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "I expect to see more task-specific models such as StructuralLM [1], MarkupLM [2] that respect the tree structure of HTML (or some other methods mentioned in the paper) to be evaluated against."}, {"seg_id": 14, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Basically, I need to see the following question answered: \"For specific web tasks, do I want to use a HTML specific model trained on the small dataset or just to fine-tune LLMs\"?"}, {"seg_id": 15, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "On MiniWoB, it's not very surprising that using a larger model with pre-training, you can beat smaller models from scratch."}, {"seg_id": 16, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should have tried to an ablation type of approach in equation 1, to check if concatenation alone, element wise dot product alone or absolute difference alone or a combination of any two would work better with the scoring function."}, {"seg_id": 17, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "As mentioned in the paper, NLI models do not transfer well to out-of-domain NLI samples, not to mention non-NLI tasks."}, {"seg_id": 18, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "However, I do have some concerns about their claims that this helps in transfer learning to other NLI tasks."}, {"seg_id": 19, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The ThinkSum framework, however, is too general such that almost all existing methods fall into this framework, if you think scoring is the Think step, and argmax is the sum step."}, {"seg_id": 20, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 1 is confusing."}, {"seg_id": 21, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Many new terminologies (in different colors) are introduced: some are completely unnecessary (e.g. product aggregation) and some do not have any clear explanations."}, {"seg_id": 22, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "There is a lack of technical details. In particular, it is not exactly clear how the sum step is performed. If this follows a probabilistic framework, please write down the steps formally."}, {"seg_id": 23, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Without clear writing, it is hard to evaluate novelty."}, {"seg_id": 24, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The writing is not clear, making it hard to evaluate the contribution of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 25, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "In general, probabilistic inference may tend to become less reliable in this case so does that have a major influence on the SUM process."}, {"seg_id": 26, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Despite the extensive demonstrations performed in this study, the authors need to present the ThinkSum paradigm in a more organized and clear way: even the core idea of ThinkSum is not clearly explained the examples throughout the paper do not help much explaining ThinkSum paradigm."}, {"seg_id": 27, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Weaknesses One general issue may be how does the model scale up as the sets produced by the prompts to LLMs become large."}, {"seg_id": 28, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "This is doable for 5 choices, but not so much for 100 choices or more (in SQuAD-type QA, the output could be any combinations of tokens) which is extremely expensive."}, {"seg_id": 29, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty may not be as high since it mainly is an integration of methods but the impact of the approach seems significant."}, {"seg_id": 30, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed method -- using contextual embeddings instead of static token embeddings in decoder for autoregressive retrieval -- is novel, intuitive, and inspiring."}, {"seg_id": 31, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed model follows the framework of generative retrieval models but applies contextualized token embeddings."}, {"seg_id": 32, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The main weakness comes from the experiment setup."}, {"seg_id": 33, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The setup is questionable."}, {"seg_id": 34, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "GENRE uses BART-large whereas this paper uses T5, so the focus on comparing directly with GENRE seems a bit misguided."}, {"seg_id": 35, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The main KILT results only have the original GENRE (using a different pre-trained model, BART) and CGR (even though the authors listed DPR and SEAL, the numbers are not in the same comparable settings)."}, {"seg_id": 36, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 1 is a bit confusing to me (especially Cape(1), Cape(2))."}, {"seg_id": 37, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "I can guess what it does, but it's never fully clear."}, {"seg_id": 38, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty contribution is somehow limited."}, {"seg_id": 39, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The paper proposes a reasonable way to use external knowledge in generative retrievers, but the quality of innovation, experiments, and writing seems to fall below the standard of ICLR."}, {"seg_id": 40, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "Based on my inference, the approach is reasonable but not terribly surprising."}, {"seg_id": 41, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "Limited technological contribution."}, {"seg_id": 42, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed method makes sense but the contribution of adding a contextualized embedding space might be considered as light."}, {"seg_id": 43, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how the model guarantees a correct generation of key descriptors of a document, if the generative retrieval model and the contextual language model were not trained with it."}, {"seg_id": 44, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The tasks may be too simple / short / toy."}, {"seg_id": 45, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It is quite hard to draw many conclusions about the best performing systems because their training strategies are not public, and it is not clear what types of task ambiguity have been seen during training."}, {"seg_id": 46, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, the paper relies heavily on opaque models, and it is a little hard to draw meaningful conclusions about the best performing systems."}, {"seg_id": 47, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, the empirical observation and proposed fine-tuning method are pretty straightforward and not surprising."}, {"seg_id": 48, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The observation that the best language model achieve similar performance to human on such ambiguous task is not surprising, even though the setting is synthetic and simple."}, {"seg_id": 49, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "As the authors mention, AmbiBench is a minimal complexity testbed."}, {"seg_id": 50, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, for the same reason, I am less convinced about the generality of findings reported here."}, {"seg_id": 51, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "This paper only studies the ambiguity of a few toy tasks, which only require simple lexical understanding and the texts are synthetic and very short."}, {"seg_id": 52, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how these findings would extend to more complex task types."}, {"seg_id": 53, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It would be more interesting to see what the ambiguity is in real-world NLP tasks, and how they can be handled."}, {"seg_id": 54, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The reproducibility is unclear, as the authors do not mention whether the benchmark will be released."}, {"seg_id": 55, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The six synthetic tasks are quite simplistic and limited in scope."}, {"seg_id": 56, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "What are some other types of task ambiguities in NLP tasks, maybe more subtle than simply occluding a category name?"}, {"seg_id": 57, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, the current study focuses on a very narrow type of ambiguity, and it's not clear how much these insights will extend to other types of task ambiguity."}, {"seg_id": 58, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "What about in tasks other than sentence classification?"}, {"seg_id": 59, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "Although it is an important challenge in fine-tuning language models, this paper only studies a few toy tasks and the proposed method is not novel."}, {"seg_id": 60, "paper_idx": 8, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_8.txt", "segment_text": "This part can be more convincing if the authors can provide more experiments and analyses."}, {"seg_id": 61, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works."}, {"seg_id": 62, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty."}, {"seg_id": 63, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Although they do make sense and are reasonable choices for a multimodal framework, I see little novelty here."}, {"seg_id": 64, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Originality: Apart from some novelties of the pretraining objectives, the method reuses some of the existing architectures in a hierarchical way."}, {"seg_id": 65, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "UniDoc does not improve the performance of document classification tasks over previous methods."}, {"seg_id": 66, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Although the authors argue that it attributes to the difference of OCR engines, they should explain the magnitude of this difference."}, {"seg_id": 67, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "They say the difference between EasyOCR and Tesseract, but should report performance metric and qualitative analysis on the RVL-CDIP dataset to compare real Microsoft OCR and EasyOCR."}, {"seg_id": 68, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "I feel that the methods/model architectures are largely putting together existing pieces of work from vision and language fields."}, {"seg_id": 69, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "In Sec 3.1, the proposed feature extraction, quantization, and gated cross-attention modules are all rather straightforward variations of existing work (i.e., the essential mechanisms are not new)."}, {"seg_id": 70, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "The methods are not novel/insightful as they are largely borrowing from existing work."}, {"seg_id": 71, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "One thing that appears weird to me is the frozen sentence encoders initialized with the weights trained on NLI and STS datasets--if they have been fine-tuned on those tasks, they may capture too corpus-specific features to generalize to other domains (like the new pre-training documents)."}, {"seg_id": 72, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "There are concerns about the generalization ability of the model since it uses dataset-specific text encoders."}, {"seg_id": 73, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "Another relevant approach to model selection is to use some additional, held-out, tasks to find model hyper-parameters and reuse them on all other tasks without any additional tuning."}, {"seg_id": 74, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "The authors include comparisons with tuning on a held-out validation set for most, but not all experiments. I think it\u2019d be helpful to include those comparisons for all experiments and lean more on these comparisons over comparisons with random baselines. In particular, could you provide the results for Figure 3?"}, {"seg_id": 75, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "A related question is are the few-shot datasets balanced? This might not affect LAMA (also another reason why LAMA is so different than other few-shot tasks) but it will other classification tasks like SuperGLUE experiments. In meta-learning literature shots usually mean the number of examples per class. So leave-one-out will leave out one example of one class and make the few-shot support data imbalanced, which can, in turn, prefer hyper-parameters that favor the minority class. The more appropriate thing to do would be leave-m-out where m is the number of classes. It will actually be useful to have an ablation over k for leave-k-out and in particular, I feel this might have more of a bearing on the experiments in section 3.5. In case the few-shot data is not balanced, which is not standard, then that introduces a lot of additional variability from unbalanced datasets (or choice of the few-shot examples/classes) that also confound the results."}, {"seg_id": 76, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "I think this setting is termed as multi-distribution few-shot learning in section 2 and is also a reasonable strategy to select hyper-parameters."}, {"seg_id": 77, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "While this may not always be possible for prompt-based methods, it is suitable for other methods like those in section 4."}, {"seg_id": 78, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "However, the contribution of this work is limited by its scope. The results for true few-shot learning are interesting but are not widely applicable. Because most previous work, including those being criticized, focus on the multi-distribution or tuned few-shot learning settings and make assumptions like a validation set is available or optimal hyperparameters for other tasks could be re-used for the new few-shot tasks. Indeed, future works should be explicit about their model selection method to make fair comparison. But this has been a well-known issue for few-shot learning under additional assumptions. For example, researchers in computer vision have been working on datasets with standard splits for fair comparison [1]. The true few-shot learning setup might not be entirely impactful."}, {"seg_id": 79, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "C. The novelty of the proposed method: It is true that using USE or BERT-Score in evaluating the quality of the generated adversarial samples is costly and cannot obtain promising results."}, {"seg_id": 80, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "I am worried about the novelty."}, {"seg_id": 81, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty may not be enough."}, {"seg_id": 82, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Yet the proposed method is somewhat trivial since it only uses an ensemble of classifiers and uses the cosine similarity to calculate the final similarity score calculation."}, {"seg_id": 83, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "The motivation is not clear. The paper found that previous encoders fail to deal well with antonyms, but it seems that the proposed method did nothing special about antonyms either."}, {"seg_id": 84, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Several technical decisions are not justified."}, {"seg_id": 85, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Some designs need more justifications. E.g., what is the criterion when choosing the 7 datasets and why the threshold is just set as 2.5."}, {"seg_id": 86, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "SPE can be considered as a sentence similarity metric."}, {"seg_id": 87, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "However, this paper neither verifies its performance on standard sentence similarity benchmarks nor justifies that this metric is only suitable for adversarial attack situations."}, {"seg_id": 88, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "There are several issues with the experiments"}, {"seg_id": 89, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: the paper has a good motivation that catches a small yet vital problem in adversarial attacks in texts and proposes a very simple method as a solution, yet the evaluation is somewhat unfair which may hurt the quality evolution of the method."}, {"seg_id": 90, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "However, the presentation/clarity of the paper can be significantly improved, as suggested in the \u201cweakness\u201d section."}, {"seg_id": 91, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, figure 5 would benefit from square-axis."}, {"seg_id": 92, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "The main text should be self-contained and the reader should not have to rely on the appendix to understand the details."}, {"seg_id": 93, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "\u2018We select 5 tasks where teacher can most significantly improve\u2026\u2019 - I don\u2019t understand the motivation behind these choices."}, {"seg_id": 94, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear why the paper chooses to do so instead of reporting all datasets, especially because the paper did not report these two sets of 5 datasets separately and discuss how their results differ."}, {"seg_id": 95, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "The use of BERT-base scale models naturally leaves open the question of whether anything changes at larger scale."}, {"seg_id": 96, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "While the fine-tuning paradigm becomes effective at these scales, recent research suggests that zero-shot capabilities tend to emerge at larger model sizes."}, {"seg_id": 97, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Regardless of the RoBERTa-base experiments, it remains unclear how the results transfer to auto-regressive models, and how relevant they are to the web-scale pre-training of very large language models."}, {"seg_id": 98, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "While experiments would be unrealistic here, I would appreciate a fairer and more in-depth discussion in the paper."}, {"seg_id": 99, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I think the analysis of model size is quite helpful, since a natural question is whether or not these results are due to the ELECTRA small model being too small to benefit from pre-training on massive upstream datasets."}, {"seg_id": 100, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Second, there is no evidence presented in this paper that using the model confidence to select the correct model should lead to good ensemble performance."}, {"seg_id": 101, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Is there any way to apply majority-vote style ensembling instead, to get around the issues with uncalibrated model confidence levels?"}, {"seg_id": 102, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "This is a general desired property of models, but not one that is guaranteed and in particular not one that is explored in this paper (at least not reported), which makes it it not very surprising that these experiments failed."}, {"seg_id": 103, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "The discussion on how to construct artificial long-term dependencies is very interesting, but it also is a little bit orthogonal to the remainder of the paper; at least with the current framing."}, {"seg_id": 104, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus."}, {"seg_id": 105, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "In the introduction the authors make the strong claim that \u201c[...] the benefits from pretraining in this case cannot be attributed to knowledge transfer\u201d, while in Section 2 the authors are more nuanced and allow for the possibility that \u201csufficient knowledge is [...] present in the downstream dataset\u201d."}, {"seg_id": 106, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I am aware that size doesn't explain it all, but I am also far from convinced that the corpora being downstream corpora makes an important difference here."}, {"seg_id": 107, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "It seems possible that the models can acquire linguistic and factual knowledge from these datasets during pre-training, which cannot be learnt efficiently from only predicting the downstream task labels."}, {"seg_id": 108, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "That is, a baseline of pretraining on a general pretrain corpus of similar size to the downstream corpora is missing and is very important to shed light on the results here."}, {"seg_id": 109, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "(2) Related to (1), these datasets are all text classification datasets that are pretty similar, so it's somewhat unsurprising to me that pre-training on downstream corpora works well when fine-tuning on other datasets."}, {"seg_id": 110, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I do think the results are somewhat surprising and novel, although there's a bit of overclaiming (since I suspect that these results are largely due to the similarity of the downstream datasets)."}, {"seg_id": 111, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "However, I do think that this conclusion could be better-qualified (e.g., this is not a general conclusion, but one about text classification datasets)."}, {"seg_id": 112, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "(1) It's not clear that the results answer the question of \"Our results suggest that in many scenarios, performance gains attributable to pretraining are driven primarily by the pretraining objective itself and are not always attributable to the incorporation of massive datasets\""}, {"seg_id": 113, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Is the conclusion from these results basically that you need far less pre-training in order to get good finetuning performance?"}, {"seg_id": 114, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Furthermore, it's not clear to me that this paper directly answers the question of whether pre-training data scale or pre-training objective is the key to better transfer performance."}, {"seg_id": 115, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Although the usage of documentation for code generation is novel, it is somewhat simple from a technical point of view."}, {"seg_id": 116, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Beyond this, the technique itself appears to be essentially the same as prior work: they use standard retrieval techniques based on existing algorithms and pre-trained models, and their generation models are also models that have been used for code or text generation before."}, {"seg_id": 117, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "However, in terms of technical novelty, already existing models and techniques are used for retrieval and generation."}, {"seg_id": 118, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: Well-designed and experiments and well-written work."}, {"seg_id": 119, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of retrieving documentation for prompting seems interesting and novel to me."}, {"seg_id": 120, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "The study is mainly empirical."}, {"seg_id": 121, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should provide more details about the three-layer neural network as the projection P (\u00b7)."}, {"seg_id": 122, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Second, the baselines used for comparison are not complete, which makes me concern about the effectiveness of the model."}, {"seg_id": 123, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "-- the motivation and contribution for XEVAL are great-- the explanation of the dataset is lacking."}, {"seg_id": 124, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Presentation can be improved, especially the organization of the paper."}, {"seg_id": 125, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "\u2013 fragmented"}, {"seg_id": 126, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Model is largely based on prior work."}, {"seg_id": 127, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Baselines: It is unfair to compare the proposed method against baselines like BERT which are not designed to produce fixed length encodings."}, {"seg_id": 128, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Another point I just noticed, although the authors claimed that their model is better than SBERT, and did a comparison with SBERT-large, they did not compare with SBERT-base, which makes the conclusion unreliable."}, {"seg_id": 129, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "There are many settings considered in the paper and it is unclear if the proposed contributions are truly significant due to weak baselines are differences in data used for training different methods."}, {"seg_id": 130, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Ideally, the model should be compared against a skip-thought baseline or an unsupervised sentence representation learning method that uses the same resources for training."}, {"seg_id": 131, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Data used for pre-training: It is difficult to gauge how good the method is in comparison to other models due to differences in the data used for pre-training."}, {"seg_id": 132, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "I would appreciate a better and fair comparison of the Skip-Thought model by using same transformer encoder and same training corpus."}, {"seg_id": 133, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "There are still some concerns about the novelty of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 134, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "The hypothesis of the form \"This text talks about a female occupation\" refers to the text of the premise, rather than the events or entities in it."}, {"seg_id": 135, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "In other words, it talks about the form of the premise, rather than its meaning."}, {"seg_id": 136, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "Of course, there's nothing wrong with this, but it breaks a crucial assumptions about how the NLI data (in particular the SNLI data) was sourced: the events and entities in the hypothesis refer to the events and entities in the premise as much as possible."}, {"seg_id": 137, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems."}, {"seg_id": 138, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "The ground truth for the proposed examples seems to be neutral (occupations are neither male nor female), so I would like to know how often evaluated systems actually predict this."}, {"seg_id": 139, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted."}, {"seg_id": 140, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label."}, {"seg_id": 141, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "2.The bias measurement forces the models to predict either entailment or contradiction, where in fact the ground truth answer, in my opinion, for the proposed NLI examples, is neutral."}, {"seg_id": 142, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For all we know, the models are correctly predicting that with high probability, but the measurement is forcing a renormalization between entailment and contradiction examples (Section 3.2)."}, {"seg_id": 143, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For example, \"the doctor is operating\" becomes \"the teacher is operating\", which may not fit the realistic situation."}, {"seg_id": 144, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "In the constructed dataset, the hypothesis is generated by templates and looks like the context is not very related to the premise."}, {"seg_id": 145, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "However, in most of NLI datasets, the premise and hypothesis are usually related."}, {"seg_id": 146, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "So the domain of training set and their constructed evaluation set are different."}, {"seg_id": 147, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "In contrast, the word \"text\" in the hypotheses constructed in this work refers to the entire text of the premise, and not its entities and events."}, {"seg_id": 148, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It can be reasonable for models to perform not well and have the bias on the evaluation set, since the domain changes a lot."}, {"seg_id": 149, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "One way to fix the issue is to change the hypothesis templates to use the same (or similar) words as the premises, and replace the occupation word with a gendered word."}, {"seg_id": 150, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, replace \"he\" or \"his\" in the existing hypothesis with \"she\" or \"her\" so you can have female hypothesis and male hypothesis."}, {"seg_id": 151, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "When analyzing the results, the authors disregard the neutral case."}, {"seg_id": 152, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "Why do we have to force a choice between entail and contradict, when a stereotype-free model would actually predict neutral?"}, {"seg_id": 153, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "The constructed dataset contains only entailment pairs. How about analyzing the contradiction cases as well?"}, {"seg_id": 154, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "This seems like it would be a problem for the \"delta P\" and \"B\" measurement."}, {"seg_id": 155, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "Calling this entire setup \u201czero-shot\u201d is a bit of a stretch."}, {"seg_id": 156, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "The generator can be seen as zero-shot for generating task-specific examples, but the fine-tuned classifier not so much."}, {"seg_id": 157, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "For this reason, the truly zero-shot baseline seems weak and it is unsurprising that the classifier fine-tuned on synthetic data outperforms it."}, {"seg_id": 158, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "Not to mention the impact brought by prompts, decoding strategies, etc."}, {"seg_id": 159, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "(i) BERT is old now. We have already seen a lot of studies of BERT (in the so-called \u2018Bertology\u2019-literature), but isn\u2019t it time to move on?"}, {"seg_id": 160, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "There\u2019s the popular alternatives (RoBERTa, GPT-2, t5, etc.), but also a lot of faster, fairer, more interpretable alternatives."}, {"seg_id": 161, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "Authors use just BERT as pretrained model and a LSTM with GloVe embeddings as part of the experimentation."}, {"seg_id": 162, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "I think authors fail to properly state all the possible explanations to support the existence of transfer learning with scrambled or random input, If transfer learning were the explanation of why scrambled input keeps showing transfer learning capabilities an experiment training with the original data while tested on scrambled or vice versa is needed."}, {"seg_id": 163, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "The analysis section can be largely improved. Three out of four experiments show inconclusive results."}, {"seg_id": 164, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "Also, the observations made are marginally novel or significant, there are not many new results compared to prior observations."}, {"seg_id": 165, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "The title is about pre-trained models, but only BERT is discussed throughout the paper."}, {"seg_id": 166, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "And the reason why LSTM is more robust is that the model size is very small such that it can relearn all weights."}, {"seg_id": 167, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "My concern is that these scrambling techniques might push your dataset outside of real word adversarial attacks."}, {"seg_id": 168, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "(b) The shift does not seem to add value over other synthetic shifts that have already been studied, e.g., word-level shuffling, character-level shuffling, etc."}, {"seg_id": 169, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "I would also have liked to see a stronger motivation for focusing on frequency."}, {"seg_id": 170, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "S4.3 For contrastive baseline, I'm not sure how to interpret \"pairwise distant but internally compact\" in the context here."}, {"seg_id": 171, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "However, I have some concerns regarding to the presentation and the empirical results: (1) CCL+few shot data augmentation: the description is \"prompt with only an instruction and zero or more ID training examples\", then how many ID training examples are involved in CCL+few shot?"}, {"seg_id": 172, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "S5.1 it was a bit difficult to follow the various comparisons made in ref to Table 1."}, {"seg_id": 173, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., I had a hard time figuring out what is being compared at \"This accuracy decrease is smaller\""}, {"seg_id": 174, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "If it is sensitive to noise, then why it renders descent results on the other three datasets?"}, {"seg_id": 175, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "Improvements over the baselines are not fully convincing."}, {"seg_id": 176, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "For example, when discussing vanilla outperforming OE+NP (\"We attribute this behavior to the noisiness of generation: some novelty prompted examples are similar to ID examples\"), although I intuitively agree with this sentiment, it would be great to have some empirical support by quantifying example similarity."}, {"seg_id": 177, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The new data augmentation, while resulting in performance improvements over kFolden and Contrastive, also has more dependencies, i.e., access to GPT3."}, {"seg_id": 178, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "S5.3 Generator size is concerned only with the example generator, while the novel-label generator is always GPT3?"}, {"seg_id": 179, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "replace GPT-3 with GPT-2) impact the performance much."}, {"seg_id": 180, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "What happens if one generates novel labels with smaller models?"}, {"seg_id": 181, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The new loss function outperforms OE mostly because OE seems to fail on TREC-10 (else, it's competitive)."}, {"seg_id": 182, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "(3) Looking at Table 1, I am curious why OE+novelty prompting performs such badly on TREC-10."}, {"seg_id": 183, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "OE seems to do badly on TREC-10 for some reason, but it generally seems to match (or sometimes outperform) the new proposed loss on the other corpora."}, {"seg_id": 184, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The biggest negative of the paper, in my view, is that the performance gains (particularly of the new loss function) are marginal."}, {"seg_id": 185, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "If the proposed method only works well on very large pretrained generative models, the computational cost could be extremely high, since the authors generate 100k OOD samples."}, {"seg_id": 186, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The authors method can only work with text classification tasks, which is great, but --- some of the competing methods are more general, and the Wikitext + CCL method (which simulates the more general setting of not having GPT-3 but only an OOD set) only marginally outperforms prior solutions (kFolden, for example, doesn't even depend on the external set)."}, {"seg_id": 187, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Another concern I have is that the proposed model can only work in the scenarios where sentence-level representation is required."}, {"seg_id": 188, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "However, as shown in Table 2, the proposed method is not better than SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 189, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "So, what are the advantages of the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 190, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how this model helps in practice since it is achieving similar performance as other models like SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 191, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "My concern is the significancy of the results."}, {"seg_id": 192, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Since the improvement of the proposed method is marginal (~ 1% avg) and fine-tuning BERT on GLUE tasks might have high variance, authors should report the mean and std over multiple runs."}, {"seg_id": 193, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Employing span-level information in PrLMs was proposed in previous works such as SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 194, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Although the tasks between the two papers are different, the novelty of the paper is limited."}, {"seg_id": 195, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "As shown in Table 1, compared with the baselines, the proposed method does not bring remarkable promotion."}, {"seg_id": 196, "paper_idx": 24, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_24.txt", "segment_text": "The paper is hard to follow and would benefit from a clearer focus rather than the broad range of topics covered here."}, {"seg_id": 197, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of statistical tests to evaluate significance."}, {"seg_id": 198, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "In some cases improvements are quite small."}, {"seg_id": 199, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "I have some difficulty to assess the improvements that this approach brings."}, {"seg_id": 200, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "A detailed analysis of precision/recall would be useful, and whether the approach improves multi-class decision boundaries, or only the boundary with the negative class."}, {"seg_id": 201, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 1 illustrates that the decision boundaries learned by conventional classification models are \"open\", which makes it hard to distribute the universum class instances accurately."}, {"seg_id": 202, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "However, I doubt if this is really the case -- in the context of using deep neural nets (DNNs) as the feature encoder, the data representations  (i.e., $\\boldsymbol{h}$) and decision boundaries ($f(\\boldsymbol{h})$) are jointly learned, so even if the universum class instances have quite different semantics, it should not be hard for DNNs to map them into one compact cluster in the last layer's representation space."}, {"seg_id": 203, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "There is little analysis to show how the learned boundaries can better model class boundaries."}, {"seg_id": 204, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "The paper is unclear in many technical details."}, {"seg_id": 205, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "the arguments are hard to follow, and there is little analysis on the results to support the arguments."}, {"seg_id": 206, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The hierarchical relationship labels are not convincing enough. It is even difficult for humans to predict whether two sentences are from the same document. Enforcing the model to learn the \"same document\" label might cause the overfitting of the sentence representations."}, {"seg_id": 207, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "However, the motivation for this idea is very vague -- how can one accurately/unambiguously define the real difference between a sentence and a paragraph, and the difference between a paragraph and a document?"}, {"seg_id": 208, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "If the distinction between sentences, paragraphs and documents is even hard to tell for humans, then I don't see how the so-called \"paragraph-level relationships\" newly introduced in TSP can actually make a big difference compared to a simple combination of previous methods (NSP+SOP)."}, {"seg_id": 209, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The ablation study also shows that order information is more important than hierarchical structure information."}, {"seg_id": 210, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 211, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The empirical gain of TSP does not seem convincing to me: Although TSP provides significant gains on GLUE/SuperGLUE average score, the gains are mainly from small/unstable tasks, like CoLA, MRPC, RTE and STS-B."}, {"seg_id": 212, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: The contribution of the paper is not convincing due to the problematic empirical evaluation (see cons above)."}, {"seg_id": 213, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work)."}, {"seg_id": 214, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models."}, {"seg_id": 215, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Many terms were introduced without definitions, the sentence frequently omitted important contexts, and syntax was often broken."}, {"seg_id": 216, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The paper is somewhat hard to read due to syntactic/semantic issues, and I guess I possibly drops some context to assess the paper correctly."}, {"seg_id": 217, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The biggest flaw is that it is unclear whether anything presented in the work is truly novel or unknown to the machine translation community."}, {"seg_id": 218, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus."}, {"seg_id": 219, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The work can be jointly summarized as \u201clanguage modeling can yield an initial translation model whose performance can boosted tremendously by back-translation\u201d, but no single aspect of this is new, nor is the success of the combination of the various pieces at all surprising to anyone who has been following the unsupervised translation literature over the past two years."}, {"seg_id": 220, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Only one non-English language is considered."}, {"seg_id": 221, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study."}, {"seg_id": 222, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "This paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair."}, {"seg_id": 223, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough."}, {"seg_id": 224, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper."}, {"seg_id": 225, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The performance on these languages is crucial because the true value of unsupervised translation lies in low-resource language pairs, and their omission puts into question the validity of this method in realistic settings."}, {"seg_id": 226, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 227, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor."}, {"seg_id": 228, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "It would be more exciting, if we also observe the significant improvement of the proposed method on some distant language pairs, such as English-Chinese and English-Japanese."}, {"seg_id": 229, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "For example, do we expect GPT-3 to also excel at Hindi < - - > English translation?"}, {"seg_id": 230, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?"}, {"seg_id": 231, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "As the proposed method replaces the bilingual dictionary with few-shot amplification, it seems to me that the proposed method may achieve better performance on distant language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 232, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 233, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty of this paper is limited."}, {"seg_id": 234, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "Multiple choices prompting (MCP) has been used in other QA tasks, such as TruthfulQA and RACE."}, {"seg_id": 235, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven."}, {"seg_id": 236, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "No novel/brand new ideas."}, {"seg_id": 237, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "The submission is an analysis paper rather than finding something new."}, {"seg_id": 238, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "Limitations of the work has not been discussed by the authors."}, {"seg_id": 239, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "The most important part, I think, is the data expansion part described in Section 2.2."}, {"seg_id": 240, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited."}, {"seg_id": 241, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation."}, {"seg_id": 242, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "However, the author didn't compare with tensor train decomposition due to time constraints."}, {"seg_id": 243, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments."}, {"seg_id": 244, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive."}, {"seg_id": 245, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers."}, {"seg_id": 246, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art."}, {"seg_id": 247, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Some other method(s) are missing in the related works."}, {"seg_id": 248, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "For example: [1]"}, {"seg_id": 249, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I also recommend exploring the systems impact of running training and inference using tucker decomposed layers and why say 50x reduction in parameter count, does not lead to a 50x improvement in RPS (Table 4)."}, {"seg_id": 250, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Also, the presentation and the rationale behind the idea are not clear to me."}, {"seg_id": 251, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to."}, {"seg_id": 252, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Since the matrices across layers often have different scaling, and since tensor decomposition is approximating some reconstruction error (i.e. L2 in the case of SVD), it's not clear that performing decomposition with all the matrices makes sense."}, {"seg_id": 253, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "For example, why do you jointly compress all the layers, including all the FFN weights and attention weights?"}, {"seg_id": 254, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged."}, {"seg_id": 255, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, the reason for splitting each FFN weight matrix into 4 seems to make it possible to combine it with attention weights."}, {"seg_id": 256, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I realize that some of variants have already been studied in the literature (e.g. Mao et al., Noach and Goldberg), but since the setup is not identical, it is crucial that the proposed approach is compared against both II and III."}, {"seg_id": 257, "paper_idx": 32, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_32.txt", "segment_text": "This paper needs analyses in terms of how the proposed method addresses the catastrophic forgetting issue compared to baseline models, since the main claim of the RIFT is to tackle this issue."}, {"seg_id": 258, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable."}, {"seg_id": 259, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "Is the added robust prefix the same for the entire test set, or the same within a batch but different across batches, or unique for every test example?"}, {"seg_id": 260, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it."}, {"seg_id": 261, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\"."}, {"seg_id": 262, "paper_idx": 34, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_34.txt", "segment_text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3)."}, {"seg_id": 263, "paper_idx": 34, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_34.txt", "segment_text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling."}, {"seg_id": 264, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas."}, {"seg_id": 265, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel."}, {"seg_id": 266, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence."}, {"seg_id": 267, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 268, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup)."}, {"seg_id": 269, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed."}, {"seg_id": 270, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 271, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning."}, {"seg_id": 272, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting."}, {"seg_id": 273, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers."}, {"seg_id": 274, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions."}, {"seg_id": 275, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses."}, {"seg_id": 276, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked."}, {"seg_id": 277, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 278, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all."}, {"seg_id": 279, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures."}, {"seg_id": 280, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 281, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training."}, {"seg_id": 282, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar."}, {"seg_id": 283, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information."}, {"seg_id": 284, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models."}, {"seg_id": 285, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors."}, {"seg_id": 286, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection."}, {"seg_id": 287, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger."}, {"seg_id": 288, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse."}, {"seg_id": 289, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them."}, {"seg_id": 290, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data."}, {"seg_id": 291, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of some human validation"}, {"seg_id": 292, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow."}, {"seg_id": 293, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?"}, {"seg_id": 294, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are."}, {"seg_id": 295, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue."}, {"seg_id": 296, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration."}, {"seg_id": 297, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate"}, {"seg_id": 298, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other."}, {"seg_id": 299, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis."}, {"seg_id": 300, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong."}, {"seg_id": 301, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood."}, {"seg_id": 302, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\"."}, {"seg_id": 303, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings."}, {"seg_id": 304, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition."}, {"seg_id": 305, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there."}, {"seg_id": 306, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication."}, {"seg_id": 307, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions."}, {"seg_id": 308, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly."}, {"seg_id": 309, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d."}, {"seg_id": 310, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition."}, {"seg_id": 311, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases."}, {"seg_id": 312, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt."}, {"seg_id": 313, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces."}, {"seg_id": 314, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level."}, {"seg_id": 315, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results."}, {"seg_id": 316, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example."}, {"seg_id": 317, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g."}, {"seg_id": 318, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines."}, {"seg_id": 319, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks."}, {"seg_id": 320, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "It might be worth evaluating there."}, {"seg_id": 321, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well."}, {"seg_id": 322, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base."}, {"seg_id": 323, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling."}, {"seg_id": 324, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model."}, {"seg_id": 325, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that)."}, {"seg_id": 326, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing."}, {"seg_id": 327, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics."}, {"seg_id": 328, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?"}, {"seg_id": 329, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 330, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method."}, {"seg_id": 331, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel."}, {"seg_id": 332, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g."}, {"seg_id": 333, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 334, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting."}, {"seg_id": 335, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size."}, {"seg_id": 336, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The scalability of the model is not shown."}, {"seg_id": 337, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale."}, {"seg_id": 338, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M."}, {"seg_id": 339, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?"}, {"seg_id": 340, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper)."}, {"seg_id": 341, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples."}, {"seg_id": 342, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored."}, {"seg_id": 343, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information."}, {"seg_id": 344, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading."}, {"seg_id": 345, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated."}, {"seg_id": 346, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M."}, {"seg_id": 347, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M)."}, {"seg_id": 348, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation."}, {"seg_id": 349, "paper_idx": 43, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_43.txt", "segment_text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks."}, {"seg_id": 350, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune."}, {"seg_id": 351, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available."}, {"seg_id": 352, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many."}, {"seg_id": 353, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible."}, {"seg_id": 354, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike."}, {"seg_id": 355, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not."}, {"seg_id": 356, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation."}, {"seg_id": 357, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\"."}, {"seg_id": 358, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims."}, {"seg_id": 359, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up."}, {"seg_id": 360, "paper_idx": 45, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_45.txt", "segment_text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\"."}, {"seg_id": 361, "paper_idx": 45, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_45.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4)."}, {"seg_id": 362, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated."}, {"seg_id": 363, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1]."}, {"seg_id": 364, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models."}, {"seg_id": 365, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak."}, {"seg_id": 366, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "Limited novelty."}, {"seg_id": 367, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "(1) limited novelty."}, {"seg_id": 368, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines."}, {"seg_id": 369, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared."}, {"seg_id": 370, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that."}, {"seg_id": 371, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared."}, {"seg_id": 372, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms."}, {"seg_id": 373, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time."}, {"seg_id": 374, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large."}, {"seg_id": 375, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages."}, {"seg_id": 376, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages."}, {"seg_id": 377, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code."}, {"seg_id": 378, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?"}, {"seg_id": 379, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions."}, {"seg_id": 380, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach."}, {"seg_id": 381, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs."}, {"seg_id": 382, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated."}, {"seg_id": 383, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself."}, {"seg_id": 384, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters."}, {"seg_id": 385, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages."}, {"seg_id": 386, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?"}, {"seg_id": 387, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation."}, {"seg_id": 388, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?"}, {"seg_id": 389, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1]."}, {"seg_id": 390, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance."}, {"seg_id": 391, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool."}, {"seg_id": 392, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue"}, {"seg_id": 393, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?"}, {"seg_id": 394, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model."}, {"seg_id": 395, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear."}, {"seg_id": 396, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption."}, {"seg_id": 397, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?"}, {"seg_id": 398, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "Three MLPs?"}, {"seg_id": 399, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?"}, {"seg_id": 400, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?"}, {"seg_id": 401, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent."}, {"seg_id": 402, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "In Introduction you talk about the frozen weights of LLM but do not motivate why you decide to keep them frozen until the Discussion section."}, {"seg_id": 403, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar."}, {"seg_id": 404, "paper_idx": 49, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_49.txt", "segment_text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial."}, {"seg_id": 405, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix."}, {"seg_id": 406, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported."}]
/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/overlap_detect_shard_1.js:25:const TASKS = [{"paper_idx": 26, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The introduction argues that \"Without text structure, a text becomes a long continuous word sequence, which is hard to read and makes it difficult to identify key concepts and logical relationships.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "While this is probably, intuitively, true for humans, the paper uses the statement to argue against prior unsupervised sentence tasks and projects intuitive understanding of human attention and reading abilities onto machines (i.e., language models) without valid proof.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "This is a logical fallacy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I do not believe that this is a major weakness for the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paragraph just needs to be rewritten to avoid the incorrect argument.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The Related Work / Word-Level Pretraining section is a little too naroow. I understand that the paper's method only applies to transformer encoders, but I believe the paper would benefit from summarizing pretraining methods for encoder-decoders too.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Section 4.1.1 cites \"the sentence splitter of NLTK (Bird et al., 2009) for the use of TSP\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "NLTK's sentence splitter is Punkt (Kiss and Strunk, 2006), see also https://www.nltk.org/_modules/nltk/tokenize/punkt.html.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The paper should cite the Punkt paper together with the NLTK citation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Giving credit to the method's authors is not only fair, but it explicitly names the sentence splitter used, thus improving clarity for readers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "See the point in Weaknesses and the Punkt sentece splitter.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The novelty of the method is quite limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The proposed objective TSP is essentially a combined (and slightly extended) version of NSP and SOP -- the \"hierarchical relation\" classification is extending NSP by further classifying the \"non-neighbor\" case into \"same-paragraph\" and \"same-document\"; the \"order relation\" classification is equivalent to SOP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In this sense, the proposed task is not new and should be compared with multi-task training that uses both NSP and SOP (this seems to be an important baseline that is missing from comparisons).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper omitted discussions and comparisons with an important family of sentence-level pretraining tasks that use contrastive learning. For example, COCO-LM and CLEAR demonstrated that contrastive learning-based sequence-level tasks can effectively improve NLU task fine-tuning performance, and they can also be combined with word-level pretraining tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The empirical evaluation is quite problematic.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "My major concern comes from the fact that the paper does not follow the conventional pretraining/downstream task settings that have been extensively used by previous studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) The pretraining corpus used in this paper is OpenWebText whereas previous pretraining methods use either Wikipedia + BookCorpus (in BERT) or further expanded corpora that include a wide variety of datasets besides OpenWebText (in RoBERTa).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The common pretraining corpora choice could be either of the two above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Even if BookCorpus is not publicly available, Wikipedia should at least be included as a formal language pretraining source.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "If the pretraining experiments are conducted only on OpenWebText, it's very unclear whether the observed performance gain of TSP can be generalized to common/larger pretraining corpora.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "(2) Most pretrained language models (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA) have been evaluated on GLUE whereas this paper only reports SuperGLUE results. This makes it hard to compare with previous pretrained models. While it is good to have SuperGLUE results, I believe it's important to conduct experiments on GLUE as well to facilitate easy comparisons with prior work. (3) The evaluation results only have the best performing results on the test set but not median results across all tasks (Table 6 only has averaged scores). This is problematic because some tasks have very small test sets and the scores could vary a lot. The common practice is to report median results over several random seeds on dev sets of all tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "I thank the authors for providing responses to my raised concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Unfortunately, I'm not convinced by the authors' arguments regarding my major concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Regarding novelty: I never implied in my review that a method needs to be complicated to be novel; instead, simple methods can be novel as long as they provide new perspectives/insights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "My critique regarding the novelty of the paper is mainly due to the fact that the proposed objective TSP is very similar to previously proposed sentence-level objectives (NSP and SOP) in formulation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The major difference between TSP and NSP+SOP, according to the authors' response, is that TSP considers paragraph-level relationships.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, the motivation for this idea is very vague -- how can one accurately/unambiguously define the real difference between a sentence and a paragraph, and the difference between a paragraph and a document?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "For example, in novels, it's very common that one or two sentences can be a paragraph; other times, a paragraph can also be an entire document.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "If the distinction between sentences, paragraphs and documents is even hard to tell for humans, then I don't see how the so-called \"paragraph-level relationships\" newly introduced in TSP can actually make a big difference compared to a simple combination of previous methods (NSP+SOP).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Regarding the comparisons to contrastive learning approaches: I'm not fully convinced by the argument that the comparisons between contrastive learning objectives and the proposed TSP objective can be omitted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "I understand that the two sets of methods are based on different principles, but they are indeed for the same goal -- improving the sequence-level representations of PLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Therefore, unless TSP has other usages/benefits that contrastive learning fails to bring, I don't see why they cannot be fairly compared to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Such comparisons do not exist in prior work probably because it is quite well known that NSP/SOP-style methods do not really work well (also shown in this paper), and many PLMs simply ended up not using any sequence-level objectives (e.g., RoBERTa, ELECTRA), so the contrastive learning papers didn't compare with NSP/SOP (of course, I do hope to see those results in the contrastive learning papers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The empirical gain of TSP does not seem convincing to me: Although TSP provides significant gains on GLUE/SuperGLUE average score, the gains are mainly from small/unstable tasks, like CoLA, MRPC, RTE and STS-B.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Fine-tuning on those tasks are notoriously unstable and is subject to high variance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "On large tasks like MNLI and SST-2 (note that they are more reliable than the previous tasks; RoBERTa paper uses these two tasks intead of the average GLUE score for ablation studies), the TSP objective barely provides any gain and even worsens the result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Therefore, I suspect that the performance gain of TSP is simply due to randomness in fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Regarding the choice of pretraining corpus (OpenWebText): I'm indeed aware that OpenWebText is larger than Wikipedia, but Wikipedia is still a more standard choice for pretraining corpus if only one corpus is used (e.g., base model exploration in BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA all used Wikipedia + BookCorpus).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "This is probably because Wikipedia's text quality is arguably higher than OpenWebText which contains many short/casual/informal texts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Therefore, I'd suggest the authors follow the standard protocol for pretraining; otherwise, the findings and conclusions may not be reliable generalize well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "This is not my major concern about the paper though.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Quality: The contribution of the paper is not convincing due to the problematic empirical evaluation (see cons above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Novelty: The novelty is quite limited. The proposed objective TSP is essentially a combined version of previous sentence-level tasks (see cons above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "(1) There should be more discussions and evaluations regarding what are the real differences from NSP and SOP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "(2) The method needs to be compared with contrastive-learning based pretraining tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "(3) The evaluation needs to follow conventional pretraining/fine-tuning settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 26, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The hierarchical relationship labels are not convincing enough. It is even difficult for humans to predict whether two sentences are from the same document. Enforcing the model to learn the \"same document\" label might cause the overfitting of the sentence representations.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The ablation study also shows that order information is more important than hierarchical structure information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The novelty of the method is quite limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The proposed objective TSP is essentially a combined (and slightly extended) version of NSP and SOP -- the \"hierarchical relation\" classification is extending NSP by further classifying the \"non-neighbor\" case into \"same-paragraph\" and \"same-document\"; the \"order relation\" classification is equivalent to SOP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In this sense, the proposed task is not new and should be compared with multi-task training that uses both NSP and SOP (this seems to be an important baseline that is missing from comparisons).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper omitted discussions and comparisons with an important family of sentence-level pretraining tasks that use contrastive learning. For example, COCO-LM and CLEAR demonstrated that contrastive learning-based sequence-level tasks can effectively improve NLU task fine-tuning performance, and they can also be combined with word-level pretraining tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The empirical evaluation is quite problematic.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "My major concern comes from the fact that the paper does not follow the conventional pretraining/downstream task settings that have been extensively used by previous studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) The pretraining corpus used in this paper is OpenWebText whereas previous pretraining methods use either Wikipedia + BookCorpus (in BERT) or further expanded corpora that include a wide variety of datasets besides OpenWebText (in RoBERTa).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The common pretraining corpora choice could be either of the two above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Even if BookCorpus is not publicly available, Wikipedia should at least be included as a formal language pretraining source.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "If the pretraining experiments are conducted only on OpenWebText, it's very unclear whether the observed performance gain of TSP can be generalized to common/larger pretraining corpora.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "(2) Most pretrained language models (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA) have been evaluated on GLUE whereas this paper only reports SuperGLUE results. This makes it hard to compare with previous pretrained models. While it is good to have SuperGLUE results, I believe it's important to conduct experiments on GLUE as well to facilitate easy comparisons with prior work. (3) The evaluation results only have the best performing results on the test set but not median results across all tasks (Table 6 only has averaged scores). This is problematic because some tasks have very small test sets and the scores could vary a lot. The common practice is to report median results over several random seeds on dev sets of all tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "I thank the authors for providing responses to my raised concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Unfortunately, I'm not convinced by the authors' arguments regarding my major concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Regarding novelty: I never implied in my review that a method needs to be complicated to be novel; instead, simple methods can be novel as long as they provide new perspectives/insights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "My critique regarding the novelty of the paper is mainly due to the fact that the proposed objective TSP is very similar to previously proposed sentence-level objectives (NSP and SOP) in formulation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The major difference between TSP and NSP+SOP, according to the authors' response, is that TSP considers paragraph-level relationships.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, the motivation for this idea is very vague -- how can one accurately/unambiguously define the real difference between a sentence and a paragraph, and the difference between a paragraph and a document?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "For example, in novels, it's very common that one or two sentences can be a paragraph; other times, a paragraph can also be an entire document.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "If the distinction between sentences, paragraphs and documents is even hard to tell for humans, then I don't see how the so-called \"paragraph-level relationships\" newly introduced in TSP can actually make a big difference compared to a simple combination of previous methods (NSP+SOP).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Regarding the comparisons to contrastive learning approaches: I'm not fully convinced by the argument that the comparisons between contrastive learning objectives and the proposed TSP objective can be omitted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "I understand that the two sets of methods are based on different principles, but they are indeed for the same goal -- improving the sequence-level representations of PLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Therefore, unless TSP has other usages/benefits that contrastive learning fails to bring, I don't see why they cannot be fairly compared to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Such comparisons do not exist in prior work probably because it is quite well known that NSP/SOP-style methods do not really work well (also shown in this paper), and many PLMs simply ended up not using any sequence-level objectives (e.g., RoBERTa, ELECTRA), so the contrastive learning papers didn't compare with NSP/SOP (of course, I do hope to see those results in the contrastive learning papers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The empirical gain of TSP does not seem convincing to me: Although TSP provides significant gains on GLUE/SuperGLUE average score, the gains are mainly from small/unstable tasks, like CoLA, MRPC, RTE and STS-B.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Fine-tuning on those tasks are notoriously unstable and is subject to high variance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "On large tasks like MNLI and SST-2 (note that they are more reliable than the previous tasks; RoBERTa paper uses these two tasks intead of the average GLUE score for ablation studies), the TSP objective barely provides any gain and even worsens the result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Therefore, I suspect that the performance gain of TSP is simply due to randomness in fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Regarding the choice of pretraining corpus (OpenWebText): I'm indeed aware that OpenWebText is larger than Wikipedia, but Wikipedia is still a more standard choice for pretraining corpus if only one corpus is used (e.g., base model exploration in BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA all used Wikipedia + BookCorpus).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "This is probably because Wikipedia's text quality is arguably higher than OpenWebText which contains many short/casual/informal texts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Therefore, I'd suggest the authors follow the standard protocol for pretraining; otherwise, the findings and conclusions may not be reliable generalize well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "This is not my major concern about the paper though.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Quality: The contribution of the paper is not convincing due to the problematic empirical evaluation (see cons above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Novelty: The novelty is quite limited. The proposed objective TSP is essentially a combined version of previous sentence-level tasks (see cons above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "(1) There should be more discussions and evaluations regarding what are the real differences from NSP and SOP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "(2) The method needs to be compared with contrastive-learning based pretraining tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "(3) The evaluation needs to follow conventional pretraining/fine-tuning settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 27, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Using such a language model to boot-strap self-training (called distillation in this paper) followed by back-translation is interesting, but should be grounded in techniques that have access to the same kind of large and diverse dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "One clear example is bi-text mining (e.g. see https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.04944) where one can simply mine a bunch of parallel text from a similar kind of dataset and then train a standard encoder-decoder machine translation model (with self-training / backtranslation if so desired).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Overall, the text is hard to read to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Many terms were introduced without definitions, the sentence frequently omitted important contexts, and syntax was often broken.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper is somewhat hard to read due to syntactic/semantic issues, and I guess I possibly drops some context to assess the paper correctly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 27, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Using such a language model to boot-strap self-training (called distillation in this paper) followed by back-translation is interesting, but should be grounded in techniques that have access to the same kind of large and diverse dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "One clear example is bi-text mining (e.g. see https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.04944) where one can simply mine a bunch of parallel text from a similar kind of dataset and then train a standard encoder-decoder machine translation model (with self-training / backtranslation if so desired).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It is better to conduct experiments on other language pairs, such as English-German, to make the result more reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Currently, the performance of UNMT model on distant language pair is very poor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be more exciting, if we also observe the significant improvement of the proposed method on some distant language pairs, such as English-Chinese and English-Japanese.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "My main concern is the experiment settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It is better to conduct experiments on other language pairs, such as English-German.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As the proposed method replaces the bilingual dictionary with few-shot amplification, it seems to me that the proposed method may achieve better performance on distant language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 27, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Using such a language model to boot-strap self-training (called distillation in this paper) followed by back-translation is interesting, but should be grounded in techniques that have access to the same kind of large and diverse dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "One clear example is bi-text mining (e.g. see https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.04944) where one can simply mine a bunch of parallel text from a similar kind of dataset and then train a standard encoder-decoder machine translation model (with self-training / backtranslation if so desired).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Lack of novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Only one non-English language is considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "No low-resource language pairs considered, which is the realistic setting for unsupervised MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "No in-depth discussion on the aspects which separate this work from other unsupervised MT works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors claim that \"with sufficient scale, pre-training, and clever prompting, a single generative language model can implement the entire unsupervised neural machine translation pipeline, avoiding optimizations such as denoising autoencoding, auxiliary / adversarial losses in latent space, or ad-hoc bilingual dictionaries\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I can agree that removing auxiliary / adversarial losses or ad-hoc bilingual dictionaries are certainly steps in the right direction, current state-of-the-art unsupervised translation models have already taken these steps and do not rely on these tools.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, MASS [6] pre-trains using only a denoising autoencoding objective and yet achieves strong results comparable to the ones found in this paper (accounting for model size) and additionally does not rely on clever prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "To be explicit, the MASS scores in Table 3 are comparable to the results of GPT-3 medium in Table 1, which brings into question if the gains found in this work are truly from the approach or simply from scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "For 2), it seems obvious that language modeling should result in a better pre-training objective, since the pre-trained model is directly trained to generate natural text.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Models trained with masking strategies tend to struggle with zero-shot generation which hinders back-translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For example, mBART [4] relied on LangID filtering in the initial stages of their back-translation routine to overcome this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The authors additionally claim that \"[d]enoising autoencoding with a bilingual encoder can be viewed as a kind of latent bilingual lexicon induction, necessary for producing sufficiently aligned embeddings to kick-start back-translation.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "However, I see no reason why we can't view language modeling on multilingual data in the same light.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The initial translation performance from GPT-3 already demonstrates that language model can serve as an acceptable substitute to denoising autoencoding for kick-starting back-translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "With these thoughts in mind, we cannot consider this work\u2019s successful removal of denoising autoencoding for training unsupervised translation models as a particularly difficult task nor a surprising finding but instead as more of a confirmation of an expected result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "For example, French is the second most populous language in GPT-3\u2019s data [8].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Moreover, the original authors of GPT-3 have already demonstrated that GPT-3 can perform English < - - > French translation, further reducing the impressiveness of the result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is also interesting to note that this English bias the authors comment on also affects traditional multilingual machine translation models (both supervised [2] and unsupervised [3]) due to the reliance on English-centric parallel data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, in this setting, this bias is even more pronounced since the English data does not arise from translation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "It would be interesting to explore whether the overabundance of English monolingual data (or perhaps simply scale?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "is enough to make up for the scarcity of data available for some of the low-resource languages which appear in GPT-3\u2019s corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Similarly, it could be interesting to see if the scripts of the languages considered or their similarity with English is an important feature for this approach to succeed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "For example, do we expect GPT-3 to also excel at Hindi < - - > English translation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Both languages appear in GPT-3\u2019s pre-training corpus, so one could test it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The performance on these languages is crucial because the true value of unsupervised translation lies in low-resource language pairs, and their omission puts into question the validity of this method in realistic settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The biggest flaw is that it is unclear whether anything presented in the work is truly novel or unknown to the machine translation community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "The work can be jointly summarized as \u201clanguage modeling can yield an initial translation model whose performance can boosted tremendously by back-translation\u201d, but no single aspect of this is new, nor is the success of the combination of the various pieces at all surprising to anyone who has been following the unsupervised translation literature over the past two years.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}]}, {"paper_idx": 27, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Overall, the text is hard to read to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Many terms were introduced without definitions, the sentence frequently omitted important contexts, and syntax was often broken.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper is somewhat hard to read due to syntactic/semantic issues, and I guess I possibly drops some context to assess the paper correctly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It is better to conduct experiments on other language pairs, such as English-German, to make the result more reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Currently, the performance of UNMT model on distant language pair is very poor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be more exciting, if we also observe the significant improvement of the proposed method on some distant language pairs, such as English-Chinese and English-Japanese.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "My main concern is the experiment settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It is better to conduct experiments on other language pairs, such as English-German.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As the proposed method replaces the bilingual dictionary with few-shot amplification, it seems to me that the proposed method may achieve better performance on distant language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 27, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Overall, the text is hard to read to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Many terms were introduced without definitions, the sentence frequently omitted important contexts, and syntax was often broken.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper is somewhat hard to read due to syntactic/semantic issues, and I guess I possibly drops some context to assess the paper correctly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Lack of novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Only one non-English language is considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "No low-resource language pairs considered, which is the realistic setting for unsupervised MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "No in-depth discussion on the aspects which separate this work from other unsupervised MT works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors claim that \"with sufficient scale, pre-training, and clever prompting, a single generative language model can implement the entire unsupervised neural machine translation pipeline, avoiding optimizations such as denoising autoencoding, auxiliary / adversarial losses in latent space, or ad-hoc bilingual dictionaries\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I can agree that removing auxiliary / adversarial losses or ad-hoc bilingual dictionaries are certainly steps in the right direction, current state-of-the-art unsupervised translation models have already taken 