es", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "With these thoughts in mind, we cannot consider this work\u2019s successful removal of denoising autoencoding for training unsupervised translation models as a particularly difficult task nor a surprising finding but instead as more of a confirmation of an expected result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "For example, French is the second most populous language in GPT-3\u2019s data [8].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Moreover, the original authors of GPT-3 have already demonstrated that GPT-3 can perform English < - - > French translation, further reducing the impressiveness of the result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is also interesting to note that this English bias the authors comment on also affects traditional multilingual machine translation models (both supervised [2] and unsupervised [3]) due to the reliance on English-centric parallel data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, in this setting, this bias is even more pronounced since the English data does not arise from translation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "It would be interesting to explore whether the overabundance of English monolingual data (or perhaps simply scale?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "is enough to make up for the scarcity of data available for some of the low-resource languages which appear in GPT-3\u2019s corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Similarly, it could be interesting to see if the scripts of the languages considered or their similarity with English is an important feature for this approach to succeed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "For example, do we expect GPT-3 to also excel at Hindi < - - > English translation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Both languages appear in GPT-3\u2019s pre-training corpus, so one could test it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The performance on these languages is crucial because the true value of unsupervised translation lies in low-resource language pairs, and their omission puts into question the validity of this method in realistic settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The biggest flaw is that it is unclear whether anything presented in the work is truly novel or unknown to the machine translation community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "The work can be jointly summarized as \u201clanguage modeling can yield an initial translation model whose performance can boosted tremendously by back-translation\u201d, but no single aspect of this is new, nor is the success of the combination of the various pieces at all surprising to anyone who has been following the unsupervised translation literature over the past two years.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}]}, {"paper_idx": 27, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It is better to conduct experiments on other language pairs, such as English-German, to make the result more reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Currently, the performance of UNMT model on distant language pair is very poor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be more exciting, if we also observe the significant improvement of the proposed method on some distant language pairs, such as English-Chinese and English-Japanese.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "My main concern is the experiment settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It is better to conduct experiments on other language pairs, such as English-German.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As the proposed method replaces the bilingual dictionary with few-shot amplification, it seems to me that the proposed method may achieve better performance on distant language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Lack of novelty", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Only one non-English language is considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "No low-resource language pairs considered, which is the realistic setting for unsupervised MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "No in-depth discussion on the aspects which separate this work from other unsupervised MT works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors claim that \"with sufficient scale, pre-training, and clever prompting, a single generative language model can implement the entire unsupervised neural machine translation pipeline, avoiding optimizations such as denoising autoencoding, auxiliary / adversarial losses in latent space, or ad-hoc bilingual dictionaries\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I can agree that removing auxiliary / adversarial losses or ad-hoc bilingual dictionaries are certainly steps in the right direction, current state-of-the-art unsupervised translation models have already taken these steps and do not rely on these tools.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, MASS [6] pre-trains using only a denoising autoencoding objective and yet achieves strong results comparable to the ones found in this paper (accounting for model size) and additionally does not rely on clever prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "To be explicit, the MASS scores in Table 3 are comparable to the results of GPT-3 medium in Table 1, which brings into question if the gains found in this work are truly from the approach or simply from scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "For 2), it seems obvious that language modeling should result in a better pre-training objective, since the pre-trained model is directly trained to generate natural text.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Models trained with masking strategies tend to struggle with zero-shot generation which hinders back-translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For example, mBART [4] relied on LangID filtering in the initial stages of their back-translation routine to overcome this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The authors additionally claim that \"[d]enoising autoencoding with a bilingual encoder can be viewed as a kind of latent bilingual lexicon induction, necessary for producing sufficiently aligned embeddings to kick-start back-translation.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "However, I see no reason why we can't view language modeling on multilingual data in the same light.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The initial translation performance from GPT-3 already demonstrates that language model can serve as an acceptable substitute to denoising autoencoding for kick-starting back-translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "With these thoughts in mind, we cannot consider this work\u2019s successful removal of denoising autoencoding for training unsupervised translation models as a particularly difficult task nor a surprising finding but instead as more of a confirmation of an expected result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "For example, French is the second most populous language in GPT-3\u2019s data [8].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Moreover, the original authors of GPT-3 have already demonstrated that GPT-3 can perform English < - - > French translation, further reducing the impressiveness of the result.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is also interesting to note that this English bias the authors comment on also affects traditional multilingual machine translation models (both supervised [2] and unsupervised [3]) due to the reliance on English-centric parallel data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, in this setting, this bias is even more pronounced since the English data does not arise from translation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "It would be interesting to explore whether the overabundance of English monolingual data (or perhaps simply scale?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "is enough to make up for the scarcity of data available for some of the low-resource languages which appear in GPT-3\u2019s corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Similarly, it could be interesting to see if the scripts of the languages considered or their similarity with English is an important feature for this approach to succeed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "For example, do we expect GPT-3 to also excel at Hindi < - - > English translation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Both languages appear in GPT-3\u2019s pre-training corpus, so one could test it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The performance on these languages is crucial because the true value of unsupervised translation lies in low-resource language pairs, and their omission puts into question the validity of this method in realistic settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The biggest flaw is that it is unclear whether anything presented in the work is truly novel or unknown to the machine translation community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "The work can be jointly summarized as \u201clanguage modeling can yield an initial translation model whose performance can boosted tremendously by back-translation\u201d, but no single aspect of this is new, nor is the success of the combination of the various pieces at all surprising to anyone who has been following the unsupervised translation literature over the past two years.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}]}, {"paper_idx": 28, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "(Weaknesses)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "No novel/brand new ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Mostly empirical analysis based on OpenAI playground.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Some major arguments are less supported.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The submission is an analysis paper rather than finding something new.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "(Major concerns) How to make sure Codex model clearly outperforms Instruct model?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "This is a critical question as the authors measures the main experiments (Table 2) that compare Multiple Chocie Prompting (MCP) and Cloze Prompting (CP) only with Codex model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The capability to perform MCSB could be due to human feedback alignment by Reinforcement Learning rather than other points indicated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Are the PPA difference between Codex and Instruct (in Figure 2) statistically significant?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "While no statistical test has been provided, it seems not easy to decline null hypothesis that says the difference is a random effect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Only Codex tested on OpenBookQA shows strong performance gain when using MCP, whereas Instruct outperforms Codex on the other two tasks in Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "More detailed experiments are necessary to convince how Codex achieve such higher accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Any reason to choose OpenBookQA which also matters the performance of retriever?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do you know how Codex model is exactly trained?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Codex model that you used could be first based on Instruct, then being further trained on code data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Equally likely, Codex model might perform it's own alignment similar to Instruct but based on the preference of generated codes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Multiple choices prompting (MCP) has been used in other QA tasks, such as TruthfulQA and RACE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the experimental results prove that the proposed multiple choices prompting (MCP) methods can outperform existing cloze prompting (CP) methods, the reasons behind it are still unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since the authors have listed several problems within CP methods, I'm curious about whether these problems are all solved or avoided by their MCP methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "More analysis is needed to show this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 28, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "(Weaknesses)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "No novel/brand new ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Mostly empirical analysis based on OpenAI playground.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Some major arguments are less supported.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The submission is an analysis paper rather than finding something new.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "(Major concerns) How to make sure Codex model clearly outperforms Instruct model?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "This is a critical question as the authors measures the main experiments (Table 2) that compare Multiple Chocie Prompting (MCP) and Cloze Prompting (CP) only with Codex model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The capability to perform MCSB could be due to human feedback alignment by Reinforcement Learning rather than other points indicated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Are the PPA difference between Codex and Instruct (in Figure 2) statistically significant?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "While no statistical test has been provided, it seems not easy to decline null hypothesis that says the difference is a random effect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Only Codex tested on OpenBookQA shows strong performance gain when using MCP, whereas Instruct outperforms Codex on the other two tasks in Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "More detailed experiments are necessary to convince how Codex achieve such higher accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Any reason to choose OpenBookQA which also matters the performance of retriever?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do you know how Codex model is exactly trained?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Codex model that you used could be first based on Instruct, then being further trained on code data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Equally likely, Codex model might perform it's own alignment similar to Instruct but based on the preference of generated codes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses: The approach is not new, just discussed and evaluated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The authors differentiate their suggested prompting from \u201cprompt engineering\u201d, which they seem to define as fine-tuning of prompts to increase model performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However, I\u2019m not convinced that these are fundamentally different, and would include research such as theirs in the general domain of prompt engineering.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 28, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Multiple choices prompting (MCP) has been used in other QA tasks, such as TruthfulQA and RACE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the experimental results prove that the proposed multiple choices prompting (MCP) methods can outperform existing cloze prompting (CP) methods, the reasons behind it are still unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since the authors have listed several problems within CP methods, I'm curious about whether these problems are all solved or avoided by their MCP methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "More analysis is needed to show this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses: The approach is not new, just discussed and evaluated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The authors differentiate their suggested prompting from \u201cprompt engineering\u201d, which they seem to define as fine-tuning of prompts to increase model performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However, I\u2019m not convinced that these are fundamentally different, and would include research such as theirs in the general domain of prompt engineering.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 29, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The authors didn\u2019t give a comprehensive explanation on how they read such information or a concrete quantitative comparison to support this claim.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Adjusting the numbers to achieve the 5/4/3/2 cuteness gets slightly in the way of understanding, unfortunately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The main reason for this is that the \"veins of CL methods\" has a different number over the course of the paper, which makes it hard to identify when a given list of N things is a list of the \"veins of CL methods\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Specifically, in the abstract and intro this number is 4, in section 2.3 this number is 3, in section 3.1 this number is 6, in Table 1 this number is 5 (two different sets of 5), in Figure 1 this number is 6, and in Figure 2 this number is 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The Figure here is too small to even attempt to parse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would be very helpful to have a sentence that gives intuition about what's being measured with the accuracy metric.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The definition is there, but it took me a second to realize that the intuitive idea is that it's measuring the accuracy of past tasks after the model has moved on to learning new tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 30, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Section 2.2 needs to have some more intuition and clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Limitations of the work has not been discussed by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "However, the overall idea is very similar to CRISS [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The CRISS approach consists of a cross-lingual retrieval module and a seq2seq module, and they are optimized alternately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Similarly, the CORA approach in this paper also consists of DPR (Dense Passage Retriever, mbert structure) and GEN(Answer Generator, mt5 structure), and they are also optimized alternately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Although CRISS [1] doesn\u2019t target at multilingual open QA, it has strong performance on cross-lingual sentence retrieval, which is related to multilingual open QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors should compare their work with CRISS [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Authors claim that they introduce a new dense passage retrieval algorithm, but don\u2019t mention any similar work about dense passage retrieval [2].", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 30, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Section 2.2 needs to have some more intuition and clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Limitations of the work has not been discussed by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "However, the result is stronger than the sum of its parts, unlocking the ability to combine information across many languages\u2019 wikipedias, and enjoy the resultant improvements in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "One limitation is that this won\u2019t always work well if the language links aren\u2019t high quality for some languages, or in other domains where language links aren\u2019t available (either because wikipedia isn\u2019t a good background corpus, or because answers are not wikipedia/wikidata entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 30, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Section 2.2 needs to have some more intuition and clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Limitations of the work has not been discussed by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "But the novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The most important part, I think, is the data expansion part described in Section 2.2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The baselines compared in Table 1 and 2 are also weak. Why not compare with the submissions on the TyDi QA leaderboards? Why stronger multilingual pre-trained models such as XLM-R or mBART are not used and fine-tuned for comparison?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 30, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "However, the overall idea is very similar to CRISS [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The CRISS approach consists of a cross-lingual retrieval module and a seq2seq module, and they are optimized alternately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Similarly, the CORA approach in this paper also consists of DPR (Dense Passage Retriever, mbert structure) and GEN(Answer Generator, mt5 structure), and they are also optimized alternately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Although CRISS [1] doesn\u2019t target at multilingual open QA, it has strong performance on cross-lingual sentence retrieval, which is related to multilingual open QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors should compare their work with CRISS [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Authors claim that they introduce a new dense passage retrieval algorithm, but don\u2019t mention any similar work about dense passage retrieval [2].", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "However, the result is stronger than the sum of its parts, unlocking the ability to combine information across many languages\u2019 wikipedias, and enjoy the resultant improvements in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "One limitation is that this won\u2019t always work well if the language links aren\u2019t high quality for some languages, or in other domains where language links aren\u2019t available (either because wikipedia isn\u2019t a good background corpus, or because answers are not wikipedia/wikidata entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 30, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "However, the overall idea is very similar to CRISS [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The CRISS approach consists of a cross-lingual retrieval module and a seq2seq module, and they are optimized alternately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Similarly, the CORA approach in this paper also consists of DPR (Dense Passage Retriever, mbert structure) and GEN(Answer Generator, mt5 structure), and they are also optimized alternately.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Although CRISS [1] doesn\u2019t target at multilingual open QA, it has strong performance on cross-lingual sentence retrieval, which is related to multilingual open QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors should compare their work with CRISS [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Authors claim that they introduce a new dense passage retrieval algorithm, but don\u2019t mention any similar work about dense passage retrieval [2].", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "But the novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The most important part, I think, is the data expansion part described in Section 2.2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The baselines compared in Table 1 and 2 are also weak. Why not compare with the submissions on the TyDi QA leaderboards? Why stronger multilingual pre-trained models such as XLM-R or mBART are not used and fine-tuned for comparison?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 30, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "However, the result is stronger than the sum of its parts, unlocking the ability to combine information across many languages\u2019 wikipedias, and enjoy the resultant improvements in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "One limitation is that this won\u2019t always work well if the language links aren\u2019t high quality for some languages, or in other domains where language links aren\u2019t available (either because wikipedia isn\u2019t a good background corpus, or because answers are not wikipedia/wikidata entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "But the novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The most important part, I think, is the data expansion part described in Section 2.2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The baselines compared in Table 1 and 2 are also weak. Why not compare with the submissions on the TyDi QA leaderboards? Why stronger multilingual pre-trained models such as XLM-R or mBART are not used and fine-tuned for comparison?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 31, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors do not include embedding layer and prediction layer size in experiments, while only report the Transformer encoder size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I know that this can make the size of compressed model really amazing (e.g., 1.8M) and the compression ratio amazing (e.g., 86M/12.3M=7) but is not fair as the whole model including the embedding layer are used when deploying.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If the embedding layer is added, the model size will increase a lot, and the compression ratio will decrease, which make the experimental results less surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "But this should be made clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Some other method(s) are missing in the related works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "For example: [1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Though the introduced methods are not new, the adaptation to the Transformer layers and the analysis are interesting, and the experiments are convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper proposed multiple potential ways of compressing weight matrices (matrix decomposition and tensor train decomposition) as some alternatives to the proposed Tucker decomposition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, the author didn't compare with tensor train decomposition due to time constraints.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I believe the paper will be more solid by adding ablation studies on tensor train decomposition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper only performs experiments on BERT-base and TinyBERT models, but I believe that the compression method proposed in the paper should be more demanded by larger models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Some design choices in the paper seem arbitrary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, why do you jointly compress all the layers, including all the FFN weights and attention weights?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In addition, the reason for splitting each FFN weight matrix into 4 seems to make it possible to combine it with attention weights.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "How does this method compare with previous works in terms of training/inference latency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The paper would be better with more experiments on larger models and ablation studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the presentation and the rationale behind the idea are not clear to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}]}, {"paper_idx": 31, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors do not include embedding layer and prediction layer size in experiments, while only report the Transformer encoder size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I know that this can make the size of compressed model really amazing (e.g., 1.8M) and the compression ratio amazing (e.g., 86M/12.3M=7) but is not fair as the whole model including the embedding layer are used when deploying.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If the embedding layer is added, the model size will increase a lot, and the compression ratio will decrease, which make the experimental results less surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "But this should be made clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Some other method(s) are missing in the related works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "For example: [1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Though the introduced methods are not new, the adaptation to the Transformer layers and the analysis are interesting, and the experiments are convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "The use of tensor decomposition for compressing neural networks has been explored extensively for CNNs, RNNs and Embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The use here to compress transformers is a natural extension of the idea", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Decomposability and low-rank nature of FFN and MHA layers has been discussed previously in the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors themselves refer to these prior works", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Cordonnier et al 2021, further discusses the use of tucker decomposition to express MHA layers, albeit in a slightly different context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I also recommend exploring the systems impact of running training and inference using tucker decomposed layers and why say 50x reduction in parameter count, does not lead to a 50x improvement in RPS (Table 4).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Further, I would encourage the authors to explore and understand why finetuning with KD in Table 6 leads to such large accuracy drop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "GD+TD should lead to better accuracy, but improving accuracy by 40% is an interesting data point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Tucker decomposition has been used extensively in NN to compress RNNs, CNNs and Embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 31, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors do not include embedding layer and prediction layer size in experiments, while only report the Transformer encoder size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I know that this can make the size of compressed model really amazing (e.g., 1.8M) and the compression ratio amazing (e.g., 86M/12.3M=7) but is not fair as the whole model including the embedding layer are used when deploying.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If the embedding layer is added, the model size will increase a lot, and the compression ratio will decrease, which make the experimental results less surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "But this should be made clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Some other method(s) are missing in the related works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "For example: [1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Though the introduced methods are not new, the adaptation to the Transformer layers and the analysis are interesting, and the experiments are convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "However, I have some reservations about whether this is sound (see weaknesses).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper introduces many variants of decomposing the matrices (Table 2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However the results only seem to be based on one of the methods (i.e. IV).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I realize that some of variants have already been studied in the literature (e.g. Mao et al., Noach and Goldberg), but since the setup is not identical, it is crucial that the proposed approach is compared against both II and III.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, it is not clear whether the improvement in performance is coming from the actual proposed method, or something else.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Since the matrices across layers often have different scaling, and since tensor decomposition is approximating some reconstruction error (i.e. L2 in the case of SVD), it's not clear that performing decomposition with all the matrices makes sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The method requires a pipelined approach where one must first perform generalized distillation against the BERT model before doing task-specific distillation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Some reservations about the soundness of the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "And it is furthermore not clear that the improvements are coming from the proposed approach as opposed to something else, since an ablation study across the different decomposition methods is missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 31, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper proposed multiple potential ways of compressing weight matrices (matrix decomposition and tensor train decomposition) as some alternatives to the proposed Tucker decomposition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, the author didn't compare with tensor train decomposition due to time constraints.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I believe the paper will be more solid by adding ablation studies on tensor train decomposition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper only performs experiments on BERT-base and TinyBERT models, but I believe that the compression method proposed in the paper should be more demanded by larger models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Some design choices in the paper seem arbitrary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, why do you jointly compress all the layers, including all the FFN weights and attention weights?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In addition, the reason for splitting each FFN weight matrix into 4 seems to make it possible to combine it with attention weights.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "How does this method compare with previous works in terms of training/inference latency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The paper would be better with more experiments on larger models and ablation studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the presentation and the rationale behind the idea are not clear to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "The use of tensor decomposition for compressing neural networks has been explored extensively for CNNs, RNNs and Embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The use here to compress transformers is a natural extension of the idea", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Decomposability and low-rank nature of FFN and MHA layers has been discussed previously in the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors themselves refer to these prior works", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Cordonnier et al 2021, further discusses the use of tucker decomposition to express MHA layers, albeit in a slightly different context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I also recommend exploring the systems impact of running training and inference using tucker decomposed layers and why say 50x reduction in parameter count, does not lead to a 50x improvement in RPS (Table 4).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Further, I would encourage the authors to explore and understand why finetuning with KD in Table 6 leads to such large accuracy drop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "GD+TD should lead to better accuracy, but improving accuracy by 40% is an interesting data point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Tucker decomposition has been used extensively in NN to compress RNNs, CNNs and Embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 31, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper proposed multiple potential ways of compressing weight matrices (matrix decomposition and tensor train decomposition) as some alternatives to the proposed Tucker decomposition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, the author didn't compare with tensor train decomposition due to time constraints.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I believe the paper will be more solid by adding ablation studies on tensor train decomposition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper only performs experiments on BERT-base and TinyBERT models, but I believe that the compression method proposed in the paper should be more demanded by larger models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Some design choices in the paper seem arbitrary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, why do you jointly compress all the layers, including all the FFN weights and attention weights?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In addition, the reason for splitting each FFN weight matrix into 4 seems to make it possible to combine it with attention weights.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "How does this method compare with previous works in terms of training/inference latency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The paper would be better with more experiments on larger models and ablation studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the presentation and the rationale behind the idea are not clear to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "However, I have some reservations about whether this is sound (see weaknesses).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper introduces many variants of decomposing the matrices (Table 2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However the results only seem to be based on one of the methods (i.e. IV).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I realize that some of variants have already been studied in the literature (e.g. Mao et al., Noach and Goldberg), but since the setup is not identical, it is crucial that the proposed approach is compared against both II and III.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, it is not clear whether the improvement in performance is coming from the actual proposed method, or something else.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Since the matrices across layers often have different scaling, and since tensor decomposition is approximating some reconstruction error (i.e. L2 in the case of SVD), it's not clear that performing decomposition with all the matrices makes sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The method requires a pipelined approach where one must first perform generalized distillation against the BERT model before doing task-specific distillation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Some reservations about the soundness of the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "And it is furthermore not clear that the improvements are coming from the proposed approach as opposed to something else, since an ablation study across the different decomposition methods is missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 31, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "The use of tensor decomposition for compressing neural networks has been explored extensively for CNNs, RNNs and Embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The use here to compress transformers is a natural extension of the idea", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Decomposability and low-rank nature of FFN and MHA layers has been discussed previously in the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors themselves refer to these prior works", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Cordonnier et al 2021, further discusses the use of tucker decomposition to express MHA layers, albeit in a slightly different context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I also recommend exploring the systems impact of running training and inference using tucker decomposed layers and why say 50x reduction in parameter count, does not lead to a 50x improvement in RPS (Table 4).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Further, I would encourage the authors to explore and understand why finetuning with KD in Table 6 leads to such large accuracy drop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "GD+TD should lead to better accuracy, but improving accuracy by 40% is an interesting data point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Tucker decomposition has been used extensively in NN to compress RNNs, CNNs and Embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "However, I have some reservations about whether this is sound (see weaknesses).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper introduces many variants of decomposing the matrices (Table 2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However the results only seem to be based on one of the methods (i.e. IV).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I realize that some of variants have already been studied in the literature (e.g. Mao et al., Noach and Goldberg), but since the setup is not identical, it is crucial that the proposed approach is compared against both II and III.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, it is not clear whether the improvement in performance is coming from the actual proposed method, or something else.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Since the matrices across layers often have different scaling, and since tensor decomposition is approximating some reconstruction error (i.e. L2 in the case of SVD), it's not clear that performing decomposition with all the matrices makes sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The method requires a pipelined approach where one must first perform generalized distillation against the BERT model before doing task-specific distillation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Some reservations about the soundness of the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "And it is furthermore not clear that the improvements are coming from the proposed approach as opposed to something else, since an ablation study across the different decomposition methods is missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 32, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, they might have even spent more space making it extra clear than is needed -- Figure 1 helps a reader understand at a glance what is being optimized, then the terms in figure one are optimized in Figure 3 and we can see the contribution from the different terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Most of the paper also goes into detail deriving and explaining these terms, and the paper could benefit (only a little) from some additional experiments taking some of that space.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would also recommend including more information about the hyperparameters used for the experiments; presumably default hparams were used, but it would be good for you to be clear about exactly what they were.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Most of the paper is focused on the derivation of the new approach, and the clarity of that part is also valuable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "One of the claimed contributions is to show the catastrophic forgetting phenomenon of adversarial finetuning of pretrained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I found this claim a bit not solid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "From Figures 1 and 2, it is based on the relative L2 distance of the pretrained and finefuned models in the parameter space.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However it does not necessarily mean it is really forgetting all the generic linguistic features.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It is more straightforward to justify this by showing downstream tasks performance or language model performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In addition, it is common to use a smaller learning rate or early stopping in fine tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Hence, showing the enlarged discrepancy in parameters space in Figure 1 is not that informative.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is much more work in the image domain for adversarial training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For example, TRADES [1], ensembling, early stopping [2], etc. I wonder whether the adversarial baseline is too simple and doesn\u2019t incorporate few newer findings in the community.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Clarity:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "When mentioning pretrained language models, it is not clear to me in the beginning whether the proposed approach is for masked language models (e.g., BERT) or for autoregressive language models (e.g., gpt-2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Significance:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The presented findings on finetuning on masked language models seems promising, but it is hard to justify as this is evaluated on new tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, it will be more insightful to see quantitative examples or more analysis for the actual adversarial attack (success, fail ones) beyond reporting accuracy -- this is because adversarial attacks could potentially be imperfect in the NLP domain, or there might be gradient obfuscation problems in claiming robust accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 32, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, they might have even spent more space making it extra clear than is needed -- Figure 1 helps a reader understand at a glance what is being optimized, then the terms in figure one are optimized in Figure 3 and we can see the contribution from the different terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Most of the paper also goes into detail deriving and explaining these terms, and the paper could benefit (only a little) from some additional experiments taking some of that space.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would also recommend including more information about the hyperparameters used for the experiments; presumably default hparams were used, but it would be good for you to be clear about exactly what they were.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Most of the paper is focused on the derivation of the new approach, and the clarity of that part is also valuable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The improvements over the baseline models are relatively marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper argues that previous adversarial fine-tuning would cost severe catastrophic forgetting issue, and I expect the improvement to be higher if the propose method can well tackle this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This paper needs analyses in terms of how the proposed method addresses the catastrophic forgetting issue compared to baseline models, since the main claim of the RIFT is to tackle this issue.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}]}, {"paper_idx": 32, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "One of the claimed contributions is to show the catastrophic forgetting phenomenon of adversarial finetuning of pretrained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I found this claim a bit not solid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "From Figures 1 and 2, it is based on the relative L2 distance of the pretrained and finefuned models in the parameter space.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However it does not necessarily mean it is really forgetting all the generic linguistic features.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It is more straightforward to justify this by showing downstream tasks performance or language model performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In addition, it is common to use a smaller learning rate or early stopping in fine tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Hence, showing the enlarged discrepancy in parameters space in Figure 1 is not that informative.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is much more work in the image domain for adversarial training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For example, TRADES [1], ensembling, early stopping [2], etc. I wonder whether the adversarial baseline is too simple and doesn\u2019t incorporate few newer findings in the community.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Clarity:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "When mentioning pretrained language models, it is not clear to me in the beginning whether the proposed approach is for masked language models (e.g., BERT) or for autoregressive language models (e.g., gpt-2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Significance:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The presented findings on finetuning on masked language models seems promising, but it is hard to justify as this is evaluated on new tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, it will be more insightful to see quantitative examples or more analysis for the actual adversarial attack (success, fail ones) beyond reporting accuracy -- this is because adversarial attacks could potentially be imperfect in the NLP domain, or there might be gradient obfuscation problems in claiming robust accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The improvements over the baseline models are relatively marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper argues that previous adversarial fine-tuning would cost severe catastrophic forgetting issue, and I expect the improvement to be higher if the propose method can well tackle this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This paper needs analyses in terms of how the proposed method addresses the catastrophic forgetting issue compared to baseline models, since the main claim of the RIFT is to tackle this issue.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}]}, {"paper_idx": 33, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "What is the batch size at test time tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Is the added robust prefix the same for the entire test set, or the same within a batch but different across batches, or unique for every test example?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is an important point to assess whether the experiments are in an online setting where test data arrives in-stream or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Section 5 is not very convincing to me:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) there are only several case studies without any quantitative results; I think it may appear in the appendix only or just uses a short paragraph in the main body (because the statements in Section 5 can only be good hypotheses which are not well-supported by quantitative evidence, also, attention is not a convincing proxy for the explanation, see point (2)), yet it takes more than one page in the current version;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) the interpretation from the perspective of attention weights bothers me a bit, attention as an explanatory tool is known to not be faithful [1] \u2013 a larger attention weight does not necessarily mean the final prediction depends on it more than others, and vice versa, thus maybe not overread it too much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "As said above, it is ok to do it this way in the appendix, but using over one page of the main content for this is not convincing to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the attention visualization figures (e.g. Figure 4), why does a word attend itself as well instead of only attending to the contexts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For LM, I think that the diagonals in the attention figure should be zero, or am I missing something?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, the presentation could be improved here, in the figure caption you can explain what the rows and columns mean in the visualization to make it easier to read.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Section 6 is difficult to follow without reading the appendix, and it is disconnected from the rest of the paper in terms of the paper structure \u2013 having a theoretical interpretation section after experiments at the end of the paper is not a good presentation structure in my view.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If you think Section 6 is important to have, move it before the experiment section and clarify more details to make it more self-contained; if it is not very important, you can just put it into the appendix while briefly mentioning it in the main body.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Besides the presentation issues above, there are some other minor presentation places which could be improved, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(1) Eq 4, an undefined variable X_C suddenly comes in without explanation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "(2) Better to explain Eq (8) with more text given that this is an important equation for the proposed method", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "[1] Serrano et al. Is Attention Interpretable? ACL 2019", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Most studies aim to match the performance of full model tuning via updating a subset of parameters but rarely study the robustness of lightweight fine-tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors argue that robustness is important for lightweight tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "But I still think it is better to provide a comparison between lightweight tuning methods will full tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Provide a basic starting observation about whether lightweight methods bring more challenges on robustness or not/", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 33, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "What is the batch size at test time tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Is the added robust prefix the same for the entire test set, or the same within a batch but different across batches, or unique for every test example?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is an important point to assess whether the experiments are in an online setting where test data arrives in-stream or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Section 5 is not very convincing to me:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) there are only several case studies without any quantitative results; I think it may appear in the appendix only or just uses a short paragraph in the main body (because the statements in Section 5 can only be good hypotheses which are not well-supported by quantitative evidence, also, attention is not a convincing proxy for the explanation, see point (2)), yet it takes more than one page in the current version;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) the interpretation from the perspective of attention weights bothers me a bit, attention as an explanatory tool is known to not be faithful [1] \u2013 a larger attention weight does not necessarily mean the final prediction depends on it more than others, and vice versa, thus maybe not overread it too much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "As said above, it is ok to do it this way in the appendix, but using over one page of the main content for this is not convincing to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the attention visualization figures (e.g. Figure 4), why does a word attend itself as well instead of only attending to the contexts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For LM, I think that the diagonals in the attention figure should be zero, or am I missing something?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, the presentation could be improved here, in the figure caption you can explain what the rows and columns mean in the visualization to make it easier to read.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Section 6 is difficult to follow without reading the appendix, and it is disconnected from the rest of the paper in terms of the paper structure \u2013 having a theoretical interpretation section after experiments at the end of the paper is not a good presentation structure in my view.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If you think Section 6 is important to have, move it before the experiment section and clarify more details to make it more self-contained; if it is not very important, you can just put it into the appendix while briefly mentioning it in the main body.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Besides the presentation issues above, there are some other minor presentation places which could be improved, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(1) Eq 4, an undefined variable X_C suddenly comes in without explanation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "(2) Better to explain Eq (8) with more text given that this is an important equation for the proposed method", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "[1] Serrano et al. Is Attention Interpretable? ACL 2019", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 19, "text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Indeed, my understanding of Section 3 and Eq.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "8 is that the specific batches used at inference will play an important role as they impact P_psi.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "This is my key concern with this paper as it brings up quite a few issues that should be mentioned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Inference for a datapoint depends also on other datapoints in the batch.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This is different enough from other ML setups that it should be highlighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "It also causes reproducibility issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For instance, batch norm avoids this by fixing batch statistics at inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is not clear that the method works for low inference batch size (opt of Eq.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "8).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The inference batch size used is not mentioned anywhere.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "If the method requires batch size > some N (or if performance varies widely with batch size), this seems a strong assumption that should be made clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "You do not always get several samples of the attack on your system.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "It is not clear how well the method works when the inference batches are a mix of unperturbed samples and perturbed ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "This seems like a more realistic attack scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It is not clear how well the method works if there are different attacks in the inference batch, which also seems like a more realistic threat model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "The writing could be improved substantially.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Some examples: p2 \u201cremaining the pretrained models unmodified\u201d -> \u201ckeeping\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Also on p3 \u201cremaining its lightweightness\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "I suggest doing another pass as it does not align with the quality of the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 33, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "What is the batch size at test time tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Is the added robust prefix the same for the entire test set, or the same within a batch but different across batches, or unique for every test example?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is an important point to assess whether the experiments are in an online setting where test data arrives in-stream or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Section 5 is not very convincing to me:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) there are only several case studies without any quantitative results; I think it may appear in the appendix only or just uses a short paragraph in the main body (because the statements in Section 5 can only be good hypotheses which are not well-supported by quantitative evidence, also, attention is not a convincing proxy for the explanation, see point (2)), yet it takes more than one page in the current version;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) the interpretation from the perspective of attention weights bothers me a bit, attention as an explanatory tool is known to not be faithful [1] \u2013 a larger attention weight does not necessarily mean the final prediction depends on it more than others, and vice versa, thus maybe not overread it too much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "As said above, it is ok to do it this way in the appendix, but using over one page of the main content for this is not convincing to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the attention visualization figures (e.g. Figure 4), why does a word attend itself as well instead of only attending to the contexts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For LM, I think that the diagonals in the attention figure should be zero, or am I missing something?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, the presentation could be improved here, in the figure caption you can explain what the rows and columns mean in the visualization to make it easier to read.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Section 6 is difficult to follow without reading the appendix, and it is disconnected from the rest of the paper in terms of the paper structure \u2013 having a theoretical interpretation section after experiments at the end of the paper is not a good presentation structure in my view.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If you think Section 6 is important to have, move it before the experiment section and clarify more details to make it more self-contained; if it is not very important, you can just put it into the appendix while briefly mentioning it in the main body.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Besides the presentation issues above, there are some other minor presentation places which could be improved, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(1) Eq 4, an undefined variable X_C suddenly comes in without explanation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "(2) Better to explain Eq (8) with more text given that this is an important equation for the proposed method", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "[1] Serrano et al. Is Attention Interpretable? ACL 2019", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "The experiments are OK but could be a bit more streamlined.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I guess two things came up when I was looking at them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It may be good to expand the scope of the paper to generation tasks, as these are likely more suceptible to adversarial attacks. What I mean by this is that the worse case scenario is much worse for generation tasks: while in binary classification the worse case is bad accuracy, for adversarial attacks on generation tasks, potentially very harmful text could be generated, which is much worse than simply getting the answer wrong.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The other thing is that it is hard to contextualize how good the numbers in your framework are.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, I have no idea how good 52% on VIPR for SST2 is.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If previously proposed defenses are able to get 90% on the same task, then it is unlikely that the proposed method will gain traction, even if it is more computationally efficient with prefix tuning. So it would strengthen this paper a lot to have this comparison.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Since a lot of work was put into the paper, I will pay my respect and make a picky point: the bibliography could be cleaned up a bit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "E.g., capitalize RoBERTa correctly, add URLs consistently, capitalize all conference names (e.g., IEEE transactions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 33, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Most studies aim to match the performance of full model tuning via updating a subset of parameters but rarely study the robustness of lightweight fine-tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors argue that robustness is important for lightweight tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "But I still think it is better to provide a comparison between lightweight tuning methods will full tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Provide a basic starting observation about whether lightweight methods bring more challenges on robustness or not/", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 19, "text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Indeed, my understanding of Section 3 and Eq.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "8 is that the specific batches used at inference will play an important role as they impact P_psi.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "This is my key concern with this paper as it brings up quite a few issues that should be mentioned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Inference for a datapoint depends also on other datapoints in the batch.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This is different enough from other ML setups that it should be highlighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "It also causes reproducibility issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For instance, batch norm avoids this by fixing batch statistics at inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is not clear that the method works for low inference batch size (opt of Eq.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "8).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The inference batch size used is not mentioned anywhere.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "If the method requires batch size > some N (or if performance varies widely with batch size), this seems a strong assumption that should be made clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "You do not always get several samples of the attack on your system.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "It is not clear how well the method works when the inference batches are a mix of unperturbed samples and perturbed ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "This seems like a more realistic attack scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It is not clear how well the method works if there are different attacks in the inference batch, which also seems like a more realistic threat model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "The writing could be improved substantially.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Some examples: p2 \u201cremaining the pretrained models unmodified\u201d -> \u201ckeeping\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Also on p3 \u201cremaining its lightweightness\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "I suggest doing another pass as it does not align with the quality of the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 33, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Most studies aim to match the performance of full model tuning via updating a subset of parameters but rarely study the robustness of lightweight fine-tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors argue that robustness is important for lightweight tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "But I still think it is better to provide a comparison between lightweight tuning methods will full tuning methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Provide a basic starting observation about whether lightweight methods bring more challenges on robustness or not/", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "The experiments are OK but could be a bit more streamlined.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I guess two things came up when I was looking at them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It may be good to expand the scope of the paper to generation tasks, as these are likely more suceptible to adversarial attacks. What I mean by this is that the worse case scenario is much worse for generation tasks: while in binary classification the worse case is bad accuracy, for adversarial attacks on generation tasks, potentially very harmful text could be generated, which is much worse than simply getting the answer wrong.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The other thing is that it is hard to contextualize how good the numbers in your framework are.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, I have no idea how good 52% on VIPR for SST2 is.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If previously proposed defenses are able to get 90% on the same task, then it is unlikely that the proposed method will gain traction, even if it is more computationally efficient with prefix tuning. So it would strengthen this paper a lot to have this comparison.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Since a lot of work was put into the paper, I will pay my respect and make a picky point: the bibliography could be cleaned up a bit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "E.g., capitalize RoBERTa correctly, add URLs consistently, capitalize all conference names (e.g., IEEE transactions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 33, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 19, "text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Indeed, my understanding of Section 3 and Eq.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "8 is that the specific batches used at inference will play an important role as they impact P_psi.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "This is my key concern with this paper as it brings up quite a few issues that should be mentioned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Inference for a datapoint depends also on other datapoints in the batch.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This is different enough from other ML setups that it should be highlighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "It also causes reproducibility issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For instance, batch norm avoids this by fixing batch statistics at inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is not clear that the method works for low inference batch size (opt of Eq.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "8).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The inference batch size used is not mentioned anywhere.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "If the method requires batch size > some N (or if performance varies widely with batch size), this seems a strong assumption that should be made clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "You do not always get several samples of the attack on your system.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "It is not clear how well the method works when the inference batches are a mix of unperturbed samples and perturbed ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "This seems like a more realistic attack scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It is not clear how well the method works if there are different attacks in the inference batch, which also seems like a more realistic threat model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "The writing could be improved substantially.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Some examples: p2 \u201cremaining the pretrained models unmodified\u201d -> \u201ckeeping\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Also on p3 \u201cremaining its lightweightness\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "I suggest doing another pass as it does not align with the quality of the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "The experiments are OK but could be a bit more streamlined.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I guess two things came up when I was looking at them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It may be good to expand the scope of the paper to generation tasks, as these are likely more suceptible to adversarial attacks. What I mean by this is that the worse case scenario is much worse for generation tasks: while in binary classification the worse case is bad accuracy, for adversarial attacks on generation tasks, potentially very harmful text could be generated, which is much worse than simply getting the answer wrong.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The other thing is that it is hard to contextualize how good the numbers in your framework are.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, I have no idea how good 52% on VIPR for SST2 is.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If previously proposed defenses are able to get 90% on the same task, then it is unlikely that the proposed method will gain traction, even if it is more computationally efficient with prefix tuning. So it would strengthen this paper a lot to have this comparison.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Since a lot of work was put into the paper, I will pay my respect and make a picky point: the bibliography could be cleaned up a bit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "E.g., capitalize RoBERTa correctly, add URLs consistently, capitalize all conference names (e.g., IEEE transactions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This method can be seen as a variation of schedule sampling (Bengio et al., 2015) or a simplified version of UniLM (Dong et al., 2019). The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "On the other hand, the authors should give more analysis about why this method can work in LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The training set of pre-training models contains a lot of noise data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wonder if the proposed method is necessary.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Bengio et al., 2015. Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Dong et al., 2019. Unified language model pre-training for natural language understanding and generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "(W.1) In Table 1, along with the mean of the 3 random realizations, the spread should be provided as well, given that many of the performance numbers are within a single percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(W.2): The performance of FCM appears to be better than PaLM, but not significantly and consistently so.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For significance, I\u2019m using a criterion of at least 1% in absolute terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(W.2.d) Scaling trends: If we look at the consistency of performance improvement as the number of shots increase, there are very few tasks, on which FCM consistently stays better than PaLM by at least a percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "On the NLU tasks, FCM seems to lose its advantage as the number of shots increase, sometimes becoming much worse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(W.3) Classification finetuning: results on SuperGLUE dev set only show significant improvement on 4 out of 8 tasks, with majority of the gains observed on WSC and CB.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors should discuss the variability in performance gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(W.4) Scalability, 0-shot: In Table 4, FCM 1B results seem better than FCM 8B on CB, RTE, and WiC tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(W.5) Scalability, k-shot: In Table 5, the performance of both PaLM and FCM sometimes, and more often for FCM, degrades as the number of shots increases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(W.6) Ablation of mask ratio: Irrespective of the average across all the tasks, FCM[0,0.15] is better only on 2 out of 8 tasks in the 0-shot setting and 3 of 8 tasks in the 1-shot setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This shows that the choice of mask ratio is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "(W.7) Comparison with Dropout: Contrary to the claim of dropout hurting FCM performance, it improves in 3 of 8 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It improves performance by 10.2 absolute % points for the MultiRC task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It seems that the benefit of dropout is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This method can be seen as a variation of schedule sampling (Bengio et al., 2015) or a simplified version of UniLM (Dong et al., 2019). The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "On the other hand, the authors should give more analysis about why this method can work in LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The training set of pre-training models contains a lot of noise data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wonder if the proposed method is necessary.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Bengio et al., 2015. Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Dong et al., 2019. Unified language model pre-training for natural language understanding and generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "While the proposed method does not add any computational cost in theory, since previous tokens are randomly masked out, won't there be an engineering overhead since the previous hidden states cannot be cached which would have remained unchanged if the previous token weren't masked out otherwise?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This method can be seen as a variation of schedule sampling (Bengio et al., 2015) or a simplified version of UniLM (Dong et al., 2019). The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "On the other hand, the authors should give more analysis about why this method can work in LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The training set of pre-training models contains a lot of noise data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wonder if the proposed method is necessary.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Bengio et al., 2015. Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Dong et al., 2019. Unified language model pre-training for natural language understanding and generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "The biggest weakness of this paper is ignoring related research papers and lack of comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "[1] presents comprehensive study about different training objectives in zero-shot priming and finetuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "They compared 6 different training objectives in table 1 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11726.pdf): NXTUNI, NXTPRE, MSKUNI, MSKBI, HYBUNI, HYBPRE.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Casual language modeling such as GPT models belong to NXTUNI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "They controlled hyper-parameters and training data to be same and studied these six different objectives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In addition, this paper should carry out apple-to-apple comparisons with other models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For example, it needs to control model sizes (model parameters), batch size, hyper-parameters, training tokens, training devices, to be exactly the same.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "And control Total train compute (flops) to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Therefore, the qualified apple-to-apple comparison is only PaLM 1B v.s. FCM 1B (in table 3), PaLM 128M v.s. FCM 128M (in table 4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For others, PaLM 8B used different training data with FCM 8B, and T5 used different model parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The difference is that [2] puts the masked tokens to the end of sentence, so the masked places can not only attend to previous tokens but also gather information from future tokens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But due to lack of comparison, I cannot see the performance differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "(W.1) In Table 1, along with the mean of the 3 random realizations, the spread should be provided as well, given that many of the performance numbers are within a single percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(W.2): The performance of FCM appears to be better than PaLM, but not significantly and consistently so.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For significance, I\u2019m using a criterion of at least 1% in absolute terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(W.2.d) Scaling trends: If we look at the consistency of performance improvement as the number of shots increase, there are very few tasks, on which FCM consistently stays better than PaLM by at least a percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "On the NLU tasks, FCM seems to lose its advantage as the number of shots increase, sometimes becoming much worse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(W.3) Classification finetuning: results on SuperGLUE dev set only show significant improvement on 4 out of 8 tasks, with majority of the gains observed on WSC and CB.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors should discuss the variability in performance gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(W.4) Scalability, 0-shot: In Table 4, FCM 1B results seem better than FCM 8B on CB, RTE, and WiC tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(W.5) Scalability, k-shot: In Table 5, the performance of both PaLM and FCM sometimes, and more often for FCM, degrades as the number of shots increases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(W.6) Ablation of mask ratio: Irrespective of the average across all the tasks, FCM[0,0.15] is better only on 2 out of 8 tasks in the 0-shot setting and 3 of 8 tasks in the 1-shot setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This shows that the choice of mask ratio is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "(W.7) Comparison with Dropout: Contrary to the claim of dropout hurting FCM performance, it improves in 3 of 8 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It improves performance by 10.2 absolute % points for the MultiRC task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It seems that the benefit of dropout is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "While the proposed method does not add any computational cost in theory, since previous tokens are randomly masked out, won't there be an engineering overhead since the previous hidden states cannot be cached which would have remained unchanged if the previous token weren't masked out otherwise?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "(W.1) In Table 1, along with the mean of the 3 random realizations, the spread should be provided as well, given that many of the performance numbers are within a single percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(W.2): The performance of FCM appears to be better than PaLM, but not significantly and consistently so.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For significance, I\u2019m using a criterion of at least 1% in absolute terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(W.2.d) Scaling trends: If we look at the consistency of performance improvement as the number of shots increase, there are very few tasks, on which FCM consistently stays better than PaLM by at least a percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "On the NLU tasks, FCM seems to lose its advantage as the number of shots increase, sometimes becoming much worse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(W.3) Classification finetuning: results on SuperGLUE dev set only show significant improvement on 4 out of 8 tasks, with majority of the gains observed on WSC and CB.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors should discuss the variability in performance gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(W.4) Scalability, 0-shot: In Table 4, FCM 1B results seem better than FCM 8B on CB, RTE, and WiC tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(W.5) Scalability, k-shot: In Table 5, the performance of both PaLM and FCM sometimes, and more often for FCM, degrades as the number of shots increases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(W.6) Ablation of mask ratio: Irrespective of the average across all the tasks, FCM[0,0.15] is better only on 2 out of 8 tasks in the 0-shot setting and 3 of 8 tasks in the 1-shot setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This shows that the choice of mask ratio is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "(W.7) Comparison with Dropout: Contrary to the claim of dropout hurting FCM performance, it improves in 3 of 8 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It improves performance by 10.2 absolute % points for the MultiRC task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It seems that the benefit of dropout is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "The biggest weakness of this paper is ignoring related research papers and lack of comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "[1] presents comprehensive study about different training objectives in zero-shot priming and finetuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "They compared 6 different training objectives in table 1 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11726.pdf): NXTUNI, NXTPRE, MSKUNI, MSKBI, HYBUNI, HYBPRE.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Casual language modeling such as GPT models belong to NXTUNI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "They controlled hyper-parameters and training data to be same and studied these six different objectives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In addition, this paper should carry out apple-to-apple comparisons with other models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For example, it needs to control model sizes (model parameters), batch size, hyper-parameters, training tokens, training devices, to be exactly the same.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "And control Total train compute (flops) to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Therefore, the qualified apple-to-apple comparison is only PaLM 1B v.s. FCM 1B (in table 3), PaLM 128M v.s. FCM 128M (in table 4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For others, PaLM 8B used different training data with FCM 8B, and T5 used different model parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The difference is that [2] puts the masked tokens to the end of sentence, so the masked places can not only attend to previous tokens but also gather information from future tokens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But due to lack of comparison, I cannot see the performance differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "While the proposed method does not add any computational cost in theory, since previous tokens are randomly masked out, won't there be an engineering overhead since the previous hidden states cannot be cached which would have remained unchanged if the previous token weren't masked out otherwise?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "The biggest weakness of this paper is ignoring related research papers and lack of comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "[1] presents comprehensive study about different training objectives in zero-shot priming and finetuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "They compared 6 different training objectives in table 1 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11726.pdf): NXTUNI, NXTPRE, MSKUNI, MSKBI, HYBUNI, HYBPRE.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Casual language modeling such as GPT models belong to NXTUNI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "They controlled hyper-parameters and training data to be same and studied these six different objectives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In addition, this paper should carry out apple-to-apple comparisons with other models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For example, it needs to control model sizes (model parameters), batch size, hyper-parameters, training tokens, training devices, to be exactly the same.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "And control Total train compute (flops) to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Therefore, the qualified apple-to-apple comparison is only PaLM 1B v.s. FCM 1B (in table 3), PaLM 128M v.s. FCM 128M (in table 4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For others, PaLM 8B used different training data with FCM 8B, and T5 used different model parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The difference is that [2] puts the masked tokens to the end of sentence, so the masked places can not only attend to previous tokens but also gather information from future tokens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But due to lack of comparison, I cannot see the performance differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Thus, the contributions of this paper are marginally novel/ significant.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Another major limitation of this paper is that it completely ignores comparison with other parameter-efficient transfer learning methods like adapters [4].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Concretely, AdapterFusion [3] work proposes to learn a task-specific composition of adapters from previous tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "How does the prompt tuning-based method compare with adapter fusion when applied to the LFLL problem?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is there a consensus that prompt tuning-based methods are better than adapters for few-shot learning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is important because as we scale the number of tasks, the input sequence length scales linearly, and with that self-attention scales quadratically.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This issue limits the practical applicability of the prompt tuning-based method for lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "What does it mean to use validation sets for hyper-parameter selection?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Ideally, during lifelong learning, we do not know the sequence of tasks apriori.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is unfair to freeze a particular task order, then search for optimal hyper-parameters, and then report results on multiple runs with the same task order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "At least, report the results on unseen task orders?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Or search on a subset of disjoint tasks [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, with the current experiments, it is unclear how they compare with adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Thus, the contributions of this paper are marginally novel/ significant.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Another major limitation of this paper is that it completely ignores comparison with other parameter-efficient transfer learning methods like adapters [4].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Concretely, AdapterFusion [3] work proposes to learn a task-specific composition of adapters from previous tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "How does the prompt tuning-based method compare with adapter fusion when applied to the LFLL problem?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is there a consensus that prompt tuning-based methods are better than adapters for few-shot learning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is important because as we scale the number of tasks, the input sequence length scales linearly, and with that self-attention scales quadratically.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This issue limits the practical applicability of the prompt tuning-based method for lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "What does it mean to use validation sets for hyper-parameter selection?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Ideally, during lifelong learning, we do not know the sequence of tasks apriori.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is unfair to freeze a particular task order, then search for optimal hyper-parameters, and then report results on multiple runs with the same task order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "At least, report the results on unseen task orders?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Or search on a subset of disjoint tasks [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, with the current experiments, it is unclear how they compare with adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "There are a few weaknesses that I hope the paper can address to improve the scoring:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the paragraph below equation 4, the loss form is an addition of 3 values: 1) the task loss L^{task}; 2) the language modeling loss L^{lm}; 3) label consistency loss L^{KL}.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Both 1) and 2) are reasonable, but 3) is new in the paper and it is unclear why it is absolutely necessary. In particular, the paper should include an ablation study on a range of \\lambda_{KL}, not just a comparison between models with and without L^{KL}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some parts of the method are not sufficiently studied in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Thus, the contributions of this paper are marginally novel/ significant.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Another major limitation of this paper is that it completely ignores comparison with other parameter-efficient transfer learning methods like adapters [4].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Concretely, AdapterFusion [3] work proposes to learn a task-specific composition of adapters from previous tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "How does the prompt tuning-based method compare with adapter fusion when applied to the LFLL problem?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is there a consensus that prompt tuning-based methods are better than adapters for few-shot learning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is important because as we scale the number of tasks, the input sequence length scales linearly, and with that self-attention scales quadratically.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This issue limits the practical applicability of the prompt tuning-based method for lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "What does it mean to use validation sets for hyper-parameter selection?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Ideally, during lifelong learning, we do not know the sequence of tasks apriori.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is unfair to freeze a particular task order, then search for optimal hyper-parameters, and then report results on multiple runs with the same task order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "At least, report the results on unseen task orders?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Or search on a subset of disjoint tasks [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, with the current experiments, it is unclear how they compare with adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The main contribution of saying that LFPT5 \"can be seen as a vital step towards general language intelligence\" is surely an overclaim, right? If I ask 100 researchers, how many of them will see this paper as a vital step towards general language intelligence?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The key part that I did not understand is why prompt tuning isn't already a sufficient life-long learning method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My understanding of prompt tuning is that no model weights are modified, only the prompt is optimized, and optimized prompts are stored for each task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Then given a new task, a new prompt can be trained and stored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This works because the size of prompts is many orders of magnitude smaller than the size of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Maybe another way of asking this question is, how is the following different from regular prompt tuning: \"while adapting to a new task type, LFPT5 includes and tunes additional prompt embeddings for the new task\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Another part I do not understand is why pseudo samples need to be generated. Why can't you just use the prior examples? I am missing why this idea of retraining on previous domains each time is a good one.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The introduction could make it more clear why the second two limitations of LLL are indeed limitations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Why NER in particular, instead of other tasks that haven't been explored thoroughly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "PT is not defined at first use in the abstract", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "All the acronyms are pretty confusing", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "I didn't understand why their method specifically applies to the few-shot scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I also struggled to understand why the proposed method solves lifelong learning over regular prompt tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One sentence in the paper seems like an overclaim to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "There are a few weaknesses that I hope the paper can address to improve the scoring:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the paragraph below equation 4, the loss form is an addition of 3 values: 1) the task loss L^{task}; 2) the language modeling loss L^{lm}; 3) label consistency loss L^{KL}.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Both 1) and 2) are reasonable, but 3) is new in the paper and it is unclear why it is absolutely necessary. In particular, the paper should include an ablation study on a range of \\lambda_{KL}, not just a comparison between models with and without L^{KL}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some parts of the method are not sufficiently studied in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The main contribution of saying that LFPT5 \"can be seen as a vital step towards general language intelligence\" is surely an overclaim, right? If I ask 100 researchers, how many of them will see this paper as a vital step towards general language intelligence?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The key part that I did not understand is why prompt tuning isn't already a sufficient life-long learning method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My understanding of prompt tuning is that no model weights are modified, only the prompt is optimized, and optimized prompts are stored for each task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Then given a new task, a new prompt can be trained and stored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This works because the size of prompts is many orders of magnitude smaller than the size of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Maybe another way of asking this question is, how is the following different from regular prompt tuning: \"while adapting to a new task type, LFPT5 includes and tunes additional prompt embeddings for the new task\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Another part I do not understand is why pseudo samples need to be generated. Why can't you just use the prior examples? I am missing why this idea of retraining on previous domains each time is a good one.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The introduction could make it more clear why the second two limitations of LLL are indeed limitations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Why NER in particular, instead of other tasks that haven't been explored thoroughly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "PT is not defined at first use in the abstract", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "All the acronyms are pretty confusing", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "I didn't understand why their method specifically applies to the few-shot scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I also struggled to understand why the proposed method solves lifelong learning over regular prompt tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One sentence in the paper seems like an overclaim to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "There are a few weaknesses that I hope the paper can address to improve the scoring:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the paragraph below equation 4, the loss form is an addition of 3 values: 1) the task loss L^{task}; 2) the language modeling loss L^{lm}; 3) label consistency loss L^{KL}.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Both 1) and 2) are reasonable, but 3) is new in the paper and it is unclear why it is absolutely necessary. In particular, the paper should include an ablation study on a range of \\lambda_{KL}, not just a comparison between models with and without L^{KL}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some parts of the method are not sufficiently studied in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The main contribution of saying that LFPT5 \"can be seen as a vital step towards general language intelligence\" is surely an overclaim, right? If I ask 100 researchers, how many of them will see this paper as a vital step towards general language intelligence?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The key part that I did not understand is why prompt tuning isn't already a sufficient life-long learning method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My understanding of prompt tuning is that no model weights are modified, only the prompt is optimized, and optimized prompts are stored for each task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Then given a new task, a new prompt can be trained and stored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This works because the size of prompts is many orders of magnitude smaller than the size of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Maybe another way of asking this question is, how is the following different from regular prompt tuning: \"while adapting to a new task type, LFPT5 includes and tunes additional prompt embeddings for the new task\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Another part I do not understand is why pseudo samples need to be generated. Why can't you just use the prior examples? I am missing why this idea of retraining on previous domains each time is a good one.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The introduction could make it more clear why the second two limitations of LLL are indeed limitations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Why NER in particular, instead of other tasks that haven't been explored thoroughly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "PT is not defined at first use in the abstract", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "All the acronyms are pretty confusing", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "I didn't understand why their method specifically applies to the few-shot scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I also struggled to understand why the proposed method solves lifelong learning over regular prompt tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One sentence in the paper seems like an overclaim to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Table 1 shows that models with different percentages of redundant parameters have similar performance, implying that performance may not be proportional to \"well-trained\" parameters. It means that making more parameters \"well-trained\" does not necessarily improve the performance. This seems not totally in line with the paper's motivation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 5 is not straightforward to visualize the difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The curve is drawn only on the right subfigure.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Sufficiently training all parameters seems a double-edged sword.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "According to Figure 3, models trained with SAGE are susceptible to parameter pruning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In general, we want efficient and compact models in deployment.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The main concern is whether SAGE has adverse effects in getting efficient deployment models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "This is unfortunate and not rigorous.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The use of Taylor approximation is only good close to the operating point for nonlinear functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However Theta_j,-j may be very large, and thus the approximation may be very bad.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I do not agree that the memory and computational costs of SAGE are \"marginal\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The EMA \"I-hat\" is a full copy of the model since we need one EMA per parameter, especially for large models this is substantial overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "6) I do not agree that one can conclude from the results that SAGE is more effective for small datasets than for large ones, since the \"points\" betwen the datasets/tasks do not live on the same scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This should be more accurately reflected in the title, for example by replacing the word \"training\" by \"fine-tuning\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would further be interesting to see how well it works when training from scratch; even if it does not work, it is still a valuable fine-tuning method, and stating this may save a lot of people a lot of resources and time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Table 1 shows that models with different percentages of redundant parameters have similar performance, implying that performance may not be proportional to \"well-trained\" parameters. It means that making more parameters \"well-trained\" does not necessarily improve the performance. This seems not totally in line with the paper's motivation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 5 is not straightforward to visualize the difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The curve is drawn only on the right subfigure.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Sufficiently training all parameters seems a double-edged sword.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "According to Figure 3, models trained with SAGE are susceptible to parameter pruning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In general, we want efficient and compact models in deployment.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The main concern is whether SAGE has adverse effects in getting efficient deployment models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The hyper-parameters for SAGE are exhaustively tuned, more so than for the baseline adaptive optimizers, so there\u2019s a potential for bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Table 3 should show results for your implementation of Adamax that\u2019s comparable to Adamax-SAGE, in addition to the Devlin et al numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Table 1 shows that models with different percentages of redundant parameters have similar performance, implying that performance may not be proportional to \"well-trained\" parameters. It means that making more parameters \"well-trained\" does not necessarily improve the performance. This seems not totally in line with the paper's motivation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 5 is not straightforward to visualize the difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The curve is drawn only on the right subfigure.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Sufficiently training all parameters seems a double-edged sword.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "According to Figure 3, models trained with SAGE are susceptible to parameter pruning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In general, we want efficient and compact models in deployment.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The main concern is whether SAGE has adverse effects in getting efficient deployment models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the context of image classification it would be interesting to evaluate it with CNN and others architecture than ViT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optimisation: In image classification, the pre-training procedure used is quite sub-optimal since the paper of Dosovitsky et al.[1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "many improvements have been proposed such as the DeiT approach [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be interesting to see if the proposed method still works when the model is trained with more regularisation and data-augmentation which may also lead to a better use of weights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Image Classification: Having only results of models pre-trained on ImageNet-21k and fine-tuned with the proposed method on downstream tasks is not very usual in image classification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Nevertheless, more complete experiments in image classification would allow to better evaluate the interest of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "This is unfortunate and not rigorous.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The use of Taylor approximation is only good close to the operating point for nonlinear functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However Theta_j,-j may be very large, and thus the approximation may be very bad.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I do not agree that the memory and computational costs of SAGE are \"marginal\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The EMA \"I-hat\" is a full copy of the model since we need one EMA per parameter, especially for large models this is substantial overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "6) I do not agree that one can conclude from the results that SAGE is more effective for small datasets than for large ones, since the \"points\" betwen the datasets/tasks do not live on the same scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This should be more accurately reflected in the title, for example by replacing the word \"training\" by \"fine-tuning\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would further be interesting to see how well it works when training from scratch; even if it does not work, it is still a valuable fine-tuning method, and stating this may save a lot of people a lot of resources and time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The hyper-parameters for SAGE are exhaustively tuned, more so than for the baseline adaptive optimizers, so there\u2019s a potential for bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Table 3 should show results for your implementation of Adamax that\u2019s comparable to Adamax-SAGE, in addition to the Devlin et al numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "This is unfortunate and not rigorous.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The use of Taylor approximation is only good close to the operating point for nonlinear functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However Theta_j,-j may be very large, and thus the approximation may be very bad.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I do not agree that the memory and computational costs of SAGE are \"marginal\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The EMA \"I-hat\" is a full copy of the model since we need one EMA per parameter, especially for large models this is substantial overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "6) I do not agree that one can conclude from the results that SAGE is more effective for small datasets than for large ones, since the \"points\" betwen the datasets/tasks do not live on the same scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This should be more accurately reflected in the title, for example by replacing the word \"training\" by \"fine-tuning\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would further be interesting to see how well it works when training from scratch; even if it does not work, it is still a valuable fine-tuning method, and stating this may save a lot of people a lot of resources and time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the context of image classification it would be interesting to evaluate it with CNN and others architecture than ViT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optimisation: In image classification, the pre-training procedure used is quite sub-optimal since the paper of Dosovitsky et al.[1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "many improvements have been proposed such as the DeiT approach [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be interesting to see if the proposed method still works when the model is trained with more regularisation and data-augmentation which may also lead to a better use of weights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Image Classification: Having only results of models pre-trained on ImageNet-21k and fine-tuned with the proposed method on downstream tasks is not very usual in image classification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Nevertheless, more complete experiments in image classification would allow to better evaluate the interest of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The hyper-parameters for SAGE are exhaustively tuned, more so than for the baseline adaptive optimizers, so there\u2019s a potential for bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Table 3 should show results for your implementation of Adamax that\u2019s comparable to Adamax-SAGE, in addition to the Devlin et al numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the context of image classification it would be interesting to evaluate it with CNN and others architecture than ViT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optimisation: In image classification, the pre-training procedure used is quite sub-optimal since the paper of Dosovitsky et al.[1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "many improvements have been proposed such as the DeiT approach [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be interesting to see if the proposed method still works when the model is trained with more regularisation and data-augmentation which may also lead to a better use of weights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Image Classification: Having only results of models pre-trained on ImageNet-21k and fine-tuned with the proposed method on downstream tasks is not very usual in image classification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Nevertheless, more complete experiments in image classification would allow to better evaluate the interest of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "If summarization models are sensitive to paraphrasing and structural features, then synthetic documents may also exhibit artifacts that influence the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be important to perform a similar analysis on selected documents from CNN/DM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The position bias analysis, the authors state that \"clearly amplified by MatchSum, PreSumm, and Azure, as shown in Fig. 4b.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, it is not clear if the observed pattern is caused by model preference or simply by truncation of inputs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "If we observe the figure in Appendix E, the distribution for PEGASUS and BART are more similar to the reference summaries distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In contrast, MatchSum, Presumm, and Azure decrease the frequency sharply between 0.4 and 0.6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Interestingly, Presumm and MatchSum use the same backbone model with a maximum input length of 512 tokens, whereas PEGASUS and BART support inputs of 1024 tokens, which might explain their capacity to select more content from the later positions of the articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For reference, CNN/DM input documents have 766 words on average (\"Extractive Summarization as Text Matching\", Zhong et al., 2020).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Do the quantile calculation in the figures take into account this truncation effect?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "No details or citation is provided for the paraphrasing model. Just the link for its checkpoint at https://huggingface.co/tuner007/pegasus_paraphrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Authors mention that they \"conduct manual analysis on a randomly selected subset of the article\" but no further details are provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Experiments are based only on CNN/DM, which is well known for its lead bias for important content.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors only considered a few models, ignoring sota models on CNN/Dailymail dataset. It may have been possible that the SOTA model behaves differently.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paper lacks any association/description between the metrics used for bias in this paper v/s different text summarisation metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been nice to consider why and how these metrics are different to give a better understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Paper lacks any results about biases based on religion and race even though they claim to have done so in the initial section of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Only one incomplete figure about religion is present which is hard to comprehend as it lacks any legend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The results and figures need to be explained further.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Explaining the ideal score for an automatic summarizer in the figures would have given a better idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I did not really understand the dashed lines in the figure given as Baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "An explanation with an example will go a long way in making things much more clear and bringing out the results effectively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "First it claims no work has been done on bias-ness in text summarization then it points out a previous work on structure biasness (Jung et al, 2019).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "In the Fig.3, two of the extractive summarisers are not mentioned namely presumm and matchsum", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "If summarization models are sensitive to paraphrasing and structural features, then synthetic documents may also exhibit artifacts that influence the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be important to perform a similar analysis on selected documents from CNN/DM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The position bias analysis, the authors state that \"clearly amplified by MatchSum, PreSumm, and Azure, as shown in Fig. 4b.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, it is not clear if the observed pattern is caused by model preference or simply by truncation of inputs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "If we observe the figure in Appendix E, the distribution for PEGASUS and BART are more similar to the reference summaries distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In contrast, MatchSum, Presumm, and Azure decrease the frequency sharply between 0.4 and 0.6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Interestingly, Presumm and MatchSum use the same backbone model with a maximum input length of 512 tokens, whereas PEGASUS and BART support inputs of 1024 tokens, which might explain their capacity to select more content from the later positions of the articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For reference, CNN/DM input documents have 766 words on average (\"Extractive Summarization as Text Matching\", Zhong et al., 2020).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Do the quantile calculation in the figures take into account this truncation effect?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "No details or citation is provided for the paraphrasing model. Just the link for its checkpoint at https://huggingface.co/tuner007/pegasus_paraphrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Authors mention that they \"conduct manual analysis on a randomly selected subset of the article\" but no further details are provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Experiments are based only on CNN/DM, which is well known for its lead bias for important content.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But it does not show what the alternative would be and whether that alternative is indeed more preferable to an end user.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is hard to say that given two different texts to an end user (since these are different as they've been reordered), they would somehow still write the same summary for both articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "There is also no end user analysis showing that these biased summaries from a model that rely more on what comes earlier in the article are offering less value to the end users than what a counterfactual unbiased summary would offer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Experiments in Figure 2 come close to this point but it does not come clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "On Page 3, its said that the generation details for the synthetic corpus are in Section 5, on Page 4 its said that they are in Appendix B, but it seems they are in neither.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "If summarization models are sensitive to paraphrasing and structural features, then synthetic documents may also exhibit artifacts that influence the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be important to perform a similar analysis on selected documents from CNN/DM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The position bias analysis, the authors state that \"clearly amplified by MatchSum, PreSumm, and Azure, as shown in Fig. 4b.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, it is not clear if the observed pattern is caused by model preference or simply by truncation of inputs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "If we observe the figure in Appendix E, the distribution for PEGASUS and BART are more similar to the reference summaries distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In contrast, MatchSum, Presumm, and Azure decrease the frequency sharply between 0.4 and 0.6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Interestingly, Presumm and MatchSum use the same backbone model with a maximum input length of 512 tokens, whereas PEGASUS and BART support inputs of 1024 tokens, which might explain their capacity to select more content from the later positions of the articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For reference, CNN/DM input documents have 766 words on average (\"Extractive Summarization as Text Matching\", Zhong et al., 2020).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Do the quantile calculation in the figures take into account this truncation effect?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "No details or citation is provided for the paraphrasing model. Just the link for its checkpoint at https://huggingface.co/tuner007/pegasus_paraphrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Authors mention that they \"conduct manual analysis on a randomly selected subset of the article\" but no further details are provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Experiments are based only on CNN/DM, which is well known for its lead bias for important content.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lack of some human validation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Overall, the paper is straightfoward to follow, the novelty is great and the analysis is interesting and reproducible with reasonable", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "effort.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors only considered a few models, ignoring sota models on CNN/Dailymail dataset. It may have been possible that the SOTA model behaves differently.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paper lacks any association/description between the metrics used for bias in this paper v/s different text summarisation metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been nice to consider why and how these metrics are different to give a better understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Paper lacks any results about biases based on religion and race even though they claim to have done so in the initial section of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Only one incomplete figure about religion is present which is hard to comprehend as it lacks any legend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The results and figures need to be explained further.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Explaining the ideal score for an automatic summarizer in the figures would have given a better idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I did not really understand the dashed lines in the figure given as Baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "An explanation with an example will go a long way in making things much more clear and bringing out the results effectively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "First it claims no work has been done on bias-ness in text summarization then it points out a previous work on structure biasness (Jung et al, 2019).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "In the Fig.3, two of the extractive summarisers are not mentioned namely presumm and matchsum", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But it does not show what the alternative would be and whether that alternative is indeed more preferable to an end user.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is hard to say that given two different texts to an end user (since these are different as they've been reordered), they would somehow still write the same summary for both articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "There is also no end user analysis showing that these biased summaries from a model that rely more on what comes earlier in the article are offering less value to the end users than what a counterfactual unbiased summary would offer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Experiments in Figure 2 come close to this point but it does not come clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "On Page 3, its said that the generation details for the synthetic corpus are in Section 5, on Page 4 its said that they are in Appendix B, but it seems they are in neither.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors only considered a few models, ignoring sota models on CNN/Dailymail dataset. It may have been possible that the SOTA model behaves differently.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paper lacks any association/description between the metrics used for bias in this paper v/s different text summarisation metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been nice to consider why and how these metrics are different to give a better understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Paper lacks any results about biases based on religion and race even though they claim to have done so in the initial section of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Only one incomplete figure about religion is present which is hard to comprehend as it lacks any legend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The results and figures need to be explained further.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Explaining the ideal score for an automatic summarizer in the figures would have given a better idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I did not really understand the dashed lines in the figure given as Baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "An explanation with an example will go a long way in making things much more clear and bringing out the results effectively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "First it claims no work has been done on bias-ness in text summarization then it points out a previous work on structure biasness (Jung et al, 2019).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "In the Fig.3, two of the extractive summarisers are not mentioned namely presumm and matchsum", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lack of some human validation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Overall, the paper is straightfoward to follow, the novelty is great and the analysis is interesting and reproducible with reasonable", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "effort.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But it does not show what the alternative would be and whether that alternative is indeed more preferable to an end user.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is hard to say that given two different texts to an end user (since these are different as they've been reordered), they would somehow still write the same summary for both articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "There is also no end user analysis showing that these biased summaries from a model that rely more on what comes earlier in the article are offering less value to the end users than what a counterfactual unbiased summary would offer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Experiments in Figure 2 come close to this point but it does not come clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "On Page 3, its said that the generation details for the synthetic corpus are in Section 5, on Page 4 its said that they are in Appendix B, but it seems they are in neither.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lack of some human validation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Overall, the paper is straightfoward to follow, the novelty is great and the analysis is interesting and reproducible with reasonable", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "effort.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 39, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It's entirely possible that a slight variation of the prompt managed to fix the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The jump from addition to combination with subtraction is relatively small at an abstract level: just four combinations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This could become a strength if presented as a simple change that becomes challenging for the LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The setting of using a shorter explanation becomes out of the general hypothesis. This might become a strength if it were presented as a limitation and not as a solution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I found the paper somewhat hard to follow and the writing style rather confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "For instance, I did not understand what \u201cdefining algorithms as skills\u201d means exactly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "This is mentioned several times in the manuscript but it looks to just boil down to using the aforementioned prompting technique.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper should make a clear statement somewhere that the language model is not re-optimized and no weight updates actually happen, since the name \u201cin-context learning\u201d might be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The main drawback of the manuscript is that technical details of the experimentation are largely missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors do not mention explicitly how many examples they provide in the prompt for each task, whether it is only one or multiple.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Details of the baseline are not provided either, such as which prompts are used, making the comparison between different ideas harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Usage of space is not well-balanced, the authors dedicated a full subsection for explaining \u201ctwo-number addition\u201d (section 3.1) and only a few paragraphs for all the others.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In particular, the algorithm for parity is not even mentioned but its results appear in table 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The fact that this prompting fails on logical tasks (section 6) poses serious concerns on its applicability on harder problems other than simple mathematical operations and, therefore, its real-world utility.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Clarity can certainly be improved (see above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I would like to raise a concern about reproducibility of the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I wanted to reproduce some of the experiments but found out that OpenAI\u2019s Codex code-davinci-002 model is not publicly available yet, which is somewhat awkward.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The best I could do was to try reproducing OOD results with the text-davinci-002 model, which exhibited similar reasoning processes but I got results (for 14 digits) that are much lower, i.e. 30%, than what is shown in Figure 3, i.e. approximately 97-98%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I tried with 10 samples where each addend has 14 digits, while providing the model with a single example of summation with 3-digit numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Perhaps the authors can explain why this happened?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Is that only due to a model difference or too few examples in the prompt?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Please find here a txt file showing the prompt I used (the same as in section G.2) and the response I got from the text-davinci-002 model: https://file.io/vqUWwuH8uZpq", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Lastly, a final concern about real-world utility as the prompting does not perform well on harder problems (logical tasks).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}]}, {"paper_idx": 39, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It's entirely possible that a slight variation of the prompt managed to fix the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The jump from addition to combination with subtraction is relatively small at an abstract level: just four combinations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This could become a strength if presented as a simple change that becomes challenging for the LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The setting of using a shorter explanation becomes out of the general hypothesis. This might become a strength if it were presented as a limitation and not as a solution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "With this level of specificity required, it is no longer necessary to use prompting to solve the tasks (we already solved it with our brain when providing the prompt and can totally write a program for it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Some generalizations (other than length) from the demonstration to the actual task would make the method much more useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But so far it is unclear whether this technique can be scaled to more complicated problems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 39, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I found the paper somewhat hard to follow and the writing style rather confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "For instance, I did not understand what \u201cdefining algorithms as skills\u201d means exactly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "This is mentioned several times in the manuscript but it looks to just boil down to using the aforementioned prompting technique.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper should make a clear statement somewhere that the language model is not re-optimized and no weight updates actually happen, since the name \u201cin-context learning\u201d might be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The main drawback of the manuscript is that technical details of the experimentation are largely missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors do not mention explicitly how many examples they provide in the prompt for each task, whether it is only one or multiple.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Details of the baseline are not provided either, such as which prompts are used, making the comparison between different ideas harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Usage of space is not well-balanced, the authors dedicated a full subsection for explaining \u201ctwo-number addition\u201d (section 3.1) and only a few paragraphs for all the others.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In particular, the algorithm for parity is not even mentioned but its results appear in table 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The fact that this prompting fails on logical tasks (section 6) poses serious concerns on its applicability on harder problems other than simple mathematical operations and, therefore, its real-world utility.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Clarity can certainly be improved (see above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I would like to raise a concern about reproducibility of the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I wanted to reproduce some of the experiments but found out that OpenAI\u2019s Codex code-davinci-002 model is not publicly available yet, which is somewhat awkward.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The best I could do was to try reproducing OOD results with the text-davinci-002 model, which exhibited similar reasoning processes but I got results (for 14 digits) that are much lower, i.e. 30%, than what is shown in Figure 3, i.e. approximately 97-98%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I tried with 10 samples where each addend has 14 digits, while providing the model with a single example of summation with 3-digit numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Perhaps the authors can explain why this happened?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Is that only due to a model difference or too few examples in the prompt?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Please find here a txt file showing the prompt I used (the same as in section G.2) and the response I got from the text-davinci-002 model: https://file.io/vqUWwuH8uZpq", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Lastly, a final concern about real-world utility as the prompting does not perform well on harder problems (logical tasks).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "With this level of specificity required, it is no longer necessary to use prompting to solve the tasks (we already solved it with our brain when providing the prompt and can totally write a program for it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Some generalizations (other than length) from the demonstration to the actual task would make the method much more useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But so far it is unclear whether this technique can be scaled to more complicated problems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 40, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "No major weaknesses identified.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Typos:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In Figure 2, the performance of Adapter is 21.00, not 20.46", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The \"v\" subscript is missing in Eq.5", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 22, "text": "Cons:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Significance of results:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There are no standard deviations for any results in the paper, which makes it hard to assess significance of many results, esp.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "since some of the performance gaps are small.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For instance, in table 6, authors have a 0.4 BLEU discrepancy in their replication of full fine-tuning performance but draw conclusions on MAM being best based on a 0.2 BLEU gap.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The gap is bigger when comparing MAM to methods not introduced by the authors, but still.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The story makes sense but I am not 100% confident in the robustness of the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Using two decimal numbers for tasks (e.g: XSum) also gives a false impression of precision.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I understand fine-tuning on some of these tasks (MT / XSUM) can be resource-intensive but having standard deviations in even a subset of the experiments would be useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Interpretation of Figure 4 in Section 5.2 and effectiveness of MAM Adapters for encoder models", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "The authors highlight that while existing methods perform well on MNLI/SST, they are underwhelming on en-ro / XSum.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "They conclude that this means existing parameter-efficient transfer learning (PETL) approaches are not great for higher-resource / more challenging tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, I would point out that this data can also support a different conclusion: existing PETL approaches work well for encoder-only models but are not great for encoder-decoder models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Including the T5 datapoint is also not very relevant since in that case Superglue is treated as a single task (w/ one adapter).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Answering \u201cFor which tasks / architectures do PETL methods perform well\u201d is an interesting question in itself and I feel like this section expedites this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "It is likely that both the architecture and #datapoints in the task matter.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The author\u2019s choice at the end of section 4.2 is to focus on XSum / en-ro MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "It would be great to highlight that not only are those higher-resource tasks, but they are also generative ones, and thus all the conclusions of this paper might not apply widely to encoder models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Indeed, the results of MAM adapters in table 2 are quite mixed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Right now the paper is claiming more generality than deserved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Two possible fixes here: (1) Make claims less general and specific to enc-dec models, (2) Conduct more experiments on encoder-only models", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Writing:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "The writing is subpar right now.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Example: section 4.4: \u201cits counterpart at attention\u201d -> \u201cits attention counterpart\u201d, \u201cFFN can better utilize modification at larger capacities\u201d -> modifications or \u201cmodification of the FFN is better at larger capacities\u201d, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Minor: The citation style is often wrong for the sentence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Use \\citet more often.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "Minor: Replace \u201cFor classifications\u201d with \u201cfor classification tasks\u201d in the Appendix (twice)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Typos: Section 5 name Discussions -> Discussion ; Appendix A3 Learning -> Learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "Minor:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "For Figure 2, I feel like the full fine-tuning number should come from the original baseline, not the replication.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "The protocol in 4.5 for choosing the scaling parameter for Scaled PA is a bit surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "I would just suggest that it should be an hyperparameter, the current description makes it seem like it is based on hyperparam tuning for LoRA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "It seems to me that 4.5 is about composition function and \\delta h functional form since LoRA changes both.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "Revised score upwards after response, see below", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "The set of experiments is well-chosen overall, but the lack of standard deviations and the focus on generative tasks / enc-decoder models makes the results less robust and general.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "The writing would benefit from more work as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Table 1-3 shows that Charformer is generally on par with Byte-level T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Sometimes Charformer wins; other times Byte-level T5 wins.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Either with small differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "In all cases, Charformer is significantly worse than subword models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The rescaled version Charformer_S is able to achieve comparable and sometimes better accuracy than subword models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(Con) It is not clear if the proposed GBST module learns meaningful subword tokenization.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "examples that highly scored subwords align with human intuition, or quantitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "how the learned tokenized subwords align with established methods, or how they are better at solving lexical tasks, would help.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It would help if the authors can provide more and clearer technical details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "(Con) No code provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Providing code will greatly help reproducibility and help clarify many technical details not fully described in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 49, "text": "However, some important design and implementation details are missing or confusing which are not helped by the lack of source code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, it does not learn to segment the input to different chunks to be fed in the contextualizer but rather downsamples the sequence in a fixed way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Second, the soft selection at each position is performed over blocks of different size that correspond to that position and does not take into account global information e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "by computing scores of all possible segmentations to maximize for the most likely one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Studies that focus on tokenization like the ones cited below evaluate on tasks where tokenization is important for reaching state-of-the-art performance like machine translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Table 1-3 shows that Charformer is generally on par with Byte-level T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Sometimes Charformer wins; other times Byte-level T5 wins.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Either with small differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "In all cases, Charformer is significantly worse than subword models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The rescaled version Charformer_S is able to achieve comparable and sometimes better accuracy than subword models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(Con) It is not clear if the proposed GBST module learns meaningful subword tokenization.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "examples that highly scored subwords align with human intuition, or quantitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "how the learned tokenized subwords align with established methods, or how they are better at solving lexical tasks, would help.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It would help if the authors can provide more and clearer technical details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "(Con) No code provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Providing code will greatly help reproducibility and help clarify many technical details not fully described in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 49, "text": "However, some important design and implementation details are missing or confusing which are not helped by the lack of source code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "(minor) The technical novelty of this paper is only GBST, a dense layer over n-grams, while training setting follows ByT5, and the best SBase model is also an implementation of the deep encoder concept.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(minor) T5 seems much better than SBase under Zero-Shot settings, though it takes more computation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This is important since in the ByT5 paper all the model variants have much heavier weight in encoders, and there is also an ablation study to prove that heavier encoder can improve performance a lot.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Thus it\u2019s a bit strange that the best settings of both models are not used for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Table 1-3 shows that Charformer is generally on par with Byte-level T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Sometimes Charformer wins; other times Byte-level T5 wins.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Either with small differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "In all cases, Charformer is significantly worse than subword models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The rescaled version Charformer_S is able to achieve comparable and sometimes better accuracy than subword models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(Con) It is not clear if the proposed GBST module learns meaningful subword tokenization.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "examples that highly scored subwords align with human intuition, or quantitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "how the learned tokenized subwords align with established methods, or how they are better at solving lexical tasks, would help.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It would help if the authors can provide more and clearer technical details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "(Con) No code provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Providing code will greatly help reproducibility and help clarify many technical details not fully described in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 49, "text": "However, some important design and implementation details are missing or confusing which are not helped by the lack of source code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear if the method works for other languages.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The evaluations are based on base models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you try large models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Besides, it would be impactful if this paper showed that GBST module could actually generalize in other kinds of models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, it does not learn to segment the input to different chunks to be fed in the contextualizer but rather downsamples the sequence in a fixed way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Second, the soft selection at each position is performed over blocks of different size that correspond to that position and does not take into account global information e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "by computing scores of all possible segmentations to maximize for the most likely one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Studies that focus on tokenization like the ones cited below evaluate on tasks where tokenization is important for reaching state-of-the-art performance like machine translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "(minor) The technical novelty of this paper is only GBST, a dense layer over n-grams, while training setting follows ByT5, and the best SBase model is also an implementation of the deep encoder concept.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(minor) T5 seems much better than SBase under Zero-Shot settings, though it takes more computation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This is important since in the ByT5 paper all the model variants have much heavier weight in encoders, and there is also an ablation study to prove that heavier encoder can improve performance a lot.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Thus it\u2019s a bit strange that the best settings of both models are not used for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, it does not learn to segment the input to different chunks to be fed in the contextualizer but rather downsamples the sequence in a fixed way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Second, the soft selection at each position is performed over blocks of different size that correspond to that position and does not take into account global information e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "by computing scores of all possible segmentations to maximize for the most likely one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Studies that focus on tokenization like the ones cited below evaluate on tasks where tokenization is important for reaching state-of-the-art performance like machine translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear if the method works for other languages.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The evaluations are based on base models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you try large models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Besides, it would be impactful if this paper showed that GBST module could actually generalize in other kinds of models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "(minor) The technical novelty of this paper is only GBST, a dense layer over n-grams, while training setting follows ByT5, and the best SBase model is also an implementation of the deep encoder concept.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(minor) T5 seems much better than SBase under Zero-Shot settings, though it takes more computation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This is important since in the ByT5 paper all the model variants have much heavier weight in encoders, and there is also an ablation study to prove that heavier encoder can improve performance a lot.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Thus it\u2019s a bit strange that the best settings of both models are not used for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear if the method works for other languages.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The evaluations are based on base models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you try large models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Besides, it would be impactful if this paper showed that GBST module could actually generalize in other kinds of models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Some limitations of this work include the lack of experiments on more diverse architectures like encoder-decoders to make sure that the proposed technique not only works on autoregressive GPT-type models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Moreover, only one approach for entity integration is explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Interesting choice of the margin loss for entity prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "More motivation should be added into why this loss instead of the cross-entropy loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optionally, an experiment comparing the performance of the model when trained with the cross entropy -vs- the margin loss would be interesting to have.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "At inference time, you are forced to put entity labels on the input text as a preprocessing step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Are the experiments on QA really zero-shot?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The examples presented in Appendix are not dummy as they reflect true questions and answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses: The paper leaves open several questions that should really be explored and discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4. The metrics used for the LAMA knowledge probing should be explained in the paper once (at least expand the abbreviations so readers can understand).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "6. Related to the above point, it seems like KALM base has more parameters than GPT-2 base (and even GPT-2 Large) and yet this aspect is not accounted for/investigated in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In fact, the paper says \u201c(KALM) more efficiently packs practical knowledge into its parameters \u2026 12L KALM is very close \u2026 to 24L GPT-2 Large\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This seems to be very misleading information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Since Transformers are essentially a form of graph networks, I\u2019m not sure entity embeddings can be treated separately from the model parameters since one can view those as edges part of the graph.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "7. I appreciate the authors efforts to test on a variety of benchmarks and I understand that training these huge models is not easy, but to me, the essence of the paper (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors say that one can discount the entity embeddings, but can we really?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Aren\u2019t they part of the model\u2019s \u2018representation\u2019 even if the inference does not use all of them in a single forward pass?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Several neural net architectures exist (including large-scale LMs) that do not use all inference paths but still count them in the total params.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The BERT vs GPT-2 example provided in the rebuttal is only a difference of 26k tokens (still significant!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "but here we're talking about a few hundred million parameters!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "At the very least, I think the true sizes of the models must be acknowledged and one can add the point on entity embeddings vs 'brain' parameters as a caveat, but it seems scientifically inaccurate to me to claim otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "To be clear, I don't think the size issue detracts much from the main contribution of adding entity embeddings here (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "this work may still be of interest even if the size of model is larger), but the current version of the paper has several claims about size savings that seem incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Some limitations of this work include the lack of experiments on more diverse architectures like encoder-decoders to make sure that the proposed technique not only works on autoregressive GPT-type models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Moreover, only one approach for entity integration is explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Interesting choice of the margin loss for entity prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "More motivation should be added into why this loss instead of the cross-entropy loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optionally, an experiment comparing the performance of the model when trained with the cross entropy -vs- the margin loss would be interesting to have.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "At inference time, you are forced to put entity labels on the input text as a preprocessing step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Are the experiments on QA really zero-shot?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The examples presented in Appendix are not dummy as they reflect true questions and answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Some limitations of this work include the lack of experiments on more diverse architectures like encoder-decoders to make sure that the proposed technique not only works on autoregressive GPT-type models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Moreover, only one approach for entity integration is explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Interesting choice of the margin loss for entity prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "More motivation should be added into why this loss instead of the cross-entropy loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optionally, an experiment comparing the performance of the model when trained with the cross entropy -vs- the margin loss would be interesting to have.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "At inference time, you are forced to put entity labels on the input text as a preprocessing step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Are the experiments on QA really zero-shot?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The examples presented in Appendix are not dummy as they reflect true questions and answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I would like to see how much KALM can transfer to downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors already show zero-shot QA results, but it would be better to see fine-tuning results on knowledge-intensive tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Even though KALM outperforms GPT-2, it is still unclear for me the advantages of KALM compared to Entities as Experts (or EaE, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07202).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, EaE outperforms KALM on the LAMA probing tasks, and EaE also outperforms T5-11B when finetuned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors mentioned fuzzy frequency-based entity matching is better than precise entity linking (e.g. EaE) on page 3, but EaE outperforms KALM on LAMA probing tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Based on the experimental results, precise entity linking seems more effective to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It would be helpful if the authors can provide a more thorough comparison with EaE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although 4 out of the 11 KILT tasks are already included in the main paper, most of them are LAMA knowledge probing tasks or zero-shot QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is still unclear how much and how robustly KALM can transfer to other downstream tasks with fine-tuning (e.g. Wizard-of-Wikipedia, FEVER, QA with fine-tuning).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For instance, CorefBERT paper (which uses a similar idea but as you mentioned it use bidirecitonal attention) shows its transferability on QUOREF, six extractive QA benchmarks, DocRED, FEVER, five coreference resolution benchmarks and GLUE, which can convince me to choose CorefBERT over BERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses: The paper leaves open several questions that should really be explored and discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4. The metrics used for the LAMA knowledge probing should be explained in the paper once (at least expand the abbreviations so readers can understand).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "6. Related to the above point, it seems like KALM base has more parameters than GPT-2 base (and even GPT-2 Large) and yet this aspect is not accounted for/investigated in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In fact, the paper says \u201c(KALM) more efficiently packs practical knowledge into its parameters \u2026 12L KALM is very close \u2026 to 24L GPT-2 Large\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This seems to be very misleading information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Since Transformers are essentially a form of graph networks, I\u2019m not sure entity embeddings can be treated separately from the model parameters since one can view those as edges part of the graph.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "7. I appreciate the authors efforts to test on a variety of benchmarks and I understand that training these huge models is not easy, but to me, the essence of the paper (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors say that one can discount the entity embeddings, but can we really?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Aren\u2019t they part of the model\u2019s \u2018representation\u2019 even if the inference does not use all of them in a single forward pass?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Several neural net architectures exist (including large-scale LMs) that do not use all inference paths but still count them in the total params.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The BERT vs GPT-2 example provided in the rebuttal is only a difference of 26k tokens (still significant!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "but here we're talking about a few hundred million parameters!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "At the very least, I think the true sizes of the models must be acknowledged and one can add the point on entity embeddings vs 'brain' parameters as a caveat, but it seems scientifically inaccurate to me to claim otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "To be clear, I don't think the size issue detracts much from the main contribution of adding entity embeddings here (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "this work may still be of interest even if the size of model is larger), but the current version of the paper has several claims about size savings that seem incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses: The paper leaves open several questions that should really be explored and discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4. The metrics used for the LAMA knowledge probing should be explained in the paper once (at least expand the abbreviations so readers can understand).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "6. Related to the above point, it seems like KALM base has more parameters than GPT-2 base (and even GPT-2 Large) and yet this aspect is not accounted for/investigated in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In fact, the paper says \u201c(KALM) more efficiently packs practical knowledge into its parameters \u2026 12L KALM is very close \u2026 to 24L GPT-2 Large\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This seems to be very misleading information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Since Transformers are essentially a form of graph networks, I\u2019m not sure entity embeddings can be treated separately from the model parameters since one can view those as edges part of the graph.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "7. I appreciate the authors efforts to test on a variety of benchmarks and I understand that training these huge models is not easy, but to me, the essence of the paper (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors say that one can discount the entity embeddings, but can we really?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Aren\u2019t they part of the model\u2019s \u2018representation\u2019 even if the inference does not use all of them in a single forward pass?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Several neural net architectures exist (including large-scale LMs) that do not use all inference paths but still count them in the total params.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The BERT vs GPT-2 example provided in the rebuttal is only a difference of 26k tokens (still significant!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "but here we're talking about a few hundred million parameters!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "At the very least, I think the true sizes of the models must be acknowledged and one can add the point on entity embeddings vs 'brain' parameters as a caveat, but it seems scientifically inaccurate to me to claim otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "To be clear, I don't think the size issue detracts much from the main contribution of adding entity embeddings here (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "this work may still be of interest even if the size of model is larger), but the current version of the paper has several claims about size savings that seem incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I would like to see how much KALM can transfer to downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors already show zero-shot QA results, but it would be better to see fine-tuning results on knowledge-intensive tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Even though KALM outperforms GPT-2, it is still unclear for me the advantages of KALM compared to Entities as Experts (or EaE, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07202).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, EaE outperforms KALM on the LAMA probing tasks, and EaE also outperforms T5-11B when finetuned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors mentioned fuzzy frequency-based entity matching is better than precise entity linking (e.g. EaE) on page 3, but EaE outperforms KALM on LAMA probing tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Based on the experimental results, precise entity linking seems more effective to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It would be helpful if the authors can provide a more thorough comparison with EaE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although 4 out of the 11 KILT tasks are already included in the main paper, most of them are LAMA knowledge probing tasks or zero-shot QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is still unclear how much and how robustly KALM can transfer to other downstream tasks with fine-tuning (e.g. Wizard-of-Wikipedia, FEVER, QA with fine-tuning).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For instance, CorefBERT paper (which uses a similar idea but as you mentioned it use bidirecitonal attention) shows its transferability on QUOREF, six extractive QA benchmarks, DocRED, FEVER, five coreference resolution benchmarks and GLUE, which can convince me to choose CorefBERT over BERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I would like to see how much KALM can transfer to downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors already show zero-shot QA results, but it would be better to see fine-tuning results on knowledge-intensive tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Even though KALM outperforms GPT-2, it is still unclear for me the advantages of KALM compared to Entities as Experts (or EaE, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07202).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, EaE outperforms KALM on the LAMA probing tasks, and EaE also outperforms T5-11B when finetuned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors mentioned fuzzy frequency-based entity matching is better than precise entity linking (e.g. EaE) on page 3, but EaE outperforms KALM on LAMA probing tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Based on the experimental results, precise entity linking seems more effective to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It would be helpful if the authors can provide a more thorough comparison with EaE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although 4 out of the 11 KILT tasks are already included in the main paper, most of them are LAMA knowledge probing tasks or zero-shot QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is still unclear how much and how robustly KALM can transfer to other downstream tasks with fine-tuning (e.g. Wizard-of-Wikipedia, FEVER, QA with fine-tuning).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For instance, CorefBERT paper (which uses a similar idea but as you mentioned it use bidirecitonal attention) shows its transferability on QUOREF, six extractive QA benchmarks, DocRED, FEVER, five coreference resolution benchmarks and GLUE, which can convince me to choose CorefBERT over BERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 43, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "I believe this can be a useful technique but before considering such an approach as a general strategy, more theoretical insights should be provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The authors report on ablation studies that demonstrate some empirical benefits.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "However, until I see more theoretical analysis on how the method improves convergence or lead to better losses by smoothing out the output of the objective function, I remain skeptical of the usefulness of this as a general training method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 3, "text": "The idea of dynamic temperature scaling has been tried in other works and tasks (e.g., attended temperature scaling).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 4, "text": "The paper parameterizes this mechanism with DNNs for the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The experiments are not solid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "The baseline only includes Mos, which is not very strong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "To validate whether this approach works with other LM of high-order attention or self-attention, a better baseline model is required (e.g., transformer, GPT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I would like to see this technique can help either NLU or NLG tasks, instead of just pure modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The case analysis section needs more examples instead of just cherry-picking few.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 43, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "I believe this can be a useful technique but before considering such an approach as a general strategy, more theoretical insights should be provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The authors report on ablation studies that demonstrate some empirical benefits.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "However, until I see more theoretical analysis on how the method improves convergence or lead to better losses by smoothing out the output of the objective function, I remain skeptical of the usefulness of this as a general training method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I vote for an acceptance, if the authors can polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "- Last but not least, the paper can be improved a lot if the authors can thoroughly polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 43, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "The idea of dynamic temperature scaling has been tried in other works and tasks (e.g., attended temperature scaling).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 4, "text": "The paper parameterizes this mechanism with DNNs for the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The experiments are not solid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "The baseline only includes Mos, which is not very strong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "To validate whether this approach works with other LM of high-order attention or self-attention, a better baseline model is required (e.g., transformer, GPT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I would like to see this technique can help either NLU or NLG tasks, instead of just pure modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The case analysis section needs more examples instead of just cherry-picking few.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I vote for an acceptance, if the authors can polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "- Last but not least, the paper can be improved a lot if the authors can thoroughly polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 44, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "This is not novel at all with many researchers have thought about this but could not execute the idea because of a lack of resources, higher API costs, and non-availability of open-source LLMs like PaLM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, it is saying that humans can look at a problem, and think of a solution many times, and the one that humans conclude the most is the most likely output and use that to think and train themselves to memorize that solution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I don't agree with this point at all (of course I am happy to listen to why authors thought about this).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Research where such an important claim is being made (LLMs are able to self-improve) needs to be shown on multiple models (small to very large) and just showing the results on a 540B model does not prove the point that large LMs can self-improve by training on the samples that they produced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "When generating outputs with let's say a smaller model like T5-large, where the accuracy will not be that high, the model outputs cannot be used to train them as most of the time, the answers will be incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "So my guess is that this approach only works for PaLM or similar-sized models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The paper is original in its implementation but not in ideation (very obvious extension that was not conducted by a lot of researchers due to lack of resources).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "So the validity of the method cannot be tested/justified by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 44, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper presented an approach followed with empirical evidence, but did not provide why such an approach works in the first place (i.e., approach is not backed well enough).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Following are my concerns on the approach:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These experiments are not there in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Like I said, there were a lot of ablations in the paper where some of them proved some points presented in previous work (like Table-7) and others are new (Figure-3).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "On the new ones, the paper failed to provide good discussion on the outcomes/observations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, in Figure-3, both step-by-step and few-shot with step-by-step are converging for total samples at 80.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Why is that the case?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 44, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "This is not novel at all with many researchers have thought about this but could not execute the idea because of a lack of resources, higher API costs, and non-availability of open-source LLMs like PaLM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, it is saying that humans can look at a problem, and think of a solution many times, and the one that humans conclude the most is the most likely output and use that to think and train themselves to memorize that solution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I don't agree with this point at all (of course I am happy to listen to why authors thought about this).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Research where such an important claim is being made (LLMs are able to self-improve) needs to be shown on multiple models (small to very large) and just showing the results on a 540B model does not prove the point that large LMs can self-improve by training on the samples that they produced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "When generating outputs with let's say a smaller model like T5-large, where the accuracy will not be that high, the model outputs cannot be used to train them as most of the time, the answers will be incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "So my guess is that this approach only works for PaLM or similar-sized models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The paper is original in its implementation but not in ideation (very obvious extension that was not conducted by a lot of researchers due to lack of resources).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "So the validity of the method cannot be tested/justified by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper presented an approach followed with empirical evidence, but did not provide why such an approach works in the first place (i.e., approach is not backed well enough).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Following are my concerns on the approach:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These experiments are not there in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Like I said, there were a lot of ablations in the paper where some of them proved some points presented in previous work (like Table-7) and others are new (Figure-3).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "On the new ones, the paper failed to provide good discussion on the outcomes/observations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, in Figure-3, both step-by-step and few-shot with step-by-step are converging for total samples at 80.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Why is that the case?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 45, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The promise of a better sentence scorer is potentially very impactful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors chose to evaluate sentence re-ranking on multiple generated candidates for NMT and for ASR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have several concerns about these experiments that made them not particularly convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, the experiments were, in my view, are a bit thin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, the gain from SLM over BERT is not very large, and there is still a large gap between the oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Furthermore, while several baselines were provided, there have been many architectures recently proposed, and probably more comparisons are needed to fully justify the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As some examples, it could have been good to compare with T5, BART, XLNet, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I am also not sure about the setup for the ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Why were the ablations not performed on all the datasets?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I think this could have been addressed by putting a final row in Table 2, with SLM with one-third of the compute so that the compute is matched.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "It is fine if it does not win in all settings under the same compute, but in this case it would be good to be transparent about how much compute is needed to get the same performance, and whether there are performance gains in multiple different compute-matched settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Finally, I am not sure why NMT and ASR were chosen as the experimental settings in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It could have been stronger if the paper had chosen a language understanding benchmark like GLUE, which could be a better fit for pretrained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I have voted for rejection primarily because the experiments are not particularly convincing, but if enough of the questions/concerns that I had are addressed in the rebuttal I will be happy to change my score.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "The authors were up-front about the additional computational cost of their method, though additional experiments would be needed for the empirical gains in compute-matched settings to be convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "IWSLT14 might be a small dataset, but I don't think German, Spanish, Italian, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "should be described as \"low-resource\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 45, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The promise of a better sentence scorer is potentially very impactful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors chose to evaluate sentence re-ranking on multiple generated candidates for NMT and for ASR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have several concerns about these experiments that made them not particularly convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, the experiments were, in my view, are a bit thin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, the gain from SLM over BERT is not very large, and there is still a large gap between the oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Furthermore, while several baselines were provided, there have been many architectures recently proposed, and probably more comparisons are needed to fully justify the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As some examples, it could have been good to compare with T5, BART, XLNet, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I am also not sure about the setup for the ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Why were the ablations not performed on all the datasets?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I think this could have been addressed by putting a final row in Table 2, with SLM with one-third of the compute so that the compute is matched.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "It is fine if it does not win in all settings under the same compute, but in this case it would be good to be transparent about how much compute is needed to get the same performance, and whether there are performance gains in multiple different compute-matched settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Finally, I am not sure why NMT and ASR were chosen as the experimental settings in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It could have been stronger if the paper had chosen a language understanding benchmark like GLUE, which could be a better fit for pretrained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I have voted for rejection primarily because the experiments are not particularly convincing, but if enough of the questions/concerns that I had are addressed in the rebuttal I will be happy to change my score.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "The authors were up-front about the additional computational cost of their method, though additional experiments would be needed for the empirical gains in compute-matched settings to be convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are still many grammar errors; please work with a copyeditor or native English speaker on any camera-ready.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I have concerns re: the clarity and significance of this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Firstly, there are two uncited works that have proposed one-pass solutions to token-wise probabilities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, the first work's proposed T-TA has many similarities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I appreciate some of the analyses which complement past work, in particular A.3.1's discussion comparing sentence vs. fixed-length pre-training, and 5.3 showing SLM and MLM's similar scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Section 5.1 and 5.2 are unsatisfying though, as increasing the number of masked tokens would be expected to be poor given the mismatch with pretraining (in BERT, masking 15%).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 45, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "IWSLT14 might be a small dataset, but I don't think German, Spanish, Italian, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "should be described as \"low-resource\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are still many grammar errors; please work with a copyeditor or native English speaker on any camera-ready.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I have concerns re: the clarity and significance of this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Firstly, there are two uncited works that have proposed one-pass solutions to token-wise probabilities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, the first work's proposed T-TA has many similarities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I appreciate some of the analyses which complement past work, in particular A.3.1's discussion comparing sentence vs. fixed-length pre-training, and 5.3 showing SLM and MLM's similar scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Section 5.1 and 5.2 are unsatisfying though, as increasing the number of masked tokens would be expected to be poor given the mismatch with pretraining (in BERT, masking 15%).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The method generally assumes that the decision of drop can be done independently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, the positions of salient information would usually exhibit certain patterns such as appearing close to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, splitting by a sentence for passages is natural.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I wonder about the additional training cost of SpanDrop compared to the standard training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The expectation of length is the same for SpanDrop and Beta-SpanDrop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the expectation of length\u2019s square would be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, I guess their computational overhead will be also different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The sampling cost would be almost negligible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Several technical details are not discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What's the tech novelty of this work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the authors focus on the \"learning problems for long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output\", the uniqueness of this kind of problems is not clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In most (if not all) real-world sequence classification and prediction problems, not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output; otherwise, the problems will become much easier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Experiments need to be improved and enhanced, e.g.,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Although these approaches aim to approximate the original pairwise interaction with lower cost and are often interested in still capturing the interactions between every pair of input elements (e.g., the long sequence benchmark proposed by Tay et al., 2020), they can still be directly applied to long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The four NLP datasets used in experiments are not representative tasks for long sequences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The method generally assumes that the decision of drop can be done independently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, the positions of salient information would usually exhibit certain patterns such as appearing close to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, splitting by a sentence for passages is natural.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I wonder about the additional training cost of SpanDrop compared to the standard training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The expectation of length is the same for SpanDrop and Beta-SpanDrop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the expectation of length\u2019s square would be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, I guess their computational overhead will be also different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The sampling cost would be almost negligible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The method generally assumes that the decision of drop can be done independently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, the positions of salient information would usually exhibit certain patterns such as appearing close to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, splitting by a sentence for passages is natural.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I wonder about the additional training cost of SpanDrop compared to the standard training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The expectation of length is the same for SpanDrop and Beta-SpanDrop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the expectation of length\u2019s square would be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, I guess their computational overhead will be also different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The sampling cost would be almost negligible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It's not surprising that this data augmentation is effective, especially for the catfinding task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Sec1. I don't quite understand why when in data the contributing portion is small or sparse, then the problem is \"underspecified\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It's still specified by the small contributing portion I think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Sec1. \"the data does not sufficiently define the goal for statistical models\", I don't understand what global is here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I have two major concerns", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(1) limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "(2) The lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Several technical details are not discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What's the tech novelty of this work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the authors focus on the \"learning problems for long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output\", the uniqueness of this kind of problems is not clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In most (if not all) real-world sequence classification and prediction problems, not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output; otherwise, the problems will become much easier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Experiments need to be improved and enhanced, e.g.,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Although these approaches aim to approximate the original pairwise interaction with lower cost and are often interested in still capturing the interactions between every pair of input elements (e.g., the long sequence benchmark proposed by Tay et al., 2020), they can still be directly applied to long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The four NLP datasets used in experiments are not representative tasks for long sequences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Several technical details are not discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What's the tech novelty of this work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the authors focus on the \"learning problems for long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output\", the uniqueness of this kind of problems is not clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In most (if not all) real-world sequence classification and prediction problems, not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output; otherwise, the problems will become much easier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Experiments need to be improved and enhanced, e.g.,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Although these approaches aim to approximate the original pairwise interaction with lower cost and are often interested in still capturing the interactions between every pair of input elements (e.g., the long sequence benchmark proposed by Tay et al., 2020), they can still be directly applied to long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The four NLP datasets used in experiments are not representative tasks for long sequences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It's not surprising that this data augmentation is effective, especially for the catfinding task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Sec1. I don't quite understand why when in data the contributing portion is small or sparse, then the problem is \"underspecified\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It's still specified by the small contributing portion I think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Sec1. \"the data does not sufficiently define the goal for statistical models\", I don't understand what global is here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I have two major concerns", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(1) limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "(2) The lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It's not surprising that this data augmentation is effective, especially for the catfinding task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Sec1. I don't quite understand why when in data the contributing portion is small or sparse, then the problem is \"underspecified\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It's still specified by the small contributing portion I think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Sec1. \"the data does not sufficiently define the goal for statistical models\", I don't understand what global is here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I have two major concerns", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(1) limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "(2) The lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 23, "text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 61, "text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 61, "text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 61, "text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The main weakness in this work is one that relates to the overall goal of fact extraction from language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The \u201cOracle'' results from Table 1 are thought provoking: with perfect knowledge regarding the predicate/relation of test examples, and a subsequent 100% consistent response, the LLM is only able to obtain ~50% correct responses from T-Rex, which is an admittedly limited evaluation (41 \u201chead\u201d predicates, mostly of well known entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While I understand that this work is clearly focused on the consistency issue, not necessarily correctness, it puts into question whether fact extraction from LMs is a worthwhile pursuit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would have liked for the paper to dig a little deeper into this headroom question from the previous point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Would it be possible to conduct a sampled qualitative evaluation of errors of the Oracle model in the ID cases?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are the errors due to unseen triples during training time (e.g., not in Wikipedia), or maybe there are issues with model capacity (maybe a 10x version of the LM would be able to recall the prompted fact)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In terms of writing, the most confusing section in the paper is Section 4.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "After re-reading it twice, I was still not able to ascertain: (1) what data was used to train the models, and (2) what data was used to evaluate the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The section makes reference to LAMA\u2019s T-REX, LPAQA, ParaRel, as well as augmentations using BERT lexical replacements, as well as data from \u201cShin et al, 2020\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The section also talks about examples from these sources as well as templates (presumably filled in with WikiData triples?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I really think this section needs to be rewritten and the training, eval and test datasets should be much more precisely described.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would also encourage authors to release the exact datasets and splits to allow others to reproduce/improve on this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But even with a data release, a precise description of how this data was constructed is very important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The depiction in Figure 2 hints that the entire sequence is rewritten using the fixed-length learned embeddings, and perhaps the subject or MASK embeddings are preserved?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Or do they rewrite the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do either the MASK or subject tokens get copied?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The LAMA benchmarks have one unfortunate characteristic: since it was constructed for BERT-style single token prediction, it has stripped down the original datasets (see the original version of T-Rex, which contains over 600 unique predicates vs. the 41 from LAMA: https://hadyelsahar.github.io/t-rex/ and https://aclanthology.org/L18-1544.pdf ).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I wonder if a more comprehensive version of this would be to evaluate on a larger sequence-to-sequence model like BART https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461 or T5 https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683 (both available as HuggingFace models).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Given that this work leverages frozen LLMs, it seems that training and evaluation could be done relatively cheaply even for larger models with proper decoders.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Major comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper does not draw a connection to prior work on robust optimization (for example, see references in [1]), which offers a more principled framework for formulating the objective of invariance to a class of input perturbations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "At the very least, it would be good to give a formal definition of robustness/consistency and cite this line of prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As this paper notes, the factual probing benchmark has class imbalances and represents a very particular use case, so while P-Adapters appear to improve consistency here it\u2019s unclear if these results will generalize to other settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to evaluate P-Adapters on a wider variety of tasks, following prior work on prompt optimization [2, 3]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, [4] provide 100 prompt templates for a wider variety of NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The paper would be more compelling if you could show that P-Adapters improve consistency in other settings too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Much of the motivation of this paper is based on assertions about user preferences, but there is no human evaluation to validate these claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, would a typical user prefer to give a natural language input but get an inconsistent response?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or would they rather pick a pre-defined relation type with better promise of consistency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In particular, it\u2019s not clear why user preferences would place any restrictions on training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "MoE model: How accurate is the relation classifier?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There is a big performance drop in the \u201cOOD Prompts\u201d setting, which leads me to wonder if the relation classifier was adequately trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Either way, relation classification results should be included and discussed in the main paper--a perfect relation classifier would lead to 100% consistency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "All of the models suffer a performance drop on the \u201cOOD Objects\u201d setting, which indicates that the models have over-fit to the (imbalanced) distribution of entities in Wikidata.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It\u2019s unclear how to interpret consistency in this setting, because the models are consistently producing the incorrect response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It might be more informative to report something like the \u201cConsistent-Acc.\u201d measurement from [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Table 2 does not compare results to any prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, can these results be compared to the \u201cConsistency Improved PLM\u201d results from [5]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Minor comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "The metric called \u201cprecision@1\u201d should be called \u201caccuracy@1\u201d", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Table 2 does not explain how the results are aggregated (prior work takes either the micro- or macro-average over relation types).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Three MLPs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The paper is missing some implementation details, such as the size of the BiLSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "The results section (section 5) might be easier to read if it were divided into subsections or paragraphs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "It would be interesting to see the accuracy breakdown by relation type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The main weakness in this work is one that relates to the overall goal of fact extraction from language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The \u201cOracle'' results from Table 1 are thought provoking: with perfect knowledge regarding the predicate/relation of test examples, and a subsequent 100% consistent response, the LLM is only able to obtain ~50% correct responses from T-Rex, which is an admittedly limited evaluation (41 \u201chead\u201d predicates, mostly of well known entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While I understand that this work is clearly focused on the consistency issue, not necessarily correctness, it puts into question whether fact extraction from LMs is a worthwhile pursuit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would have liked for the paper to dig a little deeper into this headroom question from the previous point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Would it be possible to conduct a sampled qualitative evaluation of errors of the Oracle model in the ID cases?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are the errors due to unseen triples during training time (e.g., not in Wikipedia), or maybe there are issues with model capacity (maybe a 10x version of the LM would be able to recall the prompted fact)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In terms of writing, the most confusing section in the paper is Section 4.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "After re-reading it twice, I was still not able to ascertain: (1) what data was used to train the models, and (2) what data was used to evaluate the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The section makes reference to LAMA\u2019s T-REX, LPAQA, ParaRel, as well as augmentations using BERT lexical replacements, as well as data from \u201cShin et al, 2020\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The section also talks about examples from these sources as well as templates (presumably filled in with WikiData triples?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I really think this section needs to be rewritten and the training, eval and test datasets should be much more precisely described.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would also encourage authors to release the exact datasets and splits to allow others to reproduce/improve on this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But even with a data release, a precise description of how this data was constructed is very important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The depiction in Figure 2 hints that the entire sequence is rewritten using the fixed-length learned embeddings, and perhaps the subject or MASK embeddings are preserved?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Or do they rewrite the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do either the MASK or subject tokens get copied?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The LAMA benchmarks have one unfortunate characteristic: since it was constructed for BERT-style single token prediction, it has stripped down the original datasets (see the original version of T-Rex, which contains over 600 unique predicates vs. the 41 from LAMA: https://hadyelsahar.github.io/t-rex/ and https://aclanthology.org/L18-1544.pdf ).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I wonder if a more comprehensive version of this would be to evaluate on a larger sequence-to-sequence model like BART https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461 or T5 https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683 (both available as HuggingFace models).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Given that this work leverages frozen LLMs, it seems that training and evaluation could be done relatively cheaply even for larger models with proper decoders.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although the proposed method involves experiments in four different settings including OOD keyword error, it might be valuable to investigate an OOD syntax error setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That is, in a real scenario, users write the natural language prompt which may have a grammatical error.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Assessing the robustness of the proposed method in terms of grammatical errors can enhance the quality of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In Table 2, the performances of P-tuning in RoBERTa-large are better than the ones of Oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Without any explanation, it is not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instead of listing the result in the tables, it would be clearer if the authors provide more explanations about them although they are in the appendix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}]
/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/ai_segments.json:1:[{"seg_id": 0, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "For the AMA prompts on Appendix H, it seems that some tasks (DROP, SST, BoolQ, COPA) only do either prompt() or answer() stage, not both. How can we decide \"automatically\" when to do only prompt() or answer() or both depending on the target task?"}, {"seg_id": 1, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "However, this paper contains some unclearness, especially on weak supervision and how the predictions are mapped to the output space. See Weakness part for details."}, {"seg_id": 2, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "I think still I am a little not sure, also about whether re-formatting is necessary for aggregation to work well."}, {"seg_id": 3, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs)."}, {"seg_id": 4, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Novelty: The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs)."}, {"seg_id": 5, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "However, the lack of technical novelty & contribution makes this work slightly below the acceptance bar of the conference."}, {"seg_id": 6, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?"}, {"seg_id": 7, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs."}, {"seg_id": 8, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "One key question not answered in this context is how much of HTML structure analysis and understanding is truly required for these questions."}, {"seg_id": 9, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "I examined the supplementary material provided by the authors and only found the data files."}, {"seg_id": 10, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Either code or more detailed experimental setup should be provided in the supplementary material"}, {"seg_id": 11, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "It is better to make the codes available for reproducing these results."}, {"seg_id": 12, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Models that are trained on task specific data to compare against is questionable."}, {"seg_id": 13, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "I expect to see more task-specific models such as StructuralLM [1], MarkupLM [2] that respect the tree structure of HTML (or some other methods mentioned in the paper) to be evaluated against."}, {"seg_id": 14, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Basically, I need to see the following question answered: \"For specific web tasks, do I want to use a HTML specific model trained on the small dataset or just to fine-tune LLMs\"?"}, {"seg_id": 15, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "On MiniWoB, it's not very surprising that using a larger model with pre-training, you can beat smaller models from scratch."}, {"seg_id": 16, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should have tried to an ablation type of approach in equation 1, to check if concatenation alone, element wise dot product alone or absolute difference alone or a combination of any two would work better with the scoring function."}, {"seg_id": 17, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "As mentioned in the paper, NLI models do not transfer well to out-of-domain NLI samples, not to mention non-NLI tasks."}, {"seg_id": 18, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "However, I do have some concerns about their claims that this helps in transfer learning to other NLI tasks."}, {"seg_id": 19, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The ThinkSum framework, however, is too general such that almost all existing methods fall into this framework, if you think scoring is the Think step, and argmax is the sum step."}, {"seg_id": 20, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 1 is confusing."}, {"seg_id": 21, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Many new terminologies (in different colors) are introduced: some are completely unnecessary (e.g. product aggregation) and some do not have any clear explanations."}, {"seg_id": 22, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "There is a lack of technical details. In particular, it is not exactly clear how the sum step is performed. If this follows a probabilistic framework, please write down the steps formally."}, {"seg_id": 23, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Without clear writing, it is hard to evaluate novelty."}, {"seg_id": 24, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The writing is not clear, making it hard to evaluate the contribution of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 25, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "In general, probabilistic inference may tend to become less reliable in this case so does that have a major influence on the SUM process."}, {"seg_id": 26, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Despite the extensive demonstrations performed in this study, the authors need to present the ThinkSum paradigm in a more organized and clear way: even the core idea of ThinkSum is not clearly explained the examples throughout the paper do not help much explaining ThinkSum paradigm."}, {"seg_id": 27, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Weaknesses One general issue may be how does the model scale up as the sets produced by the prompts to LLMs become large."}, {"seg_id": 28, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "This is doable for 5 choices, but not so much for 100 choices or more (in SQuAD-type QA, the output could be any combinations of tokens) which is extremely expensive."}, {"seg_id": 29, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty may not be as high since it mainly is an integration of methods but the impact of the approach seems significant."}, {"seg_id": 30, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed method -- using contextual embeddings instead of static token embeddings in decoder for autoregressive retrieval -- is novel, intuitive, and inspiring."}, {"seg_id": 31, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed model follows the framework of generative retrieval models but applies contextualized token embeddings."}, {"seg_id": 32, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The main weakness comes from the experiment setup."}, {"seg_id": 33, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The setup is questionable."}, {"seg_id": 34, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "GENRE uses BART-large whereas this paper uses T5, so the focus on comparing directly with GENRE seems a bit misguided."}, {"seg_id": 35, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The main KILT results only have the original GENRE (using a different pre-trained model, BART) and CGR (even though the authors listed DPR and SEAL, the numbers are not in the same comparable settings)."}, {"seg_id": 36, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 1 is a bit confusing to me (especially Cape(1), Cape(2))."}, {"seg_id": 37, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "I can guess what it does, but it's never fully clear."}, {"seg_id": 38, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty contribution is somehow limited."}, {"seg_id": 39, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The paper proposes a reasonable way to use external knowledge in generative retrievers, but the quality of innovation, experiments, and writing seems to fall below the standard of ICLR."}, {"seg_id": 40, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "Based on my inference, the approach is reasonable but not terribly surprising."}, {"seg_id": 41, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "Limited technological contribution."}, {"seg_id": 42, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed method makes sense but the contribution of adding a contextualized embedding space might be considered as light."}, {"seg_id": 43, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how the model guarantees a correct generation of key descriptors of a document, if the generative retrieval model and the contextual language model were not trained with it."}, {"seg_id": 44, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The tasks may be too simple / short / toy."}, {"seg_id": 45, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It is quite hard to draw many conclusions about the best performing systems because their training strategies are not public, and it is not clear what types of task ambiguity have been seen during training."}, {"seg_id": 46, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, the paper relies heavily on opaque models, and it is a little hard to draw meaningful conclusions about the best performing systems."}, {"seg_id": 47, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, the empirical observation and proposed fine-tuning method are pretty straightforward and not surprising."}, {"seg_id": 48, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The observation that the best language model achieve similar performance to human on such ambiguous task is not surprising, even though the setting is synthetic and simple."}, {"seg_id": 49, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "As the authors mention, AmbiBench is a minimal complexity testbed."}, {"seg_id": 50, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, for the same reason, I am less convinced about the generality of findings reported here."}, {"seg_id": 51, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "This paper only studies the ambiguity of a few toy tasks, which only require simple lexical understanding and the texts are synthetic and very short."}, {"seg_id": 52, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how these findings would extend to more complex task types."}, {"seg_id": 53, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It would be more interesting to see what the ambiguity is in real-world NLP tasks, and how they can be handled."}, {"seg_id": 54, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The reproducibility is unclear, as the authors do not mention whether the benchmark will be released."}, {"seg_id": 55, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The six synthetic tasks are quite simplistic and limited in scope."}, {"seg_id": 56, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "What are some other types of task ambiguities in NLP tasks, maybe more subtle than simply occluding a category name?"}, {"seg_id": 57, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, the current study focuses on a very narrow type of ambiguity, and it's not clear how much these insights will extend to other types of task ambiguity."}, {"seg_id": 58, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "What about in tasks other than sentence classification?"}, {"seg_id": 59, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "Although it is an important challenge in fine-tuning language models, this paper only studies a few toy tasks and the proposed method is not novel."}, {"seg_id": 60, "paper_idx": 8, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_8.txt", "segment_text": "This part can be more convincing if the authors can provide more experiments and analyses."}, {"seg_id": 61, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works."}, {"seg_id": 62, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty."}, {"seg_id": 63, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Although they do make sense and are reasonable choices for a multimodal framework, I see little novelty here."}, {"seg_id": 64, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Originality: Apart from some novelties of the pretraining objectives, the method reuses some of the existing architectures in a hierarchical way."}, {"seg_id": 65, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "UniDoc does not improve the performance of document classification tasks over previous methods."}, {"seg_id": 66, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Although the authors argue that it attributes to the difference of OCR engines, they should explain the magnitude of this difference."}, {"seg_id": 67, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "They say the difference between EasyOCR and Tesseract, but should report performance metric and qualitative analysis on the RVL-CDIP dataset to compare real Microsoft OCR and EasyOCR."}, {"seg_id": 68, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "I feel that the methods/model architectures are largely putting together existing pieces of work from vision and language fields."}, {"seg_id": 69, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "In Sec 3.1, the proposed feature extraction, quantization, and gated cross-attention modules are all rather straightforward variations of existing work (i.e., the essential mechanisms are not new)."}, {"seg_id": 70, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "The methods are not novel/insightful as they are largely borrowing from existing work."}, {"seg_id": 71, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "One thing that appears weird to me is the frozen sentence encoders initialized with the weights trained on NLI and STS datasets--if they have been fine-tuned on those tasks, they may capture too corpus-specific features to generalize to other domains (like the new pre-training documents)."}, {"seg_id": 72, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "There are concerns about the generalization ability of the model since it uses dataset-specific text encoders."}, {"seg_id": 73, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "Another relevant approach to model selection is to use some additional, held-out, tasks to find model hyper-parameters and reuse them on all other tasks without any additional tuning."}, {"seg_id": 74, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "The authors include comparisons with tuning on a held-out validation set for most, but not all experiments. I think it\u2019d be helpful to include those comparisons for all experiments and lean more on these comparisons over comparisons with random baselines. In particular, could you provide the results for Figure 3?"}, {"seg_id": 75, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "A related question is are the few-shot datasets balanced? This might not affect LAMA (also another reason why LAMA is so different than other few-shot tasks) but it will other classification tasks like SuperGLUE experiments. In meta-learning literature shots usually mean the number of examples per class. So leave-one-out will leave out one example of one class and make the few-shot support data imbalanced, which can, in turn, prefer hyper-parameters that favor the minority class. The more appropriate thing to do would be leave-m-out where m is the number of classes. It will actually be useful to have an ablation over k for leave-k-out and in particular, I feel this might have more of a bearing on the experiments in section 3.5. In case the few-shot data is not balanced, which is not standard, then that introduces a lot of additional variability from unbalanced datasets (or choice of the few-shot examples/classes) that also confound the results."}, {"seg_id": 76, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "I think this setting is termed as multi-distribution few-shot learning in section 2 and is also a reasonable strategy to select hyper-parameters."}, {"seg_id": 77, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "While this may not always be possible for prompt-based methods, it is suitable for other methods like those in section 4."}, {"seg_id": 78, "paper_idx": 10, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_10.txt", "segment_text": "However, the contribution of this work is limited by its scope. The results for true few-shot learning are interesting but are not widely applicable. Because most previous work, including those being criticized, focus on the multi-distribution or tuned few-shot learning settings and make assumptions like a validation set is available or optimal hyperparameters for other tasks could be re-used for the new few-shot tasks. Indeed, future works should be explicit about their model selection method to make fair comparison. But this has been a well-known issue for few-shot learning under additional assumptions. For example, researchers in computer vision have been working on datasets with standard splits for fair comparison [1]. The true few-shot learning setup might not be entirely impactful."}, {"seg_id": 79, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "C. The novelty of the proposed method: It is true that using USE or BERT-Score in evaluating the quality of the generated adversarial samples is costly and cannot obtain promising results."}, {"seg_id": 80, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "I am worried about the novelty."}, {"seg_id": 81, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty may not be enough."}, {"seg_id": 82, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Yet the proposed method is somewhat trivial since it only uses an ensemble of classifiers and uses the cosine similarity to calculate the final similarity score calculation."}, {"seg_id": 83, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "The motivation is not clear. The paper found that previous encoders fail to deal well with antonyms, but it seems that the proposed method did nothing special about antonyms either."}, {"seg_id": 84, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Several technical decisions are not justified."}, {"seg_id": 85, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Some designs need more justifications. E.g., what is the criterion when choosing the 7 datasets and why the threshold is just set as 2.5."}, {"seg_id": 86, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "SPE can be considered as a sentence similarity metric."}, {"seg_id": 87, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "However, this paper neither verifies its performance on standard sentence similarity benchmarks nor justifies that this metric is only suitable for adversarial attack situations."}, {"seg_id": 88, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "There are several issues with the experiments"}, {"seg_id": 89, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: the paper has a good motivation that catches a small yet vital problem in adversarial attacks in texts and proposes a very simple method as a solution, yet the evaluation is somewhat unfair which may hurt the quality evolution of the method."}, {"seg_id": 90, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "However, the presentation/clarity of the paper can be significantly improved, as suggested in the \u201cweakness\u201d section."}, {"seg_id": 91, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, figure 5 would benefit from square-axis."}, {"seg_id": 92, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "The main text should be self-contained and the reader should not have to rely on the appendix to understand the details."}, {"seg_id": 93, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "\u2018We select 5 tasks where teacher can most significantly improve\u2026\u2019 - I don\u2019t understand the motivation behind these choices."}, {"seg_id": 94, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear why the paper chooses to do so instead of reporting all datasets, especially because the paper did not report these two sets of 5 datasets separately and discuss how their results differ."}, {"seg_id": 95, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "The use of BERT-base scale models naturally leaves open the question of whether anything changes at larger scale."}, {"seg_id": 96, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "While the fine-tuning paradigm becomes effective at these scales, recent research suggests that zero-shot capabilities tend to emerge at larger model sizes."}, {"seg_id": 97, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Regardless of the RoBERTa-base experiments, it remains unclear how the results transfer to auto-regressive models, and how relevant they are to the web-scale pre-training of very large language models."}, {"seg_id": 98, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "While experiments would be unrealistic here, I would appreciate a fairer and more in-depth discussion in the paper."}, {"seg_id": 99, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I think the analysis of model size is quite helpful, since a natural question is whether or not these results are due to the ELECTRA small model being too small to benefit from pre-training on massive upstream datasets."}, {"seg_id": 100, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Second, there is no evidence presented in this paper that using the model confidence to select the correct model should lead to good ensemble performance."}, {"seg_id": 101, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Is there any way to apply majority-vote style ensembling instead, to get around the issues with uncalibrated model confidence levels?"}, {"seg_id": 102, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "This is a general desired property of models, but not one that is guaranteed and in particular not one that is explored in this paper (at least not reported), which makes it it not very surprising that these experiments failed."}, {"seg_id": 103, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "The discussion on how to construct artificial long-term dependencies is very interesting, but it also is a little bit orthogonal to the remainder of the paper; at least with the current framing."}, {"seg_id": 104, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus."}, {"seg_id": 105, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "In the introduction the authors make the strong claim that \u201c[...] the benefits from pretraining in this case cannot be attributed to knowledge transfer\u201d, while in Section 2 the authors are more nuanced and allow for the possibility that \u201csufficient knowledge is [...] present in the downstream dataset\u201d."}, {"seg_id": 106, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I am aware that size doesn't explain it all, but I am also far from convinced that the corpora being downstream corpora makes an important difference here."}, {"seg_id": 107, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "It seems possible that the models can acquire linguistic and factual knowledge from these datasets during pre-training, which cannot be learnt efficiently from only predicting the downstream task labels."}, {"seg_id": 108, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "That is, a baseline of pretraining on a general pretrain corpus of similar size to the downstream corpora is missing and is very important to shed light on the results here."}, {"seg_id": 109, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "(2) Related to (1), these datasets are all text classification datasets that are pretty similar, so it's somewhat unsurprising to me that pre-training on downstream corpora works well when fine-tuning on other datasets."}, {"seg_id": 110, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I do think the results are somewhat surprising and novel, although there's a bit of overclaiming (since I suspect that these results are largely due to the similarity of the downstream datasets)."}, {"seg_id": 111, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "However, I do think that this conclusion could be better-qualified (e.g., this is not a general conclusion, but one about text classification datasets)."}, {"seg_id": 112, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "(1) It's not clear that the results answer the question of \"Our results suggest that in many scenarios, performance gains attributable to pretraining are driven primarily by the pretraining objective itself and are not always attributable to the incorporation of massive datasets\""}, {"seg_id": 113, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Is the conclusion from these results basically that you need far less pre-training in order to get good finetuning performance?"}, {"seg_id": 114, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Furthermore, it's not clear to me that this paper directly answers the question of whether pre-training data scale or pre-training objective is the key to better transfer performance."}, {"seg_id": 115, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Although the usage of documentation for code generation is novel, it is somewhat simple from a technical point of view."}, {"seg_id": 116, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Beyond this, the technique itself appears to be essentially the same as prior work: they use standard retrieval techniques based on existing algorithms and pre-trained models, and their generation models are also models that have been used for code or text generation before."}, {"seg_id": 117, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "However, in terms of technical novelty, already existing models and techniques are used for retrieval and generation."}, {"seg_id": 118, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: Well-designed and experiments and well-written work."}, {"seg_id": 119, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of retrieving documentation for prompting seems interesting and novel to me."}, {"seg_id": 120, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "The study is mainly empirical."}, {"seg_id": 121, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should provide more details about the three-layer neural network as the projection P (\u00b7)."}, {"seg_id": 122, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Second, the baselines used for comparison are not complete, which makes me concern about the effectiveness of the model."}, {"seg_id": 123, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "-- the motivation and contribution for XEVAL are great-- the explanation of the dataset is lacking."}, {"seg_id": 124, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Presentation can be improved, especially the organization of the paper."}, {"seg_id": 125, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "\u2013 fragmented"}, {"seg_id": 126, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Model is largely based on prior work."}, {"seg_id": 127, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Baselines: It is unfair to compare the proposed method against baselines like BERT which are not designed to produce fixed length encodings."}, {"seg_id": 128, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Another point I just noticed, although the authors claimed that their model is better than SBERT, and did a comparison with SBERT-large, they did not compare with SBERT-base, which makes the conclusion unreliable."}, {"seg_id": 129, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "There are many settings considered in the paper and it is unclear if the proposed contributions are truly significant due to weak baselines are differences in data used for training different methods."}, {"seg_id": 130, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Ideally, the model should be compared against a skip-thought baseline or an unsupervised sentence representation learning method that uses the same resources for training."}, {"seg_id": 131, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Data used for pre-training: It is difficult to gauge how good the method is in comparison to other models due to differences in the data used for pre-training."}, {"seg_id": 132, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "I would appreciate a better and fair comparison of the Skip-Thought model by using same transformer encoder and same training corpus."}, {"seg_id": 133, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "There are still some concerns about the novelty of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 134, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "The hypothesis of the form \"This text talks about a female occupation\" refers to the text of the premise, rather than the events or entities in it."}, {"seg_id": 135, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "In other words, it talks about the form of the premise, rather than its meaning."}, {"seg_id": 136, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "Of course, there's nothing wrong with this, but it breaks a crucial assumptions about how the NLI data (in particular the SNLI data) was sourced: the events and entities in the hypothesis refer to the events and entities in the premise as much as possible."}, {"seg_id": 137, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems."}, {"seg_id": 138, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "The ground truth for the proposed examples seems to be neutral (occupations are neither male nor female), so I would like to know how often evaluated systems actually predict this."}, {"seg_id": 139, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted."}, {"seg_id": 140, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label."}, {"seg_id": 141, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "2.The bias measurement forces the models to predict either entailment or contradiction, where in fact the ground truth answer, in my opinion, for the proposed NLI examples, is neutral."}, {"seg_id": 142, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For all we know, the models are correctly predicting that with high probability, but the measurement is forcing a renormalization between entailment and contradiction examples (Section 3.2)."}, {"seg_id": 143, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For example, \"the doctor is operating\" becomes \"the teacher is operating\", which may not fit the realistic situation."}, {"seg_id": 144, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "In the constructed dataset, the hypothesis is generated by templates and looks like the context is not very related to the premise."}, {"seg_id": 145, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "However, in most of NLI datasets, the premise and hypothesis are usually related."}, {"seg_id": 146, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "So the domain of training set and their constructed evaluation set are different."}, {"seg_id": 147, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "In contrast, the word \"text\" in the hypotheses constructed in this work refers to the entire text of the premise, and not its entities and events."}, {"seg_id": 148, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It can be reasonable for models to perform not well and have the bias on the evaluation set, since the domain changes a lot."}, {"seg_id": 149, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "One way to fix the issue is to change the hypothesis templates to use the same (or similar) words as the premises, and replace the occupation word with a gendered word."}, {"seg_id": 150, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, replace \"he\" or \"his\" in the existing hypothesis with \"she\" or \"her\" so you can have female hypothesis and male hypothesis."}, {"seg_id": 151, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "When analyzing the results, the authors disregard the neutral case."}, {"seg_id": 152, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "Why do we have to force a choice between entail and contradict, when a stereotype-free model would actually predict neutral?"}, {"seg_id": 153, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "The constructed dataset contains only entailment pairs. How about analyzing the contradiction cases as well?"}, {"seg_id": 154, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "This seems like it would be a problem for the \"delta P\" and \"B\" measurement."}, {"seg_id": 155, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "Calling this entire setup \u201czero-shot\u201d is a bit of a stretch."}, {"seg_id": 156, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "The generator can be seen as zero-shot for generating task-specific examples, but the fine-tuned classifier not so much."}, {"seg_id": 157, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "For this reason, the truly zero-shot baseline seems weak and it is unsurprising that the classifier fine-tuned on synthetic data outperforms it."}, {"seg_id": 158, "paper_idx": 18, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_18.txt", "segment_text": "Not to mention the impact brought by prompts, decoding strategies, etc."}, {"seg_id": 159, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "(i) BERT is old now. We have already seen a lot of studies of BERT (in the so-called \u2018Bertology\u2019-literature), but isn\u2019t it time to move on?"}, {"seg_id": 160, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "There\u2019s the popular alternatives (RoBERTa, GPT-2, t5, etc.), but also a lot of faster, fairer, more interpretable alternatives."}, {"seg_id": 161, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "Authors use just BERT as pretrained model and a LSTM with GloVe embeddings as part of the experimentation."}, {"seg_id": 162, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "I think authors fail to properly state all the possible explanations to support the existence of transfer learning with scrambled or random input, If transfer learning were the explanation of why scrambled input keeps showing transfer learning capabilities an experiment training with the original data while tested on scrambled or vice versa is needed."}, {"seg_id": 163, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "The analysis section can be largely improved. Three out of four experiments show inconclusive results."}, {"seg_id": 164, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "Also, the observations made are marginally novel or significant, there are not many new results compared to prior observations."}, {"seg_id": 165, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "The title is about pre-trained models, but only BERT is discussed throughout the paper."}, {"seg_id": 166, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "And the reason why LSTM is more robust is that the model size is very small such that it can relearn all weights."}, {"seg_id": 167, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "My concern is that these scrambling techniques might push your dataset outside of real word adversarial attacks."}, {"seg_id": 168, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "(b) The shift does not seem to add value over other synthetic shifts that have already been studied, e.g., word-level shuffling, character-level shuffling, etc."}, {"seg_id": 169, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "I would also have liked to see a stronger motivation for focusing on frequency."}, {"seg_id": 170, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "S4.3 For contrastive baseline, I'm not sure how to interpret \"pairwise distant but internally compact\" in the context here."}, {"seg_id": 171, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "However, I have some concerns regarding to the presentation and the empirical results: (1) CCL+few shot data augmentation: the description is \"prompt with only an instruction and zero or more ID training examples\", then how many ID training examples are involved in CCL+few shot?"}, {"seg_id": 172, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "S5.1 it was a bit difficult to follow the various comparisons made in ref to Table 1."}, {"seg_id": 173, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., I had a hard time figuring out what is being compared at \"This accuracy decrease is smaller\""}, {"seg_id": 174, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "If it is sensitive to noise, then why it renders descent results on the other three datasets?"}, {"seg_id": 175, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "Improvements over the baselines are not fully convincing."}, {"seg_id": 176, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "For example, when discussing vanilla outperforming OE+NP (\"We attribute this behavior to the noisiness of generation: some novelty prompted examples are similar to ID examples\"), although I intuitively agree with this sentiment, it would be great to have some empirical support by quantifying example similarity."}, {"seg_id": 177, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The new data augmentation, while resulting in performance improvements over kFolden and Contrastive, also has more dependencies, i.e., access to GPT3."}, {"seg_id": 178, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "S5.3 Generator size is concerned only with the example generator, while the novel-label generator is always GPT3?"}, {"seg_id": 179, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "replace GPT-3 with GPT-2) impact the performance much."}, {"seg_id": 180, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "What happens if one generates novel labels with smaller models?"}, {"seg_id": 181, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The new loss function outperforms OE mostly because OE seems to fail on TREC-10 (else, it's competitive)."}, {"seg_id": 182, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "(3) Looking at Table 1, I am curious why OE+novelty prompting performs such badly on TREC-10."}, {"seg_id": 183, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "OE seems to do badly on TREC-10 for some reason, but it generally seems to match (or sometimes outperform) the new proposed loss on the other corpora."}, {"seg_id": 184, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The biggest negative of the paper, in my view, is that the performance gains (particularly of the new loss function) are marginal."}, {"seg_id": 185, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "If the proposed method only works well on very large pretrained generative models, the computational cost could be extremely high, since the authors generate 100k OOD samples."}, {"seg_id": 186, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The authors method can only work with text classification tasks, which is great, but --- some of the competing methods are more general, and the Wikitext + CCL method (which simulates the more general setting of not having GPT-3 but only an OOD set) only marginally outperforms prior solutions (kFolden, for example, doesn't even depend on the external set)."}, {"seg_id": 187, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Another concern I have is that the proposed model can only work in the scenarios where sentence-level representation is required."}, {"seg_id": 188, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "However, as shown in Table 2, the proposed method is not better than SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 189, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "So, what are the advantages of the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 190, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how this model helps in practice since it is achieving similar performance as other models like SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 191, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "My concern is the significancy of the results."}, {"seg_id": 192, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Since the improvement of the proposed method is marginal (~ 1% avg) and fine-tuning BERT on GLUE tasks might have high variance, authors should report the mean and std over multiple runs."}, {"seg_id": 193, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Employing span-level information in PrLMs was proposed in previous works such as SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 194, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Although the tasks between the two papers are different, the novelty of the paper is limited."}, {"seg_id": 195, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "As shown in Table 1, compared with the baselines, the proposed method does not bring remarkable promotion."}, {"seg_id": 196, "paper_idx": 24, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_24.txt", "segment_text": "The paper is hard to follow and would benefit from a clearer focus rather than the broad range of topics covered here."}, {"seg_id": 197, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of statistical tests to evaluate significance."}, {"seg_id": 198, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "In some cases improvements are quite small."}, {"seg_id": 199, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "I have some difficulty to assess the improvements that this approach brings."}, {"seg_id": 200, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "A detailed analysis of precision/recall would be useful, and whether the approach improves multi-class decision boundaries, or only the boundary with the negative class."}, {"seg_id": 201, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 1 illustrates that the decision boundaries learned by conventional classification models are \"open\", which makes it hard to distribute the universum class instances accurately."}, {"seg_id": 202, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "However, I doubt if this is really the case -- in the context of using deep neural nets (DNNs) as the feature encoder, the data representations  (i.e., $\\boldsymbol{h}$) and decision boundaries ($f(\\boldsymbol{h})$) are jointly learned, so even if the universum class instances have quite different semantics, it should not be hard for DNNs to map them into one compact cluster in the last layer's representation space."}, {"seg_id": 203, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "There is little analysis to show how the learned boundaries can better model class boundaries."}, {"seg_id": 204, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "The paper is unclear in many technical details."}, {"seg_id": 205, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "the arguments are hard to follow, and there is little analysis on the results to support the arguments."}, {"seg_id": 206, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The hierarchical relationship labels are not convincing enough. It is even difficult for humans to predict whether two sentences are from the same document. Enforcing the model to learn the \"same document\" label might cause the overfitting of the sentence representations."}, {"seg_id": 207, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "However, the motivation for this idea is very vague -- how can one accurately/unambiguously define the real difference between a sentence and a paragraph, and the difference between a paragraph and a document?"}, {"seg_id": 208, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "If the distinction between sentences, paragraphs and documents is even hard to tell for humans, then I don't see how the so-called \"paragraph-level relationships\" newly introduced in TSP can actually make a big difference compared to a simple combination of previous methods (NSP+SOP)."}, {"seg_id": 209, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The ablation study also shows that order information is more important than hierarchical structure information."}, {"seg_id": 210, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The empirical results are not uniformly improved with the proposed TSP methods on the downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 211, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The empirical gain of TSP does not seem convincing to me: Although TSP provides significant gains on GLUE/SuperGLUE average score, the gains are mainly from small/unstable tasks, like CoLA, MRPC, RTE and STS-B."}, {"seg_id": 212, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: The contribution of the paper is not convincing due to the problematic empirical evaluation (see cons above)."}, {"seg_id": 213, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work)."}, {"seg_id": 214, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear what the advantage of the proposed method over such an approach would be except as a curious application of existing pre-trained models."}, {"seg_id": 215, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Many terms were introduced without definitions, the sentence frequently omitted important contexts, and syntax was often broken."}, {"seg_id": 216, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The paper is somewhat hard to read due to syntactic/semantic issues, and I guess I possibly drops some context to assess the paper correctly."}, {"seg_id": 217, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The biggest flaw is that it is unclear whether anything presented in the work is truly novel or unknown to the machine translation community."}, {"seg_id": 218, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Interesting results, but I think this study is already standing at other position than so-called \"unsupervised translation\" because it uses pretrained LM as a seed and thus the model is based on a huge amount of additional resources. It would be not reasonable to say state-of-the-art by comparing its accuracy with other \"unsupervised\" models trained from only a specific corpus."}, {"seg_id": 219, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The work can be jointly summarized as \u201clanguage modeling can yield an initial translation model whose performance can boosted tremendously by back-translation\u201d, but no single aspect of this is new, nor is the success of the combination of the various pieces at all surprising to anyone who has been following the unsupervised translation literature over the past two years."}, {"seg_id": 220, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Only one non-English language is considered."}, {"seg_id": 221, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study."}, {"seg_id": 222, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "This paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair."}, {"seg_id": 223, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough."}, {"seg_id": 224, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The English-centric nature of the model (point 3) is an interesting aspect, however it is understudied in the paper."}, {"seg_id": 225, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The performance on these languages is crucial because the true value of unsupervised translation lies in low-resource language pairs, and their omission puts into question the validity of this method in realistic settings."}, {"seg_id": 226, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The absence of true low-resource language pairs for evaluating unsupervised translation is unacceptable, especially when considering most of the recent literature in unsupervised translation includes such language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 227, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Previously, the performance of UNMT methods on distant language pair is very poor."}, {"seg_id": 228, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "It would be more exciting, if we also observe the significant improvement of the proposed method on some distant language pairs, such as English-Chinese and English-Japanese."}, {"seg_id": 229, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "For example, do we expect GPT-3 to also excel at Hindi < - - > English translation?"}, {"seg_id": 230, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?"}, {"seg_id": 231, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "As the proposed method replaces the bilingual dictionary with few-shot amplification, it seems to me that the proposed method may achieve better performance on distant language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 232, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 233, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty of this paper is limited."}, {"seg_id": 234, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "Multiple choices prompting (MCP) has been used in other QA tasks, such as TruthfulQA and RACE."}, {"seg_id": 235, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven."}, {"seg_id": 236, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "No novel/brand new ideas."}, {"seg_id": 237, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "The submission is an analysis paper rather than finding something new."}, {"seg_id": 238, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "Limitations of the work has not been discussed by the authors."}, {"seg_id": 239, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "The most important part, I think, is the data expansion part described in Section 2.2."}, {"seg_id": 240, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited."}, {"seg_id": 241, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation."}, {"seg_id": 242, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "However, the author didn't compare with tensor train decomposition due to time constraints."}, {"seg_id": 243, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments."}, {"seg_id": 244, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive."}, {"seg_id": 245, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Thus use of tucker decomposition and its ability to compress BERT MHA and FFN layers is incremental improvement over the previous results, especially given the fact that prior work has also shown that MHA and FFN layers can be decomposed in a low rank structure and talked about the redundancy in the parameters in those layers."}, {"seg_id": 246, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Overall, I think this paper is an incremental improvement to previous state-of-the-art."}, {"seg_id": 247, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Some other method(s) are missing in the related works."}, {"seg_id": 248, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "For example: [1]"}, {"seg_id": 249, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I also recommend exploring the systems impact of running training and inference using tucker decomposed layers and why say 50x reduction in parameter count, does not lead to a 50x improvement in RPS (Table 4)."}, {"seg_id": 250, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Also, the presentation and the rationale behind the idea are not clear to me."}, {"seg_id": 251, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to."}, {"seg_id": 252, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Since the matrices across layers often have different scaling, and since tensor decomposition is approximating some reconstruction error (i.e. L2 in the case of SVD), it's not clear that performing decomposition with all the matrices makes sense."}, {"seg_id": 253, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "For example, why do you jointly compress all the layers, including all the FFN weights and attention weights?"}, {"seg_id": 254, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged."}, {"seg_id": 255, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, the reason for splitting each FFN weight matrix into 4 seems to make it possible to combine it with attention weights."}, {"seg_id": 256, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I realize that some of variants have already been studied in the literature (e.g. Mao et al., Noach and Goldberg), but since the setup is not identical, it is crucial that the proposed approach is compared against both II and III."}, {"seg_id": 257, "paper_idx": 32, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_32.txt", "segment_text": "This paper needs analyses in terms of how the proposed method addresses the catastrophic forgetting issue compared to baseline models, since the main claim of the RIFT is to tackle this issue."}, {"seg_id": 258, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable."}, {"seg_id": 259, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "Is the added robust prefix the same for the entire test set, or the same within a batch but different across batches, or unique for every test example?"}, {"seg_id": 260, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it."}, {"seg_id": 261, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\"."}, {"seg_id": 262, "paper_idx": 34, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_34.txt", "segment_text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3)."}, {"seg_id": 263, "paper_idx": 34, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_34.txt", "segment_text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling."}, {"seg_id": 264, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas."}, {"seg_id": 265, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel."}, {"seg_id": 266, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence."}, {"seg_id": 267, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 268, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup)."}, {"seg_id": 269, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed."}, {"seg_id": 270, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 271, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning."}, {"seg_id": 272, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting."}, {"seg_id": 273, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers."}, {"seg_id": 274, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions."}, {"seg_id": 275, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses."}, {"seg_id": 276, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked."}, {"seg_id": 277, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 278, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all."}, {"seg_id": 279, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures."}, {"seg_id": 280, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 281, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training."}, {"seg_id": 282, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar."}, {"seg_id": 283, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information."}, {"seg_id": 284, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models."}, {"seg_id": 285, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors."}, {"seg_id": 286, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection."}, {"seg_id": 287, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger."}, {"seg_id": 288, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse."}, {"seg_id": 289, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them."}, {"seg_id": 290, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data."}, {"seg_id": 291, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of some human validation"}, {"seg_id": 292, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow."}, {"seg_id": 293, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?"}, {"seg_id": 294, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are."}, {"seg_id": 295, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue."}, {"seg_id": 296, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration."}, {"seg_id": 297, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate"}, {"seg_id": 298, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other."}, {"seg_id": 299, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis."}, {"seg_id": 300, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong."}, {"seg_id": 301, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood."}, {"seg_id": 302, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\"."}, {"seg_id": 303, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings."}, {"seg_id": 304, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition."}, {"seg_id": 305, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there."}, {"seg_id": 306, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication."}, {"seg_id": 307, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions."}, {"seg_id": 308, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly."}, {"seg_id": 309, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d."}, {"seg_id": 310, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition."}, {"seg_id": 311, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases."}, {"seg_id": 312, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt."}, {"seg_id": 313, "paper_idx": 39, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_39.txt", "segment_text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces."}, {"seg_id": 314, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level."}, {"seg_id": 315, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results."}, {"seg_id": 316, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example."}, {"seg_id": 317, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g."}, {"seg_id": 318, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines."}, {"seg_id": 319, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks."}, {"seg_id": 320, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "It might be worth evaluating there."}, {"seg_id": 321, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well."}, {"seg_id": 322, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base."}, {"seg_id": 323, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling."}, {"seg_id": 324, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model."}, {"seg_id": 325, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that)."}, {"seg_id": 326, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing."}, {"seg_id": 327, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics."}, {"seg_id": 328, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?"}, {"seg_id": 329, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 330, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method."}, {"seg_id": 331, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel."}, {"seg_id": 332, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g."}, {"seg_id": 333, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 334, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting."}, {"seg_id": 335, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size."}, {"seg_id": 336, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "The scalability of the model is not shown."}, {"seg_id": 337, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale."}, {"seg_id": 338, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M."}, {"seg_id": 339, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?"}, {"seg_id": 340, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper)."}, {"seg_id": 341, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples."}, {"seg_id": 342, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored."}, {"seg_id": 343, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information."}, {"seg_id": 344, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading."}, {"seg_id": 345, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated."}, {"seg_id": 346, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M."}, {"seg_id": 347, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M)."}, {"seg_id": 348, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation."}, {"seg_id": 349, "paper_idx": 43, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_43.txt", "segment_text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks."}, {"seg_id": 350, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune."}, {"seg_id": 351, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available."}, {"seg_id": 352, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many."}, {"seg_id": 353, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible."}, {"seg_id": 354, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike."}, {"seg_id": 355, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not."}, {"seg_id": 356, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation."}, {"seg_id": 357, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\"."}, {"seg_id": 358, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims."}, {"seg_id": 359, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up."}, {"seg_id": 360, "paper_idx": 45, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_45.txt", "segment_text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\"."}, {"seg_id": 361, "paper_idx": 45, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_45.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4)."}, {"seg_id": 362, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated."}, {"seg_id": 363, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1]."}, {"seg_id": 364, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models."}, {"seg_id": 365, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak."}, {"seg_id": 366, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "Limited novelty."}, {"seg_id": 367, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "(1) limited novelty."}, {"seg_id": 368, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines."}, {"seg_id": 369, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared."}, {"seg_id": 370, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that."}, {"seg_id": 371, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared."}, {"seg_id": 372, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms."}, {"seg_id": 373, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time."}, {"seg_id": 374, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large."}, {"seg_id": 375, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages."}, {"seg_id": 376, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages."}, {"seg_id": 377, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code."}, {"seg_id": 378, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?"}, {"seg_id": 379, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions."}, {"seg_id": 380, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach."}, {"seg_id": 381, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs."}, {"seg_id": 382, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated."}, {"seg_id": 383, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself."}, {"seg_id": 384, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters."}, {"seg_id": 385, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages."}, {"seg_id": 386, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?"}, {"seg_id": 387, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation."}, {"seg_id": 388, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?"}, {"seg_id": 389, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1]."}, {"seg_id": 390, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance."}, {"seg_id": 391, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool."}, {"seg_id": 392, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue"}, {"seg_id": 393, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?"}, {"seg_id": 394, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model."}, {"seg_id": 395, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear."}, {"seg_id": 396, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption."}, {"seg_id": 397, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?"}, {"seg_id": 398, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "Three MLPs?"}, {"seg_id": 399, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?"}, {"seg_id": 400, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?"}, {"seg_id": 401, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent."}, {"seg_id": 402, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "In Introduction you talk about the frozen weights of LLM but do not motivate why you decide to keep them frozen until the Discussion section."}, {"seg_id": 403, "paper_idx": 48, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_48.txt", "segment_text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar."}, {"seg_id": 404, "paper_idx": 49, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_49.txt", "segment_text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial."}, {"seg_id": 405, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix."}, {"seg_id": 406, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported."}, {"seg_id": 407, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "As previously mentioned, the authors do not properly tackle the open question --- flat minima and generalization which might still seem to be controversial topic."}, {"seg_id": 408, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other."}, {"seg_id": 409, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "One possible explanation is that the flatness could be a consequence of scaling up the model size, and the good performance is brought by the large model size, too."}, {"seg_id": 410, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "The authors need to have more evidence to prove that flatness is the main reason that leads to good performance, otherwise, they may be both the consequence of another factor (like model size)."}, {"seg_id": 411, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "Second, while this paper points that pretraining loss is not a reliable indicator of downstream performance, a simple remedy is to evaluate on downstream tasks during pretraining and compare models accordingly, which is likely already done in practice."}, {"seg_id": 412, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "It is understandable that large-scale experiments are expensive and are not expected, but given the rather empirical motivation of the paper, some validation on real data seems desirable."}, {"seg_id": 413, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "Moreover, they need to tone down their claim since their theoretical analysis is limited to very specific controlled experiment which is far from realistic scenario."}, {"seg_id": 414, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "Although this paper tackles interesting research question, it is unclear that flatter minima actually leads to better generalizations since it does not compare relevant baselines and lack of justification."}, {"seg_id": 415, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "maybe I am missing the point and I am happy to be corrected, but the arguments made in this submission or the observations obtained are already well-known empirically, especially in the case of zero-shot and few-shot learning with very large pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 416, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, the theorems mentioned in the submission don't seem necessary, or, in any case, support the arguments or the observations."}, {"seg_id": 417, "paper_idx": 51, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_51.txt", "segment_text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper."}, {"seg_id": 418, "paper_idx": 51, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_51.txt", "segment_text": "Considering a GPT-2 is incorporated additional for data generation, it is not surprising to observe improvements."}, {"seg_id": 419, "paper_idx": 51, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_51.txt", "segment_text": "This can only show that using additional LM for data augmentation is useful and such a claim is provided in many existing papers."}, {"seg_id": 420, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "The motivation is clear but corresponding empirical analysis are missing."}, {"seg_id": 421, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1."}, {"seg_id": 422, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "From the Table 2 results, which use the WRENCH benchmark implementations (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.11377.pdf) the reported performance numbers don't seem to match what was reported in the original WRENCH paper."}, {"seg_id": 423, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "I have some concerns on the reported numbers (replicating WRENCH and reported above)"}, {"seg_id": 424, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "There are virtues to a simple method for improving performance, say an deep empirical dive into k-fold estimation when designing labeling functions, but this manuscript doesn't provide enough details to be very illuminating."}, {"seg_id": 425, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "As estimating T* and Y* is the core of ULF, the paper should have some analyses on whether T* and Y* are estimated more correctly after each iteration."}, {"seg_id": 426, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "more analyses are needed to understand the quality of T* and Y*."}, {"seg_id": 427, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "Clarity: the paper is quite difficult to read as the cross-validation-based confidence estimation description is quite dense."}, {"seg_id": 428, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "The paper suffers from clarity issues."}, {"seg_id": 429, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "For example, the feature-based and deep ULF methods need more methods elaboration and details"}, {"seg_id": 430, "paper_idx": 52, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_52.txt", "segment_text": "It's hard to draw any strong insights from the experiments as presented."}, {"seg_id": 431, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Within the paper, this is left very vague."}, {"seg_id": 432, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human."}, {"seg_id": 433, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.)."}, {"seg_id": 434, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Moreover, it is impossible to learn C4 from the single WS example."}, {"seg_id": 435, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Because there was a lack of analysis of the rule type, focusing only on an abstract idea of simplicity based on a particular method of compression, I found this unconvincing as a study of a general simplicity prior."}, {"seg_id": 436, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "This makes me unsure of the interpretation of the results in this paper."}, {"seg_id": 437, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?"}, {"seg_id": 438, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards."}, {"seg_id": 439, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed."}, {"seg_id": 440, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?"}, {"seg_id": 441, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al."}, {"seg_id": 442, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones."}, {"seg_id": 443, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Most of the paper is pretty clear, although I had trouble understanding section 3."}, {"seg_id": 444, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts."}, {"seg_id": 445, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material)."}, {"seg_id": 446, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text."}, {"seg_id": 447, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this."}, {"seg_id": 448, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after."}, {"seg_id": 449, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "From the definition of \"capturing a concept\" in #154 we have \"iff for every S we have that p \udbff\udc11 S iff c \udbff\udc11 S.\"."}, {"seg_id": 450, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy."}, {"seg_id": 451, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "In what sense does accuracy improve once you've captured a concept?"}, {"seg_id": 452, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "In particular, the paper uses \"fairness\" without clearly explaining how fairness is measured."}, {"seg_id": 453, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "The fairness metric used seemed a bit ad hoc."}, {"seg_id": 454, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "However, it is hard to understand the experiment sections without referring to the appendix in detail."}, {"seg_id": 455, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text."}, {"seg_id": 456, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "I had a hard time processing the multitude of experimental results. In particular, it's not clear what the numbers represent in each table."}, {"seg_id": 457, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "I would clearly describe in the caption of each figure what the rows/columns represent and what metric is used."}, {"seg_id": 458, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "Similar problems happen in Table 3."}, {"seg_id": 459, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "Not clear what WR50 means."}, {"seg_id": 460, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "Last but not least, I think \"fairness specifications\" is misleading and quite an overblown term for what the paper is about;"}, {"seg_id": 461, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\"."}, {"seg_id": 462, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "The word choice of \"diversity\" sounds strange, given the evaluation criterion."}, {"seg_id": 463, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "For weaknesses, the authors could be clearer on what fairness metric is being used for tables such as Table D.3."}, {"seg_id": 464, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al."}, {"seg_id": 465, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "CodeGen model (single-turn) is only evaluated on a single dataset with only 164 problems. Why is the model not evaluated on other publicly available coding datasets such as MBPP and APPS?"}, {"seg_id": 466, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "Novelty is limited since there are already several pre-training/dataset collection papers in this area."}, {"seg_id": 467, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "It is unclear why Table 2 only has information about CodeGen-Mono and not the other models. More data here would be more convincing."}, {"seg_id": 468, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "The result will stand out to compare against Codex, the state-of-the-art program synthesis model."}, {"seg_id": 469, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "The MTPB dataset contribution needs more explanation regarding its practical use cases and its limited capability to handle more complex programs."}, {"seg_id": 470, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "(-) MTPB (from a cursory qualitative evaluation of examples) seems to rely on very fine-grained prompts. In many examples in App F, the prompts are substantially longer than the required code."}, {"seg_id": 471, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern with this approach is that the paper does not discuss how to apply this approach to more general problems."}, {"seg_id": 472, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension."}, {"seg_id": 473, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "As a result, outside of tasks such as arithmetic operations where the dataset can be created automatically (and one could argue that you wouldn't use a task specific LM as the paper doesn't deal with multiple tasks or out-of-distribution problems), the applicability of this approach is minimal."}, {"seg_id": 474, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "On the negative side, the solution proposed is formulated as a fully supervised problem, meaning for each task the exact steps needed to solve the problem needs to be annotated."}, {"seg_id": 475, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "This is not a scalable approach as for realistic real-world applications obtaining detailed annotations for reasoning problems is an expert task which is expensive to scale to large datasets."}, {"seg_id": 476, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "Unfortunately, I don't see much impact from this work in practical terms."}, {"seg_id": 477, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "So, in its current form this does not enable any new abilities as we could use the base algorithm instead of using expensive LMs."}, {"seg_id": 478, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "In terms of the insight from a learning point-of-view, I also do not see anything surprising in this work."}, {"seg_id": 479, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty is minimal as the solution proposed is rather trivial as it doesn't try to solve the hard problem of reasoning without direct supervision, or other settings such as few-shot/zero-shot/multi-task and formulates the problem as a fully supervised task."}, {"seg_id": 480, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "So the high accuracy of the resulting method is not that surprising."}, {"seg_id": 481, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "Therefore, it would be great if the authors could explain the limitation of the problems that RoT could solve and how to design proper subproblems in order to use RoT to train the models."}, {"seg_id": 482, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "However, the technique only seems to work for problems for which we already have algorithms, and it is unclear to me if this could be extended to more general problems."}, {"seg_id": 483, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "The problems considered here are very simple, and an algorithm to solve them needs to be known in order to generate the training data."}, {"seg_id": 484, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems."}, {"seg_id": 485, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers)."}, {"seg_id": 486, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems."}, {"seg_id": 487, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as it requires people to feed it with hand-designed subproblems."}, {"seg_id": 488, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "One important point missing from this work is how to systematically construct subproblems given any problem in order to teach the model learn the recursion of thoughts."}, {"seg_id": 489, "paper_idx": 58, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_58.txt", "segment_text": "The baseline WebChild in Table 2 seems to extract a much higher number of tuples at 4.6M, while the proposed model ROBERTANET only extract 6741 tuples."}, {"seg_id": 490, "paper_idx": 58, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_58.txt", "segment_text": "It can be WebChild having much higher coverage, but this difference was not discussed in the paper."}, {"seg_id": 491, "paper_idx": 58, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_58.txt", "segment_text": "This, in my opinion, is not reasonable because it would result in a sparse KG."}, {"seg_id": 492, "paper_idx": 58, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_58.txt", "segment_text": "The effectiveness is not significant compared with SOTA baselines."}, {"seg_id": 493, "paper_idx": 58, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_58.txt", "segment_text": "The experiment is not comprehensive."}, {"seg_id": 494, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "The methodological novelty of the paper is fairly low."}, {"seg_id": 495, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "The paper completely reuses the T5 strategy for multi-task learning and just scales it up with more tasks."}, {"seg_id": 496, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "The scientific value of this work predominantly comes from the scale of their multi-task setting, and their analyses."}, {"seg_id": 497, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "While this scaling could have been an interesting study, the ablations and empirical investigation don\u2019t ask very interesting questions for this to be useful. I didn't find any significant recommendations for scaling tasks that can be reusable in future work or even applicable to models other than T5. Moreover, prior work (Aghajanyan et al., 2021) also studied most of these questions already, in the context of two pre-trained models, though in a slightly different setting. Over this and much other related work on multi-task and multi-lingual learning, the current paper only seems to contribute new results using the T5 model."}, {"seg_id": 498, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Many of the results presented in motivating the work are over random subsets of pre-training tasks and random seeds for training. However, none of the results seem to give an idea of the variance of performance, nor any indication of how many different random subsets of tasks were used for the results."}, {"seg_id": 499, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "The task scaling experiment (2.5) seems to be core to the contribution of the paper. Similar scaling experiments are also present in Aghajanyan et al., (2021). However, apart from the lack of an exposition of the variance in the quantities presented, I found this analysis to be very underwhelming."}, {"seg_id": 500, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "However, the models use the same R parameter across numbers of tasks, which seems unfair to low-task settings given that it was tuned on 107 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 501, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "There are some details missing regarding Section 2.4, especially with respect to random seeds."}, {"seg_id": 502, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "However, I am concerned that this will be used as a point of reference to say that randomly adding more tasks always helps, for which the paper doesn't provide sufficient evidence."}, {"seg_id": 503, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Additionally, the models use the same learning rate regardless of batch-size, ostensibly a learning rate that was tuned for a batch-size of 512."}, {"seg_id": 504, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Section 2.5 uses the same learning rate for different batch sizes, which might result in unfair comparison given there are previous works suggesting how batch size, learning rate could have compound effects on the downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 505, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Section 2.5 compares different numbers of tasks trained on different batch-sizes."}, {"seg_id": 506, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "In section 2.4, will R beyond 10 still lead to consistent improvement of the SuperGLUE performance is unclear."}, {"seg_id": 507, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Overall the idea of this work is novel, but the description is very hard to follow."}, {"seg_id": 508, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Clarity: some important terms are not defined at all or until later in the paper, including the key term of external knowledge (which commonly means something else actually, as in knowledge graphs)."}, {"seg_id": 509, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "The methods (and experiments) are described informally, or obscurely, where the specific choices made are not motivated or compared to alternatives."}, {"seg_id": 510, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "There are some typos and style issues."}, {"seg_id": 511, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "However, one might wonder why only the result of UnifiedQA is shown?"}, {"seg_id": 512, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "why not also compare to the case using knowledge 100% of the time?"}, {"seg_id": 513, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "The experiment result also need more explanations."}, {"seg_id": 514, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Comparing the results in Table 2 and 3, on many of the tasks, using Thrust to partially utilize the external knowledge drops the performance compared to when all queries are answered with external knowledge (e.g., 70.2 \u2192 56.8 (75% of queries) on e-SNLI UnifiedQA-base, 80 \u2192 73.4% (75% of queries) for TriviaQA UnifiedQA-3b)."}, {"seg_id": 515, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "This paper is very unclear."}, {"seg_id": 516, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "The experiments miss some important baselines."}, {"seg_id": 517, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Table 2, if my understanding is correct, with knowledge case is presented after the vertical bar (|)."}, {"seg_id": 518, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "If that's true, please fix the description of the model which states the opposite."}, {"seg_id": 519, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Such an important typo makes the paper difficult to understand."}, {"seg_id": 520, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now."}, {"seg_id": 521, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "For example, the authors did not indicate the number of clusters they used in the experiments, and how many knowledge instances they used to augment a query (which seems to be one)."}, {"seg_id": 522, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "The authors only compare Thrust with random baseline, but in order to show the effectiveness of Thrust as a scoring metric, they should compare the algorithm with baselines simpler than Thrust but better than random, e.g., using BM25 scores."}, {"seg_id": 523, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated."}, {"seg_id": 524, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "The authors claim that they try to simulate the real-world case where they have limited bandwidth to retrieve external knowledge from the perspective of cost-efficiency, but the claim is not persuasive because the way they choose the portion of the queries to use external knowledge is through relative comparison between queries after scoring all other queries, and not through absolute scoring on each independent query, e.g., by applying a score threshold."}, {"seg_id": 525, "paper_idx": 61, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_61.txt", "segment_text": "Few tasks here require complex reasoning, or deeper language understanding."}, {"seg_id": 526, "paper_idx": 61, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_61.txt", "segment_text": "Until then. it's unclear to me how this approach generalizes beyond simple tasks."}, {"seg_id": 527, "paper_idx": 61, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_61.txt", "segment_text": "However, I'm not quite convinced if this approach can scale to more challenging NLP tasks."}, {"seg_id": 528, "paper_idx": 61, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_61.txt", "segment_text": "Instead, most of the tasks can be solved with human-level accuracy using just the enumeration of a few input/output pairs as prompts (Fig. 4). These pairs would be available in any case since they are needed by the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 529, "paper_idx": 61, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_61.txt", "segment_text": "Translation task is only done for single words. Semantic tasks from GLUE are at 90+% accuracy with simple transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 530, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of converting all the tasks to an unified format is not new, and has been explored in earlier works like DecaNLP, UFO-Entail, T5 and more recently in the CLUES few-shot benchmark."}, {"seg_id": 531, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, the authors evaluate their models on held-out NLI tasks, but train their models on paraphrase identification tasks that bear some similarity with the entailment tasks."}, {"seg_id": 532, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Can the authors report the multi-task model performance on NLI tasks without using the paraphrase identification tasks?"}, {"seg_id": 533, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Repeated experiments with different subsets of training/eval tasks can help shed some light as well."}, {"seg_id": 534, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "However, we still rely on a vast amount of supervised data. In a way, the comparison with GPT-3 is not too fair."}, {"seg_id": 535, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?"}, {"seg_id": 536, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?"}, {"seg_id": 537, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "However, there are no comparison with auto-regressive model with the same pretraining and zero-shot task evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 538, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "What would also be interesting is to see/what's missing in the paper, is to carefully study the difference in domains of the training tasks, versus zero-shot evaluation tasks."}, {"seg_id": 539, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning."}, {"seg_id": 540, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "This is important since it is often difficult to explicitly partition tasks into disjoint sets."}, {"seg_id": 541, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "The claim of \"true\" zero-shot generalization needs to be further supported by two more experiments as highlighted in the main review, namely, (i) analyzing the overlap in text from the MTL setup with that of the unseen tasks, and (ii) ablating paraphrase identification tasks to find the impact on NLI tasks."}, {"seg_id": 542, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "The only evaluation on the size of multi-task training dataset is presented on BigBench dataset in Figure 5, which is different than FLAN zero-shot evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 543, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Therefore I think it would make the paper much stronger if you were to show how that baseline performs on the tasks in Figure 5."}, {"seg_id": 544, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "However, the evaluated model (T0) is using pretrained weights of T5 for training, which implicitly learned multi-tasking during unsupervised pretraining of T5 model. This makes the evaluation biased and an unfair comparison to GPT3 model."}, {"seg_id": 545, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text."}, {"seg_id": 546, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\"."}, {"seg_id": 547, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "But I would argue this is mostly an artifact of the experimental setup, as prefixing generation with D-string (of length L) only eliminates exposure bias up to position L, and then both evaluated systems (according to the generation process defined Section 4.1) use a standard auto-regressive LM generation process that makes them both subject to the exposure bias problem."}, {"seg_id": 548, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "A more appropriate reframing would be to talk about the kinds of effects exposure bias couldn\u2019t possibly be having, e.g. that exposure bias may only result in strange word choice but not total degeneration."}, {"seg_id": 549, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "So, the small performance difference between a preference M-prefixed and D-prefixed generation may very well be due to both setups being almost identical (same model, same auto-regressive inference algorithm, and the only difference is the prefix \u2013 which human raters are not even asked to judge directly)."}, {"seg_id": 550, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "The main issue I have is that the formalization of exposure bias is not well-founded and I do not believe a convincing argument is made the claims of relatively little (or even no) exposure bias are actually supported by the experiments."}, {"seg_id": 551, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "However, using the corpus-BLEU of individual samples in a fraction creates the potential for completely inaccurate estimates."}, {"seg_id": 552, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "Even assuming corpus-BLEU works, there is no guarantee that it acts as an estimate of individual samples, in the exact same way that BLEU is known not to be accurate for individual sentences (Burch, 2006)."}, {"seg_id": 553, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "Corpus-BLEU has been used in many papers, though I don\u2019t know of any showing actual correlation with a human metric, but we can at least say that it has precedent being used cumulatively."}, {"seg_id": 554, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "The authors claim that auto-regressive models appear to have \u201cself-recovery ability\u201d that mitigate any exposure bias, but I would say that it is hard to claim anything about self-recovery (at least in terms of automatic evaluation) when the evaluation metric operates over such a small window (<=3 words)."}, {"seg_id": 555, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2)."}, {"seg_id": 556, "paper_idx": 64, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_64.txt", "segment_text": "I am bit unconvinced about claiming that this work requires no external architecture in Table 1."}, {"seg_id": 557, "paper_idx": 64, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_64.txt", "segment_text": "But this claim could be controversial since adding new tokens (for prompts and labels) on the pre-trained language models' token embedding seems more efficient than pre-training language models that already have unused or special tokens in their token embedding. I know many PLMs already have unused tokens in practice, but I would like to treat this claim theoretically."}, {"seg_id": 558, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "Similar studies were conducted on a single node with 8 GPUs as noted by the authors. Though that setup had considerably more computational resources the total volume of computation was still a fraction of the amount used by many large research institutions. In light of that work, the scenario presented in this paper may seem somewhat derivative and only marginally interesting."}, {"seg_id": 559, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "The investigation of modifications lack convincing experiments. For example, only one task performance (MNLI) is reported for when studying the impact of training hyper-parameters. Other tasks can have a different trend."}, {"seg_id": 560, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "The biggest missing piece is an ablation study on the improvement from various architecture changes."}, {"seg_id": 561, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "But it's unknown which change(s) brought the bigger improvement."}, {"seg_id": 562, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report."}, {"seg_id": 563, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important."}, {"seg_id": 564, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "This paper (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=F5uYcwABMu), for example, clearly demonstrates that models with near-identical pretraining loss can perform very differently on downstream tasks due to implicit biases."}, {"seg_id": 565, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss."}, {"seg_id": 566, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "However, I am not sure if ICLR is the best venue for this work. There are no new theoretical or algorithmic contributions nor any new insight into representation learning. This does not change my belief that this is an informative paper to many in the community, but it might find a more suitable audience if submitted to venues such as EMNLP. (Disclaimer: I do not regularly publish in this area. I am happy to defer to more experienced reviewers and the AC on this point.)"}, {"seg_id": 567, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "Clarity is clear, however, novelty is limit."}, {"seg_id": 568, "paper_idx": 67, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_67.txt", "segment_text": "While it's good to quantify the performance of models on time-stratified datasets, many conclusions are obvious, such as the synthetic experiments on question answering by shifting the end year of training data, as well as the experiments on self-containing reading comprehension."}, {"seg_id": 569, "paper_idx": 67, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_67.txt", "segment_text": "While the conclusions in this paper might appear obvious, the analyses of this paper are thorough and insightful and the released datasets appear to be useful."}, {"seg_id": 570, "paper_idx": 67, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_67.txt", "segment_text": "Though the results might not be surprising (discussed well in L439) this paper defines the task in a good way for other work to (possibly?)"}, {"seg_id": 571, "paper_idx": 68, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_68.txt", "segment_text": "My biggest concern is on the underlying assumption of this paper---the model should not be impacted by the incorrect labels."}, {"seg_id": 572, "paper_idx": 68, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_68.txt", "segment_text": "It's controversial here because when we say in-context \"learning\", we actually want the model to be truthful to the provided demonstration examples."}, {"seg_id": 573, "paper_idx": 69, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_69.txt", "segment_text": "Notation are sometimes confusing."}, {"seg_id": 574, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA."}, {"seg_id": 575, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "As the paper acknowledge, SAP requires running the whole encoder-decoder architecture in an autoregressive loop. This can be slow."}, {"seg_id": 576, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "Particularly critical is that caching isn't anymore possible (wherein caching is generally usable to speed up autoregressive decoding in causal decoders), because the encoder is bidirectional and in the autoregressive loop."}, {"seg_id": 577, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "The paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed SAP method on the mT5 models, however, claims that the performance improvements come from the Bidirectional LMs, which is not reliable."}, {"seg_id": 578, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is, however, that if XGLM is not using bootstrapping+self-amplification etc."}, {"seg_id": 579, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "in Table 2 I am not sure how fair of a comparison it is. Perhaps with those techniques XGLM would exceed mT5 by a high margin."}, {"seg_id": 580, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "The method is applicable to mT5 style models, but the long-term solution is probably to adapt mT5 models with causal LM or prefix LM objectives."}, {"seg_id": 581, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "This part of performance improvement may only come from SAP's effect on mT5, or SAP's effect on the encoder-decoder architecture model."}, {"seg_id": 582, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "Not only that, the underperforming attempt of running SAP on the T5 1.1 proves the unreliability of this conclusion."}, {"seg_id": 583, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models."}, {"seg_id": 584, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "The training efficiency remains unclear."}, {"seg_id": 585, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "The memory/parameter efficiency of LoRA is exaggerated."}, {"seg_id": 586, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "the authors mention a 2/3 reduction in VRAM by not storing the gradients of the previous steps in Adam, which is true but it is important to mention that the gradient of the original weights is still needed (although not stored) at the current optimization step to compute the gradient for the low-ranked weights (if I understood correctly your method)."}, {"seg_id": 587, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "If it is GPU memory, then the 2/3 saving of training memory is quite surprising to me\u2014LoRA still needs to build the forward and backward computation graphs for the whole model."}, {"seg_id": 588, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "Could the authors clarify?"}, {"seg_id": 589, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency."}, {"seg_id": 590, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "Since LoRA is able to meet this requirement by not merging the pretrained weights (W_0) and low-rank weights (BA), it will be interesting if the author can report the comparison results of inference latency among (no-merging) LoRA and other methods."}, {"seg_id": 591, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters."}, {"seg_id": 592, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "The training efficiency of LoRA is a bit unclear."}, {"seg_id": 593, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "Missing comparison to the SOTA models."}, {"seg_id": 594, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "This is important for the reader to understand how far an efficient model is compared to the current SOTA."}, {"seg_id": 595, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "And why were 67 other colors inserted in the color prompts?"}, {"seg_id": 596, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "Also for the color experiments, it is not clear whether 60 or 6+57 or 70 (as mentioned in B.1.1 in the appendix) prompts were used."}, {"seg_id": 597, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "One of the few questions about the color experiment results was to what extent the model was backing off to predict less specific color names, since it is something that is not well-captured in top-1 accuracy (though maybe some combination of this and distance evaluation starts to get at it)."}, {"seg_id": 598, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "In short, by relying on the few-shot learning paradigm to extract the conceptual structure of concern, we're limited to extracting conceptual structure that can surface through few-shot learning (just like, for example, linear probes can only extract linearly separable features)."}, {"seg_id": 599, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "This ends up being more a measure of the few-shot learning ability of the model than the conceptual grounding capabilities of the model."}, {"seg_id": 600, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "For example, upon seeing the test representation (e.g., [0,1,0,0] in the spatial domain, or RGB (140, 0, 255) in the color domain), the model can use a simple strategy: copying the concept of a bunch of most similar representations in the in-line prompt examples (e.g., [0, 1, 0, 0, 0], or RGB (145, 0, 255))."}, {"seg_id": 601, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "This strategy would not involve the concept of \u201cleft\u201d or \u201cpink\u201d, and is robust to the rotation transformation (while not robust to the random transformation, if each point in the world was transformed independently)."}, {"seg_id": 602, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "For example, in Section 2.1, the authors say that all models are pretrained on the 40GB OPENAI-WT dataset, but this is not true for GPT-3?"}, {"seg_id": 603, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "There seems to be some confusion about the training data."}, {"seg_id": 604, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "(3) Though the paper investigates an interesting problem, the overall takeaway of this work is not very clear to me."}, {"seg_id": 605, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "It's not clear to me if the large models are exhibiting conceptual generalization/grounding abilities much larger than those afforded by such baselines (once we move out of the few-shot learning format)."}, {"seg_id": 606, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "Including all n-1 concepts besides the target one, or many such concepts (eg, 60 colors) provides a very strong constraint in the input (in my view contradicting the \"only a small number of examples\" claim in the paper's abstract)."}, {"seg_id": 607, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "The inclusion of a bunch of distinct concepts in the input can potentially also provide the model with a pragmatic cue that it will be presented with a new/unseen concept label if it receives a new/unseen kind of input, which adds another confound."}, {"seg_id": 608, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "It seemed arbitrary."}, {"seg_id": 609, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "Their approach is an extension of Electra rather than something fundamentally new."}, {"seg_id": 610, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "The sections do appear in my pdf\u2019s reader table of content, but not the text."}, {"seg_id": 611, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "The exposition could be improved."}, {"seg_id": 612, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "1. While the paper claim the importance of language modeling capability of pre-trained models, the authors did not conduct experments on generation tasks that are more likely to require a well-performing language model."}, {"seg_id": 613, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "What I find missing is that the paper hasn\u2019t addressed the claim about missing language modelling benefit (line 107-113)."}, {"seg_id": 614, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "Experiments on word similarity and SquAD in section 5.3 cannot really reflect the capability of language modeling."}, {"seg_id": 615, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "As the prompt-based approach [1,2,3] is gaining popularity recently, I wonder if COCO-LM can be used in this setup where the model is required to generate (i.e., assessing language modeling capability)."}, {"seg_id": 616, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "This is something MLM such as RoBERTA, BERT are doing quite well."}, {"seg_id": 617, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated."}, {"seg_id": 618, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "The performance of Megatron and COCO-LM is close to other approaches, for examples, RoBERTa, ELECTRA, and DeBERTa, which are with similar sizes as COCO-LM."}, {"seg_id": 619, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "If the author claim that COCO-LM is parameter-efficient, the conclusion is also applicable to the above related works."}, {"seg_id": 620, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "While there are clear strengths, one weakness is that one may need to define structural properties in different types of datasets that one might use."}, {"seg_id": 621, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "1.Structural locality inherent in datasets need to be captured by a set of custom locality feature functions which requires prior knowledge of the domain of the datasets."}, {"seg_id": 622, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well"}, {"seg_id": 623, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "2. Marginal improvements in results"}, {"seg_id": 624, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "However, the improvements due to the proposed method are marginal."}, {"seg_id": 625, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant."}, {"seg_id": 626, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "It is unclear how does the method generalise across different tasks and datasets, i.e., beyond two datasets."}, {"seg_id": 627, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "Using locality info in authors' experiments leads to minor improvement."}, {"seg_id": 628, "paper_idx": 76, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_76.txt", "segment_text": "One major question I have is novelty."}, {"seg_id": 629, "paper_idx": 76, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_76.txt", "segment_text": "The idea to fine-tune large LMs on private datasets is not new [1]. So is the idea to hold most of the pretrained weights constant and only update a small subset of the parameters (e.g., [3]). Thus, this paper is a combination of existing state-of-the-art techniques on several fronts including LoRA [3] for dimension reduction and FFT based method (4) for privacy accounting. While this is solid engineering work and demonstrates the feasibility of integrating DP into NLP, I am not sure if it has enough novelty to be published as a separate research paper. References: [1] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training [2] Parameter-efficient transfer learning for nlp. [3] LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models [4] Numerical composition of differential privacy"}, {"seg_id": 630, "paper_idx": 76, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_76.txt", "segment_text": "However, the contribution in terms of the technical novelty is not much. [1] The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, 2014. [2] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training.2020."}, {"seg_id": 631, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "There is no discussion or analysis on the performance of this new model on \u201ctail entities\u201d (entities that have few examples in the training set)."}, {"seg_id": 632, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "(1) if one wishes to use this type of model for much larger entity vocabularies, it is likely that a larger fraction of entities will have low number of examples, and"}, {"seg_id": 633, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis)."}, {"seg_id": 634, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "How can you improve the model to link rare entities not in the Wikipedia pages?"}, {"seg_id": 635, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "would it do better on popular entities or more long tail ones?"}, {"seg_id": 636, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "The experiments on cold start, as well as table 5 which shows performances on entities divided by name match is pretty cool!"}, {"seg_id": 637, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "Weak."}, {"seg_id": 638, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "The compared baseline is weak."}, {"seg_id": 639, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "The experiment results are relatively weak."}, {"seg_id": 640, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "However, the experiment part is relatively weak and cannot prove the empirical contribution of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 641, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high."}, {"seg_id": 642, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise."}, {"seg_id": 643, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "For example, the first section repeatedly referred to the COBOL applications, but we maybe not be able to reflect the experiments into this application due to large discrepancy between languages."}, {"seg_id": 644, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The main problem with this approach is that the performance suffers significantly with CodeNet dataset. The reason behind similarity-based data augmentation will work is because for implementing similar functionalities often similar variable names are used. However, such an assumption may not be true for CodNet where developers did independent implementations. Thus, the dropping of performance for CodeNet is not surprising. However, this raises questions about the usability of this technique."}, {"seg_id": 645, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites."}, {"seg_id": 646, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "However, the results show the approach suffered for independently developed code (CodeNet), which is the most realistic case."}, {"seg_id": 647, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other."}, {"seg_id": 648, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "Although I recognized that the main contribution of this paper is not proposing a sophisticated method to align samples, I thought the paper need more attention to design efficient strategy."}, {"seg_id": 649, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required."}, {"seg_id": 650, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "Also, the algorithm seems a simple greedy method and may be heavily affected by the sampling order."}, {"seg_id": 651, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "In its current form, it seems like this is preliminary work and with some more sophistication (in method + data sets) it is going to be a good future task."}, {"seg_id": 652, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The alignment algorithm need to be improved."}, {"seg_id": 653, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "In particular, good directions can include in-the-wild performance when tested on something outside of coding-problem data sets (especially since those are so well-organized by language and problem)."}, {"seg_id": 654, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The motivation of the paper includes \"modernizing legacy applications,\" but the experiments does not reflect this perspective."}, {"seg_id": 655, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "The technical contribution is incremental, without significant novelty."}, {"seg_id": 656, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc."}, {"seg_id": 657, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "The 3 proposed methods can all be summarized as hyperparameter/model tuning on the retrieval-based LMs, i.e., tuning the search engine, input prompting, and ensemble strategy."}, {"seg_id": 658, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better."}, {"seg_id": 659, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval."}, {"seg_id": 660, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "What is the advantage comparing to other few-shot learning of retrieval-augmented models, such as: Gautier Izacard, Patrick Lewis, Maria Lomeli, Lucas Hosseini, Fabio Petroni, Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Armand Joulin, Sebastian Riedel, Edouard Grave, Few-shot Learning with Retrieval Augmented Language Modelsm 2022:"}, {"seg_id": 661, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear whether the improvement come from the high quality of google\u2019s retrieved contents or from the retrieval-augmented method."}, {"seg_id": 662, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "The formulation of the CKL problem is overly simplified. It basically only considers a single corpus (D_1) for updating the knowledge of previously learned LMs. A general setup should consider a streaming version of D_1 and make it a sequence of sub-corpus (D_1, D_2, ..., D_T) that arrive at different time steps. Also, the associated tasks in UpdatedLAMA and NewLAMA should reflect such a time series --- that is, the streaming version of the current probing tasks. The current formulation described in Section 3.1 only has a single time step. It's more like an offline learning problem with the forgetting constraints but not an (online) continual learning problem. The proposed setup is thus a bit far from the motivation of studying CKL --- maintaining an ever-changing LM."}, {"seg_id": 663, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "However, only a two-phase setting is considered."}, {"seg_id": 664, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "More experiments can be explored, such as five-phase or controlling the differences in the distribution of data at different phases."}, {"seg_id": 665, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "The analysis of the CKL methods is not deep enough."}, {"seg_id": 666, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "The experimental findings in this paper are somewhat trivial."}, {"seg_id": 667, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "This work is quite insightful for us to understand more about how LM continuous learning works, although I think more experiments could be beneficial as I mentioned in the weakness section."}, {"seg_id": 668, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "Although the authors mentioned that \u201cWe leave more exploration of applying CKL methods on decoder-only models such as GPT-2 architecture as future work\u201d, they should still mention this critical discrepancy in the main body of the paper so that the readers are aware of the context of the findings."}, {"seg_id": 669, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "The paper only performs experiments with an encoder-decoder model (T5). The experimental results will be more convincing if more pre-trained language models (such as GPT) are included. For example, we can explore the ability of different PLMs to avoid catastrophic forgetting and to acquire new knowledge while preserving invariant knowledge."}, {"seg_id": 670, "paper_idx": 82, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_82.txt", "segment_text": "This is supported by the fact that the paper's analysis of the proposed importance score mechanism doesn't show comprehensible patterns (or the paper doesn't talk about it)."}, {"seg_id": 671, "paper_idx": 82, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_82.txt", "segment_text": "The experiments are not well-designed, and the results only show marginal improvements over RoBERTa."}, {"seg_id": 672, "paper_idx": 82, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_82.txt", "segment_text": "The main weakness is that the proposed extension to the baseline is relatively complex and rather heavy-weight in terms of new parameters (introducing ~25% more parameters compared to the baseline according to Table 2), yet only achieves a very marginal relative improvement of 0.2 percent over the baseline."}, {"seg_id": 673, "paper_idx": 82, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_82.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of HIRE model is very similar to utilizing ELMo in down-stream tasks, where the hidden representations of each layer are weighted."}, {"seg_id": 674, "paper_idx": 82, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_82.txt", "segment_text": "However, unfortunately, both components are not novel to me."}, {"seg_id": 675, "paper_idx": 83, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_83.txt", "segment_text": "Reproducibility is limited."}, {"seg_id": 676, "paper_idx": 83, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_83.txt", "segment_text": "I am not so positive about its reproducibility, since there are no statements about (a plan for open sourcing) code for reproducing the experiments."}, {"seg_id": 677, "paper_idx": 83, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_83.txt", "segment_text": "One point I did not like very much is the fact the authors mention two external baselines at the end of their related work section (the ensemble methods proposed for e.g. machine translation), but do not compare their methods against those baselines."}, {"seg_id": 678, "paper_idx": 83, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_83.txt", "segment_text": "You also did not include any such ensembles as your baselines. Why?"}, {"seg_id": 679, "paper_idx": 83, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_83.txt", "segment_text": "Novelty is a bit limited, lack of comparison with other common OOD approaches, and the generalization of the proposed approach is questionable."}, {"seg_id": 680, "paper_idx": 83, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_83.txt", "segment_text": "Page 9, Section 5, last paragraph: \"In this work, we focus on developing scores that can be readily derived from the generative model itself, without much increase in computation.\" -> You did not compute how much time it takes to run your proposed models compared to the baselines."}, {"seg_id": 681, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Qualitative analysis - Given that it is a generative paper, I'd like to see examples of how the generated texts look like for different labels. How different are those from the ones present in the training sample?"}, {"seg_id": 682, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Some automatic metrics/human evaluation should be introduced to measure the quality of the generated data."}, {"seg_id": 683, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "On the same line, I'd like to see how well the meta-weight generation can learn the importance of various tokens. For example, authors provide an example that the token \"movie\" might not be relevant to generate texts for positive/negative movie reviews. Does such properties automatically emerge from the meta-learning procedure?"}, {"seg_id": 684, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Susceptibility to hyper-parameter tuning - The optimization procedure is performing three interleaved gradient updates at every iteration."}, {"seg_id": 685, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, it is well known that meta-learning methods are notoriously hard to tune and can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices. It is hard to tune the framework on a large PLM with a small batch size."}, {"seg_id": 686, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "I'd like authors to comment on that along with the overall computational cost for this procedure."}, {"seg_id": 687, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Generalizability of the ensembled classifier - Can the ensembled classifier generalize to other methods which also produce synthetic samples to augment training set? If so, how much gain can we tentatively expect to obtain with those methods?"}, {"seg_id": 688, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "-The paper is lacking more qualitative analysis and discussion."}, {"seg_id": 689, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "It would be nice to provide more generated samples for given k-shots and for different tasks, and to discuss how they contribute to a better performance."}, {"seg_id": 690, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "-The paper uses a learnable \u201cw\u201d as a feedforward net with 100-dimension hidden layer, but it is not explained why this is the only considered option."}, {"seg_id": 691, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, other options such as self-attention layers, do not seem to be considered."}, {"seg_id": 692, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Since this part is important for learning the token weights in the meta-weighted objective function, the structure of this part can be further explored, for instance by providing more ablation analysis."}, {"seg_id": 693, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "- The compared baselines are not comprehensive."}, {"seg_id": 694, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "-The paper states that \u201cgeneral idea of meta-learning is to formulate a meta objective to enable automatic learning of hyperparameters\u201d, which is not entirely true, since that can be considered only as a subset of the meta-learning goals."}, {"seg_id": 695, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "In general, meta-learning is aiming to learn good representations and accrue meta-knowledge by observing batches of few-shot tasks."}, {"seg_id": 696, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a meta-learning framework is not clear and a bit sudden."}, {"seg_id": 697, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a bi-level problem is not clear. What is the interpretation behind it? It seems that the bi-level structure is not necessary in this case, since there is no strict hierarchy of the inner and outer objectives ($L_\\text{gen}$ and $L_{disc}$): the order of inner and outer objectives can be simply swapped to achieve the same goal(i.e. using unweighted generation loss in the outer loop and weighted discriminative loss in the inner loop). The bi-level form here is more like to introduce \u201ctechnical contribution\u201d."}, {"seg_id": 698, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "The paper does not conduct quantitative and qualitative analysis to measure the quality of the generated text."}, {"seg_id": 699, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "- Could the authors add the ablation where D_train and D_gen is together used for fine-tuning?"}, {"seg_id": 700, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "-There is a lack of experiments without the data augmentation done by the autoregressive language model. To have a better understanding of how the data generation part helps, the authors should provide a baseline where the model is fine-tuned without augmentation. This will offer a fair comparison to the models trained w/o augmentation in Table 1, and also can highlight the contribution of the classification-only part of the method."}, {"seg_id": 701, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations."}, {"seg_id": 702, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?"}, {"seg_id": 703, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "Ideally the author should look deeper into how are the retrieval results helping the model to make predictions that are otherwise difficult."}, {"seg_id": 704, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "3, The paper claims to have experimental evidence that instance-adaptive selection is superior to task-adaptive selection of the context type."}, {"seg_id": 705, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "But this claim did not seem to be backed up by numbers."}, {"seg_id": 706, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "The paper lacks ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component of the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 707, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "Suggested Ablation Studies & Analyses"}, {"seg_id": 708, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Tab 1b is confusing."}, {"seg_id": 709, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "I don't understand what each number means."}, {"seg_id": 710, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "I don't see a solid explanation evaluation in this paper."}, {"seg_id": 711, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c."}, {"seg_id": 712, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Prototype quality: prototypes do not look very convincing."}, {"seg_id": 713, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "I checked examples of prototypes in the appendix but the 1:1 label distribution (positive vs. negative) seems to me they are human-picked."}, {"seg_id": 714, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG."}, {"seg_id": 715, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Touching different approaches is important here since the way ProtoTrex handle explanation might not easily extend to all other cases."}, {"seg_id": 716, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc)."}, {"seg_id": 717, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss)."}, {"seg_id": 718, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "How to determine these hyperparameters?"}, {"seg_id": 719, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams."}, {"seg_id": 720, "paper_idx": 88, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_88.txt", "segment_text": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days)."}, {"seg_id": 721, "paper_idx": 88, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_88.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, this paper proposes to have a fixed number of global MoE layers, which is probably not suitable when a pre-training dataset has very diverse tokens."}, {"seg_id": 722, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "In other words, I'd argue that with or without recitation, the objective remains the same (i.e., masked or next-word prediction), so this motivation to me feels a bit mis-directed."}, {"seg_id": 723, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective."}, {"seg_id": 724, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "(2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models."}, {"seg_id": 725, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model)."}, {"seg_id": 726, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "The paper would be much stronger with experiments on GPT3, Instruct-GPT3 (davinci-002), and larger language models (larger in-house LMs?)."}, {"seg_id": 727, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "It's not really clear from the paper whether recitation helps with larger scale, which I think is important for the generalizability of the method [1]."}, {"seg_id": 728, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?"}, {"seg_id": 729, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "I think experiments on InstructGPT [4], T0 [3] or FLAN [2, 7] will be especially interesting, since it's been fine-tuned on instructions / examples / human preferences."}, {"seg_id": 730, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "A major advantage of retrieval augmented systems is their applicability on (1) tail distribution information; (2) generalization to information which was not present in the model's training set (like COVID for BERT)."}, {"seg_id": 731, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "I think these are important limitations of the proposed method, and (1) is not really discussed (2 is just mentioned in the conclusion)."}, {"seg_id": 732, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, ATLAS [5] gets 42% on NQ with 64 examples and a smaller model, while the best number in this paper is 32% (5-10x larger model)."}, {"seg_id": 733, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "Note that I do think it's incredible that closed book LMs are doing so well, but practically (from a performance stand-point) it may be better to just retrieve some text from the web rather than ask an LM to generate it with few-shot examples."}, {"seg_id": 734, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "What is the size of the index + docs (compressed) of BM25?"}, {"seg_id": 735, "paper_idx": 90, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_90.txt", "segment_text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples)."}, {"seg_id": 736, "paper_idx": 90, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_90.txt", "segment_text": "The number of examples is not significant, with only 250 examples, compared to GMS8K with 8.5K examples, so the results may not be generalized if more examples are translated and added to the benchmark."}, {"seg_id": 737, "paper_idx": 90, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_90.txt", "segment_text": "And the empirical verification of CoT on more datasets are also not sufficient for another new paper."}, {"seg_id": 738, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers)."}, {"seg_id": 739, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Hard to replicate: several proposed datasets are downloaded from the web, it will be hard to download the same files."}, {"seg_id": 740, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Also, there will be some challenges to reproduce this work with respect to the datasets that should be addressed as well."}, {"seg_id": 741, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "No details on the specific hardware and software used."}, {"seg_id": 742, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The paper writing ignores many details that are helpful for reproducing the method."}, {"seg_id": 743, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The model is only evaluated on the language modeling task."}, {"seg_id": 744, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach."}, {"seg_id": 745, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "More tasks would be helpful to indicate whether the proposed method is general enough in terms of data distribution."}, {"seg_id": 746, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM."}, {"seg_id": 747, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Throughout the paper the authors only compare their model to baselines that they designed and trained."}, {"seg_id": 748, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting."}, {"seg_id": 749, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?"}, {"seg_id": 750, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The numbers in the compressive transformer are very different from the numbers your models obtain, so I\u2019m assuming that you preprocessed that dataset differently and so have a different vocabulary, making those results incomparable?"}, {"seg_id": 751, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The authors don\u2019t provide any speed or memory usage statistics."}, {"seg_id": 752, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Of course, it\u2019s totally fine if this model runs slower or uses more memory than the baseline- I expect such preliminary investigations to potentially be nonoptimal, with later work making it an actually viable model."}, {"seg_id": 753, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "I didn't find clear comparisons of training speed and GPU memory usage between different models. It is valuable for the readers to have a sense of what kind of resource is needed to train or host such models."}, {"seg_id": 754, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "But I think it's important to discuss these things."}, {"seg_id": 755, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The authors do not compare their model to any previously published model."}, {"seg_id": 756, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers)."}, {"seg_id": 757, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Results are very close for each memory size and there are no error measurements (e.g., std dev)."}, {"seg_id": 758, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "This makes understanding the results on each dataset, for the different models, harder."}, {"seg_id": 759, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "For example, on the arxiv dataset the authors talk about a drop from 2.927 to 2.877 perplexity between two different models, but is a 0.05 perplexity difference really statistically significant here?"}, {"seg_id": 760, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "there are some benchmarks that require models to be able to handle long inputs, such as document-level QA datasets and document-level retrieval."}, {"seg_id": 761, "paper_idx": 93, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_93.txt", "segment_text": "Two out of the three tasks are generation tasks in which BLEU or ROUGE-2 evaluation scores are difficult to interpret, i.e. it is very difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 762, "paper_idx": 93, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_93.txt", "segment_text": "This statement is only true \"on average\" and overlooks the huge differences between different tasks."}, {"seg_id": 763, "paper_idx": 93, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_93.txt", "segment_text": "Furthermore, it is not clear where the results are significant different among systems."}, {"seg_id": 764, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "While the work has its merit in decreasing the biases in some metrics, It is bad using those dataset as leaderboard without looking into them."}, {"seg_id": 765, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "The paper mentioned that bias measurements like WEAT have been questioned."}, {"seg_id": 766, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "[1] elaborates on the few problems with CrowS-Pairs and StereoSet."}, {"seg_id": 767, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "The experimental results, especially in Table 4, are not very convincing."}, {"seg_id": 768, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "I am concerned about the evaluation of bias."}, {"seg_id": 769, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "However, Stereo-set and Crows-Pairs have also been criticized [1]."}, {"seg_id": 770, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "As recent studies showed, intrinsic measures of bias do not necessarily correlate with bias measures on a concrete, down-stream task that can be used in an application."}, {"seg_id": 771, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "Without such an evaluation for an application oriented task, it is hard to understand whether the few points gained by the new model will lead to significant bias improvement in a downstream task."}, {"seg_id": 772, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms."}, {"seg_id": 773, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "Related to the point above, I would have liked to see more ablations on the vote-k selection process. How important is it to use the model confidence \u201cstratification\u201d strategy in the algorithm? This is particularly useful to know since, given a very large set of unlabeled examples |U|, it can be costly to run all u in |U| model predictions to collect confidence scores."}, {"seg_id": 774, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "There is also no discussion on label distribution in the vote-k process."}, {"seg_id": 775, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "Since the selective annotation is based entirely on similarities derived from sentence embeddings, there is nothing explicit ensuring that the label distribution over the selected subset is not skewed."}, {"seg_id": 776, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "One particularly confusing part of vote-k: it was not immediately clear to me how this differs or is better than simple k-means clustering to discover centroids?"}, {"seg_id": 777, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "I do not think the proposed method is novel, though it is effective."}, {"seg_id": 778, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty of this work is mixed: the idea of using retrieval for in-context learning prompts is not new (this work cites Liu et al., 2022 and Rubin et al., 2022).."}, {"seg_id": 779, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "But novelty here is (1) in combining these ideas to make a practical system, and (2) the experimental work demonstrating some insights comparing this with fine-tuning and other selective annotation schemes."}, {"seg_id": 780, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results."}, {"seg_id": 781, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The gains are not super high."}, {"seg_id": 782, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "Is there an easier task where this method leads to strong performance but ordinary pretraining does not?"}, {"seg_id": 783, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "I felt this was quite separate from the theoretical analysis. It does not follow from the theoretical results that adding similar sentences will be a good thing. As a result, having these in the same paper looked incoherent to me."}, {"seg_id": 784, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The only two tasks examined are sentence similarity tasks (which seem a bit more like a sanity check), and NaturalQuestions."}, {"seg_id": 785, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "It wasn't intuitive for me that it'd be useful for NQ."}, {"seg_id": 786, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "It's particularly surprising to me that this works so well on NQ, and I wish the authors had dug a bit deeper into this, but I also recognize that page limits exist."}, {"seg_id": 787, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "3. The experimental results are weak."}, {"seg_id": 788, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks."}, {"seg_id": 789, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks."}, {"seg_id": 790, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field."}, {"seg_id": 791, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks."}, {"seg_id": 792, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "3.The experiments are limited."}, {"seg_id": 793, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger."}, {"seg_id": 794, "paper_idx": 97, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_97.txt", "segment_text": "How could the findings benefit the community? The design principle of EncDec-based NMT is the sub-optimal performance/efficiency of LM-based NMT, and this paper just says: \"yes, the intuition is true\", which is not exciting at all."}, {"seg_id": 795, "paper_idx": 97, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_97.txt", "segment_text": "Given that the encoder-decoder paradigm dominantly governs machine translation (also from the experiment part of this paper that LMs underperform EncDec most of the time. ), for what reason should we need to consider a shift to a unified language model for such a seq2seq task? If the zero-shot transfer is the case, why not directly fix the off-target issue for EncDec and preserve the good of translation performance, instead of changing the paradigm?"}, {"seg_id": 796, "paper_idx": 97, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_97.txt", "segment_text": "The paper conducted extensive experiments to show how scaling affects LMs for MT, however, few suggestions based on the findings are given for future development of MT."}, {"seg_id": 797, "paper_idx": 97, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_97.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should answer the question: how could the paper findings benefit the community?"}, {"seg_id": 798, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of using entity abstraction is straightforward and not very novel as doing POS tagging for input is widely used in many NLP models, especially in traditional NLP pipelines."}, {"seg_id": 799, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "While the performance on synthetic datasets is promising, on realistic datasets the proposed method fails to improve the performance."}, {"seg_id": 800, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Therefore the effectiveness of the method is not fully supported by the experiments."}, {"seg_id": 801, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 802, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "There is very minor boost in performance on QA datasets."}, {"seg_id": 803, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The goal of the proposed model should be improving real-world data, instead of synthetic data (CLUTTR in this paper)."}, {"seg_id": 804, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications."}, {"seg_id": 805, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "However, in that case you can not prove that the proposed methods are still beneficial on a model that achieves way better baseline results."}, {"seg_id": 806, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The experiment part lacks analysis on the model which could provide more intuitions on how the model works."}, {"seg_id": 807, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance."}, {"seg_id": 808, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal."}, {"seg_id": 809, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 810, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The performance of baselines are far below the current top performers."}, {"seg_id": 811, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Also, the empirical findings lack deeper analysis and were merely conducted on a single and toy-sized pretrained language model."}, {"seg_id": 812, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal."}, {"seg_id": 813, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, we could dive deep into why the model variants perform differently between CLUTRR and HotpotQA & CoQA."}, {"seg_id": 814, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "If there is a new reasoning dataset, which architecture in Table 1 should people use?"}]
/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/overlap_detect_tasks.json:1:t it is hard to contextualize how good the numbers in your framework are.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, I have no idea how good 52% on VIPR for SST2 is.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If previously proposed defenses are able to get 90% on the same task, then it is unlikely that the proposed method will gain traction, even if it is more computationally efficient with prefix tuning. So it would strengthen this paper a lot to have this comparison.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Since a lot of work was put into the paper, I will pay my respect and make a picky point: the bibliography could be cleaned up a bit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "E.g., capitalize RoBERTa correctly, add URLs consistently, capitalize all conference names (e.g., IEEE transactions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This method can be seen as a variation of schedule sampling (Bengio et al., 2015) or a simplified version of UniLM (Dong et al., 2019). The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "On the other hand, the authors should give more analysis about why this method can work in LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The training set of pre-training models contains a lot of noise data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wonder if the proposed method is necessary.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Bengio et al., 2015. Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Dong et al., 2019. Unified language model pre-training for natural language understanding and generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "(W.1) In Table 1, along with the mean of the 3 random realizations, the spread should be provided as well, given that many of the performance numbers are within a single percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(W.2): The performance of FCM appears to be better than PaLM, but not significantly and consistently so.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For significance, I\u2019m using a criterion of at least 1% in absolute terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(W.2.d) Scaling trends: If we look at the consistency of performance improvement as the number of shots increase, there are very few tasks, on which FCM consistently stays better than PaLM by at least a percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "On the NLU tasks, FCM seems to lose its advantage as the number of shots increase, sometimes becoming much worse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(W.3) Classification finetuning: results on SuperGLUE dev set only show significant improvement on 4 out of 8 tasks, with majority of the gains observed on WSC and CB.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors should discuss the variability in performance gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(W.4) Scalability, 0-shot: In Table 4, FCM 1B results seem better than FCM 8B on CB, RTE, and WiC tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(W.5) Scalability, k-shot: In Table 5, the performance of both PaLM and FCM sometimes, and more often for FCM, degrades as the number of shots increases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(W.6) Ablation of mask ratio: Irrespective of the average across all the tasks, FCM[0,0.15] is better only on 2 out of 8 tasks in the 0-shot setting and 3 of 8 tasks in the 1-shot setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This shows that the choice of mask ratio is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "(W.7) Comparison with Dropout: Contrary to the claim of dropout hurting FCM performance, it improves in 3 of 8 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It improves performance by 10.2 absolute % points for the MultiRC task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It seems that the benefit of dropout is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This method can be seen as a variation of schedule sampling (Bengio et al., 2015) or a simplified version of UniLM (Dong et al., 2019). The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "On the other hand, the authors should give more analysis about why this method can work in LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The training set of pre-training models contains a lot of noise data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wonder if the proposed method is necessary.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Bengio et al., 2015. Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Dong et al., 2019. Unified language model pre-training for natural language understanding and generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "While the proposed method does not add any computational cost in theory, since previous tokens are randomly masked out, won't there be an engineering overhead since the previous hidden states cannot be cached which would have remained unchanged if the previous token weren't masked out otherwise?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "This method can be seen as a variation of schedule sampling (Bengio et al., 2015) or a simplified version of UniLM (Dong et al., 2019). The novelty of this paper is limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "On the other hand, the authors should give more analysis about why this method can work in LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The training set of pre-training models contains a lot of noise data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wonder if the proposed method is necessary.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Bengio et al., 2015. Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Dong et al., 2019. Unified language model pre-training for natural language understanding and generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "The biggest weakness of this paper is ignoring related research papers and lack of comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "[1] presents comprehensive study about different training objectives in zero-shot priming and finetuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "They compared 6 different training objectives in table 1 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11726.pdf): NXTUNI, NXTPRE, MSKUNI, MSKBI, HYBUNI, HYBPRE.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Casual language modeling such as GPT models belong to NXTUNI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "They controlled hyper-parameters and training data to be same and studied these six different objectives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In addition, this paper should carry out apple-to-apple comparisons with other models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For example, it needs to control model sizes (model parameters), batch size, hyper-parameters, training tokens, training devices, to be exactly the same.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "And control Total train compute (flops) to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Therefore, the qualified apple-to-apple comparison is only PaLM 1B v.s. FCM 1B (in table 3), PaLM 128M v.s. FCM 128M (in table 4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For others, PaLM 8B used different training data with FCM 8B, and T5 used different model parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The difference is that [2] puts the masked tokens to the end of sentence, so the masked places can not only attend to previous tokens but also gather information from future tokens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But due to lack of comparison, I cannot see the performance differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "(W.1) In Table 1, along with the mean of the 3 random realizations, the spread should be provided as well, given that many of the performance numbers are within a single percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(W.2): The performance of FCM appears to be better than PaLM, but not significantly and consistently so.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For significance, I\u2019m using a criterion of at least 1% in absolute terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(W.2.d) Scaling trends: If we look at the consistency of performance improvement as the number of shots increase, there are very few tasks, on which FCM consistently stays better than PaLM by at least a percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "On the NLU tasks, FCM seems to lose its advantage as the number of shots increase, sometimes becoming much worse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": 