ince the previous hidden states cannot be cached which would have remained unchanged if the previous token weren't masked out otherwise?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "(W.1) In Table 1, along with the mean of the 3 random realizations, the spread should be provided as well, given that many of the performance numbers are within a single percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(W.2): The performance of FCM appears to be better than PaLM, but not significantly and consistently so.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For significance, I\u2019m using a criterion of at least 1% in absolute terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(W.2.d) Scaling trends: If we look at the consistency of performance improvement as the number of shots increase, there are very few tasks, on which FCM consistently stays better than PaLM by at least a percentage point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "On the NLU tasks, FCM seems to lose its advantage as the number of shots increase, sometimes becoming much worse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(W.3) Classification finetuning: results on SuperGLUE dev set only show significant improvement on 4 out of 8 tasks, with majority of the gains observed on WSC and CB.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors should discuss the variability in performance gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(W.4) Scalability, 0-shot: In Table 4, FCM 1B results seem better than FCM 8B on CB, RTE, and WiC tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(W.5) Scalability, k-shot: In Table 5, the performance of both PaLM and FCM sometimes, and more often for FCM, degrades as the number of shots increases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "(W.6) Ablation of mask ratio: Irrespective of the average across all the tasks, FCM[0,0.15] is better only on 2 out of 8 tasks in the 0-shot setting and 3 of 8 tasks in the 1-shot setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This shows that the choice of mask ratio is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Kindly discuss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "(W.7) Comparison with Dropout: Contrary to the claim of dropout hurting FCM performance, it improves in 3 of 8 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It improves performance by 10.2 absolute % points for the MultiRC task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It seems that the benefit of dropout is task dependent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "The biggest weakness of this paper is ignoring related research papers and lack of comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "[1] presents comprehensive study about different training objectives in zero-shot priming and finetuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "They compared 6 different training objectives in table 1 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11726.pdf): NXTUNI, NXTPRE, MSKUNI, MSKBI, HYBUNI, HYBPRE.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Casual language modeling such as GPT models belong to NXTUNI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "They controlled hyper-parameters and training data to be same and studied these six different objectives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In addition, this paper should carry out apple-to-apple comparisons with other models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For example, it needs to control model sizes (model parameters), batch size, hyper-parameters, training tokens, training devices, to be exactly the same.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "And control Total train compute (flops) to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Therefore, the qualified apple-to-apple comparison is only PaLM 1B v.s. FCM 1B (in table 3), PaLM 128M v.s. FCM 128M (in table 4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For others, PaLM 8B used different training data with FCM 8B, and T5 used different model parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The difference is that [2] puts the masked tokens to the end of sentence, so the masked places can not only attend to previous tokens but also gather information from future tokens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But due to lack of comparison, I cannot see the performance differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 34, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "While the proposed method does not add any computational cost in theory, since previous tokens are randomly masked out, won't there be an engineering overhead since the previous hidden states cannot be cached which would have remained unchanged if the previous token weren't masked out otherwise?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "The biggest weakness of this paper is ignoring related research papers and lack of comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "[1] presents comprehensive study about different training objectives in zero-shot priming and finetuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "They compared 6 different training objectives in table 1 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11726.pdf): NXTUNI, NXTPRE, MSKUNI, MSKBI, HYBUNI, HYBPRE.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Casual language modeling such as GPT models belong to NXTUNI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "They controlled hyper-parameters and training data to be same and studied these six different objectives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The most relevant method to this paper is HYBUNI (casual masking objective). [2] is the representative work of causal masking objective. This paper presents this new causal masking objective without comparing it with the most relevant casual masking method (CM3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In addition, this paper should carry out apple-to-apple comparisons with other models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For example, it needs to control model sizes (model parameters), batch size, hyper-parameters, training tokens, training devices, to be exactly the same.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "And control Total train compute (flops) to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Therefore, the qualified apple-to-apple comparison is only PaLM 1B v.s. FCM 1B (in table 3), PaLM 128M v.s. FCM 128M (in table 4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For others, PaLM 8B used different training data with FCM 8B, and T5 used different model parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The difference is that [2] puts the masked tokens to the end of sentence, so the masked places can not only attend to previous tokens but also gather information from future tokens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But due to lack of comparison, I cannot see the performance differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Thus, the contributions of this paper are marginally novel/ significant.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Another major limitation of this paper is that it completely ignores comparison with other parameter-efficient transfer learning methods like adapters [4].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Concretely, AdapterFusion [3] work proposes to learn a task-specific composition of adapters from previous tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "How does the prompt tuning-based method compare with adapter fusion when applied to the LFLL problem?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is there a consensus that prompt tuning-based methods are better than adapters for few-shot learning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is important because as we scale the number of tasks, the input sequence length scales linearly, and with that self-attention scales quadratically.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This issue limits the practical applicability of the prompt tuning-based method for lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "What does it mean to use validation sets for hyper-parameter selection?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Ideally, during lifelong learning, we do not know the sequence of tasks apriori.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is unfair to freeze a particular task order, then search for optimal hyper-parameters, and then report results on multiple runs with the same task order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "At least, report the results on unseen task orders?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Or search on a subset of disjoint tasks [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, with the current experiments, it is unclear how they compare with adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Thus, the contributions of this paper are marginally novel/ significant.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Another major limitation of this paper is that it completely ignores comparison with other parameter-efficient transfer learning methods like adapters [4].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Concretely, AdapterFusion [3] work proposes to learn a task-specific composition of adapters from previous tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "How does the prompt tuning-based method compare with adapter fusion when applied to the LFLL problem?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is there a consensus that prompt tuning-based methods are better than adapters for few-shot learning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is important because as we scale the number of tasks, the input sequence length scales linearly, and with that self-attention scales quadratically.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This issue limits the practical applicability of the prompt tuning-based method for lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "What does it mean to use validation sets for hyper-parameter selection?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Ideally, during lifelong learning, we do not know the sequence of tasks apriori.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is unfair to freeze a particular task order, then search for optimal hyper-parameters, and then report results on multiple runs with the same task order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "At least, report the results on unseen task orders?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Or search on a subset of disjoint tasks [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, with the current experiments, it is unclear how they compare with adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "There are a few weaknesses that I hope the paper can address to improve the scoring:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the paragraph below equation 4, the loss form is an addition of 3 values: 1) the task loss L^{task}; 2) the language modeling loss L^{lm}; 3) label consistency loss L^{KL}.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Both 1) and 2) are reasonable, but 3) is new in the paper and it is unclear why it is absolutely necessary. In particular, the paper should include an ablation study on a range of \\lambda_{KL}, not just a comparison between models with and without L^{KL}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some parts of the method are not sufficiently studied in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Thus, the contributions of this paper are marginally novel/ significant.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Another major limitation of this paper is that it completely ignores comparison with other parameter-efficient transfer learning methods like adapters [4].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Concretely, AdapterFusion [3] work proposes to learn a task-specific composition of adapters from previous tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "How does the prompt tuning-based method compare with adapter fusion when applied to the LFLL problem?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is there a consensus that prompt tuning-based methods are better than adapters for few-shot learning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is important because as we scale the number of tasks, the input sequence length scales linearly, and with that self-attention scales quadratically.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This issue limits the practical applicability of the prompt tuning-based method for lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "What does it mean to use validation sets for hyper-parameter selection?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Ideally, during lifelong learning, we do not know the sequence of tasks apriori.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "It is unfair to freeze a particular task order, then search for optimal hyper-parameters, and then report results on multiple runs with the same task order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "At least, report the results on unseen task orders?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Or search on a subset of disjoint tasks [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, with the current experiments, it is unclear how they compare with adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The main contribution of saying that LFPT5 \"can be seen as a vital step towards general language intelligence\" is surely an overclaim, right? If I ask 100 researchers, how many of them will see this paper as a vital step towards general language intelligence?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The key part that I did not understand is why prompt tuning isn't already a sufficient life-long learning method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My understanding of prompt tuning is that no model weights are modified, only the prompt is optimized, and optimized prompts are stored for each task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Then given a new task, a new prompt can be trained and stored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This works because the size of prompts is many orders of magnitude smaller than the size of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Maybe another way of asking this question is, how is the following different from regular prompt tuning: \"while adapting to a new task type, LFPT5 includes and tunes additional prompt embeddings for the new task\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Another part I do not understand is why pseudo samples need to be generated. Why can't you just use the prior examples? I am missing why this idea of retraining on previous domains each time is a good one.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The introduction could make it more clear why the second two limitations of LLL are indeed limitations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Why NER in particular, instead of other tasks that haven't been explored thoroughly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "PT is not defined at first use in the abstract", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "All the acronyms are pretty confusing", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "I didn't understand why their method specifically applies to the few-shot scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I also struggled to understand why the proposed method solves lifelong learning over regular prompt tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One sentence in the paper seems like an overclaim to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "There are a few weaknesses that I hope the paper can address to improve the scoring:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the paragraph below equation 4, the loss form is an addition of 3 values: 1) the task loss L^{task}; 2) the language modeling loss L^{lm}; 3) label consistency loss L^{KL}.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Both 1) and 2) are reasonable, but 3) is new in the paper and it is unclear why it is absolutely necessary. In particular, the paper should include an ablation study on a range of \\lambda_{KL}, not just a comparison between models with and without L^{KL}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some parts of the method are not sufficiently studied in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "To really become a benchmark to measure the progress of LFLL, more tasks/datasets will be needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The main contribution of saying that LFPT5 \"can be seen as a vital step towards general language intelligence\" is surely an overclaim, right? If I ask 100 researchers, how many of them will see this paper as a vital step towards general language intelligence?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The key part that I did not understand is why prompt tuning isn't already a sufficient life-long learning method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My understanding of prompt tuning is that no model weights are modified, only the prompt is optimized, and optimized prompts are stored for each task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Then given a new task, a new prompt can be trained and stored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This works because the size of prompts is many orders of magnitude smaller than the size of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Maybe another way of asking this question is, how is the following different from regular prompt tuning: \"while adapting to a new task type, LFPT5 includes and tunes additional prompt embeddings for the new task\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Another part I do not understand is why pseudo samples need to be generated. Why can't you just use the prior examples? I am missing why this idea of retraining on previous domains each time is a good one.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The introduction could make it more clear why the second two limitations of LLL are indeed limitations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Why NER in particular, instead of other tasks that haven't been explored thoroughly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "PT is not defined at first use in the abstract", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "All the acronyms are pretty confusing", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "I didn't understand why their method specifically applies to the few-shot scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I also struggled to understand why the proposed method solves lifelong learning over regular prompt tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One sentence in the paper seems like an overclaim to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 35, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "There are a few weaknesses that I hope the paper can address to improve the scoring:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the paragraph below equation 4, the loss form is an addition of 3 values: 1) the task loss L^{task}; 2) the language modeling loss L^{lm}; 3) label consistency loss L^{KL}.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Both 1) and 2) are reasonable, but 3) is new in the paper and it is unclear why it is absolutely necessary. In particular, the paper should include an ablation study on a range of \\lambda_{KL}, not just a comparison between models with and without L^{KL}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some parts of the method are not sufficiently studied in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The main contribution of saying that LFPT5 \"can be seen as a vital step towards general language intelligence\" is surely an overclaim, right? If I ask 100 researchers, how many of them will see this paper as a vital step towards general language intelligence?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiments are OK but not probably not good enough to support the general claims about lifelong learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The key part that I did not understand is why prompt tuning isn't already a sufficient life-long learning method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My understanding of prompt tuning is that no model weights are modified, only the prompt is optimized, and optimized prompts are stored for each task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Then given a new task, a new prompt can be trained and stored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This works because the size of prompts is many orders of magnitude smaller than the size of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Maybe another way of asking this question is, how is the following different from regular prompt tuning: \"while adapting to a new task type, LFPT5 includes and tunes additional prompt embeddings for the new task\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Another part I do not understand is why pseudo samples need to be generated. Why can't you just use the prior examples? I am missing why this idea of retraining on previous domains each time is a good one.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The introduction could make it more clear why the second two limitations of LLL are indeed limitations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Why NER in particular, instead of other tasks that haven't been explored thoroughly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "PT is not defined at first use in the abstract", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "All the acronyms are pretty confusing", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "I didn't understand why their method specifically applies to the few-shot scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I also struggled to understand why the proposed method solves lifelong learning over regular prompt tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One sentence in the paper seems like an overclaim to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Table 1 shows that models with different percentages of redundant parameters have similar performance, implying that performance may not be proportional to \"well-trained\" parameters. It means that making more parameters \"well-trained\" does not necessarily improve the performance. This seems not totally in line with the paper's motivation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 5 is not straightforward to visualize the difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The curve is drawn only on the right subfigure.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Sufficiently training all parameters seems a double-edged sword.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "According to Figure 3, models trained with SAGE are susceptible to parameter pruning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In general, we want efficient and compact models in deployment.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The main concern is whether SAGE has adverse effects in getting efficient deployment models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "This is unfortunate and not rigorous.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The use of Taylor approximation is only good close to the operating point for nonlinear functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However Theta_j,-j may be very large, and thus the approximation may be very bad.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I do not agree that the memory and computational costs of SAGE are \"marginal\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The EMA \"I-hat\" is a full copy of the model since we need one EMA per parameter, especially for large models this is substantial overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "6) I do not agree that one can conclude from the results that SAGE is more effective for small datasets than for large ones, since the \"points\" betwen the datasets/tasks do not live on the same scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This should be more accurately reflected in the title, for example by replacing the word \"training\" by \"fine-tuning\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would further be interesting to see how well it works when training from scratch; even if it does not work, it is still a valuable fine-tuning method, and stating this may save a lot of people a lot of resources and time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Table 1 shows that models with different percentages of redundant parameters have similar performance, implying that performance may not be proportional to \"well-trained\" parameters. It means that making more parameters \"well-trained\" does not necessarily improve the performance. This seems not totally in line with the paper's motivation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 5 is not straightforward to visualize the difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The curve is drawn only on the right subfigure.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Sufficiently training all parameters seems a double-edged sword.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "According to Figure 3, models trained with SAGE are susceptible to parameter pruning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In general, we want efficient and compact models in deployment.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The main concern is whether SAGE has adverse effects in getting efficient deployment models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The hyper-parameters for SAGE are exhaustively tuned, more so than for the baseline adaptive optimizers, so there\u2019s a potential for bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Table 3 should show results for your implementation of Adamax that\u2019s comparable to Adamax-SAGE, in addition to the Devlin et al numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Table 1 shows that models with different percentages of redundant parameters have similar performance, implying that performance may not be proportional to \"well-trained\" parameters. It means that making more parameters \"well-trained\" does not necessarily improve the performance. This seems not totally in line with the paper's motivation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 5 is not straightforward to visualize the difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The curve is drawn only on the right subfigure.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Sufficiently training all parameters seems a double-edged sword.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "According to Figure 3, models trained with SAGE are susceptible to parameter pruning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In general, we want efficient and compact models in deployment.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The main concern is whether SAGE has adverse effects in getting efficient deployment models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the context of image classification it would be interesting to evaluate it with CNN and others architecture than ViT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optimisation: In image classification, the pre-training procedure used is quite sub-optimal since the paper of Dosovitsky et al.[1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "many improvements have been proposed such as the DeiT approach [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be interesting to see if the proposed method still works when the model is trained with more regularisation and data-augmentation which may also lead to a better use of weights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Image Classification: Having only results of models pre-trained on ImageNet-21k and fine-tuned with the proposed method on downstream tasks is not very usual in image classification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Nevertheless, more complete experiments in image classification would allow to better evaluate the interest of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "This is unfortunate and not rigorous.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The use of Taylor approximation is only good close to the operating point for nonlinear functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However Theta_j,-j may be very large, and thus the approximation may be very bad.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I do not agree that the memory and computational costs of SAGE are \"marginal\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The EMA \"I-hat\" is a full copy of the model since we need one EMA per parameter, especially for large models this is substantial overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "6) I do not agree that one can conclude from the results that SAGE is more effective for small datasets than for large ones, since the \"points\" betwen the datasets/tasks do not live on the same scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This should be more accurately reflected in the title, for example by replacing the word \"training\" by \"fine-tuning\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would further be interesting to see how well it works when training from scratch; even if it does not work, it is still a valuable fine-tuning method, and stating this may save a lot of people a lot of resources and time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The hyper-parameters for SAGE are exhaustively tuned, more so than for the baseline adaptive optimizers, so there\u2019s a potential for bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Table 3 should show results for your implementation of Adamax that\u2019s comparable to Adamax-SAGE, in addition to the Devlin et al numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "This is unfortunate and not rigorous.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The use of Taylor approximation is only good close to the operating point for nonlinear functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "However Theta_j,-j may be very large, and thus the approximation may be very bad.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I do not agree that the memory and computational costs of SAGE are \"marginal\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The EMA \"I-hat\" is a full copy of the model since we need one EMA per parameter, especially for large models this is substantial overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "6) I do not agree that one can conclude from the results that SAGE is more effective for small datasets than for large ones, since the \"points\" betwen the datasets/tasks do not live on the same scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This should be more accurately reflected in the title, for example by replacing the word \"training\" by \"fine-tuning\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would further be interesting to see how well it works when training from scratch; even if it does not work, it is still a valuable fine-tuning method, and stating this may save a lot of people a lot of resources and time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I am not convinced that it will be generally useful at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the context of image classification it would be interesting to evaluate it with CNN and others architecture than ViT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optimisation: In image classification, the pre-training procedure used is quite sub-optimal since the paper of Dosovitsky et al.[1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "many improvements have been proposed such as the DeiT approach [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be interesting to see if the proposed method still works when the model is trained with more regularisation and data-augmentation which may also lead to a better use of weights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Image Classification: Having only results of models pre-trained on ImageNet-21k and fine-tuned with the proposed method on downstream tasks is not very usual in image classification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Nevertheless, more complete experiments in image classification would allow to better evaluate the interest of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 37, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The scaling formula seems somewhat ad hoc and hard to characterize. In particular, if the sensitivity of some parameter spikes on a given iteration, it will get a larger update than another parameter with the same moving-average sensitivity that has not spiked.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been good to test the combination with dropout or something similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The hyper-parameters for SAGE are exhaustively tuned, more so than for the baseline adaptive optimizers, so there\u2019s a potential for bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Figure 7 counters this possibility, but only by showing heatmaps, so it's hard to gauge the actual numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Table 3 should show results for your implementation of Adamax that\u2019s comparable to Adamax-SAGE, in addition to the Devlin et al numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Downsides are that the formula isn't particularly well justified, and there are a few potential experimental weaknesses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In the context of image classification it would be interesting to evaluate it with CNN and others architecture than ViT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optimisation: In image classification, the pre-training procedure used is quite sub-optimal since the paper of Dosovitsky et al.[1]", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "many improvements have been proposed such as the DeiT approach [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be interesting to see if the proposed method still works when the model is trained with more regularisation and data-augmentation which may also lead to a better use of weights.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Image Classification: Having only results of models pre-trained on ImageNet-21k and fine-tuned with the proposed method on downstream tasks is not very usual in image classification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Nevertheless, more complete experiments in image classification would allow to better evaluate the interest of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "If summarization models are sensitive to paraphrasing and structural features, then synthetic documents may also exhibit artifacts that influence the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be important to perform a similar analysis on selected documents from CNN/DM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The position bias analysis, the authors state that \"clearly amplified by MatchSum, PreSumm, and Azure, as shown in Fig. 4b.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, it is not clear if the observed pattern is caused by model preference or simply by truncation of inputs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "If we observe the figure in Appendix E, the distribution for PEGASUS and BART are more similar to the reference summaries distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In contrast, MatchSum, Presumm, and Azure decrease the frequency sharply between 0.4 and 0.6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Interestingly, Presumm and MatchSum use the same backbone model with a maximum input length of 512 tokens, whereas PEGASUS and BART support inputs of 1024 tokens, which might explain their capacity to select more content from the later positions of the articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For reference, CNN/DM input documents have 766 words on average (\"Extractive Summarization as Text Matching\", Zhong et al., 2020).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Do the quantile calculation in the figures take into account this truncation effect?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "No details or citation is provided for the paraphrasing model. Just the link for its checkpoint at https://huggingface.co/tuner007/pegasus_paraphrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Authors mention that they \"conduct manual analysis on a randomly selected subset of the article\" but no further details are provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Experiments are based only on CNN/DM, which is well known for its lead bias for important content.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors only considered a few models, ignoring sota models on CNN/Dailymail dataset. It may have been possible that the SOTA model behaves differently.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paper lacks any association/description between the metrics used for bias in this paper v/s different text summarisation metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been nice to consider why and how these metrics are different to give a better understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Paper lacks any results about biases based on religion and race even though they claim to have done so in the initial section of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Only one incomplete figure about religion is present which is hard to comprehend as it lacks any legend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The results and figures need to be explained further.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Explaining the ideal score for an automatic summarizer in the figures would have given a better idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I did not really understand the dashed lines in the figure given as Baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "An explanation with an example will go a long way in making things much more clear and bringing out the results effectively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "First it claims no work has been done on bias-ness in text summarization then it points out a previous work on structure biasness (Jung et al, 2019).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "In the Fig.3, two of the extractive summarisers are not mentioned namely presumm and matchsum", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "If summarization models are sensitive to paraphrasing and structural features, then synthetic documents may also exhibit artifacts that influence the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be important to perform a similar analysis on selected documents from CNN/DM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The position bias analysis, the authors state that \"clearly amplified by MatchSum, PreSumm, and Azure, as shown in Fig. 4b.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, it is not clear if the observed pattern is caused by model preference or simply by truncation of inputs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "If we observe the figure in Appendix E, the distribution for PEGASUS and BART are more similar to the reference summaries distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In contrast, MatchSum, Presumm, and Azure decrease the frequency sharply between 0.4 and 0.6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Interestingly, Presumm and MatchSum use the same backbone model with a maximum input length of 512 tokens, whereas PEGASUS and BART support inputs of 1024 tokens, which might explain their capacity to select more content from the later positions of the articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For reference, CNN/DM input documents have 766 words on average (\"Extractive Summarization as Text Matching\", Zhong et al., 2020).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Do the quantile calculation in the figures take into account this truncation effect?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "No details or citation is provided for the paraphrasing model. Just the link for its checkpoint at https://huggingface.co/tuner007/pegasus_paraphrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Authors mention that they \"conduct manual analysis on a randomly selected subset of the article\" but no further details are provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Experiments are based only on CNN/DM, which is well known for its lead bias for important content.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But it does not show what the alternative would be and whether that alternative is indeed more preferable to an end user.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is hard to say that given two different texts to an end user (since these are different as they've been reordered), they would somehow still write the same summary for both articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "There is also no end user analysis showing that these biased summaries from a model that rely more on what comes earlier in the article are offering less value to the end users than what a counterfactual unbiased summary would offer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Experiments in Figure 2 come close to this point but it does not come clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "On Page 3, its said that the generation details for the synthetic corpus are in Section 5, on Page 4 its said that they are in Appendix B, but it seems they are in neither.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The content bias analysis is based on a synthetic dataset generated by GPT-2, so that the summary inference was performed on out-of-domain data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "If summarization models are sensitive to paraphrasing and structural features, then synthetic documents may also exhibit artifacts that influence the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be important to perform a similar analysis on selected documents from CNN/DM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The position bias analysis, the authors state that \"clearly amplified by MatchSum, PreSumm, and Azure, as shown in Fig. 4b.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, it is not clear if the observed pattern is caused by model preference or simply by truncation of inputs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "If we observe the figure in Appendix E, the distribution for PEGASUS and BART are more similar to the reference summaries distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In contrast, MatchSum, Presumm, and Azure decrease the frequency sharply between 0.4 and 0.6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Interestingly, Presumm and MatchSum use the same backbone model with a maximum input length of 512 tokens, whereas PEGASUS and BART support inputs of 1024 tokens, which might explain their capacity to select more content from the later positions of the articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For reference, CNN/DM input documents have 766 words on average (\"Extractive Summarization as Text Matching\", Zhong et al., 2020).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Do the quantile calculation in the figures take into account this truncation effect?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "No details or citation is provided for the paraphrasing model. Just the link for its checkpoint at https://huggingface.co/tuner007/pegasus_paraphrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Authors mention that they \"conduct manual analysis on a randomly selected subset of the article\" but no further details are provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Experiments are based only on CNN/DM, which is well known for its lead bias for important content.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Experiments on additional datasets could make the claims stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lack of some human validation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Overall, the paper is straightfoward to follow, the novelty is great and the analysis is interesting and reproducible with reasonable", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "effort.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors only considered a few models, ignoring sota models on CNN/Dailymail dataset. It may have been possible that the SOTA model behaves differently.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paper lacks any association/description between the metrics used for bias in this paper v/s different text summarisation metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been nice to consider why and how these metrics are different to give a better understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Paper lacks any results about biases based on religion and race even though they claim to have done so in the initial section of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Only one incomplete figure about religion is present which is hard to comprehend as it lacks any legend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The results and figures need to be explained further.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Explaining the ideal score for an automatic summarizer in the figures would have given a better idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I did not really understand the dashed lines in the figure given as Baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "An explanation with an example will go a long way in making things much more clear and bringing out the results effectively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "First it claims no work has been done on bias-ness in text summarization then it points out a previous work on structure biasness (Jung et al, 2019).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "In the Fig.3, two of the extractive summarisers are not mentioned namely presumm and matchsum", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But it does not show what the alternative would be and whether that alternative is indeed more preferable to an end user.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is hard to say that given two different texts to an end user (since these are different as they've been reordered), they would somehow still write the same summary for both articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "There is also no end user analysis showing that these biased summaries from a model that rely more on what comes earlier in the article are offering less value to the end users than what a counterfactual unbiased summary would offer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Experiments in Figure 2 come close to this point but it does not come clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "On Page 3, its said that the generation details for the synthetic corpus are in Section 5, on Page 4 its said that they are in Appendix B, but it seems they are in neither.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Only used single embedding word2vec (word embeddings can themselves be baised) and only considered one dataset CNN/Dailymail, without explaining any rationale behind the selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors only considered a few models, ignoring sota models on CNN/Dailymail dataset. It may have been possible that the SOTA model behaves differently.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The paper lacks any association/description between the metrics used for bias in this paper v/s different text summarisation metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It would have been nice to consider why and how these metrics are different to give a better understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Paper lacks any results about biases based on religion and race even though they claim to have done so in the initial section of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Only one incomplete figure about religion is present which is hard to comprehend as it lacks any legend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The results and figures need to be explained further.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Explaining the ideal score for an automatic summarizer in the figures would have given a better idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I did not really understand the dashed lines in the figure given as Baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "An explanation with an example will go a long way in making things much more clear and bringing out the results effectively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "First it claims no work has been done on bias-ness in text summarization then it points out a previous work on structure biasness (Jung et al, 2019).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This raises the concern that if the paraphrased version of sentences itself were curated in a manner that they did not include proper information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also there are no examples from the synthetic dataset curated by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "In the Fig.3, two of the extractive summarisers are not mentioned namely presumm and matchsum", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lack of some human validation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Overall, the paper is straightfoward to follow, the novelty is great and the analysis is interesting and reproducible with reasonable", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "effort.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 38, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The exposition is confusing, several details are missing and assumptions are made without declaration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is no qualitative analysis to help the reader better follow how the authors are changing structure or content, or what a synthetic summary looks like, or what does it mean to be an article about men only but still have sentences about women in them (Figure 3) or whether the authors' several perturbation strategies aren't altering valuable information?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper relies on the premise that a certain way of choosing a summary (say weighing what comes earlier in an article more), given an article is wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But it does not show what the alternative would be and whether that alternative is indeed more preferable to an end user.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is hard to say that given two different texts to an end user (since these are different as they've been reordered), they would somehow still write the same summary for both articles.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "There is also no end user analysis showing that these biased summaries from a model that rely more on what comes earlier in the article are offering less value to the end users than what a counterfactual unbiased summary would offer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Experiments in Figure 2 come close to this point but it does not come clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "On Page 3, its said that the generation details for the synthetic corpus are in Section 5, on Page 4 its said that they are in Appendix B, but it seems they are in neither.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Appendix B comes close to sharing some information but after reading it, I could still not tell how someone could replicate this synthetic data generation because details are sparse.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Several claims have been presented as facts but no citations or data are offered to support them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I'm not convinced whether the proxies/metrics for measuring content bias are accurate", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lack of some human validation", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Overall, the paper is straightfoward to follow, the novelty is great and the analysis is interesting and reproducible with reasonable", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "effort.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I think the paper would be much stronger if the metrics were validated using some human analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 39, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It's entirely possible that a slight variation of the prompt managed to fix the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The jump from addition to combination with subtraction is relatively small at an abstract level: just four combinations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This could become a strength if presented as a simple change that becomes challenging for the LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The setting of using a shorter explanation becomes out of the general hypothesis. This might become a strength if it were presented as a limitation and not as a solution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I found the paper somewhat hard to follow and the writing style rather confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "For instance, I did not understand what \u201cdefining algorithms as skills\u201d means exactly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "This is mentioned several times in the manuscript but it looks to just boil down to using the aforementioned prompting technique.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper should make a clear statement somewhere that the language model is not re-optimized and no weight updates actually happen, since the name \u201cin-context learning\u201d might be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The main drawback of the manuscript is that technical details of the experimentation are largely missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors do not mention explicitly how many examples they provide in the prompt for each task, whether it is only one or multiple.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Details of the baseline are not provided either, such as which prompts are used, making the comparison between different ideas harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Usage of space is not well-balanced, the authors dedicated a full subsection for explaining \u201ctwo-number addition\u201d (section 3.1) and only a few paragraphs for all the others.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In particular, the algorithm for parity is not even mentioned but its results appear in table 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The fact that this prompting fails on logical tasks (section 6) poses serious concerns on its applicability on harder problems other than simple mathematical operations and, therefore, its real-world utility.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Clarity can certainly be improved (see above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I would like to raise a concern about reproducibility of the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I wanted to reproduce some of the experiments but found out that OpenAI\u2019s Codex code-davinci-002 model is not publicly available yet, which is somewhat awkward.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The best I could do was to try reproducing OOD results with the text-davinci-002 model, which exhibited similar reasoning processes but I got results (for 14 digits) that are much lower, i.e. 30%, than what is shown in Figure 3, i.e. approximately 97-98%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I tried with 10 samples where each addend has 14 digits, while providing the model with a single example of summation with 3-digit numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Perhaps the authors can explain why this happened?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Is that only due to a model difference or too few examples in the prompt?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Please find here a txt file showing the prompt I used (the same as in section G.2) and the response I got from the text-davinci-002 model: https://file.io/vqUWwuH8uZpq", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Lastly, a final concern about real-world utility as the prompting does not perform well on harder problems (logical tasks).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}]}, {"paper_idx": 39, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The interference found is a negative result in an area poorly understood.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It's entirely possible that a slight variation of the prompt managed to fix the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I'm not ready to accept strong interpretations of \"X prompt didn't work\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I suggest lowering the tone of such findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The jump from addition to combination with subtraction is relatively small at an abstract level: just four combinations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This could become a strength if presented as a simple change that becomes challenging for the LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The study of the composition of skills gets confusing around multiplication.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The setting of using a shorter explanation becomes out of the general hypothesis. This might become a strength if it were presented as a limitation and not as a solution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "My main concern is presenting some blurry methods as contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I'm not convinced that the experiments in GSM8k add clear insights beyond addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "With this level of specificity required, it is no longer necessary to use prompting to solve the tasks (we already solved it with our brain when providing the prompt and can totally write a program for it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Some generalizations (other than length) from the demonstration to the actual task would make the method much more useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But so far it is unclear whether this technique can be scaled to more complicated problems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 39, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I found the paper somewhat hard to follow and the writing style rather confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "For instance, I did not understand what \u201cdefining algorithms as skills\u201d means exactly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "This is mentioned several times in the manuscript but it looks to just boil down to using the aforementioned prompting technique.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper should make a clear statement somewhere that the language model is not re-optimized and no weight updates actually happen, since the name \u201cin-context learning\u201d might be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The main drawback of the manuscript is that technical details of the experimentation are largely missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors do not mention explicitly how many examples they provide in the prompt for each task, whether it is only one or multiple.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Details of the baseline are not provided either, such as which prompts are used, making the comparison between different ideas harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Usage of space is not well-balanced, the authors dedicated a full subsection for explaining \u201ctwo-number addition\u201d (section 3.1) and only a few paragraphs for all the others.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In particular, the algorithm for parity is not even mentioned but its results appear in table 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Section 4 shows that we can \u201cteach\u201d multiple problems at a time, such as subtraction and addition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This looks to perform worse than showing each problem separately, therefore I am not exactly sure what conclusions can be drawn from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "While it is undoubtedly interesting that \u201chard\u201d reasoning capabilities emerge from LLMs with the right prompt, the problems faced in the paper seem only to be \u201ctoy problems\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The fact that this prompting fails on logical tasks (section 6) poses serious concerns on its applicability on harder problems other than simple mathematical operations and, therefore, its real-world utility.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Clarity can certainly be improved (see above).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The technical quality and novelty is scarce, there is not actual learning but just interventions on the input prompt.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I would like to raise a concern about reproducibility of the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I wanted to reproduce some of the experiments but found out that OpenAI\u2019s Codex code-davinci-002 model is not publicly available yet, which is somewhat awkward.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The best I could do was to try reproducing OOD results with the text-davinci-002 model, which exhibited similar reasoning processes but I got results (for 14 digits) that are much lower, i.e. 30%, than what is shown in Figure 3, i.e. approximately 97-98%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I tried with 10 samples where each addend has 14 digits, while providing the model with a single example of summation with 3-digit numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Perhaps the authors can explain why this happened?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Is that only due to a model difference or too few examples in the prompt?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Please find here a txt file showing the prompt I used (the same as in section G.2) and the response I got from the text-davinci-002 model: https://file.io/vqUWwuH8uZpq", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The technical contribution is poor, i.e. no learning whatsoever, and the manuscript is not precise and does not present experimental details very clearly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Lastly, a final concern about real-world utility as the prompting does not perform well on harder problems (logical tasks).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The algorithmic prompting method conceptually requires that we know how to break down the target operation (like parity) into recurrent steps and provide traces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "With this level of specificity required, it is no longer necessary to use prompting to solve the tasks (we already solved it with our brain when providing the prompt and can totally write a program for it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Some generalizations (other than length) from the demonstration to the actual task would make the method much more useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But so far it is unclear whether this technique can be scaled to more complicated problems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 40, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "No major weaknesses identified.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Typos:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In Figure 2, the performance of Adapter is 21.00, not 20.46", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The \"v\" subscript is missing in Eq.5", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 22, "text": "Cons:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Significance of results:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There are no standard deviations for any results in the paper, which makes it hard to assess significance of many results, esp.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "since some of the performance gaps are small.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "For instance, in table 6, authors have a 0.4 BLEU discrepancy in their replication of full fine-tuning performance but draw conclusions on MAM being best based on a 0.2 BLEU gap.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The gap is bigger when comparing MAM to methods not introduced by the authors, but still.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The story makes sense but I am not 100% confident in the robustness of the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Using two decimal numbers for tasks (e.g: XSum) also gives a false impression of precision.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I understand fine-tuning on some of these tasks (MT / XSUM) can be resource-intensive but having standard deviations in even a subset of the experiments would be useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Interpretation of Figure 4 in Section 5.2 and effectiveness of MAM Adapters for encoder models", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "The authors highlight that while existing methods perform well on MNLI/SST, they are underwhelming on en-ro / XSum.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "They conclude that this means existing parameter-efficient transfer learning (PETL) approaches are not great for higher-resource / more challenging tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, I would point out that this data can also support a different conclusion: existing PETL approaches work well for encoder-only models but are not great for encoder-decoder models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Including the T5 datapoint is also not very relevant since in that case Superglue is treated as a single task (w/ one adapter).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Answering \u201cFor which tasks / architectures do PETL methods perform well\u201d is an interesting question in itself and I feel like this section expedites this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "It is likely that both the architecture and #datapoints in the task matter.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The author\u2019s choice at the end of section 4.2 is to focus on XSum / en-ro MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "It would be great to highlight that not only are those higher-resource tasks, but they are also generative ones, and thus all the conclusions of this paper might not apply widely to encoder models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Indeed, the results of MAM adapters in table 2 are quite mixed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Right now the paper is claiming more generality than deserved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Two possible fixes here: (1) Make claims less general and specific to enc-dec models, (2) Conduct more experiments on encoder-only models", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Writing:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "The writing is subpar right now.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Example: section 4.4: \u201cits counterpart at attention\u201d -> \u201cits attention counterpart\u201d, \u201cFFN can better utilize modification at larger capacities\u201d -> modifications or \u201cmodification of the FFN is better at larger capacities\u201d, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Minor: The citation style is often wrong for the sentence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Use \\citet more often.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "Minor: Replace \u201cFor classifications\u201d with \u201cfor classification tasks\u201d in the Appendix (twice)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Typos: Section 5 name Discussions -> Discussion ; Appendix A3 Learning -> Learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "Minor:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "For Figure 2, I feel like the full fine-tuning number should come from the original baseline, not the replication.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "The protocol in 4.5 for choosing the scaling parameter for Scaled PA is a bit surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "I would just suggest that it should be an hyperparameter, the current description makes it seem like it is based on hyperparam tuning for LoRA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "It seems to me that 4.5 is about composition function and \\delta h functional form since LoRA changes both.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "Revised score upwards after response, see below", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "The set of experiments is well-chosen overall, but the lack of standard deviations and the focus on generative tasks / enc-decoder models makes the results less robust and general.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "The writing would benefit from more work as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Table 1-3 shows that Charformer is generally on par with Byte-level T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Sometimes Charformer wins; other times Byte-level T5 wins.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Either with small differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "In all cases, Charformer is significantly worse than subword models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The rescaled version Charformer_S is able to achieve comparable and sometimes better accuracy than subword models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(Con) It is not clear if the proposed GBST module learns meaningful subword tokenization.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "examples that highly scored subwords align with human intuition, or quantitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "how the learned tokenized subwords align with established methods, or how they are better at solving lexical tasks, would help.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It would help if the authors can provide more and clearer technical details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "(Con) No code provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Providing code will greatly help reproducibility and help clarify many technical details not fully described in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 49, "text": "However, some important design and implementation details are missing or confusing which are not helped by the lack of source code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, it does not learn to segment the input to different chunks to be fed in the contextualizer but rather downsamples the sequence in a fixed way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Second, the soft selection at each position is performed over blocks of different size that correspond to that position and does not take into account global information e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "by computing scores of all possible segmentations to maximize for the most likely one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Studies that focus on tokenization like the ones cited below evaluate on tasks where tokenization is important for reaching state-of-the-art performance like machine translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Table 1-3 shows that Charformer is generally on par with Byte-level T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Sometimes Charformer wins; other times Byte-level T5 wins.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Either with small differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "In all cases, Charformer is significantly worse than subword models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The rescaled version Charformer_S is able to achieve comparable and sometimes better accuracy than subword models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(Con) It is not clear if the proposed GBST module learns meaningful subword tokenization.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "examples that highly scored subwords align with human intuition, or quantitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "how the learned tokenized subwords align with established methods, or how they are better at solving lexical tasks, would help.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It would help if the authors can provide more and clearer technical details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "(Con) No code provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Providing code will greatly help reproducibility and help clarify many technical details not fully described in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 49, "text": "However, some important design and implementation details are missing or confusing which are not helped by the lack of source code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "(minor) The technical novelty of this paper is only GBST, a dense layer over n-grams, while training setting follows ByT5, and the best SBase model is also an implementation of the deep encoder concept.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(minor) T5 seems much better than SBase under Zero-Shot settings, though it takes more computation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This is important since in the ByT5 paper all the model variants have much heavier weight in encoders, and there is also an ablation study to prove that heavier encoder can improve performance a lot.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Thus it\u2019s a bit strange that the best settings of both models are not used for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(Mixed) Experimental results do not show strong improvements on accuracy metrics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Table 1-3 shows that Charformer is generally on par with Byte-level T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Sometimes Charformer wins; other times Byte-level T5 wins.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Either with small differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "In all cases, Charformer is significantly worse than subword models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The rescaled version Charformer_S is able to achieve comparable and sometimes better accuracy than subword models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, the re-scaling is not tied to Charformer and can be applied to other character-level models as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It is not clear if the gain is because of the proposed GBST or solely due to the re-scaling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "(Con) It is not clear if the proposed GBST module learns meaningful subword tokenization.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Better qualitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "examples that highly scored subwords align with human intuition, or quantitative evidence, e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "how the learned tokenized subwords align with established methods, or how they are better at solving lexical tasks, would help.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(Con) Many technical details are unclear/confusing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It would help if the authors can provide more and clearer technical details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "(Con) No code provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Providing code will greatly help reproducibility and help clarify many technical details not fully described in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "All the tasks in the paper seem to be classification tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 49, "text": "However, some important design and implementation details are missing or confusing which are not helped by the lack of source code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear if the method works for other languages.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The evaluations are based on base models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you try large models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Besides, it would be impactful if this paper showed that GBST module could actually generalize in other kinds of models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, it does not learn to segment the input to different chunks to be fed in the contextualizer but rather downsamples the sequence in a fixed way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Second, the soft selection at each position is performed over blocks of different size that correspond to that position and does not take into account global information e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "by computing scores of all possible segmentations to maximize for the most likely one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Studies that focus on tokenization like the ones cited below evaluate on tasks where tokenization is important for reaching state-of-the-art performance like machine translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "(minor) The technical novelty of this paper is only GBST, a dense layer over n-grams, while training setting follows ByT5, and the best SBase model is also an implementation of the deep encoder concept.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(minor) T5 seems much better than SBase under Zero-Shot settings, though it takes more computation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This is important since in the ByT5 paper all the model variants have much heavier weight in encoders, and there is also an ablation study to prove that heavier encoder can improve performance a lot.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Thus it\u2019s a bit strange that the best settings of both models are not used for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "My main concern is that the proposed method does not seem to be satisfying the criteria for a proper tokenization method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, it does not learn to segment the input to different chunks to be fed in the contextualizer but rather downsamples the sequence in a fixed way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The latent subword representations could be captured by combing multiple convolutions and a simple pooling function per position, which makes the method seem less novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Second, the soft selection at each position is performed over blocks of different size that correspond to that position and does not take into account global information e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "by computing scores of all possible segmentations to maximize for the most likely one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The current framing would suggest comparison to existing tokenization methods like BPE or recent optimized ones to the downstream task [1,2] in a controlled setting (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The results are not that good compared to pure character-level models on average which makes results less exciting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Studies that focus on tokenization like the ones cited below evaluate on tasks where tokenization is important for reaching state-of-the-art performance like machine translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It might be worth evaluating there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "My main concerns are regarding its novelty, framing, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear if the method works for other languages.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The evaluations are based on base models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you try large models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Besides, it would be impactful if this paper showed that GBST module could actually generalize in other kinds of models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 41, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "(minor) The technical novelty of this paper is only GBST, a dense layer over n-grams, while training setting follows ByT5, and the best SBase model is also an implementation of the deep encoder concept.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The scalability of the model is not shown.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(minor) T5 seems much better than SBase under Zero-Shot settings, though it takes more computation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "From the last sentence of 3.1 Baselines, it seems that the ByT5 model used for comparison is unscaled, and I couldn\u2019t find in the paper about structure of this ByT5 (sorry if I missed that).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This is important since in the ByT5 paper all the model variants have much heavier weight in encoders, and there is also an ablation study to prove that heavier encoder can improve performance a lot.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Thus it\u2019s a bit strange that the best settings of both models are not used for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The comparison seems not between the best setting (deep encoder, shallow decoder) of both baseline and this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The performance is not surprising, though the model indeed saves model size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear if the method works for other languages.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, character-based models tend to achieve similar performance compared with word-based ones. Would this method has advantage over the character-based baseline then?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The evaluations are based on base models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you try large models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Besides, it would be impactful if this paper showed that GBST module could actually generalize in other kinds of models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Some limitations of this work include the lack of experiments on more diverse architectures like encoder-decoders to make sure that the proposed technique not only works on autoregressive GPT-type models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Moreover, only one approach for entity integration is explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Interesting choice of the margin loss for entity prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "More motivation should be added into why this loss instead of the cross-entropy loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optionally, an experiment comparing the performance of the model when trained with the cross entropy -vs- the margin loss would be interesting to have.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "At inference time, you are forced to put entity labels on the input text as a preprocessing step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Are the experiments on QA really zero-shot?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The examples presented in Appendix are not dummy as they reflect true questions and answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses: The paper leaves open several questions that should really be explored and discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4. The metrics used for the LAMA knowledge probing should be explained in the paper once (at least expand the abbreviations so readers can understand).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "6. Related to the above point, it seems like KALM base has more parameters than GPT-2 base (and even GPT-2 Large) and yet this aspect is not accounted for/investigated in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In fact, the paper says \u201c(KALM) more efficiently packs practical knowledge into its parameters \u2026 12L KALM is very close \u2026 to 24L GPT-2 Large\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This seems to be very misleading information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Since Transformers are essentially a form of graph networks, I\u2019m not sure entity embeddings can be treated separately from the model parameters since one can view those as edges part of the graph.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "7. I appreciate the authors efforts to test on a variety of benchmarks and I understand that training these huge models is not easy, but to me, the essence of the paper (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors say that one can discount the entity embeddings, but can we really?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Aren\u2019t they part of the model\u2019s \u2018representation\u2019 even if the inference does not use all of them in a single forward pass?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Several neural net architectures exist (including large-scale LMs) that do not use all inference paths but still count them in the total params.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The BERT vs GPT-2 example provided in the rebuttal is only a difference of 26k tokens (still significant!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "but here we're talking about a few hundred million parameters!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "At the very least, I think the true sizes of the models must be acknowledged and one can add the point on entity embeddings vs 'brain' parameters as a caveat, but it seems scientifically inaccurate to me to claim otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "To be clear, I don't think the size issue detracts much from the main contribution of adding entity embeddings here (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "this work may still be of interest even if the size of model is larger), but the current version of the paper has several claims about size savings that seem incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Some limitations of this work include the lack of experiments on more diverse architectures like encoder-decoders to make sure that the proposed technique not only works on autoregressive GPT-type models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Moreover, only one approach for entity integration is explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Interesting choice of the margin loss for entity prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "More motivation should be added into why this loss instead of the cross-entropy loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optionally, an experiment comparing the performance of the model when trained with the cross entropy -vs- the margin loss would be interesting to have.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "At inference time, you are forced to put entity labels on the input text as a preprocessing step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Are the experiments on QA really zero-shot?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The examples presented in Appendix are not dummy as they reflect true questions and answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Some limitations of this work include the lack of experiments on more diverse architectures like encoder-decoders to make sure that the proposed technique not only works on autoregressive GPT-type models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Moreover, only one approach for entity integration is explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Interesting choice of the margin loss for entity prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "More motivation should be added into why this loss instead of the cross-entropy loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Optionally, an experiment comparing the performance of the model when trained with the cross entropy -vs- the margin loss would be interesting to have.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "At inference time, you are forced to put entity labels on the input text as a preprocessing step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Are the experiments on QA really zero-shot?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The examples presented in Appendix are not dummy as they reflect true questions and answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I would like to see how much KALM can transfer to downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors already show zero-shot QA results, but it would be better to see fine-tuning results on knowledge-intensive tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Even though KALM outperforms GPT-2, it is still unclear for me the advantages of KALM compared to Entities as Experts (or EaE, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07202).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, EaE outperforms KALM on the LAMA probing tasks, and EaE also outperforms T5-11B when finetuned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors mentioned fuzzy frequency-based entity matching is better than precise entity linking (e.g. EaE) on page 3, but EaE outperforms KALM on LAMA probing tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Based on the experimental results, precise entity linking seems more effective to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It would be helpful if the authors can provide a more thorough comparison with EaE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although 4 out of the 11 KILT tasks are already included in the main paper, most of them are LAMA knowledge probing tasks or zero-shot QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is still unclear how much and how robustly KALM can transfer to other downstream tasks with fine-tuning (e.g. Wizard-of-Wikipedia, FEVER, QA with fine-tuning).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For instance, CorefBERT paper (which uses a similar idea but as you mentioned it use bidirecitonal attention) shows its transferability on QUOREF, six extractive QA benchmarks, DocRED, FEVER, five coreference resolution benchmarks and GLUE, which can convince me to choose CorefBERT over BERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses: The paper leaves open several questions that should really be explored and discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4. The metrics used for the LAMA knowledge probing should be explained in the paper once (at least expand the abbreviations so readers can understand).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "6. Related to the above point, it seems like KALM base has more parameters than GPT-2 base (and even GPT-2 Large) and yet this aspect is not accounted for/investigated in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In fact, the paper says \u201c(KALM) more efficiently packs practical knowledge into its parameters \u2026 12L KALM is very close \u2026 to 24L GPT-2 Large\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This seems to be very misleading information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Since Transformers are essentially a form of graph networks, I\u2019m not sure entity embeddings can be treated separately from the model parameters since one can view those as edges part of the graph.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "7. I appreciate the authors efforts to test on a variety of benchmarks and I understand that training these huge models is not easy, but to me, the essence of the paper (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors say that one can discount the entity embeddings, but can we really?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Aren\u2019t they part of the model\u2019s \u2018representation\u2019 even if the inference does not use all of them in a single forward pass?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Several neural net architectures exist (including large-scale LMs) that do not use all inference paths but still count them in the total params.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The BERT vs GPT-2 example provided in the rebuttal is only a difference of 26k tokens (still significant!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "but here we're talking about a few hundred million parameters!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "At the very least, I think the true sizes of the models must be acknowledged and one can add the point on entity embeddings vs 'brain' parameters as a caveat, but it seems scientifically inaccurate to me to claim otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "To be clear, I don't think the size issue detracts much from the main contribution of adding entity embeddings here (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "this work may still be of interest even if the size of model is larger), but the current version of the paper has several claims about size savings that seem incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses: The paper leaves open several questions that should really be explored and discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4. The metrics used for the LAMA knowledge probing should be explained in the paper once (at least expand the abbreviations so readers can understand).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "5. Table 3: The parameter sizes indicated are misleading.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Similarly base KALM has ~550M params not 100M.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "6. Related to the above point, it seems like KALM base has more parameters than GPT-2 base (and even GPT-2 Large) and yet this aspect is not accounted for/investigated in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In fact, the paper says \u201c(KALM) more efficiently packs practical knowledge into its parameters \u2026 12L KALM is very close \u2026 to 24L GPT-2 Large\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This seems to be very misleading information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Since Transformers are essentially a form of graph networks, I\u2019m not sure entity embeddings can be treated separately from the model parameters since one can view those as edges part of the graph.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "7. I appreciate the authors efforts to test on a variety of benchmarks and I understand that training these huge models is not easy, but to me, the essence of the paper (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors say that one can discount the entity embeddings, but can we really?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Aren\u2019t they part of the model\u2019s \u2018representation\u2019 even if the inference does not use all of them in a single forward pass?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Several neural net architectures exist (including large-scale LMs) that do not use all inference paths but still count them in the total params.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "The BERT vs GPT-2 example provided in the rebuttal is only a difference of 26k tokens (still significant!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "but here we're talking about a few hundred million parameters!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "At the very least, I think the true sizes of the models must be acknowledged and one can add the point on entity embeddings vs 'brain' parameters as a caveat, but it seems scientifically inaccurate to me to claim otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "To be clear, I don't think the size issue detracts much from the main contribution of adding entity embeddings here (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "this work may still be of interest even if the size of model is larger), but the current version of the paper has several claims about size savings that seem incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I would like to see how much KALM can transfer to downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors already show zero-shot QA results, but it would be better to see fine-tuning results on knowledge-intensive tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Even though KALM outperforms GPT-2, it is still unclear for me the advantages of KALM compared to Entities as Experts (or EaE, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07202).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, EaE outperforms KALM on the LAMA probing tasks, and EaE also outperforms T5-11B when finetuned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors mentioned fuzzy frequency-based entity matching is better than precise entity linking (e.g. EaE) on page 3, but EaE outperforms KALM on LAMA probing tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Based on the experimental results, precise entity linking seems more effective to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It would be helpful if the authors can provide a more thorough comparison with EaE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although 4 out of the 11 KILT tasks are already included in the main paper, most of them are LAMA knowledge probing tasks or zero-shot QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is still unclear how much and how robustly KALM can transfer to other downstream tasks with fine-tuning (e.g. Wizard-of-Wikipedia, FEVER, QA with fine-tuning).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For instance, CorefBERT paper (which uses a similar idea but as you mentioned it use bidirecitonal attention) shows its transferability on QUOREF, six extractive QA benchmarks, DocRED, FEVER, five coreference resolution benchmarks and GLUE, which can convince me to choose CorefBERT over BERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 42, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I would like to see how much KALM can transfer to downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors already show zero-shot QA results, but it would be better to see fine-tuning results on knowledge-intensive tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Even though KALM outperforms GPT-2, it is still unclear for me the advantages of KALM compared to Entities as Experts (or EaE, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07202).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, EaE outperforms KALM on the LAMA probing tasks, and EaE also outperforms T5-11B when finetuned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors mentioned fuzzy frequency-based entity matching is better than precise entity linking (e.g. EaE) on page 3, but EaE outperforms KALM on LAMA probing tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Based on the experimental results, precise entity linking seems more effective to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It would be helpful if the authors can provide a more thorough comparison with EaE.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although 4 out of the 11 KILT tasks are already included in the main paper, most of them are LAMA knowledge probing tasks or zero-shot QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It is still unclear how much and how robustly KALM can transfer to other downstream tasks with fine-tuning (e.g. Wizard-of-Wikipedia, FEVER, QA with fine-tuning).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For instance, CorefBERT paper (which uses a similar idea but as you mentioned it use bidirecitonal attention) shows its transferability on QUOREF, six extractive QA benchmarks, DocRED, FEVER, five coreference resolution benchmarks and GLUE, which can convince me to choose CorefBERT over BERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 43, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "I believe this can be a useful technique but before considering such an approach as a general strategy, more theoretical insights should be provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The authors report on ablation studies that demonstrate some empirical benefits.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "However, until I see more theoretical analysis on how the method improves convergence or lead to better losses by smoothing out the output of the objective function, I remain skeptical of the usefulness of this as a general training method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 3, "text": "The idea of dynamic temperature scaling has been tried in other works and tasks (e.g., attended temperature scaling).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 4, "text": "The paper parameterizes this mechanism with DNNs for the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The experiments are not solid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "The baseline only includes Mos, which is not very strong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "To validate whether this approach works with other LM of high-order attention or self-attention, a better baseline model is required (e.g., transformer, GPT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I would like to see this technique can help either NLU or NLG tasks, instead of just pure modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The case analysis section needs more examples instead of just cherry-picking few.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 43, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "I believe this can be a useful technique but before considering such an approach as a general strategy, more theoretical insights should be provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The authors report on ablation studies that demonstrate some empirical benefits.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "However, until I see more theoretical analysis on how the method improves convergence or lead to better losses by smoothing out the output of the objective function, I remain skeptical of the usefulness of this as a general training method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I vote for an acceptance, if the authors can polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "- Last but not least, the paper can be improved a lot if the authors can thoroughly polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 43, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "The idea of dynamic temperature scaling has been tried in other works and tasks (e.g., attended temperature scaling).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 4, "text": "The paper parameterizes this mechanism with DNNs for the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Though the idea looks interesting, it fails to explain why the scaling is better than other dynamic temperature scaling frameworks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The experiments are not solid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "The baseline only includes Mos, which is not very strong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "To validate whether this approach works with other LM of high-order attention or self-attention, a better baseline model is required (e.g., transformer, GPT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I would like to see this technique can help either NLU or NLG tasks, instead of just pure modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The case analysis section needs more examples instead of just cherry-picking few.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I vote for an acceptance, if the authors can polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "- Last but not least, the paper can be improved a lot if the authors can thoroughly polish the writing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 44, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "This is not novel at all with many researchers have thought about this but could not execute the idea because of a lack of resources, higher API costs, and non-availability of open-source LLMs like PaLM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, it is saying that humans can look at a problem, and think of a solution many times, and the one that humans conclude the most is the most likely output and use that to think and train themselves to memorize that solution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I don't agree with this point at all (of course I am happy to listen to why authors thought about this).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Research where such an important claim is being made (LLMs are able to self-improve) needs to be shown on multiple models (small to very large) and just showing the results on a 540B model does not prove the point that large LMs can self-improve by training on the samples that they produced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "When generating outputs with let's say a smaller model like T5-large, where the accuracy will not be that high, the model outputs cannot be used to train them as most of the time, the answers will be incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "So my guess is that this approach only works for PaLM or similar-sized models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The paper is original in its implementation but not in ideation (very obvious extension that was not conducted by a lot of researchers due to lack of resources).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "So the validity of the method cannot be tested/justified by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 44, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: Access to the pretrained model is not available, and the sheer size of the models make reproducibility impossible for the vast majority of labs, universities, and companies alike.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper presented an approach followed with empirical evidence, but did not provide why such an approach works in the first place (i.e., approach is not backed well enough).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Following are my concerns on the approach:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These experiments are not there in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Like I said, there were a lot of ablations in the paper where some of them proved some points presented in previous work (like Table-7) and others are new (Figure-3).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "On the new ones, the paper failed to provide good discussion on the outcomes/observations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, in Figure-3, both step-by-step and few-shot with step-by-step are converging for total samples at 80.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Why is that the case?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 44, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "This is not novel at all with many researchers have thought about this but could not execute the idea because of a lack of resources, higher API costs, and non-availability of open-source LLMs like PaLM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, it is saying that humans can look at a problem, and think of a solution many times, and the one that humans conclude the most is the most likely output and use that to think and train themselves to memorize that solution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I don't agree with this point at all (of course I am happy to listen to why authors thought about this).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Research where such an important claim is being made (LLMs are able to self-improve) needs to be shown on multiple models (small to very large) and just showing the results on a 540B model does not prove the point that large LMs can self-improve by training on the samples that they produced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "When generating outputs with let's say a smaller model like T5-large, where the accuracy will not be that high, the model outputs cannot be used to train them as most of the time, the answers will be incorrect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "So my guess is that this approach only works for PaLM or similar-sized models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The paper is original in its implementation but not in ideation (very obvious extension that was not conducted by a lot of researchers due to lack of resources).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "So the validity of the method cannot be tested/justified by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Moreover, the approach is not novel in its ideation but only novel in its implementation (as the method was not implemented by others due to resource constraints) and hence I would suggest showing the novelty of the idea on more models and datasets to justify the claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The paper presented an approach followed with empirical evidence, but did not provide why such an approach works in the first place (i.e., approach is not backed well enough).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Following are my concerns on the approach:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These experiments are not there in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Like I said, there were a lot of ablations in the paper where some of them proved some points presented in previous work (like Table-7) and others are new (Figure-3).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "On the new ones, the paper failed to provide good discussion on the outcomes/observations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, in Figure-3, both step-by-step and few-shot with step-by-step are converging for total samples at 80.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Why is that the case?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 45, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The promise of a better sentence scorer is potentially very impactful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors chose to evaluate sentence re-ranking on multiple generated candidates for NMT and for ASR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have several concerns about these experiments that made them not particularly convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, the experiments were, in my view, are a bit thin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, the gain from SLM over BERT is not very large, and there is still a large gap between the oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Furthermore, while several baselines were provided, there have been many architectures recently proposed, and probably more comparisons are needed to fully justify the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As some examples, it could have been good to compare with T5, BART, XLNet, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I am also not sure about the setup for the ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Why were the ablations not performed on all the datasets?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I think this could have been addressed by putting a final row in Table 2, with SLM with one-third of the compute so that the compute is matched.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "It is fine if it does not win in all settings under the same compute, but in this case it would be good to be transparent about how much compute is needed to get the same performance, and whether there are performance gains in multiple different compute-matched settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Finally, I am not sure why NMT and ASR were chosen as the experimental settings in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It could have been stronger if the paper had chosen a language understanding benchmark like GLUE, which could be a better fit for pretrained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I have voted for rejection primarily because the experiments are not particularly convincing, but if enough of the questions/concerns that I had are addressed in the rebuttal I will be happy to change my score.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "The authors were up-front about the additional computational cost of their method, though additional experiments would be needed for the empirical gains in compute-matched settings to be convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "IWSLT14 might be a small dataset, but I don't think German, Spanish, Italian, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "should be described as \"low-resource\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 45, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The promise of a better sentence scorer is potentially very impactful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors chose to evaluate sentence re-ranking on multiple generated candidates for NMT and for ASR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have several concerns about these experiments that made them not particularly convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "First, the experiments were, in my view, are a bit thin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, the gain from SLM over BERT is not very large, and there is still a large gap between the oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Furthermore, while several baselines were provided, there have been many architectures recently proposed, and probably more comparisons are needed to fully justify the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As some examples, it could have been good to compare with T5, BART, XLNet, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I am also not sure about the setup for the ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Why were the ablations not performed on all the datasets?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I think this could have been addressed by putting a final row in Table 2, with SLM with one-third of the compute so that the compute is matched.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "It is fine if it does not win in all settings under the same compute, but in this case it would be good to be transparent about how much compute is needed to get the same performance, and whether there are performance gains in multiple different compute-matched settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Finally, I am not sure why NMT and ASR were chosen as the experimental settings in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It could have been stronger if the paper had chosen a language understanding benchmark like GLUE, which could be a better fit for pretrained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I have voted for rejection primarily because the experiments are not particularly convincing, but if enough of the questions/concerns that I had are addressed in the rebuttal I will be happy to change my score.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "The authors were up-front about the additional computational cost of their method, though additional experiments would be needed for the empirical gains in compute-matched settings to be convincing for me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are still many grammar errors; please work with a copyeditor or native English speaker on any camera-ready.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I have concerns re: the clarity and significance of this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Firstly, there are two uncited works that have proposed one-pass solutions to token-wise probabilities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, the first work's proposed T-TA has many similarities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I appreciate some of the analyses which complement past work, in particular A.3.1's discussion comparing sentence vs. fixed-length pre-training, and 5.3 showing SLM and MLM's similar scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Section 5.1 and 5.2 are unsatisfying though, as increasing the number of masked tokens would be expected to be poor given the mismatch with pretraining (in BERT, masking 15%).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 45, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The main idea seems like fairly straightforward variant of the XLNet architecture, not too dissimilar from other past proposals to modify its base architecture by changing the nature of the \"streams\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "IWSLT14 might be a small dataset, but I don't think German, Spanish, Italian, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "should be described as \"low-resource\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are still many grammar errors; please work with a copyeditor or native English speaker on any camera-ready.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I have concerns re: the clarity and significance of this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Firstly, there are two uncited works that have proposed one-pass solutions to token-wise probabilities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, the first work's proposed T-TA has many similarities:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors should also acknowledge [18] in section 5.3, where an almost identical analysis is performed, even down to the choice of length and plot (Figure 3 here is largely [18]'s Figures 3+4).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I appreciate some of the analyses which complement past work, in particular A.3.1's discussion comparing sentence vs. fixed-length pre-training, and 5.3 showing SLM and MLM's similar scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Section 5.1 and 5.2 are unsatisfying though, as increasing the number of masked tokens would be expected to be poor given the mismatch with pretraining (in BERT, masking 15%).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The method generally assumes that the decision of drop can be done independently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, the positions of salient information would usually exhibit certain patterns such as appearing close to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, splitting by a sentence for passages is natural.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I wonder about the additional training cost of SpanDrop compared to the standard training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The expectation of length is the same for SpanDrop and Beta-SpanDrop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the expectation of length\u2019s square would be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, I guess their computational overhead will be also different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The sampling cost would be almost negligible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Several technical details are not discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What's the tech novelty of this work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the authors focus on the \"learning problems for long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output\", the uniqueness of this kind of problems is not clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In most (if not all) real-world sequence classification and prediction problems, not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output; otherwise, the problems will become much easier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Experiments need to be improved and enhanced, e.g.,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Although these approaches aim to approximate the original pairwise interaction with lower cost and are often interested in still capturing the interactions between every pair of input elements (e.g., the long sequence benchmark proposed by Tay et al., 2020), they can still be directly applied to long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The four NLP datasets used in experiments are not representative tasks for long sequences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The method generally assumes that the decision of drop can be done independently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, the positions of salient information would usually exhibit certain patterns such as appearing close to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, splitting by a sentence for passages is natural.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I wonder about the additional training cost of SpanDrop compared to the standard training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The expectation of length is the same for SpanDrop and Beta-SpanDrop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the expectation of length\u2019s square would be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, I guess their computational overhead will be also different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The sampling cost would be almost negligible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The method generally assumes that the decision of drop can be done independently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, the positions of salient information would usually exhibit certain patterns such as appearing close to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As the name of the method is SpanDrop, it would be much interesting if various selection/split methods of spans have been investigated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, splitting by a sentence for passages is natural.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I wonder about the additional training cost of SpanDrop compared to the standard training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The expectation of length is the same for SpanDrop and Beta-SpanDrop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, the expectation of length\u2019s square would be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Therefore, I guess their computational overhead will be also different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The sampling cost would be almost negligible.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It's not surprising that this data augmentation is effective, especially for the catfinding task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Sec1. I don't quite understand why when in data the contributing portion is small or sparse, then the problem is \"underspecified\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It's still specified by the small contributing portion I think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Sec1. \"the data does not sufficiently define the goal for statistical models\", I don't understand what global is here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I have two major concerns", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(1) limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "(2) The lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Several technical details are not discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What's the tech novelty of this work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the authors focus on the \"learning problems for long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output\", the uniqueness of this kind of problems is not clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In most (if not all) real-world sequence classification and prediction problems, not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output; otherwise, the problems will become much easier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Experiments need to be improved and enhanced, e.g.,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Although these approaches aim to approximate the original pairwise interaction with lower cost and are often interested in still capturing the interactions between every pair of input elements (e.g., the long sequence benchmark proposed by Tay et al., 2020), they can still be directly applied to long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The four NLP datasets used in experiments are not representative tasks for long sequences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Several technical details are not discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What's the tech novelty of this work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the authors focus on the \"learning problems for long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output\", the uniqueness of this kind of problems is not clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In most (if not all) real-world sequence classification and prediction problems, not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output; otherwise, the problems will become much easier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Experiments need to be improved and enhanced, e.g.,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Although these approaches aim to approximate the original pairwise interaction with lower cost and are often interested in still capturing the interactions between every pair of input elements (e.g., the long sequence benchmark proposed by Tay et al., 2020), they can still be directly applied to long sequences where not all input elements contribute equally to the desired output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The four NLP datasets used in experiments are not representative tasks for long sequences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It's not surprising that this data augmentation is effective, especially for the catfinding task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Sec1. I don't quite understand why when in data the contributing portion is small or sparse, then the problem is \"underspecified\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It's still specified by the small contributing portion I think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Sec1. \"the data does not sufficiently define the goal for statistical models\", I don't understand what global is here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I have two major concerns", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(1) limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "(2) The lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 46, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The procedure is fairly simple, and similar ideas have been used for regularizing models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I find the solution similar to word dropout, and would be interesting to see comparison to that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not clear if similar benefits can be obtained by using word dropout at training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The experimental results on real datasets are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It's not surprising that this data augmentation is effective, especially for the catfinding task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While the improvement over electra-base is consistent, it's not large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Sec1. I don't quite understand why when in data the contributing portion is small or sparse, then the problem is \"underspecified\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It's still specified by the small contributing portion I think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Sec1. \"the data does not sufficiently define the goal for statistical models\", I don't understand what global is here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I have two major concerns", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(1) limited novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "(2) The lack of comparison of other baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I know that SCANDROP is easier to implement, but that does not mean you don't need to compare with other algorithms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 23, "text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 61, "text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Some of the language in the paper is a little off-putting as it seems to be disconnected (in my opinion) from deeply understanding the space of natural language translation (unstructured ambiguous languages) vs. programming language translation (structured unambiguous languages).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There are many other such examples that seem to demonstrate the authors perhaps need to do a bit deeper literature review before making such bizarre claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example, the word \"transpilation\" never even appears in the paper, yet the whole paper is about transpilation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Do the authors not know about the field of \"transpilation\" and that they are working in this space?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source-to-source_compiler", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "These minor issues aside, I actually like the approach and am favorable toward the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 61, "text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "If ground truth parallel data were available for this task, then fine-tuning a model on this data would naturally result in gains in performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Since this parallel data is extremely scarce, the authors propose to utilize an automated test generation suite to build this parallel data from the model translations itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have 2 concerns with this proposal:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "It requires an automated unit test generation tool to be available for at least one of the languages translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unit tests generation is an active area of research and tools are available only for limited sets of languages and primarily for modern languages such as Java, Python, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "An important premise of automated code translation -- which the authors mention in their introduction and ethical concerns section -- is about modernizing legacy languages such as COBOL, where automated test generation systems might not be available at all.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The proposed method therefore, in my opinion, is bottlenecked on the availability of automated unit test generation tools, and is not applicable when they are not available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Automated unit test generation tools can themselves generate incorrect test cases -- test cases testing the incorrect method, incorrectly testing the method resulting in false positives or false negatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "AthenaTest [1], for instance, works better than EvoSuite and generates correct unit tests for the intended method only 16.21% of the time as per [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "26.71% of the times the test case generated by AthenaTest test for the wrong behavior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Other factors such as dynamic typing (as in Python) [2] or the use of more complicated data structures (such as Generics in Java [3]) further affects the effectiveness of these test generation systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would have been nice to get some insights into the quality of test cases generated and how this affects the performance of the translation model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "This effect, I believe, would be especially pronounced in cases where the code translated is a piece of more complicated codebase and not standalone functions that can be compiled in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "The authors utilize EvoSuite more as a test data generation tool than a test case generation tool.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "EvoSuite generates test cases for Java, while this work trains models for Java, Python and C++.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Therefore, if I understand correctly, the way test cases are created for Python and C++ is by using the expected input/output pairs and assert statements generated for corresponding Java code, and putting these in a template for the other languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I'm not completely convinced that this is an ideal strategy that can generalize to a broader set of languages or for more complicated pieces of code.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "For example: if the source code implements a Java class and the generated test operates on this class and their methods, how would this be translated to Python test without changing the procedure for Python test case generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "This further reduces the applicability of this approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "If I understand correctly, the evaluation used in this work includes a reference implementation and a translated implementation, which are tested to generate the same output given the same input?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This creates problems as authors note in Figure 4, where the gold translation is equivalent to the source code only on a small domain, while the model generated translation, though correct and better is incorrectly marked wrong.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "This, to me, indicates an issue with the utilized test set where gold implementations might not be correct translations, thereby affecting the scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "Contemporary works in code generation and translation [4,5] have utilized expected input / output pairs to test the performance of the code generation system, and I wonder why the authors here chose to compare the performance against a reference implementation and not against input-output pairs directly.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Additionally, we don't know how prevalent the problem of incorrect reference translations is in the utilized test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "However, I found the following weaknesses, resulting in my recommendation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 60, "text": "Bottleneck on the availability of test generation tools thus limiting the number of languages it can be applied to.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 61, "text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The method is trained on Java functions that can be executed in isolation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 47, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "One of the biggest limitations of the approach seems to be around how unit tests are translated between the languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper downplays this step in its expositions by simply saying that simple scripts can translate unit tests.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A related question is if tests could be translated with some of the baseline models such as Transcoder/DOBF and if such an idea was considered and was/wasn\u2019t successful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is not completely clear if the data filtering techniques (to only include functions for which tests can be generated) also play a role in the selection of the evaluation data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Using unit tests to improve quality of parallel datasets generated via backtranslation is not entirely new in the ML for software engineering domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "There were attempts to use it for bug patching applications, see for instance, DeepDebug (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09352) paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It is also frequently used during inference, as a postprocessing step, and evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While this is true in most cases, have you observed and analyzed some corner cases, for instance, when an object needs to be instantiated in the body of the unit test method prior to assertions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "How often does it happen, are these cases ignored?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Starting with 333k Java functions, authors obtain 103k high-quality test case suites with EvoSuites which are then iteratively used to generate parallel training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Have you analyzed the failed cases: \u2022 Does the model successfully generates data for small methods only (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "a single statement), and majority of failed cases are long methods?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How does it handle test cases with multiple asserts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "\u2022 When does EvoSuite fail to generate high-quality test suites?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I wonder if EvoSuite scaffolding is an issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The main weakness in this work is one that relates to the overall goal of fact extraction from language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The \u201cOracle'' results from Table 1 are thought provoking: with perfect knowledge regarding the predicate/relation of test examples, and a subsequent 100% consistent response, the LLM is only able to obtain ~50% correct responses from T-Rex, which is an admittedly limited evaluation (41 \u201chead\u201d predicates, mostly of well known entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While I understand that this work is clearly focused on the consistency issue, not necessarily correctness, it puts into question whether fact extraction from LMs is a worthwhile pursuit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would have liked for the paper to dig a little deeper into this headroom question from the previous point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Would it be possible to conduct a sampled qualitative evaluation of errors of the Oracle model in the ID cases?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are the errors due to unseen triples during training time (e.g., not in Wikipedia), or maybe there are issues with model capacity (maybe a 10x version of the LM would be able to recall the prompted fact)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In terms of writing, the most confusing section in the paper is Section 4.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "After re-reading it twice, I was still not able to ascertain: (1) what data was used to train the models, and (2) what data was used to evaluate the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The section makes reference to LAMA\u2019s T-REX, LPAQA, ParaRel, as well as augmentations using BERT lexical replacements, as well as data from \u201cShin et al, 2020\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The section also talks about examples from these sources as well as templates (presumably filled in with WikiData triples?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I really think this section needs to be rewritten and the training, eval and test datasets should be much more precisely described.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would also encourage authors to release the exact datasets and splits to allow others to reproduce/improve on this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But even with a data release, a precise description of how this data was constructed is very important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The depiction in Figure 2 hints that the entire sequence is rewritten using the fixed-length learned embeddings, and perhaps the subject or MASK embeddings are preserved?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Or do they rewrite the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do either the MASK or subject tokens get copied?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The LAMA benchmarks have one unfortunate characteristic: since it was constructed for BERT-style single token prediction, it has stripped down the original datasets (see the original version of T-Rex, which contains over 600 unique predicates vs. the 41 from LAMA: https://hadyelsahar.github.io/t-rex/ and https://aclanthology.org/L18-1544.pdf ).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I wonder if a more comprehensive version of this would be to evaluate on a larger sequence-to-sequence model like BART https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461 or T5 https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683 (both available as HuggingFace models).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Given that this work leverages frozen LLMs, it seems that training and evaluation could be done relatively cheaply even for larger models with proper decoders.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Major comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper does not draw a connection to prior work on robust optimization (for example, see references in [1]), which offers a more principled framework for formulating the objective of invariance to a class of input perturbations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "At the very least, it would be good to give a formal definition of robustness/consistency and cite this line of prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As this paper notes, the factual probing benchmark has class imbalances and represents a very particular use case, so while P-Adapters appear to improve consistency here it\u2019s unclear if these results will generalize to other settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to evaluate P-Adapters on a wider variety of tasks, following prior work on prompt optimization [2, 3]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, [4] provide 100 prompt templates for a wider variety of NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The paper would be more compelling if you could show that P-Adapters improve consistency in other settings too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Much of the motivation of this paper is based on assertions about user preferences, but there is no human evaluation to validate these claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, would a typical user prefer to give a natural language input but get an inconsistent response?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or would they rather pick a pre-defined relation type with better promise of consistency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In particular, it\u2019s not clear why user preferences would place any restrictions on training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "MoE model: How accurate is the relation classifier?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There is a big performance drop in the \u201cOOD Prompts\u201d setting, which leads me to wonder if the relation classifier was adequately trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Either way, relation classification results should be included and discussed in the main paper--a perfect relation classifier would lead to 100% consistency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "All of the models suffer a performance drop on the \u201cOOD Objects\u201d setting, which indicates that the models have over-fit to the (imbalanced) distribution of entities in Wikidata.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It\u2019s unclear how to interpret consistency in this setting, because the models are consistently producing the incorrect response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It might be more informative to report something like the \u201cConsistent-Acc.\u201d measurement from [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Table 2 does not compare results to any prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, can these results be compared to the \u201cConsistency Improved PLM\u201d results from [5]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Minor comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "The metric called \u201cprecision@1\u201d should be called \u201caccuracy@1\u201d", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Table 2 does not explain how the results are aggregated (prior work takes either the micro- or macro-average over relation types).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Three MLPs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The paper is missing some implementation details, such as the size of the BiLSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "The results section (section 5) might be easier to read if it were divided into subsections or paragraphs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "It would be interesting to see the accuracy breakdown by relation type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The main weakness in this work is one that relates to the overall goal of fact extraction from language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The \u201cOracle'' results from Table 1 are thought provoking: with perfect knowledge regarding the predicate/relation of test examples, and a subsequent 100% consistent response, the LLM is only able to obtain ~50% correct responses from T-Rex, which is an admittedly limited evaluation (41 \u201chead\u201d predicates, mostly of well known entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While I understand that this work is clearly focused on the consistency issue, not necessarily correctness, it puts into question whether fact extraction from LMs is a worthwhile pursuit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would have liked for the paper to dig a little deeper into this headroom question from the previous point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Would it be possible to conduct a sampled qualitative evaluation of errors of the Oracle model in the ID cases?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are the errors due to unseen triples during training time (e.g., not in Wikipedia), or maybe there are issues with model capacity (maybe a 10x version of the LM would be able to recall the prompted fact)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In terms of writing, the most confusing section in the paper is Section 4.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "After re-reading it twice, I was still not able to ascertain: (1) what data was used to train the models, and (2) what data was used to evaluate the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The section makes reference to LAMA\u2019s T-REX, LPAQA, ParaRel, as well as augmentations using BERT lexical replacements, as well as data from \u201cShin et al, 2020\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The section also talks about examples from these sources as well as templates (presumably filled in with WikiData triples?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I really think this section needs to be rewritten and the training, eval and test datasets should be much more precisely described.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would also encourage authors to release the exact datasets and splits to allow others to reproduce/improve on this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But even with a data release, a precise description of how this data was constructed is very important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The depiction in Figure 2 hints that the entire sequence is rewritten using the fixed-length learned embeddings, and perhaps the subject or MASK embeddings are preserved?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Or do they rewrite the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do either the MASK or subject tokens get copied?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The LAMA benchmarks have one unfortunate characteristic: since it was constructed for BERT-style single token prediction, it has stripped down the original datasets (see the original version of T-Rex, which contains over 600 unique predicates vs. the 41 from LAMA: https://hadyelsahar.github.io/t-rex/ and https://aclanthology.org/L18-1544.pdf ).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I wonder if a more comprehensive version of this would be to evaluate on a larger sequence-to-sequence model like BART https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461 or T5 https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683 (both available as HuggingFace models).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Given that this work leverages frozen LLMs, it seems that training and evaluation could be done relatively cheaply even for larger models with proper decoders.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although the proposed method involves experiments in four different settings including OOD keyword error, it might be valuable to investigate an OOD syntax error setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That is, in a real scenario, users write the natural language prompt which may have a grammatical error.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Assessing the robustness of the proposed method in terms of grammatical errors can enhance the quality of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In Table 2, the performances of P-tuning in RoBERTa-large are better than the ones of Oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Without any explanation, it is not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instead of listing the result in the tables, it would be clearer if the authors provide more explanations about them although they are in the appendix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The main weakness in this work is one that relates to the overall goal of fact extraction from language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The \u201cOracle'' results from Table 1 are thought provoking: with perfect knowledge regarding the predicate/relation of test examples, and a subsequent 100% consistent response, the LLM is only able to obtain ~50% correct responses from T-Rex, which is an admittedly limited evaluation (41 \u201chead\u201d predicates, mostly of well known entities).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While I understand that this work is clearly focused on the consistency issue, not necessarily correctness, it puts into question whether fact extraction from LMs is a worthwhile pursuit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would have liked for the paper to dig a little deeper into this headroom question from the previous point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Would it be possible to conduct a sampled qualitative evaluation of errors of the Oracle model in the ID cases?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are the errors due to unseen triples during training time (e.g., not in Wikipedia), or maybe there are issues with model capacity (maybe a 10x version of the LM would be able to recall the prompted fact)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In terms of writing, the most confusing section in the paper is Section 4.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "After re-reading it twice, I was still not able to ascertain: (1) what data was used to train the models, and (2) what data was used to evaluate the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The section makes reference to LAMA\u2019s T-REX, LPAQA, ParaRel, as well as augmentations using BERT lexical replacements, as well as data from \u201cShin et al, 2020\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The section also talks about examples from these sources as well as templates (presumably filled in with WikiData triples?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I really think this section needs to be rewritten and the training, eval and test datasets should be much more precisely described.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would also encourage authors to release the exact datasets and splits to allow others to reproduce/improve on this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But even with a data release, a precise description of how this data was constructed is very important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For the MoE and Oracle layers, the description in the paper is insufficient to determine the outputs presented to the first layer of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "The depiction in Figure 2 hints that the entire sequence is rewritten using the fixed-length learned embeddings, and perhaps the subject or MASK embeddings are preserved?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "But actually sub-section 4.2 never formally describes how the embeddings are used to create the continuous prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Are they prepended/appended to the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Or do they rewrite the original inputs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Do either the MASK or subject tokens get copied?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The LAMA benchmarks have one unfortunate characteristic: since it was constructed for BERT-style single token prediction, it has stripped down the original datasets (see the original version of T-Rex, which contains over 600 unique predicates vs. the 41 from LAMA: https://hadyelsahar.github.io/t-rex/ and https://aclanthology.org/L18-1544.pdf ).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I wonder if a more comprehensive version of this would be to evaluate on a larger sequence-to-sequence model like BART https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461 or T5 https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683 (both available as HuggingFace models).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Given that this work leverages frozen LLMs, it seems that training and evaluation could be done relatively cheaply even for larger models with proper decoders.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 23, "text": "-ves:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "research questions are dispersed all over the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "What I mean by this:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Abstract: you say that you tackle the inconsistency problem and you want to use a lightweight approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Hence, I can see that there are two research questions: 1) inconsistency of LLM and 2) efficiency - how to tackle the inconsistency problem with a light weight model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Introduction: you say that focus is the continuous prompts and user-friendly prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "These are another two research questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Discussion: you hypothesising that the success of P-Adapter is due to not updating the LLM parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Such that it is likely to maintain all of factual knowledge learned during pretraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Which to me is yet another research question - addressing the inconsistency of LLM with frozen weights vs fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "In Introduction you talk about the frozen weights of LLM but do not motivate why you decide to keep them frozen until the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Can you please state and motivate clearly your (full) research question somewhere in Introduction?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Lack of experiments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "(Minor) Why didn\u2019t you train BERT fully with the proposed natural language prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "You emphasise the importance of this experiment yourself in the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "(Minor) Why didn\u2019t you train (fine-tune) BERT with P-Adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "This experiment will also allow you to answer your hypothesis in the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "(Major) Did you try to freeze the parameters of LLM (or use an adapter) in the MoE model?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "You claim that MoE performs better than P-Adapters because of the additional supervision information (in form of relations) the model gets during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "With the present experiments it is hard to tell.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Maybe MoE perform better because you also tune parameters of LLM?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Major comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper does not draw a connection to prior work on robust optimization (for example, see references in [1]), which offers a more principled framework for formulating the objective of invariance to a class of input perturbations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "At the very least, it would be good to give a formal definition of robustness/consistency and cite this line of prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As this paper notes, the factual probing benchmark has class imbalances and represents a very particular use case, so while P-Adapters appear to improve consistency here it\u2019s unclear if these results will generalize to other settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to evaluate P-Adapters on a wider variety of tasks, following prior work on prompt optimization [2, 3]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, [4] provide 100 prompt templates for a wider variety of NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The paper would be more compelling if you could show that P-Adapters improve consistency in other settings too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Much of the motivation of this paper is based on assertions about user preferences, but there is no human evaluation to validate these claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, would a typical user prefer to give a natural language input but get an inconsistent response?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or would they rather pick a pre-defined relation type with better promise of consistency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In particular, it\u2019s not clear why user preferences would place any restrictions on training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "MoE model: How accurate is the relation classifier?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There is a big performance drop in the \u201cOOD Prompts\u201d setting, which leads me to wonder if the relation classifier was adequately trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Either way, relation classification results should be included and discussed in the main paper--a perfect relation classifier would lead to 100% consistency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "All of the models suffer a performance drop on the \u201cOOD Objects\u201d setting, which indicates that the models have over-fit to the (imbalanced) distribution of entities in Wikidata.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It\u2019s unclear how to interpret consistency in this setting, because the models are consistently producing the incorrect response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It might be more informative to report something like the \u201cConsistent-Acc.\u201d measurement from [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Table 2 does not compare results to any prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, can these results be compared to the \u201cConsistency Improved PLM\u201d results from [5]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Minor comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "The metric called \u201cprecision@1\u201d should be called \u201caccuracy@1\u201d", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Table 2 does not explain how the results are aggregated (prior work takes either the micro- or macro-average over relation types).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Three MLPs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The paper is missing some implementation details, such as the size of the BiLSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "The results section (section 5) might be easier to read if it were divided into subsections or paragraphs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "It would be interesting to see the accuracy breakdown by relation type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although the proposed method involves experiments in four different settings including OOD keyword error, it might be valuable to investigate an OOD syntax error setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That is, in a real scenario, users write the natural language prompt which may have a grammatical error.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Assessing the robustness of the proposed method in terms of grammatical errors can enhance the quality of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In Table 2, the performances of P-tuning in RoBERTa-large are better than the ones of Oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Without any explanation, it is not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instead of listing the result in the tables, it would be clearer if the authors provide more explanations about them although they are in the appendix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Major comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper does not draw a connection to prior work on robust optimization (for example, see references in [1]), which offers a more principled framework for formulating the objective of invariance to a class of input perturbations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "At the very least, it would be good to give a formal definition of robustness/consistency and cite this line of prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper does not provide a clear justification for why P-Adapters would be expected to improve consistency, and nothing in the training objective encourages the model to be consistent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument might be more convincing if you could compare it to a training objective that does explicitly promote consistency--for example, by encouraging the hidden representations of different prompt paraphrases to be similar.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As this paper notes, the factual probing benchmark has class imbalances and represents a very particular use case, so while P-Adapters appear to improve consistency here it\u2019s unclear if these results will generalize to other settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to evaluate P-Adapters on a wider variety of tasks, following prior work on prompt optimization [2, 3]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, [4] provide 100 prompt templates for a wider variety of NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The paper would be more compelling if you could show that P-Adapters improve consistency in other settings too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Much of the motivation of this paper is based on assertions about user preferences, but there is no human evaluation to validate these claims.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, would a typical user prefer to give a natural language input but get an inconsistent response?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or would they rather pick a pre-defined relation type with better promise of consistency?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In particular, it\u2019s not clear why user preferences would place any restrictions on training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "MoE model: How accurate is the relation classifier?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There is a big performance drop in the \u201cOOD Prompts\u201d setting, which leads me to wonder if the relation classifier was adequately trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Either way, relation classification results should be included and discussed in the main paper--a perfect relation classifier would lead to 100% consistency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "All of the models suffer a performance drop on the \u201cOOD Objects\u201d setting, which indicates that the models have over-fit to the (imbalanced) distribution of entities in Wikidata.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "It\u2019s unclear how to interpret consistency in this setting, because the models are consistently producing the incorrect response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It might be more informative to report something like the \u201cConsistent-Acc.\u201d measurement from [5].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Table 2 does not compare results to any prior work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, can these results be compared to the \u201cConsistency Improved PLM\u201d results from [5]?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Minor comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "The metric called \u201cprecision@1\u201d should be called \u201caccuracy@1\u201d", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Table 2 does not explain how the results are aggregated (prior work takes either the micro- or macro-average over relation types).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The description of the methods and the illustration (Figure 2) are somewhat unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Is there one MLP applied at each position?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Three MLPs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The paper is missing some implementation details, such as the size of the BiLSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "The results section (section 5) might be easier to read if it were divided into subsections or paragraphs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "It would be interesting to see the accuracy breakdown by relation type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 23, "text": "-ves:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "research questions are dispersed all over the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "What I mean by this:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Abstract: you say that you tackle the inconsistency problem and you want to use a lightweight approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Hence, I can see that there are two research questions: 1) inconsistency of LLM and 2) efficiency - how to tackle the inconsistency problem with a light weight model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Introduction: you say that focus is the continuous prompts and user-friendly prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "These are another two research questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Discussion: you hypothesising that the success of P-Adapter is due to not updating the LLM parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Such that it is likely to maintain all of factual knowledge learned during pretraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Which to me is yet another research question - addressing the inconsistency of LLM with frozen weights vs fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "In Introduction you talk about the frozen weights of LLM but do not motivate why you decide to keep them frozen until the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Can you please state and motivate clearly your (full) research question somewhere in Introduction?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Lack of experiments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "(Minor) Why didn\u2019t you train BERT fully with the proposed natural language prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "You emphasise the importance of this experiment yourself in the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "(Minor) Why didn\u2019t you train (fine-tune) BERT with P-Adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "This experiment will also allow you to answer your hypothesis in the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "(Major) Did you try to freeze the parameters of LLM (or use an adapter) in the MoE model?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "You claim that MoE performs better than P-Adapters because of the additional supervision information (in form of relations) the model gets during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "With the present experiments it is hard to tell.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Maybe MoE perform better because you also tune parameters of LLM?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 48, "reviewer1": "review#4", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although the proposed method involves experiments in four different settings including OOD keyword error, it might be valuable to investigate an OOD syntax error setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That is, in a real scenario, users write the natural language prompt which may have a grammatical error.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Assessing the robustness of the proposed method in terms of grammatical errors can enhance the quality of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In Table 2, the performances of P-tuning in RoBERTa-large are better than the ones of Oracle.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Without any explanation, it is not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Instead of listing the result in the tables, it would be clearer if the authors provide more explanations about them although they are in the appendix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 23, "text": "-ves:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "research questions are dispersed all over the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "What I mean by this:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Abstract: you say that you tackle the inconsistency problem and you want to use a lightweight approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Hence, I can see that there are two research questions: 1) inconsistency of LLM and 2) efficiency - how to tackle the inconsistency problem with a light weight model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Introduction: you say that focus is the continuous prompts and user-friendly prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "These are another two research questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Discussion: you hypothesising that the success of P-Adapter is due to not updating the LLM parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Such that it is likely to maintain all of factual knowledge learned during pretraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Which to me is yet another research question - addressing the inconsistency of LLM with frozen weights vs fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "In Introduction you talk about the frozen weights of LLM but do not motivate why you decide to keep them frozen until the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Can you please state and motivate clearly your (full) research question somewhere in Introduction?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Lack of experiments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "(Minor) Why didn\u2019t you train BERT fully with the proposed natural language prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "You emphasise the importance of this experiment yourself in the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "(Minor) Why didn\u2019t you train (fine-tune) BERT with P-Adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "This experiment will also allow you to answer your hypothesis in the Discussion section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "(Major) Did you try to freeze the parameters of LLM (or use an adapter) in the MoE model?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "You claim that MoE performs better than P-Adapters because of the additional supervision information (in form of relations) the model gets during the training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "With the present experiments it is hard to tell.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Maybe MoE perform better because you also tune parameters of LLM?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 49, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "From these experiments, it is unclear whether models are actually \"learning to follow instructions\" or just learning a very large space of tasks from the fine-tuning procedure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In other words, even though prompt variance is reported at inference time, the models could potentially perform just as well with nonsense or missing prompts during fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "As far as I can tell, no experiments that rule out this possibility exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although qualitatively useful, the analysis in 4.1 does not conclusively show that the number of instruction tuning clusters aids performance, or that this trend is likely to continue with more clusters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Most of the gain could be acquired by tasks which are most difficult, or most similar to the heldout task, and this analysis cannot disprove such an interpretation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A proper analysis would consider more heldout tasks and permutations of training data, but presumably this is prohibitively expensive.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper is missing important details about hardware usage and training time", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Some possible issues which might be resolved by the additional questions below", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Cons: There are still a few questions that can be addressed to make the analysis comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Have authors try to use the FLAN prompts on GPT3 or BaseLM and how does the performance look like?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since instruction tuning will adjust all the parameters in the original pre-trained language model, there is a question what about what is the potential impact of this tuning process?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Will it drops any knowledge of any tasks, which will be a disadvantage when the task's labeled data is available?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 49, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "From these experiments, it is unclear whether models are actually \"learning to follow instructions\" or just learning a very large space of tasks from the fine-tuning procedure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In other words, even though prompt variance is reported at inference time, the models could potentially perform just as well with nonsense or missing prompts during fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "As far as I can tell, no experiments that rule out this possibility exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although qualitatively useful, the analysis in 4.1 does not conclusively show that the number of instruction tuning clusters aids performance, or that this trend is likely to continue with more clusters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Most of the gain could be acquired by tasks which are most difficult, or most similar to the heldout task, and this analysis cannot disprove such an interpretation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A proper analysis would consider more heldout tasks and permutations of training data, but presumably this is prohibitively expensive.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper is missing important details about hardware usage and training time", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Some possible issues which might be resolved by the additional questions below", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "This seems to contradict findings from Brown et al where larger models did better on essentially all tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 49, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "From these experiments, it is unclear whether models are actually \"learning to follow instructions\" or just learning a very large space of tasks from the fine-tuning procedure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In other words, even though prompt variance is reported at inference time, the models could potentially perform just as well with nonsense or missing prompts during fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "As far as I can tell, no experiments that rule out this possibility exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although qualitatively useful, the analysis in 4.1 does not conclusively show that the number of instruction tuning clusters aids performance, or that this trend is likely to continue with more clusters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Most of the gain could be acquired by tasks which are most difficult, or most similar to the heldout task, and this analysis cannot disprove such an interpretation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "A proper analysis would consider more heldout tasks and permutations of training data, but presumably this is prohibitively expensive.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper is missing important details about hardware usage and training time", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Some possible issues which might be resolved by the additional questions below", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Additional Tasks Results (3.4)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In Appendix A.1, the paper mainly draws conclusions based on comparisons between GPT-3 and FLAN, which I do not think are fair: GPT-3 and FLAN differ in model size and pre-training data distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "It might be too late to change the name, but \u201cFinetuned LAnguage Net\u201d (FLAN) is uninformative, since it does not capture any unique aspect of this method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "What does \u201cLAnguage\u201d mean here, \"natural language instruction\" or \"language model\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "If it is the former, then directly including the word \u201cinstruction\u201d might be better; and hopefully it\u2019s not the latter, since even fine-tuned BERT on SST-2 counts as a fine-tuned language model ...", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 49, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Cons: There are still a few questions that can be addressed to make the analysis comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Have authors try to use the FLAN prompts on GPT3 or BaseLM and how does the performance look like?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since instruction tuning will adjust all the parameters in the original pre-trained language model, there is a question what about what is the potential impact of this tuning process?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Will it drops any knowledge of any tasks, which will be a disadvantage when the task's labeled data is available?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "This seems to contradict findings from Brown et al where larger models did better on essentially all tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 49, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Cons: There are still a few questions that can be addressed to make the analysis comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Have authors try to use the FLAN prompts on GPT3 or BaseLM and how does the performance look like?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Since instruction tuning will adjust all the parameters in the original pre-trained language model, there is a question what about what is the potential impact of this tuning process?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Will it drops any knowledge of any tasks, which will be a disadvantage when the task's labeled data is available?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Additional Tasks Results (3.4)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In Appendix A.1, the paper mainly draws conclusions based on comparisons between GPT-3 and FLAN, which I do not think are fair: GPT-3 and FLAN differ in model size and pre-training data distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "It might be too late to change the name, but \u201cFinetuned LAnguage Net\u201d (FLAN) is uninformative, since it does not capture any unique aspect of this method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "What does \u201cLAnguage\u201d mean here, \"natural language instruction\" or \"language model\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "If it is the former, then directly including the word \u201cinstruction\u201d might be better; and hopefully it\u2019s not the latter, since even fine-tuned BERT on SST-2 counts as a fine-tuned language model ...", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 49, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "This seems to contradict findings from Brown et al where larger models did better on essentially all tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Additional Tasks Results (3.4)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In Appendix A.1, the paper mainly draws conclusions based on comparisons between GPT-3 and FLAN, which I do not think are fair: GPT-3 and FLAN differ in model size and pre-training data distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "It might be too late to change the name, but \u201cFinetuned LAnguage Net\u201d (FLAN) is uninformative, since it does not capture any unique aspect of this method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "What does \u201cLAnguage\u201d mean here, \"natural language instruction\" or \"language model\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "If it is the former, then directly including the word \u201cinstruction\u201d might be better; and hopefully it\u2019s not the latter, since even fine-tuned BERT on SST-2 counts as a fine-tuned language model ...", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 50, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There are many factors that can affect the downstream performance, for example, the model size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "One possible explanation is that the flatness could be a consequence of scaling up the model size, and the good performance is brought by the large model size, too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If that is the case, then flatness is not the reason for good downstream performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors need to have more evidence to prove that flatness is the main reason that leads to good performance, otherwise, they may be both the consequence of another factor (like model size).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "When computing Hessian, I assume that you are using the loss of pretraining datasets, not downstream datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "It is, however, unclear what the practical implications of this work are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "First of all, current large language models are not in the saturation regime, and it is hard to estimate when they will be as datasets grow with model size in tandem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Second, while this paper points that pretraining loss is not a reliable indicator of downstream performance, a simple remedy is to evaluate on downstream tasks during pretraining and compare models accordingly, which is likely already done in practice.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Finally, this paper does not demonstrate if the insight gleaned in this work can lead to additional \u201cflatness regularization\u201d that induces better downstream performance on real datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It is understandable that large-scale experiments are expensive and are not expected, but given the rather empirical motivation of the paper, some validation on real data seems desirable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, more empirical results on real data, especially on how the discovered insight can enable better downstream performance, would strength this work given the rather empirical motivation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 50, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There are many factors that can affect the downstream performance, for example, the model size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "One possible explanation is that the flatness could be a consequence of scaling up the model size, and the good performance is brought by the large model size, too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If that is the case, then flatness is not the reason for good downstream performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors need to have more evidence to prove that flatness is the main reason that leads to good performance, otherwise, they may be both the consequence of another factor (like model size).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "When computing Hessian, I assume that you are using the loss of pretraining datasets, not downstream datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The other issue is that it does require many samples to obtain a reasonable estimate of the trace.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "the trace of the Hessian matrix is only an overview of the magnitude of all eigenvalues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "A smaller trace could mean that the minimum might be flatter than other minima with the same loss, but it could also mean that the minimum might be steep in only a few directions, and completely non-informative in others.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The latter case wouldn't necessarily give us a strong transferable model, and yet the trace of the Hessian matrix wouldn't be able to tell.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "maybe I am missing the point and I am happy to be corrected, but the arguments made in this submission or the observations obtained are already well-known empirically, especially in the case of zero-shot and few-shot learning with very large pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In addition, the theorems mentioned in the submission don't seem necessary, or, in any case, support the arguments or the observations.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 50, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There are many factors that can affect the downstream performance, for example, the model size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "One possible explanation is that the flatness could be a consequence of scaling up the model size, and the good performance is brought by the large model size, too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If that is the case, then flatness is not the reason for good downstream performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors need to have more evidence to prove that flatness is the main reason that leads to good performance, otherwise, they may be both the consequence of another factor (like model size).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "When computing Hessian, I assume that you are using the loss of pretraining datasets, not downstream datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The authors claim that smaller transformer architecture is a subset of the larger transformer architecture family, but it does not make sense.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If we increase the width of transformer or increase the vocabulary size, the smaller model cannot be a special case of larger model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would be better to explain why larger model converges to flatter minima than smaller one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Minor typo: In page 4, (2) training for different number of steps -> (1) training for different number of steps", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Although this paper tackles interesting research question, it is unclear that flatter minima actually leads to better generalizations since it does not compare relevant baselines and lack of justification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "As previously mentioned, the authors do not properly tackle the open question --- flat minima and generalization which might still seem to be controversial topic.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Moreover, they need to tone down their claim since their theoretical analysis is limited to very specific controlled experiment which is far from realistic scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 50, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "It is, however, unclear what the practical implications of this work are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "First of all, current large language models are not in the saturation regime, and it is hard to estimate when they will be as datasets grow with model size in tandem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Second, while this paper points that pretraining loss is not a reliable indicator of downstream performance, a simple remedy is to evaluate on downstream tasks during pretraining and compare models accordingly, which is likely already done in practice.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Finally, this paper does not demonstrate if the insight gleaned in this work can lead to additional \u201cflatness regularization\u201d that induces better downstream performance on real datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It is understandable that large-scale experiments are expensive and are not expected, but given the rather empirical motivation of the paper, some validation on real data seems desirable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, more empirical results on real data, especially on how the discovered insight can enable better downstream performance, would strength this work given the rather empirical motivation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The other issue is that it does require many samples to obtain a reasonable estimate of the trace.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "the trace of the Hessian matrix is only an overview of the magnitude of all eigenvalues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "A smaller trace could mean that the minimum might be flatter than other minima with the same loss, but it could also mean that the minimum might be steep in only a few directions, and completely non-informative in others.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The latter case wouldn't necessarily give us a strong transferable model, and yet the trace of the Hessian matrix wouldn't be able to tell.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "maybe I am missing the point and I am happy to be corrected, but the arguments made in this submission or the observations obtained are already well-known empirically, especially in the case of zero-shot and few-shot learning with very large pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In addition, the theorems mentioned in the submission don't seem necessary, or, in any case, support the arguments or the observations.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 50, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "It is, however, unclear what the practical implications of this work are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "First of all, current large language models are not in the saturation regime, and it is hard to estimate when they will be as datasets grow with model size in tandem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Second, while this paper points that pretraining loss is not a reliable indicator of downstream performance, a simple remedy is to evaluate on downstream tasks during pretraining and compare models accordingly, which is likely already done in practice.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Finally, this paper does not demonstrate if the insight gleaned in this work can lead to additional \u201cflatness regularization\u201d that induces better downstream performance on real datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It is understandable that large-scale experiments are expensive and are not expected, but given the rather empirical motivation of the paper, some validation on real data seems desirable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, more empirical results on real data, especially on how the discovered insight can enable better downstream performance, would strength this work given the rather empirical motivation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The authors claim that smaller transformer architecture is a subset of the larger transformer architecture family, but it does not make sense.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If we increase the width of transformer or increase the vocabulary size, the smaller model cannot be a special case of larger model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would be better to explain why larger model converges to flatter minima than smaller one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Minor typo: In page 4, (2) training for different number of steps -> (1) training for different number of steps", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Although this paper tackles interesting research question, it is unclear that flatter minima actually leads to better generalizations since it does not compare relevant baselines and lack of justification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "As previously mentioned, the authors do not properly tackle the open question --- flat minima and generalization which might still seem to be controversial topic.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Moreover, they need to tone down their claim since their theoretical analysis is limited to very specific controlled experiment which is far from realistic scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 50, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The other issue is that it does require many samples to obtain a reasonable estimate of the trace.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "the trace of the Hessian matrix is only an overview of the magnitude of all eigenvalues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "A smaller trace could mean that the minimum might be flatter than other minima with the same loss, but it could also mean that the minimum might be steep in only a few directions, and completely non-informative in others.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The latter case wouldn't necessarily give us a strong transferable model, and yet the trace of the Hessian matrix wouldn't be able to tell.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "maybe I am missing the point and I am happy to be corrected, but the arguments made in this submission or the observations obtained are already well-known empirically, especially in the case of zero-shot and few-shot learning with very large pre-trained transformer models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In addition, the theorems mentioned in the submission don't seem necessary, or, in any case, support the arguments or the observations.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The authors claim that smaller transformer architecture is a subset of the larger transformer architecture family, but it does not make sense.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If we increase the width of transformer or increase the vocabulary size, the smaller model cannot be a special case of larger model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It would be better to explain why larger model converges to flatter minima than smaller one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Minor typo: In page 4, (2) training for different number of steps -> (1) training for different number of steps", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Although this paper tackles interesting research question, it is unclear that flatter minima actually leads to better generalizations since it does not compare relevant baselines and lack of justification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "As previously mentioned, the authors do not properly tackle the open question --- flat minima and generalization which might still seem to be controversial topic.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Moreover, they need to tone down their claim since their theoretical analysis is limited to very specific controlled experiment which is far from realistic scenario.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 51, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Maybe inappropriate to say outperforming XGBoost on 2 out of 4 tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(I think showing gains are fine over transformers, but besides connect-4 in Table 7, I am not sure the comparison to the XGBoost is that meaningful. The gaps between XGBoost and RoBERTa are remaining similar)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Table 2, \"We see consistent improvements with more GAL iterations,\", I am not sure if MNLI and RTE show that?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The experimental results show performance gains, however, I am not convinced that this approach is novel as it has been shown in previous work that self-training can improve performance and as we know that GPT-2, 3 can generate quality examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "-- (probable) high computation cost", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "-- anonymization issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The fact that GAL appears in the GLUE learderbord associated with the name XXX is a major issue if XXX is indeed the author of this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "As the GLUE leaderboard is mentioned a lot of time, this anonymization leak is important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The fact that GAL appears in the GLUE learderbord associated with the name XXX [to be discussed privately] is a major issue if XXX is indeed the author of this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "As the GLUE leaderboard is mentioned a lot of time, this anonymization leak is important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The authors do not discuss the computation cost of combining GPT2 & RoBERTa-large modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 51, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Maybe inappropriate to say outperforming XGBoost on 2 out of 4 tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(I think showing gains are fine over transformers, but besides connect-4 in Table 7, I am not sure the comparison to the XGBoost is that meaningful. The gaps between XGBoost and RoBERTa are remaining similar)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Table 2, \"We see consistent improvements with more GAL iterations,\", I am not sure if MNLI and RTE show that?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The experimental results show performance gains, however, I am not convinced that this approach is novel as it has been shown in previous work that self-training can improve performance and as we know that GPT-2, 3 can generate quality examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "First, the authors explicitly mention the small amount of labeled data in Figure 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Most of the experiments are conducted under full supervision except Table 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not very true that only a small amount of labeled data is available considering MNLI includes 400k examples and RTE also has more than thousands of labeled examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This claim matters since data augmentation is much more challenging and important for low-resource scenarios.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, this claim is only investigated in Table 6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This still leaves many unanswered questions, like whether this observation is only validated for autoregressive models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "especially considering that the previous papers[1] show class-conditional LM also brings sizable gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Even though authors review many related works, they did not include other data augmentation works as baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The model with data augmentation and without augmentation is only ablation studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Considering a GPT-2 is incorporated additional for data generation, it is not surprising to observe improvements.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "If KD is based on trained labeled data, this seems not a fair comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This can only show that using additional LM for data augmentation is useful and such a claim is provided in many existing papers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "I am not sure about the claim on class-conditional generative models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Since class-conditional generative models still generate noisy data, how about using pseudo labeling on class-conditional generated data?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The negative effect seems to be brought by noisy labels instead of conditional generated data quality since codntional-class generation is also shown to be effective in a recent paper [4] with a more challenging setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "However, this claim is still validated based on comparison with other state-of-the-art data augmentation methods.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Contradiction"}]}, {"paper_idx": 51, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Maybe inappropriate to say outperforming XGBoost on 2 out of 4 tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "(I think showing gains are fine over transformers, but besides connect-4 in Table 7, I am not sure the comparison to the XGBoost is that meaningful. The gaps between XGBoost and RoBERTa are remaining similar)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Table 2, \"We see consistent improvements with more GAL iterations,\", I am not sure if MNLI and RTE show that?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "The experimental results show performance gains, however, I am not convinced that this approach is novel as it has been shown in previous work that self-training can improve performance and as we know that GPT-2, 3 can generate quality examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "I do not have many weaknesses to highlight.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 51, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "-- (probable) high computation cost", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "-- anonymization issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The fact that GAL appears in the GLUE learderbord associated with the name XXX is a major issue if XXX is indeed the author of this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "As the GLUE leaderboard is mentioned a lot of time, this anonymization leak is important.", 