data augmentation and without augmentation is only ablation studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Considering a GPT-2 is incorporated additional for data generation, it is not surprising to observe improvements.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "If KD is based on trained labeled data, this seems not a fair comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This can only show that using additional LM for data augmentation is useful and such a claim is provided in many existing papers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "I am not sure about the claim on class-conditional generative models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Since class-conditional generative models still generate noisy data, how about using pseudo labeling on class-conditional generated data?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The negative effect seems to be brought by noisy labels instead of conditional generated data quality since codntional-class generation is also shown to be effective in a recent paper [4] with a more challenging setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "However, this claim is still validated based on comparison with other state-of-the-art data augmentation methods.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Contradiction"}]}, {"paper_idx": 51, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "-- (probable) high computation cost", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "-- anonymization issue", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The fact that GAL appears in the GLUE learderbord associated with the name XXX is a major issue if XXX is indeed the author of this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "As the GLUE leaderboard is mentioned a lot of time, this anonymization leak is important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The fact that GAL appears in the GLUE learderbord associated with the name XXX [to be discussed privately] is a major issue if XXX is indeed the author of this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "As the GLUE leaderboard is mentioned a lot of time, this anonymization leak is important.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The authors do not discuss the computation cost of combining GPT2 & RoBERTa-large modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "I do not have many weaknesses to highlight.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 51, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "First, the authors explicitly mention the small amount of labeled data in Figure 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Most of the experiments are conducted under full supervision except Table 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not very true that only a small amount of labeled data is available considering MNLI includes 400k examples and RTE also has more than thousands of labeled examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This claim matters since data augmentation is much more challenging and important for low-resource scenarios.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, this claim is only investigated in Table 6.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This still leaves many unanswered questions, like whether this observation is only validated for autoregressive models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "especially considering that the previous papers[1] show class-conditional LM also brings sizable gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Even though authors review many related works, they did not include other data augmentation works as baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The model with data augmentation and without augmentation is only ablation studies.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Considering a GPT-2 is incorporated additional for data generation, it is not surprising to observe improvements.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "If KD is based on trained labeled data, this seems not a fair comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This can only show that using additional LM for data augmentation is useful and such a claim is provided in many existing papers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "I am not sure about the claim on class-conditional generative models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Since class-conditional generative models still generate noisy data, how about using pseudo labeling on class-conditional generated data?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The negative effect seems to be brought by noisy labels instead of conditional generated data quality since codntional-class generation is also shown to be effective in a recent paper [4] with a more challenging setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "However, this claim is still validated based on comparison with other state-of-the-art data augmentation methods.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Contradiction"}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "I do not have many weaknesses to highlight.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 52, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It is unclear on whether this method can be applied beyond the language modality, and if so, how would that be implemented", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The [1] shares a similar motivation, which also aims to learn the weights of LFs to data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The reported performance on TREC from [2] is 82.59, which is higher than that of the proposed method and its implementation in Table 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It is suggested to add more experiments on the some datasets from [2] to carefully validate the progress.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The motivation is clear but corresponding empirical analysis are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 52, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It is unclear on whether this method can be applied beyond the language modality, and if so, how would that be implemented", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The stated goal of the paper is to \"primary goal is to improve the LFs to classes allocation in order to correct the systematically biased label assignments\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "This targets fundamentally misspecified labeling functions, since a core assumption of vanilla DP is that LFs perform better than random chance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "These can happen in practice, but I'm not convinced flipping the label (essentially) is always beneficial especially in rare/infrequent classes settings.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper suffers from clarity issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, the feature-based and deep ULF methods need more methods elaboration and details", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "From the Table 2 results, which use the WRENCH benchmark implementations (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.11377.pdf) the reported performance numbers don't seem to match what was reported in the original WRENCH paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It's hard to draw any strong insights from the experiments as presented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have some concerns on the reported numbers (replicating WRENCH and reported above)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I don't think the paper, overall, is particularly novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "There are virtues to a simple method for improving performance, say an deep empirical dive into k-fold estimation when designing labeling functions, but this manuscript doesn't provide enough details to be very illuminating.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 52, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The method is based on an intuition that a \u201cmismatch between predictions of a model and labels can indicate candidates of noise specific to all held out labeling functions.\u201d This intuition needs stronger support and explanation beyond a few examples mentioned in Figure 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It is unclear on whether this method can be applied beyond the language modality, and if so, how would that be implemented", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "However, using this confidence estimation is not straightforward (as pointed out in the experiments -- comparison against wscrossweigh and wscleanlab).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper however lacks a theory to support the idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Incrementally updating the matrix T* and noise labels Y* sounds reasonable but can be dangerous as we don't know to where they will converge (or will they converge at all).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, there's even no objective for ULF to work on.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(On the contrary, the noisy-channel matrix is part of the loss to be minimized.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to view T* and Y* as latent variables and put them in a theoretical framework (as in Snorkel or noisy-channel)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "When comparing ULF to data programming Snorkel, it is unclear whether ULF is able to deal with dependency between labelling functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The use of development sets in experiments for early stopping and hyper param tuning is typical. However, if these sets are clean (annotated labels are not noisy), then they don't seem to be used efficiently. For instance, why not using them to estimate the confidence directly? Or, at least they maybe more helpful than only to select p. Also, having clean dev. sets can be tricky as one can train a model directly on these sets in a supervised learning fashion.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "As estimating T* and Y* is the core of ULF, the paper should have some analyses on whether T* and Y* are estimated more correctly after each iteration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Clarity: the paper is quite difficult to read as the cross-validation-based confidence estimation description is quite dense.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The experiments results and case study are quite lengthy and not really helpful.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Although I recommend to accept the paper, there are several points that the paper can be improved:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "the writing can be clearer (especially the description for cross-validation-based confidence estimation), some parts can be shorten for analyses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "the paper will be stronger if ULF can be framed in a theoretical framework.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "more analyses are needed to understand the quality of T* and Y*.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 52, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The [1] shares a similar motivation, which also aims to learn the weights of LFs to data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The reported performance on TREC from [2] is 82.59, which is higher than that of the proposed method and its implementation in Table 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It is suggested to add more experiments on the some datasets from [2] to carefully validate the progress.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The motivation is clear but corresponding empirical analysis are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The stated goal of the paper is to \"primary goal is to improve the LFs to classes allocation in order to correct the systematically biased label assignments\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "This targets fundamentally misspecified labeling functions, since a core assumption of vanilla DP is that LFs perform better than random chance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "These can happen in practice, but I'm not convinced flipping the label (essentially) is always beneficial especially in rare/infrequent classes settings.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper suffers from clarity issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, the feature-based and deep ULF methods need more methods elaboration and details", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "From the Table 2 results, which use the WRENCH benchmark implementations (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.11377.pdf) the reported performance numbers don't seem to match what was reported in the original WRENCH paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It's hard to draw any strong insights from the experiments as presented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have some concerns on the reported numbers (replicating WRENCH and reported above)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I don't think the paper, overall, is particularly novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "There are virtues to a simple method for improving performance, say an deep empirical dive into k-fold estimation when designing labeling functions, but this manuscript doesn't provide enough details to be very illuminating.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 52, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The [1] shares a similar motivation, which also aims to learn the weights of LFs to data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The reported performance on TREC from [2] is 82.59, which is higher than that of the proposed method and its implementation in Table 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It is suggested to add more experiments on the some datasets from [2] to carefully validate the progress.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The motivation is clear but corresponding empirical analysis are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "However, using this confidence estimation is not straightforward (as pointed out in the experiments -- comparison against wscrossweigh and wscleanlab).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper however lacks a theory to support the idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Incrementally updating the matrix T* and noise labels Y* sounds reasonable but can be dangerous as we don't know to where they will converge (or will they converge at all).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, there's even no objective for ULF to work on.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(On the contrary, the noisy-channel matrix is part of the loss to be minimized.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to view T* and Y* as latent variables and put them in a theoretical framework (as in Snorkel or noisy-channel)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "When comparing ULF to data programming Snorkel, it is unclear whether ULF is able to deal with dependency between labelling functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The use of development sets in experiments for early stopping and hyper param tuning is typical. However, if these sets are clean (annotated labels are not noisy), then they don't seem to be used efficiently. For instance, why not using them to estimate the confidence directly? Or, at least they maybe more helpful than only to select p. Also, having clean dev. sets can be tricky as one can train a model directly on these sets in a supervised learning fashion.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "As estimating T* and Y* is the core of ULF, the paper should have some analyses on whether T* and Y* are estimated more correctly after each iteration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Clarity: the paper is quite difficult to read as the cross-validation-based confidence estimation description is quite dense.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The experiments results and case study are quite lengthy and not really helpful.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Although I recommend to accept the paper, there are several points that the paper can be improved:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "the writing can be clearer (especially the description for cross-validation-based confidence estimation), some parts can be shorten for analyses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "the paper will be stronger if ULF can be framed in a theoretical framework.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "more analyses are needed to understand the quality of T* and Y*.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 52, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The stated goal of the paper is to \"primary goal is to improve the LFs to classes allocation in order to correct the systematically biased label assignments\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "This targets fundamentally misspecified labeling functions, since a core assumption of vanilla DP is that LFs perform better than random chance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "These can happen in practice, but I'm not convinced flipping the label (essentially) is always beneficial especially in rare/infrequent classes settings.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper suffers from clarity issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "For example, the feature-based and deep ULF methods need more methods elaboration and details", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "From the Table 2 results, which use the WRENCH benchmark implementations (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.11377.pdf) the reported performance numbers don't seem to match what was reported in the original WRENCH paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It's hard to draw any strong insights from the experiments as presented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "I have some concerns on the reported numbers (replicating WRENCH and reported above)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I don't think the paper, overall, is particularly novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "There are virtues to a simple method for improving performance, say an deep empirical dive into k-fold estimation when designing labeling functions, but this manuscript doesn't provide enough details to be very illuminating.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "However, using this confidence estimation is not straightforward (as pointed out in the experiments -- comparison against wscrossweigh and wscleanlab).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper however lacks a theory to support the idea.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Incrementally updating the matrix T* and noise labels Y* sounds reasonable but can be dangerous as we don't know to where they will converge (or will they converge at all).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, there's even no objective for ULF to work on.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(On the contrary, the noisy-channel matrix is part of the loss to be minimized.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Would it be possible to view T* and Y* as latent variables and put them in a theoretical framework (as in Snorkel or noisy-channel)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "When comparing ULF to data programming Snorkel, it is unclear whether ULF is able to deal with dependency between labelling functions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The use of development sets in experiments for early stopping and hyper param tuning is typical. However, if these sets are clean (annotated labels are not noisy), then they don't seem to be used efficiently. For instance, why not using them to estimate the confidence directly? Or, at least they maybe more helpful than only to select p. Also, having clean dev. sets can be tricky as one can train a model directly on these sets in a supervised learning fashion.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "As estimating T* and Y* is the core of ULF, the paper should have some analyses on whether T* and Y* are estimated more correctly after each iteration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Clarity: the paper is quite difficult to read as the cross-validation-based confidence estimation description is quite dense.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The experiments results and case study are quite lengthy and not really helpful.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Although I recommend to accept the paper, there are several points that the paper can be improved:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "the writing can be clearer (especially the description for cross-validation-based confidence estimation), some parts can be shorten for analyses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "the paper will be stronger if ULF can be framed in a theoretical framework.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "more analyses are needed to understand the quality of T* and Y*.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 53, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses: ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Even though there are experiments in section 7 trying to give an intuitive feel for the reason this method is working, there can be more discussion from a model-structure perspective on how the internal structure of the transformer is creating these embeddings for policy networks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Limitations:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors don't discuss the limitations enough of the model in the environment of VirtualHome and BabyAI. I'd like to see a discussion of failure modes of the model and how that traces back to the encoded embeddings.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}], "items2": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "1. Scalability: ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The proposed encoding method is templated-based (Line 155-156). Although the input encoding scheme (Section 7.1) may be a trivial problem, the encoding scheme may still affect the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Searching for the optimal encoding scheme is an expensive process, which may bring a high cost of hand-crafted engineering. ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Besides, the data gathering method also relies on hand-designed templates (Line 220).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "2. Presentation:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The related work of PLM is adequately cited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But the authors should also introduce the background of policy learning so that the significance of this work can be highlighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "3. Performance: ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Compared to the work that uses traditional networks like DQN, the integration of PLM may affect the inference speed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "4. Clarity:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Societal Impact: No potential negative societal impact. The authors provide a new perspective to aid policy learning with a pre-trained language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 53, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses: ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Even though there are experiments in section 7 trying to give an intuitive feel for the reason this method is working, there can be more discussion from a model-structure perspective on how the internal structure of the transformer is creating these embeddings for policy networks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Limitations:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Authors don't discuss the limitations enough of the model in the environment of VirtualHome and BabyAI. I'd like to see a discussion of failure modes of the model and how that traces back to the encoded embeddings.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "One concern is about the reproducibility of the paper. The authors do not provide the detailed architecture and hyperparameters of the method. Although there is a short description in Appendix D.2. I don't think it is sufficient for reproduction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be better if the authors can provide standard deviations in Tables 1 and 4. With a small number of demos, I believe that the standard deviation would be large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 53, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "1. Scalability: ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The proposed encoding method is templated-based (Line 155-156). Although the input encoding scheme (Section 7.1) may be a trivial problem, the encoding scheme may still affect the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Searching for the optimal encoding scheme is an expensive process, which may bring a high cost of hand-crafted engineering. ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Besides, the data gathering method also relies on hand-designed templates (Line 220).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "2. Presentation:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The related work of PLM is adequately cited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "But the authors should also introduce the background of policy learning so that the significance of this work can be highlighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "3. Performance: ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Compared to the work that uses traditional networks like DQN, the integration of PLM may affect the inference speed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "4. Clarity:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Societal Impact: No potential negative societal impact. The authors provide a new perspective to aid policy learning with a pre-trained language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "One concern is about the reproducibility of the paper. The authors do not provide the detailed architecture and hyperparameters of the method. Although there is a short description in Appendix D.2. I don't think it is sufficient for reproduction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be better if the authors can provide standard deviations in Tables 1 and 4. With a small number of demos, I believe that the standard deviation would be large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "My main complaint with this paper is that it describes what's happening as a simplicity prior, or Occam's razor, but a more in-depth analysis of the pattern they observe might reveal that these systems have a prior towards specific types of rules or methods of compression, such as those that exploit hierarchical patterns or memorized chunks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Because there was a lack of analysis of the rule type, focusing only on an abstract idea of simplicity based on a particular method of compression, I found this unconvincing as a study of a general simplicity prior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wasn't satisfied with the attempt to explain what a concept was.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Within the paper, this is left very vague.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What does it mean to say that an explanation is \"minimally acceptable\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A more systematic analysis is needed to determine the optimal prompts that make the model capture a range of different patterns. This sentence seems to indicate that this will be the focus of the paper, but it isn't my understanding of the purpose of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "most representative AI systems - This is a really unclear claim", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I found the example about \"go stop go\" pretty confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I wonder if you could come up with a better example that doesn't involve iffy phrases like \"go stop go stop\" or even obscure English idioms like \"ready steady go\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I was confused as to the purpose of the paragraph on liens 114-123.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It didn't seem to particularly relate to the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "GPT-3D-T0 is one of the most similar systems to humans - What does this mean?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "line 163 \"being w\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Most of the paper is pretty clear, although I had trouble understanding section 3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "My main complaint with this paper is that it describes what's happening as a simplicity prior, or Occam's razor, but a more in-depth analysis of the pattern they observe might reveal that these systems have a prior towards specific types of rules or methods of compression, such as those that exploit hierarchical patterns or memorized chunks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Because there was a lack of analysis of the rule type, focusing only on an abstract idea of simplicity based on a particular method of compression, I found this unconvincing as a study of a general simplicity prior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wasn't satisfied with the attempt to explain what a concept was.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Within the paper, this is left very vague.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What does it mean to say that an explanation is \"minimally acceptable\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A more systematic analysis is needed to determine the optimal prompts that make the model capture a range of different patterns. This sentence seems to indicate that this will be the focus of the paper, but it isn't my understanding of the purpose of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "most representative AI systems - This is a really unclear claim", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I found the example about \"go stop go\" pretty confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I wonder if you could come up with a better example that doesn't involve iffy phrases like \"go stop go stop\" or even obscure English idioms like \"ready steady go\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I was confused as to the purpose of the paragraph on liens 114-123.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It didn't seem to particularly relate to the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "GPT-3D-T0 is one of the most similar systems to humans - What does this mean?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "line 163 \"being w\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "In terms of presentation, I found the paper difficult to understand at points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since much of section 3 is not used subsequently anyway, I would suggest that the authors instead use the space to emphasize this and how this leads to the experimental setup.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would also recommend the authors put their GPT prompts and the way they attempted to elicit explanations from GPT in the main text, since it's very hard to interpret any of the results otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This seems potentially problematic in situations where programs/concepts can have very similar outputs but differ drastically in their complexity, which is presumably the case here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(For instance, do we have any way of telling from the experiments that GPT hasn't learned the exact desired program except on a list of 10,000 exception inputs where it behaves differently?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think if we were able to get reliable explanations from the models we could resolve these questions to some extent, but as the authors note this is difficult to do.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I also wonder whether the machine teaching dimension of the paper changes the story/experiments much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, if instead of feeding the learners the optimal witness sets the learners just received random examples, would the plots change?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(This experiment might be worth doing!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "If not, these experiments may tell us more about the priors/biases of these learners in general rather than about how these learners perform when being taught, with ramifications for questions Q1 and Q2 in section 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "My main complaint with this paper is that it describes what's happening as a simplicity prior, or Occam's razor, but a more in-depth analysis of the pattern they observe might reveal that these systems have a prior towards specific types of rules or methods of compression, such as those that exploit hierarchical patterns or memorized chunks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Because there was a lack of analysis of the rule type, focusing only on an abstract idea of simplicity based on a particular method of compression, I found this unconvincing as a study of a general simplicity prior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wasn't satisfied with the attempt to explain what a concept was.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Within the paper, this is left very vague.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What does it mean to say that an explanation is \"minimally acceptable\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A more systematic analysis is needed to determine the optimal prompts that make the model capture a range of different patterns. This sentence seems to indicate that this will be the focus of the paper, but it isn't my understanding of the purpose of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "most representative AI systems - This is a really unclear claim", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I found the example about \"go stop go\" pretty confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I wonder if you could come up with a better example that doesn't involve iffy phrases like \"go stop go stop\" or even obscure English idioms like \"ready steady go\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I was confused as to the purpose of the paragraph on liens 114-123.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It didn't seem to particularly relate to the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "GPT-3D-T0 is one of the most similar systems to humans - What does this mean?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "line 163 \"being w\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, I wasn't fully convinced by the setup used by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I am looking forward to the author response that might clarify some of these points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Moreover, it is impossible to learn C4 from the single WS example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, none of the examples contain the first rule (if input is '', print '00'), so how can any system be expected to learn this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This makes me unsure of the interpretation of the results in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Q1 asks \"Is a language model such as GPT able to capture algorithmic patterns from very few examples selected with a simplicity prior?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": ", but then Q2 asks Does accuracy increase from the theoretically minimal witness sets needed for teaching the concept with additional examples?\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the definition of \"capturing a concept\" in #154 we have \"iff for every S we have that p \udbff\udc11 S iff c \udbff\udc11 S.\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In what sense does accuracy improve once you've captured a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "#193: in which sense can a language relate to \"the way language models work internally (transformers)\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "My main complaint with this paper is that it describes what's happening as a simplicity prior, or Occam's razor, but a more in-depth analysis of the pattern they observe might reveal that these systems have a prior towards specific types of rules or methods of compression, such as those that exploit hierarchical patterns or memorized chunks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Because there was a lack of analysis of the rule type, focusing only on an abstract idea of simplicity based on a particular method of compression, I found this unconvincing as a study of a general simplicity prior.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I wasn't satisfied with the attempt to explain what a concept was.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Within the paper, this is left very vague.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What does it mean to say that an explanation is \"minimally acceptable\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A more systematic analysis is needed to determine the optimal prompts that make the model capture a range of different patterns. This sentence seems to indicate that this will be the focus of the paper, but it isn't my understanding of the purpose of the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "most representative AI systems - This is a really unclear claim", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I found the example about \"go stop go\" pretty confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I wonder if you could come up with a better example that doesn't involve iffy phrases like \"go stop go stop\" or even obscure English idioms like \"ready steady go\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I was confused as to the purpose of the paragraph on liens 114-123.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It didn't seem to particularly relate to the rest of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "GPT-3D-T0 is one of the most similar systems to humans - What does this mean?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "line 163 \"being w\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "My biggest concern is that nearly all of the results in the paper are based on the same 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It makes sense that the human experiments needed a limited set, but it would greatly strengthen the paper if additional machine-only results were added with results averaged over hundreds or thousands of concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I am also concerned about the relative mismatch between the average accuracies of humans (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2b) versus their ability to provide correct explanations (Figure 3a).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Should we conclude that humans are sometimes guessing correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or that there are some quirks in the concepts, where the examples are suggestive of some other concept that is wrong, but happens to align with the test examples?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the relatively low rate of explanation correctness across the board suggests that this task may just be too difficult for humans (it is certainly difficult for me, looking at the examples).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For the \u201caccuracy\u201d results, if I were to guess 2 out of 5 tests correctly for a single concept, would that contribute 0.4 or 0.0 to the accuracy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Please clarify in the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am wondering if the inclusion of the inductive programming methods (Louise and MH) are more distracting than informative, at least in the way that they are presented now, as first-class citizens alongside the GPT and human results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "My understanding is that the behavior of these methods is much less mysterious than that of GPT or humans -- if the background knowledge given to the inductive programming methods were perfectly aligned with P3, then we would see perfect agreement in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "So really the results for these methods are just a function of the background knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I\u2019m not sure what we are meant to take away from these results in particular.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "See \u201cconcerns\u201d above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Most of the paper is pretty clear, although I had trouble understanding section 3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "In terms of presentation, I found the paper difficult to understand at points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since much of section 3 is not used subsequently anyway, I would suggest that the authors instead use the space to emphasize this and how this leads to the experimental setup.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would also recommend the authors put their GPT prompts and the way they attempted to elicit explanations from GPT in the main text, since it's very hard to interpret any of the results otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This seems potentially problematic in situations where programs/concepts can have very similar outputs but differ drastically in their complexity, which is presumably the case here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(For instance, do we have any way of telling from the experiments that GPT hasn't learned the exact desired program except on a list of 10,000 exception inputs where it behaves differently?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think if we were able to get reliable explanations from the models we could resolve these questions to some extent, but as the authors note this is difficult to do.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I also wonder whether the machine teaching dimension of the paper changes the story/experiments much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, if instead of feeding the learners the optimal witness sets the learners just received random examples, would the plots change?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(This experiment might be worth doing!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "If not, these experiments may tell us more about the priors/biases of these learners in general rather than about how these learners perform when being taught, with ramifications for questions Q1 and Q2 in section 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Most of the paper is pretty clear, although I had trouble understanding section 3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, I wasn't fully convinced by the setup used by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I am looking forward to the author response that might clarify some of these points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Moreover, it is impossible to learn C4 from the single WS example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, none of the examples contain the first rule (if input is '', print '00'), so how can any system be expected to learn this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This makes me unsure of the interpretation of the results in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Q1 asks \"Is a language model such as GPT able to capture algorithmic patterns from very few examples selected with a simplicity prior?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": ", but then Q2 asks Does accuracy increase from the theoretically minimal witness sets needed for teaching the concept with additional examples?\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the definition of \"capturing a concept\" in #154 we have \"iff for every S we have that p \udbff\udc11 S iff c \udbff\udc11 S.\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In what sense does accuracy improve once you've captured a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "#193: in which sense can a language relate to \"the way language models work internally (transformers)\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Most of the paper is pretty clear, although I had trouble understanding section 3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition to the mathematical definitions, I would appreciate if the authors could explain more thoroughly in plain text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "My biggest concern is that nearly all of the results in the paper are based on the same 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It makes sense that the human experiments needed a limited set, but it would greatly strengthen the paper if additional machine-only results were added with results averaged over hundreds or thousands of concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I am also concerned about the relative mismatch between the average accuracies of humans (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2b) versus their ability to provide correct explanations (Figure 3a).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Should we conclude that humans are sometimes guessing correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or that there are some quirks in the concepts, where the examples are suggestive of some other concept that is wrong, but happens to align with the test examples?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the relatively low rate of explanation correctness across the board suggests that this task may just be too difficult for humans (it is certainly difficult for me, looking at the examples).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For the \u201caccuracy\u201d results, if I were to guess 2 out of 5 tests correctly for a single concept, would that contribute 0.4 or 0.0 to the accuracy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Please clarify in the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am wondering if the inclusion of the inductive programming methods (Louise and MH) are more distracting than informative, at least in the way that they are presented now, as first-class citizens alongside the GPT and human results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "My understanding is that the behavior of these methods is much less mysterious than that of GPT or humans -- if the background knowledge given to the inductive programming methods were perfectly aligned with P3, then we would see perfect agreement in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "So really the results for these methods are just a function of the background knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I\u2019m not sure what we are meant to take away from these results in particular.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "See \u201cconcerns\u201d above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "In terms of presentation, I found the paper difficult to understand at points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since much of section 3 is not used subsequently anyway, I would suggest that the authors instead use the space to emphasize this and how this leads to the experimental setup.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would also recommend the authors put their GPT prompts and the way they attempted to elicit explanations from GPT in the main text, since it's very hard to interpret any of the results otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This seems potentially problematic in situations where programs/concepts can have very similar outputs but differ drastically in their complexity, which is presumably the case here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(For instance, do we have any way of telling from the experiments that GPT hasn't learned the exact desired program except on a list of 10,000 exception inputs where it behaves differently?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think if we were able to get reliable explanations from the models we could resolve these questions to some extent, but as the authors note this is difficult to do.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I also wonder whether the machine teaching dimension of the paper changes the story/experiments much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, if instead of feeding the learners the optimal witness sets the learners just received random examples, would the plots change?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(This experiment might be worth doing!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "If not, these experiments may tell us more about the priors/biases of these learners in general rather than about how these learners perform when being taught, with ramifications for questions Q1 and Q2 in section 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, I wasn't fully convinced by the setup used by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I am looking forward to the author response that might clarify some of these points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Moreover, it is impossible to learn C4 from the single WS example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, none of the examples contain the first rule (if input is '', print '00'), so how can any system be expected to learn this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This makes me unsure of the interpretation of the results in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Q1 asks \"Is a language model such as GPT able to capture algorithmic patterns from very few examples selected with a simplicity prior?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": ", but then Q2 asks Does accuracy increase from the theoretically minimal witness sets needed for teaching the concept with additional examples?\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the definition of \"capturing a concept\" in #154 we have \"iff for every S we have that p \udbff\udc11 S iff c \udbff\udc11 S.\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In what sense does accuracy improve once you've captured a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "#193: in which sense can a language relate to \"the way language models work internally (transformers)\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "In terms of presentation, I found the paper difficult to understand at points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even after reading section 3 it was unclear to me how witness sets could be so small and what exactly the authors meant by priors being aligned between learner and teacher, and I was only able to understand what the authors intended by reading the Telle et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In particular, I think what I was missing was that witness sets only need to distinguish the shortest satisfying program from earlier, shorter ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since much of section 3 is not used subsequently anyway, I would suggest that the authors instead use the space to emphasize this and how this leads to the experimental setup.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would also recommend the authors put their GPT prompts and the way they attempted to elicit explanations from GPT in the main text, since it's very hard to interpret any of the results otherwise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This seems potentially problematic in situations where programs/concepts can have very similar outputs but differ drastically in their complexity, which is presumably the case here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(For instance, do we have any way of telling from the experiments that GPT hasn't learned the exact desired program except on a list of 10,000 exception inputs where it behaves differently?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think if we were able to get reliable explanations from the models we could resolve these questions to some extent, but as the authors note this is difficult to do.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I also wonder whether the machine teaching dimension of the paper changes the story/experiments much.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, if instead of feeding the learners the optimal witness sets the learners just received random examples, would the plots change?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "(This experiment might be worth doing!)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "If not, these experiments may tell us more about the priors/biases of these learners in general rather than about how these learners perform when being taught, with ramifications for questions Q1 and Q2 in section 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "My biggest concern is that nearly all of the results in the paper are based on the same 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It makes sense that the human experiments needed a limited set, but it would greatly strengthen the paper if additional machine-only results were added with results averaged over hundreds or thousands of concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I am also concerned about the relative mismatch between the average accuracies of humans (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2b) versus their ability to provide correct explanations (Figure 3a).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Should we conclude that humans are sometimes guessing correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or that there are some quirks in the concepts, where the examples are suggestive of some other concept that is wrong, but happens to align with the test examples?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the relatively low rate of explanation correctness across the board suggests that this task may just be too difficult for humans (it is certainly difficult for me, looking at the examples).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For the \u201caccuracy\u201d results, if I were to guess 2 out of 5 tests correctly for a single concept, would that contribute 0.4 or 0.0 to the accuracy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Please clarify in the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am wondering if the inclusion of the inductive programming methods (Louise and MH) are more distracting than informative, at least in the way that they are presented now, as first-class citizens alongside the GPT and human results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "My understanding is that the behavior of these methods is much less mysterious than that of GPT or humans -- if the background knowledge given to the inductive programming methods were perfectly aligned with P3, then we would see perfect agreement in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "So really the results for these methods are just a function of the background knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I\u2019m not sure what we are meant to take away from these results in particular.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "See \u201cconcerns\u201d above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 54, "reviewer1": "review#4", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, I wasn't fully convinced by the setup used by the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "I am looking forward to the author response that might clarify some of these points.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main concern I have with this paper is that I am not sure how the concept of witness set as used in this paper is learnable by a machine or a human.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Looking at the examples in Table 1, there are numerous ways of generalizing the first WS, some of them of similar simplicity (e.g., copy the first character, copy the last character, copy the entire sequence, etc.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Moreover, it is impossible to learn C4 from the single WS example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In fact, none of the examples contain the first rule (if input is '', print '00'), so how can any system be expected to learn this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This makes me unsure of the interpretation of the results in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors discuss it rather informally in the intro and related work (e.g., the concept of an odd number), but then treat it as a function in #150 and onwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Q1 asks \"Is a language model such as GPT able to capture algorithmic patterns from very few examples selected with a simplicity prior?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": ", but then Q2 asks Does accuracy increase from the theoretically minimal witness sets needed for teaching the concept with additional examples?\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the definition of \"capturing a concept\" in #154 we have \"iff for every S we have that p \udbff\udc11 S iff c \udbff\udc11 S.\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In what sense does accuracy improve once you've captured a concept?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "#193: in which sense can a language relate to \"the way language models work internally (transformers)\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "My biggest concern is that nearly all of the results in the paper are based on the same 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It makes sense that the human experiments needed a limited set, but it would greatly strengthen the paper if additional machine-only results were added with results averaged over hundreds or thousands of concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I am also concerned about the relative mismatch between the average accuracies of humans (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2b) versus their ability to provide correct explanations (Figure 3a).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Should we conclude that humans are sometimes guessing correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Or that there are some quirks in the concepts, where the examples are suggestive of some other concept that is wrong, but happens to align with the test examples?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, the relatively low rate of explanation correctness across the board suggests that this task may just be too difficult for humans (it is certainly difficult for me, looking at the examples).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "For the \u201caccuracy\u201d results, if I were to guess 2 out of 5 tests correctly for a single concept, would that contribute 0.4 or 0.0 to the accuracy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Please clarify in the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am wondering if the inclusion of the inductive programming methods (Louise and MH) are more distracting than informative, at least in the way that they are presented now, as first-class citizens alongside the GPT and human results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "My understanding is that the behavior of these methods is much less mysterious than that of GPT or humans -- if the background knowledge given to the inductive programming methods were perfectly aligned with P3, then we would see perfect agreement in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "So really the results for these methods are just a function of the background knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I\u2019m not sure what we are meant to take away from these results in particular.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "See \u201cconcerns\u201d above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 55, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some ad-hoc decisions (e.g., shortening the max sequence size) that are not well explained", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lots of experimental results that are not well explained", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I had a hard time processing the multitude of experimental results. In particular, it's not clear what the numbers represent in each table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would clearly describe in the caption of each figure what the rows/columns represent and what metric is used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In particular, the paper uses \"fairness\" without clearly explaining how fairness is measured.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Some of the design decisions are not well explained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Not clear what WR50 means.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In Table 1, all columns except for BA represent some measure of fairness? If that's the case and if I understand the results WR (which I'm guessing stands for word replacement) seems quite performant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The fairness metric used seemed a bit ad hoc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "One of the more standard metrics could be use (see Fairness Definitions Explained and the following paper for an example of using equalized odds: Your Fairness May Vary: Pretrained Language Model Fairness in Toxic Text Classification - analysis of many LMs wrt fairness and model size/training size/random seed (in the context of toxic text prediction)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Last but not least, I think \"fairness specifications\" is misleading and quite an overblown term for what the paper is about;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "it made me think of some theoretical formalism/specification. Similar pairs of text across protected groups is a much more honest description of what the paper generates.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "For weaknesses, the authors could be clearer on what fairness metric is being used for tables such as Table D.3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Additionally, an ablation study could be added in terms of how changing the similarity classification threshold affects performance of the similarity model (and the downstream performance of downstream classifiers)?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Finally, I would have liked to see in the appendix table E1-4, the generations from the three methods on the same examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "There are also a few nits, for example, make sure to reference all variables in graphs (for example, D in Figure 1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As a note, the authors should reference this paper as well for automatic generation of evaluation data: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.03286.pdf.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 55, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some ad-hoc decisions (e.g., shortening the max sequence size) that are not well explained", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lots of experimental results that are not well explained", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I had a hard time processing the multitude of experimental results. In particular, it's not clear what the numbers represent in each table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would clearly describe in the caption of each figure what the rows/columns represent and what metric is used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In particular, the paper uses \"fairness\" without clearly explaining how fairness is measured.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Some of the design decisions are not well explained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Not clear what WR50 means.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In Table 1, all columns except for BA represent some measure of fairness? If that's the case and if I understand the results WR (which I'm guessing stands for word replacement) seems quite performant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The fairness metric used seemed a bit ad hoc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "One of the more standard metrics could be use (see Fairness Definitions Explained and the following paper for an example of using equalized odds: Your Fairness May Vary: Pretrained Language Model Fairness in Toxic Text Classification - analysis of many LMs wrt fairness and model size/training size/random seed (in the context of toxic text prediction)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Last but not least, I think \"fairness specifications\" is misleading and quite an overblown term for what the paper is about;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "it made me think of some theoretical formalism/specification. Similar pairs of text across protected groups is a much more honest description of what the paper generates.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It seems like the approach would be really task dependent. I think it is good for authors to acknowledge this somehow and mention how the task can affect the outcomes.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Related to my above comment, authors only study toxicity problem.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be good to study other tasks and compare the differences and provide more in depth discussion on what the differences are in terms of the filtering approach per task as well as human alignment results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the approach is effective in achieving more \"fair\" outcomes, the approach imposes loss on accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Given that the task of toxicity detection is a subjective task and in many cases culturally dependent, what mechanisms authors take into account in their human studies other than recruiting more than one worker to label a hit and taking the maximum.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Please provide some discussion on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper is of good quality, but it would be better if additional experiments are added to investigate more various tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 55, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some ad-hoc decisions (e.g., shortening the max sequence size) that are not well explained", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Lots of experimental results that are not well explained", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I had a hard time processing the multitude of experimental results. In particular, it's not clear what the numbers represent in each table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would clearly describe in the caption of each figure what the rows/columns represent and what metric is used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In particular, the paper uses \"fairness\" without clearly explaining how fairness is measured.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Some of the design decisions are not well explained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Not clear what WR50 means.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In Table 1, all columns except for BA represent some measure of fairness? If that's the case and if I understand the results WR (which I'm guessing stands for word replacement) seems quite performant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The fairness metric used seemed a bit ad hoc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "One of the more standard metrics could be use (see Fairness Definitions Explained and the following paper for an example of using equalized odds: Your Fairness May Vary: Pretrained Language Model Fairness in Toxic Text Classification - analysis of many LMs wrt fairness and model size/training size/random seed (in the context of toxic text prediction)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Last but not least, I think \"fairness specifications\" is misleading and quite an overblown term for what the paper is about;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "it made me think of some theoretical formalism/specification. Similar pairs of text across protected groups is a much more honest description of what the paper generates.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses (see more details in the section below):", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper needs further improvement in clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Beyond individual fairness, a discussion of how the proposed dataset with individual fairness would affect group fairness notions is needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The broader impact of how relevant stakeholders would use the generated workflow is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, I think the paper could be further improved in terms of clarity and discussion of multiple related notions (e.g., group fairness) and the potential broader impacts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "While the paper is fairly readable, there is substantial room for improvement in clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The techniques involved in this paper span multiple domains, requiring the reader to have a good knowledge of algorithmic fairness, NLP, user study, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, it is hard to understand the experiment sections without referring to the appendix in detail.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Similar problems happen in Table 3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I also have a question about evaluating the diversity of generated pairs: the paper uses the fairness metric as the evaluation criterion for models trained on different constraint sets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The word choice of \"diversity\" sounds strange, given the evaluation criterion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 55, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "For weaknesses, the authors could be clearer on what fairness metric is being used for tables such as Table D.3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Additionally, an ablation study could be added in terms of how changing the similarity classification threshold affects performance of the similarity model (and the downstream performance of downstream classifiers)?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Finally, I would have liked to see in the appendix table E1-4, the generations from the three methods on the same examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "There are also a few nits, for example, make sure to reference all variables in graphs (for example, D in Figure 1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As a note, the authors should reference this paper as well for automatic generation of evaluation data: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.03286.pdf.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It seems like the approach would be really task dependent. I think it is good for authors to acknowledge this somehow and mention how the task can affect the outcomes.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Related to my above comment, authors only study toxicity problem.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be good to study other tasks and compare the differences and provide more in depth discussion on what the differences are in terms of the filtering approach per task as well as human alignment results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the approach is effective in achieving more \"fair\" outcomes, the approach imposes loss on accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Given that the task of toxicity detection is a subjective task and in many cases culturally dependent, what mechanisms authors take into account in their human studies other than recruiting more than one worker to label a hit and taking the maximum.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Please provide some discussion on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper is of good quality, but it would be better if additional experiments are added to investigate more various tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 55, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "For weaknesses, the authors could be clearer on what fairness metric is being used for tables such as Table D.3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Additionally, an ablation study could be added in terms of how changing the similarity classification threshold affects performance of the similarity model (and the downstream performance of downstream classifiers)?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Finally, I would have liked to see in the appendix table E1-4, the generations from the three methods on the same examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "There are also a few nits, for example, make sure to reference all variables in graphs (for example, D in Figure 1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As a note, the authors should reference this paper as well for automatic generation of evaluation data: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.03286.pdf.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses (see more details in the section below):", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper needs further improvement in clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Beyond individual fairness, a discussion of how the proposed dataset with individual fairness would affect group fairness notions is needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The broader impact of how relevant stakeholders would use the generated workflow is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, I think the paper could be further improved in terms of clarity and discussion of multiple related notions (e.g., group fairness) and the potential broader impacts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "While the paper is fairly readable, there is substantial room for improvement in clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The techniques involved in this paper span multiple domains, requiring the reader to have a good knowledge of algorithmic fairness, NLP, user study, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, it is hard to understand the experiment sections without referring to the appendix in detail.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Similar problems happen in Table 3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I also have a question about evaluating the diversity of generated pairs: the paper uses the fairness metric as the evaluation criterion for models trained on different constraint sets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The word choice of \"diversity\" sounds strange, given the evaluation criterion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 55, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "It seems like the approach would be really task dependent. I think it is good for authors to acknowledge this somehow and mention how the task can affect the outcomes.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Related to my above comment, authors only study toxicity problem.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It would be good to study other tasks and compare the differences and provide more in depth discussion on what the differences are in terms of the filtering approach per task as well as human alignment results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the approach is effective in achieving more \"fair\" outcomes, the approach imposes loss on accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Given that the task of toxicity detection is a subjective task and in many cases culturally dependent, what mechanisms authors take into account in their human studies other than recruiting more than one worker to label a hit and taking the maximum.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Please provide some discussion on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper is of good quality, but it would be better if additional experiments are added to investigate more various tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses (see more details in the section below):", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper needs further improvement in clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Beyond individual fairness, a discussion of how the proposed dataset with individual fairness would affect group fairness notions is needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The broader impact of how relevant stakeholders would use the generated workflow is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, I think the paper could be further improved in terms of clarity and discussion of multiple related notions (e.g., group fairness) and the potential broader impacts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "While the paper is fairly readable, there is substantial room for improvement in clarity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The techniques involved in this paper span multiple domains, requiring the reader to have a good knowledge of algorithmic fairness, NLP, user study, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, it is hard to understand the experiment sections without referring to the appendix in detail.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Similar problems happen in Table 3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I also have a question about evaluating the diversity of generated pairs: the paper uses the fairness metric as the evaluation criterion for models trained on different constraint sets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The word choice of \"diversity\" sounds strange, given the evaluation criterion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 56, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The model is evaluated only on one existing dataset, HumanEval, and not other similar datasets like APPS or MBPP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the work represents a significant expenditure of resources, the amount of technical novelty is relatively low, and some of the new aspects in the construction of the model itself are not studied or described as much as they could be:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The \\textsc{BigPython} dataset seems responsible for much of the gains in the work, but the description of it in the paper is limited to \"We have compiled public, non-personal information from GitHub consisting of permissively licensed Python code in October 2021\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is significant space dedicated to describing the TPU-based training setup with JAX, but not much about the reasoning behind it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper doesn't have much experiments about hyperparameters and training data selection (e.g. the order in which the datasets were used for training), or further information/justification for the values used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "There is no release of training datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The deduplication methodology used during dataset preprocessing may be too weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Only removing exact duplicate files may still leave many files which are very similar (for example, varying only in some comments at the top of the file).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For more discussion on another code dataset, see https://twitter.com/miltos1/status/1497126435261083649", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Considering that copies of the HumanEval dataset may have been posted to GitHub, the authors should investigate the possibility of training dataset contamination.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I did not realize until quite late that the CodeGen-Multi and CodeGen-Mono models are trained on, respectively, The Pile plus the BigQuery dataset, and The Pile plus BigQuery plus BigPython. That fact is only mentioned in Section 4.3 and in the appendix, and not where the models are initially described (Section 2.1 and 2.2). This can be clearer in Section 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The multi-turn dataset introduced in the paper seems to have quite a bit of similarity to SPoC (from Kulal et al., 2019), although SPoC is generally at a lower level of granularity.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "A more detailed discussion about it would have been helpful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "It is unclear why Table 2 only has information about CodeGen-Mono and not the other models. More data here would be more convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "(~) MTPB does not allow for \"going back\" (i.e., a user indicating that a generated response is wrong & clarifying their prompt).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "(-) MTPB (from a cursory qualitative evaluation of examples) seems to rely on very fine-grained prompts. In many examples in App F, the prompts are substantially longer than the required code.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 56, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The model is evaluated only on one existing dataset, HumanEval, and not other similar datasets like APPS or MBPP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the work represents a significant expenditure of resources, the amount of technical novelty is relatively low, and some of the new aspects in the construction of the model itself are not studied or described as much as they could be:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The \\textsc{BigPython} dataset seems responsible for much of the gains in the work, but the description of it in the paper is limited to \"We have compiled public, non-personal information from GitHub consisting of permissively licensed Python code in October 2021\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is significant space dedicated to describing the TPU-based training setup with JAX, but not much about the reasoning behind it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper doesn't have much experiments about hyperparameters and training data selection (e.g. the order in which the datasets were used for training), or further information/justification for the values used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "There is no release of training datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The deduplication methodology used during dataset preprocessing may be too weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Only removing exact duplicate files may still leave many files which are very similar (for example, varying only in some comments at the top of the file).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For more discussion on another code dataset, see https://twitter.com/miltos1/status/1497126435261083649", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Considering that copies of the HumanEval dataset may have been posted to GitHub, the authors should investigate the possibility of training dataset contamination.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I did not realize until quite late that the CodeGen-Multi and CodeGen-Mono models are trained on, respectively, The Pile plus the BigQuery dataset, and The Pile plus BigQuery plus BigPython. That fact is only mentioned in Section 4.3 and in the appendix, and not where the models are initially described (Section 2.1 and 2.2). This can be clearer in Section 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The multi-turn dataset introduced in the paper seems to have quite a bit of similarity to SPoC (from Kulal et al., 2019), although SPoC is generally at a lower level of granularity.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "A more detailed discussion about it would have been helpful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "It is unclear why Table 2 only has information about CodeGen-Mono and not the other models. More data here would be more convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "CodeGen model (single-turn) is only evaluated on a single dataset with only 164 problems. Why is the model not evaluated on other publicly available coding datasets such as MBPP and APPS?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "While the multi-turn dataset helps the models generate more correct code, I am not sure about its practical use cases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Would a user be able to specify the sub-tasks in the required format (such as defining appropriate variables) as in Figure 1?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How does multi-turn task break down work for programs with control flow structures (for e.g. write code for binary search)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "From the examples, it appears that the MTPB dataset treats small loops and branches as a single sub-task, but how can a user specify sub-tasks within a loop/branch?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Novelty is limited since there are already several pre-training/dataset collection papers in this area.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For the MTPB dataset, I don't fully understand the part about embedding the test case inputs within the prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The MTPB dataset contribution needs more explanation regarding its practical use cases and its limited capability to handle more complex programs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 56, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The model is evaluated only on one existing dataset, HumanEval, and not other similar datasets like APPS or MBPP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While the work represents a significant expenditure of resources, the amount of technical novelty is relatively low, and some of the new aspects in the construction of the model itself are not studied or described as much as they could be:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The \\textsc{BigPython} dataset seems responsible for much of the gains in the work, but the description of it in the paper is limited to \"We have compiled public, non-personal information from GitHub consisting of permissively licensed Python code in October 2021\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "There is significant space dedicated to describing the TPU-based training setup with JAX, but not much about the reasoning behind it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The paper doesn't have much experiments about hyperparameters and training data selection (e.g. the order in which the datasets were used for training), or further information/justification for the values used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "There is no release of training datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The deduplication methodology used during dataset preprocessing may be too weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Only removing exact duplicate files may still leave many files which are very similar (for example, varying only in some comments at the top of the file).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For more discussion on another code dataset, see https://twitter.com/miltos1/status/1497126435261083649", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Considering that copies of the HumanEval dataset may have been posted to GitHub, the authors should investigate the possibility of training dataset contamination.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I did not realize until quite late that the CodeGen-Multi and CodeGen-Mono models are trained on, respectively, The Pile plus the BigQuery dataset, and The Pile plus BigQuery plus BigPython. That fact is only mentioned in Section 4.3 and in the appendix, and not where the models are initially described (Section 2.1 and 2.2). This can be clearer in Section 2.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The multi-turn dataset introduced in the paper seems to have quite a bit of similarity to SPoC (from Kulal et al., 2019), although SPoC is generally at a lower level of granularity.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "A more detailed discussion about it would have been helpful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "It is unclear why Table 2 only has information about CodeGen-Mono and not the other models. More data here would be more convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 28, "text": "Code comment analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some programmers like to write comments, while some are not. Also, different programmers write comments very differently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I suggest the authors investigate the effect of such differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "One very simple way could be to analyze the occurrence frequency of interleaved natural and programming patterns in the dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "While it is hard to formally define meaningful comments, it would be insightful to at least calculate the document frequency of interleaved natural and programming language.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "In Table 3, the paper only compares the proposed method against GPT-Neo and GPT-J, which is not sufficient.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "The result will stand out to compare against Codex, the state-of-the-art program synthesis model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 56, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "(~) MTPB does not allow for \"going back\" (i.e., a user indicating that a generated response is wrong & clarifying their prompt).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "(-) MTPB (from a cursory qualitative evaluation of examples) seems to rely on very fine-grained prompts. In many examples in App F, the prompts are substantially longer than the required code.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "CodeGen model (single-turn) is only evaluated on a single dataset with only 164 problems. Why is the model not evaluated on other publicly available coding datasets such as MBPP and APPS?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "While the multi-turn dataset helps the models generate more correct code, I am not sure about its practical use cases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Would a user be able to specify the sub-tasks in the required format (such as defining appropriate variables) as in Figure 1?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How does multi-turn task break down work for programs with control flow structures (for e.g. write code for binary search)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "From the examples, it appears that the MTPB dataset treats small loops and branches as a single sub-task, but how can a user specify sub-tasks within a loop/branch?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Novelty is limited since there are already several pre-training/dataset collection papers in this area.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For the MTPB dataset, I don't fully understand the part about embedding the test case inputs within the prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The MTPB dataset contribution needs more explanation regarding its practical use cases and its limited capability to handle more complex programs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 56, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "(~) MTPB does not allow for \"going back\" (i.e., a user indicating that a generated response is wrong & clarifying their prompt).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "(-) MTPB (from a cursory qualitative evaluation of examples) seems to rely on very fine-grained prompts. In many examples in App F, the prompts are substantially longer than the required code.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I find the MTPB dataset to be largely uninteresting, as it relies on very fine-grained natural language supervision. It's not clear to me whether it adds value over the Django dataset of Oda et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 28, "text": "Code comment analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some programmers like to write comments, while some are not. Also, different programmers write comments very differently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I suggest the authors investigate the effect of such differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "One very simple way could be to analyze the occurrence frequency of interleaved natural and programming patterns in the dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "While it is hard to formally define meaningful comments, it would be insightful to at least calculate the document frequency of interleaved natural and programming language.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "In Table 3, the paper only compares the proposed method against GPT-Neo and GPT-J, which is not sufficient.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "The result will stand out to compare against Codex, the state-of-the-art program synthesis model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 56, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "CodeGen model (single-turn) is only evaluated on a single dataset with only 164 problems. Why is the model not evaluated on other publicly available coding datasets such as MBPP and APPS?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "While the multi-turn dataset helps the models generate more correct code, I am not sure about its practical use cases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Would a user be able to specify the sub-tasks in the required format (such as defining appropriate variables) as in Figure 1?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How does multi-turn task break down work for programs with control flow structures (for e.g. write code for binary search)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "From the examples, it appears that the MTPB dataset treats small loops and branches as a single sub-task, but how can a user specify sub-tasks within a loop/branch?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Novelty is limited since there are already several pre-training/dataset collection papers in this area.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For the MTPB dataset, I don't fully understand the part about embedding the test case inputs within the prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The MTPB dataset contribution needs more explanation regarding its practical use cases and its limited capability to handle more complex programs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 28, "text": "Code comment analysis", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some programmers like to write comments, while some are not. Also, different programmers write comments very differently.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I suggest the authors investigate the effect of such differences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "One very simple way could be to analyze the occurrence frequency of interleaved natural and programming patterns in the dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "While it is hard to formally define meaningful comments, it would be insightful to at least calculate the document frequency of interleaved natural and programming language.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "In Table 3, the paper only compares the proposed method against GPT-Neo and GPT-J, which is not sufficient.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "The result will stand out to compare against Codex, the state-of-the-art program synthesis model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 57, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "My main concern with this approach is that the paper does not discuss how to apply this approach to more general problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "The problems considered here are very simple, and an algorithm to solve them needs to be known in order to generate the training data.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "So, in its current form this does not enable any new abilities as we could use the base algorithm instead of using expensive LMs.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 7, "text": "In terms of the insight from a learning point-of-view, I also do not see anything surprising in this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "So the high accuracy of the resulting method is not that surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, the technique only seems to work for problems for which we already have algorithms, and it is unclear to me if this could be extended to more general problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "On the negative side, the solution proposed is formulated as a fully supervised problem, meaning for each task the exact steps needed to solve the problem needs to be annotated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "This is not a scalable approach as for realistic real-world applications obtaining detailed annotations for reasoning problems is an expert task which is expensive to scale to large datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "As a result, outside of tasks such as arithmetic operations where the dataset can be created automatically (and one could argue that you wouldn't use a task specific LM as the paper doesn't deal with multiple tasks or out-of-distribution problems), the applicability of this approach is minimal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The novelty is minimal as the solution proposed is rather trivial as it doesn't try to solve the hard problem of reasoning without direct supervision, or other settings such as few-shot/zero-shot/multi-task and formulates the problem as a fully supervised task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Unfortunately, I don't see much impact from this work in practical terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 57, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "My main concern with this approach is that the paper does not discuss how to apply this approach to more general problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "The problems considered here are very simple, and an algorithm to solve them needs to be known in order to generate the training data.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "So, in its current form this does not enable any new abilities as we could use the base algorithm instead of using expensive LMs.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 7, "text": "In terms of the insight from a learning point-of-view, I also do not see anything surprising in this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "So the high accuracy of the resulting method is not that surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, the technique only seems to work for problems for which we already have algorithms, and it is unclear to me if this could be extended to more general problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "One important point missing from this work is how to systematically construct subproblems given any problem in order to teach the model learn the recursion of thoughts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as it requires people to feed it with hand-designed subproblems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Therefore, it would be great if the authors could explain the limitation of the problems that RoT could solve and how to design proper subproblems in order to use RoT to train the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 57, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "On the negative side, the solution proposed is formulated as a fully supervised problem, meaning for each task the exact steps needed to solve the problem needs to be annotated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "This is not a scalable approach as for realistic real-world applications obtaining detailed annotations for reasoning problems is an expert task which is expensive to scale to large datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "As a result, outside of tasks such as arithmetic operations where the dataset can be created automatically (and one could argue that you wouldn't use a task specific LM as the paper doesn't deal with multiple tasks or out-of-distribution problems), the applicability of this approach is minimal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The novelty is minimal as the solution proposed is rather trivial as it doesn't try to solve the hard problem of reasoning without direct supervision, or other settings such as few-shot/zero-shot/multi-task and formulates the problem as a fully supervised task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Unfortunately, I don't see much impact from this work in practical terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "One important point missing from this work is how to systematically construct subproblems given any problem in order to teach the model learn the recursion of thoughts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as it requires people to feed it with hand-designed subproblems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Therefore, it would be great if the authors could explain the limitation of the problems that RoT could solve and how to design proper subproblems in order to use RoT to train the models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 58, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One potential shortcoming of the work is that its technical contribution may not be sufficient for a research paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In Sect. 4.2, the authors state that \"we restrict every entity to appear no more than 10 times to improve the diversity of generated knowledge\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This, in my opinion, is not reasonable because it would result in a sparse KG.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Table 2 also demonstrates this problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The framework of iterative KG construction (i.e., bootstrapping) is not novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some important technical details, e.g., how to generate multi-token entities and how to reduce the candidate set, are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The experiment is not comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "There is no objective evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Quality: The model is only evaluated by crowdsourcing, with no objective evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The effectiveness is not significant compared with SOTA baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The proposed framework is a kind of bootstrapping-based knowledge graph construction approach, which, reminisce of the Hearst patterns for hypernym relation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "My major concern is how to effectively handle the semantic drift, i.e., the semantics of new patterns and instance harvest may change drastically along with iteration or paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But authors did not discuss this problem in-depth or provide a method with a plausible bound.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "What we have in the model is the LM-based paraphrasing and weighting, however, the key point is, LM does not necessarily guarantee the semantics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 58, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One potential shortcoming of the work is that its technical contribution may not be sufficient for a research paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In Sect. 4.2, the authors state that \"we restrict every entity to appear no more than 10 times to improve the diversity of generated knowledge\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This, in my opinion, is not reasonable because it would result in a sparse KG.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Table 2 also demonstrates this problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses: Although the model shows better results than the baseline (COMET), it still lacks more analysis in the evaluation to convince the effectiveness of the proposed model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "First, the result in Table 2 shows the precision of the prediction but not the recall.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "For example, the recall can be estimated on the known set of factual relations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The P-R curves in Figures 3 and 4 seem to partly answer this question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "However, looking at the 0.8 level of precision, the recall is only less than 0.2, which raises another question about the overall effectiveness of the extraction method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The baseline WebChild in Table 2 seems to extract a much higher number of tuples at 4.6M, while the proposed model ROBERTANET only extract 6741 tuples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It can be WebChild having much higher coverage, but this difference was not discussed in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Second, the quality of the extraction heavily depends on the compatibility scoring function (Eq.1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be better to evaluate more on the design choice of this formula and explain why the proposed formula is optional for this purpose.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 58, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The framework of iterative KG construction (i.e., bootstrapping) is not novel.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some important technical details, e.g., how to generate multi-token entities and how to reduce the candidate set, are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The experiment is not comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "There is no objective evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Quality: The model is only evaluated by crowdsourcing, with no objective evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The effectiveness is not significant compared with SOTA baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The proposed framework is a kind of bootstrapping-based knowledge graph construction approach, which, reminisce of the Hearst patterns for hypernym relation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "My major concern is how to effectively handle the semantic drift, i.e., the semantics of new patterns and instance harvest may change drastically along with iteration or paraphrasing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But authors did not discuss this problem in-depth or provide a method with a plausible bound.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "What we have in the model is the LM-based paraphrasing and weighting, however, the key point is, LM does not necessarily guarantee the semantics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses: Although the model shows better results than the baseline (COMET), it still lacks more analysis in the evaluation to convince the effectiveness of the proposed model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "First, the result in Table 2 shows the precision of the prediction but not the recall.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "For example, the recall can be estimated on the known set of factual relations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The P-R curves in Figures 3 and 4 seem to partly answer this question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "However, looking at the 0.8 level of precision, the recall is only less than 0.2, which raises another question about the overall effectiveness of the extraction method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The baseline WebChild in Table 2 seems to extract a much higher number of tuples at 4.6M, while the proposed model ROBERTANET only extract 6741 tuples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It can be WebChild having much higher coverage, but this difference was not discussed in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Second, the quality of the extraction heavily depends on the compatibility scoring function (Eq.1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It would be better to evaluate more on the design choice of this formula and explain why the proposed formula is optional for this purpose.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 59, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Nothing significant", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Selecting subsets of tasks and exploring fine-tuning (2.1, 2.2) takes up a lot of the paper and it did not perform as well as pre-training or impact the performance of pre-training much and so maybe less space could have been dedicated to these sections.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The paper completely reuses the T5 strategy for multi-task learning and just scales it up with more tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While this scaling could have been an interesting study, the ablations and empirical investigation don\u2019t ask very interesting questions for this to be useful. I didn't find any significant recommendations for scaling tasks that can be reusable in future work or even applicable to models other than T5. Moreover, prior work (Aghajanyan et al., 2021) also studied most of these questions already, in the context of two pre-trained models, though in a slightly different setting. Over this and much other related work on multi-task and multi-lingual learning, the current paper only seems to contribute new results using the T5 model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Many of the results presented in motivating the work are over random subsets of pre-training tasks and random seeds for training. However, none of the results seem to give an idea of the variance of performance, nor any indication of how many different random subsets of tasks were used for the results.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The task scaling experiment (2.5) seems to be core to the contribution of the paper. Similar scaling experiments are also present in Aghajanyan et al., (2021). However, apart from the lack of an exposition of the variance in the quantities presented, I found this analysis to be very underwhelming.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Without any meaningful consideration of the choices available here, I am not sure what future work can take away from this paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Many hyper-parameters and design choices for the pre-training are also difficult to find and not clearly specified.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "However, I am concerned that this will be used as a point of reference to say that randomly adding more tasks always helps, for which the paper doesn't provide sufficient evidence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 59, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Nothing significant", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Selecting subsets of tasks and exploring fine-tuning (2.1, 2.2) takes up a lot of the paper and it did not perform as well as pre-training or impact the performance of pre-training much and so maybe less space could have been dedicated to these sections.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Section 2.5 uses the same learning rate for different batch sizes, which might result in unfair comparison given there are previous works suggesting how batch size, learning rate could have compound effects on the downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In section 2.4, will R beyond 10 still lead to consistent improvement of the SuperGLUE performance is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 59, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Nothing significant", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Selecting subsets of tasks and exploring fine-tuning (2.1, 2.2) takes up a lot of the paper and it did not perform as well as pre-training or impact the performance of pre-training much and so maybe less space could have been dedicated to these sections.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 28, "text": "The methodological novelty of the paper is fairly low.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "ExMix is a collection of pre-existing datasets and using multi-task signals during pre-training is not new either.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The scientific value of this work predominantly comes from the scale of their multi-task setting, and their analyses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "It is perhaps not surprising that a larger collection of supervised pre-training datasets would improve performance over these baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "All of section 2 is evaluated on average SuperGLUE performance only.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "While SuperGLUE is a strong baseline given the diverse set of tasks it encompasses, there is always the possibility that a subset of tasks within SuperGLUE are particularly well-suited for the ExT5 training setup and, as such, Section 2 risks overfitting to the SuperGLUE baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "There are some details missing regarding Section 2.4, especially with respect to random seeds.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "In particular, Figure 3 shows some interesting behavior regarding the hyperparameter R - it is surprising to me that performance does not monotonically decrease after a certain point but rather seems to be trending upwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Section 2.5 compares different numbers of tasks trained on different batch-sizes.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 39, "text": "However, the models use the same R parameter across numbers of tasks, which seems unfair to low-task settings given that it was tuned on 107 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Additionally, the models use the same learning rate regardless of batch-size, ostensibly a learning rate that was tuned for a batch-size of 512.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "It has been previously noted (e.g. by McCandlish et al., 2018) that there is a clear relationship between batch-size and learning rate; thus, if the same learning rates are being used across the 512 and 128 batch-size settings, it's likely that the results are heavily influenced by a poor learning rate for the 128 batch-size setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 59, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The paper completely reuses the T5 strategy for multi-task learning and just scales it up with more tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While this scaling could have been an interesting study, the ablations and empirical investigation don\u2019t ask very interesting questions for this to be useful. I didn't find any significant recommendations for scaling tasks that can be reusable in future work or even applicable to models other than T5. Moreover, prior work (Aghajanyan et al., 2021) also studied most of these questions already, in the context of two pre-trained models, though in a slightly different setting. Over this and much other related work on multi-task and multi-lingual learning, the current paper only seems to contribute new results using the T5 model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Many of the results presented in motivating the work are over random subsets of pre-training tasks and random seeds for training. However, none of the results seem to give an idea of the variance of performance, nor any indication of how many different random subsets of tasks were used for the results.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The task scaling experiment (2.5) seems to be core to the contribution of the paper. Similar scaling experiments are also present in Aghajanyan et al., (2021). However, apart from the lack of an exposition of the variance in the quantities presented, I found this analysis to be very underwhelming.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Without any meaningful consideration of the choices available here, I am not sure what future work can take away from this paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Many hyper-parameters and design choices for the pre-training are also difficult to find and not clearly specified.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "However, I am concerned that this will be used as a point of reference to say that randomly adding more tasks always helps, for which the paper doesn't provide sufficient evidence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Section 2.5 uses the same learning rate for different batch sizes, which might result in unfair comparison given there are previous works suggesting how batch size, learning rate could have compound effects on the downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In section 2.4, will R beyond 10 still lead to consistent improvement of the SuperGLUE performance is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 59, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The paper completely reuses the T5 strategy for multi-task learning and just scales it up with more tasks.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "While this scaling could have been an interesting study, the ablations and empirical investigation don\u2019t ask very interesting questions for this to be useful. I didn't find any significant recommendations for scaling tasks that can be reusable in future work or even applicable to models other than T5. Moreover, prior work (Aghajanyan et al., 2021) also studied most of these questions already, in the context of two pre-trained models, though in a slightly different setting. Over this and much other related work on multi-task and multi-lingual learning, the current paper only seems to contribute new results using the T5 model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Many of the results presented in motivating the work are over random subsets of pre-training tasks and random seeds for training. However, none of the results seem to give an idea of the variance of performance, nor any indication of how many different random subsets of tasks were used for the results.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The task scaling experiment (2.5) seems to be core to the contribution of the paper. Similar scaling experiments are also present in Aghajanyan et al., (2021). However, apart from the lack of an exposition of the variance in the quantities presented, I found this analysis to be very underwhelming.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Without any meaningful consideration of the choices available here, I am not sure what future work can take away from this paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Many hyper-parameters and design choices for the pre-training are also difficult to find and not clearly specified.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "However, I am concerned that this will be used as a point of reference to say that randomly adding more tasks always helps, for which the paper doesn't provide sufficient evidence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 28, "text": "The methodological novelty of the paper is fairly low.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "ExMix is a collection of pre-existing datasets and using multi-task signals during pre-training is not new either.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The scientific value of this work predominantly comes from the scale of their multi-task setting, and their analyses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "It is perhaps not surprising that a larger collection of supervised pre-training datasets would improve performance over these baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "All of section 2 is evaluated on average SuperGLUE performance only.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "While SuperGLUE is a strong baseline given the diverse set of tasks it encompasses, there is always the possibility that a subset of tasks within SuperGLUE are particularly well-suited for the ExT5 training setup and, as such, Section 2 risks overfitting to the SuperGLUE baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "There are some details missing regarding Section 2.4, especially with respect to random seeds.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "In particular, Figure 3 shows some interesting behavior regarding the hyperparameter R - it is surprising to me that performance does not monotonically decrease after a certain point but rather seems to be trending upwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Section 2.5 compares different numbers of tasks trained on different batch-sizes.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 39, "text": "However, the models use the same R parameter across numbers of tasks, which seems unfair to low-task settings given that it was tuned on 107 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Additionally, the models use the same learning rate regardless of batch-size, ostensibly a learning rate that was tuned for a batch-size of 512.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "It has been previously noted (e.g. by McCandlish et al., 2018) that there is a clear relationship between batch-size and learning rate; thus, if the same learning rates are being used across the 512 and 128 batch-size settings, it's likely that the results are heavily influenced by a poor learning rate for the 128 batch-size setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 59, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Section 2.5 uses the same learning rate for different batch sizes, which might result in unfair comparison given there are previous works suggesting how batch size, learning rate could have compound effects on the downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In section 2.4, will R beyond 10 still lead to consistent improvement of the SuperGLUE performance is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 28, "text": "The methodological novelty of the paper is fairly low.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "ExMix is a collection of pre-existing datasets and using multi-task signals during pre-training is not new either.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The scientific value of this work predominantly comes from the scale of their multi-task setting, and their analyses.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "It is perhaps not surprising that a larger collection of supervised pre-training datasets would improve performance over these baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "All of section 2 is evaluated on average SuperGLUE performance only.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "While SuperGLUE is a strong baseline given the diverse set of tasks it encompasses, there is always the possibility that a subset of tasks within SuperGLUE are particularly well-suited for the ExT5 training setup and, as such, Section 2 risks overfitting to the SuperGLUE baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "There are some details missing regarding Section 2.4, especially with respect to random seeds.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "In particular, Figure 3 shows some interesting behavior regarding the hyperparameter R - it is surprising to me that performance does not monotonically decrease after a certain point but rather seems to be trending upwards.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "Section 2.5 compares different numbers of tasks trained on different batch-sizes.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 39, "text": "However, the models use the same R parameter across numbers of tasks, which seems unfair to low-task settings given that it was tuned on 107 tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Additionally, the models use the same learning rate regardless of batch-size, ostensibly a learning rate that was tuned for a batch-size of 512.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "It has been previously noted (e.g. by McCandlish et al., 2018) that there is a clear relationship between batch-size and learning rate; thus, if the same learning rates are being used across the 512 and 128 batch-size settings, it's likely that the results are heavily influenced by a poor learning rate for the 128 batch-size setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 60, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Overall the idea of this work is novel, but the description is very hard to follow.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The lack of formal definitions, make it hard to understand how thrust is computed e.g., \"casting a set of instances ( the training data ) into the representation space \" The writing is mostly sloppy, e.g., c_0 is used before it is defined", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experiment result also need more explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example in Table 3 it is good to see that using knowledge 25% of the time is as good as using it 75% of the time for many tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, one might wonder why only the result of UnifiedQA is shown?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "why not also compare to the case using knowledge 100% of the time?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This paper is very unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This paper is very unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Evaluation with recent models like RETRO [1] and Atlas [2] is missing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Table 2, if my understanding is correct, with knowledge case is presented after the vertical bar (|).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "If that's true, please fix the description of the model which states the opposite.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 60, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Overall the idea of this work is novel, but the description is very hard to follow.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The lack of formal definitions, make it hard to understand how thrust is computed e.g., \"casting a set of instances ( the training data ) into the representation space \" The writing is mostly sloppy, e.g., c_0 is used before it is defined", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experiment result also need more explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example in Table 3 it is good to see that using knowledge 25% of the time is as good as using it 75% of the time for many tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, one might wonder why only the result of UnifiedQA is shown?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "why not also compare to the case using knowledge 100% of the time?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This paper is very unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This paper is very unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "A main weakness is that the work is not reproducible, with respect to both the methods and datasets.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Clarity: some important terms are not defined at all or until later in the paper, including the key term of external knowledge (which commonly means something else actually, as in knowledge graphs).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The experiments miss some important baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "There are some technical inaccuracies, e.g., the definition of the F1 measure is incorrect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "A main weakness is that the work is not reproducible.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It is not clear if the external knowledge used is published or not, and if and how it is possible to obtain it.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The methods (and experiments) are described informally, or obscurely, where the specific choices made are not motivated or compared to alternatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The related work section comes at a late phase of the reading, and finally makes it clear that the modeling of explicit knowledge as discussed throughout the paper is not at all a common practice (\"In this work, we pioneer the study by adding the knowledge in the plain text format.\")", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "This leap is hardly discussed or motivated in comparison to the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Also, the choice or selecting some fixed portion of the queries (e.g., 25 percent) for expansion is not motivated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "A baseline of selecting random instances rather the based on difficulty may be informative.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "There are some technical inaccuracies, e.g., the definition of the F1 measure is incorrect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "There are some typos and style issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 60, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Overall the idea of this work is novel, but the description is very hard to follow.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Subjective"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The lack of formal definitions, make it hard to understand how thrust is computed e.g., \"casting a set of instances ( the training data ) into the representation space \" The writing is mostly sloppy, e.g., c_0 is used before it is defined", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experiment result also need more explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For example in Table 3 it is good to see that using knowledge 25% of the time is as good as using it 75% of the time for many tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, one might wonder why only the result of UnifiedQA is shown?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "why not also compare to the case using knowledge 100% of the time?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This paper is very unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This paper is very unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The motivation and suggested method are not well-aligned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors claim that they simulate real-world cases, but the experimental setup is highly artificial, making the practicality of the algorithm dubious.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experiments are insufficient to show the effectiveness of Thrust.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors claim that they try to simulate the real-world case where they have limited bandwidth to retrieve external knowledge from the perspective of cost-efficiency, but the claim is not persuasive because the way they choose the portion of the queries to use external knowledge is through relative comparison between queries after scoring all other queries, and not through absolute scoring on each independent query, e.g., by applying a score threshold.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In other words, the suggested method seems impractical because each query would come to the system independently in the real world, not as a batch as in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Therefore, if cost-effectiveness is a concern, the authors should have proposed a method that works on each query independently to determine whether that query itself requires external knowledge or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Comparing the results in Table 2 and 3, on many of the tasks, using Thrust to partially utilize the external knowledge drops the performance compared to when all queries are answered with external knowledge (e.g., 70.2 \u2192 56.8 (75% of queries) on e-SNLI UnifiedQA-base, 80 \u2192 73.4% (75% of queries) for TriviaQA UnifiedQA-3b).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Therefore, the cost-effectiveness of Thrust, if it exists, comes with a sacrifice of accuracy for many cases, creating a trade-off.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Meanwhile, it is also unclear whether the method is actually cost-effective because the calculation of the Thrust score would also take up some inference time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The authors should compare the average inference speed between when all queries use the external knowledge as the input, and when part of the queries use the external knowledge while the Thrust score is calculated for all queries.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors only compare Thrust with random baseline, but in order to show the effectiveness of Thrust as a scoring metric, they should compare the algorithm with baselines simpler than Thrust but better than random, e.g., using BM25 scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "[Minor point] While the number of external knowledge instances to utilize and the method of utilizing knowledge might vary, the authors seem to test the effectiveness of Thrust with only one combination of them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The authors did not clearly indicate how many external knowledge instances are used for a query, but it seems like one by inferring from the context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also, they test only the case of using external knowledge as part of the input and did not explore the cross-attention-based method (e.g., FiD).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The misalignment between the motivation and the experiments makes the paper\u2019s point unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, the authors did not indicate the number of clusters they used in the experiments, and how many knowledge instances they used to augment a query (which seems to be one).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "In the caption of Table 2, \u201cPerformances with/without knowledge external knowledge are presented before/after the vertical bar, respectively,\u201d it should be without/with based on the description of the results in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Such an important typo makes the paper difficult to understand.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 60, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Evaluation with recent models like RETRO [1] and Atlas [2] is missing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Table 2, if my understanding is correct, with knowledge case is presented after the vertical bar (|).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "If that's true, please fix the description of the model which states the opposite.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "A main weakness is that the work is not reproducible, with respect to both the methods and datasets.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Clarity: some important terms are not defined at all or until later in the paper, including the key term of external knowledge (which commonly means something else actually, as in knowledge graphs).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The experiments miss some important baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "There are some technical inaccuracies, e.g., the definition of the F1 measure is incorrect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "A main weakness is that the work is not reproducible.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It is not clear if the external knowledge used is published or not, and if and how it is possible to obtain it.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The methods (and experiments) are described informally, or obscurely, where the specific choices made are not motivated or compared to alternatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The related work section comes at a late phase of the reading, and finally makes it clear that the modeling of explicit knowledge as discussed throughout the paper is not at all a common practice (\"In this work, we pioneer the study by adding the knowledge in the plain text format.\")", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "This leap is hardly discussed or motivated in comparison to the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Also, the choice or selecting some fixed portion of the queries (e.g., 25 percent) for expansion is not motivated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "A baseline of selecting random instances rather the based on difficulty may be informative.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "There are some technical inaccuracies, e.g., the definition of the F1 measure is incorrect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "There are some typos and style issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 60, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Evaluation with recent models like RETRO [1] and Atlas [2] is missing.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Table 2, if my understanding is correct, with knowledge case is presented after the vertical bar (|).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "If that's true, please fix the description of the model which states the opposite.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The motivation and suggested method are not well-aligned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors claim that they simulate real-world cases, but the experimental setup is highly artificial, making the practicality of the algorithm dubious.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experiments are insufficient to show the effectiveness of Thrust.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors claim that they try to simulate the real-world case where they have limited bandwidth to retrieve external knowledge from the perspective of cost-efficiency, but the claim is not persuasive because the way they choose the portion of the queries to use external knowledge is through relative comparison between queries after scoring all other queries, and not through absolute scoring on each independent query, e.g., by applying a score threshold.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In other words, the suggested method seems impractical because each query would come to the system independently in the real world, not as a batch as in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Therefore, if cost-effectiveness is a concern, the authors should have proposed a method that works on each query independently to determine whether that query itself requires external knowledge or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Comparing the results in Table 2 and 3, on many of the tasks, using Thrust to partially utilize the external knowledge drops the performance compared to when all queries are answered with external knowledge (e.g., 70.2 \u2192 56.8 (75% of queries) on e-SNLI UnifiedQA-base, 80 \u2192 73.4% (75% of queries) for TriviaQA UnifiedQA-3b).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Therefore, the cost-effectiveness of Thrust, if it exists, comes with a sacrifice of accuracy for many cases, creating a trade-off.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Meanwhile, it is also unclear whether the method is actually cost-effective because the calculation of the Thrust score would also take up some inference time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The authors should compare the average inference speed between when all queries use the external knowledge as the input, and when part of the queries use the external knowledge while the Thrust score is calculated for all queries.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors only compare Thrust with random baseline, but in order to show the effectiveness of Thrust as a scoring metric, they should compare the algorithm with baselines simpler than Thrust but better than random, e.g., using BM25 scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "[Minor point] While the number of external knowledge instances to utilize and the method of utilizing knowledge might vary, the authors seem to test the effectiveness of Thrust with only one combination of them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The authors did not clearly indicate how many external knowledge instances are used for a query, but it seems like one by inferring from the context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also, they test only the case of using external knowledge as part of the input and did not explore the cross-attention-based method (e.g., FiD).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The misalignment between the motivation and the experiments makes the paper\u2019s point unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, the authors did not indicate the number of clusters they used in the experiments, and how many knowledge instances they used to augment a query (which seems to be one).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "In the caption of Table 2, \u201cPerformances with/without knowledge external knowledge are presented before/after the vertical bar, respectively,\u201d it should be without/with based on the description of the results in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Such an important typo makes the paper difficult to understand.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 60, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "A main weakness is that the work is not reproducible, with respect to both the methods and datasets.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Clarity: some important terms are not defined at all or until later in the paper, including the key term of external knowledge (which commonly means something else actually, as in knowledge graphs).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Writing"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The experiments miss some important baselines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "There are some technical inaccuracies, e.g., the definition of the F1 measure is incorrect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "A main weakness is that the work is not reproducible.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It is not clear if the external knowledge used is published or not, and if and how it is possible to obtain it.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The methods (and experiments) are described informally, or obscurely, where the specific choices made are not motivated or compared to alternatives.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The related work section comes at a late phase of the reading, and finally makes it clear that the modeling of explicit knowledge as discussed throughout the paper is not at all a common practice (\"In this work, we pioneer the study by adding the knowledge in the plain text format.\")", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "This leap is hardly discussed or motivated in comparison to the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Also, the choice or selecting some fixed portion of the queries (e.g., 25 percent) for expansion is not motivated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "A baseline of selecting random instances rather the based on difficulty may be informative.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "There are some technical inaccuracies, e.g., the definition of the F1 measure is incorrect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "There are some typos and style issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The motivation and suggested method are not well-aligned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The authors claim that they simulate real-world cases, but the experimental setup is highly artificial, making the practicality of the algorithm dubious.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experiments are insufficient to show the effectiveness of Thrust.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors claim that they try to simulate the real-world case where they have limited bandwidth to retrieve external knowledge from the perspective of cost-efficiency, but the claim is not persuasive because the way they choose the portion of the queries to use external knowledge is through relative comparison between queries after scoring all other queries, and not through absolute scoring on each independent query, e.g., by applying a score threshold.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In other words, the suggested method seems impractical because each query would come to the system independently in the real world, not as a batch as in the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Therefore, if cost-effectiveness is a concern, the authors should have proposed a method that works on each query independently to determine whether that query itself requires external knowledge or not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Comparing the results in Table 2 and 3, on many of the tasks, using Thrust to partially utilize the external knowledge drops the performance compared to when all queries are answered with external knowledge (e.g., 70.2 \u2192 56.8 (75% of queries) on e-SNLI UnifiedQA-base, 80 \u2192 73.4% (75% of queries) for TriviaQA UnifiedQA-3b).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Therefore, the cost-effectiveness of Thrust, if it exists, comes with a sacrifice of accuracy for many cases, creating a trade-off.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Meanwhile, it is also unclear whether the method is actually cost-effective because the calculation of the Thrust score would also take up some inference time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The authors should compare the average inference speed between when all queries use the external knowledge as the input, and when part of the queries use the external knowledge while the Thrust score is calculated for all queries.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The authors only compare Thrust with random baseline, but in order to show the effectiveness of Thrust as a scoring metric, they should compare the algorithm with baselines simpler than Thrust but better than random, e.g., using BM25 scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "[Minor point] While the number of external knowledge instances to utilize and the method of utilizing knowledge might vary, the authors seem to test the effectiveness of Thrust with only one combination of them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The authors did not clearly indicate how many external knowledge instances are used for a query, but it seems like one by inferring from the context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Also, they test only the case of using external knowledge as part of the input and did not explore the cross-attention-based method (e.g., FiD).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The misalignment between the motivation and the experiments makes the paper\u2019s point unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "For example, the authors did not indicate the number of clusters they used in the experiments, and how many knowledge instances they used to augment a query (which seems to be one).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "In the caption of Table 2, \u201cPerformances with/without knowledge external knowledge are presented before/after the vertical bar, respectively,\u201d it should be without/with based on the description of the results in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Such an important typo makes the paper difficult to understand.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 61, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper does not achieve the expectations set by the title and abstract.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I was expecting challenging tasks where humans struggle to find a proper prompt (like leading Dall-e to generate a horse riding an astronaut, which was cited as a limitation of Dall-e until someone found the proper prompt) but the proposed method can automatically find it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Instead, most of the tasks can be solved with human-level accuracy using just the enumeration of a few input/output pairs as prompts (Fig. 4). These pairs would be available in any case since they are needed by the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Taking a look at Table1, most tasks require surface-level understanding of language syntax.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Translation task is only done for single words. Semantic tasks from GLUE are at 90+% accuracy with simple transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Few tasks here require complex reasoning, or deeper language understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I think those tasks require creating more nuanced instructions and seeing how this approach compares against humans would be very interesting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Until then. it's unclear to me how this approach generalizes beyond simple tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "To build on my previous point, looking at figure 3, results on relatively harder tasks such as sentence similarity and membership show APE is doing worse than humans and vanilla generated prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The authors acknowledge this in the paper: \"Additionally, tasks such as Membership and Second Letter are intrinsically challenging for the model, and APE consistently performs worse than humans.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "However, I'm not quite convinced if this approach can scale to more challenging NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 61, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper does not achieve the expectations set by the title and abstract.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I was expecting challenging tasks where humans struggle to find a proper prompt (like leading Dall-e to generate a horse riding an astronaut, which was cited as a limitation of Dall-e until someone found the proper prompt) but the proposed method can automatically find it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Instead, most of the tasks can be solved with human-level accuracy using just the enumeration of a few input/output pairs as prompts (Fig. 4). These pairs would be available in any case since they are needed by the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The paper though is not very well written and there are a few parts that require some clarification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The assumptions/intuitions in section 3.1 are not clear. For example why \"would like the model to predict the missing context before the demonstrations\"? What does it mean more versatile approach?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I understand that for every set of training pair there will be a possible different instruction, do you consolidate them?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Which one do you choose at inference time?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The paper is hard to read although the approach is quite straightforward.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 61, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Taking a look at Table1, most tasks require surface-level understanding of language syntax.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Translation task is only done for single words. Semantic tasks from GLUE are at 90+% accuracy with simple transformer models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Few tasks here require complex reasoning, or deeper language understanding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I think those tasks require creating more nuanced instructions and seeing how this approach compares against humans would be very interesting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Until then. it's unclear to me how this approach generalizes beyond simple tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "To build on my previous point, looking at figure 3, results on relatively harder tasks such as sentence similarity and membership show APE is doing worse than humans and vanilla generated prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The authors acknowledge this in the paper: \"Additionally, tasks such as Membership and Second Letter are intrinsically challenging for the model, and APE consistently performs worse than humans.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "However, I'm not quite convinced if this approach can scale to more challenging NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The paper though is not very well written and there are a few parts that require some clarification.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The assumptions/intuitions in section 3.1 are not clear. For example why \"would like the model to predict the missing context before the demonstrations\"? What does it mean more versatile approach?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I understand that for every set of training pair there will be a possible different instruction, do you consolidate them?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Which one do you choose at inference time?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The paper is hard to read although the approach is quite straightforward.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 62, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "One could say that the paper lacks technical novelty, but to me, a simple paper with extensive results that work is a better than a technically rich paper that doesn't work as well.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misplaced attributes"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In a way, we can see the multi tasks as a single giant task with many domains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If we view this as question answering, the paper is essentially training a system that learns to answer well given various domains of questions/tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What would also be interesting is to see/what's missing in the paper, is to carefully study the difference in domains of the training tasks, versus zero-shot evaluation tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Repeated experiments with different subsets of training/eval tasks can help shed some light as well.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I do understand that it would be an empirically expensive set of experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The conclusion of this paper is that multi-task supervised learning makes the model generalize well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, we still rely on a vast amount of supervised data. In a way, the comparison with GPT-3 is not too fair.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The idea of converting all the tasks to an unified format is not new, and has been explored in earlier works like DecaNLP, UFO-Entail, T5 and more recently in the CLUES few-shot benchmark.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Now, coming back to the experiments, the authors partition the task types into training and hold-out a subset of the tasks, including all the datasets therein, for evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors perform an important study to verify that data for those tasks is not leaked through the pretraining corpus which addresses memorization concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "However, this analysis is missing for the training tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Since the objective of this work, is to evaluate \"true\" zero-shot performance of the models, it is necessary to perform this analysis to ensure that any text for the \"unseen\" tasks and datasets is not leaked from the training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This is important since it is often difficult to explicitly partition tasks into disjoint sets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For instance, the authors evaluate their models on held-out NLI tasks, but train their models on paraphrase identification tasks that bear some similarity with the entailment tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can the authors report the multi-task model performance on NLI tasks without using the paraphrase identification tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors compare the performance of their model and demonstrate the multi-task version (T0) to perform better than the single-task version (T5-LM) and outperform GPT-3 in primarily NLI tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The performance in WiC seems a bit strange given that random choice should give an accuracy of 50%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally, some analysis on transferability of the tasks, to study the impact of different source tasks on target tasks, will be an interesting contribution for the multi-task setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The claim of \"true\" zero-shot generalization needs to be further supported by two more experiments as highlighted in the main review, namely, (i) analyzing the overlap in text from the MTL setup with that of the unseen tasks, and (ii) ablating paraphrase identification tasks to find the impact on NLI tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 62, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "One could say that the paper lacks technical novelty, but to me, a simple paper with extensive results that work is a better than a technically rich paper that doesn't work as well.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misplaced attributes"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In a way, we can see the multi tasks as a single giant task with many domains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If we view this as question answering, the paper is essentially training a system that learns to answer well given various domains of questions/tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What would also be interesting is to see/what's missing in the paper, is to carefully study the difference in domains of the training tasks, versus zero-shot evaluation tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Repeated experiments with different subsets of training/eval tasks can help shed some light as well.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I do understand that it would be an empirically expensive set of experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The conclusion of this paper is that multi-task supervised learning makes the model generalize well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, we still rely on a vast amount of supervised data. In a way, the comparison with GPT-3 is not too fair.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are some major comments which are presented here,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For the same reason, question answering or paraphrasing can be selected, since humans are not explicitly trained on these tasks!", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 34, "text": "13- the contribution is not clear. if it is about the benefit of encoder-decoder model to decoder-only LM models for zero-shot learning, then a decoder-only LM model should be trained on the multi-tasked prompted training set. if the contribution is about the proposed multi-tasked prompted dataset", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 44, "text": "16- The performance of T0 can also be compared with FLAN model in Figure 4 for each zero-shot task, despite different training set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "17- Section 7: the author mentions a key difference between T0 and FLAN model is that T0 is an encoder-decoder model which is pretrained and finetuned with different objectives. However, no clear comparison with FLAN model is presented in the paper", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 46, "text": "18- Section 7: author mentioned that FLAN model with comparable size to T0 (8B) finds that increasing multi-task training reduce zero-shot performance, whereas they find the opposite.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "The only evaluation on the size of multi-task training dataset is presented on BigBench dataset in Figure 5, which is different than FLAN zero-shot evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "However, there are some shortcomings,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "1- The zero-shot evaluations are based on some selected tasks, including NLI, story completion, word sense disambiguation, and coreference resolution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 54, "text": "2- The author mentioned the benefits of encoder-decoder pretraining objective (target-only generation) to auto-regressive objective (input generation) as a key factor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "However, there are no comparison with auto-regressive model with the same pretraining and zero-shot task evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "The only comparison is on the BigBench dataset, where Transformer-based language models are pretrained with different dataset, and also performance comparison on NLI, story completion, word sense disambiguation, and coreference resolution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "3- The paper is centered around understanding the multi-task pretraining, using explicit supervised learning of model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "However, the evaluated model (T0) is using pretrained weights of T5 for training, which implicitly learned multi-tasking during unsupervised pretraining of T5 model. This makes the evaluation biased and an unfair comparison to GPT3 model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 62, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "One could say that the paper lacks technical novelty, but to me, a simple paper with extensive results that work is a better than a technically rich paper that doesn't work as well.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misplaced attributes"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In a way, we can see the multi tasks as a single giant task with many domains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If we view this as question answering, the paper is essentially training a system that learns to answer well given various domains of questions/tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What would also be interesting is to see/what's missing in the paper, is to carefully study the difference in domains of the training tasks, versus zero-shot evaluation tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Repeated experiments with different subsets of training/eval tasks can help shed some light as well.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, I do understand that it would be an empirically expensive set of experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The conclusion of this paper is that multi-task supervised learning makes the model generalize well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, we still rely on a vast amount of supervised data. In a way, the comparison with GPT-3 is not too fair.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I feel like your main baseline is the T5+LM model (T5 finetuned on the causal LMing task).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Therefore I think it would make the paper much stronger if you were to show how that baseline performs on the tasks in Figure 5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I understand that the baselines that you use in Figure 5 were built and trained by an external third party, so I trust that these baselines aren\u2019t too weak or unoptimzed somehow, but it would still be very important to show that you also improve over T5 in this setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It would also be very interesting to see how well GPT-3 performs on those tasks, but I did not and will not penalize you for not having those numbers, as the GPT-3 API is not fully open and free.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "This paper seems like an extension of ideas discussed in iPET/PET (\u201cIt\u2019s Not Just Size That Matters: Small Language Models Are Also Few-Shot Learners\u201d, Schick & Schutze), and so to me it feels like it would be quite important to compare to that model in at least some of the experiments that you run, for example, if Figure 4 compared to iPET I feel the paper would be much stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "What do you think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Are T0 and iPET comparable?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The authors ask two very interesting research questions with regards to prompting and finetuning of large LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "A wide array of experiments answers these questions, and I think this will lead to better usage of LMs in the future.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "I did point out that there are some baselines that the authors missed, but I don\u2019t think that that\u2019s a big deal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "I recommend that this paper be accepted, and feel that it will be very valuable to the NLP community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 62, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The idea of converting all the tasks to an unified format is not new, and has been explored in earlier works like DecaNLP, UFO-Entail, T5 and more recently in the CLUES few-shot benchmark.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Now, coming back to the experiments, the authors partition the task types into training and hold-out a subset of the tasks, including all the datasets therein, for evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors perform an important study to verify that data for those tasks is not leaked through the pretraining corpus which addresses memorization concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "However, this analysis is missing for the training tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Since the objective of this work, is to evaluate \"true\" zero-shot performance of the models, it is necessary to perform this analysis to ensure that any text for the \"unseen\" tasks and datasets is not leaked from the training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This is important since it is often difficult to explicitly partition tasks into disjoint sets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For instance, the authors evaluate their models on held-out NLI tasks, but train their models on paraphrase identification tasks that bear some similarity with the entailment tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can the authors report the multi-task model performance on NLI tasks without using the paraphrase identification tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors compare the performance of their model and demonstrate the multi-task version (T0) to perform better than the single-task version (T5-LM) and outperform GPT-3 in primarily NLI tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The performance in WiC seems a bit strange given that random choice should give an accuracy of 50%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally, some analysis on transferability of the tasks, to study the impact of different source tasks on target tasks, will be an interesting contribution for the multi-task setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The claim of \"true\" zero-shot generalization needs to be further supported by two more experiments as highlighted in the main review, namely, (i) analyzing the overlap in text from the MTL setup with that of the unseen tasks, and (ii) ablating paraphrase identification tasks to find the impact on NLI tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are some major comments which are presented here,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For the same reason, question answering or paraphrasing can be selected, since humans are not explicitly trained on these tasks!", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 34, "text": "13- the contribution is not clear. if it is about the benefit of encoder-decoder model to decoder-only LM models for zero-shot learning, then a decoder-only LM model should be trained on the multi-tasked prompted training set. if the contribution is about the proposed multi-tasked prompted dataset", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 44, "text": "16- The performance of T0 can also be compared with FLAN model in Figure 4 for each zero-shot task, despite different training set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "17- Section 7: the author mentions a key difference between T0 and FLAN model is that T0 is an encoder-decoder model which is pretrained and finetuned with different objectives. However, no clear comparison with FLAN model is presented in the paper", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 46, "text": "18- Section 7: author mentioned that FLAN model with comparable size to T0 (8B) finds that increasing multi-task training reduce zero-shot performance, whereas they find the opposite.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "The only evaluation on the size of multi-task training dataset is presented on BigBench dataset in Figure 5, which is different than FLAN zero-shot evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "However, there are some shortcomings,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "1- The zero-shot evaluations are based on some selected tasks, including NLI, story completion, word sense disambiguation, and coreference resolution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 54, "text": "2- The author mentioned the benefits of encoder-decoder pretraining objective (target-only generation) to auto-regressive objective (input generation) as a key factor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "However, there are no comparison with auto-regressive model with the same pretraining and zero-shot task evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "The only comparison is on the BigBench dataset, where Transformer-based language models are pretrained with different dataset, and also performance comparison on NLI, story completion, word sense disambiguation, and coreference resolution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "3- The paper is centered around understanding the multi-task pretraining, using explicit supervised learning of model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "However, the evaluated model (T0) is using pretrained weights of T5 for training, which implicitly learned multi-tasking during unsupervised pretraining of T5 model. This makes the evaluation biased and an unfair comparison to GPT3 model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 62, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The idea of converting all the tasks to an unified format is not new, and has been explored in earlier works like DecaNLP, UFO-Entail, T5 and more recently in the CLUES few-shot benchmark.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Now, coming back to the experiments, the authors partition the task types into training and hold-out a subset of the tasks, including all the datasets therein, for evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors perform an important study to verify that data for those tasks is not leaked through the pretraining corpus which addresses memorization concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "However, this analysis is missing for the training tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Since the objective of this work, is to evaluate \"true\" zero-shot performance of the models, it is necessary to perform this analysis to ensure that any text for the \"unseen\" tasks and datasets is not leaked from the training corpus.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This is important since it is often difficult to explicitly partition tasks into disjoint sets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For instance, the authors evaluate their models on held-out NLI tasks, but train their models on paraphrase identification tasks that bear some similarity with the entailment tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Can the authors report the multi-task model performance on NLI tasks without using the paraphrase identification tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors compare the performance of their model and demonstrate the multi-task version (T0) to perform better than the single-task version (T5-LM) and outperform GPT-3 in primarily NLI tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The performance in WiC seems a bit strange given that random choice should give an accuracy of 50%.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Finally, some analysis on transferability of the tasks, to study the impact of different source tasks on target tasks, will be an interesting contribution for the multi-task setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The claim of \"true\" zero-shot generalization needs to be further supported by two more experiments as highlighted in the main review, namely, (i) analyzing the overlap in text from the MTL setup with that of the unseen tasks, and (ii) ablating paraphrase identification tasks to find the impact on NLI tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I feel like your main baseline is the T5+LM model (T5 finetuned on the causal LMing task).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Therefore I think it would make the paper much stronger if you were to show how that baseline performs on the tasks in Figure 5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I understand that the baselines that you use in Figure 5 were built and trained by an external third party, so I trust that these baselines aren\u2019t too weak or unoptimzed somehow, but it would still be very important to show that you also improve over T5 in this setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It would also be very interesting to see how well GPT-3 performs on those tasks, but I did not and will not penalize you for not having those numbers, as the GPT-3 API is not fully open and free.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "This paper seems like an extension of ideas discussed in iPET/PET (\u201cIt\u2019s Not Just Size That Matters: Small Language Models Are Also Few-Shot Learners\u201d, Schick & Schutze), and so to me it feels like it would be quite important to compare to that model in at least some of the experiments that you run, for example, if Figure 4 compared to iPET I feel the paper would be much stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "What do you think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Are T0 and iPET comparable?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The authors ask two very interesting research questions with regards to prompting and finetuning of large LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "A wide array of experiments answers these questions, and I think this will lead to better usage of LMs in the future.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "I did point out that there are some baselines that the authors missed, but I don\u2019t think that that\u2019s a big deal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "I recommend that this paper be accepted, and feel that it will be very valuable to the NLP community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 62, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "There are some major comments which are presented here,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "For the same reason, question answering or paraphrasing can be selected, since humans are not explicitly trained on these tasks!", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 34, "text": "13- the contribution is not clear. if it is about the benefit of encoder-decoder model to decoder-only LM models for zero-shot learning, then a decoder-only LM model should be trained on the multi-tasked prompted training set. if the contribution is about the proposed multi-tasked prompted dataset", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 44, "text": "16- The performance of T0 can also be compared with FLAN model in Figure 4 for each zero-shot task, despite different training set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "17- Section 7: the author mentions a key difference between T0 and FLAN model is that T0 is an encoder-decoder model which is pretrained and finetuned with different objectives. However, no clear comparison with FLAN model is presented in the paper", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 46, "text": "18- Section 7: author mentioned that FLAN model with comparable size to T0 (8B) finds that increasing multi-task training reduce zero-shot performance, whereas they find the opposite.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "The only evaluation on the size of multi-task training dataset is presented on BigBench dataset in Figure 5, which is different than FLAN zero-shot evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "However, there are some shortcomings,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "1- The zero-shot evaluations are based on some selected tasks, including NLI, story completion, word sense disambiguation, and coreference resolution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "In this setting, other tasks such as paraphrasing, QA, sentiment analysis, summarization, topic classification are not considered as zero-shot tasks, and only used for training, which makes an incomplete evaluation for zero-shot learning.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 54, "text": "2- The author mentioned the benefits of encoder-decoder pretraining objective (target-only generation) to auto-regressive objective (input generation) as a key factor.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "However, there are no comparison with auto-regressive model with the same pretraining and zero-shot task evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "The only comparison is on the BigBench dataset, where Transformer-based language models are pretrained with different dataset, and also performance comparison on NLI, story completion, word sense disambiguation, and coreference resolution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "3- The paper is centered around understanding the multi-task pretraining, using explicit supervised learning of model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "However, the evaluated model (T0) is using pretrained weights of T5 for training, which implicitly learned multi-tasking during unsupervised pretraining of T5 model. This makes the evaluation biased and an unfair comparison to GPT3 model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I feel like your main baseline is the T5+LM model (T5 finetuned on the causal LMing task).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Therefore I think it would make the paper much stronger if you were to show how that baseline performs on the tasks in Figure 5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I understand that the baselines that you use in Figure 5 were built and trained by an external third party, so I trust that these baselines aren\u2019t too weak or unoptimzed somehow, but it would still be very important to show that you also improve over T5 in this setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It would also be very interesting to see how well GPT-3 performs on those tasks, but I did not and will not penalize you for not having those numbers, as the GPT-3 API is not fully open and free.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "This paper seems like an extension of ideas discussed in iPET/PET (\u201cIt\u2019s Not Just Size That Matters: Small Language Models Are Also Few-Shot Learners\u201d, Schick & Schutze), and so to me it feels like it would be quite important to compare to that model in at least some of the experiments that you run, for example, if Figure 4 compared to iPET I feel the paper would be much stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "What do you think?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Are T0 and iPET comparable?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The authors ask two very interesting research questions with regards to prompting and finetuning of large LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "A wide array of experiments answers these questions, and I think this will lead to better usage of LMs in the future.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "I did point out that there are some baselines that the authors missed, but I don\u2019t think that that\u2019s a big deal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "I recommend that this paper be accepted, and feel that it will be very valuable to the NLP community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 63, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "That said, I have several concerns that make me question some of the claims of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The human evaluation shows very little performance difference between generation with data prefix (D-prefix) and model prefix (M-prefix), suggesting exposure bias is not a problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "But I would argue this is mostly an artifact of the experimental setup, as prefixing generation with D-string (of length L) only eliminates exposure bias up to position L, and then both evaluated systems (according to the generation process defined Section 4.1) use a standard auto-regressive LM generation process that makes them both subject to the exposure bias problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Since these generated strings are of length 30, there is plenty of room for exposure bias to crop in.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "(Indeed, Zhang et al., (2019) and Holtzman et al. (2019) have shown concrete examples of exposure bias artifacts appearing in much shorter sequences).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "So, the small performance difference between a preference M-prefixed and D-prefixed generation may very well be due to both setups being almost identical (same model, same auto-regressive inference algorithm, and the only difference is the prefix \u2013 which human raters are not even asked to judge directly).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If exposure bias is indeed a problem, then it would affect both systems almost the same way, so this human evaluation can\u2019t be used to either affirm or deny exposure bias is a significant problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I also have concerns regarding the automatic evaluation based on BLEU.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What the authors call \u201ccorpus-BLEU\u201d is actually not the standard version of BLEU (see \u201cOther Comments\u201d below), as the version of the paper looks at n-gram (n=1 to 3) matches between the generated sentences and a large set of references that is not specific to a particular context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "As the paper\u2019s automatic evaluation is done in a completely context-agnostic way and relative to a large pool of references, it essentially only measures whether the model (with or w/o exposure bias) is able to generate plausible trigrams, but that sets the bar very low as we already have plenty of evidence showing neural language models are quite capable of generating reasonable trigrams (whether there is exposure bias or not), and in fact often much longer n-grams.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors claim that auto-regressive models appear to have \u201cself-recovery ability\u201d that mitigate any exposure bias, but I would say that it is hard to claim anything about self-recovery (at least in terms of automatic evaluation) when the evaluation metric operates over such a small window (<=3 words).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Abstract: \u201cAlthough a lot of algorithms have been proposed to avoid teacher forcing and therefore alleviate exposure bias, there is little work showing how serious the exposure bias problem actually is.\u201d I find this claim a bit misguided, as the numerous papers addressing exposure bias are empirical ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "They have shown improvements in various tasks such as machine translation, image captioning, and other generation tasks thanks to techniques aimed at reducing exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Now, one could claim that significant improvements shown in these papers are due to reasons other than exposure bias, but if that is the case then the submission doesn\u2019t do a much better job at isolating exposure bias from other factors (given my concerns in (1) and (2)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Note that Zhang et al. (2019) actually does include a short section attempting to quantify the effect of exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For a paper that attempts to challenge the current understanding of a problem that has received very significant attention (i.e., exposure bias), it is quite thin in terms of related work (2 paragraphs).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The paper omitted important related work (e.g., Schmidt, 2019; Tan et al., 2019; Rennie et al., 2016), including an ACL best paper on the same topic and whose findings appear to be at odds with the current submission.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Concerns: The experiments only look at pure sampling from the model---i.e., they don't use temperature, nucleus sampling, greedy or beam search.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "These other generation settings are more important for applications, compared to pure generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Also, they have been reported to exhibit more deviation from the corpus distribution (especially with respect to particular pathologies like repetition).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Evaluating generation in these other settings would increase the impact of the paper's conclusions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 63, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "That said, I have several concerns that make me question some of the claims of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The human evaluation shows very little performance difference between generation with data prefix (D-prefix) and model prefix (M-prefix), suggesting exposure bias is not a problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "But I would argue this is mostly an artifact of the experimental setup, as prefixing generation with D-string (of length L) only eliminates exposure bias up to position L, and then both evaluated systems (according to the generation process defined Section 4.1) use a standard auto-regressive LM generation process that makes them both subject to the exposure bias problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Since these generated strings are of length 30, there is plenty of room for exposure bias to crop in.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "(Indeed, Zhang et al., (2019) and Holtzman et al. (2019) have shown concrete examples of exposure bias artifacts appearing in much shorter sequences).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "So, the small performance difference between a preference M-prefixed and D-prefixed generation may very well be due to both setups being almost identical (same model, same auto-regressive inference algorithm, and the only difference is the prefix \u2013 which human raters are not even asked to judge directly).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If exposure bias is indeed a problem, then it would affect both systems almost the same way, so this human evaluation can\u2019t be used to either affirm or deny exposure bias is a significant problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I also have concerns regarding the automatic evaluation based on BLEU.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What the authors call \u201ccorpus-BLEU\u201d is actually not the standard version of BLEU (see \u201cOther Comments\u201d below), as the version of the paper looks at n-gram (n=1 to 3) matches between the generated sentences and a large set of references that is not specific to a particular context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "As the paper\u2019s automatic evaluation is done in a completely context-agnostic way and relative to a large pool of references, it essentially only measures whether the model (with or w/o exposure bias) is able to generate plausible trigrams, but that sets the bar very low as we already have plenty of evidence showing neural language models are quite capable of generating reasonable trigrams (whether there is exposure bias or not), and in fact often much longer n-grams.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors claim that auto-regressive models appear to have \u201cself-recovery ability\u201d that mitigate any exposure bias, but I would say that it is hard to claim anything about self-recovery (at least in terms of automatic evaluation) when the evaluation metric operates over such a small window (<=3 words).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Abstract: \u201cAlthough a lot of algorithms have been proposed to avoid teacher forcing and therefore alleviate exposure bias, there is little work showing how serious the exposure bias problem actually is.\u201d I find this claim a bit misguided, as the numerous papers addressing exposure bias are empirical ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "They have shown improvements in various tasks such as machine translation, image captioning, and other generation tasks thanks to techniques aimed at reducing exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Now, one could claim that significant improvements shown in these papers are due to reasons other than exposure bias, but if that is the case then the submission doesn\u2019t do a much better job at isolating exposure bias from other factors (given my concerns in (1) and (2)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Note that Zhang et al. (2019) actually does include a short section attempting to quantify the effect of exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For a paper that attempts to challenge the current understanding of a problem that has received very significant attention (i.e., exposure bias), it is quite thin in terms of related work (2 paragraphs).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The paper omitted important related work (e.g., Schmidt, 2019; Tan et al., 2019; Rennie et al., 2016), including an ACL best paper on the same topic and whose findings appear to be at odds with the current submission.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The JS divergence is not a distance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 63, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "That said, I have several concerns that make me question some of the claims of the paper:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "The human evaluation shows very little performance difference between generation with data prefix (D-prefix) and model prefix (M-prefix), suggesting exposure bias is not a problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "But I would argue this is mostly an artifact of the experimental setup, as prefixing generation with D-string (of length L) only eliminates exposure bias up to position L, and then both evaluated systems (according to the generation process defined Section 4.1) use a standard auto-regressive LM generation process that makes them both subject to the exposure bias problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Since these generated strings are of length 30, there is plenty of room for exposure bias to crop in.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "(Indeed, Zhang et al., (2019) and Holtzman et al. (2019) have shown concrete examples of exposure bias artifacts appearing in much shorter sequences).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "So, the small performance difference between a preference M-prefixed and D-prefixed generation may very well be due to both setups being almost identical (same model, same auto-regressive inference algorithm, and the only difference is the prefix \u2013 which human raters are not even asked to judge directly).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "If exposure bias is indeed a problem, then it would affect both systems almost the same way, so this human evaluation can\u2019t be used to either affirm or deny exposure bias is a significant problem.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I also have concerns regarding the automatic evaluation based on BLEU.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What the authors call \u201ccorpus-BLEU\u201d is actually not the standard version of BLEU (see \u201cOther Comments\u201d below), as the version of the paper looks at n-gram (n=1 to 3) matches between the generated sentences and a large set of references that is not specific to a particular context.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "As the paper\u2019s automatic evaluation is done in a completely context-agnostic way and relative to a large pool of references, it essentially only measures whether the model (with or w/o exposure bias) is able to generate plausible trigrams, but that sets the bar very low as we already have plenty of evidence showing neural language models are quite capable of generating reasonable trigrams (whether there is exposure bias or not), and in fact often much longer n-grams.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors claim that auto-regressive models appear to have \u201cself-recovery ability\u201d that mitigate any exposure bias, but I would say that it is hard to claim anything about self-recovery (at least in terms of automatic evaluation) when the evaluation metric operates over such a small window (<=3 words).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Abstract: \u201cAlthough a lot of algorithms have been proposed to avoid teacher forcing and therefore alleviate exposure bias, there is little work showing how serious the exposure bias problem actually is.\u201d I find this claim a bit misguided, as the numerous papers addressing exposure bias are empirical ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "They have shown improvements in various tasks such as machine translation, image captioning, and other generation tasks thanks to techniques aimed at reducing exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Now, one could claim that significant improvements shown in these papers are due to reasons other than exposure bias, but if that is the case then the submission doesn\u2019t do a much better job at isolating exposure bias from other factors (given my concerns in (1) and (2)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Note that Zhang et al. (2019) actually does include a short section attempting to quantify the effect of exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For a paper that attempts to challenge the current understanding of a problem that has received very significant attention (i.e., exposure bias), it is quite thin in terms of related work (2 paragraphs).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The paper omitted important related work (e.g., Schmidt, 2019; Tan et al., 2019; Rennie et al., 2016), including an ACL best paper on the same topic and whose findings appear to be at odds with the current submission.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The main issue I have is that the formalization of exposure bias is not well-founded and I do not believe a convincing argument is made the claims of relatively little (or even no) exposure bias are actually supported by the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Corpus-BLEU has been used in many papers, though I don\u2019t know of any showing actual correlation with a human metric, but we can at least say that it has precedent being used cumulatively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, using the corpus-BLEU of individual samples in a fraction creates the potential for completely inaccurate estimates.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Even assuming corpus-BLEU works, there is no guarantee that it acts as an estimate of individual samples, in the exact same way that BLEU is known not to be accurate for individual sentences (Burch, 2006).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Furthermore the EB-C metric is basically proposed from thin air.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The qualitative evaluation is just the narrative of what the authors think.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "No intuition is given, simply \u201cwe didn\u2019t find exposure bias when we looked at the data\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It especially shouldn\u2019t be described as failing \u201cto show the significance of exposure bias\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors refer back to these thoughts as experiments, e.g. on page 5 with \u201cThis agrees with our observations in the qualitative experiments.\u201d This seems inappropriate.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The authors show that their metric is not complete via a toy example, but talk about how MLE does not produce these kinds of solutions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "That\u2019s fine and I believe that part of their argument, but I still feel that there is not much evidence that the metric would actually show significant differences if the quality of the writing went down.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Quality is a tricky thing to measure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "To the authors\u2019 credit they conduct a human study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "However, it is unclear to me whether humans that are exposed to a generated prefix (which may already deviate somewhat from human language) would feel that the mistakes made due to exposure bias \u201cmatch\u201d the mistakes in the prefix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "If the generations were truly horrible, I agree we would see more deviation in this score, but the paper attempts to disprove the very existence of exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "A more appropriate reframing would be to talk about the kinds of effects exposure bias couldn\u2019t possibly be having, e.g. that exposure bias may only result in strange word choice but not total degeneration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "This over-claim, and the lack of smaller claims to build-up to it that might have been more appropriate, make it difficult for me accept the described conclusions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Finally, previous works on open-ended text generation have used the portion of the corpus released by OpenAI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "It\u2019s hard to know how comparable these results are, and since both GPT-2 and a significant number of validation text examples from WebText are freely available this seems like a flaw.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 63, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Concerns: The experiments only look at pure sampling from the model---i.e., they don't use temperature, nucleus sampling, greedy or beam search.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "These other generation settings are more important for applications, compared to pure generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Also, they have been reported to exhibit more deviation from the corpus distribution (especially with respect to particular pathologies like repetition).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Evaluating generation in these other settings would increase the impact of the paper's conclusions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The JS divergence is not a distance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 63, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Concerns: The experiments only look at pure sampling from the model---i.e., they don't use temperature, nucleus sampling, greedy or beam search.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "These other generation settings are more important for applications, compared to pure generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Also, they have been reported to exhibit more deviation from the corpus distribution (especially with respect to particular pathologies like repetition).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Evaluating generation in these other settings would increase the impact of the paper's conclusions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The main issue I have is that the formalization of exposure bias is not well-founded and I do not believe a convincing argument is made the claims of relatively little (or even no) exposure bias are actually supported by the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Corpus-BLEU has been used in many papers, though I don\u2019t know of any showing actual correlation with a human metric, but we can at least say that it has precedent being used cumulatively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, using the corpus-BLEU of individual samples in a fraction creates the potential for completely inaccurate estimates.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Even assuming corpus-BLEU works, there is no guarantee that it acts as an estimate of individual samples, in the exact same way that BLEU is known not to be accurate for individual sentences (Burch, 2006).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Furthermore the EB-C metric is basically proposed from thin air.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The qualitative evaluation is just the narrative of what the authors think.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "No intuition is given, simply \u201cwe didn\u2019t find exposure bias when we looked at the data\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It especially shouldn\u2019t be described as failing \u201cto show the significance of exposure bias\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors refer back to these thoughts as experiments, e.g. on page 5 with \u201cThis agrees with our observations in the qualitative experiments.\u201d This seems inappropriate.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The authors show that their metric is not complete via a toy example, but talk about how MLE does not produce these kinds of solutions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "That\u2019s fine and I believe that part of their argument, but I still feel that there is not much evidence that the metric would actually show significant differences if the quality of the writing went down.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Quality is a tricky thing to measure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "To the authors\u2019 credit they conduct a human study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "However, it is unclear to me whether humans that are exposed to a generated prefix (which may already deviate somewhat from human language) would feel that the mistakes made due to exposure bias \u201cmatch\u201d the mistakes in the prefix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "If the generations were truly horrible, I agree we would see more deviation in this score, but the paper attempts to disprove the very existence of exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "A more appropriate reframing would be to talk about the kinds of effects exposure bias couldn\u2019t possibly be having, e.g. that exposure bias may only result in strange word choice but not total degeneration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "This over-claim, and the lack of smaller claims to build-up to it that might have been more appropriate, make it difficult for me accept the described conclusions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Finally, previous works on open-ended text generation have used the portion of the corpus released by OpenAI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "It\u2019s hard to know how comparable these results are, and since both GPT-2 and a significant number of validation text examples from WebText are freely available this seems like a flaw.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 63, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The JS divergence is not a distance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The main issue I have is that the formalization of exposure bias is not well-founded and I do not believe a convincing argument is made the claims of relatively little (or even no) exposure bias are actually supported by the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Corpus-BLEU has been used in many papers, though I don\u2019t know of any showing actual correlation with a human metric, but we can at least say that it has precedent being used cumulatively.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, using the corpus-BLEU of individual samples in a fraction creates the potential for completely inaccurate estimates.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Even assuming corpus-BLEU works, there is no guarantee that it acts as an estimate of individual samples, in the exact same way that BLEU is known not to be accurate for individual sentences (Burch, 2006).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Furthermore the EB-C metric is basically proposed from thin air.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The qualitative evaluation is just the narrative of what the authors think.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "No intuition is given, simply \u201cwe didn\u2019t find exposure bias when we looked at the data\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It especially shouldn\u2019t be described as failing \u201cto show the significance of exposure bias\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors refer back to these thoughts as experiments, e.g. on page 5 with \u201cThis agrees with our observations in the qualitative experiments.\u201d This seems inappropriate.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The authors show that their metric is not complete via a toy example, but talk about how MLE does not produce these kinds of solutions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "That\u2019s fine and I believe that part of their argument, but I still feel that there is not much evidence that the metric would actually show significant differences if the quality of the writing went down.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Quality is a tricky thing to measure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "To the authors\u2019 credit they conduct a human study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "However, it is unclear to me whether humans that are exposed to a generated prefix (which may already deviate somewhat from human language) would feel that the mistakes made due to exposure bias \u201cmatch\u201d the mistakes in the prefix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "If the generations were truly horrible, I agree we would see more deviation in this score, but the paper attempts to disprove the very existence of exposure bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "A more appropriate reframing would be to talk about the kinds of effects exposure bias couldn\u2019t possibly be having, e.g. that exposure bias may only result in strange word choice but not total degeneration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "This over-claim, and the lack of smaller claims to build-up to it that might have been more appropriate, make it difficult for me accept the described conclusions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Finally, previous works on open-ended text generation have used the portion of the corpus released by OpenAI.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "It\u2019s hard to know how comparable these results are, and since both GPT-2 and a significant number of validation text examples from WebText are freely available this seems like a flaw.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 64, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I am bit unconvinced about claiming that this work requires no external architecture in Table 1.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There are new parameters that are getting trained/fine-tuned before we can do inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The main difference I see between this and another approach like p-tuning is that the most of the existing architecture is getting reused vs in p-tuning there is explicitly an external LSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would've been nice to see what happens if we constrain the additional tokens to be a part of the same vocabulary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This is more or less the approach taken in AutoPrompt, except with the current training methodology.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "In section 4.5, the authors said that their method requires no external parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "But this claim could be controversial since adding new tokens (for prompts and labels) on the pre-trained language models' token embedding seems more efficient than pre-training language models that already have unused or special tokens in their token embedding. I know many PLMs already have unused tokens in practice, but I would like to treat this claim theoretically.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 64, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I am bit unconvinced about claiming that this work requires no external architecture in Table 1.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There are new parameters that are getting trained/fine-tuned before we can do inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The main difference I see between this and another approach like p-tuning is that the most of the existing architecture is getting reused vs in p-tuning there is explicitly an external LSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would've been nice to see what happens if we constrain the additional tokens to be a part of the same vocabulary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This is more or less the approach taken in AutoPrompt, except with the current training methodology.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Even though the results in Table 3 for relation extraction and event extraction datasets suggest that the proposed methods perform significantly better than LM-BFF, the results on the 10 popular datasets seems to be very close to LM-BFF.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "From the results, only two datasets (SST-2 and MR) seem to have significant improvement over LM-BFF.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would be great to further discuss these results in detail.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Summary Of The Review:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Overall, the approach is technically sound and is also very easy to apply to other pre-trained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The empirical results on the 10 popular datasets seem closer to LM-BFF and it\u2019s hard to interpret if the approach empirically performs better than static/fixed prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "On the other hand, the proposed method performs much better on relation extraction and event extraction datasets, and it is not clearly discussed in the paper on these variations in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 64, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I am bit unconvinced about claiming that this work requires no external architecture in Table 1.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There are new parameters that are getting trained/fine-tuned before we can do inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The main difference I see between this and another approach like p-tuning is that the most of the existing architecture is getting reused vs in p-tuning there is explicitly an external LSTM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would've been nice to see what happens if we constrain the additional tokens to be a part of the same vocabulary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This is more or less the approach taken in AutoPrompt, except with the current training methodology.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The notation/description of section 4 is not immediately intuitive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I had to read it a couple of times before I could fully follow the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors could add more content to figure 1, which may resolve this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The choice in deciding how many template tokens are used is unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "An additional ablation experiment trying different number of tokens to optimize could be illuminating (even for just 1 dataset).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The ablation study would be better if specific numbers were provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I know GPT3 access is hard to get, but I wish experiments with k=8 prompts could be compared against", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Post rebuttal: I think another pass for clarity over the paper would be good for the final version, but otherwise I'm happy with the paper updates and I'm happy to see the ablation numbers aren't too sensitive to token count.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I have updated my score to reflect this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 64, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "In section 4.5, the authors said that their method requires no external parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "But this claim could be controversial since adding new tokens (for prompts and labels) on the pre-trained language models' token embedding seems more efficient than pre-training language models that already have unused or special tokens in their token embedding. I know many PLMs already have unused tokens in practice, but I would like to treat this claim theoretically.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Even though the results in Table 3 for relation extraction and event extraction datasets suggest that the proposed methods perform significantly better than LM-BFF, the results on the 10 popular datasets seems to be very close to LM-BFF.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "From the results, only two datasets (SST-2 and MR) seem to have significant improvement over LM-BFF.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would be great to further discuss these results in detail.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Summary Of The Review:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Overall, the approach is technically sound and is also very easy to apply to other pre-trained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The empirical results on the 10 popular datasets seem closer to LM-BFF and it\u2019s hard to interpret if the approach empirically performs better than static/fixed prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "On the other hand, the proposed method performs much better on relation extraction and event extraction datasets, and it is not clearly discussed in the paper on these variations in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 64, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "In section 4.5, the authors said that their method requires no external parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "But this claim could be controversial since adding new tokens (for prompts and labels) on the pre-trained language models' token embedding seems more efficient than pre-training language models that already have unused or special tokens in their token embedding. I know many PLMs already have unused tokens in practice, but I would like to treat this claim theoretically.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The notation/description of section 4 is not immediately intuitive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I had to read it a couple of times before I could fully follow the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors could add more content to figure 1, which may resolve this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The choice in deciding how many template tokens are used is unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "An additional ablation experiment trying different number of tokens to optimize could be illuminating (even for just 1 dataset).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The ablation study would be better if specific numbers were provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I know GPT3 access is hard to get, but I wish experiments with k=8 prompts could be compared against", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Post rebuttal: I think another pass for clarity over the paper would be good for the final version, but otherwise I'm happy with the paper updates and I'm happy to see the ablation numbers aren't too sensitive to token count.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I have updated my score to reflect this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 64, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Even though the results in Table 3 for relation extraction and event extraction datasets suggest that the proposed methods perform significantly better than LM-BFF, the results on the 10 popular datasets seems to be very close to LM-BFF.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "From the results, only two datasets (SST-2 and MR) seem to have significant improvement over LM-BFF.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would be great to further discuss these results in detail.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Summary Of The Review:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Overall, the approach is technically sound and is also very easy to apply to other pre-trained language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The empirical results on the 10 popular datasets seem closer to LM-BFF and it\u2019s hard to interpret if the approach empirically performs better than static/fixed prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "On the other hand, the proposed method performs much better on relation extraction and event extraction datasets, and it is not clearly discussed in the paper on these variations in the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The notation/description of section 4 is not immediately intuitive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "I had to read it a couple of times before I could fully follow the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The authors could add more content to figure 1, which may resolve this issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The choice in deciding how many template tokens are used is unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "An additional ablation experiment trying different number of tokens to optimize could be illuminating (even for just 1 dataset).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The ablation study would be better if specific numbers were provided.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I know GPT3 access is hard to get, but I wish experiments with k=8 prompts could be compared against", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Post rebuttal: I think another pass for clarity over the paper would be good for the final version, but otherwise I'm happy with the paper updates and I'm happy to see the ablation numbers aren't too sensitive to token count.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I have updated my score to reflect this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 65, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 2, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weakness: authors\u2019 notation of r_i is a bit unclear to me, my understanding is that they are just the generated text before the final answer (what authors call reasoning path).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It is not clear to me what is the difference between the generated text before the final answer and what authors call the reasoning paths.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Did authors do something specific to exclude some potentially irrelevant text that were generated but did not contribute to reasoning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "If not, perhaps calling them reasoning paths is a bit confusing?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems r_i and a_i always appear together, which gives me the impression that the only distinction is that a_i is the final answer and r_i is everything before that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Another confusion is the presentation of the weighted sum, is it correct to understand the probability weight is just the logsumexp of the logprobs of the generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That makes sense to be, equations 1 is a bit misleading to me because it seems to place an emphasis on r_i as being the reasoning path when it\u2019s just the probability of a specific generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I think it is okay to mention the generation is supposed to be reasoning paths, but it might be more clear to just note them as generated text in those formal expressions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 65, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses The decoding strategy itself has limited novelty since sampling strategies (including nucleus sampling) and ranking according to model scores have been previously used for pre-trained LMs albeit not with CoT prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even though it generally leads to improvements, the proposed decoding strategy creates a significant overhead proportional to the number of outputs sampled e.g 5x latency for 5 samples assuming that samples have equal lengths.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The marginalization process of self-consistency assumes that answers are coming from a fixed answer set, which decreases the scope of applicability.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 65, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weak: Self-consistency is an interesting point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "However, my main consideration is that this paper does not go deep enough into the study of self-consistency.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This lack of depth is mainly reflected in two perspectives (1) only the majority vote of multiple randomly generated answers is considered.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There is a lack of in-depth observation on how to combine different answers; (2) different answers are obtained by controlling the temperature and k. For text generation, the effect of these two hyperparameters is unclear. In particular, how these two hyperparameters affect answer consistency is unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Other weak points: 1 Experiment results suggest that LM cannot distinguish well between correct and incorrect answers (at least from the aspect of token probability), but why taking majority vote over answers generated from different reasoning paths works?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "More in-depth studies should be done.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2 Since \u201cdiversity of reasoning paths is the key to better performance\u201d, why other ensemble-based approaches (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "the multiple prompts setting) didn\u2019t get larger gain for their diversity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3 Figure 2 is confusing: why greedy decode has different number of sampled paths?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "4 It would be better to show full sampled paths in table 4 and other examples if the purpose is to claim self-consistency can repair errored reasoning process.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 65, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 2, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weakness: authors\u2019 notation of r_i is a bit unclear to me, my understanding is that they are just the generated text before the final answer (what authors call reasoning path).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It is not clear to me what is the difference between the generated text before the final answer and what authors call the reasoning paths.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Did authors do something specific to exclude some potentially irrelevant text that were generated but did not contribute to reasoning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "If not, perhaps calling them reasoning paths is a bit confusing?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems r_i and a_i always appear together, which gives me the impression that the only distinction is that a_i is the final answer and r_i is everything before that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Another confusion is the presentation of the weighted sum, is it correct to understand the probability weight is just the logsumexp of the logprobs of the generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That makes sense to be, equations 1 is a bit misleading to me because it seems to place an emphasis on r_i as being the reasoning path when it\u2019s just the probability of a specific generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I think it is okay to mention the generation is supposed to be reasoning paths, but it might be more clear to just note them as generated text in those formal expressions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses The decoding strategy itself has limited novelty since sampling strategies (including nucleus sampling) and ranking according to model scores have been previously used for pre-trained LMs albeit not with CoT prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even though it generally leads to improvements, the proposed decoding strategy creates a significant overhead proportional to the number of outputs sampled e.g 5x latency for 5 samples assuming that samples have equal lengths.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The marginalization process of self-consistency assumes that answers are coming from a fixed answer set, which decreases the scope of applicability.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 65, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 2, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weakness: authors\u2019 notation of r_i is a bit unclear to me, my understanding is that they are just the generated text before the final answer (what authors call reasoning path).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It is not clear to me what is the difference between the generated text before the final answer and what authors call the reasoning paths.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Did authors do something specific to exclude some potentially irrelevant text that were generated but did not contribute to reasoning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "If not, perhaps calling them reasoning paths is a bit confusing?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems r_i and a_i always appear together, which gives me the impression that the only distinction is that a_i is the final answer and r_i is everything before that.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Another confusion is the presentation of the weighted sum, is it correct to understand the probability weight is just the logsumexp of the logprobs of the generation?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "That makes sense to be, equations 1 is a bit misleading to me because it seems to place an emphasis on r_i as being the reasoning path when it\u2019s just the probability of a specific generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I think it is okay to mention the generation is supposed to be reasoning paths, but it might be more clear to just note them as generated text in those formal expressions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weak: Self-consistency is an interesting point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "However, my main consideration is that this paper does not go deep enough into the study of self-consistency.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This lack of depth is mainly reflected in two perspectives (1) only the majority vote of multiple randomly generated answers is considered.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There is a lack of in-depth observation on how to combine different answers; (2) different answers are obtained by controlling the temperature and k. For text generation, the effect of these two hyperparameters is unclear. In particular, how these two hyperparameters affect answer consistency is unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Other weak points: 1 Experiment results suggest that LM cannot distinguish well between correct and incorrect answers (at least from the aspect of token probability), but why taking majority vote over answers generated from different reasoning paths works?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "More in-depth studies should be done.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2 Since \u201cdiversity of reasoning paths is the key to better performance\u201d, why other ensemble-based approaches (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "the multiple prompts setting) didn\u2019t get larger gain for their diversity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3 Figure 2 is confusing: why greedy decode has different number of sampled paths?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "4 It would be better to show full sampled paths in table 4 and other examples if the purpose is to claim self-consistency can repair errored reasoning process.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 65, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses The decoding strategy itself has limited novelty since sampling strategies (including nucleus sampling) and ranking according to model scores have been previously used for pre-trained LMs albeit not with CoT prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Even though it generally leads to improvements, the proposed decoding strategy creates a significant overhead proportional to the number of outputs sampled e.g 5x latency for 5 samples assuming that samples have equal lengths.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The marginalization process of self-consistency assumes that answers are coming from a fixed answer set, which decreases the scope of applicability.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weak: Self-consistency is an interesting point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "However, my main consideration is that this paper does not go deep enough into the study of self-consistency.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This lack of depth is mainly reflected in two perspectives (1) only the majority vote of multiple randomly generated answers is considered.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There is a lack of in-depth observation on how to combine different answers; (2) different answers are obtained by controlling the temperature and k. For text generation, the effect of these two hyperparameters is unclear. In particular, how these two hyperparameters affect answer consistency is unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Other weak points: 1 Experiment results suggest that LM cannot distinguish well between correct and incorrect answers (at least from the aspect of token probability), but why taking majority vote over answers generated from different reasoning paths works?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "More in-depth studies should be done.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2 Since \u201cdiversity of reasoning paths is the key to better performance\u201d, why other ensemble-based approaches (e.g.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "the multiple prompts setting) didn\u2019t get larger gain for their diversity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3 Figure 2 is confusing: why greedy decode has different number of sampled paths?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "4 It would be better to show full sampled paths in table 4 and other examples if the purpose is to claim self-consistency can repair errored reasoning process.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 66, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Similar studies were conducted on a single node with 8 GPUs as noted by the authors. Though that setup had considerably more computational resources the total volume of computation was still a fraction of the amount used by many large research institutions. In light of that work, the scenario presented in this paper may seem somewhat derivative and only marginally interesting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is not clear if or how the observations made in the cramming regime may be used to make more informed decisions regarding the training process in the normal training setting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The biggest missing piece is an ablation study on the improvement from various architecture changes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In the current version, the authors provided a comprehensive list of things they tried, what helped and what didn't.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "But it's unknown which change(s) brought the bigger improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Related to this is the poor performance on CoLA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the authors provided several hypotheses, some of them should be tested to verify whether they're actually related to any of the architecture or data changes, or mostly due to reduced model size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For example, one possible cause provided by the authors is that reasonable performance CoLA would need more training data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 66, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Similar studies were conducted on a single node with 8 GPUs as noted by the authors. Though that setup had considerably more computational resources the total volume of computation was still a fraction of the amount used by many large research institutions. In light of that work, the scenario presented in this paper may seem somewhat derivative and only marginally interesting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is not clear if or how the observations made in the cramming regime may be used to make more informed decisions regarding the training process in the normal training setting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The investigation of modifications lack convincing experiments. For example, only one task performance (MNLI) is reported for when studying the impact of training hyper-parameters. Other tasks can have a different trend.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The technical novelty of this paper is a little limit. The total contributions are also limit.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Clarity is clear, however, novelty is limit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 66, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Similar studies were conducted on a single node with 8 GPUs as noted by the authors. Though that setup had considerably more computational resources the total volume of computation was still a fraction of the amount used by many large research institutions. In light of that work, the scenario presented in this paper may seem somewhat derivative and only marginally interesting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is not clear if or how the observations made in the cramming regime may be used to make more informed decisions regarding the training process in the normal training setting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "However, I am not sure if ICLR is the best venue for this work. There are no new theoretical or algorithmic contributions nor any new insight into representation learning. This does not change my belief that this is an informative paper to many in the community, but it might find a more suitable audience if submitted to venues such as EMNLP. (Disclaimer: I do not regularly publish in this area. I am happy to defer to more experienced reviewers and the AC on this point.)", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding of the Submission Rule"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "An additional concern is that it is not clear if the MLM loss is the best metric to track when comparing different model/training design choices.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "This paper (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=F5uYcwABMu), for example, clearly demonstrates that models with near-identical pretraining loss can perform very differently on downstream tasks due to implicit biases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This should be taken into consideration since many conclusions in this work are based on the MLM loss alone.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "A related concern is that since the dominating factor for performance is the number of FLOPs we can squeeze out of a GPU within a given timeframe, this makes the conclusions of this work somewhat hardware-specific, e.g., they might not hold on TPUs or newer/older GPUs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 66, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The biggest missing piece is an ablation study on the improvement from various architecture changes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In the current version, the authors provided a comprehensive list of things they tried, what helped and what didn't.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "But it's unknown which change(s) brought the bigger improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Related to this is the poor performance on CoLA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the authors provided several hypotheses, some of them should be tested to verify whether they're actually related to any of the architecture or data changes, or mostly due to reduced model size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For example, one possible cause provided by the authors is that reasonable performance CoLA would need more training data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The investigation of modifications lack convincing experiments. For example, only one task performance (MNLI) is reported for when studying the impact of training hyper-parameters. Other tasks can have a different trend.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The technical novelty of this paper is a little limit. The total contributions are also limit.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Clarity is clear, however, novelty is limit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 66, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The biggest missing piece is an ablation study on the improvement from various architecture changes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In the current version, the authors provided a comprehensive list of things they tried, what helped and what didn't.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "But it's unknown which change(s) brought the bigger improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Related to this is the poor performance on CoLA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the authors provided several hypotheses, some of them should be tested to verify whether they're actually related to any of the architecture or data changes, or mostly due to reduced model size.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For example, one possible cause provided by the authors is that reasonable performance CoLA would need more training data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "However, I am not sure if ICLR is the best venue for this work. There are no new theoretical or algorithmic contributions nor any new insight into representation learning. This does not change my belief that this is an informative paper to many in the community, but it might find a more suitable audience if submitted to venues such as EMNLP. (Disclaimer: I do not regularly publish in this area. I am happy to defer to more experienced reviewers and the AC on this point.)", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding of the Submission Rule"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "An additional concern is that it is not clear if the MLM loss is the best metric to track when comparing different model/training design choices.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "This paper (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=F5uYcwABMu), for example, clearly demonstrates that models with near-identical pretraining loss can perform very differently on downstream tasks due to implicit biases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This should be taken into consideration since many conclusions in this work are based on the MLM loss alone.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "A related concern is that since the dominating factor for performance is the number of FLOPs we can squeeze out of a GPU within a given timeframe, this makes the conclusions of this work somewhat hardware-specific, e.g., they might not hold on TPUs or newer/older GPUs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 66, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The investigation of modifications lack convincing experiments. For example, only one task performance (MNLI) is reported for when studying the impact of training hyper-parameters. Other tasks can have a different trend.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The performance of downstream tasks can be also important.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The technical novelty of this paper is a little limit. The total contributions are also limit.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Clarity is clear, however, novelty is limit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "However, I am not sure if ICLR is the best venue for this work. There are no new theoretical or algorithmic contributions nor any new insight into representation learning. This does not change my belief that this is an informative paper to many in the community, but it might find a more suitable audience if submitted to venues such as EMNLP. (Disclaimer: I do not regularly publish in this area. I am happy to defer to more experienced reviewers and the AC on this point.)", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding of the Submission Rule"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "An additional concern is that it is not clear if the MLM loss is the best metric to track when comparing different model/training design choices.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "This paper (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=F5uYcwABMu), for example, clearly demonstrates that models with near-identical pretraining loss can perform very differently on downstream tasks due to implicit biases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This should be taken into consideration since many conclusions in this work are based on the MLM loss alone.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "A related concern is that since the dominating factor for performance is the number of FLOPs we can squeeze out of a GPU within a given timeframe, this makes the conclusions of this work somewhat hardware-specific, e.g., they might not hold on TPUs or newer/older GPUs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 67, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The paper investigates temporal generalization in language model pretraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although the evaluation task and benchmarks are novel, some recent works are studying this kind of generalization on tasks like sentiment analysis, machine translation, and named entity recognition. This paper seems an obvious extension to these works with evaluations on a generic language modeling task.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Missing Reference"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Moreover, the considered online learning approach is a straightforward application of the existing dynamic evaluation method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While it's good to quantify the performance of models on time-stratified datasets, many conclusions are obvious, such as the synthetic experiments on question answering by shifting the end year of training data, as well as the experiments on self-containing reading comprehension.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Typos:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "L149: Fig. 2 -> you meant Table 2?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "L234: Fig. 3 -> Table 3", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "L245: Fig -> Table", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "While the conclusions in this paper might appear obvious, the analyses of this paper are thorough and insightful and the released datasets appear to be useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "More importantly, the recommendation of this paper is relevant to the broad community beyond researchers in text generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Therefore, I recommend the acceptance of this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 67, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The paper investigates temporal generalization in language model pretraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although the evaluation task and benchmarks are novel, some recent works are studying this kind of generalization on tasks like sentiment analysis, machine translation, and named entity recognition. This paper seems an obvious extension to these works with evaluations on a generic language modeling task.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Missing Reference"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Moreover, the considered online learning approach is a straightforward application of the existing dynamic evaluation method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "To this reviewer, there aren't any significant issues with the paper that make me want to reject it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Though the results might not be surprising (discussed well in L439) this paper defines the task in a good way for other work to (possibly?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "take a stab at it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Limitations And Societal Impact:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This paper discussed \"limitations\" but it did not discuss possible negative societal impact of the work -- the paper could be improved by adding such a section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Even though this benchmark is not likely to cause harm, it might be good to think about what might result from future work in this area (on both modeling and evaluation side, with respect to the field and society).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "You could also discuss the relevance of evaluation datasets like this one in terms of reducing harms of NLP (e.g. bias being amplified? or historical prejudices being baked in?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 67, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "The paper investigates temporal generalization in language model pretraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although the evaluation task and benchmarks are novel, some recent works are studying this kind of generalization on tasks like sentiment analysis, machine translation, and named entity recognition. This paper seems an obvious extension to these works with evaluations on a generic language modeling task.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Missing Reference"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Moreover, the considered online learning approach is a straightforward application of the existing dynamic evaluation method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I'm actually surprised by how relatively ineffective dynamic evaluation is, especially for the news data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It'd be great to know why.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "One theory, for example, is that catastrophic forgetting is occurring and the LM is being adapted to the news domain but becoming worse in overall performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A simple test for this would be to run the model on held-out data that is not temporally shifted and see how much perplexity changes before/after dynamic evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 67, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While it's good to quantify the performance of models on time-stratified datasets, many conclusions are obvious, such as the synthetic experiments on question answering by shifting the end year of training data, as well as the experiments on self-containing reading comprehension.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Typos:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "L149: Fig. 2 -> you meant Table 2?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "L234: Fig. 3 -> Table 3", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "L245: Fig -> Table", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "While the conclusions in this paper might appear obvious, the analyses of this paper are thorough and insightful and the released datasets appear to be useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "More importantly, the recommendation of this paper is relevant to the broad community beyond researchers in text generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Therefore, I recommend the acceptance of this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "To this reviewer, there aren't any significant issues with the paper that make me want to reject it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Though the results might not be surprising (discussed well in L439) this paper defines the task in a good way for other work to (possibly?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "take a stab at it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Limitations And Societal Impact:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This paper discussed \"limitations\" but it did not discuss possible negative societal impact of the work -- the paper could be improved by adding such a section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Even though this benchmark is not likely to cause harm, it might be good to think about what might result from future work in this area (on both modeling and evaluation side, with respect to the field and society).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "You could also discuss the relevance of evaluation datasets like this one in terms of reducing harms of NLP (e.g. bias being amplified? or historical prejudices being baked in?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 67, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "While it's good to quantify the performance of models on time-stratified datasets, many conclusions are obvious, such as the synthetic experiments on question answering by shifting the end year of training data, as well as the experiments on self-containing reading comprehension.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Typos:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "L149: Fig. 2 -> you meant Table 2?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "L234: Fig. 3 -> Table 3", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "L245: Fig -> Table", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "While the conclusions in this paper might appear obvious, the analyses of this paper are thorough and insightful and the released datasets appear to be useful.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "More importantly, the recommendation of this paper is relevant to the broad community beyond researchers in text generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Therefore, I recommend the acceptance of this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I'm actually surprised by how relatively ineffective dynamic evaluation is, especially for the news data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It'd be great to know why.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "One theory, for example, is that catastrophic forgetting is occurring and the LM is being adapted to the news domain but becoming worse in overall performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A simple test for this would be to run the model on held-out data that is not temporally shifted and see how much perplexity changes before/after dynamic evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 67, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "To this reviewer, there aren't any significant issues with the paper that make me want to reject it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Though the results might not be surprising (discussed well in L439) this paper defines the task in a good way for other work to (possibly?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "take a stab at it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Limitations And Societal Impact:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This paper discussed \"limitations\" but it did not discuss possible negative societal impact of the work -- the paper could be improved by adding such a section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Even though this benchmark is not likely to cause harm, it might be good to think about what might result from future work in this area (on both modeling and evaluation side, with respect to the field and society).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "You could also discuss the relevance of evaluation datasets like this one in terms of reducing harms of NLP (e.g. bias being amplified? or historical prejudices being baked in?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I'm actually surprised by how relatively ineffective dynamic evaluation is, especially for the news data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It'd be great to know why.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "One theory, for example, is that catastrophic forgetting is occurring and the LM is being adapted to the news domain but becoming worse in overall performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "A simple test for this would be to run the model on held-out data that is not temporally shifted and see how much perplexity changes before/after dynamic evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 68, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My biggest concern is on the underlying assumption of this paper---the model should not be impacted by the incorrect labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It's controversial here because when we say in-context \"learning\", we actually want the model to be truthful to the provided demonstration examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I am willing to hear the author's thoughts on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "I think the overall analysis presents quite interesting findings but the robustness and generalizability of the analysis is questionable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "There are many arbitrary choices in performing the analysis/mitigation, e.g., to ablate the heads in Section 4, for the head choice (Appendix A.1 has the details), the authors \"first consider the 25 heads with the highest label-attending score, then select the 10 heads with the highest class-sensitivity score\"; further some bias was added \"towards heads in the later layers, like selecting the 5 heads of 25 that belong to layers 20 and later\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "How are those choices made?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "They look very arbitrary to me and I'm not sure if it can generalize to other models or other tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In addition, this layer choice is based on the overall pattern analysis on these 8 datasets, would this conclusion generalize to a new task/dataset?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "how would people apply the method in practice?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition, it seems like people need to perform extensive analysis for each model/task combination to identify the critical layers / false prefix-matching heads (as they all seem different across models/tasks), how can one apply the method in a scalable way for any new/unseen tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "I do have some doubts in terms of the robustness of the analysis and its generalizability across different/unseen tasks, so not sure if the method can be applied generally to any task, which requires further study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 68, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My biggest concern is on the underlying assumption of this paper---the model should not be impacted by the incorrect labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It's controversial here because when we say in-context \"learning\", we actually want the model to be truthful to the provided demonstration examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I am willing to hear the author's thoughts on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Strength: Clarity, quality (see next section) ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 68, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "My biggest concern is on the underlying assumption of this paper---the model should not be impacted by the incorrect labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It's controversial here because when we say in-context \"learning\", we actually want the model to be truthful to the provided demonstration examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I am willing to hear the author's thoughts on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "\u201cFalse demonstrations\u201d: As I briefly mentioned earlier, I think the term \u201cfalse demonstrations\u201d might be too abstract and broad to describe what the paper actually does.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "However, I have a critical concern in the most important underlying assumption in the paper \u2013 that the \u201cfalse demonstrations\u201d in the paper is not actually the false demonstrations but is rather a re-definition of the task, and it is unclear why the goal is to make the model predict the original, true label.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "If authors/other reviewers agree with this, this means the paper has to be almost completely rewritten:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "for instance, the authors can re-run the same experiments/analysis with random labels (instead of permuted labels) and likely the same findings will still hold;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "or alternatively, the paper can be re-written to be about how the model is able to perform the task when the task is redefined with permuted labels whose semantic meaning doesn\u2019t match with pre-training, attribute this to a few attention heads, and prove it is true by showing zero-ing out such attention heads significantly degrades performance again redefined (permuted) true labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 68, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "I think the overall analysis presents quite interesting findings but the robustness and generalizability of the analysis is questionable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "There are many arbitrary choices in performing the analysis/mitigation, e.g., to ablate the heads in Section 4, for the head choice (Appendix A.1 has the details), the authors \"first consider the 25 heads with the highest label-attending score, then select the 10 heads with the highest class-sensitivity score\"; further some bias was added \"towards heads in the later layers, like selecting the 5 heads of 25 that belong to layers 20 and later\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "How are those choices made?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "They look very arbitrary to me and I'm not sure if it can generalize to other models or other tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In addition, this layer choice is based on the overall pattern analysis on these 8 datasets, would this conclusion generalize to a new task/dataset?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "how would people apply the method in practice?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition, it seems like people need to perform extensive analysis for each model/task combination to identify the critical layers / false prefix-matching heads (as they all seem different across models/tasks), how can one apply the method in a scalable way for any new/unseen tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "I do have some doubts in terms of the robustness of the analysis and its generalizability across different/unseen tasks, so not sure if the method can be applied generally to any task, which requires further study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Strength: Clarity, quality (see next section) ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 68, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "I think the overall analysis presents quite interesting findings but the robustness and generalizability of the analysis is questionable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "There are many arbitrary choices in performing the analysis/mitigation, e.g., to ablate the heads in Section 4, for the head choice (Appendix A.1 has the details), the authors \"first consider the 25 heads with the highest label-attending score, then select the 10 heads with the highest class-sensitivity score\"; further some bias was added \"towards heads in the later layers, like selecting the 5 heads of 25 that belong to layers 20 and later\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "How are those choices made?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "They look very arbitrary to me and I'm not sure if it can generalize to other models or other tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In addition, this layer choice is based on the overall pattern analysis on these 8 datasets, would this conclusion generalize to a new task/dataset?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "how would people apply the method in practice?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In addition, it seems like people need to perform extensive analysis for each model/task combination to identify the critical layers / false prefix-matching heads (as they all seem different across models/tasks), how can one apply the method in a scalable way for any new/unseen tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "I do have some doubts in terms of the robustness of the analysis and its generalizability across different/unseen tasks, so not sure if the method can be applied generally to any task, which requires further study.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "\u201cFalse demonstrations\u201d: As I briefly mentioned earlier, I think the term \u201cfalse demonstrations\u201d might be too abstract and broad to describe what the paper actually does.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "However, I have a critical concern in the most important underlying assumption in the paper \u2013 that the \u201cfalse demonstrations\u201d in the paper is not actually the false demonstrations but is rather a re-definition of the task, and it is unclear why the goal is to make the model predict the original, true label.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "If authors/other reviewers agree with this, this means the paper has to be almost completely rewritten:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "for instance, the authors can re-run the same experiments/analysis with random labels (instead of permuted labels) and likely the same findings will still hold;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "or alternatively, the paper can be re-written to be about how the model is able to perform the task when the task is redefined with permuted labels whose semantic meaning doesn\u2019t match with pre-training, attribute this to a few attention heads, and prove it is true by showing zero-ing out such attention heads significantly degrades performance again redefined (permuted) true labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 68, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Strength: Clarity, quality (see next section) ", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "\u201cFalse demonstrations\u201d: As I briefly mentioned earlier, I think the term \u201cfalse demonstrations\u201d might be too abstract and broad to describe what the paper actually does.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "However, I have a critical concern in the most important underlying assumption in the paper \u2013 that the \u201cfalse demonstrations\u201d in the paper is not actually the false demonstrations but is rather a re-definition of the task, and it is unclear why the goal is to make the model predict the original, true label.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "If authors/other reviewers agree with this, this means the paper has to be almost completely rewritten:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "for instance, the authors can re-run the same experiments/analysis with random labels (instead of permuted labels) and likely the same findings will still hold;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "or alternatively, the paper can be re-written to be about how the model is able to perform the task when the task is redefined with permuted labels whose semantic meaning doesn\u2019t match with pre-training, attribute this to a few attention heads, and prove it is true by showing zero-ing out such attention heads significantly degrades performance again redefined (permuted) true labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 69, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "Limitations And Societal Impact:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Notation are sometimes confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "(1) In Eq.3 the function d is overloaded.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The function outputs the task embedding in the first part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It outputs RNN hidden representation in the second part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) In line 7-8 in Algorithm 1, e_j is a new variable that seems to be newly-introduced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Please also provide hints for the reader to link e_j with p(y|x_j), e.g., refer the reader to Eq. 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 69, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "Limitations And Societal Impact:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Overall, I found the paper very hard to follow, there are numerous typos and the exposition made it really difficult to extract the key contribution, the motivations for the specific choices, and the key take-aways.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The paper seems a lot like an amalgamation of many related concepts, which is not a bad thing but in the current version of the paper there is no clear motivation for the various choices.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "To provide just one instance, if the main motivation is to use task embeddings then there are many straight-forward ways to incorporate them in existing models, such as in the input to the encoder or the classifier, and carefully considering/evaluating such choices would have been useful in motivating the approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Another choice made by the authors is to use only the values in FIM corresponding to the adapters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Why is it sufficient to only use these, is there any analysis on the quality of the task embedding when restricting to only the adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Similarly, the motivation for using task embeddings to generate Film adapters is not clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For instance, one can use Film adapters with regular fine-tuning on the few-shot set to learn \u201ctask-specific\u201d Film adapters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Note that this baseline is not present in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "So how is including the task embedding (which also uses gradient information) helpful over fine-tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Motivation as well as empirical results in this direction are lacking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Moreover, there are several concerns regarding experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "First, as mentioned above, there are relevant baselines that need to be provided to understand the value of the proposed approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "These also include: ProtoNet with fine-tuning, ProtoNet with adapters fine-tuning, ProtoNet-BN with only Film fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Currently, the main baselines for the paper are ProtoNet and ProtoNet-BN, none of which use gradient information like the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "These baselines will help demonstrate the utility of task embeddings over fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors use datasets from [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "However, I see there are 17 datasets used in that paper and the authors picked 7 of them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am not convinced that seq-length is an appropriate reason to drop 10 datasets, for instance, the authors can run the model with a larger sequence length during evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Moreover, there are decisions like only using a subset of classes in one of the datasets (Huffpost), and why this is done is also not clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "As such, due to the use of non-standard datasets, I am not sure how to interpret the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Ablations are carried on all the downstream tasks used for final evaluation, which makes me think the results are overfit to these tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I didn\u2019t find these particularly insightful either.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "For example, ablations on these choices would have been more helpful: using/not-using adapters or Film adapters, hidden-size of adapters, fine-tuning adapters vs using task embeddings, alternatives to incorporate task embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Some hyper-parameter choices are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "For example in section 4.2, what are the hyper-parameters of the RNN d()?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Similarly, what is the architecture and hyper-parameters of the MLPs a(.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "in adaptation network?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "There is no analysis on what the task embeddings are capturing and why they are useful here. This would have helped motivate the paper quite a bit.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 37, "text": "These concerns together make me hesitant about the value of the task-embedding approach presented here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors should consider a thorough re-writing of the paper to provide motivation and to explain their approach clearly, and consider the suggested baselines and improvements to experiments explained above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The authors provided a lengthy response with additional experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "These partly address some concerns but raise a few more.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "When using more tasks and relevant baselines as reported here, I can see that the margins of improvements are significantly less than what is reported in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "For ex, FT-PN-BN is competitive with the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Some of the new results are also very puzzling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "Like why does prototypical networks after fine-tuning becomes much worse than without fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Another related problem I hadn't noticed in the first review was the difference between prototypical and other fine-tuning baselines, this difference seems too large, contrary to previous literature, and warrants more exploration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Comparing some results in the rebuttal and in Table 1, it seems that most of the improvements for the proposed approach are coming from the 3 new introduced datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "These results are also very surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "For instance, what is the property of SNIPS dataset on which Leopard and PN suddenly perform at half the accuracy of Grad2Task (21, 28 vs 52)!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 69, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 23, "text": "Limitations And Societal Impact:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "It is not clear how many labeled instances are required, and at which threshold the method degrades.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "Not convincing; issues raised have to do with text classification n in general, b not so much with the paper", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 69, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Notation are sometimes confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "(1) In Eq.3 the function d is overloaded.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The function outputs the task embedding in the first part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It outputs RNN hidden representation in the second part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) In line 7-8 in Algorithm 1, e_j is a new variable that seems to be newly-introduced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Please also provide hints for the reader to link e_j with p(y|x_j), e.g., refer the reader to Eq. 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Overall, I found the paper very hard to follow, there are numerous typos and the exposition made it really difficult to extract the key contribution, the motivations for the specific choices, and the key take-aways.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The paper seems a lot like an amalgamation of many related concepts, which is not a bad thing but in the current version of the paper there is no clear motivation for the various choices.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "To provide just one instance, if the main motivation is to use task embeddings then there are many straight-forward ways to incorporate them in existing models, such as in the input to the encoder or the classifier, and carefully considering/evaluating such choices would have been useful in motivating the approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Another choice made by the authors is to use only the values in FIM corresponding to the adapters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Why is it sufficient to only use these, is there any analysis on the quality of the task embedding when restricting to only the adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Similarly, the motivation for using task embeddings to generate Film adapters is not clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For instance, one can use Film adapters with regular fine-tuning on the few-shot set to learn \u201ctask-specific\u201d Film adapters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Note that this baseline is not present in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "So how is including the task embedding (which also uses gradient information) helpful over fine-tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Motivation as well as empirical results in this direction are lacking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Moreover, there are several concerns regarding experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "First, as mentioned above, there are relevant baselines that need to be provided to understand the value of the proposed approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "These also include: ProtoNet with fine-tuning, ProtoNet with adapters fine-tuning, ProtoNet-BN with only Film fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Currently, the main baselines for the paper are ProtoNet and ProtoNet-BN, none of which use gradient information like the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "These baselines will help demonstrate the utility of task embeddings over fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors use datasets from [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "However, I see there are 17 datasets used in that paper and the authors picked 7 of them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am not convinced that seq-length is an appropriate reason to drop 10 datasets, for instance, the authors can run the model with a larger sequence length during evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Moreover, there are decisions like only using a subset of classes in one of the datasets (Huffpost), and why this is done is also not clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "As such, due to the use of non-standard datasets, I am not sure how to interpret the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Ablations are carried on all the downstream tasks used for final evaluation, which makes me think the results are overfit to these tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I didn\u2019t find these particularly insightful either.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "For example, ablations on these choices would have been more helpful: using/not-using adapters or Film adapters, hidden-size of adapters, fine-tuning adapters vs using task embeddings, alternatives to incorporate task embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Some hyper-parameter choices are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "For example in section 4.2, what are the hyper-parameters of the RNN d()?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Similarly, what is the architecture and hyper-parameters of the MLPs a(.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "in adaptation network?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "There is no analysis on what the task embeddings are capturing and why they are useful here. This would have helped motivate the paper quite a bit.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 37, "text": "These concerns together make me hesitant about the value of the task-embedding approach presented here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors should consider a thorough re-writing of the paper to provide motivation and to explain their approach clearly, and consider the suggested baselines and improvements to experiments explained above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The authors provided a lengthy response with additional experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "These partly address some concerns but raise a few more.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "When using more tasks and relevant baselines as reported here, I can see that the margins of improvements are significantly less than what is reported in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "For ex, FT-PN-BN is competitive with the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Some of the new results are also very puzzling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "Like why does prototypical networks after fine-tuning becomes much worse than without fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Another related problem I hadn't noticed in the first review was the difference between prototypical and other fine-tuning baselines, this difference seems too large, contrary to previous literature, and warrants more exploration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Comparing some results in the rebuttal and in Table 1, it seems that most of the improvements for the proposed approach are coming from the 3 new introduced datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "These results are also very surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "For instance, what is the property of SNIPS dataset on which Leopard and PN suddenly perform at half the accuracy of Grad2Task (21, 28 vs 52)!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 69, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "Notation are sometimes confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "(1) In Eq.3 the function d is overloaded.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The function outputs the task embedding in the first part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It outputs RNN hidden representation in the second part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) In line 7-8 in Algorithm 1, e_j is a new variable that seems to be newly-introduced.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Please also provide hints for the reader to link e_j with p(y|x_j), e.g., refer the reader to Eq. 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "It is not clear how many labeled instances are required, and at which threshold the method degrades.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "Not convincing; issues raised have to do with text classification n in general, b not so much with the paper", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 69, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Overall, I found the paper very hard to follow, there are numerous typos and the exposition made it really difficult to extract the key contribution, the motivations for the specific choices, and the key take-aways.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The paper seems a lot like an amalgamation of many related concepts, which is not a bad thing but in the current version of the paper there is no clear motivation for the various choices.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "To provide just one instance, if the main motivation is to use task embeddings then there are many straight-forward ways to incorporate them in existing models, such as in the input to the encoder or the classifier, and carefully considering/evaluating such choices would have been useful in motivating the approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Another choice made by the authors is to use only the values in FIM corresponding to the adapters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Why is it sufficient to only use these, is there any analysis on the quality of the task embedding when restricting to only the adapters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Similarly, the motivation for using task embeddings to generate Film adapters is not clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "For instance, one can use Film adapters with regular fine-tuning on the few-shot set to learn \u201ctask-specific\u201d Film adapters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Note that this baseline is not present in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "So how is including the task embedding (which also uses gradient information) helpful over fine-tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Motivation as well as empirical results in this direction are lacking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Moreover, there are several concerns regarding experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "First, as mentioned above, there are relevant baselines that need to be provided to understand the value of the proposed approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "These also include: ProtoNet with fine-tuning, ProtoNet with adapters fine-tuning, ProtoNet-BN with only Film fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Currently, the main baselines for the paper are ProtoNet and ProtoNet-BN, none of which use gradient information like the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "These baselines will help demonstrate the utility of task embeddings over fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors use datasets from [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "However, I see there are 17 datasets used in that paper and the authors picked 7 of them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I am not convinced that seq-length is an appropriate reason to drop 10 datasets, for instance, the authors can run the model with a larger sequence length during evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Moreover, there are decisions like only using a subset of classes in one of the datasets (Huffpost), and why this is done is also not clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "As such, due to the use of non-standard datasets, I am not sure how to interpret the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Ablations are carried on all the downstream tasks used for final evaluation, which makes me think the results are overfit to these tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "I didn\u2019t find these particularly insightful either.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "For example, ablations on these choices would have been more helpful: using/not-using adapters or Film adapters, hidden-size of adapters, fine-tuning adapters vs using task embeddings, alternatives to incorporate task embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Some hyper-parameter choices are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "For example in section 4.2, what are the hyper-parameters of the RNN d()?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Similarly, what is the architecture and hyper-parameters of the MLPs a(.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "in adaptation network?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "There is no analysis on what the task embeddings are capturing and why they are useful here. This would have helped motivate the paper quite a bit.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 37, "text": "These concerns together make me hesitant about the value of the task-embedding approach presented here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "The authors should consider a thorough re-writing of the paper to provide motivation and to explain their approach clearly, and consider the suggested baselines and improvements to experiments explained above.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The authors provided a lengthy response with additional experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "These partly address some concerns but raise a few more.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "When using more tasks and relevant baselines as reported here, I can see that the margins of improvements are significantly less than what is reported in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "For ex, FT-PN-BN is competitive with the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Some of the new results are also very puzzling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "Like why does prototypical networks after fine-tuning becomes much worse than without fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Another related problem I hadn't noticed in the first review was the difference between prototypical and other fine-tuning baselines, this difference seems too large, contrary to previous literature, and warrants more exploration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Comparing some results in the rebuttal and in Table 1, it seems that most of the improvements for the proposed approach are coming from the 3 new introduced datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "These results are also very surprising.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "For instance, what is the property of SNIPS dataset on which Leopard and PN suddenly perform at half the accuracy of Grad2Task (21, 28 vs 52)!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "It is not clear how many labeled instances are required, and at which threshold the method degrades.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 6, "text": "Not convincing; issues raised have to do with text classification n in general, b not so much with the paper", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 70, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I am unclear of some of the experimental settings and how much we can conclude from them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Particularly, when is bootstrapping+self-amplification+ensembling are used? Is it used only during translation?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It also seems odd to use those techniques only for zero-shot and not adapt them (or explore possible adaptions) for 2-shot (given the explanation in 4.4)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My main concern is, however, that if XGLM is not using bootstrapping+self-amplification etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "in Table 2 I am not sure how fair of a comparison it is. Perhaps with those techniques XGLM would exceed mT5 by a high margin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(In appendix G the additional techniques over SAP seems to make a huge difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Appendix B does show that mT5 performs on par with a much larger GPT3 on translation when both are self-amplified but still would be curious about XGLM given its multilingual emphasis similar to mT5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, I acknolwedge the effectiveness of SAP compared to other variants of prompts to mT5)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As the paper acknowledge, SAP requires running the whole encoder-decoder architecture in an autoregressive loop. This can be slow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Particularly critical is that caching isn't anymore possible (wherein caching is generally usable to speed up autoregressive decoding in causal decoders), because the encoder is bidirectional and in the autoregressive loop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The supervised translation baseline include in Table 2 should perhaps be replaced with a more recent and stronger multilingual model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I would definitely challenge the classification of languages such as ko, ar, sw etc. as being low-resource.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These are all languages for which at least 1M training sentences can be found (see e.g. Table 1 in https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.04672)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Minor: I believe the meaning of \"Supervised\" in Table 2 is only explained in the Appendix. I would recommend explaining it in the table caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 70, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I am unclear of some of the experimental settings and how much we can conclude from them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Particularly, when is bootstrapping+self-amplification+ensembling are used? Is it used only during translation?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It also seems odd to use those techniques only for zero-shot and not adapt them (or explore possible adaptions) for 2-shot (given the explanation in 4.4)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My main concern is, however, that if XGLM is not using bootstrapping+self-amplification etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "in Table 2 I am not sure how fair of a comparison it is. Perhaps with those techniques XGLM would exceed mT5 by a high margin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(In appendix G the additional techniques over SAP seems to make a huge difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Appendix B does show that mT5 performs on par with a much larger GPT3 on translation when both are self-amplified but still would be curious about XGLM given its multilingual emphasis similar to mT5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, I acknolwedge the effectiveness of SAP compared to other variants of prompts to mT5)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As the paper acknowledge, SAP requires running the whole encoder-decoder architecture in an autoregressive loop. This can be slow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Particularly critical is that caching isn't anymore possible (wherein caching is generally usable to speed up autoregressive decoding in causal decoders), because the encoder is bidirectional and in the autoregressive loop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The technical novelty of the paper is somewhat limited, since the SAP prompt technique is just feeding the generated token back the prompt again so as to get the new representation of the encoder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed SAP method on the mT5 models, however, claims that the performance improvements come from the Bidirectional LMs, which is not reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "This part of performance improvement may only come from SAP's effect on mT5, or SAP's effect on the encoder-decoder architecture model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Not only that, the underperforming attempt of running SAP on the T5 1.1 proves the unreliability of this conclusion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Lack of detail analysis on the computational efficiency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The quality is relatively good.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 70, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I am unclear of some of the experimental settings and how much we can conclude from them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Particularly, when is bootstrapping+self-amplification+ensembling are used? Is it used only during translation?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It also seems odd to use those techniques only for zero-shot and not adapt them (or explore possible adaptions) for 2-shot (given the explanation in 4.4)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "My main concern is, however, that if XGLM is not using bootstrapping+self-amplification etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "in Table 2 I am not sure how fair of a comparison it is. Perhaps with those techniques XGLM would exceed mT5 by a high margin.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "(In appendix G the additional techniques over SAP seems to make a huge difference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Appendix B does show that mT5 performs on par with a much larger GPT3 on translation when both are self-amplified but still would be curious about XGLM given its multilingual emphasis similar to mT5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "However, I acknolwedge the effectiveness of SAP compared to other variants of prompts to mT5)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "As the paper acknowledge, SAP requires running the whole encoder-decoder architecture in an autoregressive loop. This can be slow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Particularly critical is that caching isn't anymore possible (wherein caching is generally usable to speed up autoregressive decoding in causal decoders), because the encoder is bidirectional and in the autoregressive loop.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The method is applicable to mT5 style models, but the long-term solution is probably to adapt mT5 models with causal LM or prefix LM objectives.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This will make the inference more efficient as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 70, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The supervised translation baseline include in Table 2 should perhaps be replaced with a more recent and stronger multilingual model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I would definitely challenge the classification of languages such as ko, ar, sw etc. as being low-resource.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These are all languages for which at least 1M training sentences can be found (see e.g. Table 1 in https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.04672)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Minor: I believe the meaning of \"Supervised\" in Table 2 is only explained in the Appendix. I would recommend explaining it in the table caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The technical novelty of the paper is somewhat limited, since the SAP prompt technique is just feeding the generated token back the prompt again so as to get the new representation of the encoder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed SAP method on the mT5 models, however, claims that the performance improvements come from the Bidirectional LMs, which is not reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "This part of performance improvement may only come from SAP's effect on mT5, or SAP's effect on the encoder-decoder architecture model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Not only that, the underperforming attempt of running SAP on the T5 1.1 proves the unreliability of this conclusion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Lack of detail analysis on the computational efficiency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The quality is relatively good.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 70, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The supervised translation baseline include in Table 2 should perhaps be replaced with a more recent and stronger multilingual model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I would definitely challenge the classification of languages such as ko, ar, sw etc. as being low-resource.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "These are all languages for which at least 1M training sentences can be found (see e.g. Table 1 in https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.04672)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Minor: I believe the meaning of \"Supervised\" in Table 2 is only explained in the Appendix. I would recommend explaining it in the table caption.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The method is applicable to mT5 style models, but the long-term solution is probably to adapt mT5 models with causal LM or prefix LM objectives.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This will make the inference more efficient as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 70, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The technical novelty of the paper is somewhat limited, since the SAP prompt technique is just feeding the generated token back the prompt again so as to get the new representation of the encoder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper only verifies the effectiveness of the proposed SAP method on the mT5 models, however, claims that the performance improvements come from the Bidirectional LMs, which is not reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "This part of performance improvement may only come from SAP's effect on mT5, or SAP's effect on the encoder-decoder architecture model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Not only that, the underperforming attempt of running SAP on the T5 1.1 proves the unreliability of this conclusion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Lack of detail analysis on the computational efficiency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The quality is relatively good.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The method is applicable to mT5 style models, but the long-term solution is probably to adapt mT5 models with causal LM or prefix LM objectives.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This will make the inference more efficient as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 71, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "But its execution can be significantly improved:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The paper overclaims the benefit of low-rank finetuning without enough experiments to back it up.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "LoRA only finetunes W_q and W_v. One missing baseline is a full-rank finetuning model, which only updates W_q and W_v.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The memory/parameter efficiency of LoRA is exaggerated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Thorough proof reading is needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Please see below for a long, but by no means exhaustive list of typos.-", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "One may argue that LoRA can take more training steps to converge due to low-rank updates.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "So it would be great to compare learning curves and training time overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Details:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "What does VRAM stand for?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If it is GPU memory, then the 2/3 saving of training memory is quite surprising to me\u2014LoRA still needs to build the forward and backward computation graphs for the whole model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Could the authors clarify?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "A (probably more general) variant of LoRA would be, instead of learning A and B, one forces the gradients to be of low-rank.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "So accumulating multiple low-rank steps could still yield a higher-rank update to the parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Have the authors considered this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One limitation of this method (also mentioned in the paper) is how the model works for multi-task serving setting, which is possibly the most common scenario in deploying large-scale pretrained models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since LoRA is able to meet this requirement by not merging the pretrained weights (W_0) and low-rank weights (BA), it will be interesting if the author can report the comparison results of inference latency among (no-merging) LoRA and other methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Another line of work on mixture of experts (MoE) also shows promising results on mult-task/domain training (e.g., DEMix Layers: https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.05036), where we can selectively train expert modules for specific downstream task (while freezing the rest parameters).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It will be interesting to see how RoLA compares with it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 71, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "But its execution can be significantly improved:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The paper overclaims the benefit of low-rank finetuning without enough experiments to back it up.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "LoRA only finetunes W_q and W_v. One missing baseline is a full-rank finetuning model, which only updates W_q and W_v.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The memory/parameter efficiency of LoRA is exaggerated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Thorough proof reading is needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Please see below for a long, but by no means exhaustive list of typos.-", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "One may argue that LoRA can take more training steps to converge due to low-rank updates.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "So it would be great to compare learning curves and training time overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Details:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "What does VRAM stand for?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If it is GPU memory, then the 2/3 saving of training memory is quite surprising to me\u2014LoRA still needs to build the forward and backward computation graphs for the whole model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Could the authors clarify?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "A (probably more general) variant of LoRA would be, instead of learning A and B, one forces the gradients to be of low-rank.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "So accumulating multiple low-rank steps could still yield a higher-rank update to the parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Have the authors considered this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The idea is incremental. The additional parameters have a similar intuition as adapters.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Missing comparison to the SOTA models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The training efficiency remains unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Summary Of The Review:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 71, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "But its execution can be significantly improved:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The paper overclaims the benefit of low-rank finetuning without enough experiments to back it up.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "LoRA only finetunes W_q and W_v. One missing baseline is a full-rank finetuning model, which only updates W_q and W_v.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The memory/parameter efficiency of LoRA is exaggerated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Thorough proof reading is needed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Please see below for a long, but by no means exhaustive list of typos.-", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "One may argue that LoRA can take more training steps to converge due to low-rank updates.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "So it would be great to compare learning curves and training time overhead.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Details:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "What does VRAM stand for?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If it is GPU memory, then the 2/3 saving of training memory is quite surprising to me\u2014LoRA still needs to build the forward and backward computation graphs for the whole model.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Could the authors clarify?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "A (probably more general) variant of LoRA would be, instead of learning A and B, one forces the gradients to be of low-rank.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "So accumulating multiple low-rank steps could still yield a higher-rank update to the parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Have the authors considered this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Minor weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The training efficiency of LoRA is a bit unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In \"Practical Benefits and Limitations.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "the authors mention a 2/3 reduction in VRAM by not storing the gradients of the previous steps in Adam, which is true but it is important to mention that the gradient of the original weights is still needed (although not stored) at the current optimization step to compute the gradient for the low-ranked weights (if I understood correctly your method).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Anyhow, this step is needed also by other efficient fine-tuning methods (e.g. Adapter and Prompt-Tuning) and can be very costly in very large models (e.g., GPT-3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The authors did not report any baselines outside of the efficient fine-tuning setting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "This is important for the reader to understand how far an efficient model is compared to the current SOTA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "By no means, the reader, and me, expect new SOTA, but it is good to have these references in the table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 71, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One limitation of this method (also mentioned in the paper) is how the model works for multi-task serving setting, which is possibly the most common scenario in deploying large-scale pretrained models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since LoRA is able to meet this requirement by not merging the pretrained weights (W_0) and low-rank weights (BA), it will be interesting if the author can report the comparison results of inference latency among (no-merging) LoRA and other methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Another line of work on mixture of experts (MoE) also shows promising results on mult-task/domain training (e.g., DEMix Layers: https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.05036), where we can selectively train expert modules for specific downstream task (while freezing the rest parameters).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It will be interesting to see how RoLA compares with it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The idea is incremental. The additional parameters have a similar intuition as adapters.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Missing comparison to the SOTA models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The training efficiency remains unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Summary Of The Review:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 71, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One limitation of this method (also mentioned in the paper) is how the model works for multi-task serving setting, which is possibly the most common scenario in deploying large-scale pretrained models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Since LoRA is able to meet this requirement by not merging the pretrained weights (W_0) and low-rank weights (BA), it will be interesting if the author can report the comparison results of inference latency among (no-merging) LoRA and other methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Another line of work on mixture of experts (MoE) also shows promising results on mult-task/domain training (e.g., DEMix Layers: https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.05036), where we can selectively train expert modules for specific downstream task (while freezing the rest parameters).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It will be interesting to see how RoLA compares with it.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Minor weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The training efficiency of LoRA is a bit unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In \"Practical Benefits and Limitations.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "the authors mention a 2/3 reduction in VRAM by not storing the gradients of the previous steps in Adam, which is true but it is important to mention that the gradient of the original weights is still needed (although not stored) at the current optimization step to compute the gradient for the low-ranked weights (if I understood correctly your method).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Anyhow, this step is needed also by other efficient fine-tuning methods (e.g. Adapter and Prompt-Tuning) and can be very costly in very large models (e.g., GPT-3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The authors did not report any baselines outside of the efficient fine-tuning setting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "This is important for the reader to understand how far an efficient model is compared to the current SOTA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "By no means, the reader, and me, expect new SOTA, but it is good to have these references in the table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 71, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The idea is incremental. The additional parameters have a similar intuition as adapters.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Missing comparison to the SOTA models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The training efficiency remains unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Summary Of The Review:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Minor weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The training efficiency of LoRA is a bit unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "In \"Practical Benefits and Limitations.\"", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "the authors mention a 2/3 reduction in VRAM by not storing the gradients of the previous steps in Adam, which is true but it is important to mention that the gradient of the original weights is still needed (although not stored) at the current optimization step to compute the gradient for the low-ranked weights (if I understood correctly your method).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Anyhow, this step is needed also by other efficient fine-tuning methods (e.g. Adapter and Prompt-Tuning) and can be very costly in very large models (e.g., GPT-3).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The authors did not report any baselines outside of the efficient fine-tuning setting.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "This is important for the reader to understand how far an efficient model is compared to the current SOTA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "By no means, the reader, and me, expect new SOTA, but it is good to have these references in the table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 72, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "One of my big concerns is regarding the privacy evaluation of the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The evaluation focuses specifically on preventing exact verbatim memorization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "A trivial baseline for stopping verbatim memorization is to just manually block the model from generating exact strings in the training set, potentially using a suffix tree like is done in Kandpal et al. 2022 and Katherine Lee et al. 2022.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, this trivial baseline cannot prevent models from privacy risks such as (1) generating paraphrases of training texts, (2) leaking private information in a QA or dialogue setting (e.g., ask someone's SSN), and (3) cannot stop membership inference attacks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have some concerns regarding the technical novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The general idea of gradient ascent on some data to cause models to avoid learning is an idea that has been well-explored in the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, the canonical Ganin et al. 2015 paper that proposes \"gradient reversal\" layers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "More recently, there is the large body of work on machine unlearning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Although the paper is correct that this is mainly focused on CV and basic regression tasks, there isn't a ton of new technical novelty required to apply the ideas to NLP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I find it weird to discuss de-duplication as a sort of baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Deduplication should be almost always used as a first data processing stage to mitigate memorization (Kandpal et al. 2022; Carlini et al. 2022), and in that sense, it's quite orthogonal to the method proposed here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The Ablation/Understanding section of this paper is weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 72, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "One of my big concerns is regarding the privacy evaluation of the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The evaluation focuses specifically on preventing exact verbatim memorization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "A trivial baseline for stopping verbatim memorization is to just manually block the model from generating exact strings in the training set, potentially using a suffix tree like is done in Kandpal et al. 2022 and Katherine Lee et al. 2022.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, this trivial baseline cannot prevent models from privacy risks such as (1) generating paraphrases of training texts, (2) leaking private information in a QA or dialogue setting (e.g., ask someone's SSN), and (3) cannot stop membership inference attacks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have some concerns regarding the technical novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The general idea of gradient ascent on some data to cause models to avoid learning is an idea that has been well-explored in the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, the canonical Ganin et al. 2015 paper that proposes \"gradient reversal\" layers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "More recently, there is the large body of work on machine unlearning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Although the paper is correct that this is mainly focused on CV and basic regression tasks, there isn't a ton of new technical novelty required to apply the ideas to NLP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I find it weird to discuss de-duplication as a sort of baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Deduplication should be almost always used as a first data processing stage to mitigate memorization (Kandpal et al. 2022; Carlini et al. 2022), and in that sense, it's quite orthogonal to the method proposed here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 72, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "One of my big concerns is regarding the privacy evaluation of the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The evaluation focuses specifically on preventing exact verbatim memorization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "A trivial baseline for stopping verbatim memorization is to just manually block the model from generating exact strings in the training set, potentially using a suffix tree like is done in Kandpal et al. 2022 and Katherine Lee et al. 2022.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Of course, this trivial baseline cannot prevent models from privacy risks such as (1) generating paraphrases of training texts, (2) leaking private information in a QA or dialogue setting (e.g., ask someone's SSN), and (3) cannot stop membership inference attacks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I have some concerns regarding the technical novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The general idea of gradient ascent on some data to cause models to avoid learning is an idea that has been well-explored in the literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, the canonical Ganin et al. 2015 paper that proposes \"gradient reversal\" layers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "More recently, there is the large body of work on machine unlearning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Although the paper is correct that this is mainly focused on CV and basic regression tasks, there isn't a ton of new technical novelty required to apply the ideas to NLP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I find it weird to discuss de-duplication as a sort of baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Deduplication should be almost always used as a first data processing stage to mitigate memorization (Kandpal et al. 2022; Carlini et al. 2022), and in that sense, it's quite orthogonal to the method proposed here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Minor Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 72, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The Ablation/Understanding section of this paper is weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 72, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The Ablation/Understanding section of this paper is weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Minor Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 72, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Minor Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 73, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "However, I think the concerns that some readers might raise and complain include:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "However, is it possible that the model doesn\u2019t understand the relationship between the concepts and the grounded representations, but instead utilizes a similarity between the test grounded representation and the grounded representations in the in-context prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, upon seeing the test representation (e.g., [0,1,0,0] in the spatial domain, or RGB (140, 0, 255) in the color domain), the model can use a simple strategy: copying the concept of a bunch of most similar representations in the in-line prompt examples (e.g., [0, 1, 0, 0, 0], or RGB (145, 0, 255)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "This strategy would not involve the concept of \u201cleft\u201d or \u201cpink\u201d, and is robust to the rotation transformation (while not robust to the random transformation, if each point in the world was transformed independently).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This would align with the results in Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But the performance is not very strong in these cases even for GPT-3 (top-1 accuracy).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Again, the performance is not strong, if the number of prompts goes below 20 or 60.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "(3) Though the paper investigates an interesting problem, the overall takeaway of this work is not very clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "How is the analysis useful for future work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "(4) Some details in the paper should be checked.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "For example, in Section 2.1, the authors say that all models are pretrained on the 40GB OPENAI-WT dataset, but this is not true for GPT-3?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Also for the color experiments, it is not clear whether 60 or 6+57 or 70 (as mentioned in B.1.1 in the appendix) prompts were used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Also, the takeaway and impact of the work are not very clear to me, other than showing the somewhat inscrutable power of GPT-3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "However, I find many statements not thought out as much as they deserve.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "For one, colours are as relative as left/right.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Something red looks dramatically different under white light than it does under other colours of light, and so on.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Also see visual illusions where the same shade of grey looks like different colours as a function of where it is in the image.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "This is why categorical cognition plays a role in colours in humans.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Another example is on the equivocation of \"text\" and \"language\" throughout the manuscript.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I also find it a bit strange to say \"north\" in humans is not grounded.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I assume it is always grounded to where the Sun is and what hemisphere we are in and so if somebody were to lie to us about where north is, we would eventually figure it out when we saw the movement of shadows, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There is a lot of research on what humans indeed do here, so some literature review could be done if claims need to be made about actual human behaviour.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 73, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "However, I think the concerns that some readers might raise and complain include:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "However, is it possible that the model doesn\u2019t understand the relationship between the concepts and the grounded representations, but instead utilizes a similarity between the test grounded representation and the grounded representations in the in-context prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, upon seeing the test representation (e.g., [0,1,0,0] in the spatial domain, or RGB (140, 0, 255) in the color domain), the model can use a simple strategy: copying the concept of a bunch of most similar representations in the in-line prompt examples (e.g., [0, 1, 0, 0, 0], or RGB (145, 0, 255)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "This strategy would not involve the concept of \u201cleft\u201d or \u201cpink\u201d, and is robust to the rotation transformation (while not robust to the random transformation, if each point in the world was transformed independently).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This would align with the results in Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But the performance is not very strong in these cases even for GPT-3 (top-1 accuracy).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Again, the performance is not strong, if the number of prompts goes below 20 or 60.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "(3) Though the paper investigates an interesting problem, the overall takeaway of this work is not very clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "How is the analysis useful for future work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "(4) Some details in the paper should be checked.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "For example, in Section 2.1, the authors say that all models are pretrained on the 40GB OPENAI-WT dataset, but this is not true for GPT-3?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Also for the color experiments, it is not clear whether 60 or 6+57 or 70 (as mentioned in B.1.1 in the appendix) prompts were used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Also, the takeaway and impact of the work are not very clear to me, other than showing the somewhat inscrutable power of GPT-3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The scope was fairly narrow and the experiments were not diverse enough to provide a sense of a thorough understanding of how LM's are forming this space, what possible confounds might be at play, or what other domains might have similar effects.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "One of the few questions about the color experiment results was to what extent the model was backing off to predict less specific color names, since it is something that is not well-captured in top-1 accuracy (though maybe some combination of this and distance evaluation starts to get at it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "And why were 67 other colors inserted in the color prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It seemed arbitrary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 73, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "However, I think the concerns that some readers might raise and complain include:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "However, is it possible that the model doesn\u2019t understand the relationship between the concepts and the grounded representations, but instead utilizes a similarity between the test grounded representation and the grounded representations in the in-context prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For example, upon seeing the test representation (e.g., [0,1,0,0] in the spatial domain, or RGB (140, 0, 255) in the color domain), the model can use a simple strategy: copying the concept of a bunch of most similar representations in the in-line prompt examples (e.g., [0, 1, 0, 0, 0], or RGB (145, 0, 255)).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "This strategy would not involve the concept of \u201cleft\u201d or \u201cpink\u201d, and is robust to the rotation transformation (while not robust to the random transformation, if each point in the world was transformed independently).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This would align with the results in Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But the performance is not very strong in these cases even for GPT-3 (top-1 accuracy).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Again, the performance is not strong, if the number of prompts goes below 20 or 60.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "(3) Though the paper investigates an interesting problem, the overall takeaway of this work is not very clear to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "How is the analysis useful for future work?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "(4) Some details in the paper should be checked.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "For example, in Section 2.1, the authors say that all models are pretrained on the 40GB OPENAI-WT dataset, but this is not true for GPT-3?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Also for the color experiments, it is not clear whether 60 or 6+57 or 70 (as mentioned in B.1.1 in the appendix) prompts were used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Also, the takeaway and impact of the work are not very clear to me, other than showing the somewhat inscrutable power of GPT-3.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "I think there are some subtle but pretty important problems with the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "To fix them, it might be okay to just change the framing and back off from the \"isomorphism\" angle, but that feels like losing the whole point of the paper and leaving it kind of uninteresting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "In particular the Abdou et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "paper already provides pretty good evidence of conceptual grounding of colors using representational similarity analysis and linear mappings on color representations extracted from the LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "These methods have some underlying assumptions but it seems to me that those assumptions are much weaker than the ones underlying the method used in this paper, which relies on the few-shot learning capabilities of the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "More on that in the next point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I don't think the R-IV baseline is more fair than the R-ID one, as claimed in Sec. 2.5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This paper is about producing the correct term for the provided grounding, which is a different problem from producing an in-domain term.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Generating an in-domain term is a priori significantly easier (e.g., by hard-coding it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "By including the requirement to generate an in-domain term, these experiments conflate the two problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "In short, by relying on the few-shot learning paradigm to extract the conceptual structure of concern, we're limited to extracting conceptual structure that can surface through few-shot learning (just like, for example, linear probes can only extract linearly separable features).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "This ends up being more a measure of the few-shot learning ability of the model than the conceptual grounding capabilities of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "This is particularly problematic because it makes the baselines (ie, GPT-2 models) seem weaker than they probably should be, and it limits the baselines that can be used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "I would point particularly to Abdou et al., who obtained alignments similar in quality to those from masked LMs (BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA) using just PMI statistics and fastText embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "It's not clear to me if the large models are exhibiting conceptual generalization/grounding abilities much larger than those afforded by such baselines (once we move out of the few-shot learning format).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "To add to this issue, the headline experiment of generalizing to new concepts has at least one big issue that I find concerning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Including all n-1 concepts besides the target one, or many such concepts (eg, 60 colors) provides a very strong constraint in the input (in my view contradicting the \"only a small number of examples\" claim in the paper's abstract).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "While, yes, generalizing to new concepts does demonstrate that the model understands something about the domain, I think it's important that we understand this in graded terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The question this paper seems designed to address, based on the framing in the introduction and conclusion, is about whether language models can or do construct conceptual spaces that are roughly \"isomorphic\" to grounded ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "In order to test this, of course, we have to construct the isomorphism.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "But \u2014 and this is one of the classic problems with probing \u2014 if we are looking at it in terms of set isomorphism (i.e., without extra homomorphism structure \u2014 and we are, if we're measuring prediction accuracy), then it is always possible to reconstruct such an isomorphism if we have enough supervision (See Pimentel et al.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "The question is how much supervision we need versus how much is usefully obtainable from the pretrained model, hence approaches like Voita and Titov (2020)'s information-theoretic probing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "So it's not really a black and white question whether there is an \"isomorphic\" conceptual space inside the model, but a graded one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "And when we talk about things in terms of an isomorphism, what we're really implying is that the vast majority of the information required to construct the isomorphism is easily accessible from the model representations, so less supervision is required.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "This is the basic thinking behind the idea of a \"minimal grounding toehold\" which has been discussed as a prerequisite to grounding language models in conceptual spaces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Some of this discussion in the NLP community subsequent to Bender and Koller's 2020 paper was catalogued in a blog post that goes into a lot more detail on the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "That post proposed a color experiment extremely close to what is done in this paper, but an important difference in their proposal is that the input used by the LM to reconstruct the grounded lexicon is the minimal size that is required to span the conceptual space (i.e., constraining only the necessary degrees of freedom).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "One might use light/dark, red/green, and blue/yellow as the three basis dimensions to align with CIELAB, or even just to try red, green, and blue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "I think such a minimal experiment would be required to effectively address the isomorphism question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "This paper, by providing many more inputs, is doing something of a mix of probing (because of the few-shot/supervision aspect) and behavioral testing (because it's relying on inherent generalization behavior in the LM distribution).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "The inclusion of a bunch of distinct concepts in the input can potentially also provide the model with a pragmatic cue that it will be presented with a new/unseen concept label if it receives a new/unseen kind of input, which adds another confound.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "This may not be much of an issue in the case of color since the space is fairly open and only 1/6 of the set of colors was covered anyway, but it still means a totally ungrounded baseline could do better than random.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The results of Abdou et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "seem to indicate (to me) that it should be possible to do quite a bit better than the GPT-2 results in this paper using some kind of simple model (for example with word embeddings or a probing model on LM representations).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 64, "text": "It's important to have these baselines so as not to misrepresent the performance trend with respect to model growth, which I suspect reflects more the ability of the model to do \"few-shot learning\" than to ground concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 68, "text": "I understand you would probably want to express the color in RGB for the purposes of LM testing, but Rotations in RGB seems like it isn't the best way of doing the isomorphism control: rotating in a space where distances are more perceptually meaningful (such as CIELAB) seems to me like it would have been a better approach, as while rotation preserves distances, the same distances in different parts of RGB space may correspond to different perceptual distances.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 69, "text": "On that note, I'm not sure if I totally understand the random world baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 70, "text": "It sounds from the description in the text (Sec.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 71, "text": "2.4) that you ground words entirely randomly, but Figure 2 illustrates a \"random rotation\" which is much more constrained than this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 72, "text": "It suggests that a random rotation should destroy the underlying structure, but I don't see why this would be the case.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 73, "text": "As long as distances are preserved (under the assumption necessary for the control that distances are meaningful in the first place) the structure should be preserved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 74, "text": "So any rotation should work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 75, "text": "In what sense does a random rotation destroy the structure?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 76, "text": "I don't buy it based just on the example shown in Figure 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 77, "text": "The string matching Top-1 accuracy metric seems quite permissive \u2014 are there examples, or is there a human verification step you can provide to show that it is reasonable in this case and not overly permissive?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 78, "text": "There seems to be some confusion about the training data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 79, "text": "When you first mention it, you use the term OPENAI-WT with no further description or reference (Sec2.1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 80, "text": "I don't think that is the dataset's name \u2014 I found it used as an abbreviation in one paper, Gehman et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 81, "text": "2020, but nowhere else.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 82, "text": "The dataset is just called WebText.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 83, "text": "Can you just call it WebText (or OpenAI WebText) and cite the GPT-2 paper directly when you name it (so it's clear where it's from)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 84, "text": "Also, in Section 2.1 it says all models are trained on this data, but this is incorrect \u2014 GPT-3 was trained on a different mix of data including two corpora of books, Wikipedia, and Common Crawl.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 85, "text": "Also, Appendix A calls the data \"OpenWT\", which sounds like \"OpenWebText\", which is an open dataset scraped using the same methods as WebText \u2014 but isn't the same dataset as was used to train GPT-2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 86, "text": "Then, Section 2.4 does cite the GPT-2 paper for the data, but calls it CommonCrawl, which is incorrect \u2014 that's a different web scrape.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 95, "text": "The biggest flaws in my view are the inclusion of too much supervision in the unseen-concept experiments, which make the results less relevant to the \"isomorphism\" question inasmuch as it is interesting to the community, and the exclusive reliance on the few-shot learning paradigm, which adds a big confounding factor to the results that makes it hard to interpret any clear trends.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 73, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "However, I find many statements not thought out as much as they deserve.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "For one, colours are as relative as left/right.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Something red looks dramatically different under white light than it does under other colours of light, and so on.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Also see visual illusions where the same shade of grey looks like different colours as a function of where it is in the image.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "This is why categorical cognition plays a role in colours in humans.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Another example is on the equivocation of \"text\" and \"language\" throughout the manuscript.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I also find it a bit strange to say \"north\" in humans is not grounded.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I assume it is always grounded to where the Sun is and what hemisphere we are in and so if somebody were to lie to us about where north is, we would eventually figure it out when we saw the movement of shadows, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There is a lot of research on what humans indeed do here, so some literature review could be done if claims need to be made about actual human behaviour.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The scope was fairly narrow and the experiments were not diverse enough to provide a sense of a thorough understanding of how LM's are forming this space, what possible confounds might be at play, or what other domains might have similar effects.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "One of the few questions about the color experiment results was to what extent the model was backing off to predict less specific color names, since it is something that is not well-captured in top-1 accuracy (though maybe some combination of this and distance evaluation starts to get at it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "And why were 67 other colors inserted in the color prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It seemed arbitrary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 73, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "However, I find many statements not thought out as much as they deserve.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "For one, colours are as relative as left/right.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Something red looks dramatically different under white light than it does under other colours of light, and so on.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Also see visual illusions where the same shade of grey looks like different colours as a function of where it is in the image.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "This is why categorical cognition plays a role in colours in humans.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Another example is on the equivocation of \"text\" and \"language\" throughout the manuscript.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I also find it a bit strange to say \"north\" in humans is not grounded.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I assume it is always grounded to where the Sun is and what hemisphere we are in and so if somebody were to lie to us about where north is, we would eventually figure it out when we saw the movement of shadows, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "There is a lot of research on what humans indeed do here, so some literature review could be done if claims need to be made about actual human behaviour.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "I think there are some subtle but pretty important problems with the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "To fix them, it might be okay to just change the framing and back off from the \"isomorphism\" angle, but that feels like losing the whole point of the paper and leaving it kind of uninteresting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "In particular the Abdou et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "paper already provides pretty good evidence of conceptual grounding of colors using representational similarity analysis and linear mappings on color representations extracted from the LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "These methods have some underlying assumptions but it seems to me that those assumptions are much weaker than the ones underlying the method used in this paper, which relies on the few-shot learning capabilities of the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "More on that in the next point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I don't think the R-IV baseline is more fair than the R-ID one, as claimed in Sec. 2.5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This paper is about producing the correct term for the provided grounding, which is a different problem from producing an in-domain term.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Generating an in-domain term is a priori significantly easier (e.g., by hard-coding it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "By including the requirement to generate an in-domain term, these experiments conflate the two problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "In short, by relying on the few-shot learning paradigm to extract the conceptual structure of concern, we're limited to extracting conceptual structure that can surface through few-shot learning (just like, for example, linear probes can only extract linearly separable features).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "This ends up being more a measure of the few-shot learning ability of the model than the conceptual grounding capabilities of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "This is particularly problematic because it makes the baselines (ie, GPT-2 models) seem weaker than they probably should be, and it limits the baselines that can be used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "I would point particularly to Abdou et al., who obtained alignments similar in quality to those from masked LMs (BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA) using just PMI statistics and fastText embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "It's not clear to me if the large models are exhibiting conceptual generalization/grounding abilities much larger than those afforded by such baselines (once we move out of the few-shot learning format).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "To add to this issue, the headline experiment of generalizing to new concepts has at least one big issue that I find concerning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Including all n-1 concepts besides the target one, or many such concepts (eg, 60 colors) provides a very strong constraint in the input (in my view contradicting the \"only a small number of examples\" claim in the paper's abstract).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "While, yes, generalizing to new concepts does demonstrate that the model understands something about the domain, I think it's important that we understand this in graded terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The question this paper seems designed to address, based on the framing in the introduction and conclusion, is about whether language models can or do construct conceptual spaces that are roughly \"isomorphic\" to grounded ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "In order to test this, of course, we have to construct the isomorphism.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "But \u2014 and this is one of the classic problems with probing \u2014 if we are looking at it in terms of set isomorphism (i.e., without extra homomorphism structure \u2014 and we are, if we're measuring prediction accuracy), then it is always possible to reconstruct such an isomorphism if we have enough supervision (See Pimentel et al.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "The question is how much supervision we need versus how much is usefully obtainable from the pretrained model, hence approaches like Voita and Titov (2020)'s information-theoretic probing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "So it's not really a black and white question whether there is an \"isomorphic\" conceptual space inside the model, but a graded one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "And when we talk about things in terms of an isomorphism, what we're really implying is that the vast majority of the information required to construct the isomorphism is easily accessible from the model representations, so less supervision is required.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "This is the basic thinking behind the idea of a \"minimal grounding toehold\" which has been discussed as a prerequisite to grounding language models in conceptual spaces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Some of this discussion in the NLP community subsequent to Bender and Koller's 2020 paper was catalogued in a blog post that goes into a lot more detail on the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "That post proposed a color experiment extremely close to what is done in this paper, but an important difference in their proposal is that the input used by the LM to reconstruct the grounded lexicon is the minimal size that is required to span the conceptual space (i.e., constraining only the necessary degrees of freedom).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "One might use light/dark, red/green, and blue/yellow as the three basis dimensions to align with CIELAB, or even just to try red, green, and blue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "I think such a minimal experiment would be required to effectively address the isomorphism question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "This paper, by providing many more inputs, is doing something of a mix of probing (because of the few-shot/supervision aspect) and behavioral testing (because it's relying on inherent generalization behavior in the LM distribution).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "The inclusion of a bunch of distinct concepts in the input can potentially also provide the model with a pragmatic cue that it will be presented with a new/unseen concept label if it receives a new/unseen kind of input, which adds another confound.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "This may not be much of an issue in the case of color since the space is fairly open and only 1/6 of the set of colors was covered anyway, but it still means a totally ungrounded baseline could do better than random.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The results of Abdou et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "seem to indicate (to me) that it should be possible to do quite a bit better than the GPT-2 results in this paper using some kind of simple model (for example with word embeddings or a probing model on LM representations).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 64, "text": "It's important to have these baselines so as not to misrepresent the performance trend with respect to model growth, which I suspect reflects more the ability of the model to do \"few-shot learning\" than to ground concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 68, "text": "I understand you would probably want to express the color in RGB for the purposes of LM testing, but Rotations in RGB seems like it isn't the best way of doing the isomorphism control: rotating in a space where distances are more perceptually meaningful (such as CIELAB) seems to me like it would have been a better approach, as while rotation preserves distances, the same distances in different parts of RGB space may correspond to different perceptual distances.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 69, "text": "On that note, I'm not sure if I totally understand the random world baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 70, "text": "It sounds from the description in the text (Sec.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 71, "text": "2.4) that you ground words entirely randomly, but Figure 2 illustrates a \"random rotation\" which is much more constrained than this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 72, "text": "It suggests that a random rotation should destroy the underlying structure, but I don't see why this would be the case.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 73, "text": "As long as distances are preserved (under the assumption necessary for the control that distances are meaningful in the first place) the structure should be preserved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 74, "text": "So any rotation should work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 75, "text": "In what sense does a random rotation destroy the structure?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 76, "text": "I don't buy it based just on the example shown in Figure 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 77, "text": "The string matching Top-1 accuracy metric seems quite permissive \u2014 are there examples, or is there a human verification step you can provide to show that it is reasonable in this case and not overly permissive?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 78, "text": "There seems to be some confusion about the training data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 79, "text": "When you first mention it, you use the term OPENAI-WT with no further description or reference (Sec2.1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 80, "text": "I don't think that is the dataset's name \u2014 I found it used as an abbreviation in one paper, Gehman et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 81, "text": "2020, but nowhere else.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 82, "text": "The dataset is just called WebText.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 83, "text": "Can you just call it WebText (or OpenAI WebText) and cite the GPT-2 paper directly when you name it (so it's clear where it's from)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 84, "text": "Also, in Section 2.1 it says all models are trained on this data, but this is incorrect \u2014 GPT-3 was trained on a different mix of data including two corpora of books, Wikipedia, and Common Crawl.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 85, "text": "Also, Appendix A calls the data \"OpenWT\", which sounds like \"OpenWebText\", which is an open dataset scraped using the same methods as WebText \u2014 but isn't the same dataset as was used to train GPT-2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 86, "text": "Then, Section 2.4 does cite the GPT-2 paper for the data, but calls it CommonCrawl, which is incorrect \u2014 that's a different web scrape.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 95, "text": "The biggest flaws in my view are the inclusion of too much supervision in the unseen-concept experiments, which make the results less relevant to the \"isomorphism\" question inasmuch as it is interesting to the community, and the exclusive reliance on the few-shot learning paradigm, which adds a big confounding factor to the results that makes it hard to interpret any clear trends.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 73, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The scope was fairly narrow and the experiments were not diverse enough to provide a sense of a thorough understanding of how LM's are forming this space, what possible confounds might be at play, or what other domains might have similar effects.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "One of the few questions about the color experiment results was to what extent the model was backing off to predict less specific color names, since it is something that is not well-captured in top-1 accuracy (though maybe some combination of this and distance evaluation starts to get at it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "And why were 67 other colors inserted in the color prompts?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It seemed arbitrary.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "I think there are some subtle but pretty important problems with the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "To fix them, it might be okay to just change the framing and back off from the \"isomorphism\" angle, but that feels like losing the whole point of the paper and leaving it kind of uninteresting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "In particular the Abdou et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "paper already provides pretty good evidence of conceptual grounding of colors using representational similarity analysis and linear mappings on color representations extracted from the LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "These methods have some underlying assumptions but it seems to me that those assumptions are much weaker than the ones underlying the method used in this paper, which relies on the few-shot learning capabilities of the language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "More on that in the next point.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I don't think the R-IV baseline is more fair than the R-ID one, as claimed in Sec. 2.5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "This paper is about producing the correct term for the provided grounding, which is a different problem from producing an in-domain term.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "Generating an in-domain term is a priori significantly easier (e.g., by hard-coding it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "By including the requirement to generate an in-domain term, these experiments conflate the two problems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "In short, by relying on the few-shot learning paradigm to extract the conceptual structure of concern, we're limited to extracting conceptual structure that can surface through few-shot learning (just like, for example, linear probes can only extract linearly separable features).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "This ends up being more a measure of the few-shot learning ability of the model than the conceptual grounding capabilities of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "This is particularly problematic because it makes the baselines (ie, GPT-2 models) seem weaker than they probably should be, and it limits the baselines that can be used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "I would point particularly to Abdou et al., who obtained alignments similar in quality to those from masked LMs (BERT, RoBERTa, ELECTRA) using just PMI statistics and fastText embeddings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "It's not clear to me if the large models are exhibiting conceptual generalization/grounding abilities much larger than those afforded by such baselines (once we move out of the few-shot learning format).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "To add to this issue, the headline experiment of generalizing to new concepts has at least one big issue that I find concerning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Including all n-1 concepts besides the target one, or many such concepts (eg, 60 colors) provides a very strong constraint in the input (in my view contradicting the \"only a small number of examples\" claim in the paper's abstract).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "While, yes, generalizing to new concepts does demonstrate that the model understands something about the domain, I think it's important that we understand this in graded terms.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "The question this paper seems designed to address, based on the framing in the introduction and conclusion, is about whether language models can or do construct conceptual spaces that are roughly \"isomorphic\" to grounded ones.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "In order to test this, of course, we have to construct the isomorphism.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "But \u2014 and this is one of the classic problems with probing \u2014 if we are looking at it in terms of set isomorphism (i.e., without extra homomorphism structure \u2014 and we are, if we're measuring prediction accuracy), then it is always possible to reconstruct such an isomorphism if we have enough supervision (See Pimentel et al.).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "The question is how much supervision we need versus how much is usefully obtainable from the pretrained model, hence approaches like Voita and Titov (2020)'s information-theoretic probing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "So it's not really a black and white question whether there is an \"isomorphic\" conceptual space inside the model, but a graded one.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "And when we talk about things in terms of an isomorphism, what we're really implying is that the vast majority of the information required to construct the isomorphism is easily accessible from the model representations, so less supervision is required.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "This is the basic thinking behind the idea of a \"minimal grounding toehold\" which has been discussed as a prerequisite to grounding language models in conceptual spaces.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Some of this discussion in the NLP community subsequent to Bender and Koller's 2020 paper was catalogued in a blog post that goes into a lot more detail on the issue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "That post proposed a color experiment extremely close to what is done in this paper, but an important difference in their proposal is that the input used by the LM to reconstruct the grounded lexicon is the minimal size that is required to span the conceptual space (i.e., constraining only the necessary degrees of freedom).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "One might use light/dark, red/green, and blue/yellow as the three basis dimensions to align with CIELAB, or even just to try red, green, and blue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "I think such a minimal experiment would be required to effectively address the isomorphism question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 56, "text": "This paper, by providing many more inputs, is doing something of a mix of probing (because of the few-shot/supervision aspect) and behavioral testing (because it's relying on inherent generalization behavior in the LM distribution).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 58, "text": "The inclusion of a bunch of distinct concepts in the input can potentially also provide the model with a pragmatic cue that it will be presented with a new/unseen concept label if it receives a new/unseen kind of input, which adds another confound.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 59, "text": "This may not be much of an issue in the case of color since the space is fairly open and only 1/6 of the set of colors was covered anyway, but it still means a totally ungrounded baseline could do better than random.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 62, "text": "The results of Abdou et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 63, "text": "seem to indicate (to me) that it should be possible to do quite a bit better than the GPT-2 results in this paper using some kind of simple model (for example with word embeddings or a probing model on LM representations).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 64, "text": "It's important to have these baselines so as not to misrepresent the performance trend with respect to model growth, which I suspect reflects more the ability of the model to do \"few-shot learning\" than to ground concepts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 68, "text": "I understand you would probably want to express the color in RGB for the purposes of LM testing, but Rotations in RGB seems like it isn't the best way of doing the isomorphism control: rotating in a space where distances are more perceptually meaningful (such as CIELAB) seems to me like it would have been a better approach, as while rotation preserves distances, the same distances in different parts of RGB space may correspond to different perceptual distances.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 69, "text": "On that note, I'm not sure if I totally understand the random world baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 70, "text": "It sounds from the description in the text (Sec.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 71, "text": "2.4) that you ground words entirely randomly, but Figure 2 illustrates a \"random rotation\" which is much more constrained than this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 72, "text": "It suggests that a random rotation should destroy the underlying structure, but I don't see why this would be the case.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 73, "text": "As long as distances are preserved (under the assumption necessary for the control that distances are meaningful in the first place) the structure should be preserved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 74, "text": "So any rotation should work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 75, "text": "In what sense does a random rotation destroy the structure?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 76, "text": "I don't buy it based just on the example shown in Figure 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 77, "text": "The string matching Top-1 accuracy metric seems quite permissive \u2014 are there examples, or is there a human verification step you can provide to show that it is reasonable in this case and not overly permissive?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 78, "text": "There seems to be some confusion about the training data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 79, "text": "When you first mention it, you use the term OPENAI-WT with no further description or reference (Sec2.1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 80, "text": "I don't think that is the dataset's name \u2014 I found it used as an abbreviation in one paper, Gehman et al.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 81, "text": "2020, but nowhere else.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 82, "text": "The dataset is just called WebText.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 83, "text": "Can you just call it WebText (or OpenAI WebText) and cite the GPT-2 paper directly when you name it (so it's clear where it's from)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 84, "text": "Also, in Section 2.1 it says all models are trained on this data, but this is incorrect \u2014 GPT-3 was trained on a different mix of data including two corpora of books, Wikipedia, and Common Crawl.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 85, "text": "Also, Appendix A calls the data \"OpenWT\", which sounds like \"OpenWebText\", which is an open dataset scraped using the same methods as WebText \u2014 but isn't the same dataset as was used to train GPT-2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 86, "text": "Then, Section 2.4 does cite the GPT-2 paper for the data, but calls it CommonCrawl, which is incorrect \u2014 that's a different web scrape.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 95, "text": "The biggest flaws in my view are the inclusion of too much supervision in the unseen-concept experiments, which make the results less relevant to the \"isomorphism\" question inasmuch as it is interesting to the community, and the exclusive reliance on the few-shot learning paradigm, which adds a big confounding factor to the results that makes it hard to interpret any clear trends.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 74, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Their approach is an extension of Electra rather than something fundamentally new.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The exposition could be improved.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "In terms of originality, COCO-LM is built on ELECTRA, thus it lacks a novelty aspect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "ELECTRA does not have a sequence-level objective, COCO-LM uses a contrasting objective (SCL) for sequences thus it performs better than ELECTRA on sequence level tasks (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "GLUE).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As can be seen in table 2, SCL + RTD improves the average score on GLUE significantly compared to RTD (i.e., ELECTRA).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Having said that, I think the contribution of the paper is quite marginal, however I think its strong point is the efficiency in training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper is well-presented and contains a comprehensive list of experiments showing the effect of each of the modified components.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What I find missing is that the paper hasn\u2019t addressed the claim about missing language modelling benefit (line 107-113).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although the authors presented word-similarity (table 3, line 292) results, I think that it\u2019s rather a proxy evaluation but is not convincing enough about the benefit of language modeling capabilities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As the prompt-based approach [1,2,3] is gaining popularity recently, I wonder if COCO-LM can be used in this setup where the model is required to generate (i.e., assessing language modeling capability).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This is something MLM such as RoBERTA, BERT are doing quite well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 74, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Their approach is an extension of Electra rather than something fundamentally new.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The exposition could be improved.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "1. While the paper claim the importance of language modeling capability of pre-trained models, the authors did not conduct experments on generation tasks that are more likely to require a well-performing language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Experiments on word similarity and SquAD in section 5.3 cannot really reflect the capability of language modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors may consider to include tasks like language modeling, machine translation or text sumarization to strenghen this part, as this is one of the main motivations of COCO-LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "2. Analysis of SCL in section 5.2 regarding few-shot abaility looks not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The paper claims that a more regularized representation space by SCL may result in better generalization ability in few-shot scenarios.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, results in Figure 7(c) and (d) do not meet our expectation such that COCO-LM achieves much more improvements with less labels and the improvements will gradually disappear with more labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Besides, the authors may check if COCO-LM brings benefits to sentence retrieval tasks with the learned anisotropy text representations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The performance of Megatron and COCO-LM is close to other approaches, for examples, RoBERTa, ELECTRA, and DeBERTa, which are with similar sizes as COCO-LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "If the author claim that COCO-LM is parameter-efficient, the conclusion is also applicable to the above related works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 74, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Their approach is an extension of Electra rather than something fundamentally new.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The exposition could be improved.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 32, "text": "The biggest missing piece in the paper is the lack of hyperparameter details, because the appendix is not included.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The sections do appear in my pdf\u2019s reader table of content, but not the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "There are also some result concerns, which I detail below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "The appendix is currently missing and with it many of the hyperparameter details required for reproducibility.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Please share at least one run / set of results on the test set for each setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This is necessary to ensure that the difference is not just in hyperparameter overfitting during fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "I understand that the claim \u201cmatches Megatron-3.9Bn, a model 10x bigger on MNLI\u201d is compelling and makes for a great abstract.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "However, I think it is misleading and poor - scholarship.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Indeed, Megatron-3.9Bn is not a great baseline for a 1Bn+ model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "For instance, DeBERTA 1.5B+SIFT achieves 92.0/92.1 on MNLI (see Table 12 in their appendix, though not clear whether this is dev or test), quite a bit higher than Megatron 3.9Bn (and COCO-LM).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Minor: Some typos: Throughout the paper (3 times), please replace data argumentation with data augmentation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "\u201cThe analyses uses base setting\u201d -> \u201cThe analysis uses the base setting\u201d or \u201cThe analyses use the base setting\u201d, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Minor: You can keep only one significant digit for GLUE/SQuAD results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Would make tables easier to read and I am not convinced a difference of <0.1 is significant on those tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 74, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "In terms of originality, COCO-LM is built on ELECTRA, thus it lacks a novelty aspect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "ELECTRA does not have a sequence-level objective, COCO-LM uses a contrasting objective (SCL) for sequences thus it performs better than ELECTRA on sequence level tasks (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "GLUE).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As can be seen in table 2, SCL + RTD improves the average score on GLUE significantly compared to RTD (i.e., ELECTRA).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Having said that, I think the contribution of the paper is quite marginal, however I think its strong point is the efficiency in training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper is well-presented and contains a comprehensive list of experiments showing the effect of each of the modified components.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What I find missing is that the paper hasn\u2019t addressed the claim about missing language modelling benefit (line 107-113).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although the authors presented word-similarity (table 3, line 292) results, I think that it\u2019s rather a proxy evaluation but is not convincing enough about the benefit of language modeling capabilities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As the prompt-based approach [1,2,3] is gaining popularity recently, I wonder if COCO-LM can be used in this setup where the model is required to generate (i.e., assessing language modeling capability).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This is something MLM such as RoBERTA, BERT are doing quite well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "1. While the paper claim the importance of language modeling capability of pre-trained models, the authors did not conduct experments on generation tasks that are more likely to require a well-performing language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Experiments on word similarity and SquAD in section 5.3 cannot really reflect the capability of language modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors may consider to include tasks like language modeling, machine translation or text sumarization to strenghen this part, as this is one of the main motivations of COCO-LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "2. Analysis of SCL in section 5.2 regarding few-shot abaility looks not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The paper claims that a more regularized representation space by SCL may result in better generalization ability in few-shot scenarios.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, results in Figure 7(c) and (d) do not meet our expectation such that COCO-LM achieves much more improvements with less labels and the improvements will gradually disappear with more labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Besides, the authors may check if COCO-LM brings benefits to sentence retrieval tasks with the learned anisotropy text representations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The performance of Megatron and COCO-LM is close to other approaches, for examples, RoBERTa, ELECTRA, and DeBERTa, which are with similar sizes as COCO-LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "If the author claim that COCO-LM is parameter-efficient, the conclusion is also applicable to the above related works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 74, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "In terms of originality, COCO-LM is built on ELECTRA, thus it lacks a novelty aspect.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "ELECTRA does not have a sequence-level objective, COCO-LM uses a contrasting objective (SCL) for sequences thus it performs better than ELECTRA on sequence level tasks (i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "GLUE).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As can be seen in table 2, SCL + RTD improves the average score on GLUE significantly compared to RTD (i.e., ELECTRA).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Having said that, I think the contribution of the paper is quite marginal, however I think its strong point is the efficiency in training time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper is well-presented and contains a comprehensive list of experiments showing the effect of each of the modified components.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What I find missing is that the paper hasn\u2019t addressed the claim about missing language modelling benefit (line 107-113).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Although the authors presented word-similarity (table 3, line 292) results, I think that it\u2019s rather a proxy evaluation but is not convincing enough about the benefit of language modeling capabilities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As the prompt-based approach [1,2,3] is gaining popularity recently, I wonder if COCO-LM can be used in this setup where the model is required to generate (i.e., assessing language modeling capability).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "This is something MLM such as RoBERTA, BERT are doing quite well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 32, "text": "The biggest missing piece in the paper is the lack of hyperparameter details, because the appendix is not included.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The sections do appear in my pdf\u2019s reader table of content, but not the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "There are also some result concerns, which I detail below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "The appendix is currently missing and with it many of the hyperparameter details required for reproducibility.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Please share at least one run / set of results on the test set for each setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This is necessary to ensure that the difference is not just in hyperparameter overfitting during fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "I understand that the claim \u201cmatches Megatron-3.9Bn, a model 10x bigger on MNLI\u201d is compelling and makes for a great abstract.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "However, I think it is misleading and poor - scholarship.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Indeed, Megatron-3.9Bn is not a great baseline for a 1Bn+ model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "For instance, DeBERTA 1.5B+SIFT achieves 92.0/92.1 on MNLI (see Table 12 in their appendix, though not clear whether this is dev or test), quite a bit higher than Megatron 3.9Bn (and COCO-LM).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Minor: Some typos: Throughout the paper (3 times), please replace data argumentation with data augmentation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "\u201cThe analyses uses base setting\u201d -> \u201cThe analysis uses the base setting\u201d or \u201cThe analyses use the base setting\u201d, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Minor: You can keep only one significant digit for GLUE/SQuAD results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Would make tables easier to read and I am not convinced a difference of <0.1 is significant on those tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 74, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "1. While the paper claim the importance of language modeling capability of pre-trained models, the authors did not conduct experments on generation tasks that are more likely to require a well-performing language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Experiments on word similarity and SquAD in section 5.3 cannot really reflect the capability of language modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The authors may consider to include tasks like language modeling, machine translation or text sumarization to strenghen this part, as this is one of the main motivations of COCO-LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "2. Analysis of SCL in section 5.2 regarding few-shot abaility looks not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The paper claims that a more regularized representation space by SCL may result in better generalization ability in few-shot scenarios.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, results in Figure 7(c) and (d) do not meet our expectation such that COCO-LM achieves much more improvements with less labels and the improvements will gradually disappear with more labels.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Besides, the authors may check if COCO-LM brings benefits to sentence retrieval tasks with the learned anisotropy text representations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The performance of Megatron and COCO-LM is close to other approaches, for examples, RoBERTa, ELECTRA, and DeBERTa, which are with similar sizes as COCO-LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "If the author claim that COCO-LM is parameter-efficient, the conclusion is also applicable to the above related works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 32, "text": "The biggest missing piece in the paper is the lack of hyperparameter details, because the appendix is not included.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The sections do appear in my pdf\u2019s reader table of content, but not the text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "There are also some result concerns, which I detail below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "The appendix is currently missing and with it many of the hyperparameter details required for reproducibility.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "Please share at least one run / set of results on the test set for each setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "This is necessary to ensure that the difference is not just in hyperparameter overfitting during fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "I understand that the claim \u201cmatches Megatron-3.9Bn, a model 10x bigger on MNLI\u201d is compelling and makes for a great abstract.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "However, I think it is misleading and poor - scholarship.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "Indeed, Megatron-3.9Bn is not a great baseline for a 1Bn+ model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "For instance, DeBERTA 1.5B+SIFT achieves 92.0/92.1 on MNLI (see Table 12 in their appendix, though not clear whether this is dev or test), quite a bit higher than Megatron 3.9Bn (and COCO-LM).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Minor: Some typos: Throughout the paper (3 times), please replace data argumentation with data augmentation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "\u201cThe analyses uses base setting\u201d -> \u201cThe analysis uses the base setting\u201d or \u201cThe analyses use the base setting\u201d, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Minor: You can keep only one significant digit for GLUE/SQuAD results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Would make tables easier to read and I am not convinced a difference of <0.1 is significant on those tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 75, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Negatives:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "1.Structural locality inherent in datasets need to be captured by a set of custom locality feature functions which requires prior knowledge of the domain of the datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "2. Marginal improvements in results", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3. No detailed discussion of learned parameters presented in Table 3. a. It appears that w doesn't matter so much for all non l_0 features. So it would be interesting to set w to 1 for these features and learn only b and for l_0 learn w.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In Eqn 4. y should be w_t?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, the improvements due to the proposed method are marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "While the results demonstrate that the method improves upon the existing methods, there are some weaknesses too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "While there are clear strengths, one weakness is that one may need to define structural properties in different types of datasets that one might use.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For instance, it is clear that the model works for source codes and Wikipedia because associated structural information can be mined from the data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It is unclear how does the method generalise across different tasks and datasets, i.e., beyond two datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "While the authors have addressed these limitations towards the end of the paper, the question is will the work be useful only to a small set of audience, or people from different domains can manually or automatically build such prior knowledge and incorporate it in this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The key advantages are clear from the paper, this seems to be the weakness that is hard to defend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "One possible way to improve the argument so that we could obtain Wikidata-type structure for most datasets is to exploit entity detection and linking including automatically learning their relation (vector) information in a completely unsupervised way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors must note that I am simply giving ideas on how to strongly defend this weakness of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "In terms of experiments, these can be further improved by conducting some downstream application tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Can the model be useful for document classification tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Currently, it is very difficult to gauge the usefulness of the model through the limited experiments presented in the paper which mainly revolves around perplexity analysis and table 5 in the appendix has additional token prediction results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 75, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Negatives:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "1.Structural locality inherent in datasets need to be captured by a set of custom locality feature functions which requires prior knowledge of the domain of the datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "2. Marginal improvements in results", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3. No detailed discussion of learned parameters presented in Table 3. a. It appears that w doesn't matter so much for all non l_0 features. So it would be interesting to set w to 1 for these features and learn only b and for l_0 learn w.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In Eqn 4. y should be w_t?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, the improvements due to the proposed method are marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The only weakness that I can identify is that the authors have used one dataset of each domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 75, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Negatives:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "1.Structural locality inherent in datasets need to be captured by a set of custom locality feature functions which requires prior knowledge of the domain of the datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "2. Marginal improvements in results", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3. No detailed discussion of learned parameters presented in Table 3. a. It appears that w doesn't matter so much for all non l_0 features. So it would be interesting to set w to 1 for these features and learn only b and for l_0 learn w.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "In Eqn 4. y should be w_t?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, the improvements due to the proposed method are marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It seems that the structural locality and its value are known and not new.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper's contribution then is adding the structural locality to the non-parametric language models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The way to add structural locality is straightforward.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems to me that this contribution is not significantly novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There is no comparison or discussion of other ways to add structural locality to LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It seems authors tried just a single approach that they present in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Using locality info in authors' experiments leads to minor improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This is not a significant weakness since there might be other tasks where locality will contribute more.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 75, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "While the results demonstrate that the method improves upon the existing methods, there are some weaknesses too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "While there are clear strengths, one weakness is that one may need to define structural properties in different types of datasets that one might use.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For instance, it is clear that the model works for source codes and Wikipedia because associated structural information can be mined from the data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It is unclear how does the method generalise across different tasks and datasets, i.e., beyond two datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "While the authors have addressed these limitations towards the end of the paper, the question is will the work be useful only to a small set of audience, or people from different domains can manually or automatically build such prior knowledge and incorporate it in this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The key advantages are clear from the paper, this seems to be the weakness that is hard to defend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "One possible way to improve the argument so that we could obtain Wikidata-type structure for most datasets is to exploit entity detection and linking including automatically learning their relation (vector) information in a completely unsupervised way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors must note that I am simply giving ideas on how to strongly defend this weakness of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "In terms of experiments, these can be further improved by conducting some downstream application tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Can the model be useful for document classification tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Currently, it is very difficult to gauge the usefulness of the model through the limited experiments presented in the paper which mainly revolves around perplexity analysis and table 5 in the appendix has additional token prediction results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The only weakness that I can identify is that the authors have used one dataset of each domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 75, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "While the results demonstrate that the method improves upon the existing methods, there are some weaknesses too.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "While there are clear strengths, one weakness is that one may need to define structural properties in different types of datasets that one might use.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "For instance, it is clear that the model works for source codes and Wikipedia because associated structural information can be mined from the data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It is unclear how does the method generalise across different tasks and datasets, i.e., beyond two datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "While the authors have addressed these limitations towards the end of the paper, the question is will the work be useful only to a small set of audience, or people from different domains can manually or automatically build such prior knowledge and incorporate it in this model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The key advantages are clear from the paper, this seems to be the weakness that is hard to defend.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "One possible way to improve the argument so that we could obtain Wikidata-type structure for most datasets is to exploit entity detection and linking including automatically learning their relation (vector) information in a completely unsupervised way.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors must note that I am simply giving ideas on how to strongly defend this weakness of the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "In terms of experiments, these can be further improved by conducting some downstream application tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Can the model be useful for document classification tasks?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Currently, it is very difficult to gauge the usefulness of the model through the limited experiments presented in the paper which mainly revolves around perplexity analysis and table 5 in the appendix has additional token prediction results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It seems that the structural locality and its value are known and not new.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper's contribution then is adding the structural locality to the non-parametric language models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The way to add structural locality is straightforward.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems to me that this contribution is not significantly novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There is no comparison or discussion of other ways to add structural locality to LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It seems authors tried just a single approach that they present in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Using locality info in authors' experiments leads to minor improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This is not a significant weakness since there might be other tasks where locality will contribute more.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 75, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The only weakness that I can identify is that the authors have used one dataset of each domain.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It seems that the structural locality and its value are known and not new.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The paper's contribution then is adding the structural locality to the non-parametric language models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The way to add structural locality is straightforward.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems to me that this contribution is not significantly novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There is no comparison or discussion of other ways to add structural locality to LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It seems authors tried just a single approach that they present in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Using locality info in authors' experiments leads to minor improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "This is not a significant weakness since there might be other tasks where locality will contribute more.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 76, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "It will be better if the model can achieve good performnace when \\espilon is less than 5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Usually, \\espilon in a range of 0.1 ~ 5 can provide meaningful private protection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Also, the \\delta is large than it should be.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The value of \\delta on the order of 1/|D_train| are very dangerous according to [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In that level of \\delta, they permit \u201cpreserving privacy\u201d by publishing the complete records of a small number of database participants.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "(But in the experiments of E2E, the data size is 42000 and the \\delta is 1/50000.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Unsuitable \\epsilon and \\delta can ensure the utility (model performance) but the privacy protection becomes too weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors claim that \"we are the first to fine-tune GPT-2-Large using differential privacy\". Actually, [2] also fine-tune on large GPT-2 with DP-SGD.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "There's some hyper-parameters play an important role for model training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But, tuning hyper-parameters requires additional information about the private information (accessing validation set or testing set), which leads to private leakage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "So, how to count the information leakags of users?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "How to avoid it?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This paper propose a meta-framework that handles several existing fine-tuning algorithms with DP-SGD.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The performance is amazing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "However, the contribution in terms of the technical novelty is not much. [1] The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, 2014. [2] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training.2020.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I do not have any major concerns with the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 76, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "It will be better if the model can achieve good performnace when \\espilon is less than 5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Usually, \\espilon in a range of 0.1 ~ 5 can provide meaningful private protection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Also, the \\delta is large than it should be.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The value of \\delta on the order of 1/|D_train| are very dangerous according to [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In that level of \\delta, they permit \u201cpreserving privacy\u201d by publishing the complete records of a small number of database participants.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "(But in the experiments of E2E, the data size is 42000 and the \\delta is 1/50000.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Unsuitable \\epsilon and \\delta can ensure the utility (model performance) but the privacy protection becomes too weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors claim that \"we are the first to fine-tune GPT-2-Large using differential privacy\". Actually, [2] also fine-tune on large GPT-2 with DP-SGD.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "There's some hyper-parameters play an important role for model training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But, tuning hyper-parameters requires additional information about the private information (accessing validation set or testing set), which leads to private leakage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "So, how to count the information leakags of users?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "How to avoid it?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This paper propose a meta-framework that handles several existing fine-tuning algorithms with DP-SGD.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The performance is amazing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "However, the contribution in terms of the technical novelty is not much. [1] The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, 2014. [2] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training.2020.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One major question I have is novelty.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The idea to fine-tune large LMs on private datasets is not new [1]. So is the idea to hold most of the pretrained weights constant and only update a small subset of the parameters (e.g., [3]). Thus, this paper is a combination of existing state-of-the-art techniques on several fronts including LoRA [3] for dimension reduction and FFT based method (4) for privacy accounting. While this is solid engineering work and demonstrates the feasibility of integrating DP into NLP, I am not sure if it has enough novelty to be published as a separate research paper. References: [1] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training [2] Parameter-efficient transfer learning for nlp. [3] LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models [4] Numerical composition of differential privacy", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}]}, {"paper_idx": 76, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 17, "text": "It will be better if the model can achieve good performnace when \\espilon is less than 5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Usually, \\espilon in a range of 0.1 ~ 5 can provide meaningful private protection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Also, the \\delta is large than it should be.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The value of \\delta on the order of 1/|D_train| are very dangerous according to [2].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "In that level of \\delta, they permit \u201cpreserving privacy\u201d by publishing the complete records of a small number of database participants.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "(But in the experiments of E2E, the data size is 42000 and the \\delta is 1/50000.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Unsuitable \\epsilon and \\delta can ensure the utility (model performance) but the privacy protection becomes too weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The authors claim that \"we are the first to fine-tune GPT-2-Large using differential privacy\". Actually, [2] also fine-tune on large GPT-2 with DP-SGD.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 25, "text": "There's some hyper-parameters play an important role for model training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "But, tuning hyper-parameters requires additional information about the private information (accessing validation set or testing set), which leads to private leakage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "So, how to count the information leakags of users?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "How to avoid it?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This paper propose a meta-framework that handles several existing fine-tuning algorithms with DP-SGD.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The performance is amazing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "However, the contribution in terms of the technical novelty is not much. [1] The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, 2014. [2] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training.2020.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}], "items2": [{"idx": 19, "text": "The comparison for RGP is also a bit unfair because it was originally proposed for training with privacy from scratch.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Is it likely that training large models from scratch with DP could be better with the parameter-efficient frameworks as well?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 76, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I do not have any major concerns with the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One major question I have is novelty.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The idea to fine-tune large LMs on private datasets is not new [1]. So is the idea to hold most of the pretrained weights constant and only update a small subset of the parameters (e.g., [3]). Thus, this paper is a combination of existing state-of-the-art techniques on several fronts including LoRA [3] for dimension reduction and FFT based method (4) for privacy accounting. While this is solid engineering work and demonstrates the feasibility of integrating DP into NLP, I am not sure if it has enough novelty to be published as a separate research paper. References: [1] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training [2] Parameter-efficient transfer learning for nlp. [3] LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models [4] Numerical composition of differential privacy", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}]}, {"paper_idx": 76, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I do not have any major concerns with the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 19, "text": "The comparison for RGP is also a bit unfair because it was originally proposed for training with privacy from scratch.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Is it likely that training large models from scratch with DP could be better with the parameter-efficient frameworks as well?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 76, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "One major question I have is novelty.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The idea to fine-tune large LMs on private datasets is not new [1]. So is the idea to hold most of the pretrained weights constant and only update a small subset of the parameters (e.g., [3]). Thus, this paper is a combination of existing state-of-the-art techniques on several fronts including LoRA [3] for dimension reduction and FFT based method (4) for privacy accounting. While this is solid engineering work and demonstrates the feasibility of integrating DP into NLP, I am not sure if it has enough novelty to be published as a separate research paper. References: [1] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training [2] Parameter-efficient transfer learning for nlp. [3] LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models [4] Numerical composition of differential privacy", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}], "items2": [{"idx": 19, "text": "The comparison for RGP is also a bit unfair because it was originally proposed for training with privacy from scratch.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Is it likely that training large models from scratch with DP could be better with the parameter-efficient frameworks as well?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 77, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Lack of clarity at many places:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "a) It's clearer to demonstrate how you reformulate the input and output for the 3 tasks, as they seem very different compared to previous methods", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "b) It's better to add some description about the baseline systems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "c) I'm especially interested in the decoding details on end-to-end entity linking, it's better to use a formular to demonstrate how you compute the log-probabilities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "One limitation of this paper is that they required the vocabulary of the entities to be the ones that have a Wikipedia page.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "And that their model relied on copying the surface form of entities (as suggested in the paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "From the experiments, the \"copying\" approach worked very well on Wikipedia entities, that are often common entities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "How can you improve the model to link rare entities not in the Wikipedia pages?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Another concern is the efficiency at inference time.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Compared to the models with a large entity memory whose retrieval is performed with Maximum inner product search, how is the efficiency of your decoding strategy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 77, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Lack of clarity at many places:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "a) It's clearer to demonstrate how you reformulate the input and output for the 3 tasks, as they seem very different compared to previous methods", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "b) It's better to add some description about the baseline systems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "c) I'm especially interested in the decoding details on end-to-end entity linking, it's better to use a formular to demonstrate how you compute the log-probabilities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weak points:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "There is no discussion or analysis on the performance of this new model on \u201ctail entities\u201d (entities that have few examples in the training set).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "A believe such a discussion would be interesting for two reasons:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(1) if one wishes to use this type of model for much larger entity vocabularies, it is likely that a larger fraction of entities will have low number of examples, and", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "(2) one effect of contrastive learning (e.g., negative sampling) has on systems with explicit entity representations is some implicit training of all entities, which is lacking in the described autoregressive proposal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Finally on this topic of tail entities: the \u201cIDs\u201d experiment, shown in Table 5, indicates that when entity mention and decode target are not related, performance suffers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Hard to say without some analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "While the constrained decoding is really interesting, it was not clear from the paper how crucial this was for good performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is this something absolutely critical for performance overall, or does it provide a modest performance improvement?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "While it may be clear to the authors, it would be highly informative to explicitly describe the performance impact on unconstrained beam decoding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 77, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Lack of clarity at many places:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "a) It's clearer to demonstrate how you reformulate the input and output for the 3 tasks, as they seem very different compared to previous methods", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "b) It's better to add some description about the baseline systems", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "c) I'm especially interested in the decoding details on end-to-end entity linking, it's better to use a formular to demonstrate how you compute the log-probabilities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I have one big concern with the current format of the presentation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "In the current draft, It is not very clear whether the strong gain is coming from large scale pretr aining or the proposed method itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "To this end, the ablations shown in Table 7 and Table 8 should be reported in the main paper, with clear explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As we all know, the model architecture (which is the focus of this paper) cannot be properly evaluated when the training set up is different (i.e., how much pretraining has been done, on what dataset?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Could you elaborate on this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In appendix, there's only result tables without in-line explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The experiments on cold start, as well as table 5 which shows performances on entities divided by name match is pretty cool!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If space allows, adding some more analysis on what types of entities do this model do better compared to other methods would be interesting (would lengthier names easier or harder?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "would it do better on popular entities or more long tail ones?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "are these systems complementary to existing methods or mostly succeed and fail on the same set of examples?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 77, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "One limitation of this paper is that they required the vocabulary of the entities to be the ones that have a Wikipedia page.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "And that their model relied on copying the surface form of entities (as suggested in the paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "From the experiments, the \"copying\" approach worked very well on Wikipedia entities, that are often common entities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "How can you improve the model to link rare entities not in the Wikipedia pages?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Another concern is the efficiency at inference time.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Compared to the models with a large entity memory whose retrieval is performed with Maximum inner product search, how is the efficiency of your decoding strategy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weak points:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "There is no discussion or analysis on the performance of this new model on \u201ctail entities\u201d (entities that have few examples in the training set).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "A believe such a discussion would be interesting for two reasons:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(1) if one wishes to use this type of model for much larger entity vocabularies, it is likely that a larger fraction of entities will have low number of examples, and", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "(2) one effect of contrastive learning (e.g., negative sampling) has on systems with explicit entity representations is some implicit training of all entities, which is lacking in the described autoregressive proposal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Finally on this topic of tail entities: the \u201cIDs\u201d experiment, shown in Table 5, indicates that when entity mention and decode target are not related, performance suffers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Hard to say without some analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "While the constrained decoding is really interesting, it was not clear from the paper how crucial this was for good performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is this something absolutely critical for performance overall, or does it provide a modest performance improvement?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "While it may be clear to the authors, it would be highly informative to explicitly describe the performance impact on unconstrained beam decoding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 77, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "One limitation of this paper is that they required the vocabulary of the entities to be the ones that have a Wikipedia page.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 8, "text": "And that their model relied on copying the surface form of entities (as suggested in the paper).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "From the experiments, the \"copying\" approach worked very well on Wikipedia entities, that are often common entities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "How can you improve the model to link rare entities not in the Wikipedia pages?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Another concern is the efficiency at inference time.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Compared to the models with a large entity memory whose retrieval is performed with Maximum inner product search, how is the efficiency of your decoding strategy?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I have one big concern with the 