s that have few examples in the training set).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "A believe such a discussion would be interesting for two reasons:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(1) if one wishes to use this type of model for much larger entity vocabularies, it is likely that a larger fraction of entities will have low number of examples, and", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "(2) one effect of contrastive learning (e.g., negative sampling) has on systems with explicit entity representations is some implicit training of all entities, which is lacking in the described autoregressive proposal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Finally on this topic of tail entities: the \u201cIDs\u201d experiment, shown in Table 5, indicates that when entity mention and decode target are not related, performance suffers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Similarly, for \u201ctail entities\u201d that are ambiguous with another \u201cpopular entity\u201d, the model may be biased with the popular entity (results from \u201cCold Start\u201d may support this hypothesis).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Hard to say without some analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "While the constrained decoding is really interesting, it was not clear from the paper how crucial this was for good performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Is this something absolutely critical for performance overall, or does it provide a modest performance improvement?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "While it may be clear to the authors, it would be highly informative to explicitly describe the performance impact on unconstrained beam decoding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "I have one big concern with the current format of the presentation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "In the current draft, It is not very clear whether the strong gain is coming from large scale pretr aining or the proposed method itself.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "To this end, the ablations shown in Table 7 and Table 8 should be reported in the main paper, with clear explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "As we all know, the model architecture (which is the focus of this paper) cannot be properly evaluated when the training set up is different (i.e., how much pretraining has been done, on what dataset?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Could you elaborate on this?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In appendix, there's only result tables without in-line explanations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The experiments on cold start, as well as table 5 which shows performances on entities divided by name match is pretty cool!", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If space allows, adding some more analysis on what types of entities do this model do better compared to other methods would be interesting (would lengthier names easier or harder?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "would it do better on popular entities or more long tail ones?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "are these systems complementary to existing methods or mostly succeed and fail on the same set of examples?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 78, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The compared baseline is weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "However, the experiment part is relatively weak and cannot prove the empirical contribution of this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 78, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The experiment results are relatively weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "the main results are only shown for two synthetic tasks, math and ANLI, and are only compared to two relatively weak baselines, AutoPrompt and suffix decoding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Why there is no human-written baselines?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In addition, there are more recent methods that try to find better discrete prompts like:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Overall the problem studies is interesting and the method is somewhat novel, but the experimental results are fairly weak and the interpretability of the generated prompts are not well supported.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 78, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The compared baseline is weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "However, the experiment part is relatively weak and cannot prove the empirical contribution of this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The experiment results are relatively weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "the main results are only shown for two synthetic tasks, math and ANLI, and are only compared to two relatively weak baselines, AutoPrompt and suffix decoding.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Why there is no human-written baselines?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "In addition, there are more recent methods that try to find better discrete prompts like:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Overall the problem studies is interesting and the method is somewhat novel, but the experimental results are fairly weak and the interpretability of the generated prompts are not well supported.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 79, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The authors did not provide any explanation/intuition/evidence why such a simple data augmentation technique works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The main problem with this approach is that the performance suffers significantly with CodeNet dataset. The reason behind similarity-based data augmentation will work is because for implementing similar functionalities often similar variable names are used. However, such an assumption may not be true for CodNet where developers did independent implementations. Thus, the dropping of performance for CodeNet is not surprising. However, this raises questions about the usability of this technique.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "However, the results show the approach suffered for independently developed code (CodeNet), which is the most realistic case.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "My main doubt is the results presented in \"Section 5.3 RQ3: TRANSLATING BETWEEN A WIDER SET OF PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The authors report CA@5 scores here but in the other place, BLEU/CodeBLEU/EM is used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "So, what is the intuition that uses different metrics?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "BLEU can also handle multi-reference evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be nice if the authors could add these results in the author response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The research is somewhat superficial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors only show the models can tolerate certain noises but do not propose any simple heuristics to alleviate the impact of noises.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, penalizing the noises (i.e., the targets not belonging to the source) during model training (fine-tuning). [1] might inspire the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 79, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The authors did not provide any explanation/intuition/evidence why such a simple data augmentation technique works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The main problem with this approach is that the performance suffers significantly with CodeNet dataset. The reason behind similarity-based data augmentation will work is because for implementing similar functionalities often similar variable names are used. However, such an assumption may not be true for CodNet where developers did independent implementations. Thus, the dropping of performance for CodeNet is not surprising. However, this raises questions about the usability of this technique.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "However, the results show the approach suffered for independently developed code (CodeNet), which is the most realistic case.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Although I recognized that the main contribution of this paper is not proposing a sophisticated method to align samples, I thought the paper need more attention to design efficient strategy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The entire alignment algorithm takes O(D*f(D')) where f(D') is the complexity of GetSimilarDocuments().", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Unless any assumption this takes O(DD') meaning that it tends to be infeasible by increasing the data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Also, the algorithm seems a simple greedy method and may be heavily affected by the sampling order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I recognized that Figure 1 shows every range of noise ratio, except around 100%, has certain gradient against the accuracy, and got the opposite conclusion \"the less noise the better\" against that the paper concluded \"we expect a certain amount of noise, we can expect the models to perform reasonably well.\"", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The root cause of this is that the paper did not provide a reasonable criterion about acceptance of this experiment first.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As far as I saw the Figure 2 and 3, the accuracy is heavily reflected by the amount data available during training and some normalization should be needed to compare these metrics each other without biases. Figure 3 also show some remarkable characteristics in translating only into C++, but the paper did not mention about this point.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The motivation of the paper includes \"modernizing legacy applications,\" but the experiments does not reflect this perspective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, the first section repeatedly referred to the COBOL applications, but we maybe not be able to reflect the experiments into this application due to large discrepancy between languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The alignment algorithm need to be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "This is not critical: this is still acceptable for the first observation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Conclusion sounds sometimes not reasonable according to experiments. Some results are hard to discuss due to unnormalized data.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 79, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The authors did not provide any explanation/intuition/evidence why such a simple data augmentation technique works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The main problem with this approach is that the performance suffers significantly with CodeNet dataset. The reason behind similarity-based data augmentation will work is because for implementing similar functionalities often similar variable names are used. However, such an assumption may not be true for CodNet where developers did independent implementations. Thus, the dropping of performance for CodeNet is not surprising. However, this raises questions about the usability of this technique.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The contrast between performance with noise is useful, but again that shows how sensitive this technique is with noise and for some legacy code (e.g., COBOL --- a language authors use to motivate the study) the amount of noise might be high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "However, the results show the approach suffered for independently developed code (CodeNet), which is the most realistic case.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The approach is also susceptible to noise.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "In its current form, the problem statement and approach seem very trivial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "First, the data sets seem rather unsophisticated and it is not clear what the pathway is from this method to say mining parallel corpora from github or some large-scale code repository websites.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "In its current form, it seems like this is preliminary work and with some more sophistication (in method + data sets) it is going to be a good future task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "I suggest that this be submitted to an appropriate workshop and a future attempt be made to submit this to ICLR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "In particular, good directions can include in-the-wild performance when tested on something outside of coding-problem data sets (especially since those are so well-organized by language and problem).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 79, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "My main doubt is the results presented in \"Section 5.3 RQ3: TRANSLATING BETWEEN A WIDER SET OF PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The authors report CA@5 scores here but in the other place, BLEU/CodeBLEU/EM is used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "So, what is the intuition that uses different metrics?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "BLEU can also handle multi-reference evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be nice if the authors could add these results in the author response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The research is somewhat superficial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors only show the models can tolerate certain noises but do not propose any simple heuristics to alleviate the impact of noises.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, penalizing the noises (i.e., the targets not belonging to the source) during model training (fine-tuning). [1] might inspire the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Although I recognized that the main contribution of this paper is not proposing a sophisticated method to align samples, I thought the paper need more attention to design efficient strategy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The entire alignment algorithm takes O(D*f(D')) where f(D') is the complexity of GetSimilarDocuments().", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Unless any assumption this takes O(DD') meaning that it tends to be infeasible by increasing the data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Also, the algorithm seems a simple greedy method and may be heavily affected by the sampling order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I recognized that Figure 1 shows every range of noise ratio, except around 100%, has certain gradient against the accuracy, and got the opposite conclusion \"the less noise the better\" against that the paper concluded \"we expect a certain amount of noise, we can expect the models to perform reasonably well.\"", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The root cause of this is that the paper did not provide a reasonable criterion about acceptance of this experiment first.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As far as I saw the Figure 2 and 3, the accuracy is heavily reflected by the amount data available during training and some normalization should be needed to compare these metrics each other without biases. Figure 3 also show some remarkable characteristics in translating only into C++, but the paper did not mention about this point.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The motivation of the paper includes \"modernizing legacy applications,\" but the experiments does not reflect this perspective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, the first section repeatedly referred to the COBOL applications, but we maybe not be able to reflect the experiments into this application due to large discrepancy between languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The alignment algorithm need to be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "This is not critical: this is still acceptable for the first observation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Conclusion sounds sometimes not reasonable according to experiments. Some results are hard to discuss due to unnormalized data.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 79, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "My main doubt is the results presented in \"Section 5.3 RQ3: TRANSLATING BETWEEN A WIDER SET OF PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The authors report CA@5 scores here but in the other place, BLEU/CodeBLEU/EM is used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "So, what is the intuition that uses different metrics?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "BLEU can also handle multi-reference evaluation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be nice if the authors could add these results in the author response.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The research is somewhat superficial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors only show the models can tolerate certain noises but do not propose any simple heuristics to alleviate the impact of noises.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "For example, penalizing the noises (i.e., the targets not belonging to the source) during model training (fine-tuning). [1] might inspire the authors.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "In its current form, the problem statement and approach seem very trivial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "First, the data sets seem rather unsophisticated and it is not clear what the pathway is from this method to say mining parallel corpora from github or some large-scale code repository websites.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "In its current form, it seems like this is preliminary work and with some more sophistication (in method + data sets) it is going to be a good future task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "I suggest that this be submitted to an appropriate workshop and a future attempt be made to submit this to ICLR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "In particular, good directions can include in-the-wild performance when tested on something outside of coding-problem data sets (especially since those are so well-organized by language and problem).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 79, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Although I recognized that the main contribution of this paper is not proposing a sophisticated method to align samples, I thought the paper need more attention to design efficient strategy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The entire alignment algorithm takes O(D*f(D')) where f(D') is the complexity of GetSimilarDocuments().", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Unless any assumption this takes O(DD') meaning that it tends to be infeasible by increasing the data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Also, the algorithm seems a simple greedy method and may be heavily affected by the sampling order.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "According to Table 2 and 3, I observed that the pseudo-match accuracy introduced here does not reasonably reflect the real characteristics needed to be considered in code translation models since the BLEU of several systems compete with each other.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I recognized that Figure 1 shows every range of noise ratio, except around 100%, has certain gradient against the accuracy, and got the opposite conclusion \"the less noise the better\" against that the paper concluded \"we expect a certain amount of noise, we can expect the models to perform reasonably well.\"", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The root cause of this is that the paper did not provide a reasonable criterion about acceptance of this experiment first.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As far as I saw the Figure 2 and 3, the accuracy is heavily reflected by the amount data available during training and some normalization should be needed to compare these metrics each other without biases. Figure 3 also show some remarkable characteristics in translating only into C++, but the paper did not mention about this point.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The motivation of the paper includes \"modernizing legacy applications,\" but the experiments does not reflect this perspective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "For example, the first section repeatedly referred to the COBOL applications, but we maybe not be able to reflect the experiments into this application due to large discrepancy between languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The alignment algorithm need to be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "This is not critical: this is still acceptable for the first observation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Conclusion sounds sometimes not reasonable according to experiments. Some results are hard to discuss due to unnormalized data.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "In its current form, the problem statement and approach seem very trivial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "First, the data sets seem rather unsophisticated and it is not clear what the pathway is from this method to say mining parallel corpora from github or some large-scale code repository websites.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "In its current form, it seems like this is preliminary work and with some more sophistication (in method + data sets) it is going to be a good future task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "I suggest that this be submitted to an appropriate workshop and a future attempt be made to submit this to ICLR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "In particular, good directions can include in-the-wild performance when tested on something outside of coding-problem data sets (especially since those are so well-organized by language and problem).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 80, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "My main concern about the paper is its very limited technical novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It uses Google search for retrieval and a set of static prompts (per dataset) to query the LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It proposes three re-ranking methods (without much motivation).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Using Google search for retrieval and static prompting has been explored before.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "There also is a lot of work that explores re-ranking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although the paper claims that obtaining probabilities/scores from LMs have not been explored, there exist works such as Izacard and Grave 2021, ART (Sachan et al 2022) that use scores from LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The paper also does not explore the easy-to-try extension of prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For example, retrieving similar questions w.r.t a given question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is easy to try and might work better and I would argue is a more practical prompting strategy than the current strategy of designing dataset-specific prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "One interesting result was Gopher 7B + retrieval was better than Gopher 280B without retrieval for NQ and HotpotQA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "However, this result does not transfer to reasoning benchmarks, so I am not sure if this result is widely applicable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Can you explain the product-of-experts and noisy-channel re-ranking in more detail?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would be also good to know the motivation behind applying this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though open-book models perform better than closed-book models the overall results when compared to SoTA performance is quite lacking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "For example (55.9 v/s 38.4 in NQ, 65.2 v/s 30.3 in HotpotQA).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Although it is not required for a paper to achieve state-of-the-art for publication, but I couldn't find any discussion of this wide gap in results and what could be done to fix it.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 38, "text": "I think the paper would benefit a lot if a section is added which discusses how can this gap be possibly closed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Table 1 shows the retrieval performance of Google search and the text in section 5.1 compares that to DPR and MDR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Can you also report the retrieval performance wrt more recent (and better) retrievers such as contriever, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "[Minor] The abbreviation LSLM is first introduced in Section 3 and used in a few places later, but it is unclear what it stands for.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Large Semiparametric Language Models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "[Minor] The second paragraph of Sec A.4 seems to be out of place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Clarity: I think the paper is easy to understand with the exception of an important section (3.3) where the re-ranking is discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "I think the paper could also benefit from a discussion on how to close the performance gap.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The motivation of this work is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The technical contribution is incremental, without significant novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The 3 proposed methods can all be summarized as hyperparameter/model tuning on the retrieval-based LMs, i.e., tuning the search engine, input prompting, and ensemble strategy.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the authors have a lot of discussions on their empirical results, they don't finally deliver impressive insights or conclusions, besides using Google search and prompting performs better.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper has good clarity and easy to understand, but the motivation is unclear and the novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 80, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "My main concern about the paper is its very limited technical novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It uses Google search for retrieval and a set of static prompts (per dataset) to query the LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It proposes three re-ranking methods (without much motivation).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Using Google search for retrieval and static prompting has been explored before.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "There also is a lot of work that explores re-ranking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although the paper claims that obtaining probabilities/scores from LMs have not been explored, there exist works such as Izacard and Grave 2021, ART (Sachan et al 2022) that use scores from LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The paper also does not explore the easy-to-try extension of prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For example, retrieving similar questions w.r.t a given question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is easy to try and might work better and I would argue is a more practical prompting strategy than the current strategy of designing dataset-specific prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "One interesting result was Gopher 7B + retrieval was better than Gopher 280B without retrieval for NQ and HotpotQA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "However, this result does not transfer to reasoning benchmarks, so I am not sure if this result is widely applicable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Can you explain the product-of-experts and noisy-channel re-ranking in more detail?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would be also good to know the motivation behind applying this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though open-book models perform better than closed-book models the overall results when compared to SoTA performance is quite lacking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "For example (55.9 v/s 38.4 in NQ, 65.2 v/s 30.3 in HotpotQA).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Although it is not required for a paper to achieve state-of-the-art for publication, but I couldn't find any discussion of this wide gap in results and what could be done to fix it.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 38, "text": "I think the paper would benefit a lot if a section is added which discusses how can this gap be possibly closed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Table 1 shows the retrieval performance of Google search and the text in section 5.1 compares that to DPR and MDR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Can you also report the retrieval performance wrt more recent (and better) retrievers such as contriever, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "[Minor] The abbreviation LSLM is first introduced in Section 3 and used in a few places later, but it is unclear what it stands for.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Large Semiparametric Language Models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "[Minor] The second paragraph of Sec A.4 seems to be out of place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Clarity: I think the paper is easy to understand with the exception of an important section (3.3) where the re-ranking is discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "I think the paper could also benefit from a discussion on how to close the performance gap.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only tested on the Gopher model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It would make this paper much stronger if it's also tested on other large-scale LMs such as OPT and GPT-3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "More experiments should be done: How does the number of shots k affect the model performance?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Can we improve the results by simply increasing k?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Another baseline is to simply combine multiple paragraphs into one paragraph and pass into the model instead of generate different answers using each paragraph separately. In this case we hope the LM can produce more accurate answers by joint inference over multiple paragraphs.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 80, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "My main concern about the paper is its very limited technical novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It uses Google search for retrieval and a set of static prompts (per dataset) to query the LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It proposes three re-ranking methods (without much motivation).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Using Google search for retrieval and static prompting has been explored before.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "There also is a lot of work that explores re-ranking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Although the paper claims that obtaining probabilities/scores from LMs have not been explored, there exist works such as Izacard and Grave 2021, ART (Sachan et al 2022) that use scores from LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The paper also does not explore the easy-to-try extension of prompting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For example, retrieving similar questions w.r.t a given question.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This is easy to try and might work better and I would argue is a more practical prompting strategy than the current strategy of designing dataset-specific prompts.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "One interesting result was Gopher 7B + retrieval was better than Gopher 280B without retrieval for NQ and HotpotQA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "However, this result does not transfer to reasoning benchmarks, so I am not sure if this result is widely applicable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Can you explain the product-of-experts and noisy-channel re-ranking in more detail?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "It would be also good to know the motivation behind applying this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Even though open-book models perform better than closed-book models the overall results when compared to SoTA performance is quite lacking.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "For example (55.9 v/s 38.4 in NQ, 65.2 v/s 30.3 in HotpotQA).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Although it is not required for a paper to achieve state-of-the-art for publication, but I couldn't find any discussion of this wide gap in results and what could be done to fix it.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 38, "text": "I think the paper would benefit a lot if a section is added which discusses how can this gap be possibly closed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Table 1 shows the retrieval performance of Google search and the text in section 5.1 compares that to DPR and MDR.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Can you also report the retrieval performance wrt more recent (and better) retrievers such as contriever, etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "[Minor] The abbreviation LSLM is first introduced in Section 3 and used in a few places later, but it is unclear what it stands for.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "Large Semiparametric Language Models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "[Minor] The second paragraph of Sec A.4 seems to be out of place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Clarity: I think the paper is easy to understand with the exception of an important section (3.3) where the re-ranking is discussed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "I think the paper could also benefit from a discussion on how to close the performance gap.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear whether the improvement come from the high quality of google\u2019s retrieved contents or from the retrieval-augmented method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Other collection sources for retrieval such as Wikipedia or the training corpus of Gopher 280B also need to be used for evaluation, in addition to Google\u2019s results on internet.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, the authors further need to whether the improvements over CB (in Table mainly come from by keeping or perfectly memorizing the corpus used for pretraining CB, or whether it comes from the use of new but well-matched corpus that is NOT seen when pretraining CB.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The proposed method is applied to the autoregressive transformer using the \u201cin-context\u201d learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But, it is not clear how the method is extended to the encoder-decoder model like T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Evaluation datasets are limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "KILT or other knowledge-intensive datasets need to be further used for evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "What is the advantage comparing to other few-shot learning of retrieval-augmented models, such as: Gautier Izacard, Patrick Lewis, Maria Lomeli, Lucas Hosseini, Fabio Petroni, Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Armand Joulin, Sebastian Riedel, Edouard Grave, Few-shot Learning with Retrieval Augmented Language Modelsm 2022:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "How the proposed method is complementary applied to the above few-shot learning method?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 80, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The motivation of this work is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The technical contribution is incremental, without significant novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The 3 proposed methods can all be summarized as hyperparameter/model tuning on the retrieval-based LMs, i.e., tuning the search engine, input prompting, and ensemble strategy.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the authors have a lot of discussions on their empirical results, they don't finally deliver impressive insights or conclusions, besides using Google search and prompting performs better.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper has good clarity and easy to understand, but the motivation is unclear and the novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only tested on the Gopher model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It would make this paper much stronger if it's also tested on other large-scale LMs such as OPT and GPT-3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "More experiments should be done: How does the number of shots k affect the model performance?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Can we improve the results by simply increasing k?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Another baseline is to simply combine multiple paragraphs into one paragraph and pass into the model instead of generate different answers using each paragraph separately. In this case we hope the LM can produce more accurate answers by joint inference over multiple paragraphs.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 80, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The motivation of this work is unclear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The technical contribution is incremental, without significant novelty.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The 3 proposed methods can all be summarized as hyperparameter/model tuning on the retrieval-based LMs, i.e., tuning the search engine, input prompting, and ensemble strategy.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Although the authors have a lot of discussions on their empirical results, they don't finally deliver impressive insights or conclusions, besides using Google search and prompting performs better.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper has good clarity and easy to understand, but the motivation is unclear and the novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear whether the improvement come from the high quality of google\u2019s retrieved contents or from the retrieval-augmented method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Other collection sources for retrieval such as Wikipedia or the training corpus of Gopher 280B also need to be used for evaluation, in addition to Google\u2019s results on internet.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, the authors further need to whether the improvements over CB (in Table mainly come from by keeping or perfectly memorizing the corpus used for pretraining CB, or whether it comes from the use of new but well-matched corpus that is NOT seen when pretraining CB.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The proposed method is applied to the autoregressive transformer using the \u201cin-context\u201d learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But, it is not clear how the method is extended to the encoder-decoder model like T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Evaluation datasets are limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "KILT or other knowledge-intensive datasets need to be further used for evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "What is the advantage comparing to other few-shot learning of retrieval-augmented models, such as: Gautier Izacard, Patrick Lewis, Maria Lomeli, Lucas Hosseini, Fabio Petroni, Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Armand Joulin, Sebastian Riedel, Edouard Grave, Few-shot Learning with Retrieval Augmented Language Modelsm 2022:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "How the proposed method is complementary applied to the above few-shot learning method?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 80, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The method is only tested on the Gopher model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It would make this paper much stronger if it's also tested on other large-scale LMs such as OPT and GPT-3.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "More experiments should be done: How does the number of shots k affect the model performance?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Can we improve the results by simply increasing k?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Another baseline is to simply combine multiple paragraphs into one paragraph and pass into the model instead of generate different answers using each paragraph separately. In this case we hope the LM can produce more accurate answers by joint inference over multiple paragraphs.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It is not clear whether the improvement come from the high quality of google\u2019s retrieved contents or from the retrieval-augmented method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Other collection sources for retrieval such as Wikipedia or the training corpus of Gopher 280B also need to be used for evaluation, in addition to Google\u2019s results on internet.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "In other words, the authors further need to whether the improvements over CB (in Table mainly come from by keeping or perfectly memorizing the corpus used for pretraining CB, or whether it comes from the use of new but well-matched corpus that is NOT seen when pretraining CB.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The proposed method is applied to the autoregressive transformer using the \u201cin-context\u201d learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But, it is not clear how the method is extended to the encoder-decoder model like T5.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Evaluation datasets are limited.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "KILT or other knowledge-intensive datasets need to be further used for evaluation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "What is the advantage comparing to other few-shot learning of retrieval-augmented models, such as: Gautier Izacard, Patrick Lewis, Maria Lomeli, Lucas Hosseini, Fabio Petroni, Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Armand Joulin, Sebastian Riedel, Edouard Grave, Few-shot Learning with Retrieval Augmented Language Modelsm 2022:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "How the proposed method is complementary applied to the above few-shot learning method?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 81, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The formulation of the CKL problem is overly simplified. It basically only considers a single corpus (D_1) for updating the knowledge of previously learned LMs. A general setup should consider a streaming version of D_1 and make it a sequence of sub-corpus (D_1, D_2, ..., D_T) that arrive at different time steps. Also, the associated tasks in UpdatedLAMA and NewLAMA should reflect such a time series --- that is, the streaming version of the current probing tasks. The current formulation described in Section 3.1 only has a single time step. It's more like an offline learning problem with the forgetting constraints but not an (online) continual learning problem. The proposed setup is thus a bit far from the motivation of studying CKL --- maintaining an ever-changing LM.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The experiments are very limited to the LAMA probing which does not necessarily connect to real downstream applications of these LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It's also hard to justify whether such methods can maintain performance in general NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument about the KILT experiments seems to only focus on testing the retention but not about the new/updated knowledge about D_1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The analysis of the CKL methods is not deep enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The presentation and the writing of the paper can be further improved with more illustrative examples and case studies for readers to qualitatively see the problem setup and the differences between these methods.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This paper is a pilot study of an improtant problem (continual knowledge learning of LMs), but the problem formulation is overly simple and there are still many important yet missing points in both data construction and experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, there are no much insightful and non-trivial findings with deep analysis of existing methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The paper only performs experiments with an encoder-decoder model (T5). The experimental results will be more convincing if more pre-trained language models (such as GPT) are included. For example, we can explore the ability of different PLMs to avoid catastrophic forgetting and to acquire new knowledge while preserving invariant knowledge.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Consisting with the traditional setting of CL, the paper also creates a setting for multiple CKL phases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, only a two-phase setting is considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "More experiments can be explored, such as five-phase or controlling the differences in the distribution of data at different phases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The experimental findings in this paper are somewhat trivial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, there are some concerns about experimental settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 81, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The formulation of the CKL problem is overly simplified. It basically only considers a single corpus (D_1) for updating the knowledge of previously learned LMs. A general setup should consider a streaming version of D_1 and make it a sequence of sub-corpus (D_1, D_2, ..., D_T) that arrive at different time steps. Also, the associated tasks in UpdatedLAMA and NewLAMA should reflect such a time series --- that is, the streaming version of the current probing tasks. The current formulation described in Section 3.1 only has a single time step. It's more like an offline learning problem with the forgetting constraints but not an (online) continual learning problem. The proposed setup is thus a bit far from the motivation of studying CKL --- maintaining an ever-changing LM.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The experiments are very limited to the LAMA probing which does not necessarily connect to real downstream applications of these LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It's also hard to justify whether such methods can maintain performance in general NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument about the KILT experiments seems to only focus on testing the retention but not about the new/updated knowledge about D_1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The analysis of the CKL methods is not deep enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The presentation and the writing of the paper can be further improved with more illustrative examples and case studies for readers to qualitatively see the problem setup and the differences between these methods.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This paper is a pilot study of an improtant problem (continual knowledge learning of LMs), but the problem formulation is overly simple and there are still many important yet missing points in both data construction and experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, there are no much insightful and non-trivial findings with deep analysis of existing methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "In a real-world scenario, how can one know in advance that the new task is truly \"new\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "As mentioned on page 5, it is possible that the FUAR score is very large if \"no gain\" and \"preserve knowledge\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors did not show experiments or analysis in such a setting, where the new task has some \"knowledge overlapping\" with the learned tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "To make this concern more general, I feel the measurement of \"task similarity\" is missing in this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "This work is quite insightful for us to understand more about how LM continuous learning works, although I think more experiments could be beneficial as I mentioned in the weakness section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 81, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "The formulation of the CKL problem is overly simplified. It basically only considers a single corpus (D_1) for updating the knowledge of previously learned LMs. A general setup should consider a streaming version of D_1 and make it a sequence of sub-corpus (D_1, D_2, ..., D_T) that arrive at different time steps. Also, the associated tasks in UpdatedLAMA and NewLAMA should reflect such a time series --- that is, the streaming version of the current probing tasks. The current formulation described in Section 3.1 only has a single time step. It's more like an offline learning problem with the forgetting constraints but not an (online) continual learning problem. The proposed setup is thus a bit far from the motivation of studying CKL --- maintaining an ever-changing LM.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The experiments are very limited to the LAMA probing which does not necessarily connect to real downstream applications of these LMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "It's also hard to justify whether such methods can maintain performance in general NLP tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The argument about the KILT experiments seems to only focus on testing the retention but not about the new/updated knowledge about D_1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The analysis of the CKL methods is not deep enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The presentation and the writing of the paper can be further improved with more illustrative examples and case studies for readers to qualitatively see the problem setup and the differences between these methods.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "This paper is a pilot study of an improtant problem (continual knowledge learning of LMs), but the problem formulation is overly simple and there are still many important yet missing points in both data construction and experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Also, there are no much insightful and non-trivial findings with deep analysis of existing methods.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses: Using T5, the authors showed that parameter-expansion methods have the most robust performance throughout all of the experimental settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, in the experiments with GPT-2 (a decoder-only model) in the Appendix, GPT2-MixReview (a rehearsal method) performs the best.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Although the authors mentioned that \u201cWe leave more exploration of applying CKL methods on decoder-only models such as GPT-2 architecture as future work\u201d, they should still mention this critical discrepancy in the main body of the paper so that the readers are aware of the context of the findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "After all, large language models take various forms and both T5 and GPT-2 are examples of large language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 81, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The paper only performs experiments with an encoder-decoder model (T5). The experimental results will be more convincing if more pre-trained language models (such as GPT) are included. For example, we can explore the ability of different PLMs to avoid catastrophic forgetting and to acquire new knowledge while preserving invariant knowledge.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Consisting with the traditional setting of CL, the paper also creates a setting for multiple CKL phases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, only a two-phase setting is considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "More experiments can be explored, such as five-phase or controlling the differences in the distribution of data at different phases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The experimental findings in this paper are somewhat trivial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, there are some concerns about experimental settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "In a real-world scenario, how can one know in advance that the new task is truly \"new\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "As mentioned on page 5, it is possible that the FUAR score is very large if \"no gain\" and \"preserve knowledge\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors did not show experiments or analysis in such a setting, where the new task has some \"knowledge overlapping\" with the learned tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "To make this concern more general, I feel the measurement of \"task similarity\" is missing in this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "This work is quite insightful for us to understand more about how LM continuous learning works, although I think more experiments could be beneficial as I mentioned in the weakness section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 81, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The paper only performs experiments with an encoder-decoder model (T5). The experimental results will be more convincing if more pre-trained language models (such as GPT) are included. For example, we can explore the ability of different PLMs to avoid catastrophic forgetting and to acquire new knowledge while preserving invariant knowledge.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Consisting with the traditional setting of CL, the paper also creates a setting for multiple CKL phases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, only a two-phase setting is considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "More experiments can be explored, such as five-phase or controlling the differences in the distribution of data at different phases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The experimental findings in this paper are somewhat trivial.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "However, there are some concerns about experimental settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses: Using T5, the authors showed that parameter-expansion methods have the most robust performance throughout all of the experimental settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, in the experiments with GPT-2 (a decoder-only model) in the Appendix, GPT2-MixReview (a rehearsal method) performs the best.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Although the authors mentioned that \u201cWe leave more exploration of applying CKL methods on decoder-only models such as GPT-2 architecture as future work\u201d, they should still mention this critical discrepancy in the main body of the paper so that the readers are aware of the context of the findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "After all, large language models take various forms and both T5 and GPT-2 are examples of large language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 81, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "In a real-world scenario, how can one know in advance that the new task is truly \"new\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "As mentioned on page 5, it is possible that the FUAR score is very large if \"no gain\" and \"preserve knowledge\".", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The authors did not show experiments or analysis in such a setting, where the new task has some \"knowledge overlapping\" with the learned tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "To make this concern more general, I feel the measurement of \"task similarity\" is missing in this work.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "This work is quite insightful for us to understand more about how LM continuous learning works, although I think more experiments could be beneficial as I mentioned in the weakness section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "Weaknesses: Using T5, the authors showed that parameter-expansion methods have the most robust performance throughout all of the experimental settings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However, in the experiments with GPT-2 (a decoder-only model) in the Appendix, GPT2-MixReview (a rehearsal method) performs the best.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Although the authors mentioned that \u201cWe leave more exploration of applying CKL methods on decoder-only models such as GPT-2 architecture as future work\u201d, they should still mention this critical discrepancy in the main body of the paper so that the readers are aware of the context of the findings.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "After all, large language models take various forms and both T5 and GPT-2 are examples of large language models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 82, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The main weakness is that the proposed extension to the baseline is relatively complex and rather heavy-weight in terms of new parameters (introducing ~25% more parameters compared to the baseline according to Table 2), yet only achieves a very marginal relative improvement of 0.2 percent over the baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems likely that this improvement could be achieved through much simpler means, e.g., additional self-attention layers on top of the last pretrained layer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "This is supported by the fact that the paper's analysis of the proposed importance score mechanism doesn't show comprehensible patterns (or the paper doesn't talk about it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I lean slightly towards rejection, because, although the proposed model is reasonable and could have potential, the experiments do not currently demonstrate that potential, and I hence expect it hard for the community to learn from this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I have several concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "All the models are evaluated on the GLUE dataset, experiments on more challenging tasks like QA (e.g. SQuAD 1.1/2.0) should be added.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It would be interesting to visualize the importance scores of each layer for this challenging task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "As illustrated in Figure 2, different downstream task highly depends on different intermediate layers, the authors are suggested to conduct experiments under the multi-task setting, since the HIRE can adaptively select the intermediate layers, the proposed method should have a big advantage for this setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 82, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The main weakness is that the proposed extension to the baseline is relatively complex and rather heavy-weight in terms of new parameters (introducing ~25% more parameters compared to the baseline according to Table 2), yet only achieves a very marginal relative improvement of 0.2 percent over the baseline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "It seems likely that this improvement could be achieved through much simpler means, e.g., additional self-attention layers on top of the last pretrained layer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "This is supported by the fact that the paper's analysis of the proposed importance score mechanism doesn't show comprehensible patterns (or the paper doesn't talk about it).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I lean slightly towards rejection, because, although the proposed model is reasonable and could have potential, the experiments do not currently demonstrate that potential, and I hence expect it hard for the community to learn from this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The authors' main contribution lies in two components: HIRE and fusion network.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, unfortunately, both components are not novel to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The idea of HIRE model is very similar to utilizing ELMo in down-stream tasks, where the hidden representations of each layer are weighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The central part of the fusion network utilizes a concatenation scheme of [A;B;A+B;A*B], which is also a widely used approach, similar to Chen et al. (2017), Seo et al. (2017), Hu et al. (2019), etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Besides the novelty of the proposed components, the experiments are also not well-designed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I assume the proposed method could apply to various pre-trained language models (PLMs), as this is an approach that aims to apply on the fine-tuning stage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "However, the authors only present the results on top of RoBERTa model, which cannot demonstrate that the proposed model can be generally applied to various PLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I would suggest the authors evaluate their approach in a more general way, such as build on top of various PLMs, or apply on PLM in another language.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "These would have helped to better demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Considering the novelty and generalizability of the proposed method, I recommend rejection for this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "[1] Chen et al. Enhanced LSTM for Natural Language Inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "[2] Seo et al. Bidirectional attention flow for machine comprehension.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "[3] Hu et al. Read+ verify: Machine reading comprehension with unanswerable questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "CONs", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The design of the components is not novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The experiments are not well-designed, and the results only show marginal improvements over RoBERTa.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The generality of the proposed method is not well-studied, as the experiments are only performed on top of RoBERTa.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Questions", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unlike Transformer-based models, RNN models (such as Bi-GRU) are not computationally efficient.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "How about your training time compared to the baselines?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "According to Table 2, using HIRE will increase the total parameter from 355 to 437, which will slow down the inference speed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors did not cite ELECTRA (Clark et al., 2020) for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "After comparing the results, the proposed model is not competitive against ELECTRA, while it would cost more training and inference time, which will weaken the impact of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "I strongly recommend the authors also carry out experiments on top of ELECTRA (or ALBERT-xxlarge, etc.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "to see if your approach generalizes well on various PLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Minor Reviews", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I did not go through every detail of the writing and only list a few of the issues here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "page 1, section 1: CoNNL-2003 -> CoNLL-2003", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "page 1, section 1: (Peter et al., 2018b) points out ... -> Peter et al. (2018b) points out ...", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "page 3, section 2.3: Tansformer -> Transformer; yeild -> yield; elementwise -> element-wise;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 82, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "I have several concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "All the models are evaluated on the GLUE dataset, experiments on more challenging tasks like QA (e.g. SQuAD 1.1/2.0) should be added.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "It would be interesting to visualize the importance scores of each layer for this challenging task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "As illustrated in Figure 2, different downstream task highly depends on different intermediate layers, the authors are suggested to conduct experiments under the multi-task setting, since the HIRE can adaptively select the intermediate layers, the proposed method should have a big advantage for this setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "The authors' main contribution lies in two components: HIRE and fusion network.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "However, unfortunately, both components are not novel to me.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The idea of HIRE model is very similar to utilizing ELMo in down-stream tasks, where the hidden representations of each layer are weighted.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The central part of the fusion network utilizes a concatenation scheme of [A;B;A+B;A*B], which is also a widely used approach, similar to Chen et al. (2017), Seo et al. (2017), Hu et al. (2019), etc.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Besides the novelty of the proposed components, the experiments are also not well-designed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I assume the proposed method could apply to various pre-trained language models (PLMs), as this is an approach that aims to apply on the fine-tuning stage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "However, the authors only present the results on top of RoBERTa model, which cannot demonstrate that the proposed model can be generally applied to various PLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I would suggest the authors evaluate their approach in a more general way, such as build on top of various PLMs, or apply on PLM in another language.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "These would have helped to better demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Considering the novelty and generalizability of the proposed method, I recommend rejection for this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "[1] Chen et al. Enhanced LSTM for Natural Language Inference.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "[2] Seo et al. Bidirectional attention flow for machine comprehension.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "[3] Hu et al. Read+ verify: Machine reading comprehension with unanswerable questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "CONs", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The design of the components is not novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The experiments are not well-designed, and the results only show marginal improvements over RoBERTa.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The generality of the proposed method is not well-studied, as the experiments are only performed on top of RoBERTa.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Questions", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Unlike Transformer-based models, RNN models (such as Bi-GRU) are not computationally efficient.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "How about your training time compared to the baselines?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "According to Table 2, using HIRE will increase the total parameter from 355 to 437, which will slow down the inference speed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors did not cite ELECTRA (Clark et al., 2020) for comparison.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "After comparing the results, the proposed model is not competitive against ELECTRA, while it would cost more training and inference time, which will weaken the impact of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "I strongly recommend the authors also carry out experiments on top of ELECTRA (or ALBERT-xxlarge, etc.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "to see if your approach generalizes well on various PLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Minor Reviews", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I did not go through every detail of the writing and only list a few of the issues here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "page 1, section 1: CoNNL-2003 -> CoNLL-2003", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "page 1, section 1: (Peter et al., 2018b) points out ... -> Peter et al. (2018b) points out ...", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "page 3, section 2.3: Tansformer -> Transformer; yeild -> yield; elementwise -> element-wise;", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 83, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Nothing major.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "There are a couple of typos.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "(1) Novelty is limited as MD-based OOD metrics have been widely studied and used, though this paper used it in a different setting (non-classification).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(2) It is not compared with other common OOD detection strategies, such as threshold based, energy based, contrastive based approaches. (3) It\u2019s not clear how the proposed method generalizes to other tasks and model structures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(4) It is unclear why the embeddings from the final layers are chosen.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "(5) It is unclear how the background data is selected, and how well the background Gaussian is expected to generalize.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It seems fitting the background Gaussian is nontrivial and the proposed method is not as light-weight as it claimed to be.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "More importantly, what if we know nothing about the OOD? It seems the background Gaussian would fail to work when there is no good definition of background data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, there is too much important material in the Appendix. Would suggest to improve the paper structure and format.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The proposed approaches are effective. However, MD-based OOD metrics have been extensively studied and used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Reproducibility is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The OOD score fitting and computation are nontrivial.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Though the experiment settings and results are thoroughly explained, it is relatively challenging to reproduce all of them due to lack of necessary implementation details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Novelty is a bit limited, lack of comparison with other common OOD approaches, and the generalization of the proposed approach is questionable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 83, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Nothing major.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "There are a couple of typos.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "There are some statements/claims that are either not well developed in the text (leaving to the reader to guess the intention of the authors when saying something) or that seem overeaching/inconsistent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Please refer to my comments for detailed examples of these.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The paper can be hard to follow at times, and there are a lot of different experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Perhaps the authors would have a perceivingly stronger paper by making it less wide and more deep (i.e., cover less different points but go deeper into the points it does cover).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I am not so positive about its reproducibility, since there are no statements about (a plan for open sourcing) code for reproducing the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "One point I did not like very much is the fact the authors mention two external baselines at the end of their related work section (the ensemble methods proposed for e.g. machine translation), but do not compare their methods against those baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The reson for \"borderline\" is detailed in my comments/questions below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The examples of existing works on OOD detection could be greatly improved (2nd paragraph in the introduction).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Not sure the cow example is the best one, since it is more an example of \"noisy\" rather than \"out-of-distribution\" input.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It is also an example in the vision domain, should you not include an example in the (conditional) language modelling domain?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Page 2, paragraph 1, last sentence (\"In Section 4, we show that while model perplexity is a reasonable choice for [performing selective generation with] in-domain examples, combining with our OOD score works much better when the input distribution is shifted\") -> Please make it clear that you are talking about selective generation, e.g., I added the text in-between brackets to do that as a suggestion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Page 2, section 2: In the first paragraph, it is not clear what is the (practical) relationship between maximum softmax probability (MSP) and perplexity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Please better elaborate your point, the paragraph reads like two more-or-less independent points glued together.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "A method that comes to mind is the mean over the MSP for a sequential output, for instance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Page 3, last paragraph (\"MD0(ztest) := MD(ztest; \u03bcz0 , \u03a3z0 ) is the MD to a background Gaussian N (\u03bcz0 , \u03a3z0 ), fit using a large, broad dataset to approximately represent all domains.\") -> Even though I understand the point, in your introduction you mention that, compared to OOD detection in a standard classification problem, performing OOD detection for CLMs have the added difficulty that CLMs have virtually infinite, unbounded output spaces. This idea of \"approximately\" representing \"all domains\" goes directly against that point. Please defend why this claim/idea makes sense for OOD detection of CLMs. Is the point made in the introduction not so important afterall?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Page 4, 2nd paragraph (\"While we use the ground-truth outputs to fit N (\u03bcw, \u03a3w), we do not have ground-truth in general for the background examples (e.g. C4). We decode outputs from the trained CLMs and use those output embeddings to fit the background output Gaussian, N (\u03bcw\u03b4 , \u03a3w\u03b4 ).\") -> This sentence is not clear. Please rephrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Your model requires input/output for the two tasks, i.e., summarisation and translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "You have C4 for summarisation, and ParaCrawl for translation. Please elaborate that ParaCrawl does not have translation pairs, but paraphrases (right?), and that C4 does not include summaries (to the best of my knowledge).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Your descriptions seem incomplete and assume that the reader will make the leap about important points that should be made clear in your manuscript.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Page 4: \"Since we have implicitly defined two classes (...)\" -> There was no implicit definition, I would say this definition of the two classes was explicitly made.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "You are using AUROC as your metric, but I would like to see the plots rather than the summary metric (i.e., the actual curves), perhaps in the appendix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "As baselines, you are using the perplexity score but not the maximum softmax probability MSP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Could you not use the mean MSP (over the sequence) as a proxy for OOD? Does it consistently underperform compared to using perplexity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Page 6: \"Though RMD and Binary logits OOD scores both perform well at OOD detection, RMD OOD score is better at distinguishing near-OOD from far-OOD.\" -> Why does that matter? That is not what models were trained for.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "If you had trained models (RMD vs. Binary logits) to distinguish between near-OOD and far-OOD, perhaps the outcome would have been different. This split of near- vs. far-OOD is also a bit arbitrary, if you will make it central in your evaluation/discussion (like you do), perhaps it is a good idea to be more principled about it (e.g., using some objective/concrete measure to make one dataset \"more OOD than\" another).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "You hint at unigram overlap as a measure of OOD-ness later on, perhaps you should use the \"degree of OOD-ness\" as the percentage of unigram overlap between the two domains, for example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Page 6: \"We observed that law has the highest unigram overlap rate (48.8%) and the second highest overall overlap with the in-domain data (Table A.7).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "This confirms that law is actually not OOD data and explains why no method can detect it.\" -> If your definition of OOD only depends on unigram overlap, you do not need anything other than computing the unigram overlap between domains to perform OOD detection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "I find this a very misleading comment, since the fact a dataset has high unigram overlap with another does not automatically rule out this dataset being OOD.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "You should include more nuance in these statements.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Page 7: \"Amazon Mechanical Turk workers were asked to evaluate summaries generated by the xsum model on a scale of 1-5 (bad-good) using 100 examples from xsum, cnn dailymail, reddit tifu, and samsum. We collected 3 ratings per example and took the median to reduce inter-rater noise\" -> This is a bad strategy to reduce inter-annotator agreement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "You are basically hiding the (possible) disagreement by only using the median, whereas in order to reduce the disagreement (and not mask it)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "you should instead 1) select high-quality annotators,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "2) make the annotation guidelines clear including concrete examples of how to annotate difficult cases,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "3) include control examples for annotators, etc. Please at least report the variance of the annotators, so the reader knows how noisy they are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Page 8, Section 4.4: \"To evaluate that, we propose using the Quality vs Abstention Curve (QA), analogous to accuracy versus rejection curve used for selective prediction in the classification.\" -> Citation missing. You mention Malinin & Gales (2020); Xiao et al. (2020) later, but it is not clear how exactly they relate to QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "If you are the one proposing this measure, please make it clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Page 8, Section 4.4: \"For translation, the OOD score is better than perplexity when abstention rate \u03b1 > 0.5 and worse than perplexity when \u03b1 < 0.5.\" -> From Figure 5, the cut-off point looks more like 0.6 or 0.65 rather than 0.5. Is that so?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Page 9, Section 5, last paragraph: \"In this work, we focus on developing scores that can be readily derived from the generative model itself, without much increase in computation.\" -> You did not compute how much time it takes to run your proposed models compared to the baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "I find it could be a limitation of your work, especially in light of the existing work using ensembles mentioned in your related work section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "You also did not include any such ensembles as your baselines. Why?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 83, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "(1) Novelty is limited as MD-based OOD metrics have been widely studied and used, though this paper used it in a different setting (non-classification).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "(2) It is not compared with other common OOD detection strategies, such as threshold based, energy based, contrastive based approaches. (3) It\u2019s not clear how the proposed method generalizes to other tasks and model structures.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(4) It is unclear why the embeddings from the final layers are chosen.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "(5) It is unclear how the background data is selected, and how well the background Gaussian is expected to generalize.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It seems fitting the background Gaussian is nontrivial and the proposed method is not as light-weight as it claimed to be.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "More importantly, what if we know nothing about the OOD? It seems the background Gaussian would fail to work when there is no good definition of background data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, there is too much important material in the Appendix. Would suggest to improve the paper structure and format.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Novelty is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The proposed approaches are effective. However, MD-based OOD metrics have been extensively studied and used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Reproducibility is limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "The OOD score fitting and computation are nontrivial.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Though the experiment settings and results are thoroughly explained, it is relatively challenging to reproduce all of them due to lack of necessary implementation details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Novelty is a bit limited, lack of comparison with other common OOD approaches, and the generalization of the proposed approach is questionable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "There are some statements/claims that are either not well developed in the text (leaving to the reader to guess the intention of the authors when saying something) or that seem overeaching/inconsistent.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Please refer to my comments for detailed examples of these.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The paper can be hard to follow at times, and there are a lot of different experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Perhaps the authors would have a perceivingly stronger paper by making it less wide and more deep (i.e., cover less different points but go deeper into the points it does cover).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I am not so positive about its reproducibility, since there are no statements about (a plan for open sourcing) code for reproducing the experiments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "One point I did not like very much is the fact the authors mention two external baselines at the end of their related work section (the ensemble methods proposed for e.g. machine translation), but do not compare their methods against those baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The reson for \"borderline\" is detailed in my comments/questions below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The examples of existing works on OOD detection could be greatly improved (2nd paragraph in the introduction).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Not sure the cow example is the best one, since it is more an example of \"noisy\" rather than \"out-of-distribution\" input.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It is also an example in the vision domain, should you not include an example in the (conditional) language modelling domain?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Page 2, paragraph 1, last sentence (\"In Section 4, we show that while model perplexity is a reasonable choice for [performing selective generation with] in-domain examples, combining with our OOD score works much better when the input distribution is shifted\") -> Please make it clear that you are talking about selective generation, e.g., I added the text in-between brackets to do that as a suggestion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Page 2, section 2: In the first paragraph, it is not clear what is the (practical) relationship between maximum softmax probability (MSP) and perplexity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Please better elaborate your point, the paragraph reads like two more-or-less independent points glued together.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "A method that comes to mind is the mean over the MSP for a sequential output, for instance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Page 3, last paragraph (\"MD0(ztest) := MD(ztest; \u03bcz0 , \u03a3z0 ) is the MD to a background Gaussian N (\u03bcz0 , \u03a3z0 ), fit using a large, broad dataset to approximately represent all domains.\") -> Even though I understand the point, in your introduction you mention that, compared to OOD detection in a standard classification problem, performing OOD detection for CLMs have the added difficulty that CLMs have virtually infinite, unbounded output spaces. This idea of \"approximately\" representing \"all domains\" goes directly against that point. Please defend why this claim/idea makes sense for OOD detection of CLMs. Is the point made in the introduction not so important afterall?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Page 4, 2nd paragraph (\"While we use the ground-truth outputs to fit N (\u03bcw, \u03a3w), we do not have ground-truth in general for the background examples (e.g. C4). We decode outputs from the trained CLMs and use those output embeddings to fit the background output Gaussian, N (\u03bcw\u03b4 , \u03a3w\u03b4 ).\") -> This sentence is not clear. Please rephrase.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Your model requires input/output for the two tasks, i.e., summarisation and translation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "You have C4 for summarisation, and ParaCrawl for translation. Please elaborate that ParaCrawl does not have translation pairs, but paraphrases (right?), and that C4 does not include summaries (to the best of my knowledge).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Your descriptions seem incomplete and assume that the reader will make the leap about important points that should be made clear in your manuscript.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Page 4: \"Since we have implicitly defined two classes (...)\" -> There was no implicit definition, I would say this definition of the two classes was explicitly made.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "You are using AUROC as your metric, but I would like to see the plots rather than the summary metric (i.e., the actual curves), perhaps in the appendix.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "As baselines, you are using the perplexity score but not the maximum softmax probability MSP.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Could you not use the mean MSP (over the sequence) as a proxy for OOD? Does it consistently underperform compared to using perplexity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Page 6: \"Though RMD and Binary logits OOD scores both perform well at OOD detection, RMD OOD score is better at distinguishing near-OOD from far-OOD.\" -> Why does that matter? That is not what models were trained for.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "If you had trained models (RMD vs. Binary logits) to distinguish between near-OOD and far-OOD, perhaps the outcome would have been different. This split of near- vs. far-OOD is also a bit arbitrary, if you will make it central in your evaluation/discussion (like you do), perhaps it is a good idea to be more principled about it (e.g., using some objective/concrete measure to make one dataset \"more OOD than\" another).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "You hint at unigram overlap as a measure of OOD-ness later on, perhaps you should use the \"degree of OOD-ness\" as the percentage of unigram overlap between the two domains, for example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Page 6: \"We observed that law has the highest unigram overlap rate (48.8%) and the second highest overall overlap with the in-domain data (Table A.7).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "This confirms that law is actually not OOD data and explains why no method can detect it.\" -> If your definition of OOD only depends on unigram overlap, you do not need anything other than computing the unigram overlap between domains to perform OOD detection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "I find this a very misleading comment, since the fact a dataset has high unigram overlap with another does not automatically rule out this dataset being OOD.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 43, "text": "You should include more nuance in these statements.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 44, "text": "Page 7: \"Amazon Mechanical Turk workers were asked to evaluate summaries generated by the xsum model on a scale of 1-5 (bad-good) using 100 examples from xsum, cnn dailymail, reddit tifu, and samsum. We collected 3 ratings per example and took the median to reduce inter-rater noise\" -> This is a bad strategy to reduce inter-annotator agreement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "You are basically hiding the (possible) disagreement by only using the median, whereas in order to reduce the disagreement (and not mask it)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 46, "text": "you should instead 1) select high-quality annotators,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 47, "text": "2) make the annotation guidelines clear including concrete examples of how to annotate difficult cases,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 48, "text": "3) include control examples for annotators, etc. Please at least report the variance of the annotators, so the reader knows how noisy they are.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 49, "text": "Page 8, Section 4.4: \"To evaluate that, we propose using the Quality vs Abstention Curve (QA), analogous to accuracy versus rejection curve used for selective prediction in the classification.\" -> Citation missing. You mention Malinin & Gales (2020); Xiao et al. (2020) later, but it is not clear how exactly they relate to QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 50, "text": "If you are the one proposing this measure, please make it clear.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 51, "text": "Page 8, Section 4.4: \"For translation, the OOD score is better than perplexity when abstention rate \u03b1 > 0.5 and worse than perplexity when \u03b1 < 0.5.\" -> From Figure 5, the cut-off point looks more like 0.6 or 0.65 rather than 0.5. Is that so?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 52, "text": "Page 9, Section 5, last paragraph: \"In this work, we focus on developing scores that can be readily derived from the generative model itself, without much increase in computation.\" -> You did not compute how much time it takes to run your proposed models compared to the baselines.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 53, "text": "I find it could be a limitation of your work, especially in light of the existing work using ensembles mentioned in your related work section.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 54, "text": "You also did not include any such ensembles as your baselines. Why?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 84, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses/Comments", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Qualitative analysis - Given that it is a generative paper, I'd like to see examples of how the generated texts look like for different labels. How different are those from the ones present in the training sample?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "On the same line, I'd like to see how well the meta-weight generation can learn the importance of various tokens. For example, authors provide an example that the token \"movie\" might not be relevant to generate texts for positive/negative movie reviews. Does such properties automatically emerge from the meta-learning procedure?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Susceptibility to hyper-parameter tuning - The optimization procedure is performing three interleaved gradient updates at every iteration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I'd imagine that such an optimization procedure will require sophisticated hyper-parameter tuning to obtain stable convergence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I'd like authors to comment on that along with the overall computational cost for this procedure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The whole generator-discriminator paradigm is very similar to many works from the literature which are based on GANs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I'd like the authors to clarify in the paper how their optimization process varies differently from the GAN.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Generalizability of the ensembled classifier - Can the ensembled classifier generalize to other methods which also produce synthetic samples to augment training set? If so, how much gain can we tentatively expect to obtain with those methods?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a bi-level problem is not clear. What is the interpretation behind it? It seems that the bi-level structure is not necessary in this case, since there is no strict hierarchy of the inner and outer objectives ($L_\\text{gen}$ and $L_{disc}$): the order of inner and outer objectives can be simply swapped to achieve the same goal(i.e. using unweighted generation loss in the outer loop and weighted discriminative loss in the inner loop). The bi-level form here is more like to introduce \u201ctechnical contribution\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The two reason for not directly combining $L_\\text{disc}$ and $L_\\text{gen}$ is not well justified ( \"the generation-irrelevant loss  $L_\\text{disc}$ will unavoidably interfere with the language modeling process\u201d and \u201cA hyperparameter needs to be introduced to balance the weights of the two losses \u201d.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I do not see why the bi-level formulation helps solve these issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Specifically, suppose the token weights are learned to minimize the discriminative loss, how can it guarantee such weights will not affect the language modeling process?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In fact, even without direct intervention, the $L_\\text{disc}$ will affect $w$ and therefore affect the optimization of $L_\\text{gen}$.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Besides, it seems that introducing a hyperparameter to balance  $L_\\text{disc}$ and $L_\\text{gen}$ is acceptable, as the author introduces such a hyperparameter in Eqn. 5 to balance the two loss term for finetuning, which is actually in a similar situation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I remain skeptical about the performance gain of the proposed method. Firstly, the authors do not compare with the important baseline that directly uses few shots in the demonstration(as in-context examples) to generate augmentation data, and then use the labeled few shots and synthetic data to fine-tune a PLM classifier.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I find that the cited paper (Meng et al., 2022) uses the same PLM generator and classifier, as well as a much restricted zero-shot setting, which can achieve comparable performance with FewGen in some task. It looks like the performance gain (if any) is quite marginal, given that few-shot should already introduce some performance boost compared with zero-shot.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Besides, I am curious about to which extent more label-discriminative data can improve the moderate-size PLM classifier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Table2 should include all the glue tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Complete results of the important baseline(w. $L_\\text{gen}$) can help justify which margin of improvement can the proposed method achieve.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The paper does not conduct quantitative and qualitative analysis to measure the quality of the generated text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Can the improved generator really generate more label-discriminative text?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Some automatic metrics/human evaluation should be introduced to measure the quality of the generated data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Usually, the bi-level formulations used in previous work present justifications of why such formulation is adopted, such as meta weight net, MAML, etc. However, no theoretical justification or intuitive explanation is given to explain the necessity and superiority of such a formulation in this paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The performance bottleneck of the proposed method is quite obvious, since it is not applicable to large PLMs, and moderate PLMs(e.g. 1.6B Crtl) usually do not have good generation ability. From the experimental details, it is quite concerning that for the experiments using Ctrl as PLM-generator, the batch size can be only set to 2 on A100 GPUs. This brings the usability of this method on larger PLM into question.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Besides, it is well known that meta-learning methods are notoriously hard to tune and can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices. It is hard to tune the framework on a large PLM with a small batch size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In addition, the updating process of the generator looks time-consuming, as the gradient calculation for prefix tuning needs to backward pass all the gradients through the PLM\u2019s parameters in each iteration.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors only conduct experiments on 2-class or 3-class NLU classification tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "It is questionable whether the proposed method is suitable for other tasks(e.g., multi-class classification and QA tasks).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Clarity, Quality, and Novelty: The paper is well-written and easy to understand.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a meta-learning framework is not clear and a bit sudden.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}]}, {"paper_idx": 84, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses/Comments", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Qualitative analysis - Given that it is a generative paper, I'd like to see examples of how the generated texts look like for different labels. How different are those from the ones present in the training sample?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "On the same line, I'd like to see how well the meta-weight generation can learn the importance of various tokens. For example, authors provide an example that the token \"movie\" might not be relevant to generate texts for positive/negative movie reviews. Does such properties automatically emerge from the meta-learning procedure?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Susceptibility to hyper-parameter tuning - The optimization procedure is performing three interleaved gradient updates at every iteration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I'd imagine that such an optimization procedure will require sophisticated hyper-parameter tuning to obtain stable convergence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I'd like authors to comment on that along with the overall computational cost for this procedure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The whole generator-discriminator paradigm is very similar to many works from the literature which are based on GANs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I'd like the authors to clarify in the paper how their optimization process varies differently from the GAN.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Generalizability of the ensembled classifier - Can the ensembled classifier generalize to other methods which also produce synthetic samples to augment training set? If so, how much gain can we tentatively expect to obtain with those methods?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "- Could the authors add the ablation where D_train and D_gen is together used for fine-tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I could not find it in the paper and it would be beneficial to add this to truly understand the effectiveness of the proposed fine-tuning strategy (with temporal ensembles) for the classifier tuning stage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In Table. (2), I do find -temporal ensemble, however I am assuming it is a two step fine-tuning - first on D_train and then on D_gen without the regularizer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "- The compared baselines are not comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors should add more baselines where data-augmentation is used for few-shot learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Some of the baseline techniques from https://aclanthology.org/2021.findings-acl.84.pdf can be investigated to strengthen the contribution of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While a comprehensive analysis of all the augmentations is out of scope for the paper, a few strongly performing augmentation techniques can be added as baselines to Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would like to see some quantitative analysis on what proportion of label noise is mitigated with the use of meta-objective in training the generator?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Downstream performance is definitely one indirect way to measure it, but having a quantitative analysis would strengthen the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 84, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 13, "text": "Weaknesses/Comments", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Qualitative analysis - Given that it is a generative paper, I'd like to see examples of how the generated texts look like for different labels. How different are those from the ones present in the training sample?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "On the same line, I'd like to see how well the meta-weight generation can learn the importance of various tokens. For example, authors provide an example that the token \"movie\" might not be relevant to generate texts for positive/negative movie reviews. Does such properties automatically emerge from the meta-learning procedure?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Susceptibility to hyper-parameter tuning - The optimization procedure is performing three interleaved gradient updates at every iteration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I'd imagine that such an optimization procedure will require sophisticated hyper-parameter tuning to obtain stable convergence.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I'd like authors to comment on that along with the overall computational cost for this procedure.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The whole generator-discriminator paradigm is very similar to many works from the literature which are based on GANs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I'd like the authors to clarify in the paper how their optimization process varies differently from the GAN.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Generalizability of the ensembled classifier - Can the ensembled classifier generalize to other methods which also produce synthetic samples to augment training set? If so, how much gain can we tentatively expect to obtain with those methods?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses (and questions):", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "-There is a lack of experiments without the data augmentation done by the autoregressive language model. To have a better understanding of how the data generation part helps, the authors should provide a baseline where the model is fine-tuned without augmentation. This will offer a fair comparison to the models trained w/o augmentation in Table 1, and also can highlight the contribution of the classification-only part of the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "-The paper states that \u201cgeneral idea of meta-learning is to formulate a meta objective to enable automatic learning of hyperparameters\u201d, which is not entirely true, since that can be considered only as a subset of the meta-learning goals.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In general, meta-learning is aiming to learn good representations and accrue meta-knowledge by observing batches of few-shot tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Therefore, it would be good if the authors can elaborate more how their method achieves this, i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "to explain thoroughly the learning strategy, which is only briefly mentioned to be the online optimization strategy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "-The paper uses a learnable \u201cw\u201d as a feedforward net with 100-dimension hidden layer, but it is not explained why this is the only considered option.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, other options such as self-attention layers, do not seem to be considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Since this part is important for learning the token weights in the meta-weighted objective function, the structure of this part can be further explored, for instance by providing more ablation analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "-The paper is lacking more qualitative analysis and discussion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It would be nice to provide more generated samples for given k-shots and for different tasks, and to discuss how they contribute to a better performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "-It is not entirely clear how the shots are defined in the downstream tasks. Are they predefined in the scope of the datasets? This part can be more elaborated.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Also, it would also strengthen the paper if the authors consider the points and concerns raised in the \u201cweakness\u201d part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 84, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a bi-level problem is not clear. What is the interpretation behind it? It seems that the bi-level structure is not necessary in this case, since there is no strict hierarchy of the inner and outer objectives ($L_\\text{gen}$ and $L_{disc}$): the order of inner and outer objectives can be simply swapped to achieve the same goal(i.e. using unweighted generation loss in the outer loop and weighted discriminative loss in the inner loop). The bi-level form here is more like to introduce \u201ctechnical contribution\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The two reason for not directly combining $L_\\text{disc}$ and $L_\\text{gen}$ is not well justified ( \"the generation-irrelevant loss  $L_\\text{disc}$ will unavoidably interfere with the language modeling process\u201d and \u201cA hyperparameter needs to be introduced to balance the weights of the two losses \u201d.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I do not see why the bi-level formulation helps solve these issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Specifically, suppose the token weights are learned to minimize the discriminative loss, how can it guarantee such weights will not affect the language modeling process?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In fact, even without direct intervention, the $L_\\text{disc}$ will affect $w$ and therefore affect the optimization of $L_\\text{gen}$.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Besides, it seems that introducing a hyperparameter to balance  $L_\\text{disc}$ and $L_\\text{gen}$ is acceptable, as the author introduces such a hyperparameter in Eqn. 5 to balance the two loss term for finetuning, which is actually in a similar situation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I remain skeptical about the performance gain of the proposed method. Firstly, the authors do not compare with the important baseline that directly uses few shots in the demonstration(as in-context examples) to generate augmentation data, and then use the labeled few shots and synthetic data to fine-tune a PLM classifier.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I find that the cited paper (Meng et al., 2022) uses the same PLM generator and classifier, as well as a much restricted zero-shot setting, which can achieve comparable performance with FewGen in some task. It looks like the performance gain (if any) is quite marginal, given that few-shot should already introduce some performance boost compared with zero-shot.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Besides, I am curious about to which extent more label-discriminative data can improve the moderate-size PLM classifier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Table2 should include all the glue tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Complete results of the important baseline(w. $L_\\text{gen}$) can help justify which margin of improvement can the proposed method achieve.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The paper does not conduct quantitative and qualitative analysis to measure the quality of the generated text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Can the improved generator really generate more label-discriminative text?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Some automatic metrics/human evaluation should be introduced to measure the quality of the generated data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Usually, the bi-level formulations used in previous work present justifications of why such formulation is adopted, such as meta weight net, MAML, etc. However, no theoretical justification or intuitive explanation is given to explain the necessity and superiority of such a formulation in this paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The performance bottleneck of the proposed method is quite obvious, since it is not applicable to large PLMs, and moderate PLMs(e.g. 1.6B Crtl) usually do not have good generation ability. From the experimental details, it is quite concerning that for the experiments using Ctrl as PLM-generator, the batch size can be only set to 2 on A100 GPUs. This brings the usability of this method on larger PLM into question.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Besides, it is well known that meta-learning methods are notoriously hard to tune and can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices. It is hard to tune the framework on a large PLM with a small batch size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 29, "text": "In addition, the updating process of the generator looks time-consuming, as the gradient calculation for prefix tuning needs to backward pass all the gradients through the PLM\u2019s parameters in each iteration.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors only conduct experiments on 2-class or 3-class NLU classification tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "It is questionable whether the proposed method is suitable for other tasks(e.g., multi-class classification and QA tasks).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Clarity, Quality, and Novelty: The paper is well-written and easy to understand.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a meta-learning framework is not clear and a bit sudden.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "- Could the authors add the ablation where D_train and D_gen is together used for fine-tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I could not find it in the paper and it would be beneficial to add this to truly understand the effectiveness of the proposed fine-tuning strategy (with temporal ensembles) for the classifier tuning stage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In Table. (2), I do find -temporal ensemble, however I am assuming it is a two step fine-tuning - first on D_train and then on D_gen without the regularizer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "- The compared baselines are not comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors should add more baselines where data-augmentation is used for few-shot learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Some of the baseline techniques from https://aclanthology.org/2021.findings-acl.84.pdf can be investigated to strengthen the contribution of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While a comprehensive analysis of all the augmentations is out of scope for the paper, a few strongly performing augmentation techniques can be added as baselines to Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would like to see some quantitative analysis on what proportion of label noise is mitigated with the use of meta-objective in training the generator?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Downstream performance is definitely one indirect way to measure it, but having a quantitative analysis would strengthen the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 84, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a bi-level problem is not clear. What is the interpretation behind it? It seems that the bi-level structure is not necessary in this case, since there is no strict hierarchy of the inner and outer objectives ($L_\\text{gen}$ and $L_{disc}$): the order of inner and outer objectives can be simply swapped to achieve the same goal(i.e. using unweighted generation loss in the outer loop and weighted discriminative loss in the inner loop). The bi-level form here is more like to introduce \u201ctechnical contribution\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The two reason for not directly combining $L_\\text{disc}$ and $L_\\text{gen}$ is not well justified ( \"the generation-irrelevant loss  $L_\\text{disc}$ will unavoidably interfere with the language modeling process\u201d and \u201cA hyperparameter needs to be introduced to balance the weights of the two losses \u201d.)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I do not see why the bi-level formulation helps solve these issues.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Specifically, suppose the token weights are learned to minimize the discriminative loss, how can it guarantee such weights will not affect the language modeling process?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In fact, even without direct intervention, the $L_\\text{disc}$ will affect $w$ and therefore affect the optimization of $L_\\text{gen}$.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Besides, it seems that introducing a hyperparameter to balance  $L_\\text{disc}$ and $L_\\text{gen}$ is acceptable, as the author introduces such a hyperparameter in Eqn. 5 to balance the two loss term for finetuning, which is actually in a similar situation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I remain skeptical about the performance gain of the proposed method. Firstly, the authors do not compare with the important baseline that directly uses few shots in the demonstration(as in-context examples) to generate augmentation data, and then use the labeled few shots and synthetic data to fine-tune a PLM classifier.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I find that the cited paper (Meng et al., 2022) uses the same PLM generator and classifier, as well as a much restricted zero-shot setting, which can achieve comparable performance with FewGen in some task. It looks like the performance gain (if any) is quite marginal, given that few-shot should already introduce some performance boost compared with zero-shot.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Besides, I am curious about to which extent more label-discriminative data can improve the moderate-size PLM classifier.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Table2 should in