or looks time-consuming, as the gradient calculation for prefix tuning needs to backward pass all the gradients through the PLM\u2019s parameters in each iteration.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors only conduct experiments on 2-class or 3-class NLU classification tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "It is questionable whether the proposed method is suitable for other tasks(e.g., multi-class classification and QA tasks).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Clarity, Quality, Novelty And Reproducibility:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Clarity, Quality, and Novelty: The paper is well-written and easy to understand.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "The intuition that formulates label-discriminative text generation into a meta-learning framework is not clear and a bit sudden.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses (and questions):", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "-There is a lack of experiments without the data augmentation done by the autoregressive language model. To have a better understanding of how the data generation part helps, the authors should provide a baseline where the model is fine-tuned without augmentation. This will offer a fair comparison to the models trained w/o augmentation in Table 1, and also can highlight the contribution of the classification-only part of the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "-The paper states that \u201cgeneral idea of meta-learning is to formulate a meta objective to enable automatic learning of hyperparameters\u201d, which is not entirely true, since that can be considered only as a subset of the meta-learning goals.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In general, meta-learning is aiming to learn good representations and accrue meta-knowledge by observing batches of few-shot tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Therefore, it would be good if the authors can elaborate more how their method achieves this, i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "to explain thoroughly the learning strategy, which is only briefly mentioned to be the online optimization strategy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "-The paper uses a learnable \u201cw\u201d as a feedforward net with 100-dimension hidden layer, but it is not explained why this is the only considered option.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, other options such as self-attention layers, do not seem to be considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Since this part is important for learning the token weights in the meta-weighted objective function, the structure of this part can be further explored, for instance by providing more ablation analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "-The paper is lacking more qualitative analysis and discussion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It would be nice to provide more generated samples for given k-shots and for different tasks, and to discuss how they contribute to a better performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "-It is not entirely clear how the shots are defined in the downstream tasks. Are they predefined in the scope of the datasets? This part can be more elaborated.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Also, it would also strengthen the paper if the authors consider the points and concerns raised in the \u201cweakness\u201d part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 84, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "- Could the authors add the ablation where D_train and D_gen is together used for fine-tuning?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I could not find it in the paper and it would be beneficial to add this to truly understand the effectiveness of the proposed fine-tuning strategy (with temporal ensembles) for the classifier tuning stage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "In Table. (2), I do find -temporal ensemble, however I am assuming it is a two step fine-tuning - first on D_train and then on D_gen without the regularizer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "- The compared baselines are not comprehensive.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors should add more baselines where data-augmentation is used for few-shot learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Some of the baseline techniques from https://aclanthology.org/2021.findings-acl.84.pdf can be investigated to strengthen the contribution of the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "While a comprehensive analysis of all the augmentations is out of scope for the paper, a few strongly performing augmentation techniques can be added as baselines to Table 1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I would like to see some quantitative analysis on what proportion of label noise is mitigated with the use of meta-objective in training the generator?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Downstream performance is definitely one indirect way to measure it, but having a quantitative analysis would strengthen the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses (and questions):", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "-There is a lack of experiments without the data augmentation done by the autoregressive language model. To have a better understanding of how the data generation part helps, the authors should provide a baseline where the model is fine-tuned without augmentation. This will offer a fair comparison to the models trained w/o augmentation in Table 1, and also can highlight the contribution of the classification-only part of the method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "-The paper states that \u201cgeneral idea of meta-learning is to formulate a meta objective to enable automatic learning of hyperparameters\u201d, which is not entirely true, since that can be considered only as a subset of the meta-learning goals.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In general, meta-learning is aiming to learn good representations and accrue meta-knowledge by observing batches of few-shot tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Therefore, it would be good if the authors can elaborate more how their method achieves this, i.e.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "to explain thoroughly the learning strategy, which is only briefly mentioned to be the online optimization strategy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "-The paper uses a learnable \u201cw\u201d as a feedforward net with 100-dimension hidden layer, but it is not explained why this is the only considered option.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For instance, other options such as self-attention layers, do not seem to be considered.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Since this part is important for learning the token weights in the meta-weighted objective function, the structure of this part can be further explored, for instance by providing more ablation analysis.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "-The paper is lacking more qualitative analysis and discussion.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It would be nice to provide more generated samples for given k-shots and for different tasks, and to discuss how they contribute to a better performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "-It is not entirely clear how the shots are defined in the downstream tasks. Are they predefined in the scope of the datasets? This part can be more elaborated.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Also, it would also strengthen the paper if the authors consider the points and concerns raised in the \u201cweakness\u201d part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 85, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The author should include more baseline results, in particular the result where the same (initial) model is trained with the same hyperparameters with no non-trivial knowledge sources available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In their language this baseline should have the generalist only.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "They also did not analyze e.g. how retrieval noise/quality affect the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Ideally the author should look deeper into how are the retrieval results helping the model to make predictions that are otherwise difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2, A unified retrieval system that works across different knowledge sources and end-to-end trained with the language model would be more elegant and removes the expert dispatcher and make the method more freely generalizable to expanded knowledge sources without retraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "But this reviewer acknowledge the value of attempting to use out-of-the-box retrieval systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3, The paper claims to have experimental evidence that instance-adaptive selection is superior to task-adaptive selection of the context type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But this claim did not seem to be backed up by numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4, The author should comment in the paper on the degradation on tasks such as OpenBookQA and PIQA when going from the no-context finetuned baseline of table 1 to their flagship model in table 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Some analysis on the win/loss patterns would be valuable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper lacks ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Several important details of the method are missing, making it less clear and the experimental results difficult to reproduce.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Suggested Ablation Studies & Analyses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 85, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The author should include more baseline results, in particular the result where the same (initial) model is trained with the same hyperparameters with no non-trivial knowledge sources available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In their language this baseline should have the generalist only.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "They also did not analyze e.g. how retrieval noise/quality affect the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Ideally the author should look deeper into how are the retrieval results helping the model to make predictions that are otherwise difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2, A unified retrieval system that works across different knowledge sources and end-to-end trained with the language model would be more elegant and removes the expert dispatcher and make the method more freely generalizable to expanded knowledge sources without retraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "But this reviewer acknowledge the value of attempting to use out-of-the-box retrieval systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3, The paper claims to have experimental evidence that instance-adaptive selection is superior to task-adaptive selection of the context type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But this claim did not seem to be backed up by numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4, The author should comment in the paper on the degradation on tasks such as OpenBookQA and PIQA when going from the no-context finetuned baseline of table 1 to their flagship model in table 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Some analysis on the win/loss patterns would be valuable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The weakness is that it does not show any computational comparison compared to prior multitask prompted finetuning approaches.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Initial thought is that the proposed method might require much more computation (fine-tuning stage) since the MoE layer has to be trained to be able to dynamically select which knowledge source to route to AND also train the underlying LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 85, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The author should include more baseline results, in particular the result where the same (initial) model is trained with the same hyperparameters with no non-trivial knowledge sources available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "In their language this baseline should have the generalist only.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "They also did not analyze e.g. how retrieval noise/quality affect the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Ideally the author should look deeper into how are the retrieval results helping the model to make predictions that are otherwise difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2, A unified retrieval system that works across different knowledge sources and end-to-end trained with the language model would be more elegant and removes the expert dispatcher and make the method more freely generalizable to expanded knowledge sources without retraining.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "But this reviewer acknowledge the value of attempting to use out-of-the-box retrieval systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "3, The paper claims to have experimental evidence that instance-adaptive selection is superior to task-adaptive selection of the context type.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "But this claim did not seem to be backed up by numbers.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "4, The author should comment in the paper on the degradation on tasks such as OpenBookQA and PIQA when going from the no-context finetuned baseline of table 1 to their flagship model in table 4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Some analysis on the win/loss patterns would be valuable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness: This paper lacks detailed ablation and analysis of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The performance of retrieving from a mixture of 6 categories (treating a mixture of 6 categories as a single large corpus) is needed to show the effectiveness of MoE architecture for knowledge selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "A naive mixture of 6 categories would not need any knowledge selection step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Please also report the standard deviation across different evaluation prompts for each task in Table 2. Prompt sensitivity is also important in addition to median accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "For each task, what is the occurrence of selection of each knowledge category for Table 2? This might answer the question of \"why does KiC works?\", if there are some patterns of the target task and selected expert.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 85, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper lacks ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Several important details of the method are missing, making it less clear and the experimental results difficult to reproduce.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Suggested Ablation Studies & Analyses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The weakness is that it does not show any computational comparison compared to prior multitask prompted finetuning approaches.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Initial thought is that the proposed method might require much more computation (fine-tuning stage) since the MoE layer has to be trained to be able to dynamically select which knowledge source to route to AND also train the underlying LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 85, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The paper lacks ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Several important details of the method are missing, making it less clear and the experimental results difficult to reproduce.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Suggested Ablation Studies & Analyses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness: This paper lacks detailed ablation and analysis of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The performance of retrieving from a mixture of 6 categories (treating a mixture of 6 categories as a single large corpus) is needed to show the effectiveness of MoE architecture for knowledge selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "A naive mixture of 6 categories would not need any knowledge selection step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Please also report the standard deviation across different evaluation prompts for each task in Table 2. Prompt sensitivity is also important in addition to median accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "For each task, what is the occurrence of selection of each knowledge category for Table 2? This might answer the question of \"why does KiC works?\", if there are some patterns of the target task and selected expert.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 85, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "The weakness is that it does not show any computational comparison compared to prior multitask prompted finetuning approaches.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Initial thought is that the proposed method might require much more computation (fine-tuning stage) since the MoE layer has to be trained to be able to dynamically select which knowledge source to route to AND also train the underlying LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weakness: This paper lacks detailed ablation and analysis of the proposed method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The performance of retrieving from a mixture of 6 categories (treating a mixture of 6 categories as a single large corpus) is needed to show the effectiveness of MoE architecture for knowledge selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "A naive mixture of 6 categories would not need any knowledge selection step.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Please also report the standard deviation across different evaluation prompts for each task in Table 2. Prompt sensitivity is also important in addition to median accuracy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "For each task, what is the occurrence of selection of each knowledge category for Table 2? This might answer the question of \"why does KiC works?\", if there are some patterns of the target task and selected expert.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 86, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "W1 - I'm quite unsure about the set of prototypes. It seems not described in the main contents.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I checked examples of prototypes in the appendix but the 1:1 label distribution (positive vs. negative) seems to me they are human-picked.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "W2 - The main table is not clear, especially on faithfulness.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 6, "text": "This paper seems motivated by a prior NeurIPS 19 work \"This Looks Like That...\" in the sense that the architecture and loss designs are sourced from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Related works have limited coverage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The explanation generation part is vague.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiment section is weak.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Analysis on generated explanation is also weak.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However a couple confusing points still.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "There are many lines of explanation works, such as those use prompt engineering, information bottleneck, and purely generative approaches.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This paper has limited coverage on these topics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Touching different approaches is important here since the way ProtoTrex handle explanation might not easily extend to all other cases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The explanation generation (sec 3.5) needs elaboration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It seems this paper uses prototype embedding to find a training example as it nearest neighbor, and then use this data point as explanation to its prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "This design has certain limitations: a) not context/example dependent; b) this is hardly generation, instead, it is more in line with salience-based explanation works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Tab2 indeed shows some examples with explanation that partially depends on the input example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But no idea how they were generated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Tab 1b is confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I don't understand what each number means.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "This paper very briefly went over some statements without getting into details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I don't see a solid explanation evaluation in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Tab 1c shows rationale performances however these numbers are quite low compared with prior works (e.g. Paranjape's work at EMNLP 20).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "And not sure if rationale performances are based on token or sentence selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Either way, this evaluation has nothing to do with generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "And when it comes to generative explanations, ideally, there should be some human-based evaluation over a subset of testing data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "But no such thing in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 86, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "W1 - I'm quite unsure about the set of prototypes. It seems not described in the main contents.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I checked examples of prototypes in the appendix but the 1:1 label distribution (positive vs. negative) seems to me they are human-picked.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "W2 - The main table is not clear, especially on faithfulness.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The training loss of the model seems to be too complicated. In Eq.1, there are 6 parts for the overall training loss, and each of them associates with a lambda term (I checked the Appendix, but failed to find how you set lambda terms). Each of them sounds reasonable, but unfortunately, there is no specific ablations on how these parts attribute to the final prediction/explanation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How to determine these hyperparameters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "As the model incorporates additional modules (compared to pure classification model), it is questionable how efficient is Proto-Trex (both for parameter size and inference time).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Yelp?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Movie?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Toxicity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 86, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "W1 - I'm quite unsure about the set of prototypes. It seems not described in the main contents.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I checked examples of prototypes in the appendix but the 1:1 label distribution (positive vs. negative) seems to me they are human-picked.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "W2 - The main table is not clear, especially on faithfulness.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Results:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "different models respond differently to the proposed approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For instance, we see large performance gains on BERT but performance drops for SBERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be good to know why this happens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "User interaction results are not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "From Table-3, it seems like the interactions barely help, even though the prototype changes sometimes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Can the authors provide with confidence intervals for all results so we can compare across different settings better?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Prototype quality: prototypes do not look very convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I glanced at the prototypes in the Appendix and they seem to be quite redundant / of low diversity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, from Table-6 a lot of the prototypes either model \u201cbad service and bad food\u201d or \u201cgood service and good food\u201d (P2/P4/P6/P8/P10).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "However yelp also has examples with \u201cbad service, good food\u201d, \u201cgood food, bad service\u201d, examples not related to restaurants at all but the prototypes do not uncover these.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Overall, I think this paper is currently borderline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 86, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "This paper seems motivated by a prior NeurIPS 19 work \"This Looks Like That...\" in the sense that the architecture and loss designs are sourced from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Related works have limited coverage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The explanation generation part is vague.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiment section is weak.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Analysis on generated explanation is also weak.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However a couple confusing points still.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "There are many lines of explanation works, such as those use prompt engineering, information bottleneck, and purely generative approaches.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This paper has limited coverage on these topics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Touching different approaches is important here since the way ProtoTrex handle explanation might not easily extend to all other cases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The explanation generation (sec 3.5) needs elaboration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It seems this paper uses prototype embedding to find a training example as it nearest neighbor, and then use this data point as explanation to its prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "This design has certain limitations: a) not context/example dependent; b) this is hardly generation, instead, it is more in line with salience-based explanation works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Tab2 indeed shows some examples with explanation that partially depends on the input example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But no idea how they were generated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Tab 1b is confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I don't understand what each number means.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "This paper very briefly went over some statements without getting into details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I don't see a solid explanation evaluation in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Tab 1c shows rationale performances however these numbers are quite low compared with prior works (e.g. Paranjape's work at EMNLP 20).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "And not sure if rationale performances are based on token or sentence selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Either way, this evaluation has nothing to do with generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "And when it comes to generative explanations, ideally, there should be some human-based evaluation over a subset of testing data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "But no such thing in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The training loss of the model seems to be too complicated. In Eq.1, there are 6 parts for the overall training loss, and each of them associates with a lambda term (I checked the Appendix, but failed to find how you set lambda terms). Each of them sounds reasonable, but unfortunately, there is no specific ablations on how these parts attribute to the final prediction/explanation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How to determine these hyperparameters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "As the model incorporates additional modules (compared to pure classification model), it is questionable how efficient is Proto-Trex (both for parameter size and inference time).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Yelp?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Movie?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Toxicity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 86, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "This paper seems motivated by a prior NeurIPS 19 work \"This Looks Like That...\" in the sense that the architecture and loss designs are sourced from there.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Related works have limited coverage.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The explanation generation part is vague.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "The experiment section is weak.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Analysis on generated explanation is also weak.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "However a couple confusing points still.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "There are many lines of explanation works, such as those use prompt engineering, information bottleneck, and purely generative approaches.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "This paper has limited coverage on these topics.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Touching different approaches is important here since the way ProtoTrex handle explanation might not easily extend to all other cases.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The explanation generation (sec 3.5) needs elaboration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "It seems this paper uses prototype embedding to find a training example as it nearest neighbor, and then use this data point as explanation to its prediction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "This design has certain limitations: a) not context/example dependent; b) this is hardly generation, instead, it is more in line with salience-based explanation works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Tab2 indeed shows some examples with explanation that partially depends on the input example.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But no idea how they were generated.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Tab 1b is confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I don't understand what each number means.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "This paper very briefly went over some statements without getting into details.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "I don't see a solid explanation evaluation in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "Tab 1c shows rationale performances however these numbers are quite low compared with prior works (e.g. Paranjape's work at EMNLP 20).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 31, "text": "And not sure if rationale performances are based on token or sentence selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Either way, this evaluation has nothing to do with generation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "And when it comes to generative explanations, ideally, there should be some human-based evaluation over a subset of testing data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "But no such thing in this paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Results:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "different models respond differently to the proposed approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For instance, we see large performance gains on BERT but performance drops for SBERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be good to know why this happens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "User interaction results are not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "From Table-3, it seems like the interactions barely help, even though the prototype changes sometimes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Can the authors provide with confidence intervals for all results so we can compare across different settings better?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Prototype quality: prototypes do not look very convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I glanced at the prototypes in the Appendix and they seem to be quite redundant / of low diversity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, from Table-6 a lot of the prototypes either model \u201cbad service and bad food\u201d or \u201cgood service and good food\u201d (P2/P4/P6/P8/P10).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "However yelp also has examples with \u201cbad service, good food\u201d, \u201cgood food, bad service\u201d, examples not related to restaurants at all but the prototypes do not uncover these.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Overall, I think this paper is currently borderline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 86, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The training loss of the model seems to be too complicated. In Eq.1, there are 6 parts for the overall training loss, and each of them associates with a lambda term (I checked the Appendix, but failed to find how you set lambda terms). Each of them sounds reasonable, but unfortunately, there is no specific ablations on how these parts attribute to the final prediction/explanation.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How to determine these hyperparameters?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "As the model incorporates additional modules (compared to pure classification model), it is questionable how efficient is Proto-Trex (both for parameter size and inference time).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It is unclear where are the results from in Table 1b and 1c.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Yelp?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Movie?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Toxicity?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Results:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "different models respond differently to the proposed approach.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "For instance, we see large performance gains on BERT but performance drops for SBERT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It would be good to know why this happens.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "User interaction results are not convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "From Table-3, it seems like the interactions barely help, even though the prototype changes sometimes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Can the authors provide with confidence intervals for all results so we can compare across different settings better?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Prototype quality: prototypes do not look very convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I glanced at the prototypes in the Appendix and they seem to be quite redundant / of low diversity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "For instance, from Table-6 a lot of the prototypes either model \u201cbad service and bad food\u201d or \u201cgood service and good food\u201d (P2/P4/P6/P8/P10).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "However yelp also has examples with \u201cbad service, good food\u201d, \u201cgood food, bad service\u201d, examples not related to restaurants at all but the prototypes do not uncover these.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I think for presentation purposes, it would be good to also compare this with SHAP/LIME/IG.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Overall, I think this paper is currently borderline.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 87, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Cons & suggestions for improvement Items (6-8) refer to the code and reproducibility, and items (9-14) are minor comments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 87, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Cons & suggestions for improvement Items (6-8) refer to the code and reproducibility, and items (9-14) are minor comments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Given the small size of the dataset, some of the results for smaller categories may not be significant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 87, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Cons & suggestions for improvement Items (6-8) refer to the code and reproducibility, and items (9-14) are minor comments.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Furthermore, the current paper does not have enough analysis to understand why the current LM models fail and hence suggest directions for improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 87, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Given the small size of the dataset, some of the results for smaller categories may not be significant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 87, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Furthermore, the current paper does not have enough analysis to understand why the current LM models fail and hence suggest directions for improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 87, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 5, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Given the small size of the dataset, some of the results for smaller categories may not be significant.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Furthermore, the current paper does not have enough analysis to understand why the current LM models fail and hence suggest directions for improvement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 88, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The gains in the downstream tasks are marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It's a big jump from 1+ to 70+.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "However, the gains in the downstream tasks are only marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The paper does not provide a fair comparison by fixing total parameters but ignore the computational cost (FLOPs) or activated parameters. In traditional MoE research, the general goal is to achieve better quality with a fixed computational cost (FLOPs), not with a fixed total parameters. The reviewer understand that this method provides a efficient way saving total parameters, but the reviewer suspects to achieve better quality, this method would also significantly increase the FLOPs compared to traditional top1 or top2 based routing used in switch transformer and GLaM.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "According to Table 1, activated parameters are made fixed around 1B, however, it might not be clear about computational cost in this work's setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper misses fair comparisons with many significant related work including autoregressive sparse MoE, GLaM [1].", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The proposed method is a minor improvement over traditional parameter sharing scheme like suggested in Universal Transformer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper can be reproduced but the results are not valid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For example, the paper can be increasing the number of layers or expert width or number of experts per token compared to GLaM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "All these increase activated parameters, thus inference time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 88, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The gains in the downstream tasks are marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It's a big jump from 1+ to 70+.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "However, the gains in the downstream tasks are only marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "(2).This article used only one MoE model to draw analysis conclusions, which I cannot agree with.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Because different MoE models may have different performance, analysis conclusions need to conduct experiments with at least two representative MoE models when talking about common challenges with MoE models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "(3).I am very suspicious about the expert pool method proposed in this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How to choose the size of the expert pool. I speculate that the amount of experts required by a MoE model may be related to the diversity of the dataset.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Table 1 in BASE layers paper [1] shows similar words usually gathered to the same expert unit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, this article only uses one dataset for pretraining, and does not use multiple datasets to test the required expert pool size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(4).Followed by the third problem, this paper selected the Pile dataset as the pre-training dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, the Pile dataset is full of duplicate documents (see [2] page 2), and this paper does not perform additional de-duplication processing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Because the dataset selected in the article has a lot of repetition and the tokens are not diverse, the size of the expert pool does not need to be large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The conclusion is likely to change when changing to a different (diverse) pre-training dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(6).I also have some questions about the experimental results of table 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, I notice usually a dense model (350M) could get a score of 70.2 on piqa.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This SaMoE with more flops achieves a score 68.9.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, in the analysis part, it only carried out analysis experiments with one MoE model, which is hard to tell if findings applied to all MoE models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "In addition, this paper proposes to have a fixed number of global MoE layers, which is probably not suitable when a pre-training dataset has very diverse tokens.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 36, "text": "It happens that this paper selects the Pile as the pretraining dataset, and Pile is widely considered to contain many repeated sentences. (see [2] page 2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 88, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The paper does not provide a fair comparison by fixing total parameters but ignore the computational cost (FLOPs) or activated parameters. In traditional MoE research, the general goal is to achieve better quality with a fixed computational cost (FLOPs), not with a fixed total parameters. The reviewer understand that this method provides a efficient way saving total parameters, but the reviewer suspects to achieve better quality, this method would also significantly increase the FLOPs compared to traditional top1 or top2 based routing used in switch transformer and GLaM.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "According to Table 1, activated parameters are made fixed around 1B, however, it might not be clear about computational cost in this work's setting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper misses fair comparisons with many significant related work including autoregressive sparse MoE, GLaM [1].", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The proposed method is a minor improvement over traditional parameter sharing scheme like suggested in Universal Transformer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper can be reproduced but the results are not valid.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "For example, the paper can be increasing the number of layers or expert width or number of experts per token compared to GLaM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "All these increase activated parameters, thus inference time.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "(2).This article used only one MoE model to draw analysis conclusions, which I cannot agree with.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Because different MoE models may have different performance, analysis conclusions need to conduct experiments with at least two representative MoE models when talking about common challenges with MoE models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "(3).I am very suspicious about the expert pool method proposed in this article.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "How to choose the size of the expert pool. I speculate that the amount of experts required by a MoE model may be related to the diversity of the dataset.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Table 1 in BASE layers paper [1] shows similar words usually gathered to the same expert unit.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "However, this article only uses one dataset for pretraining, and does not use multiple datasets to test the required expert pool size.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "(4).Followed by the third problem, this paper selected the Pile dataset as the pre-training dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "However, the Pile dataset is full of duplicate documents (see [2] page 2), and this paper does not perform additional de-duplication processing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Because the dataset selected in the article has a lot of repetition and the tokens are not diverse, the size of the expert pool does not need to be large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The conclusion is likely to change when changing to a different (diverse) pre-training dataset.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(6).I also have some questions about the experimental results of table 2.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "However, I notice usually a dense model (350M) could get a score of 70.2 on piqa.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "This SaMoE with more flops achieves a score 68.9.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "However, in the analysis part, it only carried out analysis experiments with one MoE model, which is hard to tell if findings applied to all MoE models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "In addition, this paper proposes to have a fixed number of global MoE layers, which is probably not suitable when a pre-training dataset has very diverse tokens.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 36, "text": "It happens that this paper selects the Pile as the pretraining dataset, and Pile is widely considered to contain many repeated sentences. (see [2] page 2).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 89, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The analysis section on what works and why is a bit thin; Table 5 overall is quite confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I believe answering the following questions may help add more clarity and strength to this part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What does Hits mean?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What's the difference between @1 and @20?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What is Hits@1 (no explanation in text)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "What is (EM) following Hits@1?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I'm guessing that's the subset of examples that the model answers correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If I'm guessing correctly, the definition of Not Recit, hits@20-recit., and hits@20-path follows a binary decision tree, on whether the ground truth answer appears in any recitation or any QA output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is rather difficult to figure this out based on the text in 4.3.4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Maybe a logical tree of some sort can help improve readability here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are there cases where, e.g., the ground-truth answer is not recited but the model still answers the question correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "E.g., a more detailed characterization of Hits@1 would be interesting, unless reciting the gt answer is an empirical necessity \u2013 which by itself is interesting to point out if true.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How are the 1024 examples selected?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "On a more minor note, the names for categories are very not self-explanatory and could be improved for better readability.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There are some missing details that might slightly hurt readability", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Judging from Fig 2-(1), <Recitation N> is generated from \u2013 and directly related to \u2013 <Question N>, with other Q-R pairs as in-context examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Moving on to Fig 2-(2), it seems there are multiple QA pairs (Q0, A0, ...) inserted between <Recitation N> and <Question N>.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Are the questions in these QA pairs the same as those in the QR pairs in the top-left block of Fig 2?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Are they somehow related to <Question N>?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "If not, is there going to be some long-distance memory issues between <Recitation N> and <Question N>?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "In Fig 2-(3), are the multiple paths (containing Recitations-dots-Answers) repeating Fig 2-(2)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "In the multi-hop setting, do the numbered prompts like \"Recitation 1\" and \"Recitation 2\" naturally give rise to generating recitations of different topics, or does one have to train/update the LM to solicit such behaviour?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Since the multiple recited passages are generated in one-pass from the LLM decoding sequentially, ... How to use multiple numbered prompts to generate multiple recitations in one-pass sequentially?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "I'm personally curious about more details on the generated recitations, e.g., in NQ dataset, do the recitations resemble the \"ground truth\" passages in number of words/sentences/paragraphs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "What percentage of recitations contain the correct answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Minor points on motivations", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One motivation mentioned for recitation is to match the form of causal LM pre-training objective (with the decimal of \u03c0 example).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Personally, I tend to believe that recitation helps by making certain knowledge more explicit (value of \u03c0), and when primed on this explicit piece of information (in the form of recitation), the LM is then more likely to arrive at the correct answer (the 10th decimal of \u03c0).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "In other words, I'd argue that with or without recitation, the objective remains the same (i.e., masked or next-word prediction), so this motivation to me feels a bit mis-directed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Human's ability to recite relevant factoid knowledge before answering knowledge-intensive questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Do humans have this ability?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Regardless of the answer, having this ability doesn't necessarily help humans answer knowledge-intensive questions more accurately, which I guess should be the true human-inspired motivation for the proposal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "In any case, citations are perhaps needed to make either claim.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Missing details may well be compensated by open sourcing the code for reproducibility.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 19, "text": "(Weaknesses)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) Major concerns that will be listed below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "(3) Insufficient rationale to support gains and no gains", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 89, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The analysis section on what works and why is a bit thin; Table 5 overall is quite confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I believe answering the following questions may help add more clarity and strength to this part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What does Hits mean?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What's the difference between @1 and @20?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What is Hits@1 (no explanation in text)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "What is (EM) following Hits@1?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I'm guessing that's the subset of examples that the model answers correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If I'm guessing correctly, the definition of Not Recit, hits@20-recit., and hits@20-path follows a binary decision tree, on whether the ground truth answer appears in any recitation or any QA output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is rather difficult to figure this out based on the text in 4.3.4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Maybe a logical tree of some sort can help improve readability here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are there cases where, e.g., the ground-truth answer is not recited but the model still answers the question correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "E.g., a more detailed characterization of Hits@1 would be interesting, unless reciting the gt answer is an empirical necessity \u2013 which by itself is interesting to point out if true.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How are the 1024 examples selected?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "On a more minor note, the names for categories are very not self-explanatory and could be improved for better readability.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There are some missing details that might slightly hurt readability", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Judging from Fig 2-(1), <Recitation N> is generated from \u2013 and directly related to \u2013 <Question N>, with other Q-R pairs as in-context examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Moving on to Fig 2-(2), it seems there are multiple QA pairs (Q0, A0, ...) inserted between <Recitation N> and <Question N>.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Are the questions in these QA pairs the same as those in the QR pairs in the top-left block of Fig 2?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Are they somehow related to <Question N>?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "If not, is there going to be some long-distance memory issues between <Recitation N> and <Question N>?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "In Fig 2-(3), are the multiple paths (containing Recitations-dots-Answers) repeating Fig 2-(2)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "In the multi-hop setting, do the numbered prompts like \"Recitation 1\" and \"Recitation 2\" naturally give rise to generating recitations of different topics, or does one have to train/update the LM to solicit such behaviour?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Since the multiple recited passages are generated in one-pass from the LLM decoding sequentially, ... How to use multiple numbered prompts to generate multiple recitations in one-pass sequentially?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "I'm personally curious about more details on the generated recitations, e.g., in NQ dataset, do the recitations resemble the \"ground truth\" passages in number of words/sentences/paragraphs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "What percentage of recitations contain the correct answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Minor points on motivations", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One motivation mentioned for recitation is to match the form of causal LM pre-training objective (with the decimal of \u03c0 example).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Personally, I tend to believe that recitation helps by making certain knowledge more explicit (value of \u03c0), and when primed on this explicit piece of information (in the form of recitation), the LM is then more likely to arrive at the correct answer (the 10th decimal of \u03c0).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "In other words, I'd argue that with or without recitation, the objective remains the same (i.e., masked or next-word prediction), so this motivation to me feels a bit mis-directed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Human's ability to recite relevant factoid knowledge before answering knowledge-intensive questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Do humans have this ability?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Regardless of the answer, having this ability doesn't necessarily help humans answer knowledge-intensive questions more accurately, which I guess should be the true human-inspired motivation for the proposal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "In any case, citations are perhaps needed to make either claim.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Missing details may well be compensated by open sourcing the code for reproducibility.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Weaknessess", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper would be much stronger with experiments on GPT3, Instruct-GPT3 (davinci-002), and larger language models (larger in-house LMs?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It's not really clear from the paper whether recitation helps with larger scale, which I think is important for the generalizability of the method [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This could work both ways --- I'm suspecting larger LMs will be better at both recitation and directly performing QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I think experiments on InstructGPT [4], T0 [3] or FLAN [2, 7] will be especially interesting, since it's been fine-tuned on instructions / examples / human preferences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "A major advantage of retrieval augmented systems is their applicability on (1) tail distribution information; (2) generalization to information which was not present in the model's training set (like COVID for BERT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think these are important limitations of the proposed method, and (1) is not really discussed (2 is just mentioned in the conclusion).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Are most of the correct recitations cases which were seen often during training?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Overall, the performance of closed-book models in this paper seems to significantly lag behind recent few-shot retrieval-augmented systems [5, 6].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "For instance, ATLAS [5] gets 42% on NQ with 64 examples and a smaller model, while the best number in this paper is 32% (5-10x larger model).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "While I agree that setting up retrieval is technically cumbersome, there are very good retrieval APIs available, which were utilized in [6] without any extra LM fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Note that I do think it's incredible that closed book LMs are doing so well, but practically (from a performance stand-point) it may be better to just retrieve some text from the web rather than ask an LM to generate it with few-shot examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 89, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "The analysis section on what works and why is a bit thin; Table 5 overall is quite confusing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I believe answering the following questions may help add more clarity and strength to this part.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What does Hits mean?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "What's the difference between @1 and @20?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "What is Hits@1 (no explanation in text)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "What is (EM) following Hits@1?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I'm guessing that's the subset of examples that the model answers correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If I'm guessing correctly, the definition of Not Recit, hits@20-recit., and hits@20-path follows a binary decision tree, on whether the ground truth answer appears in any recitation or any QA output.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It is rather difficult to figure this out based on the text in 4.3.4.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Maybe a logical tree of some sort can help improve readability here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are there cases where, e.g., the ground-truth answer is not recited but the model still answers the question correctly?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "E.g., a more detailed characterization of Hits@1 would be interesting, unless reciting the gt answer is an empirical necessity \u2013 which by itself is interesting to point out if true.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "How are the 1024 examples selected?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "On a more minor note, the names for categories are very not self-explanatory and could be improved for better readability.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "There are some missing details that might slightly hurt readability", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Judging from Fig 2-(1), <Recitation N> is generated from \u2013 and directly related to \u2013 <Question N>, with other Q-R pairs as in-context examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Moving on to Fig 2-(2), it seems there are multiple QA pairs (Q0, A0, ...) inserted between <Recitation N> and <Question N>.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Are the questions in these QA pairs the same as those in the QR pairs in the top-left block of Fig 2?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Are they somehow related to <Question N>?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "If not, is there going to be some long-distance memory issues between <Recitation N> and <Question N>?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "In Fig 2-(3), are the multiple paths (containing Recitations-dots-Answers) repeating Fig 2-(2)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "In the multi-hop setting, do the numbered prompts like \"Recitation 1\" and \"Recitation 2\" naturally give rise to generating recitations of different topics, or does one have to train/update the LM to solicit such behaviour?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 32, "text": "Since the multiple recited passages are generated in one-pass from the LLM decoding sequentially, ... How to use multiple numbered prompts to generate multiple recitations in one-pass sequentially?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "I'm personally curious about more details on the generated recitations, e.g., in NQ dataset, do the recitations resemble the \"ground truth\" passages in number of words/sentences/paragraphs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "What percentage of recitations contain the correct answers?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "Minor points on motivations", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "One motivation mentioned for recitation is to match the form of causal LM pre-training objective (with the decimal of \u03c0 example).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Personally, I tend to believe that recitation helps by making certain knowledge more explicit (value of \u03c0), and when primed on this explicit piece of information (in the form of recitation), the LM is then more likely to arrive at the correct answer (the 10th decimal of \u03c0).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "In other words, I'd argue that with or without recitation, the objective remains the same (i.e., masked or next-word prediction), so this motivation to me feels a bit mis-directed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "Human's ability to recite relevant factoid knowledge before answering knowledge-intensive questions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "Do humans have this ability?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Regardless of the answer, having this ability doesn't necessarily help humans answer knowledge-intensive questions more accurately, which I guess should be the true human-inspired motivation for the proposal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 42, "text": "In any case, citations are perhaps needed to make either claim.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 45, "text": "Missing details may well be compensated by open sourcing the code for reproducibility.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "As also mentioned is Limitation, the main drawback is the the updated of the model with new knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Questions/Suggestion:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What is the size of the index + docs (compressed) of BM25?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "is the size larger or smaller of the model parameter?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "if the size of the DB is larger of the model parameter, why not constrain it (e.g., randomly removing articles) and check if the performance in Table 5 changes?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "What is the inference speed of LM-Recitation K-shot with t-path?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "How's compared to BM25?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Does scale matter?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 4 shows the performance difference by sampling more self-consistency paths, but what append if the model samples more responses for the direct method (or chain-of-thoughts)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 89, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 19, "text": "(Weaknesses)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) Major concerns that will be listed below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "(3) Insufficient rationale to support gains and no gains", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Weaknessess", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper would be much stronger with experiments on GPT3, Instruct-GPT3 (davinci-002), and larger language models (larger in-house LMs?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It's not really clear from the paper whether recitation helps with larger scale, which I think is important for the generalizability of the method [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This could work both ways --- I'm suspecting larger LMs will be better at both recitation and directly performing QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I think experiments on InstructGPT [4], T0 [3] or FLAN [2, 7] will be especially interesting, since it's been fine-tuned on instructions / examples / human preferences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "A major advantage of retrieval augmented systems is their applicability on (1) tail distribution information; (2) generalization to information which was not present in the model's training set (like COVID for BERT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think these are important limitations of the proposed method, and (1) is not really discussed (2 is just mentioned in the conclusion).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Are most of the correct recitations cases which were seen often during training?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Overall, the performance of closed-book models in this paper seems to significantly lag behind recent few-shot retrieval-augmented systems [5, 6].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "For instance, ATLAS [5] gets 42% on NQ with 64 examples and a smaller model, while the best number in this paper is 32% (5-10x larger model).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "While I agree that setting up retrieval is technically cumbersome, there are very good retrieval APIs available, which were utilized in [6] without any extra LM fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Note that I do think it's incredible that closed book LMs are doing so well, but practically (from a performance stand-point) it may be better to just retrieve some text from the web rather than ask an LM to generate it with few-shot examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 89, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 19, "text": "(Weaknesses)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "(1) Major concerns that will be listed below.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "(2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "(3) Insufficient rationale to support gains and no gains", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "As also mentioned is Limitation, the main drawback is the the updated of the model with new knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Questions/Suggestion:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What is the size of the index + docs (compressed) of BM25?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "is the size larger or smaller of the model parameter?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "if the size of the DB is larger of the model parameter, why not constrain it (e.g., randomly removing articles) and check if the performance in Table 5 changes?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "What is the inference speed of LM-Recitation K-shot with t-path?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "How's compared to BM25?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Does scale matter?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 4 shows the performance difference by sampling more self-consistency paths, but what append if the model samples more responses for the direct method (or chain-of-thoughts)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 89, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 16, "text": "Weaknessess", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "The paper would be much stronger with experiments on GPT3, Instruct-GPT3 (davinci-002), and larger language models (larger in-house LMs?).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "It's not really clear from the paper whether recitation helps with larger scale, which I think is important for the generalizability of the method [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "This could work both ways --- I'm suspecting larger LMs will be better at both recitation and directly performing QA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I think experiments on InstructGPT [4], T0 [3] or FLAN [2, 7] will be especially interesting, since it's been fine-tuned on instructions / examples / human preferences.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "A major advantage of retrieval augmented systems is their applicability on (1) tail distribution information; (2) generalization to information which was not present in the model's training set (like COVID for BERT).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "I think these are important limitations of the proposed method, and (1) is not really discussed (2 is just mentioned in the conclusion).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Are most of the correct recitations cases which were seen often during training?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Overall, the performance of closed-book models in this paper seems to significantly lag behind recent few-shot retrieval-augmented systems [5, 6].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "For instance, ATLAS [5] gets 42% on NQ with 64 examples and a smaller model, while the best number in this paper is 32% (5-10x larger model).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "While I agree that setting up retrieval is technically cumbersome, there are very good retrieval APIs available, which were utilized in [6] without any extra LM fine-tuning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Note that I do think it's incredible that closed book LMs are doing so well, but practically (from a performance stand-point) it may be better to just retrieve some text from the web rather than ask an LM to generate it with few-shot examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "As also mentioned is Limitation, the main drawback is the the updated of the model with new knowledge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "This is even more evident when compared to simple DB retrieval in Table 5, where BM25 performs better that LM-Recitation 5-shot with 20-path (the best proposed model).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Questions/Suggestion:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "What is the size of the index + docs (compressed) of BM25?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "is the size larger or smaller of the model parameter?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "if the size of the DB is larger of the model parameter, why not constrain it (e.g., randomly removing articles) and check if the performance in Table 5 changes?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "What is the inference speed of LM-Recitation K-shot with t-path?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "How's compared to BM25?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Does scale matter?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 4 shows the performance difference by sampling more self-consistency paths, but what append if the model samples more responses for the direct method (or chain-of-thoughts)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 90, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of examples is not significant, with only 250 examples, compared to GMS8K with 8.5K examples, so the results may not be generalized if more examples are translated and added to the benchmark.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The verification process is less discussed, and not convincing to guarantee the quality of the benchmark.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The approach of translating a problem to English does not test the multilingual reasoning abilities of LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "No variance is presented in the few-shot experiment.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}]}, {"paper_idx": 90, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of examples is not significant, with only 250 examples, compared to GMS8K with 8.5K examples, so the results may not be generalized if more examples are translated and added to the benchmark.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The verification process is less discussed, and not convincing to guarantee the quality of the benchmark.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The approach of translating a problem to English does not test the multilingual reasoning abilities of LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Some experiments and additional clarification can be considered and added (see Questions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 90, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 6, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The number of examples is not significant, with only 250 examples, compared to GMS8K with 8.5K examples, so the results may not be generalized if more examples are translated and added to the benchmark.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The verification process is less discussed, and not convincing to guarantee the quality of the benchmark.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The approach of translating a problem to English does not test the multilingual reasoning abilities of LLMs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "1. The paper does not provide much novelty, insights and inspiration, the technique design simply follows existing works of CoT.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "2. The empirical investigation is not sufficient for a solid benchmarking of the new datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Specifically, I believe several recently-proposed methods like ZeroShotCoT[1] and SelfConsistencyCoT[2] are also necessary and not difficult to implement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "3. The extension of the multilingual version of an existing benchmark seems providing only limited contribution for a ICLR paper to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "And the empirical verification of CoT on more datasets are also not sufficient for another new paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Novelty: The novelty is limited. The technique is merely another verification of existing published paper CoT with only minimum adaptation, where a naive translation of examples provides the best performance.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: The dataset is released and the code is available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Although I assume most people do not have access to GPT3 or the refered 540B LLM, which are the only keys to obtain such SoTA results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But the technique is almost identical to CoT, and the results can be well expected, leaving it only a very incremental contribution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Besides, such empirical verification is also not sufficient as important baselines are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 90, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "No variance is presented in the few-shot experiment.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Some experiments and additional clarification can be considered and added (see Questions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 90, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 5, "text": "Weakness", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 6, "text": "No variance is presented in the few-shot experiment.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "1. The paper does not provide much novelty, insights and inspiration, the technique design simply follows existing works of CoT.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "2. The empirical investigation is not sufficient for a solid benchmarking of the new datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Specifically, I believe several recently-proposed methods like ZeroShotCoT[1] and SelfConsistencyCoT[2] are also necessary and not difficult to implement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "3. The extension of the multilingual version of an existing benchmark seems providing only limited contribution for a ICLR paper to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "And the empirical verification of CoT on more datasets are also not sufficient for another new paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Novelty: The novelty is limited. The technique is merely another verification of existing published paper CoT with only minimum adaptation, where a naive translation of examples provides the best performance.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: The dataset is released and the code is available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Although I assume most people do not have access to GPT3 or the refered 540B LLM, which are the only keys to obtain such SoTA results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But the technique is almost identical to CoT, and the results can be well expected, leaving it only a very incremental contribution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Besides, such empirical verification is also not sufficient as important baselines are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 90, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 3, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 7, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Some experiments and additional clarification can be considered and added (see Questions).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 4, "text": "Strength And Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "1. The paper does not provide much novelty, insights and inspiration, the technique design simply follows existing works of CoT.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "2. The empirical investigation is not sufficient for a solid benchmarking of the new datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Specifically, I believe several recently-proposed methods like ZeroShotCoT[1] and SelfConsistencyCoT[2] are also necessary and not difficult to implement.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "3. The extension of the multilingual version of an existing benchmark seems providing only limited contribution for a ICLR paper to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "And the empirical verification of CoT on more datasets are also not sufficient for another new paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Novelty: The novelty is limited. The technique is merely another verification of existing published paper CoT with only minimum adaptation, where a naive translation of examples provides the best performance.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misinterpret Novelty"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Reproducibility: The dataset is released and the code is available.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Although I assume most people do not have access to GPT3 or the refered 540B LLM, which are the only keys to obtain such SoTA results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "But the technique is almost identical to CoT, and the results can be well expected, leaving it only a very incremental contribution.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Besides, such empirical verification is also not sufficient as important baselines are missing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 91, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There is no ablation study in terms of modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I didn't find clear comparisons of training speed and GPU memory usage between different models. It is valuable for the readers to have a sense of what kind of resource is needed to train or host such models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Is the application restricted to sequence decoding?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Can we finetune the proposed model as BERT on downstream tasks, and how about the performance?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The above question is related to generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Such kind of kNN method seems to suffer from too much memorization instead of generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If the model cannot generalize well, it would not be good at zero- or few-shot learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I agree that language modeling is a meaningful task, but it is more like a testbed for modeling techniques that improve generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Otherwise, the shortcut of memorizing context would hide the real challenge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The code seems unavailable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It is unclear how many engineering efforts are required to reproduce the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper writing ignores many details that are helpful for reproducing the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Even the code would be released, the standalone paper should be informative enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The kNN layer is densely triggered for each position, which seems quite heavy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "How about using a gate to determine whether we need to perform kNN?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Is there any evaluation about the performance of the learned kNN search? For example, we could conduct some human evaluations to see whether the retrieved knowledge makes sense for prediction.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The model is only evaluated on the language modeling task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "More tasks would be helpful to indicate whether the proposed method is general enough in terms of data distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "But it can be improved as mentioned in the above review (e.g., including ablation studies, more tasks, comparisons with previous models, and analysis).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Weak points:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Section 3 could see more formalization and better description on what are the input for searching the kNN, how the model is trained (maybe, an algorithm figure).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Memory sizes are too big for the data selected.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "It is unclear why such big memories are still improving the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Results are very close for each memory size and there are no error measurements (e.g., std dev).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It is hard to conclude anything about memory sizes and their effectiveness.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Hard to replicate: several proposed datasets are downloaded from the web, it will be hard to download the same files.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "No details on the specific hardware and software used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "What do you refer with \"16 attention heads of size 64\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Describe the content of the figures in the caption (e.g., what is the architecture about?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Also, there will be some challenges to reproduce this work with respect to the datasets that should be addressed as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 91, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There is no ablation study in terms of modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I didn't find clear comparisons of training speed and GPU memory usage between different models. It is valuable for the readers to have a sense of what kind of resource is needed to train or host such models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Is the application restricted to sequence decoding?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Can we finetune the proposed model as BERT on downstream tasks, and how about the performance?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The above question is related to generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Such kind of kNN method seems to suffer from too much memorization instead of generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If the model cannot generalize well, it would not be good at zero- or few-shot learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I agree that language modeling is a meaningful task, but it is more like a testbed for modeling techniques that improve generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Otherwise, the shortcut of memorizing context would hide the real challenge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The code seems unavailable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It is unclear how many engineering efforts are required to reproduce the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper writing ignores many details that are helpful for reproducing the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Even the code would be released, the standalone paper should be informative enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The kNN layer is densely triggered for each position, which seems quite heavy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "How about using a gate to determine whether we need to perform kNN?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Is there any evaluation about the performance of the learned kNN search? For example, we could conduct some human evaluations to see whether the retrieved knowledge makes sense for prediction.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The model is only evaluated on the language modeling task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "More tasks would be helpful to indicate whether the proposed method is general enough in terms of data distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "But it can be improved as mentioned in the above review (e.g., including ablation studies, more tasks, comparisons with previous models, and analysis).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "there are some benchmarks that require models to be able to handle long inputs, such as document-level QA datasets and document-level retrieval.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}]}, {"paper_idx": 91, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "There is no ablation study in terms of modeling.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I didn't find clear comparisons of training speed and GPU memory usage between different models. It is valuable for the readers to have a sense of what kind of resource is needed to train or host such models.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Is the application restricted to sequence decoding?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Can we finetune the proposed model as BERT on downstream tasks, and how about the performance?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The above question is related to generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Such kind of kNN method seems to suffer from too much memorization instead of generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "If the model cannot generalize well, it would not be good at zero- or few-shot learning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I agree that language modeling is a meaningful task, but it is more like a testbed for modeling techniques that improve generalization.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Otherwise, the shortcut of memorizing context would hide the real challenge.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The code seems unavailable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "It is unclear how many engineering efforts are required to reproduce the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The paper writing ignores many details that are helpful for reproducing the method.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Even the code would be released, the standalone paper should be informative enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The kNN layer is densely triggered for each position, which seems quite heavy.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "How about using a gate to determine whether we need to perform kNN?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Is there any evaluation about the performance of the learned kNN search? For example, we could conduct some human evaluations to see whether the retrieved knowledge makes sense for prediction.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "The model is only evaluated on the language modeling task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "More tasks would be helpful to indicate whether the proposed method is general enough in terms of data distribution.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "But it can be improved as mentioned in the above review (e.g., including ablation studies, more tasks, comparisons with previous models, and analysis).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 20, "text": "The authors do not compare their model to any previously published model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Throughout the paper the authors only compare their model to baselines that they designed and trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The numbers in the compressive transformer are very different from the numbers your models obtain, so I\u2019m assuming that you preprocessed that dataset differently and so have a different vocabulary, making those results incomparable?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Could you re-train your model using the same settings as the compressive transformer so that we could compare the results of your method and theirs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The baseline has just 6 layers, which is significantly smaller than the current transformer LMs that are being discussed by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I\u2019m definitely not expecting to see GPT-3 sized models in every research paper (or even close to that) but I think 16 layers would make more sense for such an exploration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "I\u2019m afraid the gains shown here would not transfer to larger models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "On the other hand, larger models might produce better representations resulting in even bigger gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors do not show standard deviation numbers for any of the baselines on the various datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "This makes understanding the results on each dataset, for the different models, harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "For example, on the arxiv dataset the authors talk about a drop from 2.927 to 2.877 perplexity between two different models, but is a 0.05 perplexity difference really statistically significant here?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The authors don\u2019t provide any speed or memory usage statistics.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Of course, it\u2019s totally fine if this model runs slower or uses more memory than the baseline- I expect such preliminary investigations to potentially be nonoptimal, with later work making it an actually viable model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I would not penalize this paper for having slow runtime or using too much memory.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "But I think it's important to discuss these things.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 91, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Weak points:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Section 3 could see more formalization and better description on what are the input for searching the kNN, how the model is trained (maybe, an algorithm figure).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Memory sizes are too big for the data selected.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "It is unclear why such big memories are still improving the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Results are very close for each memory size and there are no error measurements (e.g., std dev).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It is hard to conclude anything about memory sizes and their effectiveness.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Hard to replicate: several proposed datasets are downloaded from the web, it will be hard to download the same files.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "No details on the specific hardware and software used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "What do you refer with \"16 attention heads of size 64\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Describe the content of the figures in the caption (e.g., what is the architecture about?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Also, there will be some challenges to reproduce this work with respect to the datasets that should be addressed as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "there are some benchmarks that require models to be able to handle long inputs, such as document-level QA datasets and document-level retrieval.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}]}, {"paper_idx": 91, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 21, "text": "Weak points:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Section 3 could see more formalization and better description on what are the input for searching the kNN, how the model is trained (maybe, an algorithm figure).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "Memory sizes are too big for the data selected.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "It is unclear why such big memories are still improving the model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "Results are very close for each memory size and there are no error measurements (e.g., std dev).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "It is hard to conclude anything about memory sizes and their effectiveness.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "Hard to replicate: several proposed datasets are downloaded from the web, it will be hard to download the same files.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "No details on the specific hardware and software used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "What do you refer with \"16 attention heads of size 64\"?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Describe the content of the figures in the caption (e.g., what is the architecture about?)", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 41, "text": "Also, there will be some challenges to reproduce this work with respect to the datasets that should be addressed as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 20, "text": "The authors do not compare their model to any previously published model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Throughout the paper the authors only compare their model to baselines that they designed and trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The numbers in the compressive transformer are very different from the numbers your models obtain, so I\u2019m assuming that you preprocessed that dataset differently and so have a different vocabulary, making those results incomparable?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Could you re-train your model using the same settings as the compressive transformer so that we could compare the results of your method and theirs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The baseline has just 6 layers, which is significantly smaller than the current transformer LMs that are being discussed by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I\u2019m definitely not expecting to see GPT-3 sized models in every research paper (or even close to that) but I think 16 layers would make more sense for such an exploration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "I\u2019m afraid the gains shown here would not transfer to larger models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "On the other hand, larger models might produce better representations resulting in even bigger gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors do not show standard deviation numbers for any of the baselines on the various datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "This makes understanding the results on each dataset, for the different models, harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "For example, on the arxiv dataset the authors talk about a drop from 2.927 to 2.877 perplexity between two different models, but is a 0.05 perplexity difference really statistically significant here?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The authors don\u2019t provide any speed or memory usage statistics.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Of course, it\u2019s totally fine if this model runs slower or uses more memory than the baseline- I expect such preliminary investigations to potentially be nonoptimal, with later work making it an actually viable model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I would not penalize this paper for having slow runtime or using too much memory.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "But I think it's important to discuss these things.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 91, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "there are some benchmarks that require models to be able to handle long inputs, such as document-level QA datasets and document-level retrieval.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Out-of-scope"}], "items2": [{"idx": 20, "text": "The authors do not compare their model to any previously published model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "Throughout the paper the authors only compare their model to baselines that they designed and trained.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The numbers in the compressive transformer are very different from the numbers your models obtain, so I\u2019m assuming that you preprocessed that dataset differently and so have a different vocabulary, making those results incomparable?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "Could you re-train your model using the same settings as the compressive transformer so that we could compare the results of your method and theirs?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "The baseline has just 6 layers, which is significantly smaller than the current transformer LMs that are being discussed by the community.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "I\u2019m definitely not expecting to see GPT-3 sized models in every research paper (or even close to that) but I think 16 layers would make more sense for such an exploration.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "I\u2019m afraid the gains shown here would not transfer to larger models.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "On the other hand, larger models might produce better representations resulting in even bigger gains.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "The authors do not show standard deviation numbers for any of the baselines on the various datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "This makes understanding the results on each dataset, for the different models, harder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "For example, on the arxiv dataset the authors talk about a drop from 2.927 to 2.877 perplexity between two different models, but is a 0.05 perplexity difference really statistically significant here?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "The authors don\u2019t provide any speed or memory usage statistics.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 34, "text": "Of course, it\u2019s totally fine if this model runs slower or uses more memory than the baseline- I expect such preliminary investigations to potentially be nonoptimal, with later work making it an actually viable model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "I would not penalize this paper for having slow runtime or using too much memory.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "But I think it's important to discuss these things.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 92, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Currently, FLEET only contains one entity typing dataset (CoNLL).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "This dataset is only used for task & pre-train transfer.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "One could a) include more entity typing datasets b) and offer class transfer or domain transfer on the entity typing task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "On the other hand, it practically makes understanding, reporting, and using the benchmark a bit messy. For example, people will report very specific settings such as FLEET with {4-shot, with meta learning, task transfer}.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 93, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Another question is, the reported ROUGE-2 score on XSUM is quite low (21.2) on ID compared to the original reported result in BART paper (22.27) on the full test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The experimental setup of this paper might be problematic.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Two out of the three tasks are generation tasks in which BLEU or ROUGE-2 evaluation scores are difficult to interpret, i.e. it is very difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experimental results are mixed, i.e. it is difficult to draw strong conclusions from the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Furthermore, it is not clear where the results are significant different among systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 93, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Another question is, the reported ROUGE-2 score on XSUM is quite low (21.2) on ID compared to the original reported result in BART paper (22.27) on the full test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "This is because of two major concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This statement is only true \"on average\" and overlooks the huge differences between different tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The other is that when evaluating the Data2Text generation, only BLEU is used. However, there has been a bank of work that has proved the BLEU has low validity on NLG tasks (e.g., Reiter, 2018). This makes, at least the results on WebNLG, do not reliable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 93, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 20, "text": "Another question is, the reported ROUGE-2 score on XSUM is quite low (21.2) on ID compared to the original reported result in BART paper (22.27) on the full test set.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Their methods only perform comparably to the ensemble method but not consistently and significantly better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although cocktail fine-tuning only does inference once, it's not free and needs a knowledge distillation process before training, which requires inference over whole-training data using the adapter-finetuning model and can be time-consuming.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 93, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The experimental setup of this paper might be problematic.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Two out of the three tasks are generation tasks in which BLEU or ROUGE-2 evaluation scores are difficult to interpret, i.e. it is very difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experimental results are mixed, i.e. it is difficult to draw strong conclusions from the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Furthermore, it is not clear where the results are significant different among systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "This is because of two major concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This statement is only true \"on average\" and overlooks the huge differences between different tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The other is that when evaluating the Data2Text generation, only BLEU is used. However, there has been a bank of work that has proved the BLEU has low validity on NLG tasks (e.g., Reiter, 2018). This makes, at least the results on WebNLG, do not reliable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 93, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The experimental setup of this paper might be problematic.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Two out of the three tasks are generation tasks in which BLEU or ROUGE-2 evaluation scores are difficult to interpret, i.e. it is very difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "The experimental results are mixed, i.e. it is difficult to draw strong conclusions from the results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Furthermore, it is not clear where the results are significant different among systems.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Their methods only perform comparably to the ensemble method but not consistently and significantly better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although cocktail fine-tuning only does inference once, it's not free and needs a knowledge distillation process before training, which requires inference over whole-training data using the adapter-finetuning model and can be time-consuming.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 93, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "This is because of two major concerns.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This statement is only true \"on average\" and overlooks the huge differences between different tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "The other is that when evaluating the Data2Text generation, only BLEU is used. However, there has been a bank of work that has proved the BLEU has low validity on NLG tasks (e.g., Reiter, 2018). This makes, at least the results on WebNLG, do not reliable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Their methods only perform comparably to the ensemble method but not consistently and significantly better.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Although cocktail fine-tuning only does inference once, it's not free and needs a knowledge distillation process before training, which requires inference over whole-training data using the adapter-finetuning model and can be time-consuming.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 94, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The experimental results, especially in Table 4, are not very convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The proposed method seems only work well on reducing race bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Gender bias seems to be the hardest one to mitigate across all methods and Kaneko is doing better on reducing religion bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The paper also need to provide more details on hyper-parameter tuning, e.g. lambda in Eq (3).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "And also more details and ablation studies on negative sampling and layer selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I am not fully convinced about the motivation (intuition) in Sec. 3.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Figure1 definitely presents some good examples of bias in encoders but it is hard to conclude that bias is mostly from the encoder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The paper needs more quantitative analysis on bias in encoders and also decoders to support this claim.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The paper mentioned that bias measurements like WEAT have been questioned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, Stereo-set and Crows-Pairs have also been criticized [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "5.I am wondering that the positions of words in s_g may also affect the attention scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I may have missed something here but has any analysis on the relative positions has been done? E.g. \"man\" comes first or \"woman\" comes first.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "If not, it would be interesting to see if we need to randomly change the order of words in the tuples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "While the work has its merit in decreasing the biases in some metrics, It is bad using those dataset as leaderboard without looking into them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "[1] elaborates on the few problems with CrowS-Pairs and StereoSet.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In [2], it criticizes all the work focusing on word level debiasing and those are really not removing them, but hiding them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "To me, this paper is no different from prior work in this aspect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Also given most of the differences are quite small, I am not sure whether the paper is making meaningful progress.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Also, as described in section 5, it is not scalable for words that need not be debiased.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is an extension of counterfactural data augmentation in the attention with regularization with original attention.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 94, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "The experimental results, especially in Table 4, are not very convincing.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The proposed method seems only work well on reducing race bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Gender bias seems to be the hardest one to mitigate across all methods and Kaneko is doing better on reducing religion bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The paper also need to provide more details on hyper-parameter tuning, e.g. lambda in Eq (3).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "And also more details and ablation studies on negative sampling and layer selection.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I am not fully convinced about the motivation (intuition) in Sec. 3.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Figure1 definitely presents some good examples of bias in encoders but it is hard to conclude that bias is mostly from the encoder.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The paper needs more quantitative analysis on bias in encoders and also decoders to support this claim.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The paper mentioned that bias measurements like WEAT have been questioned.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "However, Stereo-set and Crows-Pairs have also been criticized [1].", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "5.I am wondering that the positions of words in s_g may also affect the attention scores.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "I may have missed something here but has any analysis on the relative positions has been done? E.g. \"man\" comes first or \"woman\" comes first.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "If not, it would be interesting to see if we need to randomly change the order of words in the tuples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "I have a couple of concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The method requires a teacher, unaltered model to be trained in parallel, which can be computationally involved.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I would have liked to understand how much the performance of the model (perhaps on the GLUE tasks) is changed if such a teacher model is not used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I am concerned about the evaluation of bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As recent studies showed, intrinsic measures of bias do not necessarily correlate with bias measures on a concrete, down-stream task that can be used in an application.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.15859 The paper includes the NLI task, however, I perceive that task very similar to the intrinsic tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It doesn't provide insights in what it would happen wrt bias in a down-stream task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I would have liked the evaluation of the model to include a task for which group annotations exist and the bias for the original and modified model are presented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "For example, the Jigsaw dataset could be used for such an evaluation (https://www.kaggle.com/c/jigsaw-unintended-bias-in-toxicity-classification/data), or, for a smaller dataset, HateXplain could be used (https://github.com/hate-alert/HateXplain).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Without such an evaluation for an application oriented task, it is hard to understand whether the few points gained by the new model will lead to significant bias improvement in a downstream task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 94, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "While the work has its merit in decreasing the biases in some metrics, It is bad using those dataset as leaderboard without looking into them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "[1] elaborates on the few problems with CrowS-Pairs and StereoSet.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "In [2], it criticizes all the work focusing on word level debiasing and those are really not removing them, but hiding them.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "To me, this paper is no different from prior work in this aspect.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Also given most of the differences are quite small, I am not sure whether the paper is making meaningful progress.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Also, as described in section 5, it is not scalable for words that need not be debiased.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "It is an extension of counterfactural data augmentation in the attention with regularization with original attention.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 17, "text": "I have a couple of concerns:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "The method requires a teacher, unaltered model to be trained in parallel, which can be computationally involved.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "I would have liked to understand how much the performance of the model (perhaps on the GLUE tasks) is changed if such a teacher model is not used.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "I am concerned about the evaluation of bias.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "As recent studies showed, intrinsic measures of bias do not necessarily correlate with bias measures on a concrete, down-stream task that can be used in an application.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.15859 The paper includes the NLI task, however, I perceive that task very similar to the intrinsic tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "It doesn't provide insights in what it would happen wrt bias in a down-stream task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 24, "text": "I would have liked the evaluation of the model to include a task for which group annotations exist and the bias for the original and modified model are presented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "For example, the Jigsaw dataset could be used for such an evaluation (https://www.kaggle.com/c/jigsaw-unintended-bias-in-toxicity-classification/data), or, for a smaller dataset, HateXplain could be used (https://github.com/hate-alert/HateXplain).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Without such an evaluation for an application oriented task, it is hard to understand whether the few points gained by the new model will lead to significant bias improvement in a downstream task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 95, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 18, "text": "The idea of automatically constructing graphs of unlabeled examples using similarity measures is relatively old in the label propagation literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "One particularly confusing part of vote-k: it was not immediately clear to me how this differs or is better than simple k-means clustering to discover centroids?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Related to the point above, I would have liked to see more ablations on the vote-k selection process. How important is it to use the model confidence \u201cstratification\u201d strategy in the algorithm? This is particularly useful to know since, given a very large set of unlabeled examples |U|, it can be costly to run all u in |U| model predictions to collect confidence scores.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "One source of bias that was not addressed in the paper relates to the choice of unlabeled examples (|U|).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "In this study, the unlabeled corpus for all 10 datasets has been extracted from an existing labeled/supervised corpus (that has been previously annotated).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This may not seem like it, but I believe there is huge amount of supervision and work simply choosing which examples to label in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "In practice, however, for a new task/dataset, this will not be the case if we want to do only \u201cselective annotation\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "I was quite unsure (maybe a little skeptical) that the proposed procedure would work for a \u201crandom\u201d or uncurated corpus of unlabeled examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "There is also no discussion on label distribution in the vote-k process.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Again, I think the current experimental evaluation is taking advantage of curated training datasets where labels tend to be nicely distributed/represented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "So focusing on performing input similarity and model confidence may be enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "For example, for some tasks, a fairly random unlabeled corpus may contain a huge number of \u201cnegative\u201d examples (say you want to automatically find pairs of sentences for entailment - most random pairs of sentences will be NEUTRAL).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Measuring similarities of inputs alone does not seem to be sufficient to match the expected posterior distribution of labels?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Can you please elaborate on this point?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "With respect to the experimental setup, it is a bit jarring the number of combinations of (dataset, model) pairs (Table 1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I understand that it is expensive/infeasible to run all datasets on all models, and I don\u2019t have a good suggestion to improve on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "But I fear that the end result is that subsequent/future work that builds on vote-k may need to re-evaluate your implementation on a partial set of dataset/tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "The novelty of this work is mixed: the idea of using retrieval for in-context learning prompts is not new (this work cites Liu et al., 2022 and Rubin et al., 2022)..", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 56, "text": "The idea of constructing graphs of unlabeled examples is also not new (common in label propagation literature).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "But novelty here is (1) in combining these ideas to make a practical system, and (2) the experimental work demonstrating some insights comparing this with fine-tuning and other selective annotation schemes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 15, "text": "I elaborate on these further below for the authors to address.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Since the selective annotation is based entirely on similarities derived from sentence embeddings, there is nothing explicit ensuring that the label distribution over the selected subset is not skewed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It seems like the optimal value of k in vote-k would depend on the number of instances in the unlabeled set that changes with the tasks. A single value of k=150 was chosen for experiments across all tasks. Could the authors justify this choice further?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the diversity and representativeness measures shown in Table 10 in Appendix F, the difference between Random and vote-k does not appear to be very large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In the least, it's unclear how to assess the differences shown in this table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "A qualitative analysis might be more revealing here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 95, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 18, "text": "The idea of automatically constructing graphs of unlabeled examples using similarity measures is relatively old in the label propagation literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "One particularly confusing part of vote-k: it was not immediately clear to me how this differs or is better than simple k-means clustering to discover centroids?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Related to the point above, I would have liked to see more ablations on the vote-k selection process. How important is it to use the model confidence \u201cstratification\u201d strategy in the algorithm? This is particularly useful to know since, given a very large set of unlabeled examples |U|, it can be costly to run all u in |U| model predictions to collect confidence scores.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "One source of bias that was not addressed in the paper relates to the choice of unlabeled examples (|U|).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "In this study, the unlabeled corpus for all 10 datasets has been extracted from an existing labeled/supervised corpus (that has been previously annotated).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This may not seem like it, but I believe there is huge amount of supervision and work simply choosing which examples to label in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "In practice, however, for a new task/dataset, this will not be the case if we want to do only \u201cselective annotation\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "I was quite unsure (maybe a little skeptical) that the proposed procedure would work for a \u201crandom\u201d or uncurated corpus of unlabeled examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "There is also no discussion on label distribution in the vote-k process.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Again, I think the current experimental evaluation is taking advantage of curated training datasets where labels tend to be nicely distributed/represented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "So focusing on performing input similarity and model confidence may be enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "For example, for some tasks, a fairly random unlabeled corpus may contain a huge number of \u201cnegative\u201d examples (say you want to automatically find pairs of sentences for entailment - most random pairs of sentences will be NEUTRAL).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Measuring similarities of inputs alone does not seem to be sufficient to match the expected posterior distribution of labels?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Can you please elaborate on this point?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "With respect to the experimental setup, it is a bit jarring the number of combinations of (dataset, model) pairs (Table 1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I understand that it is expensive/infeasible to run all datasets on all models, and I don\u2019t have a good suggestion to improve on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "But I fear that the end result is that subsequent/future work that builds on vote-k may need to re-evaluate your implementation on a partial set of dataset/tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "The novelty of this work is mixed: the idea of using retrieval for in-context learning prompts is not new (this work cites Liu et al., 2022 and Rubin et al., 2022)..", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 56, "text": "The idea of constructing graphs of unlabeled examples is also not new (common in label propagation literature).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "But novelty here is (1) in combining these ideas to make a practical system, and (2) the experimental work demonstrating some insights comparing this with fine-tuning and other selective annotation schemes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I have a few comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I believe this was not mentioned in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In section 2.1, It is not very clear how the first M/10 examples are labeled. It is mentioned that \u201cthe current labeled L has M/10 samples\u201d which is used to label other instances. Are they manually labeled?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It is stated that the vote-k method encourages diversity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2 shows a direct comparison between fine-tuned RoBERTa large and models such as DaVinci-002 while it is stated that the authors do not aim to conduct a head-to-head comparison. What is the comparison the authors are making here and perhaps could it be shown better with another figure? Results from this direct and not fair comparison are also mentioned in the abstract which could be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Some parts about the proposed method are not clear, and there are a few missing and potentially misleading pieces in the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 95, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 18, "text": "The idea of automatically constructing graphs of unlabeled examples using similarity measures is relatively old in the label propagation literature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "The main weakness of the paper is the lack of a thorough description and analysis (including ablations) of the vote-k algorithm and its relationship to existing clustering algorithms.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 23, "text": "One particularly confusing part of vote-k: it was not immediately clear to me how this differs or is better than simple k-means clustering to discover centroids?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Related to the point above, I would have liked to see more ablations on the vote-k selection process. How important is it to use the model confidence \u201cstratification\u201d strategy in the algorithm? This is particularly useful to know since, given a very large set of unlabeled examples |U|, it can be costly to run all u in |U| model predictions to collect confidence scores.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 27, "text": "One source of bias that was not addressed in the paper relates to the choice of unlabeled examples (|U|).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "In this study, the unlabeled corpus for all 10 datasets has been extracted from an existing labeled/supervised corpus (that has been previously annotated).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 29, "text": "This may not seem like it, but I believe there is huge amount of supervision and work simply choosing which examples to label in the first place.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 30, "text": "In practice, however, for a new task/dataset, this will not be the case if we want to do only \u201cselective annotation\u201d.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 31, "text": "I was quite unsure (maybe a little skeptical) that the proposed procedure would work for a \u201crandom\u201d or uncurated corpus of unlabeled examples.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 32, "text": "There is also no discussion on label distribution in the vote-k process.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 33, "text": "Again, I think the current experimental evaluation is taking advantage of curated training datasets where labels tend to be nicely distributed/represented.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 34, "text": "So focusing on performing input similarity and model confidence may be enough.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 35, "text": "For example, for some tasks, a fairly random unlabeled corpus may contain a huge number of \u201cnegative\u201d examples (say you want to automatically find pairs of sentences for entailment - most random pairs of sentences will be NEUTRAL).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 36, "text": "Measuring similarities of inputs alone does not seem to be sufficient to match the expected posterior distribution of labels?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 37, "text": "Can you please elaborate on this point?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 38, "text": "With respect to the experimental setup, it is a bit jarring the number of combinations of (dataset, model) pairs (Table 1).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 39, "text": "I understand that it is expensive/infeasible to run all datasets on all models, and I don\u2019t have a good suggestion to improve on this.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 40, "text": "But I fear that the end result is that subsequent/future work that builds on vote-k may need to re-evaluate your implementation on a partial set of dataset/tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 55, "text": "The novelty of this work is mixed: the idea of using retrieval for in-context learning prompts is not new (this work cites Liu et al., 2022 and Rubin et al., 2022)..", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 56, "text": "The idea of constructing graphs of unlabeled examples is also not new (common in label propagation literature).", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 57, "text": "But novelty here is (1) in combining these ideas to make a practical system, and (2) the experimental work demonstrating some insights comparing this with fine-tuning and other selective annotation schemes.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "I do not think the proposed method is novel, though it is effective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It is an ensemble of existing techniques.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 95, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "I elaborate on these further below for the authors to address.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Since the selective annotation is based entirely on similarities derived from sentence embeddings, there is nothing explicit ensuring that the label distribution over the selected subset is not skewed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It seems like the optimal value of k in vote-k would depend on the number of instances in the unlabeled set that changes with the tasks. A single value of k=150 was chosen for experiments across all tasks. Could the authors justify this choice further?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the diversity and representativeness measures shown in Table 10 in Appendix F, the difference between Random and vote-k does not appear to be very large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In the least, it's unclear how to assess the differences shown in this table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "A qualitative analysis might be more revealing here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I have a few comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I believe this was not mentioned in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In section 2.1, It is not very clear how the first M/10 examples are labeled. It is mentioned that \u201cthe current labeled L has M/10 samples\u201d which is used to label other instances. Are they manually labeled?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It is stated that the vote-k method encourages diversity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2 shows a direct comparison between fine-tuned RoBERTa large and models such as DaVinci-002 while it is stated that the authors do not aim to conduct a head-to-head comparison. What is the comparison the authors are making here and perhaps could it be shown better with another figure? Results from this direct and not fair comparison are also mentioned in the abstract which could be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Some parts about the proposed method are not clear, and there are a few missing and potentially misleading pieces in the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}]}, {"paper_idx": 95, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 15, "text": "I elaborate on these further below for the authors to address.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Since the selective annotation is based entirely on similarities derived from sentence embeddings, there is nothing explicit ensuring that the label distribution over the selected subset is not skewed.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "It seems like the optimal value of k in vote-k would depend on the number of instances in the unlabeled set that changes with the tasks. A single value of k=150 was chosen for experiments across all tasks. Could the authors justify this choice further?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "From the diversity and representativeness measures shown in Table 10 in Appendix F, the difference between Random and vote-k does not appear to be very large.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "In the least, it's unclear how to assess the differences shown in this table.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "A qualitative analysis might be more revealing here.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "I do not think the proposed method is novel, though it is effective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It is an ensemble of existing techniques.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 95, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "I have a few comments:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "I believe this was not mentioned in the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "In section 2.1, It is not very clear how the first M/10 examples are labeled. It is mentioned that \u201cthe current labeled L has M/10 samples\u201d which is used to label other instances. Are they manually labeled?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It is stated that the vote-k method encourages diversity.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Figure 2 shows a direct comparison between fine-tuned RoBERTa large and models such as DaVinci-002 while it is stated that the authors do not aim to conduct a head-to-head comparison. What is the comparison the authors are making here and perhaps could it be shown better with another figure? Results from this direct and not fair comparison are also mentioned in the abstract which could be misleading.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Some parts about the proposed method are not clear, and there are a few missing and potentially misleading pieces in the paper.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "I do not think the proposed method is novel, though it is effective.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It is an ensemble of existing techniques.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 22, "text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The results about kNN-task adaptive pretraining seem to have a confound: The method for retrieving data for this intermediate training step involves finding sentences with high similarity, but the task on which this method is shown to lead to improvements is itself a semantic similarity task. The claim is that the method should be helpful for many kinds of tasks, but my intuition is that this \"coincidental\" alignment of the data collection and the evaluation task makes the result less generalizable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The empirical results about zero-shot QA don't help much because the task is clearly too difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The improvement on the models trained with the new method is from F1 of ~0.001 to ~0.01.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Do larger LMs succeed at this task?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If so, then it seems that models tested in this paper are just too small or ineffective for the task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Is there an easier task where this method leads to strong performance but ordinary pretraining does not?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1.The introduction of the motivation (the concept of in-context bias) is not easy to understand at the very beginning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper said: \u201cthe pretrained NLM can model much stronger dependencies between text segments that appeared in the same training example, than it can between text segments that appeared in different training examples.\u201d Acutally it seems quite natural for me and I did not realize it is a problem until I saw more explanations in section 1.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2.The theory is a bit complicated and not easy to follow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "3.The experiments are limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, there are many other tasks that involve sentence pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 22, "text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The results about kNN-task adaptive pretraining seem to have a confound: The method for retrieving data for this intermediate training step involves finding sentences with high similarity, but the task on which this method is shown to lead to improvements is itself a semantic similarity task. The claim is that the method should be helpful for many kinds of tasks, but my intuition is that this \"coincidental\" alignment of the data collection and the evaluation task makes the result less generalizable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The empirical results about zero-shot QA don't help much because the task is clearly too difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The improvement on the models trained with the new method is from F1 of ~0.001 to ~0.01.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Do larger LMs succeed at this task?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If so, then it seems that models tested in this paper are just too small or ineffective for the task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Is there an easier task where this method leads to strong performance but ordinary pretraining does not?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1. I think the presentation of the paper needs to be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "For the theoretical analysis, it was not clear to me what is the contribution of the current analysis compared to the Levine 2020 paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I felt similar conclusions can be drawn from the results of that paper as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It will be good to rewrite highlighting the contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I also noticed a lot of repeated text in section 2 from Levine 2020 paper; will be good to modify.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "2. The empirical part of the paper shows improved performance of adding similar sentences to the context of LM training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I felt this was quite separate from the theoretical analysis. It does not follow from the theoretical results that adding similar sentences will be a good thing. As a result, having these in the same paper looked incoherent to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "3. The experimental results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The gains are not super high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The empirical section sounded disparate from the theoretical part of the paper, and the results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 22, "text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The results about kNN-task adaptive pretraining seem to have a confound: The method for retrieving data for this intermediate training step involves finding sentences with high similarity, but the task on which this method is shown to lead to improvements is itself a semantic similarity task. The claim is that the method should be helpful for many kinds of tasks, but my intuition is that this \"coincidental\" alignment of the data collection and the evaluation task makes the result less generalizable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The empirical results about zero-shot QA don't help much because the task is clearly too difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The improvement on the models trained with the new method is from F1 of ~0.001 to ~0.01.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Do larger LMs succeed at this task?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If so, then it seems that models tested in this paper are just too small or ineffective for the task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Is there an easier task where this method leads to strong performance but ordinary pretraining does not?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "No major weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some minor aspects:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "correct on less than 50 questions out of 20, 000 in the evaluation set \u2013 Separating thousands by commas (American system) is ok.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Separating them by space is also ok.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Doing both is not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "correct on roughly than 250 questions in the evaluation set \u2013 \"than\" shouldn't be there", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Equation 4 would have been easier to read if the right-hand side was expanded to S1 and S2's {w_2^j} notation from the previous line.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 22, "text": "I also have some doubts about the empirical results.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 23, "text": "The results about kNN-task adaptive pretraining seem to have a confound: The method for retrieving data for this intermediate training step involves finding sentences with high similarity, but the task on which this method is shown to lead to improvements is itself a semantic similarity task. The claim is that the method should be helpful for many kinds of tasks, but my intuition is that this \"coincidental\" alignment of the data collection and the evaluation task makes the result less generalizable.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 24, "text": "The empirical results about zero-shot QA don't help much because the task is clearly too difficult.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The improvement on the models trained with the new method is from F1 of ~0.001 to ~0.01.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 26, "text": "Do larger LMs succeed at this task?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 27, "text": "If so, then it seems that models tested in this paper are just too small or ineffective for the task.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 28, "text": "Is there an easier task where this method leads to strong performance but ordinary pretraining does not?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The only two tasks examined are sentence similarity tasks (which seem a bit more like a sanity check), and NaturalQuestions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It's particularly surprising to me that this works so well on NQ, and I wish the authors had dug a bit deeper into this, but I also recognize that page limits exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Where else do you think the in-context bias could be useful?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It wasn't intuitive for me that it'd be useful for NQ.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you have any initial experiments on the \"self-improving\" aspect of this technique?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is mentioned several times, but there are no initial results or anything suggesting that it might be a promising direction to pursue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1.The introduction of the motivation (the concept of in-context bias) is not easy to understand at the very beginning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper said: \u201cthe pretrained NLM can model much stronger dependencies between text segments that appeared in the same training example, than it can between text segments that appeared in different training examples.\u201d Acutally it seems quite natural for me and I did not realize it is a problem until I saw more explanations in section 1.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2.The theory is a bit complicated and not easy to follow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "3.The experiments are limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, there are many other tasks that involve sentence pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1. I think the presentation of the paper needs to be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "For the theoretical analysis, it was not clear to me what is the contribution of the current analysis compared to the Levine 2020 paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I felt similar conclusions can be drawn from the results of that paper as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It will be good to rewrite highlighting the contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I also noticed a lot of repeated text in section 2 from Levine 2020 paper; will be good to modify.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "2. The empirical part of the paper shows improved performance of adding similar sentences to the context of LM training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I felt this was quite separate from the theoretical analysis. It does not follow from the theoretical results that adding similar sentences will be a good thing. As a result, having these in the same paper looked incoherent to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "3. The experimental results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The gains are not super high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The empirical section sounded disparate from the theoretical part of the paper, and the results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1.The introduction of the motivation (the concept of in-context bias) is not easy to understand at the very beginning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper said: \u201cthe pretrained NLM can model much stronger dependencies between text segments that appeared in the same training example, than it can between text segments that appeared in different training examples.\u201d Acutally it seems quite natural for me and I did not realize it is a problem until I saw more explanations in section 1.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2.The theory is a bit complicated and not easy to follow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "3.The experiments are limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, there are many other tasks that involve sentence pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "No major weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some minor aspects:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "correct on less than 50 questions out of 20, 000 in the evaluation set \u2013 Separating thousands by commas (American system) is ok.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Separating them by space is also ok.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Doing both is not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "correct on roughly than 250 questions in the evaluation set \u2013 \"than\" shouldn't be there", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Equation 4 would have been easier to read if the right-hand side was expanded to S1 and S2's {w_2^j} notation from the previous line.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weakness:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1.The introduction of the motivation (the concept of in-context bias) is not easy to understand at the very beginning.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The paper said: \u201cthe pretrained NLM can model much stronger dependencies between text segments that appeared in the same training example, than it can between text segments that appeared in different training examples.\u201d Acutally it seems quite natural for me and I did not realize it is a problem until I saw more explanations in section 1.1.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "2.The theory is a bit complicated and not easy to follow.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "3.The experiments are limited.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, there are many other tasks that involve sentence pairs.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The only two tasks examined are sentence similarity tasks (which seem a bit more like a sanity check), and NaturalQuestions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It's particularly surprising to me that this works so well on NQ, and I wish the authors had dug a bit deeper into this, but I also recognize that page limits exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Where else do you think the in-context bias could be useful?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It wasn't intuitive for me that it'd be useful for NQ.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you have any initial experiments on the \"self-improving\" aspect of this technique?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is mentioned several times, but there are no initial results or anything suggesting that it might be a promising direction to pursue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "1. I think the presentation of the paper needs to be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "For the theoretical analysis, it was not clear to me what is the contribution of the current analysis compared to the Levine 2020 paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "I felt similar conclusions can be drawn from the results of that paper as well.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "It will be good to rewrite highlighting the contributions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I also noticed a lot of repeated text in section 2 from Levine 2020 paper; will be good to modify.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "2. The empirical part of the paper shows improved performance of adding similar sentences to the context of LM training.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I felt this was quite separate from the theoretical analysis. It does not follow from the theoretical results that adding similar sentences will be a good thing. As a result, having these in the same paper looked incoherent to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "3. The experimental results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The gains are not super high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The empirical section sounded disparate from the theoretical part of the paper, and the results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "