type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "I felt this was quite separate from the theoretical analysis. It does not follow from the theoretical results that adding similar sentences will be a good thing. As a result, having these in the same paper looked incoherent to me.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 19, "text": "3. The experimental results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "The gains are not super high.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 21, "text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 25, "text": "The empirical section sounded disparate from the theoretical part of the paper, and the results are weak.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The only two tasks examined are sentence similarity tasks (which seem a bit more like a sanity check), and NaturalQuestions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It's particularly surprising to me that this works so well on NQ, and I wish the authors had dug a bit deeper into this, but I also recognize that page limits exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Where else do you think the in-context bias could be useful?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It wasn't intuitive for me that it'd be useful for NQ.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you have any initial experiments on the \"self-improving\" aspect of this technique?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is mentioned several times, but there are no initial results or anything suggesting that it might be a promising direction to pursue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 96, "reviewer1": "review#4", "reviewer2": "review#5", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "No major weaknesses", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Some minor aspects:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "correct on less than 50 questions out of 20, 000 in the evaluation set \u2013 Separating thousands by commas (American system) is ok.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Separating them by space is also ok.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Doing both is not.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "correct on roughly than 250 questions in the evaluation set \u2013 \"than\" shouldn't be there", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Equation 4 would have been easier to read if the right-hand side was expanded to S1 and S2's {w_2^j} notation from the previous line.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "Weaknesses:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The only two tasks examined are sentence similarity tasks (which seem a bit more like a sanity check), and NaturalQuestions.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "It's particularly surprising to me that this works so well on NQ, and I wish the authors had dug a bit deeper into this, but I also recognize that page limits exist.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Where else do you think the in-context bias could be useful?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "It wasn't intuitive for me that it'd be useful for NQ.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Do you have any initial experiments on the \"self-improving\" aspect of this technique?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "This is mentioned several times, but there are no initial results or anything suggesting that it might be a promising direction to pursue.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 97, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "However, after a careful reading of the paper, I still could hardly find enough insights from the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "How could the findings benefit the community? The design principle of EncDec-based NMT is the sub-optimal performance/efficiency of LM-based NMT, and this paper just says: \"yes, the intuition is true\", which is not exciting at all.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "One insight is the zero-shot performance of multilingual NMT, however, I do not think there are many differences between 4.80 BLEU scores and 7.95 BLEU scores, where are too low to make sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This is an OK paper but the current version is not exciting, which could hardly attract attention from the community.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors should answer the question: how could the paper findings benefit the community?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Despite good empirical efforts, the motivation of this paper seems somewhat unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Given that the encoder-decoder paradigm dominantly governs machine translation (also from the experiment part of this paper that LMs underperform EncDec most of the time. ), for what reason should we need to consider a shift to a unified language model for such a seq2seq task? If the zero-shot transfer is the case, why not directly fix the off-target issue for EncDec and preserve the good of translation performance, instead of changing the paradigm?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I feel like the authors didn't convey an incentive for this.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper conducted extensive experiments to show how scaling affects LMs for MT, however, few suggestions based on the findings are given for future development of MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The setting of CausalLM seems a bit weird that a unidirectional encoding behavior makes obviously no sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}]}, {"paper_idx": 97, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 7, "text": "However, after a careful reading of the paper, I still could hardly find enough insights from the paper.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 8, "text": "How could the findings benefit the community? The design principle of EncDec-based NMT is the sub-optimal performance/efficiency of LM-based NMT, and this paper just says: \"yes, the intuition is true\", which is not exciting at all.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 9, "text": "One insight is the zero-shot performance of multilingual NMT, however, I do not think there are many differences between 4.80 BLEU scores and 7.95 BLEU scores, where are too low to make sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This is an OK paper but the current version is not exciting, which could hardly attract attention from the community.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The authors should answer the question: how could the paper findings benefit the community?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses: Some experiments require more careful design to make findings more reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Such as, in Figure 2(d), the Test Bleu of EndDec, CasualLM-Deep, and Prefix-TopOnly-Deep decrease as the parameters increase in the interval of #Params 0.19-0.43E8.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This phenomenon is not in line with the general trend, also the intuition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I suspect that the experimental settings could be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Othervise, the authors need to give a reasonable explanation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 97, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "Despite good empirical efforts, the motivation of this paper seems somewhat unclear.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Given that the encoder-decoder paradigm dominantly governs machine translation (also from the experiment part of this paper that LMs underperform EncDec most of the time. ), for what reason should we need to consider a shift to a unified language model for such a seq2seq task? If the zero-shot transfer is the case, why not directly fix the off-target issue for EncDec and preserve the good of translation performance, instead of changing the paradigm?", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "I feel like the authors didn't convey an incentive for this.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "The paper conducted extensive experiments to show how scaling affects LMs for MT, however, few suggestions based on the findings are given for future development of MT.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The setting of CausalLM seems a bit weird that a unidirectional encoding behavior makes obviously no sense.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Weaknesses: Some experiments require more careful design to make findings more reliable.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "Such as, in Figure 2(d), the Test Bleu of EndDec, CasualLM-Deep, and Prefix-TopOnly-Deep decrease as the parameters increase in the interval of #Params 0.19-0.43E8.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "This phenomenon is not in line with the general trend, also the intuition.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I suspect that the experimental settings could be improved.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "Othervise, the authors need to give a reasonable explanation.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 98, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#2", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The idea of using entity abstraction is straightforward and not very novel as doing POS tagging for input is widely used in many NLP models, especially in traditional NLP pipelines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "While the performance on synthetic datasets is promising, on realistic datasets the proposed method fails to improve the performance.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Therefore the effectiveness of the method is not fully supported by the experiments.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Like the authors pointed out, the current conclusion could be due to the noisy entity type predictions or the fact that the tasks do not require entity abstraction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As the related work section discussed, there are a lot of other related works also incorporating knowledge from named entities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "To me, using only the entity type is marginally novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Also, the empirical findings lack deeper analysis and were merely conducted on a single and toy-sized pretrained language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 98, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The idea of using entity abstraction is straightforward and not very novel as doing POS tagging for input is widely used in many NLP models, especially in traditional NLP pipelines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "While the performance on synthetic datasets is promising, on realistic datasets the proposed method fails to improve the performance.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Therefore the effectiveness of the method is not fully supported by the experiments.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The experiment part lacks analysis on the model which could provide more intuitions on how the model works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "More analytic experiments will bring more interesting findings of the proposed model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "For instance, we could dive deep into why the model variants perform differently between CLUTRR and HotpotQA & CoQA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 98, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 10, "text": "The idea of using entity abstraction is straightforward and not very novel as doing POS tagging for input is widely used in many NLP models, especially in traditional NLP pipelines.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 11, "text": "While the performance on synthetic datasets is promising, on realistic datasets the proposed method fails to improve the performance.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Therefore the effectiveness of the method is not fully supported by the experiments.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "My major concern is about the experiments. Specifically,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There is very minor boost in performance on QA datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The goal of the proposed model should be improving real-world data, instead of synthetic data (CLUTTR in this paper).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The performance of baselines are far below the current top performers.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I understand that the paper is not interested in achieving new state-of-the-art, and the test set used might be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, in that case you can not prove that the proposed methods are still beneficial on a model that achieves way better baseline results.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "An analogy here is that something working under low-resource setting is not necessarily effective when there is adequate access to data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "We\u2019ve seen different methods work for different datasets.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Missing Reference"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If there is a new reasoning dataset, which architecture in Table 1 should people use?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Testing all five is very inefficient.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}]}, {"paper_idx": 98, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Like the authors pointed out, the current conclusion could be due to the noisy entity type predictions or the fact that the tasks do not require entity abstraction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As the related work section discussed, there are a lot of other related works also incorporating knowledge from named entities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "To me, using only the entity type is marginally novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Also, the empirical findings lack deeper analysis and were merely conducted on a single and toy-sized pretrained language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The experiment part lacks analysis on the model which could provide more intuitions on how the model works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "More analytic experiments will bring more interesting findings of the proposed model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "For instance, we could dive deep into why the model variants perform differently between CLUTRR and HotpotQA & CoQA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 98, "reviewer1": "review#2", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 9, "text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "Like the authors pointed out, the current conclusion could be due to the noisy entity type predictions or the fact that the tasks do not require entity abstraction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 11, "text": "Without the deeper analysis, readers cannot generalize the conclusion beyond the three datasets mentioned in this paper to other unexplored downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "As the related work section discussed, there are a lot of other related works also incorporating knowledge from named entities.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "To me, using only the entity type is marginally novel.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Also, the empirical findings lack deeper analysis and were merely conducted on a single and toy-sized pretrained language model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "My major concern is about the experiments. Specifically,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There is very minor boost in performance on QA datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The goal of the proposed model should be improving real-world data, instead of synthetic data (CLUTTR in this paper).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The performance of baselines are far below the current top performers.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I understand that the paper is not interested in achieving new state-of-the-art, and the test set used might be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, in that case you can not prove that the proposed methods are still beneficial on a model that achieves way better baseline results.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "An analogy here is that something working under low-resource setting is not necessarily effective when there is adequate access to data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "We\u2019ve seen different methods work for different datasets.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Missing Reference"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If there is a new reasoning dataset, which architecture in Table 1 should people use?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Testing all five is very inefficient.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}]}, {"paper_idx": 98, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 8, "text": "The experiment part lacks analysis on the model which could provide more intuitions on how the model works.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 9, "text": "More analytic experiments will bring more interesting findings of the proposed model.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 10, "text": "For instance, we could dive deep into why the model variants perform differently between CLUTRR and HotpotQA & CoQA.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 11, "text": "My major concern is about the experiments. Specifically,", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 12, "text": "There is very minor boost in performance on QA datasets.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The goal of the proposed model should be improving real-world data, instead of synthetic data (CLUTTR in this paper).", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "The performance of baselines are far below the current top performers.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I understand that the paper is not interested in achieving new state-of-the-art, and the test set used might be different.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "However, in that case you can not prove that the proposed methods are still beneficial on a model that achieves way better baseline results.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Misunderstanding"}, {"idx": 18, "text": "An analogy here is that something working under low-resource setting is not necessarily effective when there is adequate access to data.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "We\u2019ve seen different methods work for different datasets.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Missing Reference"}, {"idx": 21, "text": "If there is a new reasoning dataset, which architecture in Table 1 should people use?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Testing all five is very inefficient.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Vague Critique"}]}, {"paper_idx": 99, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#3", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Representational similarity For beginners, the word of representation similarity is not easy to follow. At least, authors should define the terminology in the introduction.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The title In particular, the linkage between title and abstract seems not clear at the first glance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Please consider to update the title and abstract so that readers can easily understand the topic theme.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Is English best? There are many languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In my opinion, I view that authors selected English language to represent the basis of all things in nature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Why English(e.g., language system such as polysemous, grammar, dataset availability\u2026)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Justification of English employment would make the paper better.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are they all Americans and share the same contexts? (e.g., cultures\u2026)", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Discussion I feel strange because the paper end with \u201cDiscussion\u201d. Please consider adding conclusion section or rename the last section as \u201cDiscussion and Conclusion\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The paper doesn't have too many weaknesses.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I was wondering if we could get some numbers on if the stacked representations help in additional downstream tasks like say classification (i.e. does the performance on imagenet improve if you use imagenet + text). However, I understand that this can be significant undertaking and do not want to base my review on this experiment but it is a potential future direction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 99, "reviewer1": "review#1", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 12, "text": "Representational similarity For beginners, the word of representation similarity is not easy to follow. At least, authors should define the terminology in the introduction.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Invalid Criticism"}, {"idx": 13, "text": "The title In particular, the linkage between title and abstract seems not clear at the first glance.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "Please consider to update the title and abstract so that readers can easily understand the topic theme.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "Is English best? There are many languages.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "In my opinion, I view that authors selected English language to represent the basis of all things in nature.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "Why English(e.g., language system such as polysemous, grammar, dataset availability\u2026)?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "Justification of English employment would make the paper better.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 20, "text": "Are they all Americans and share the same contexts? (e.g., cultures\u2026)", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Neglect"}, {"idx": 22, "text": "Discussion I feel strange because the paper end with \u201cDiscussion\u201d. Please consider adding conclusion section or rename the last section as \u201cDiscussion and Conclusion\u201d.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Inexpert Statement"}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "My main concerns are that this set of experiments suggests some clear next setups that I don't nesc. think are out of scope for this work. Specifically:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If the goal in practice is to reconstruct human judgments --- I would have liked the authors to compare against a supervised setup.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "How much of the remaining misalignments can be fixed by gathering n << N^2 additional pairwise judgments and then training a supervised model ontop of the models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would have liked to have seen more experiments with even less supervision --- is it possible to gather n < N captions, and train a model on those very few pairs to map to text, and then use the resulting modality --> text model as input to the models shown here?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I would have appreciated more discussion of tags vs. captions for the images.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In figure 3A, I assume that the text only methods use captions unless specified otherwise. But --- because the tags were not fed to, e.g., BERT/RoBERTa, I can't tell if the performance gains are due to the full captions being used, or if the gains are due to the more expressive models. A trivial linearization of, e.g., \"A photo of a tag1, tag2, and tag3\" handed to a LLM would have been a nice comparison to see.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Finally, I would have been interested in a fully LLM setup where instead of pairwise cosine similarities, both text pairs are fed to a very large LLM (e.g., OPT or GPT3) and the model outputs a likert rating as text.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}]}, {"paper_idx": 99, "reviewer1": "review#3", "reviewer2": "review#4", "items1": [{"idx": 14, "text": "The paper doesn't have too many weaknesses.", "reliability": "No", "error_type": "Superficial Review"}, {"idx": 15, "text": "I was wondering if we could get some numbers on if the stacked representations help in additional downstream tasks like say classification (i.e. does the performance on imagenet improve if you use imagenet + text). However, I understand that this can be significant undertaking and do not want to base my review on this experiment but it is a potential future direction.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}], "items2": [{"idx": 13, "text": "My main concerns are that this set of experiments suggests some clear next setups that I don't nesc. think are out of scope for this work. Specifically:", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 14, "text": "If the goal in practice is to reconstruct human judgments --- I would have liked the authors to compare against a supervised setup.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 15, "text": "How much of the remaining misalignments can be fixed by gathering n << N^2 additional pairwise judgments and then training a supervised model ontop of the models?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 16, "text": "I would have liked to have seen more experiments with even less supervision --- is it possible to gather n < N captions, and train a model on those very few pairs to map to text, and then use the resulting modality --> text model as input to the models shown here?", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 17, "text": "I would have appreciated more discussion of tags vs. captions for the images.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 18, "text": "In figure 3A, I assume that the text only methods use captions unless specified otherwise. But --- because the tags were not fed to, e.g., BERT/RoBERTa, I can't tell if the performance gains are due to the full captions being used, or if the gains are due to the more expressive models. A trivial linearization of, e.g., \"A photo of a tag1, tag2, and tag3\" handed to a LLM would have been a nice comparison to see.", "reliability": "Yes", "error_type": null}, {"idx": 19, "text": "Finally, I would have been interested in a fully LLM setup where instead of pairwise cosine similarities, both text pairs are fed to a very large LLM (e.g., OPT 